24\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: We will take you there. Tonight, don't forget, \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  host a town hall former President Trump in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania. You can watch that live right here on Fox News Channel, 09:00 p.m. Eastern Time. And as always, we'll cover everything. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. The Ingraham Angle is now.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. Kamala is on the move,\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE U.S., (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Traveled our country, meeting with our young leaders. By the way, I love Gen Z, I just love Gen Z.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Turns out Gen Z may not love her back. We're going to dig into the latest polls, plus her phony appeal to populism is not fooling anyone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), 2024 VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: She's trying to say elect me President and I'll somehow do all the things that I haven't done for the last three and a half years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And we'll take you inside camp Kamala, the debate prep.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Madam Vice President, how is debate prep going?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Compelling. But first, her anti-freedom agenda. That's the focus of tonight's Angle. All right, my friends, tonight, Trump's got the momentum. More Hispanic voters, more black men, more Asian Americans and independents are now willing to hear Republicans out on the big issues where before they weren't. Now remember, most are poorer after going on four years of Biden and Harris and now many are left wondering, why am I a Democrat again?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Democratic Party has been hijacked by radical extremists under the guise of being, quote, progressive. The party has really given in to these extremists who no longer care about the safety well-being of children and women, literally pushing for sex changes for underage children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: At a time like this, the Democrats needed a candidate who could actually defend what her own party is doing and defend her own record and ideas. But with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , all they can do is play this tedious game of hide the ball. And today, the Harris campaign wouldn't answer a simple question about her supposed EV mandate walk back dodging the matter, according to Axios.\par \par \uc2 Again, she is not discussing policy because she's a nightmare off the cuff. It's not some game of 3D chess here. And also, because her policies aren't popular, her campaign flex decided to have her simply pretend that she's had a change of heart.\par \par \uc2 Well, Harris' campaign has said, she no longer supports many of her past progressive positions and has embraced more centrist stances on health care, immigration, gun control and fracking, Axios reports. Well, centrist, wow, knowing what we know about her, that's just not at all credible, because if you are in favor of using the power of the state to surveil and prosecute your political opponents, and if you can justify censoring those political opponents, whether through government pressure on social media companies or through straight on intimidation, then I hate to tell it to you, hate to break it to you, you're a wild eyed extremist, a committed, even if inarticulate, radical Leftist, and that's what Kamala is.\par \par \uc2 She hasn't even tried, by the way, to walk back her support back in 2019 for banning Trump from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and remember, he was the sitting U.S. President.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Here we have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with 65 million \uc2 Twitter\uc2  followers, he and his account should be taken down. This is a matter of corporate responsibility. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  should be held accountable and shut down that site. It is a matter of safety and corporate accountability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: While running in the Democrat primary, she was fine with bullying social media companies that she saw as a threat to the Democrats' chances of winning in 2020 so what was the clear message? Don't even try to hide behind the right to free speech, because if you do social media companies, the government hammer is going to come down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: There has to be a responsibility that is placed on these social media sites to understand their power. They are directly speaking to millions and millions of people without any level of oversight or regulation. That has to stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, if she could, she would obviously be comfortable with something closer to the new Brazil model of dealing with free speech, which is basically just shut it down. A corrupt Brazilian Supreme Court agreed to block \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's X there nationwide. So, the President is a Left-wing thug. He's desperate to protect his own power by any means necessary, which would, yes, include arresting Mr. Musk himself or one of his in country representatives if X continued to try to operate without requested censorship by the government that it demanded.\par \par \uc2 Now, the President vowed that Brazil was not obliged to put up with Musk's extreme Right-wing ideology. The president of Brazil. Sometimes it's hard to tell. But naturally, American liberals who hate Musk and hate what he stands for, which is free speech, they're defending Brazil's actions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER\uc2 , AUTHOR, BURN BOOK: If you want to operate in Brazil, you operate by their rules, and these are their rules. It's their country, and they don't -- they are very worried about misinformation there. A lot of tech companies are finally being held accountable for certain behaviors and practices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Funny how behaviors and practices that are most offensive to them always seem to come from the other side of the political aisle. But isn't it reasonable to assume that, as governments continue to fail their own people, and they are failing their own people worldwide, they fail to adhere to their wishes and to grow the standard of living of their own citizens, and when those people grow restless, that those same governments, rather than responding to the will of the voters, that they'll simply respond with censorship.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GWU LAW PROFESSOR: If Brazil can succeed in banning \uc2 Twitter\uc2  from the entire country, it will be replicated. This is the ultimate destination for this movement, where, if they can't convince people to give up their own freedoms to embrace censorship, they're going to start to take sites offline to prevent people from hearing opposing views.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And keep an eye on Europe. Because, as Newsweek noted today, its own regulatory landscape for social media platforms is becoming more stringent. Translation, it's becoming more hostile to free speech. Their Digital Services Act, DSA, could be used to impose massive fines and even shut down Musk's X. Of course, authorities would cite concerns about online safety and the need for content moderation for justifying their censorship.\par \par \uc2 The truth is, America increasingly stands alone in support of free speech, and yet more often, Democrats see free speech as an impediment, rather than a God given right, an impediment to their agenda, to their grip on power. Now remember, the Angle warned you that when the federal bureaucracy cracked down against independent minded doctors like our own Ramin Oskoui during COVID, they were seen as a threat to the medical industrial complex ruled and ruled over by his lowness \uc2 Tony Fauci\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , FORMER NIAID DIRECTOR: It can be dangerous to the health of the nation when disinformation dissuades people from making use of what could be life-saving interventions like vaccinations, wearing a mask, and abiding by good public health practices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Should be in jail. Now, this was a harbinger of the anti-free speech forces at work in American politics, of course, clever Democrat strategists that know that attacks on our free speech, they're not very popular, so they always deflect by dubbing \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' plans as well, that's the real freedom agenda. But now we see that their view of freedom only extends to a limited number of activities, like you have the freedom to abort your own offspring. You have the freedom, consume as much porn as you want, do as many drugs as you want, maybe gamble your life savings away. You can do all that.\par \par \uc2 But they do not believe in the freedom of voters who have questions that they want answered. Voters who want to know what policies candidates are actually standing for. And Kamala's idea of freedom doesn't include the Second Amendment either. We know that.\par \par \uc2 So, if they can win this election with an empty pantsuit candidate who can't and won't discuss the issues, well, why won't they use the same playbook in four or eight or 12 years? And at that point, would we even qualify as being a free country with free speech? And that's the Angle.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , former GOP presidential candidate. Vivek, a lot of folks watching tonight will say to themselves, perhaps, oh, this is just in places like Brazil and the loopy liberals over there in Europe. But what's your warning tonight for America if \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with her supposed freedom agenda gets elected?\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2  (R) FORMER U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Look, I think that's coming to a social media platform near you, right here in the United States of America. One of the most mysterious parts of this, Laura, is some of the most vocal proponents of the U.S. preventing authoritarianism abroad have nothing to say about the censorship and banning of a platform like X in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 This is part of a broader trend. You look at \uc2 Pavel Durov\uc2 , the founder of Telegram arrested in France, allegedly for at least violating some of their diktats relating to the suppression of free speech. He wasn't arrested in Russia. He was arrested in France, an ally of the United States. So, I do think there's an irony for the very people who preach the importance of freedom and democracy and spreading that abroad happen to be the ones who are most silent when it comes to actually defending democratic norms, even amongst our allied nations, from France to Brazil to Canada to the UK.\par \par \uc2 So, I do believe the same \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  many ways that threaten those social media companies to silence information here at home, are at least complicit in saying not a peep, or worse, we don't know the facts yet, or worse, as it relates to permitting and even condoning our allies suppressing their own speech in their supposed democracies. So, it's a complicated issue, and there's a lot more to say, but I think that's an interesting start --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Vivek, we know that they speak out on issues that matter to them, even if they happen overseas, issues that really are important to them, a lot of the social issues. But when we have British authorities threatening Americans with extradition if they violate their social media company rules with what they post or what they say. And we don't have -- I didn't see a single Democrat politician say, don't even think about it.\par \par \uc2 To me, I mean what does that say to America about our own free speech and how protected it really is?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I think it goes to the heart of American identity, Laura, because what separates the United States of America from most other countries is that we are bound by a common set of ideals enshrined in our Constitution and our Bill of Rights. The First Amendment came first for a reason, free expression of speech and free expression and practice of your religion, that's what makes America itself.\par \par \uc2 So, when other allied nations are betraying those values, it is ironic that the very people who go intervene in the name of protecting democracy in places like Ukraine have nothing to say to actually defend the values that define America amongst our own allied nations.\par \par \uc2 This goes a little bit off of the traditional Republican or conservative view here, Laura, but I will also say that it's a moment for us to look ourselves in the mirror and as we properly criticize Brazil for banning X, I do think we would put ourselves in a compromise position if we also, as the United States, get in the business of banning entire social media platforms as well, and we're speaking at a moment where TikTok is likely to be banned if nothing changes with respect to its ownership in about seven or eight months --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, I disagree with you on that --\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: -- different issues --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I want to debate that, yes --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: -- respectfully.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You're smart, but it's an inapt analogy, having the Communist Chinese run a company in the United States to clearly influence U.S. elections, and that's what's going on. That's very different from having a free media platform --\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Which we (inaudible) against.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, so, but I get what you're saying, and I understand --\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: But the truth of the matter is, I don't like any government deciding what people can or cannot see. And I think America is at our strongest when we stand on that core principle. And I think this rises above just the Republican versus Democrat issue --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: -- but if we're committed to free speech, that means we're committed to free speech. That's who we are as Americans. And I proudly call myself an absolutist when it comes to the First Amendment --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Vivek. I want to move to another topic, which is that DOJ seems to be back to Russia, Russia, Russia because they announced indictments against Russians for alleged election interference. So, the media's favorite talking point, they think is back. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, NBC NEWS JUSTICE REPORTER: They're not saying explicitly that Russia is trying to help Republicans, but if you just read the context of it, it's extremely easy to figure out what we're talking about here and what the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  of the Justice Department is alleging that Russia was doing really to booster \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's efforts in 2024 after doing that eight years ago in 2016.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Vivek, what's going on here? Are they laying the groundwork for more censorship?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, the reality is 