6 when he won.\par \par \uc2 The group on the other hand, though, that you have to watch out for are college-educated women, particularly in the suburbs. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has got to do better with them than he did in 2020. That's the one group I'll give an advantage to the Democrats on, compared to how Trump previously did.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's inroads with groups that typically don't look like Republicans are real and they're lasting, and that is one of his biggest factors going into this election.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, I think they're trying to, maybe, Chris, get such a big banked percentage with male voters that will swamp the gender gap. But I think when you see Trump in these smaller events, when he's just mixing it up with people, I think he should do more of that and focus on the issues that women care about, their family, their safety, their pocketbook.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, on the abortion issue, the states will decide that. But in the primary issues that people care about most, I think Trump should just do a lot of, you know, outreach among female voters. I think he actually will do better than a lot of people believe.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BEDFORD, AUTHOR, THE BELTWAY BRIEF: Yeah, he's a very actually mobile candidate or changeable candidate for a 78-year-old billionaire. Usually, you don't get folks being willing to change that much. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- one of the things his campaign has pushed, I think, that has a lot of success in this, is portraying him as a family man, as a grandfather.\par \par \uc2 Some of those advertisements have been put out, some of the more heartwarming ones, his relationship with his grandchildren, his closeness with his family is something that's been, that's kind of softened the edges around a person who's made a career for 40 years of being a pretty brash New York businessman. Now he's kind of tacking away differently to that, toning down some of the attacks.\par \par \uc2