up like this. And then he wanders in this syntax. Now you see where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gets it from. So, what's the difference? What did you really get when the switcheroo was done? Very little. Certainly not clarity.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, now did you see this photo on social media of distant Walz family members supporting Trump?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. Well, I guess they're close relatives, but still it's kind of --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: They're not close relatives, but they're related. They're related via the grandfather. And it is authentic. And look, Jim Walz -- \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 's brother, Jeff Walz, rather, he has -- is really the person to focus on here. He's come out against his brother's candidacy.\par \par \uc2 And look, I'm not a big fan of airing the family grievances in public, but the legacy media tripped over themselves, Laura, to bring out Mary Trump and Kerry Kennedy and the Kennedy clan taking potshots at RFK Jr. Do you think they'll be featuring the Walz's family anytime soon? The defection watch.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, good point. I see what you're saying. Now, I do not think we will be seeing any of the distant tree branches in the family tree bearing much anti-Walz fruit on CNN. No, I don't see that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Right. Either near nor far. You won't hear anything about them. But look, Harris was on the stump today speaking, not quite hearing what people are saying. It appears she was the keynote speaker at a bromide convention.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Let us understand what we do, our purpose at this very moment. Let us remember the broad shoulders upon which we stand, and the traditions of this very fight that have led to what we have been able to accomplish.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What if the shoulders aren't broad? What if the shoulders are narrow shoulders? Does that count? It always has to be broad shoulders.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: A fight is a tradition. None of it made any sense. I tried to follow it. I even rewound it a few times. Can't make sense of it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No. Well, she's going to have to do better than that, though, Raymond, when she faces \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  next week in the debate. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: How's debate prep?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Madam Vice President, how's debate prep?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Don't let that answer fool you, Laura. She is spending a lot of time in debate prep. In fact, Philippe Reigns, remember him? He used to guest on this show early on. He is playing Trump in the mock sessions with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And according to NBC News, Harris is now wrestling with how to throw Biden under the bus on things like Afghanistan, the immigration crisis. So, it looks like they're trying to find an artful way to, you know, say, well, that was the old man's fault. I was just an innocent bystander. I thought she was the last person in the room, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I think Philip Reins is a good choice for that, getting under the skin of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . I think that actually probably is a pretty good choice.\par \par \uc2 But Raymond, I go back to that little desk, OK? Putting Corbyn in the little desk.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I knew you'd fixate on the little desk.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It just bothers me. I mean, he was, or he is the President of the United States. And he doesn't come up with this idea.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But look how tiny it is. It's embarrassingly small.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, wait a second. I saw that desk before. Now I know. That was Robert Reich's desk at the Labor Department.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I knew you'd go for the cheap height gag.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, they go low, we go lower. No, Raymond, I just -- all right, Raymond, that was just -- I'm not going to get that out of my head. Raymond, thank you, thank you, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I'm going to go get one of those.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on social media. Thank you for watching. Oh, we have some oyster men from Martha's Vineyard who are Trumpers. Go online.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240905ek9400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Soon, Update On Deadly Georgia High School Shooting; Now, V.P. Harris On Deadly Georgia High School Shooting; V.P. Harris Says, Have To End Epidemic Of Gun Violence; Now, Vice President Harris Campaigns In New Hampshire; Khanna Slams Harris' Plan To Tax Unrealized Gains; VP Harris Talks Economy; Early Voting Days Away; Feds Accuse Russia And Iran Of Meddling In Election\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Steve Harrigan, Nicole Parker, Brian Brenberg,  Jessica Tarlov, Dagen McDowell, Kerri Urbahn, Paul Mauro \par \uc2 9,829 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [15:00:00]\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: John and Sandra, we do expect an update shortly and we will take you there live. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum and this is The Story.\par \par \uc2 Keeping an eye on this, Vice President Harris, this is in New Hampshire. We expect to hear from her shortly. She will be speaking about the economy. We do expect that we'll get some reaction from her with regard to this tragedy that is unfolding now in Georgia. Four high school students killed at Apalachee High School in Winder, Georgia, outside of Atlanta. Nine others are in the hospital. We watched some of them being taken away on stretchers in this horrific scene playing out in the early weeks of the brand new school year.\par \par \uc2 The suspect is in custody and is alive, which happens, but not often. in these situations. And we're going to learn more about who that suspect is shortly, we imagine.\par \par \uc2 Senior Correspondent Steve Harrigan reports live from outside the school, which is in Barrow County, Georgia. Good afternoon to you, Steve. Good afternoon, Martha. The facts now, as we know them, the numbers, four people killed by this shooter, at least nine wounded, serious enough to be hospitalized, many of them taken away by helicopter, and as many as 30 wounded overall, some of those apparently wounded trying to run away from the shooting, perhaps injured limbs. The shooter himself in custody and alive.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: School age, we don't know anything more about him. We're likely to hear more details about the identities of the victims and the shooter at a law enforcement press conference sometime within the next hour. And there's a huge law enforcement presence on the ground, really all hands on deck here, all branches answering the call very quickly. You have local law enforcement, Georgia State law enforcement, as well as the \uc2 FBI\uc2  on the scene here.\par \par \uc2 And just walking up to this scene, it was a really chaotic sense. For about a mile around this school, you could tell that parents simply jumped out of their cars, left doors swinging, left them parked wherever to try and run here to get their children, not only from this high school, but from a nearby elementary school and a daycare center as well.\par \par \uc2 The stories are what we're going to hear next. Those stories just beginning to come out. A young man who saw his math teacher shot, another young man in the restroom, hearing shots and fleeing out a window. So, these stories really have panic and it's panic you see in many of those parents faces after being reunited with their fortunate children. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Steve. Thank you. Continuing coverage of this and a lot of breaking news today on a number of fronts, you see the ambulance is going by in the scene here. And your heart just goes into your stomach. You think about how these parents feel when they get the word that this time is their school that is in this situation.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Nicole Parker, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent and Fox News contributor. Nicole, you have been on the frontlines of these situations. Take us through what you believe is happening now as we wait for another briefing where we expect we are likely to learn a little bit more about this suspect who is alive, which was also the situation in the Parkland shooting.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE PARKER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: So, Martha, obviously, the initial response is extremely chaotic. We are highly trained in law enforcement to respond to active shooters right now, especially school shootings, and you can train all you want, but there's no textbook response. Everyone is doing the best that they can right now. All -- it's always to, you know, stop the killing and stop the dying. In this instance, the suspect is in custody, so it slows the tempo down for law enforcement drastically.\par \par \uc2 The next focus now is on the victims. It's a victim-centered approach for law enforcement. There's a massive group of law enforcement that is at the school processing the crime scene, going through piece by piece, and that takes hours and hours and hours, days, frankly. And then on the other side, you still have students that are probably trying to reunite with their parents. There may be parents who don't know where their child is. And that is a very difficult situation, because when you send your child off to school, you are not thinking that you're going to have to worry that they are going to get killed. And, sadly, it's happening in America all too often.\par \par \uc2 And so the reunification with the parents and the children is on one side, and then you've got the crime scene, but also the investigative efforts of law enforcement, you've got the federal, state and local law enforcement. Local law enforcement always takes jurisdiction in active shooters. Unless it's a terroristic related shooting, the local law enforcement is going to have jurisdiction. And in this instance, that definitely appears to be the case. Local law enforcement is in charge.\par \par \uc2 [15:05:00]\par \par \uc2 You have local law enforcement sheriff's office, \uc2 Georgia Bureau of Investigation\uc2 , but also the ATF and \uc2 FBI\uc2 . After the Sandy Hook Elementary School shooting, that's when the investigative Assistance for Violent Crimes Act of 2012 was signed into law by \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in January 2013, and that's what allows the federal agencies to come in to assist.\par \par \uc2 But let's make no mistake. This is a local law enforcement-headed investigation incident and they are doing, I believe, an excellent job. When you respond to something like this, it is important to keep the public aware. People need to know that their community is safe. We talked about the fact that there was a high school and a neighboring elementary.\par \par \uc2 Parents, after what they saw happen at Uvalde, Martha, they don't trust necessarily that law enforcement is going to keep their child safe. The fact that there were law enforcement officers in that hallway for a substantial period of time, and there was unnecessary death that occurred, based on my understanding, as a parent, you have to know that it's ultimately on you to get to that school and to make sure that your child is safe.\par \par \uc2 But the toughest part Martha is when your child is not being reunited with you. And if there has not been that notifications conducted yet, which, in my experience, it takes a substantial period of time. And that's why you haven't heard them yet released the names because the next of kin and the guardians, the parents, legal guardians, always have to be notified.\par \par \uc2 We (INAUDIBLE) if they're students or a combination. But based on what I heard, there was some heroic actions going on by the teachers. And we don't have facts, but we heard from someone that their teacher was shot. Again, not verified by law enforcement, but if that is the case, that is what I saw in Parkland as well. There were staff members, three staff members killed in the Parkland school shooting, and they did everything that they could to protect the innocent. And that's what they are there to do. And we need to remember that your teachers are so important and they play a vital role and you need to thank them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. We saw that in Connecticut as well. It's Sandy Hook teachers putting their bodies in front of their students and they shouldn't have to be in that environment. It's not what you sign up for as a teacher. No doubt there are heroic situations, but it doesn't make it any easier at this moment then to imagine these four families and also the extent of the injuries of these other students that have.\par \par \uc2 And I should have pointed out at the very top, Nicole, that you were an agent Parkland, at that school shooting. In that case, the shooter also -- he drove away and then he was apprehended. How different is this? Because in the typical situation, these shooters take their own lives at the scenes of these crimes, but in that case, he wanted to get away, and we don't know, you know, what transpired in terms of whether or not this shooter was trying to escape. We understand he is a school age person. That's all we've got so far. How quickly do they investigate and start questioning this individual? What is that side of all of this look like at this moment?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: That's a very important element, right, because the suspect, they need to face justice. And in the Parkland incident, he actually didn't drive away. He actually blended in with a group of students, and like a sick individual was, running out of the school as if he himself was a victim as well, along with all the other students that were absolutely terrified. He blended in with the crowd. He went off. He ended up going to a local \uc2 Walmart\uc2 . He went to a \uc2 McDonald's\uc2 . He went to the subway inside the \uc2 Walmart\uc2 . You know, he actually was chatting with one of the siblings of an individual that was shot and asked if he could get a ride.\par \par \uc2 So, again, that's what my experience was with what happened at Parkland. But the moment that they could identify who it was and they took him down, the local law enforcement in Parkland, took him down and arrested him, that was absolutely critical to the investigation. And, again, you're right, a lot of times they do take their own lives or they are neutralized. But in this instance --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nicole, I want to jump in. I want to thank you so much for your firsthand experience as an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent on other school shooting scenes, including Parkland. Stand by if you will. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the vice president of the United States, speaking right now in New Hampshire. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT, DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: This is just a senseless tragedy on top of so many senseless tragedies, and it's just outrageous. It's that every day in our country, in the United States of America, that parents have to send their children to school worried about whether or not their child will come home alive. It's senseless. It is. We've got to stop it. And we have this epidemic of gun violence in our country once and for all. You know, it doesn't have to be this way. It doesn't have to be this way.\par \par \uc2 So, we will continue, of course, to send our prayers and our thoughts to the families and all those who were affected, including -- you know, I'm going off script right now, but, listen, I mean, you know, at the last year, I started a college tour, and I traveled our country meeting with our young leaders, right?\par \par \uc2 [15:10:17]\par \par \uc2 And so it was college age, you know, so I did trade schools, colleges, universities, community colleges. By the way, I love Gen Z. I just love Gen Z.\par \par \uc2 But I'll tell you, one of the things, one of the things that I asked every time I went to the auditorium, and it would be filled with these young leaders, students, and I'd ask them, raise your hand if at any point between kindergarten and 12th grade, you had to endure an active shooter drill. And for the young leaders who are here who are raising their hand, I'm telling you, every time the auditorium was packed and almost every hand went up.\par \par \uc2 You know, a lot of us, I'll talk -- I'll speak about myself, you know, we had -- well I grew up in California -- earthquake drills, we had fire drills, but our kids are sitting in a classroom where they should be fulfilling their God-given potential and some part of their big beautiful brain is concerned about a shooter busting through the door of the classroom. It does not have to be this way. It does not have to be this way. And, you know, this is one of the many issues that's at stake in this election.\par \par \uc2 So, New Hampshire, we have 62 days to go, 62 days. I'm going to tell you what you already know. This race is going to be tight until the very end. So, please let's not pay too much attention to the polls because we are running as the underdog. We know what they're capable of on the other side. The only thing we can take for granted is the love that we as Americans have for each other, knowing we have so much more in common than what separates us. And we've got some hard work ahead. But we like hard work. Hard work is good work, hard work can be joyful work. And so we are up for the task. And with your help, we will win in November. We will win in November.\par \par \uc2 And that, in large part, is because we know what we stand for. And when you know what you stand for, you know what to fight for. We love our country and we believe in the promise. The promise of America that's so much of what I think brings us all together this afternoon. We believe in the promise of America, and that includes a topic we're going to discuss today, which is what I call an opportunity economy, building an opportunity economy.\par \par \uc2 And my vision of an opportunity economy is one where everyone can compete and have a real chance, to succeed where everyone, regardless of who they are, where they start, can build wealth, including intergenerational wealth, where workers are treated with dignity and everyone has the freedom to join a union if they choose, where we remove barriers to opportunity, so anyone who wants to start a business can have access to the tools and the resources they need to do that.\par \par \uc2 I believe, and I said it before to Nicole, I believe America's small businesses are an essential foundation to our entire economy. Think about it. Think about it. Small businesses in our country employ half of all private sector workers. Half of all private sector workers own or run a small business or work for a small business. They do trillions of dollars of business every year. They generate revenue that helps repair our roads and pay our teachers.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, for all of those who are or know of small business owners, the thing I love about you is that you're not only leaders in business, you are civic leaders. You are community leaders. You are mentors. You hire locally. You believe in the community and you're part of the glue of the fabric that holds communities together. You provide the local meeting space. You are the types of folks where you know who your regulars are and when someone's walking in the door and you can tell they've had a bad day, you know exactly what they need.\par \par \uc2 [15:15:07]\par \par \uc2 Isn't that the best of who we are? Isn't that the best of who we are?\par \par \uc2 And I have met so many entrepreneurs across the country who take the incredible leap of faith that is required to start a small business, folks who put their life savings on the line and work through the weekends and holidays, because they aren't just building a business, they're pursuing a dream. They're building a better future for their employees and for the people they love and their communities. And by extension, they're building a stronger middle class and a stronger America for us all.\par \par \uc2 And so all of this is why, as president, one of my highest priorities will be to strengthen America's small businesses. And here, I am in New Hampshire to announce a few elements of my plan to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, first, we're going to help more small businesses and innovators get off the ground, okay? Now, I'm setting what some I'm sure are going to call a very ambitious goal, but you know what? I think we should admire ambition in each other. So, I want to see 25 million new small business applications by the end of my first term.\par \par \uc2 And to help achieve this, we will lower the cost of starting a new business. So, here's the thing. On average, it costs about $40,000 to start a new business in America. That is a great financial barrier for a lot of folks. And it can hold entrepreneurs back. And the current tax deduction for a startup is just $5,000, okay? So then you got to make up the delta there, got to figure out how you're going to do that. Not everyone has access to that kind of wealth and capital. So, part of my plan is we will expand the tax deduction for startups to $50,000. It's essentially a tax cut for starting a small business.\par \par \uc2 Second, my plan will help existing small businesses grow. We will provide low and no interest loans to small businesses that want to expand. And we will, and this is very important, cut the red tape that can make starting and growing a small business more difficult than it needs to be, more difficult than it needs to be. For example, we will make it cheaper and easier for small businesses to file their taxes, similar to how individuals can take a standard deduction.\par \par \uc2 You know, I said to my team, now I'm going to date myself again because they no longer do it, but kind of like you remember the 1040EZ, like, that kind of idea, right? Like let's just take away some of the bureaucracy in the process to make it easier for people to actually do something that's going to benefit our entire economy.\par \par \uc2 Third, my plan will invest in small businesses and innovators throughout America. And here's why. We know that talent exists everywhere in our country, from rural towns to city centers. But not everyone has access to the financing, to venture capital or expert advice. It's not that they don't have the skills. It's not that they don't have the work ethic. It's access to these resources.\par \par \uc2 So, under my plan, we will expand access to venture capital. We will support innovation hubs and business incubators, and we will increase federal contracts with small businesses. And we will have a particular focus on small businesses in rural communities, like right here in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Finally, my plan will make our tax code more fair while also prioritizing investment and innovation. So, let us be clear. Billionaires and big corporations must pay their fair share in taxes.\par \par \uc2 [15:20:03]\par \par \uc2 Because here's the thing, it's just not right that those who can most afford it are often paying a lower tax rate than our teachers and our nurses and our firefighters. It's just not right. It's just not right.\par \par \uc2 So, that's why I support a billionaire minimum tax and corporations paying their fair share. And while we ensure that the wealthy and big corporations pay their fair share, we will tax capital gains at a rate that rewards investment in America's innovators, founders, and small businesses.\par \par \uc2 So, here's the detail. If you earn a million dollars a year or more, the tax rate on your long-term capital gains will be 28 percent under my plan, because we know when the government encourages investment, it leads to broad-based economic growth, and it creates jobs, which makes our economy stronger.\par \par \uc2 Now, compare that to what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  plans. He intends to cut off federal programs that give loans to small businesses. He plans to give billionaires massive tax cuts and to cut corporate taxes by over a trillion dollars, even as they pull in record profits. His plans will add more than $5 trillion to the national debt and that is on top. Of the $2 trillion tax cut he gave them when he was president and when he exploded the deficit.\par \par \uc2 We know how to count. We know how to count. And now he also wants to impose what, in effect, is a national sales tax on everyday products and basic necessities, which will skyrocket the cost for families and small businesses.\par \par \uc2 And New Hampshire, on top of all of this, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were to win in November, he intends to end the Affordable Care Act, which would significantly increase costs on small businesses, as we know. And, well, you know what? The courts are going to handle that and we will handle November. How about that? That's how we'll handle that. How about that?\par \par \uc2 But think about it. He wants to end the Affordable Care Act, taking us back to a time when insurance companies could deny people with pre-existing conditions. You remember what that was, children with asthma, breast cancer survivors, grandparents with diabetes?\par \par \uc2 So, yes, look, we are here to tell him and them we are not going back. We're not going back. We're not going back. We're not going back. No, we are not, and we will move forward, because ours is a fight for the future, and it is a fight for freedom. And it is a fight for freedom.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don't have to tell the folks of New Hampshire, the live free or die state, you know the importance of individual freedoms, fundamental freedoms. You know how hard people fought for these freedoms, and that they are foundational to who we are and what we stand for as America, including the fundamental freedom of a woman to make decisions about her own body and not her government telling her what to do.\par \par \uc2 And understand how we got here. Understand how we got here, and, of course, we're not going to fall for the gaslighting he's pushing, right? When he was president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  handpicked three members of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  with the intention that they would undo the protections of Roe v. Wade. And as he intended, they did. And now, in more than 20 states in our nation, there is a Trump abortion ban, many with no exceptions, even for rape and incest, which is immoral.\par \par \uc2 [15:25:07]\par \par \uc2 And let us agree, and I know we do. One does not have to abandon their faith or deeply held beliefs to agree the government should not be telling her what to do with her body. And if he wins, you can be sure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will go further. And he will sign a national abortion ban, and, you know, Project 2025, which I just -- I mean, can you believe they put that thing in writing? And under Project 2025, he would create a national anti- abortion coordinator, which would force states -- I mean, this is unbelievable, right, which would force states to report on women's miscarriages and abortions.\par \par \uc2 You know what they are making really quite clear? They just don't trust women. They just don't trust women. But we trust women. And Congress passes a bill to restore reproductive freedom as president of the United States, I will proudly sign it into law.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay, we'll keep a close eye on that. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the vice president campaigning in New Hampshire right now.\par \par \uc2 With me now, Brian Brenberg, co-host of the Big Money Show on Fox Business and host of the special Anti-Semitism on Campus, Students Speak, that's tomorrow on Fox Nation, very timely, and Dagan McDowell, co-host of The Bottom Line on Fox Business, and Jessica Tarlov, co-host of The Five.\par \par \uc2 Okay. There's a lot there. Obviously, Harris is up by about five, I believe, in New Hampshire right now. I don't think any Republican has won in New Hampshire since \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . But there was a lot in there that I think the Trump campaign would take serious issue with, Brian.\par \par \uc2 There was a plan in the beginning when but not a lot of specifics in terms of how these things would happen. There would be a tax benefit to starting a company. She talked about cutting red tape, but didn't talk about what kind of regulations would specifically be cut. And then we can get into some of the rest of it, but let's start there.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BRENBERG, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes. The red tape Biden administration was a regulation. Let's just be clear. So, if she's going to be cutting red tape, tell me how and where you're going to do that. I can think of some places you could start, but they haven't shown any willingness to do it.\par \par \uc2 I'd also say this, on the startups, they want to encourage more startups by giving you a bigger tax deduction if you start a business. But she also said the minute you get successful, you're going to have to start paying higher taxes, higher corporate taxes, higher capital gains rate taxes. So, tell me why you want to plant a bunch of flowers, and then as soon as they start getting beautiful spray, we'd killer all over him, because that's basically the Harris plan. That doesn't work. And I don't think small businesses are excited about getting punished the minute they get good at their small business.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. In fact, let's play this from \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , this was this morning, reacting to some of this plan. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): Let's say you're an entrepreneur you create a company and it gets to a $100 million or $200 million on paper. Now, if you're taxing that, you're probably going to force that person to sell it. They're probably going to sell it to private equity. Do you really want the entrepreneurs to be forced to sell their companies to larger institutions? I get why but this is not the right way to do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay. Jessica, your take on what you're hearing.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, I think that debate within the party is extremely healthy and I'm glad that we're doing it and that people are coming out and representing their point of view on this and I'm sure that Vice President Harris will take someone like \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 's ideas on board and perhaps improvise on that. But he is talking about someone getting to a $100 million or $200 hundred million. He's not talking about someone who is making $300,000 a year, right, and trying to be able to go to \uc2 Disney\uc2  and still send their kids to school and have a good retirement.\par \par \uc2 And I think that what you're seeing, and it's reflected in all of these polls, is that the American people are hearing \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  loud and clear that she is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for the people. And in contrast, they are hearing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as someone who is out to protect people who have as much money as he does. And it's going to get very difficult for her detractors to keep saying, she's got nothing, she's got nothing, she's got nothing. If she is rolling out these policies, they're going to have to keep nitpicking, moving the goalposts about all of this.\par \par \uc2 [15:30:00]\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's exactly what people want to hear who are in the middle class, that they're going to help me start a business, that you're going to bring back --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm looking forward to the debate because there were so many things in there that are not part of the Trump campaign at all. And so, there's no opportunity when you're listening to this, to hear the other side.\par \par \uc2 She said that he wanted a national sales tax, not true. That he wanted to end Obamacare, not true. That he wanted to cut off cuts to tax cuts to small businesses, also not true. And then, she labeled a ton of 2025, the project thing that he has completely --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But JD Vance --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- said he'd back and is not a part of the Republican platform.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But -- so what's going on with Project 2025, and the problem for Trump and Vance is that, the people who lead this project are doing interviews and undercover footage is coming out of them, talking about how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really does support this. And JD Vance writes the forward to the book of one of the main leaders of this. And the stuff that she's talking about is in Project 2025, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But it's not in his platform or his campaign, and his campaign has actual policies on the website.\par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: He's not talking about --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: He's not talking about the intentional hardship that they, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , inflicted on the American people that they have had to suffer with the last three-and-a-half years. And I'm not just talking about inflation, prices are up 20 percent or more. They're not falling and they're continuing to rise.\par \par \uc2 And then the American people, the business owner and operator, and businesses are failing at a much faster rate because of inflation, because of the regulation. They have been put in the vice grip of high prices still rising, and higher interest rates, and delinquencies are spiraling out of control. And so, Biden and Harris, yes, she's been there for three-and-a- half years, have put people in a vice grip and turned it at every moment.\par \par \uc2 I want to add one more thing. There are two things that she is proposing that will dry up startup capital. So she's handing out money with one hand, and she is kicking small businesses and startups in the ass with her left foot. One, unrealized capital gains tax, that deprives -- that is taxing money on stocks that have not been sold, that will dry up funding for startups in this country.\par \par \uc2 It is a tax on capital. It is an existential threat to innovation. I have heard this from the biggest liberals in Silicon Valley, number one. And number two, there is no VC funding right now out in Silicon Valley because of Lina Khan and the threat to mergers in the technology industry. And so, this hurts innovation, it hurts development.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it's interesting, Jessica. She says we can count. I think people on all sides of the aisle can count.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And they're talk -- that's what Dagen is talking about here. They can also count. They understand that when the government hands you money, someone -- another taxpayer is paying for it on the other side. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes. We have excellent counters of all political persuasions, but we also do have nonpartisan groups like the Penn-Wharton group that put out their budget model and said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would take the deficit 5.8 trillion. She'd only add 1.2 trillion. You had \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  that came out today, and they said that the GDP will significantly decrease under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and will continue to increase under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Look at what \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has said, also nonpartisan, talking about how -- if you're going to round up a bunch of immigrants and you're going to have tariffs, which he keeps saying isn't a tax on consumers, and that's what the national taxes that people are talking about, if you're going to implement tariffs, guess what's going to happen?\par \par \uc2 The economy is going to suffer for it. That's not what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is saying proposing --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm going to steal 30 seconds for Brian here, because you've been listening patiently. I want to get your last thought on this.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BRENBERG, FOX NEWS HOST: I love the conversation here, but when you're talking about deficits, just remember this. If we get any kind of deficit under Trump, it's because he's cutting taxes. People keeping their own money. Kamala wants to spend money. You keep your own money, government spend your money. That is a stark contrast and that's what this election is about economically. Don't forget that amidst all of this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But, you know what --\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: This year, $2 trillion budget deficit and we have lost 100,000 manufacturing jobs in the last year-and-a-half. So, what is that investment out of the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Joe Biden White House gotten you?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Eight hundred thousand manufacturing jobs --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: We have lost 100,000 manufacturing jobs in the last year-and-a- half, and the manufacturing industry has been in a recession for more than two years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All you have to do is take a good drive around a lot of rural areas in this country and you can see what has happened to manufacturing in the country. But this is all fodder for the great debate that we are now engaged in for the next 60 plus days in this country. And everybody needs to listen to all sides here and figure out what is right for you.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all for being here. It's great to see you.\par \par \uc2 So our breaking coverage continues of this such a sad story today for these families whose children went off to high school this morning in Winder, Georgia. We have a new update on what happened here as they are questioning this suspect in the case. I'll be back in moments with \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Chief John Chell next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Are we going to listen to this young man? Sound coming in to us from students at the Apalachee High School in Barrow County, Georgia about an hour east of Atlanta. Four people are dead, we don't know if all of them are students or not. We're waiting for some more clarity on this. Nine people are injured who were in that school.\par \par \uc2 And again, we're not 100 percent sure whether they are students or faculty, or both. The suspected gunman is in custody and here is a student describing what he saw.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Multiple gunshots, rapid fires and, like two times, maybe like two rounds. Like it was just constant and it just didn't stop.\par \par \uc2 So we just thought it was like there's something falling down because we always hear those type of stuff, but it was like rapid. And then, we had the little hardware lockdown thing on the little board, and then they closed the doors and we barricaded those and everything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What a terrifying situation for that young man. Let's bring in \uc2 New York Police Department\uc2  Chief of Patrol John Chell who oversees most of the department's uniformed officers who would respond to and responds to shootings on a daily basis nearly. So let's bring him in. Chief, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 We were going to talk to you about some other aspects of what's going on in the city and crime, but we want to get your reaction to this and to the response that you saw. And any thoughts on what kind of gun may have been used in the description that young man gives.\par \par \uc2 JOHN CHELL, CHIEF OF PATROL, NEW YORK POLICE DEPARTMENT: Well, first, it's a horrific incident down here in Georgia. I mean, so sad for the families and, you know, what happened here. My questions would be, you know, where did he get the gun? Were there indicators that this young man obviously needs help? What was his security apparatus at the school? Are they trained in active shooter drills? So a lot of questions have to be answered.\par \par \uc2 Thank God he's apprehended. Hopefully the other nine survived this. But, you know, here in the city we have 1.1 million students, I have 1,400 buildings to secure and we have a really large plan active shooter communication with principals after school, preschool.\par \par \uc2 Communication is key here when it comes to intelligence. What's the intel on this? We'll find out and how to react. And that's so horrific, but schools are so vulnerable and valuable to us. Here in the city we take it very seriously, like most of the country does, obviously.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. A school resource officer, we understand, was on the scene. I'm not sure if there was more than one. How effective do you think that program is?\par \par \uc2 CHELL: Well, when it comes to active shooter, whether you won by yourself or 20, there's one mandate to stop that threat. And we know as law enforcement what that can mean to us. You have to stop the threat. There is no alternative in the active shooter situation.\par \par \uc2 So it was one, I don't know how he reacted or not, but he had done something. You have to do something. There's no alternative here. We understand what it is.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, in terms of -- we didn't get a lot of specifics from Vice President Harris. She was obviously saddened by what happened and for the families as well. She said it was a senseless tragedy. She said it doesn't have to be this way, but she didn't get specific about what she felt would end this.\par \par \uc2 And we always, you know, have the gun discussion around this. You hear the rapid fire that is described by the student in terms of what heard, which leads me to believe, having covered a lot of these, that it's probably an automatic.\par \par \uc2 CHELL: It could be a semi, could be an auto, I don't know if it's a handgun or an AR-15, any type of gun but --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A lot of thing.\par \par \uc2 CHELL: -- where did he get that gun? There's a lot of questions that will be answered down the road. I think it's too early get into that. But what were the indications, like I said, family, where they get the gun from, what was the motive, what did the school have in place, all these will be answered, but terrible.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What about hardening schools? What is your policy here in New York in terms of going through mags (ph), in terms of having an armed officer on the scene at schools?\par \par \uc2 CHELL: So, great question. We have a school safety division that's not armed. We do mag (ph). We also do roving mag (ph) where you never know we're going to show up. We also do reverse magging (ph). So people say, what's that? When you're leaving school, we might show up and see what you had in school. That's key for us.\par \par \uc2 But the communication as intel -- we constantly get school threats every day, and we will start to get them now. We have to vet them out properly. And you got to start at a level really high, because to take it for granted, obviously you can have something like this.\par \par \uc2 So we have a large plan, like I said, in the city, to do that. And again, the communication is key between principals, school safety and the local priest. And each priest in New York City has their own tailored -- rotate other ones to keep them off balance, if you will.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Before I let you go, we wanted to speak to you about a pretty alarming statement from a midtown police officer who said that about 75 percent of arrests in Midtown Manhattan are migrants, mostly robberies, assaults, domestic incidents, and selling counterfeit items.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe the number is that high, that 75 percent of arrests in Midtown are coming from people who have arrived here in recent years?\par \par \uc2 CHELL: I can't speak the number. We don't know where that source is coming from. What I can tell you is we don't run every person for this status. We're not allowed to do it. We don't do it.\par \par \uc2 But there is something to it in terms of a lot of the migrants do live. There's a cluster in Manhattan, and they are committing crimes in our city. We've had two of our cops shot, two -- petty crime and everything in the middle. So it would lend to that we do have an influx.\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) I don't think so. I don't believe that number. And, you know, we deal with it. And those arrests wrap around Tren de Aragua, that gang that's here. So kind of all conflates --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The Venezuelan gang.\par \par \uc2 CHELL: The Venezuelan gang, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, we have seen images of someone who was believed to be part of that gang, who basically wrapped a woman around a parking meter when he was on a scooter. And they have had a significant presence in a lot of cities in America.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Two hundred and twelve thousand migrants have descended in the city since 2022. The majority here, for good reasons, but a small came just to facilitate their criminal activities using New York City as their playpen to do so. So we know the gang, we know who they are, we will deal with them and they will be dealt with.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to hear. Thank you, chief. Always nice to see you, Chief Chell.\par \par \uc2 CHELL: Yes, pleasure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you so much, \uc2 NYPD\uc2 . Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 With that let's bring in Kerri Urbahn, former Counselor to Attorney General Bill Barr and Fox News Legal Editor. Kerri, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, just off the top here, reacting to this horrible news at this high school about an hour outside of Atlanta, Georgia, we can see the live shot. We're waiting for an update. We expect that we're going to get some information about the suspect potentially, and some of those who were lost obviously have to wait for all of their family members to be notified before they can come forward with that.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts on that and perhaps on what we heard from Vice President Harris in reaction to this as well just moments ago.\par \par \uc2 KERRI URBAHN, FOX NEWS FOX NEWS LEGAL EDITOR: Attorney General Garland and Director Wray of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  addressed this earlier at the press conference, expressing the dismay that all of us feel as we watch yet another horrible scene unfold. And they, of course, pledged federal resources to the local law enforcement there, which is always helpful.\par \par \uc2 You know, having been at DOJ and having worked with so many local law enforcement partners across the country, it's always helpful to have DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2  involved in these kinds of scenes. But as you said, Martha, there's so much we don't know still. Everything from motive to did the school even receive, say, a phone call, warning them what was to come.\par \par \uc2 And so, we all have to just wait and see before we make any conclusions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. You know, while we're waiting for this news conference, I do want to ask you about the other big story today. And we watched Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  was next to him during this. They outlined operations and investigations around election interference in this country, and were specific about some of the threats that have been happening around Russian interference operations and also Iranian operations.\par \par \uc2 Let's play this soundbite from Attorney General Garland about Iran. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , US ATTORNEY GENERAL: As the US intelligence community noted two weeks ago, we have observed increasingly aggressive Iranian activity during this election cycle. That includes recently reported activities by Iran to compromise former President Trumps campaign and to avoid an election outcome that it regards as against its interests.\par \par \uc2 Those recently reported Iranian activities also include efforts to obtain access to individuals who themselves have access to the presidential campaigns of both political parties.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Your reaction to that? I heard -- I was listening to you speak about this earlier, Kerri, and you were talking about the fact that they were specific one front in terms of what the influence operation is coming from Russia, as opposed to what the influence operation appears to be coming from Iran. Explain that to everybody.\par \par \uc2 URBAHN: Yes. So the Russian operation appears to be fairly simple in that. It looks like the Russian government directed some public relations people in Russia to create social media content that was pro-Russia and disseminate it across the US.\par \par \uc2 And not just the US, Martha, I don't know that a lot of people caught this. The attorney general said other countries as well. So it wasn't just an operation targeting us. It's targeting a number of countries.\par \par \uc2 And these PR people work to create the social media content using AI to generate pro-Russian messaging and targeting various people who they thought should hear that messaging. That was basically the extent of it. Interestingly, despite the fact that this was about election interference, when Attorney General Garland was speaking about the Russian operation, there was no mention of any candidate.\par \par \uc2 He didn't mention \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . He didn't mention \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He did mention \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , though, when he went on to speak about the threat of election interference from Iran, which doesn't come as a surprise to maybe us at Fox News. We've been covering this now for some time.\par \par \uc2 You know, in recent weeks we've learned that Iran has been pushing an operation, a disinformation operation, across the country, across social media and the Internet in various ways to undermine \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's chances to win the election. And, of course, that doesn't even compare with the death threats and plots that have also been tied to Iran as far as targeting Trump and trying to kill him.\par \par \uc2 And so, that was the thing that caught my attention. We only heard one candidate's name once. It was with respect to Iran. They clearly don't want him to be president yet. There are no consequences coming yet from our country towards Iran on that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's a great point. And you made the point that they see the Harris-Biden administration as, in your words, surprisingly soft against Iran. And obviously, that would be a good reason for them to want to continue with this administration.\par \par \uc2 You also mentioned, and we have to leave it there, but it's significant to remind people of the 51 intelligence officials who signed a letter saying that there was Russian disinformation had created the Hunter Biden laptop story, that they had every reason to believe that it was Russian disinformation, that it had all the signs of it. And that did have an influence on the election according to polling that we saw after the election. It did persuade people and no mention of any of that.\par \par \uc2 So, Kerri, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 URBAHN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 So we're just moments away now from an update on the shooting that took the lives of four people today, earlier today, at a high school in Georgia. Nine others were injured. We don't know the extent of their injuries but we did see people airlifted, and we saw people carried out on stretchers.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff has informed us that the suspect is in custody, and here's what one student had to say about what they witnessed and experienced today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I heard boom-boom-boom-boom. I think of some kids fighting, but I thought they were just hitting heads under, what's it called, lockers but it was not. As soon as I walked out, I'm looking down - - I see a faint look of him running up the hallway. I'm just like, what's happening?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With me now, retired \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Inspector Paul Mauro is here but first, let's go to senior correspondent on the scene, Steve Harrigan, reporting live from Winder, Georgia, outside of Atlanta and near this high school. Steve, what's the latest? And we're waiting to get an update any minute now, right?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Martha. And parents are reuniting with children. We've been talking to people asking them, how is everybody? Is everybody OK?\par \par \uc2 And what they've been through, even if everybody is OK, I just spoke to a mother on the sidewalk here who said during the shooting she was texting with her daughter. Her daughter said, you know, if I get killed just know that I love you. So those are the kind of exchanges even the survivors had to go through today.\par \par \uc2 The numbers as they are stand at four dead, up to 30 people injured. Many of those injuries really in the stampede to get away from the shooting. Nine of those injured hospitalized. So the numbers, the death toll could change. The shooter we're likely to find more about in a few minutes at that press conference.\par \par \uc2 But we're really hearing now the stories of what people had to see, what people had to go through, and some real acts of heroism as well. Here's a 12th grader who on a typical Wednesday, just three weeks into the term, watched his math teacher get shot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mister Aswell (ph) did have a gunshot wound to the bottom of his chest. He was bleeding out. Some other kids got up and they dragged his body inside and closed the door. After they dragged him in, I started barricading the door with all the things I could find.\par \par \uc2 Me and some other kids, we lifted up his shirt, see where the gunshot wound was, maybe try and stop it, put some pressure on it. The bleeding would not stop. So we kind of just stopped until the cops got there. (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Twelfth grader trying to save his math teacher. We're likely to hear in a few minutes from national, state and local law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's very emotional and something that no 12th grader should have to do, to drag the body of his math teacher and try to save his life after an attempt on his life. Steve, thank you very much, Steve Harrigan, on scene. We'll go back there in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in retired \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Inspector Paul Mauro, Founder of opsdest.org and a Fox News contributor. And you know what, we do -- we cover these stories way too often. It's horrifying. There's too many children who have witnessed the kinds of scenes that young man witnessed, and they have to live with it for the rest of their lives, Paul.\par \par \uc2 And every time we don't seem to get anywhere towards eliminating this threat at our schools.\par \par \uc2 PAUL MAURO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. I mean, look, it's a conundrum, Martha. We've been on the air so many times together, both of us, in different situations, and you run out of words, right? You just get to that point where you just say, is there anything else we can do?\par \par \uc2 Look, we're going to look very closely at what happened here. All the media, all of law enforcement will be focusing intently on this, as always happens in these school shooters. There are some universal lessons that can be taken away. We do know that Georgia has a statute that requires schools to do active shooting drills, so that may have been done here, that may have saved lives.\par \par \uc2 There's some reporting that some teachers reacted very professionally and heroically. So, you know, that'd be a lesson. There's also the school resource officer issue, should they be armed?\par \par \uc2 And I've talked in the past about the idea that one of the things we should consider is a program former cops --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, absolutely. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Well, you know, they -- excuse me. They tend to be good with kids. They know how to handle firearms. They know how to respond. They have experience operating under pressure.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, if you had a program where they -- what happens with cops is they miss the mission, you know?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: They work for their retirement. They get their retirement. And then all of a sudden they realize, you know, I don't have that focus anymore to my life. And I think you'd get a real response, you'd get a lot of people who would volunteer to manage it and put in protocols. And I think it is a positive step that we could potentially -- it's not going to be about the money. We don't need some enormous bureaucracy to get set up here.\par \par \uc2 But we -- I do think that a program like that would do something to harden these schools as targets.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it's not unlike a voluntary neighborhood patrol program, right? And if you have -- there are so many former police officers in this country, generally they retire after 25 years. Is that right, Paul?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Twenty, twenty five depends on the agency.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right, so they're young. They're young and they like a mission, as you say. I don't understand why we never hear officials try to actually implement a program like this. And, you know, they already have the ability to be armed. They have a license to be armed.\par \par \uc2 And having a gun on a school resource officer seems like it would be deterrent, wouldn't it?\par \par \uc2 MAURO: Of course it would, of course it would. And, you know, nothing is ever going to be 100 percent safe. But one of the things that you get, and I've seen this with school safety officers, is that they develop a rapport with the students. And so, what happens is in addition to the deterrence and the fact that they could react in an armed situation, they're the ones who would hear earlier potentially about somebody who is leading a slipping down life, somebody who may be going down a bad path, somebody the other students say, hey, you know what, I'm a little concerned about this guy.\par \par \uc2 I've seen things that he's said and done, and maybe we need to take a look at him. And then you need, of course, the protocols to do that effectively and legally.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, we're waiting for more information in a few minutes. There was one witness who suggested that this was a former student. That's also what happened in Parkland.\par \par \uc2 And it's not unlike the scenario that we see in workplace shootings, someone who has been let go, someone who is unhappy with the way that they were treated. And in that case, you know, if you have images, pictures of students who are in that situation, that could be shared among these officers, I'm not saying they should, you know, put their picture on the hall like a, you know, like someone at the post office. But awareness, raising awareness, would that help? I don't know. I don't know.\par \par \uc2 MAURO: You would want somebody at least to be able to get to this kid, talk to this kid and see if there's something there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes, Paul, this is -- it's horrific. And now we are back to school and already we have a shooting situation and four families, at least, who are having the worst day of their lives.\par \par \uc2 And we pray for them right now. We pray that they'll be comforted and held up. Paul, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 As we wait for this news conference, you can see that they are getting set up for it in Winder, Georgia, in Appalachia, Georgia where they have suffered an extraordinary blow today at a local high school about an hour outside of Atlanta. So we'll continue our live coverage. And, you know, statement just coming in from former President Trump. Our hearts are with those who are suffering these losses this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 That is "The Story" for this Wednesday, lots to cover throughout the afternoon and evening. We will see you right back here tomorrow on "The Story." Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240905ek9400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Funeral Held For Israel-American Killed In Gaza; \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  Deliver Remarks After Unsuccessful Mission; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Campaign Together In Pennsylvania; Trump Rips Harris And Revives Past Grievances; Abortion, Crime, And Border Issues On State Ballots; Border States Debate Immigration And Crime Measures; Federal Regulators Allow \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  to Launch Falcon 9 Rockets after Brief Grounding Due to Failed Booster Landing; Former Bush Administration Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice Interviewed on State of Foreign Affairs and U.S. Role in World Today; President Biden Goes on Campaign Trail in Support of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ; \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Kills Six Israeli Hostages.\b0\par\par\uc2 Gillian Turner, Jeff Paul, Lucas Tomlinson, William La Jeunesse,  Jonathan Serrie \par \uc2 7,272 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it for us on our Labor Day Special, have a great night and we'll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN POLICY CORRESPONDENT: Good evening from Washington. I'm Gillian Turner in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, you were listening to President Biden, who now is finally hitting the campaign trail in support of his Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , joining her right now in the Keystone State. Pennsylvania is going to be a key to a potential Harris-Walz victory come November.\par \par \uc2 On this Labor Day, former President Trump meanwhile, unleashing another tirade on his political rival, calling Harris a "radical left Marxist who will destroy the country." Both sides now hardening into position as this race for the White House enters its final stretch.\par \par \uc2 Plus, down ballot races moving to the top of voters' minds right around now as Election Day gets closer to big issues, as well as a battle for control of Congress all on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 First, we begin tonight with major developments in the war in Gaza. Six hostages, including a 23-year-old American were shot and killed at close range by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists. A cease fire negotiations are at the same time ongoing between Israel, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , Qatar and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has condemned those brutal murders, but also dinging the Israeli side today, telling reporters that Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is not doing enough to secure that elusive deal.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in national correspondent Jeff Paul. He has the latest reporting from Tel Aviv tonight. Hi, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Well, yes, Gillian, a very tough day today here in Israel as this country begins to lay to rest the hostages whose bodies were found in Gaza this past weekend, but a lot of that overwhelming sense of sadness is now transforming into outrage, as some Israelis believe more could have been done to prevent their deaths.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): Thousands packing the streets of Jerusalem saying a final goodbye to American-Israeli Hersh Goldberg-Polin, kidnapped back on October 7th, the 23-year-old is one of six hostages recovered this weekend from Gaza after being killed in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL GOLDBERG, MOTHER OF HERSH GOLDBERG-POLIN: I will love you and I will miss you every single day for the rest of my life.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): His mother Rachel said her son was finally free.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: If there was something we could have done to save you and we didn't think of it, I beg your forgiveness. We tried so very hard, so deeply and desperately.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): The deaths are sparking strikes and protests. Tens of thousands rallied in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: As the days drag on without a ceasefire hostage deal, frustration among many Israelis, as you can hear, is growing. They are calling on not only the Israeli government, but Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  to make a deal and bring them all home now.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): But speaking tonight, Netanyahu was defiant.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): I apologize that we were not able to bring them back alive. We were close, but we were not successful. I repeat again, Israel will not act as if nothing has happened during this massacre. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  will pay a very heavy price.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): However, for the families of the 101 hostages who are still being held, time is running out. After 300 days, Amit Levy fears for his sister, Naama.\par \par \uc2 AMIT LEVY, BROTHER OF HOSTAGE: At these moments, I'm scared. It could have been -- it could have happened to her just as much as it could have happened to them. And if she won't be released soon, as much of an amazing survivor and a hero she is, it could happen to her. It could happen to anyone, any minute.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): Now, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  put out a statement tonight blaming Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  for the deaths of those six hostages. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  went on to accuse Prime Minister Netanyahu of obstructing any possible ceasefire and hostage deal, and say any added Israeli military pressure will result in more hostages dying, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Jeff Paul in Tel Aviv for us tonight. Thank you for staying up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you planning to present a final hostage deal to both sides of this week?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're very close to that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What makes you think that this deal will be successful in a way that the other deals were not?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Hope springs eternal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, do you think it's time for Prime Minister Netanyahu to do more on this issue, do you think he's doing enough?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: That's President Biden earlier today putting the onus on Israel's Prime Minister to secure a ceasefire and hostage deal. Israel insists, nothing is going to come between total and complete victory over \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 I'm joined now by Orna Neutra, she's mother to 22-year-old Israeli American hostage Omer Neutra. He grew up in New York, later served in the \uc2 IDF\uc2 . Omer was kidnapped by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists on October 7th. He remains in captivity now. Orna joins us.\par \par \uc2 Orna, thank you for taking time with us today on what I'm sure is an especially difficult week for you. I want to know how you're doing, how you're feeling, how you and your husband are holding up, and then also, of course, what your reaction is to the latest slaughter by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ORNA NEUTRA, MOTHER OF HAMAS HOSTAGE OMER NEUTRA: It's been, you know, completely devastating getting the news from Israel.\par \par \uc2 Over the course of the past 11 months, we've been fighting alongside some of these families, especially the family of Hersh Goldberg-Polin, who is also an American citizen, and it's just heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 You know, we've been doing so much. We've been doing our share to make sure the issue is not forgotten, to fight for our kids. And this shouldn't have happened.\par \par \uc2 You know, the writing was on the wall. We kept saying, this is urgent, that this deal needs to be signed as soon as possible, that military activity is not enough. And here we are. Here we are.\par \par \uc2 These are six people that shouldn't have died. At least three of them were supposed to come out in the first stage of this deal that's been discussed, and as Hersh's mom said today, they felt that in a visceral way that this was going to happen, that they're going to be reunited with their son. And we really believed it. And this is just heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, I know that you and your husband, who I had the privilege of interviewing a couple of months ago have done an incredible job of keeping Omer's name alive in the press, keeping attention on him here in the American administration, overseas, in the Israeli administration, I know that you were -- your family was part of the group that met with the National Security Advisor Jake Sullivan earlier in the week. Did you hear a message of hope from him? What did he tell you?\par \par \uc2 NEUTRA: Well, we heard from him exactly what the president said today, that they're in the final stages of coming up with a deal that they're hoping that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Israel will accept.\par \par \uc2 To be honest, you know, we're -- we have -- we're -- we have to keep a hope, but we're skeptical. We're not seeing \uc2 Hamas\uc2  or Israel come to the table seriously enough or willing to sign the deal.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, we completely identify with our brothers and sisters in Israel who are out in the streets who don't feel that their own government is doing enough to release the hostages, and in Benjamin's Netanyahu statement about annihilating \uc2 Hamas\uc2  as a top priority, we're not hearing exactly how that is going to bring our loved ones home.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Negotiating with terrorists is always a very intricate, very difficult, challenging process. I want to ask you before I let you go if you have a message for Omer, for his captives, if anybody is indeed listening to us tonight.\par \par \uc2 NEUTRA: Well, if anyone of the captives and my son is listening to us, you know, I will carry Rachel's torch and tell my son, Omer, that I love you. We all love you. We're fighting for you, and we're begging you to survive until we are able to finally bring you up.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We are, that was beautifully said. We all certainly here are praying for his recovery and for him to stay strong as well. Thank you so much for taking time with us. Our thoughts and our prayers and our hearts are with you.\par \par \uc2 NEUTRA: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  right now co-headlining a campaign event in Pittsburgh. It's their first time sharing a speaking slot since Harris replaced him at the top of the ticket. Let's take a listen in.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Let me tell you about this woman. I know her. I trust her. Not a joke, I trust her.\par \par \uc2 Number two, the first decision I made to nominate a nominee in 2020 was selecting her as my vice president. And by the way, it was the single best decision I made as president United States of America.\par \par \uc2 I was watching something said to me when they asked Barack, they said, Barack picked me as vice president because knew I could be president. I know she'll be a good one. I know it.\par \par \uc2 I've watched her with all the experts, foreign and domestic policy would give us advice that we sit alone in a room and she has a backbone like a ramrod. She has the moral compass of a saint. This woman knows what she's doing.\par \par \uc2 Folks, I promise you, if you elect \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as president, it will be the best decision you will have ever made.\par \par \uc2 Kamala, believe me as I do, unions are the spine of this economy. She'll be a historic pro-union president.\par \par \uc2 So, folks, we got one more job to do together. Let me ask you, are you ready to fight?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Are ready to win?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Are you ready to let \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  our next president of the United State of America?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And in the process, are you ready to make \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a loser again?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I've never been more optimistic about America, because we have to remember who we are. We're the United States of America. There's nothing, nothing -- I mean this from the bottom of my heart, there's nothing beyond our capacity, nothing, when we do it together. And that means elect, my friend, our great Vice President the President United States, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You've been listening to former President Trump who just made remarks there in Pittsburgh at a campaign event with V.P. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Let's listen in to her for just a moment.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Thank you, Joe. Thank you, Joe. Thank you, Joe. Thank you, Joe. Thank you, Joe. Thank you, Joe.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: It is good to be in the house of labor, and it is good to be back at IBEW Local 5. And can we please give it up again for our President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, I don't have to tell the brothers and sisters of labor that you really get to know somebody when you're in the middle of a fight, when times are hard, when the forces are mighty, when people don't believe something can get done, and they have a thousand excuses for why it can't get done.\par \par \uc2 And I have spent more time with this extraordinary human being when the cameras were not in the room, when the stakes were high, when the heat was intense, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has always stood with the workers of America and labor unions of America, always, always.\par \par \uc2 I've been with him when he'll bring folks into the Oval Office. And you know how Joe can get sometimes, he doesn't spare words. It's good that sometimes the cameras are not in the room when he has those conversations.\par \par \uc2 Because the thing about the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  I know, and I know you know, because he has been a friend of labor for so long for his whole life. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can be quite impatient. And that's a good thing for that kind of leader, quite impatient.\par \par \uc2 And I say to all of the friends here, the press that's in the room, history will show what we here know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been one of the most transformative presidents in the United States that we have ever witnessed.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, you have been listening to President Biden and V.P. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at a campaign event in Pittsburgh where they're addressing union workers, union representatives. We're going to take you back there as soon as any news breaks coming out of that presser.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, former President Trump is turning up the heat today on his political rival with more social media attacks on the vice president, this as the race enters its most critical and final stage.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson has those details from Washington tonight. Hi, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Gillian, in a wide-ranging interview with Mark Levin, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he thinks God saved him from an assassin's bullet to help fix the country's problems.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'd like to think that God thinks that I'm going to straighten out our country. Our country is so sick and it's so broken. Our country is just broken.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): Trump said, after he was nearly killed at a rally in Butler, Pennsylvania on July 13th, he received many well wishes, with one notable exception.\par \par \uc2 MARK LEVIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Did you get a call or a note, I'm just curious, from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No.\par \par \uc2 LEVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Not that I know of.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): While speaking to Moms for Liberty in the nation's capital, the former president said he still thinks about the last election.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I also wouldn't have run if I didn't think I won the election. I mean, I think we won that election by so much -- by so much.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Even some of Trump's biggest supporters are encouraging him to tighten up his message with voters.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Focus on the issues, Mr. President. Every poll says that -- says the same thing. The American people trust you with what matters the most to them, the economy, inflation, border security.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): And avoid comments like this.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Whoever heard you get indicted for interfering with a presidential election, where you have every right to do it, you get indicted and your poll numbers go up.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): Monday afternoon, the Harris campaign responded, saying, "While \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is pushing his false history about the past, the American people are ready for a new way forward."\par \par \uc2 On "FOX NEWS SUNDAY", a senior adviser to the Trump campaign defended the former president's pitch.\par \par \uc2 COREY LEWANDOWSKI\uc2 , TRUMP 2024 CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is giving specific policy speeches, and again, you mentioned it in your last segment, no policy speeches on the Harris web site. It's amazing to me that she has not been held accountable by most of the mainstream media for that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will sit down with our own \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  for a Fox News Town Hall in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania on Wednesday night, beginning at 9:00 Eastern. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Lucas Tomlinson here in Washington for us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock markets were closed today in observance of the Labor Day holiday. We'll have a market read for you again tomorrow, right here on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, why a rookie wide receiver for the \uc2 San Francisco 49ers\uc2  who was shot in the chest, says he now considers himself a very, very lucky man.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. "BREAKING TONIGHT", at least five people have been shot at a parade in New York City. Folks had gathered there to celebrate West Indian culture earlier today. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  says one suspect fired directly into the crowd, but there is no longer an active shooter threat.\par \par \uc2 According to officials, two of the victims are now in critical condition. The other three are expected to recover fully from their injuries.\par \par \uc2 And this, rookie wide receiver for the \uc2 San Francisco 49ers\uc2 , Ricky Pearsall is out of the hospital tonight. This after being shot in the chest during an attempted robbery on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 His mother said he is, "extremely lucky" to be alive following the incident. 17-year-old suspect from Tracy, California has been arrested.\par \par \uc2 Voters' top issues this election cycle are not just critical in the presidential race, but also down ballot, where voters could enact sweeping changes on everything from reproductive rights to immigration. Here is national correspondent William La Jeunesse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For me, it's Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): From abortion, to crime, and immigration, voter turnout for state ballot measures could help decide who controls Congress and the White House.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Support for abortion crosses all political lines.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): Voters in 10 states will consider abortion- related initiatives, most focused on guaranteeing that right until viability.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is a monumental achievement for our campaign.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): A measure in Missouri would repeal the state's ban on nearly all abortions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That I think Missouri is going to defeat this measure.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): The issue is also on the ballot in the presidential battlegrounds of Arizona and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: When this issue has been on the ballot, the voters voted in favor of freedoms.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): The latest Fox News polls show three quarters of voters back the abortion measures, providing a potential down ballot boost for Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Open borders, equals more crime.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): Arizona voters will also consider a Republican- led initiative making it a state crime to cross the Mexican border illegally, and allow state judges to order deportations.\par \par \uc2 MIKE O'NEIL, POLITICAL ANALYST, ARIZONA: It could add conservative voters to the -- to the polls, not unlike the abortion, one, adding more liberal voters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Let's make crime illegal again.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): In California, with several competitive House races, voters can undo so-called reform measures, reinstituting penalties for petty theft and making fentanyl possession a felony.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our intent is to hold those who prey on our communities accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Usually in presidential years, coattails flow down ballot. However, this year, with these Hopland state issues, abortion, immigration, and crime, we could see that trend reversed. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Thank you, William, in L.A. for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  now has given the green light to \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , once again, no longer standing in the way of its Falcon 9 rocket launch. This, ahead of very -- two important missions.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, the supreme court panel in Brazil has upheld a decision by one of its justices to block X, the social media platform famously owned by billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , across the entire country. That panel says the decision was made in accordance with Brazilian law. X, they say, had refused to name a local legal representative in that battle. Musk says that the court orders have been issued without any due process.\par \par \uc2 And this -- federal regulators now giving the go ahead to \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  to launch its Falcon 9 rockets after they had been briefly grounded due to concerns over a failed booster landing. Correspondent Jonathan Serrie has those details from Atlanta tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And liftoff. Go Starlink, go DTT, go falcon.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 's Falcon 9 rocket returned to surface with back-to-back Starlink satellite launches early Saturday, just three days after the \uc2 FAA\uc2  temporarily grounded the fleet to investigate a first stage booster that tipped over while landing on a drone ship off the Florida coast. With Falcon 9 back in flight, a launch may be eminent for Polaris Dawn, a privately funded mission that will attempt the first commercial spacewalk.\par \par \uc2 JARED ISAACMAN, POLARIS DAWN MISSION COMMANDER: If we are to unlock this last great frontier and people are going to venture out in space, which, by the way, whatever risks associated with it, it is worth it. We have no idea what it could do to really, you know, again, change the trajectory of humankind.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Point five meters.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: On September 24, a Falcon 9 is scheduled to launch the next \uc2 NASA\uc2  mission to the International Space Station which will return in February with Boeing Starliner astronauts Suni Williams and Butch Wilmore. On Saturday, Wilmore reported a strange pulsing noise inside the Starliner capsule. \uc2 NASA\uc2  determined it was coming a communications link with the ISS, explaining in a social media post "The space station audio system is complex, allowing multiple spacecraft and modules to be interconnected. And it is common to experience noise and feedback." Retired astronaut Clayton Anderson recalls a similar incident from 2007.\par \par \uc2 CLAYTON ANDERSON, NASA ASTRONAUT (RET): There was a night when I was awakened. We were flying over South America, and I was hearing a bunch of Spanish speakers from the ground, and it would turn out that we had the comms system misconfigured.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): And Gillian, it is not strange noises but thruster problems that prompted \uc2 NASA\uc2  officials who decide to bring those two Starliner astronauts back to earth on a different spacecraft. As for Starliner, it's scheduled to undock autonomously on Friday evening, and then return to earth overnight, parachuting to a dessert landing site in New Mexico. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Jonathan Serrie in Atlanta for us tonight. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, coming up next, my colleague Bret Baier has a conversation with former Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice, including talking about what she thinks is the number one threat facing the U.S. today.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, take a look at this. The U.S. seizing an airplane belonging to corrupt Venezuelan President \uc2 Nicolas Maduro\uc2 . The aircraft was purchased through a straw company in violation of U.S. sanctions laws and exports controls. Maduro had used the aircraft to travel virtually around the world before it was remanded.\par \par \uc2 And this, a far-right party in Germany had a stark success in two elections on Sunday, shaking the already divided \uc2 German government\uc2  and continuing the resurgence of anti-immigrant and populist politics seen across western Europe.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look at Rome. One of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  is setting off now for his longest, his farthest, and certainly his most challenging international trips. He is heading to Asia where he will visit four countries in under two weeks. The 88-year-old Pope had to cancel his last foreign trip, which was scheduled back in November, after he came down with a bad case of the flu.\par \par \uc2 These are just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. Tonight, my colleague Bret Baier sits down with Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice. She served during 9/11 and the start of the Iraq and Afghanistan wars. She tells him about the number one threat America faces today.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Gillian, thank you. Tonight, we are joined by former secretary of state, former national security adviser, Dr. Condoleezza Rice. There's a new essay in "Foreign Affairs" magazine. Secretary Rice, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 CONDOLEEZZA RICE, FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Pleasure to be with you, Bret, as always.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to talk about your essay, and I read through it. It's really interesting. But I want to just ask broadly, as you look at foreign affairs right now, it's a chaotic world. What do you think the biggest threat for the U.S. is right now?\par \par \uc2 RICE: Well, there are multiple threats. I think one of the biggest things that's changed recently is that we have this great power conflict with China, with Russia, that's territorial. That brings militaries into play in ways that we haven't seen for a very long time. Obviously, the Middle East is always the source of tension and problem, and Iran's activity there, Iran's aggressiveness there. And, of course, in the Asia Pacific, there's always North Korea.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, to your point, this essay, "The Perils of Isolationism." I will quote from it, "The new four horse men of the apocalypse -- populism, nativism, isolationism, and protectionism -- tend to ride together. And they are challenging the political center. There can be no more platitudes about the advantages of globalization for all. There must be real effort to give people meaningful education skills and job training. The task is even more urgent since the technological progress will severely punish those who cannot keep up." What's the elevator pitch that you're trying to get across here?\par \par \uc2 RICE: That it's not possible to, again, leave behind so many of our citizens while we talk about the macro-benefits of globalization. So many Americans are no longer really a part of the American dream. And we have to fix that if we're going to be confident enough to lead the world. And really, we are the only choice.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Though we have this competition and sometimes conflict with China and Russia, you still think we should accept those kids from China and Russia to schools in the U.S. to establish this long-term benefit?\par \par \uc2 RICE: If we want to see ultimately change in Russia, change in China, we have to keep connection to those young people who expect a different life. We have to be certain that in our labs and universities, in our research and universities, that we are protecting against what has been a very aggressive and quite consequential effort, particularly by China, to use the access to American universities to empower the People's Liberation Army. Let's not cut off these people who about will be for generations the leadership of China and Russia.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: About China, you write that China has an insatiable ambition. The current is not a cold redux. It is more dangerous. What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 RICE: Well, China is not the Soviet Union. The Soviet Union was a military giant, but it was a technological and economic midget. When we confront China, we recognize that it's very integrated into the international system. Everybody is very interested right now in the race for A.I. That's a race that democracies have to win. These technologies in the hands of authoritarians are much more dangerous than they will ever be in a democracy. So we need to gird ourselves for what is going to be a long-term struggle.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You write that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  cannot lose this war and he is willing to sacrifice everything to stave off disaster.\par \par \uc2 RICE: It is true that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  does not think he can lose this war. And, in a sense, he has already lost the war that he thought he was fighting. He planned to land his forces in Kyiv, change the government, and snuff out Ukrainian independence. That failed.\par \par \uc2 Now we need to make sure that Russia pays a price for what it did so that other authoritarians don't get the idea that territorial conquest comes free. I think the Ukrainians are showing still remarkable skill. They are showing remarkable resilience, but they are going to need our help. They are going to need the help of the Europeans. If Ukraine loses, I don't want to be the person who watches \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Xi Jinping on their victory tour, and Xi Jinping emboldened in the Asia Pacific.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As for the people who say we have spent a lot of money there, as you mentioned, gotten the weapons that Ukraine has asked for over there, but that the onus should be on Europe in their territory. For those people, what do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 RICE: Well, I fully agree that our allies need to do more all over the world. And the Europeans are finally really stepping up in important ways. I do think, by the way, it's remarkable and probably not what Putin had in mind that we have Sweden and Finland now in the alliance who have real military capability. Yes, the allies should do more. But it doesn't let us off the hook to do the things that we need to do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I sat down with you a number of times at the State Department, talking about the issues of the day. We talked a lot about Iraq.\par \par \uc2 RICE: It's impossible to imagine a different kind of Middle East with \uc2 Saddam Hussein\uc2  at the middle of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Middle East has changed.\par \par \uc2 RICE: What happened on October 7th to Israel was an act of barbarity and sophistication on the part of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  that I didn't think I would ever see. Anybody who thinks \uc2 Hamas\uc2  represents the legitimate interests of the Palestinian people in any way is delusional. And so the Israelis will have to establish, reestablish, deterrence. They will have to weaken \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to the degree that they can, something, by the way, that most Arab states want to see.\par \par \uc2 One way that the Middle East changed before this conflagration was a change for the better. When through really some quite elegant diplomacy of the Trump administration you got the Abraham Accords. And the Gulf Arab states, the UAE, Kuwait, and others were ready to end the state of war with Israel and recognize Israel.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Iran's fingerprints are all over October 7th and supporting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  financially. It's also moving quickly on its nuclear front. Just Thursday, Iran has further increased its stockpile of uranium enriched to near weapons grade levels in defiance of international demands according to an \uc2 IAEA\uc2  report. They are moving forward even as the supreme leader says maybe it's time to renegotiate with the U.S. They're a bad actor. How do we deal with them?\par \par \uc2 RICE: They're a terribly bad actor. And anybody who keeps looking for moderates in Iran is also delusional. And so the only answer with Iran is to recognize that never give another penny back to Iran from frozen assets, because what we do know about the money that was returned to Iran was that it went mostly to fund \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're obviously in the middle of a presidential election. As things get heated, how much do you think foreign policy is going to play in the end game? We didn't think it was going to play in 2004. It did. How much do you think it will play this time around?\par \par \uc2 RICE: I think there are some really important questions out there about foreign policy, but, perhaps, the most important one, is where we began. How are you going to make sure that a more confident America is willing to play the role that we have in the past of making sure that it's a more prosperous and peaceful world?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Two more very important questions. One, how are the Broncos going to be this year?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 RICE: I'm excited about the Broncos this year. We're going to be young. We're going to be young. That brings a lot with it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And lastly, how is the golf game?\par \par \uc2 RICE: When I have gotten out there, I have played pretty well, Bret. My short game is improving. I'm ready tyke you on whenever you want to go out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we'll do it, we'll do it. Secretary Rice, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 RICE: Thank you. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian, I'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, thank you for that, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the all-star panel is going to debate today's moves for the White House, plus a tribute to American workers on this Labor Day. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2 , (D) ILLINOIS: I think that he is helping quite a lot. Remember, the most important thing right now is getting the enthusiasm level up and making sure that people get out to vote. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been able to do that in every election that he has run in.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I will be on the sidelines, but I will do everything I can to help.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: All of a sudden, she is wonderful. But she is not wonderful. So we have a debate coming up. And I look forward to that, and she will be exposed.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): On the issues, Trump, economy, plus eight, inflation, plus eight, border, plus nine. My God, that's a blowout. Focus on the issues, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, it's time to bring in our panel. We've got Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," Real Clear Politics White House correspondent Philip Wegmann, and Meghan Hays, Democratic strategist and deputy communications director for the Biden- Harris 2020 presidential campaign. Thanks to everybody for being with us tonight. Happy Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 Meghan, let's start with you. Ahead of this first presidential debate, President Biden finally getting out on the trail in support of V.P. Harris, a bit of an 11th hour move. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN HAYS, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I don't necessarily think it's an 11th hour move. I think after the convention he went on vacation, and now he's out on the trail like he said he was going to do. He did the same thing in 2016 for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . He was out after Labor Day a significant amount for her, hitting the same sorts of states and the same sorts of voting contingent with the blue-collar workers of the blue wall. So I don't think it's next to 11th hour. I think that this has been the plan since the top of the ticket changed.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, Byron, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was not, to be fair, Biden sort of hand-selected candidate to replace him at the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": And she didn't win. That was the big problem. Listen, I think that we have seen what we can say without any doubt at all that Democratic enthusiasm is way, way higher than it was before the big switch. All you had to do was look at the event we were showing earlier today in Pittsburgh with this aged and infirm yelling Biden, and then \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . The contrast was absolutely huge.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, also, if you look at some of the key polls, the state polls, I think there are a number of states, most notably Georgia, Arizona, and North Carolina that some Republicans thought were kind of in the bag for Trump, and now they are in play. Not saying he is going to lose them, but they are in play.\par \par \uc2 And then the last thing is we have to see if this six-week media fueled honeymoon that Harris has had is going to end anytime soon. The CNN debate did not inspire confidence, but we're still going to have to see whether if people see more of her, they will think less of her.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So Phil, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  just said in that soundbite we played off the top that Trump should focus really on the issues -- we didn't air this part, but rather than the personal attacks against Harris and her campaign.\par \par \uc2 So let's take a look at what Trump has got going in his favor right now. Latest ABC poll showed that Harris did not have, despite raucous attendance and crowds at the DNC, did not get a boost in the ratings for that. The other sort of top tier plus in Trump's corner right now is that the latest "Wall Street Journal" poll shows more Americans right now trust him to manage the economy than the vice president. He is up eight points. She is up five, as you can see there. Do you think -- do you agree? Do you think she should avoid the personal attacks on Harris and just stick to the issues.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP WEGMANN, REAL CLEAR POLITICS: Certainly, the personalities have changed, but the fundamentals of this race have not, because the issues that the country still faces are the same. The polling that you just referenced generally tracks with what we are seeing in the Real Clear Politics average right now. Harris suspect over Trump by about two points. And right now, among Democrats, the vibe is immaculate, because the same machine that sort of showed Biden the door now is working overtime and in overdrive for Harris.\par \par \uc2 But one thing that I have been struck by is that win elections in the summer, and you don't win them by vibes alone. So while this race sort of mirrors what we saw in 2016 with Hillary Clinton who was up by about three points, four points at this point in the race, Harris isn't taking anything for granted. She is going to Michigan and Wisconsin. She is not making identity politics an issue. And her entire campaign is set up to run as an underdog through the finish line.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I was going to say, Meghan, both the campaigns now running what to some voters appears to be absurd argument who is the real underdog here. Take a listen to President Biden speaking a few moments ago at that rally in Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The first decision I made as a nominee in 2020 was selecting her as vice president.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And by the way, it was the single best decision I've made as president of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 This woman knows what she's doing.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Folks, I promise you, if you elect \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as president, it will be the best decision you will have ever made.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I think, Meghan, and correct me if I am wrong or if you disagree, that might be the most powerful statement we have heard the president make in support of the vice president.\par \par \uc2 HAYS: He has been saying that for a long time now, and that's always been his mantra when he's been --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: That she was his best decision --\par \par \uc2 HAYS: Correct.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: -- but maybe not that he promises all of America that it will be their best decision if they elect her.\par \par \uc2 HAYS: That's semantics. I think that is just adding a line into his speech. But I think that he is saying that this was the best decision and you should also follow suit, and that she is a solid person to be the president of the United States. I think that he made that very clear during all of his remarks thus far and in all of his endorsements to this point. He has she is going to be the best decision for you as Americans to choose to be the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I want to make sure we get this in, Byron. One issue that the Harris-Walz campaign definitely not focusing on today was the slaughter by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  over the weekend of six more hostages. Take a look at this. The vice presidential Democratic nominee avoided answering the question straight up. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  avoided speaking to the press at all today. I think we have some video of that so our viewers can see. She kind of put on --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What was your reaction to the six hostages being found dead in Gaza?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ, (D) MINNESOTA: Thanks, everybody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is she on her phone?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Last couple seconds go to you, Byron. Of course, one of those slaughtered, an American.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Yes. It certainly looked like Walz was just blowing off the question. And it's a very, very serious question. Maybe he says he didn't hear it. But it appears that he was blowing it off. And also it makes is -- it's a very difficult situation to answer this since they are still in favor of cease-fire talks after what \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has done just now.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, Byron, thanks so much. Panel, thanks so much for being here. Great to see you. Happy Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, we celebrate American labor, honoring the people who make up the backbone of the nation's economy, hardworking folks that keep our country moving forward year after year, decade after decade. Let's take a moment to recognize and celebrate their incredible contributions.\par \par \uc2 That does it tonight. Thanks for inviting us into your home on this Labor Day. That's it for SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240903ek9200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Israel Recovers Bodies Of Six Hostages In Gaza; Sen. Kaine on How VP Harris Evolved on Border Issue; Lewandowski On Trump Campaign's Pivot to Harris Moving to Top of the Ticket; Harris, Trump Campaigns Squabble Over Debate Rules; Trump Faces Criticism from Pro-Life Supporters Over Shifting Abortion Stance; Reaction To \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' First TV Interview Since Becoming Dem Nominee; Recent Polling Shows Trump Losing Ground To Harris On Economy; Trump Faces Criticism From Pro-Lifer Supporters Over Shifting Abortion Stance\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Marie Harf, Trey Yingst, Lucas Tomlinson \par \uc2 10,470 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 After more than a month of avoiding the media, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  finally sits down for a joint interview as the Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I made very clear where I stand. Let's be clear, my values have not changed.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had four nearly years to show us who she is. She's a Marxist, despite her campaign of lies and flip-flops.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Former President Trump seizes on the vice president's changing positions on everything from fracking to border enforcement, and says she'd be no different than a second Biden term. We'll get reaction from Harris-Walz's campaign surrogate, Senator Tim Kaine, the party's VP nominee back in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2 , fresh off his return to the Trump campaign, talks with us in an exclusive interview about brand new polls that show tightening numbers in key battlegrounds \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  must win to return to the White House.\par \par \uc2 And as America celebrates Labor Day this long holiday weekend, our Sunday panel takes a deep dive into the state of the economy, the key issue for voters in poll after poll.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 There is breaking news out of the Middle East. Israel says it has recovered the bodies of six hostages in Gaza. The Israeli military says all six were killed shortly before \uc2 IDF\uc2  troops attempted to rescue them. Among those lost, 23-year-old American Hersh Goldberg-Polin who was taken from the music festival on October 7th, where so many young civilians lost their lives or were captured by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists. His parents Jon Polin and Rachel Goldberg have been high-profile advocates for families of all the hostages, speaking out at the Democratic National Convention in Chicago last month.\par \par \uc2 Reacting to the news, President Biden said, quote: \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders will pay for these crimes, and that he would, quote, keep working around the clock for a deal to secure the release of the remaining hostages.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst is live at Hostage Square in Tel Aviv with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Devastating news for the Israeli people who woke up to learn that the bodies of six hostages were recovered from Gaza, three of those hostages were set to be released as part of a humanitarian exchange once a ceasefire was implemented, I'm told.\par \par \uc2 Among the group was 23-year-old American citizen Hersh Goldberg-Polin. President Biden released a statement overnight saying he was devastated and outraged, adding that the bodies were recovered from a tunnel underneath Raffa. The Israeli military says the hostages were executed by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL DANIEL HAGARI, IDF SPOKESMAN (through translator): A few hours ago, we updated the families that the bodies of their loved ones were located by \uc2 IDF\uc2  forces in an underground tunnel in Raffa. According to a preliminary assessment available to us, they were brutally murdered by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists shortly before we reach them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The horrible news comes as there is a huge effort underway as we speak to get the children of Gaza vaccinated against polio. Starting today, there will be daily humanitarian pauses in certain parts of the strip to allow for vaccine distribution. This will allow for 640,000 children to receive their doses. The U.N.-led effort began after the first case of polio was detected in Gaza in 25 years.\par \par \uc2 With the war raging on inside the strip, there are still hopes for the possibility of a ceasefire agreement. Working groups are still gathering this week to discuss a possible deal between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Trey, we will check back with you later in the show to talk more about that on this breaking news story. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now turning to the final weeks of campaign 2024 as both former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Vice President Harris focus on key battleground states that will likely decide which of them takes the White House in November.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll talk with Harris-Walz's campaign surrogate, Senator Tim Kaine, and later, Trump-Vance campaign senior official, \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, we go to Lucas Tomlinson at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, Democrats have represented the White House for 12 the past 16 years. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  believes voters want change and thinks she's the one who best represents it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: People are ready to turn the page on that.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): In her first sit-down interview since becoming her party's nominee, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants voters to believe she has also turned the page on a host of issues including fracking.\par \par \uc2 JESSIE BLUEDORN, CLIMATE ACTIVIST: Will you commit to implementing a federal ban on fracking your first day in office?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: There's no question I'm in favor of banning fracking.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN CHIEF POLITICAL ANALYST: Do you still want to ban fracking?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: No, and I made that clear on the debate stage in 2020, that I would not ban fracking. As vice president, I did not ban fracking. As president, I will not ban fracking.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Here's Harris in 2020 at the vice presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will not end fracking. He's been very clear about that. A new FOX News poll shows \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with a slight lead over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Arizona, Georgia and Nevada, states Trump lost four years ago. In North Carolina, Trump is up one point in a state he won back in 2020. Trump was leading in all four states earlier this year when President Biden was his opponent.\par \par \uc2 In February, Trump was up five points in North Carolina. In April, Trump had a six-point lead in Georgia and in June, Trump was up five points in both Arizona and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Vice President Harris returns to the campaign trail tomorrow on Labor Day making stops in Detroit and joins President Biden in Pittsburgh. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sits down with our own \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  for a town hall Wednesday night in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas Tomlinson at the White House -- thank you, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Harris-Walz campaign surrogate, Virginia Senator Tim Kaine.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you back, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): Shannon, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you know what it's like to run this gauntlet --\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- of the campaign, having done it yourself as vice presidential candidate in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Before we get to politics though, I want to get your reaction to the news of the loss of Hersh Goldberg-Polin. I know he spent some of his childhood in Richmond and has family there, too.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: It is a -- it's a real tragedy. I've met his parents and they have been tireless in coming to the Hill and talking about their son, but talking about the need to find the hostage release deal. So I can only imagine how painful this news is to them. I really feel for them today.\par \par \uc2 And it just emphasizes even more, we've got to get this hostage release and ceasefire deal, that the two halves have to come together. You're not going to see one without the other.\par \par \uc2 It's been tantalizingly close. We're not there yet, but hopefully, the tragedy will even put more of a sense of urgency in our efforts.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you worry that it may drive things the other way?\par \par \uc2 You've got Prime Minister Netanyahu saying out there this morning that they have put forth aggressive, what he calls, proposals and that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has rejected them again and again. And while they were in talks now executes these six, he says if you're murdering hostages, you don't want a deal.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: It -- that could be. I mean, we've certainly wondered along the way whether \uc2 Hamas\uc2  wants a deal or not. They -- they don't care about the suffering of the Palestinian people.\par \par \uc2 By the same token, there's also reports of internal dissension between the prime minister and his chief defense officials about the Israeli position.\par \par \uc2 So my sense is this is very, very tough. We're getting closer. You know, again, let's -- let's step back and reflect on the tragedy, the loss of these six and see if we can really push through to a solution that would involve not just hostage release and ceasefire, but, obviously, the humanitarian need with polo -- polio cases now being seen in Gaza. We've got a lot to do on the humanitarian front, too.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We absolutely do.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so let's talk politics. We got our first sit-down interview from the vice president this week --\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- along with her running mate, Governor Walz.\par \par \uc2 A lot of folks, they'll think we didn't get the detail, the level of detail that they think the American people deserve before they go vote for their commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bret Stephens writing over at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ": She was vague to the point of vacuous. She struggled to give straight answers to her shifting positions on fracking and border security, other than to say, "My values have not changed".\par \par \uc2 The interview also didn't answer the question of why she reversed her former positions or whether she has higher values other than political expediency.\par \par \uc2 So we're just a few days out from people beginning early voting. Last I checked, kamalaharris.com still does not have the details or any policy prescriptions there.\par \par \uc2 Is the idea to ride this out on vibes? Because they've been good, the momentum has been good for the ticket.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: I think vibes and accomplishments. I remember that first interview that I did with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . We did it, you know, right after the convention or maybe the day before the convention, and it was similar. You can't get into everything in a short interview. In that case, it was "60 Minutes".\par \par \uc2 But look, on fracking, let's use that as an example, she took a position in a presidential debate in 2019, but now she's been part of an administration that is rocketed ahead with alternative energy investments because of the Inflation Reduction Act, but has also used the production of natural gas to backstop our allies who are trying to wean themselves away from relying on Russia.\par \par \uc2 America's the largest exporter of LNG in the world under the Biden-Harris administration. Fracking's one of the reasons for it and we're helping our allies especially in Europe not have to rely on Russia.\par \par \uc2 Campaigning is one thing. Governing is another. And what governing has shown with Vice President Harris is she's going to try to bring the team together and make something happen, and that team includes the administration. It includes Congress. It includes governors. It includes the private sector.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It did seem that fracking was where we got the most level of detail for her about her evolution on the issue. But one of the places we really didn't get as much of an explanation was the border.\par \par \uc2 Bret Stephen goes on to write in that same "New York Times" piece.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He says: She evaded the question of why it took the Biden administration more than three years to gain better control of the border which it ultimately did through an executive order that he says could have been in place years earlier.\par \par \uc2 So you can evolve, but I think people want more of an explanation.\par \par \uc2 Here is a little then and now the vice president on the border.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  THEN 2020 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We're not going to treat people who are undocumented crossing the border as criminals.\par \par \uc2 DEBATE MODERATOR: Raise your hand if you think it should be a civil offense rather than a crime to cross the border without documentation? Can we keep the hands up so we could see them?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE U.S. AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I believe there should be consequence. We have laws that have to be followed and enforced that address and deal with people who cross our border illegally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, not treating it as a criminal offense. Now that's very different language from her. They've got to be held accountable. There are laws broken.\par \par \uc2 Do you know how she evolved to that position?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, I've watched it in real time from Congress. You know, the Biden-Harris administration has removed more people from the United States, about 4.4 million, than any administration except the second \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  administration, which came in at about 5 million.\par \par \uc2 So the actual practice and especially beginning with executive orders in May of 2023 has been rem -- create some positive paths where people can come in, but when they come in the wrong way, they're being removed. And that's been going on for the last year and a half. You really see the numbers improving now.\par \par \uc2 The right answer, of course, is for Congress to finally engage in immigration reform and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was very involved in the negotiation of a very tough border security deal with Republicans in late '23 and early '24.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told Republicans don't vote against this. We don't want to fix the problem. We want to complain about the problem.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let's talk about pre and post that because those are two different issues.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But the fact is there were record high numbers, critics will easily point to, and say millions of people have entered during the first three and a half years, even if you want to talk about the improvement the last few months of this administration because of their policies. Our polling shows people put the blame at the Biden-Harris administration. I think it's north of 70 percent say it's the lack of enforcing current laws that led us to where we are.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, again, I have a little bit of a different perspective. First, the Biden administration began putting executive orders in place in May of 2023, that I've seen the numbers come down and then they did more after Republicans bailed on the bipartisan proposal in the spring of this year.\par \par \uc2 And again, when you see those numbers on deportations and removals, those don't lie. I wish Republicans would quit repeating over and over again that borders are open because when they do that, they make the problem worse by encouraging people to come.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But people think they're open. I mean, they feel magnets pulling them here. I mean --\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Four-point-four million people being deported? I mean, that's not open.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: If you got seven, eight, maybe north of, that we don't know about all the gotaways, millions of people who say they're coming here because they do believe the border is open.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: And every time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says the borders are open, that message goes loud and clear. Cartels get people to pay 'em thousands of dollars to bring them to the United States because of that message.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But do you not think that policies of this administration, work permits, free housing, free healthcare, in some places, free tuition, does that not serve as a powerful magnet to make people believe they come here and things are going to be certainly much better than in their home countries?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Shannon, here's what I think -- I was a missionary in Honduras in 1980 and '81. I think I know why people come here.\par \par \uc2 They come here because they -- they believe there's opportunity in this country and they're so tired of violence and corruption and lack of opportunity in their own. They come here for the same reason my eight great-grandparents came here from Ireland in the 1860s.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't mean we shouldn't have rules and that doesn't mean we shouldn't enforce them. I like what the Biden-Harris administration has done with respect to not allowing people to cross the border between ports of entry and then claim refugee or asylum status if they are picked up.\par \par \uc2 You need to get an appointment. You need to go to a port of entry. You need to wait your turn. You may or may not be allowed in if you meet the standard or don't. Putting that kind of order in place is important, and that's what the administration has done.\par \par \uc2 But again, Congress really bears the lion's share of responsibility in this. Three times since I've been in the Senate, we've done bipartisan border security deals and Republicans have killed it each of the three times.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and also though, if you look back to her time as a senator, there was a point at which senator -- then-Senator Harris, vice president now, she tried to stop deals that were coming through. A letter that she sent to Senate appropriators, Democrats and Republicans, urged them to stop one.\par \par \uc2 She said in 2018, April: We ask you to reject President Trump's fiscal year funding for a border wall, new Border Patrol agents, and a large increase in U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement personnel and detention beds.\par \par \uc2 That was a funding request but some of the same things she says are imperative for fixing things now. She was against when there was a Republican president.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: I remember that part of 2018 very well because we did a bipartisan deal in the Senate that gave \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  every bit of his $25 billion and also protected Dreamers which he said he wanted to do.\par \par \uc2 As soon as we put that bill on the table, he did a 180 and killed it. That's what I remember about 2018 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , talking a big but when we tried to do $25 billion in border security, he told his Republicans --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But he was president then. Why wouldn't he want it solved? I can understand your argument now that he wants it as a campaign issue.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: At the time, he didn't want it solved because he decided to backtrack on his support for Dreamers. He had said over and over again, DREAMers are good people. We should have a solution for Dreamers.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Rounds\uc2  and Angus King and I, we presented a proposal that was, Mr. Trump, can you take yes for an answer? $25 billion in border security, protect Dreamers. As soon as we put the deal on the table with bipartisan support, he did a $180 billion, just like he did earlier this year when he told the Republicans to vote against the tough border deal.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You know that there were a number of your colleagues who were going to vote against it anyway. They think that this bill has way too much amnesty and other issues that they said they could not vote for, regardless of how President Trump talks about it.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: You're talking about the 2024 bill.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yeah, but look, that bill was negotiated by one of the most conservative members of the Senate, \uc2 James Lankford\uc2 , who is a real expert on border issues. He's -- at every point along the way, he went to his colleagues and said, we've negotiated this point, is this OK or isn't it? But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was very plain. He said, don't vote against this because we want to complain about the problem, not fix the problem. And his repetition again and again that there's open borders at the same time as we're deporting millions of people makes the problem worse. Let's join hands and fix the problem.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: People feel the policies speak for themselves. In the meantime, I want to show this. This is a video from suburban Denver. This is Aurora, Colorado, where DHS confirms these guys at this apartment complex going around and apparently, you know, terrorizing people with guns. They are members of a Venezuelan gang believed to be in this country illegally. A city council member there said she helped people move out of that complex and that there are multiple others there in Aurora.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This is a direct result of people flooding across the border with complete lack of accountability. I mean, no American thinks that this is OK.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: No, and I don't think it's OK either. In 2013, we did a bill, $45 billion in border security, bipartisan in the Senate. House Republicans killed it. 2018, $25 billion in border security. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  killed it and he killed the deal in February. If we had done that bipartisan bill in 2013, you wouldn't see problems like this being so severe.\par \par \uc2 Every time we put a package on the table that's bipartisan, Kamala worked on the deal with Senator Lankford and others. We put a package on the deal --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He said she didn't show up. He said she was no part of negotiating that deal.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, she might not have been a negotiator in the room.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, her staff, he says that they were not part of it.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: She has said she will sign this bill as president when it gets to her desk. A tough deal negotiated with Republicans and she'll sign it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, as Lankford says, it does include the border wall.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: He shall sign it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And so if she said that she's going to sign it, OK, so now for the border wall. All right, Senator, always good to have you in.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you so much for coming by.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Glad to.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, the final sprints of the 2024 race begins. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with a boost in poll numbers and enthusiasm. As President Trump denies that his rival is taking the lead. We're going to talk to one of Trump's top advisors, \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2 , as some states are just days away from starting to vote. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Amid some major twists and turns in the presidential race, former President Trump, brought back his former 2016 Campaign Manager. So will there be changes to the campaign strategy?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Trump-Vance campaign Senior Official \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Corey.\par \par \uc2 COREY LEWANDOWSKI\uc2 , TRUMP-VANCE CAMPAIGN SENIOR OFFICIAL: Thank you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's start here. You are back freshly with the campaign. Is it your sense that there will be changes at the top? Is there going to be a new strategy? Have you made suggestions? What's your role so far?\par \par \uc2 LEWANDOWSKI: Well, no changes at the top, Shannon. Look, I have the opportunity to work with Susie Wiles and Chris LaCivita. I like to call us the three amigos. We are solely focused on one thing, which is making sure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has the opportunity to lay out his vision for America over the next nine weeks and let the American people go to the ballot to understand what's at stake here.\par \par \uc2 And so, no changes at the top. But what we have seen from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and what I've always recommended is his ability to get in front of the American people. And he has traversed these battleground states, whether it's Michigan or Wisconsin, Pennsylvania. You know, you have seen a schedule which has been a gauntlet that -- and you juxtapose that to where the Harris-Walz campaign has been. Really, they haven't been on the road. They've been waiting for the unofficial summer to end. They've done one interview in 40 days, which really wasn't a hard-hitting interview.\par \par \uc2 So when you put the two campaigns side by side, there is no question that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is out working, out talking about policy, and out maneuvering the Biden-Harris-Walz machine.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we're going to talk about the momentum, though, that seems to be running in their direction, at least now coming off the convention and with the ticket shakeup. We'll get to the polls in just a minute, but I want to start here. This race is a totally different one than it was a few weeks ago. You've got a different opponent. You've got to look at this differently.\par \par \uc2 "Politico" reports this. "Privately, Trump has suggested he understands that he could lose in November if he does not dramatically change his approach to the race. What's the internal assessment about a pivot, a change? Will we see a different strategy?\par \par \uc2 LEWANDOWSKI: No, absolutely not. Look, honestly, I think she's a much easier opponent. You know why? Because she is saddled by and accountable to the American people for all of the failures for the last three and a half years under the Biden administration. What we see now is she's trying to change her positions. I know we'll talk about that. But how is she not being held accountable for all of the failures, whether it's the economic calamity that has faced this country, whether it's the national security calamity, whether it's the homeland security issues?\par \par \uc2 You know, we have seen an open border. Just last week, Shannon, we've seen the three-year anniversary of the disastrous Afghanistan withdrawal and the 13 Americans who were killed under her watch. And not only was she the last person in the room, but she was proud of that and asked in an individual interview this week if she has any regrets. She said no.\par \par \uc2 So, look, our position isn't changing. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been very clear. His values haven't shifted. His positions haven't shifted. He's been very clear. We're going to close the border. We're going to provide economic security for the American people. And you'll be better off under the Trump administration than you would under a Harris administration. And we have the record to prove it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So that succinct summary that you give is one that a lot of President Trump supporters would like to hear him also giving. And they're frustrated that he seems to be drawn off message quite a bit. Here are a couple of opinion columns. They say, "Trump's biggest pitfall is his inability to focus on his opponent in any serious way. Because of Trump's severe obsession with himself, he's been unable to expose and exploit Harris' weakness."\par \par \uc2 Another one says, "His odds of victory will increase if he can find the self-discipline to bury his ego and talk incessantly,' as you just did, 'about issues voters care about, such as the economy, inflation, crime, illegal immigration, and Harris' promised tax increase."\par \par \uc2 We're just days away from early voting starting. You know you have the base. But those things that you touched on, they may be more appealing to independents, to suburban women, people that you need in a race this tight.\par \par \uc2 LEWANDOWSKI: Shannon, listen, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has had the opportunity over the course of the last multiple weeks to do very specific policy speeches. He'll be doing another one this week. He went to the border. I had the privilege to be there. I listened to the families who have been devastated by the Biden-Harris administration, who lost loved ones on that border because of the crisis that we continue to see.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is giving specific policy speeches. And again, you mentioned it in your last segment. No policy speeches on the Harris website. It's amazing to me that she has not been held accountable by most of the mainstream media for that. She has a continuously shifting position. She's a bit like a chameleon. She decides to say whatever she wants, depending on who she's in front of. And the question is, what position does she really have, and when will she be held accountable for it?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So in the meantime, let's talk about those polls, which have been shifting. Our polls this week on Swing State show a shift in favor of the Vice President. Very tight, most of these polls are within the margin of error. But "USA Today" and "\uc2 Suffolk University\uc2 " say the shift they've now seen, giving her a national lead, is based on this. They say, "big shifts among some key demographic groups, traditionally crucial for Democrats, including Hispanic and black voters and young people. Among those with annual incomes of less than $20,000, the biggest change, a three-point Trump edge over Biden in June, has become a 23-point Harris advantage over Trump in August." How do you turn around the momentum? Do you have time?\par \par \uc2 LEWANDOWSKI: Well, Shannon, let me just remind the viewers of this. At this point in the race in 2016, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was down to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  by an average of 5.9. At this point in the race in 2020, it was 6.9 to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Today, if you look at the Rasmussen poll, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a four-point lead.\par \par \uc2 We're in the strongest position we've ever been at this point in the race. We have an incredible ground game operation, which is running in our battleground states. We have a candidate who's willing to work 24 hours a day on behalf of the American people.\par \par \uc2 And we feel very good about what our message is, which is economic freedom and economic opportunity for everybody, because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's ability, unlike the Biden-Harris-Walz administration, to continue to put regulations and rules on people is to let people have more of their own money. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  passed the largest tax cut in American history, which gave more people opportunity. Small businesses got to grow.\par \par \uc2 We had more people working pre-COVID than at any time in American history. That's what we're going to bring to the table. So, you know, the American people have an opportunity come November. They can stay like they have for the last three years and be crushed from economic disasters, our terrible trade policies, a terrible foreign policy, or they can change the page. They can go back to the way it was under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which was an opportunity for everyone to be successful, regardless of their race or their religion, their ethnicity or their age. Everybody prospered under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And that's what I think the American people want.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, you're going to get a chance to vet that. The president will on this September 10th debate. There's been a lot of back and forth about whether or not the rules are settled. Let's talk about this. The Vice President posted on "X" this. "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is surrendering to his advisers who won't allow him to debate with a live microphone. If his own team doesn't have confidence in him, the American people definitely can't. We are running for president of the United States. Let's debate in a transparent way with the microphones on the whole time."\par \par \uc2 Has the microphone thing been settled? Will it be settled? Is everybody showing up on September 10th?\par \par \uc2 LEWANDOWSKI: We'll be there. I can't speak if \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to be there, but we'll be there. And remember this, Shannon, when we originally set the parameters of the debates for, at the time, President Biden, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' team was also part of that because we set the same parameters for the vice presidential debate. And what those parameters were, were you would stand at a podium or a lectern, you would have no notes available to you, and the microphones would be off.\par \par \uc2 Well, after she has been coronated to be the presidential nominee for the Democrats, what she's done is come back and said, I don't like that criteria anymore. I want to be seated so I can be the same height as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I'd like to bring notes to the table.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, her team denied that.\par \par \uc2 LEWANDOWSKI: Oh, and by the way -- well, I understand, but we've been part of the negotiation back and forth for many, many days here. And I don't know why, once again, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and her team are changing what their positions have been, just like she's done on the border, just like she's done on no tax on tips. She's now trying to change the rules of debate. We've agreed to the rules of the debate. We are looking forward to being there on September 10th. The microphones will be off. People will be standing at a lectern and there'll be no notes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we'll be watching. The president, by the way, is making a lot of news this week on the issues of abortion, of IVF. So I want to talk about that. Here's what he told our Bryan Llenas Friday about how pro-lifers who are increasingly publicly and privately talking about their frustrations. Here's what the president told Bryan Llenas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Oh, I don't think so. I don't think they feel betrayed at all. They love me. I'm the one that got rid of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But here's what one prominent pro-life activist, \uc2 Lila Rose\uc2 , told "Politico." She says, "I think it's foolish what he's doing. It's politically unwise. It may cost him the election and it's morally unprincipled as well. Right now, it's all about turnout. If he wants to galvanize his base, he needs to stop trying to pander to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' base because they're never going to vote for him anyway."\par \par \uc2 She says no one owns the pro-life vote. She says, listen, she doesn't think a bunch of people are going to go vote for Harris, but she may stay home. She's telling other people to stay home. In a race this tight, can you afford to offend that constituency?\par \par \uc2 LEWANDOWSKI: Well, look, if she chooses to stay home, then by, you know, tacit endorsement, she's supporting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who has had a radical position on the issue of abortion. Many Democrats believe that you can have an abortion not only up until the last week, but also in some cases after the baby's been born. What \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said was, let's have the states decide.\par \par \uc2 They are the laboratories of democracy. Let's turn this back. And that's what Roe v. Wade really did. And so, we see across this country right now opportunities for individuals to go and vote at the ballot box of what they want to see transpire in their state. And every state is going to look a little different.\par \par \uc2 And, Shannon, we have seen some relatively very conservative states go to a position where women are giving opportunities that you would not have expected because of that.\par \par \uc2 But those states understand whether it's Ohio or Kansas that women have the opportunity to make their own decisions and the states are allowing that to happen.\par \par \uc2 So what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has done, and I think by and large the American people support this is Roe v. Wade has been overturned because of his three Supreme Court nominees that are now sitting on the bench and they put it back to the states and it's a decision at the local level now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and I will say that to be clear in a number of states, there is a pregnancy -- there is abortion allowed through pregnancy and that includes a measure that Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  signed in Minnesota, but Democrats say they absolutely disavow the idea of infanticide or of a baby. That does survive an abortion. Somehow their life being taken at that point.\par \par \uc2 We can continue that debate because there are a number of states that do allow it through pregnancy, but I think everybody agrees infanticide is illegal and wrong.\par \par \uc2 Corey, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LEWANDOWSKI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: After weeks as the nominee, Vice President Harris did her first sit- down interview as a presidential candidate this week with her VP pick Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  by her side. Did we learn anything new?\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel is here to break down the vice president's policy shifts and her running mate's interesting answers about his own misstatements, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think the most important and most significant aspect of my policy perspective and decisions is my values have not changed.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She's lived with policies for many, many years and they've been the same. And all of a sudden, like, a short while ago for purposes of getting elected, she switched.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So that was Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Thursday, finally sitting down for her first formal interview as the nominee and President Trump says she's now running from her positions.\par \par \uc2 It's time now to talk about it with our Sunday group, Ronald Reagan Institute Director, Roger Zakheim, Fox News contributor and former State Department spokesperson, Marie Harf. Targeted Victory executive vice president and former senior advisor to Senator Tim Scott, Matt Gorman. And Reuters White House correspondent, Jeff Mason. Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 OK. So, Jeff, we got the interview. When do we get the solo interview? Because, you know, nobody's going to be satisfied with that.\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, REUTERS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We want a lot more answers and a lot more access.\par \par \uc2 MASON: Yes. I mean, I think all of us want more access and that's the role of the press is to have a chance to talk to the candidates.\par \par \uc2 I think this first one, you know, she did say I was in a gaggle. We call it a gaggle with her on a tarmac a few weeks ago when she said she was planning to do this interview. It took a few weeks to get there. She went through a whole lot of questions and answers.\par \par \uc2 You can't get that many done in 20 minutes, which is 20, 25 minutes, I think is about what they had.\par \par \uc2 I think the answer that you just played about her values is something that Democrats are going to like and Republicans are going to use against her. And that's -- I think we're already seeing that since.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Roger, we talked about vibes. And, of course, when you talk about freedom and prosperity, those are things that everybody is for.\par \par \uc2 But we've got such a short condensed race here. Will she be pressed to really give us more detail?\par \par \uc2 ROGER ZAKHEIM, DIRECTOR, \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2  INSTITUTE: Well, I think she'll be pressed. The question is whether or not she feels obligated to go beyond the vibes, as you just noted.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has policies in about 20 bullet points. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  doesn't have it yet.\par \par \uc2 And policy, I think, is ultimately going to be what people are demanding of her. And she'll do it once she sees those polls demand it.\par \par \uc2 So far, as you reported today, there hasn't been demand.\par \par \uc2 And I think President Trump is probably going to drive out this more and more. We saw that with some of the recordings. We see it actually in the debate over Abbey Gate and Arlington this week. That is fundamentally about policy.\par \par \uc2 And the more they drive at it, the more she'll have to decide, am I going to continue what President Biden pushed forward during his administration? Or am I going to depart for it? It's a bit of a Catch-22 form.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. Marie, how does she do that? Because she was really pressed on Bidenomics by Dana Bash.\par \par \uc2 Like, do you -- do you support it? She would use the word. She did talk about, listen, we've got a lot of good things done. People can debate that. The polls show people don't think the economy is in good shape right now. She says, we've got more work to do.\par \par \uc2 How does she walk this line between, I'm part of this administration and the good things that happen, but I'm not, if it's not good, and I'm going to do something different.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: That's always hard for a vice president, right? That's why we've seen a mixed results when vice presidents run for president.\par \par \uc2 But I think what she did the other night and what she will continue to do is say, look, we have gotten a lot done. Inflation is down, wages are up, you know, unemployment, especially among certain communities, including the black community is really low, but we have to build on that.\par \par \uc2 And she has outlined policies on the economy. She's talked about making housing more affordable, childcare more affordable, education more affordable, healthcare more affordable. She has talked about all of this.\par \par \uc2 And, look, this election is shaping up to have the largest gender gap in American history. Every poll is showing that that's where we're headed, in large part because she is talking about a more hopeful future, a more optimistic future, including on things like freedom.\par \par \uc2 And we know that the third or fourth, depending on polls, most important issue that voters care about is abortion and healthcare.\par \par \uc2 And this week, you saw \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has no strategy to win back women voters. He is flailing on what to do about women's freedom and healthcare. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been incredibly strong on that, although she's not leaning into it. She's talking a lot about the economy. But that's why the polls, as of today, she is leading in the national average and in many battleground states.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's talk about that because one of the issues where President Trump has had a distinct advantage all through both the Biden and now a Harris campaign has been on the economy, but that's actually moved.\par \par \uc2 Reuters reports this, they say that Trump's approach to the economy and employment was preferred by 43 percent of registered voters in their polling compared to 40 percent of preferred Harris' approach.\par \par \uc2 The prior poll, in late July, showed Trump with an 11-point advantage on the economy.\par \par \uc2 So she is not, not getting tagged with whatever it seems like people don't like about the Biden economy.\par \par \uc2 MATT GORMAN, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, TARGETED VICTORY: No. And you saw Corey try and do that in the interview just now. I think what you're seeing also remnants of the convention bump in these sorts of poll numbers, right? She, essentially, front loaded the head-to-head bump when she took over the ticket in late July.\par \par \uc2 But what the Democrat convention helped was her image, her favorability around some of these policies. So now it's incumbent in these next 10 days, certainly at the debate for the Trump campaign to press the case and get those advantages a little bit wider, what they were saying early August or late July.\par \par \uc2 That's how they can really you'll start to see the head-to-head move in some of these battleground states.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the interesting thing is there's another poll out there that does a deep dive onto the economy and they -- that people chose when they - - could choose between Trump and Harris head-to-head.\par \par \uc2 They gave Trump the advantage on handling jobs, unemployment, the stock market, taxes, interest rates, balancing the national budget, the cost of everyday goods and gas prices.\par \par \uc2 And when they were asked how the country is doing in the economy, 69 percent said, we're on the wrong track.\par \par \uc2 So, Roger, why isn't he doing better on that issue?\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: I actually think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  underperforms in polls and actually --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: he does too.\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: This is -- and this is actually ultimately going to bounce in his favor. I expected the debate just pointed out he's going to press on these points. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is advancing big government, big spending solutions. He can advance that his economy when he was president, prior to COVID, put more Americans into jobs was a great economy.\par \par \uc2 And that's where he's going to -- he's going to push on, particularly on these differences in terms of tax cuts where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going in an entirely different direction.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So there's also this issue of RFK Jr. who sat here last week talking about his decision to leave the race to essentially throw support to President Trump. And we talked about how many states he was going to try to get off the ballots.\par \par \uc2 Well, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " notes this. It turns out Kennedy is actually going to remain on the ballot in three key states, Michigan, North Carolina, Wisconsin. Those are critical.\par \par \uc2 It is a headache, they say, for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  whom Kennedy endorsed and whom Kennedy's supporters tended to favor more than Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, you know, North Carolina is one of those states, Matt, where the democratic dominated kind of election board situation they have there, even though he pulled out a week ago endorsed Trump and asked to have his name removed from the ballot. They said, nope, you're staying on.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 Look, he's not going to hit six or eight percent. But what you tend to see with third parties is they'll get maybe about one percent.\par \par \uc2 When people go to the ballot box, it tends to slim the margin a little bit, but I think we're headed to probably the close election since Bush v. Gore in 2000.\par \par \uc2 So that one percent, maybe less, could make a big difference in some of these most crucial states to the effect that he can't get off the ballot.\par \par \uc2 That could, and some of these states, absolutely pretend a little bit of trouble for the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: When you say Bush v. Gore, we all have a little bit of a nightmare. I'm sorry that time.\par \par \uc2 I'll be on the steps of the Supreme Court trying to figure out what the justices who don't want to be dragged into this have decided.\par \par \uc2 But to this issue that I talked with Corey about, pro-lifers, Jeff, I mean, in a race this tight, they're really voicing their consternation.\par \par \uc2 And he said, listen, if you vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  or if you stay home, that's essentially a vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . But they feel, on principle, they don't want to vote for someone they feel like is advancing policies they don't think are pro-life.\par \par \uc2 MASON: It's a risk. It's a risk. And it also, I think in general, goes to show politicians on both sides, Republicans and Democrats change their positions, alter their positions a little bit to bring in new sets of voters.\par \par \uc2 We saw that with Vice President Harris' discussion on fracking. You're seeing that now with President Trump's discussion on abortion. He stands by and is proud of the fact that his Supreme Court justices overturned Roe v. Wade, but now he's trying a little bit to get some of the women who are walking away from his ticket to come back by moderating on abortion. It's a risk on both sides.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And to also saying that he is exploring ways to mandate that IVF be available by (INAUDIBLE) for.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Suddenly, the Republican candidate went to healthcare mandate, when for decades they have run against healthcare mandates.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't know what to do on this issue. He has flip-fluffed on this for decades. He is so proud of the fact that they overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And we know, every time abortions on the ballot, that the pro-freedom, pro- choice, democratic position has won, even in deep red states, like Ohio and Kentucky and others.\par \par \uc2 So he has terrified of this issue, politically. But everything we've seen from him, in office, shows that he will take away women's freedoms, that he will take away women's ability to make their own decisions about their healthcare.\par \par \uc2 And that anything he is saying now on the campaign trail where he's flailing -- first of all, a Republican Congress would never pass a mandate to make government pay for IVF or make healthcare companies pay for it.\par \par \uc2 So this is purely political. And I don't believe it because everything he's ever done in office is exactly the opposite.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll see. He says he will not sign a national abortion ban if it comes to his desk. J.D. Vance says the same thing. So it's an issue to watch.\par \par \uc2 Panel, don't go far.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to tackle foreign policy and how a Trump versus Harris administration will plan to handle the world's most dangerous conflicts, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Israel had a right - has a right to defend itself. We would. And how it does so matters. Far too many innocent Palestinians have been killed. And we have got to get a deal done.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Her government has limited the supply of precision weapons to Israel that would allow them to cut down on civilian casualties and end this war as rapidly as possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Vice President Harris, Thursday, insisting Israel has a right to defend itself while seemingly criticizing the way it's currently doing so.\par \par \uc2 And you heard Senator Vance there saying she could be doing more to end the conflict as part of this current administration.\par \par \uc2 We're back with the panel. And obviously want to get back to our top story today about the six hostages that have been recovered from Gaza, found dead, including an Israeli American, Hersh Goldberg-Polin. President Biden saying this in reaction, "I'm devastated and outraged. Make no mistake, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders will pay for these crimes. And we will keep working around the clock for a deal to secure the release of the remaining hostages."\par \par \uc2 Roger, I know you know this family and the parents, and you've worked with them to try and get their son home.\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: Well, it's tragic. And I know you've had Rachel on the show. And it's wishing them just strength and comfort during this devastating time. Israel is mourning and the United States is mourning too on - on Hersh's execution as we've learned in Rafah.\par \par \uc2 But we ought to remember what Rachel and Jon spoke about when they came to the United States throughout this 330 day nightmare, and that is, this problem is urgent. That's what she said to the president of the United States. That's what she said to the members of Congress. And we haven't sufficiently addressed this urgency.\par \par \uc2 The president of the United States, in his statement, talked about there needs to be a price paid by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Those words ring hollow. There have been 330 days for \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to pay a price. And they have not. They've executed an American citizen. There needs to be an accounting before anything else. And we haven't seen that. And that is something I think Americans look for.\par \par \uc2 Last thing on it, and it goes to a broader policy point, is \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is part of this network. It's part of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . It's part of the Houthis. It stems from Iran and Tehran. And we have not seen, in the Biden-Harris administration, a policy shift since October 7th for what is the source of the attacks, is the source of what \uc2 Hamas\uc2  did to Hersh Goldberg-Polin.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Marie, you've been inside an administration. You know how difficult these issues are. And there are plenty of critics who say that this administration has taken a number of actions that have eased sanctions, that have made things easier for Iran, that have refilled their coffers, so they've been able to fund groups like \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . It's got to be one of those things that President Biden is thinking about for his legacy, trying to find an end to this and get these people home.\par \par \uc2 HARF: I mean this administration, and my former colleagues, are working every day to get a deal for getting the hostages home. Every single day. \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2 , his team has been working tirelessly to do just that.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' statement this morning, it was unequivocal, that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is an evil terrorist organization, that we need a deal to bring these hostages home.\par \par \uc2 And the reason that some on the left have been upset with the Biden administration is because they have stood by Israel, they have helped them with intelligence and weapons that have helped take \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders off the battlefield, that have helped decimate \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ' ability to attack Israel again.\par \par \uc2 But we've also seen, in the Israeli press, Israeli intelligence and military officials warning that time is not unlimited for these hostages. They are pushing their own government. There is a debate in Israel where the military intelligence officials are saying, we need to get them home now. Time is not on our side.\par \par \uc2 And so that is an entirely different process that we're watching play out. And our hearts are broken this morning. I mean I met with Carmel Gat's family when I was in Israel this summer. It is - they need to come home. And the Biden administration is working every day to get them home through the best way we know how, which is a deal that includes a cease-fire and bringing them home.\par \par \uc2 And it's not just saying, Israel, do whatever you want because, quite frankly, they get to decide how they prosecute this war. And there's a debate going on right now about Prime Minister Netanyahu's leadership of that. At the same time, you know, I've been - I've been heartened that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been so supportive in the face of some progressive opposition, candidly, that we saw at the debates - I mean at the convention.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we did.\par \par \uc2 Well, and, Jeff, also there was consternation that somebody representing the Palestinian voice was not allowed to take the stage at the DNC.\par \par \uc2 MASON: Yes, there was controversy. And I think it's - it's a tough issue for Vice President Harris. I think her position, as Marie said, is a hundred percent in line with President Biden. But her tone has been a little bit different. And that has been in part to appeal to pro- Palestinian voices in the Democratic Party, and in states like Michigan, to show that this is something that she cares about as well. Not - she obviously cares very deeply about the hostages and a ceasefire, but also wanting to show some empathy for the Palestinians and their plight.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, there's also this development there. We were covering this last Sunday, the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , the back and forth, in and out of Lebanon. And there are real worries about that escalating in the midst of potentially the Gaza ceasefire falling apart.\par \par \uc2 "The Hill" reports this. This is an opinion piece. They say "The Israel- \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  escalation is far from over. All signs suggest it will get much more intense rather an subside - a clear trend decision-makers in Washington need to prepare for."\par \par \uc2 And, Matt, that just complicates everything else in that region.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Absolutely right. This is certainly coming to - could be coming to a head in the next month or so right in the shadow of a massive presidential election.\par \par \uc2 And one thing I'm also watching more stateside is, we have college students back on campus, the weather's relatively warm. We're approaching, obviously, the one ear since October 7th. Do we see some of these protests, riots, anti-Semitic demonstrations we saw in the spring? And that sort of general lawlessness, to kind of go back to what we talked about a few minutes ago, does that affect the tone of the campaign at all? Does it affect the presidential election?\par \par \uc2 I think no doubt what we saw in the spring helped President Trump in a political sense. If we see it again right before, or while people are early voting, right before Election Day, this could make a difference in the ballot box as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we've also had this week the recognition of the tragic anniversary of what happened in Afghanistan. And "Foreign Policy" reports this, it may be worse than we think. "Al Qaeda has set up nine new terrorist camps in Afghanistan in 2024, a sign of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's increasing tolerance of terror groups in their backyard in spite of pledges to crack down." That's according to an Afghan resistance leader visiting Washington this week.\par \par \uc2 Roger, that's not to mention the absolute horror that is befalling every woman and daughter and young woman who is there in that country living through their nightmare.\par \par \uc2 ZAKHEIM: Look, it's a tragedy. I mean this is history repeating itself. We absolutely saw this coming. There were some sort of kind of notion of overwatch we could do with remotely piloted vehicles from Qatar to focus on what's going on in Afghanistan. We knew the terrorist threat was going to return.\par \par \uc2 Our intelligence agencies have actually said it's blinking red across the country, across the world from Afghanistan into the Middle East. This is the problem, what happened with Abbey Gate. It was ill-advising.\par \par \uc2 You know, you look at what President Biden said is that after Abbey Gate, we completed one of the biggest airlifts in history. Completing missing the tragedy and the botched withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 We have lost deterrence under the Biden administration. It is absolutely clear. And it's played out consistently from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's invasion of Ukraine, to what we're seeing in the Middle East with Iran using all of its proxies to render chaos across the Middle East, undermining our national interests.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we've got to leave it there. We are out of time, panel. Thank you all very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to take you back to Israel for the very latest on headlines there, the news of the deaths of six hostages in Gaza. What all this means for attempts to try to hold together some kind of ceasefire deal there. We'll go live, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Back to our top stories.\par \par \uc2 The recovery of the bodies of six hostages in Gaza by Israeli troops, including Israeli American Hersh Goldberg-Polin. All had been seized by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on October 7th.\par \par \uc2 Fox News chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst joins us live in Israel with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Shannon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 We continue to follow the aftermath. The Israelis really mourning today. Six of their fellow citizens killed by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  according to the Israeli military. And as a result, tomorrow a general strike has been announced across Israel by the largest labor union here. This will shut down the airport, businesses, and banks. There are civilians calling for a ceasefire agreement, and they're willing to do whatever it takes to get it.\par \par \uc2 Now, tonight, in Tel Aviv, Israel's second-largest city, there will be a vigil for the six individuals who were killed by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and a demonstration. The pressure is rising on the Israeli government to reach a deal. And as we speak, the Israeli cabinet is meeting in Jerusalem, trying to figure out what they will do next.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst, in Israel, thank you very much always to you and your team.\par \par \uc2 And a programming note, Fox News Channel's \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  will present a town hall with President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this week from Harrisburg, Pennsylvania. The one-hour town hall airs Wednesday, September 4th from 9:00 to 10:00 p.m., that's Eastern, on the Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Enjoy your holiday and have a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020240901ek9100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Says He Will Vote Against Abortion Amendment; Trump Does Not Commit To Federal Abortion Ban Veto; Harris Faces Backlash For Policy Reversals; Harris Denies Support For Fracking Ban; Trump Campaign Faces Scrutiny Over Arlington Incident; President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  Fires Air Force Commander; Suspect In Custody For Possibly Driving Under The Influence After His Car Struck And Killed NHL Player Johnny Gaudreau And His Brother Matthew; Repair Work On Notre Dame Cathedral In Paris After Fire Five Years Ago Continues; Democratic Presidential And Vice President Candidate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  And \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  Give Interview To CNN.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Bryan Llenas, Jacqui Heinrich, Jennifer Griffin, Griff  Jenkins, Alex Hogan \par \uc2 7,979 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Emily, we got to leave it there. That does it for us. Bret, over to you.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Hey, Gillian. Thank you. Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We begin tonight with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the campaign trail. The Republican presidential nominee, making headlines on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 Just moments ago, the former president telling Fox News he thinks a six- week ban is too short, but that he would vote against an amendment in his home state of Florida that would enshrine abortion rights in the state's constitution and overturn a current six-week abortion ban there.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas spoke with the former president ahead of his rally in Johnstown, Pennsylvania a short time ago, and he joins us live.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Brian.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Will former President Trump, a Florida resident, maintains that, that six week -- six-week abortion ban in Florida is too strict. But for the first time, he confirmed unequivocally that he will be voting no on Florida's Fourth Amendment, which would have -- which will, if voted on by the people of Florida, enshrine the right to abortion without government interference into Florida's constitution. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Bobby, voting no for that reason.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: No on Amendment Four.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: For that reason, because it's irradical. And when you are talking about radical, having a baby abort -- doing an abortion in the ninth month is unacceptable to anybody.\par \par \uc2 There is something in between, but the six is too short. It's just too short a period, and the nine months is unacceptable. But for that reason, for the radicalization on the Democrat side, we're voting no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Trump also was not willing to commit that he would veto a federal abortion ban if it landed on his desk to sign if elected president. That is something that his running mate, J.D. Vance said Trump would be willing to do just a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Would you veto a federal abortion ban?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'm not going to have to think about it, because it's working out so well right now. The states are doing it. It's a state's issue.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Only asking that because J.D. Vance said that you would veto an abortion ban if it was sent to your desk.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, what's happening is you're never going to have to do it, because it's being done by the states. The states are voting, and the people are now getting a chance to vote. And this is the way everybody wanted it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Yesterday, the former president unveiled a plan to provide free IVF to all women in the United States. That's in vitro fertilization.\par \par \uc2 But when I pushed him on the details, he admitted the details are still being ironed out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Willing to put up guardrails, for instance, perhaps, thinking about banning the discardment of unused embryos, for instance?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're going to be issuing a statement over the next two weeks on some of the details, but we're going to be paying for IVF for women.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Same sex couples. More and more same sex couples are using IVF. Are they going to be able to get free IVF under your plan?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, we're going to look at that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: And finally, the former president spoke about Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's interview last night, after getting a chance to digest it and watch it like the rest of us. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I think it was not exactly a great performance. There was -- it was a low energy performance to take all of that time and get ready for that kind of an interview. It was a softball interview.\par \par \uc2 Well, I think she should stop with the flip flopping, because it's not on her. She'd be better off if she just stuck with what she's been, you know, she's lived with policies for many, many years, and they have been the same. And all of a sudden, like a short while ago, for purposes of getting elected, she switched.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Former President Trump's stances on abortion and IVF will loom very large in this election, particularly for suburban voters, moms.\par \par \uc2 Here in Pennsylvania, tonight. Bret, former President Trump is headed to D.C. to speak to Moms for Liberty, moms who are fighting for more parental rights for their kids' education. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas, live in Pennsylvania. Bryan, thanks. \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2 , with the campaign joins us in a short time.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is defending her shift from some liberal positions, several of them. But her critics are pouncing on her interview performance over a lack of clarity and specifics. Her first interview on the trail.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has our interview fact check live from the North Lawn. Good evening. Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Vice President Harris, earned some praise for an interview that did just enough to keep up her momentum without any big stumbles that would cost her politically. But she kept her answers to the big picture, forcing another cycle of pressure to fully explain her positions.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Vice President Harris says her values haven't changed since she last ran for president, even though her positions have.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Do you still want to ban fracking?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: No, and I made that clear on the debate stage in 2020.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Harris made \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's position clear, as his running mate.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , will not ban fracking. That is a fact.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Even CNN's fact checker calling Harris out.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL DALE, CNN REPORTER: The fact-check, bottom line, Abby, is that she did not actually make clear at a 2020 debate that she had changed her previous support for a fracking ban.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): While Harris's campaign says her evolution stems from three years governing at Biden's side.\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, SENIOR SPOKESPERSON, HARRIS-WALTZ CAMPAIGN: Some of her positions have evolved. She leaned into her position on fracking.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): It's not her own position she is leaned into. It's Biden's she adopted. Harris's 2020 campaign pitch said, "After immediately halting all new fossil fuel leases on federal lands and waters, Kamala will use existing authorities and work with Congress to phase out existing leases."\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration did the opposite, outpacing Trump in approving permits for drilling on public lands, leaving open questions about how Harris would govern if her values haven't changed.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARKWAYNE MULLIN (R-OK): What she is going to do through the regulatory environment? Is she going to actually embrace it?\par \par \uc2 If she is serious about bringing down inflation, she's got to be serious also, then, about drilling.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Lack of detail extends to other issues. A \uc2 New York Times\uc2  opinion piece states, "She was vague to the point of vacuous. She evaded the question of why it took the Biden administration more than three years to gain better control of the border, which it ultimately did, through an executive order that could have been in place years earlier."\par \par \uc2 And Harris has not deviated from Biden's approach at all, but is framing herself as a change agent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He acts like he is the incumbent and she is the insurgent, when it's really just the opposite.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Harris said last night, she believes the U.S. should hold itself to deadlines on climate, but it's not clear what they are. Her 2020 campaign said by 2030, all new buildings would be carbon neutral and there would be no new gas-powered cars by 2035. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: He had crashed, in large part, all of that because of mismanagement by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he was going to do a number of things, including allowing Medicare to negotiate drug prices. Never happened. We did it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, there is a lot of takes on the Vice President Harris-\uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  interview. Some of the headlines. "Harris defends her record, slams Trump in first T.V. interview with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 "A good-enough prime-time debut."\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's much-hyped, first big interview was radically normal."\par \par \uc2 Well, there is other takes as well. Let's get reaction to Vice President Harris's interview and the issues of the day on the trail. Joining us now, senior Trump campaign adviser \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2 . Jason, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 JASON MILLER\uc2 , SENIOR ADVISOR TO THE TRUMP CAMPAIGN: Bret, thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What was the biggest takeaway from that interview on your -- from your campaign's perception, and your perception of how the media digested it after a day of digestion?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, Bret, simply put that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's values have not changed. So, all of the crazy ideas, whether it be completely repealing the Trump tax cuts, whether it be banning fracking, whether it be legalizing amnesty, and decriminalizing illegal entries into the United States, cashless bail, defunding the police, all the radical positions that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has put out there is still what she believes.\par \par \uc2 She might go try to nibble around the edges and say, well, I'm going to describe it a bit differently, but she said that her values have not changed, more often than any other policy position that she discussed in that interview.\par \par \uc2 Another thing too is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is going to have to own the fact that she is the one who's taking the country in the wrong direction. She can't get up there and talk about turning the page and going to a new chapter in the U.S. when you're literally in the White House right now.\par \par \uc2 For the last three and a half years, she could have taken action on all of these things, but it's on her. So, there has to be some accountability there. She doesn't deserve a promotion. She deserves to be voted out of office.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is a common answer when asked questions about the border and their actions on the border. Here is what she said last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: A bill was crafted, which we supported, which I support, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got word of this bill that would have contributed to securing our border, and because he believes that it would not have helped him politically, he told his folks in Congress, don't put it forward. He killed the bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, what is the response to that? It's clear that the former president did tell lawmakers that politically, they shouldn't go forward. But what about this bill, and what the stance of the campaign is?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, this is just a fake shiny object, Bret. And the fact to the matter is, is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did not need an act of Congress to go and ruin the border. She didn't need an act of Congress to go and fix it.\par \par \uc2 All they had to do is put President Trump's policies back in place, whether trying to gaslight the American people on this issue. The other thing too, with regard to this legislation is this was not some big broad bipartisan action. This still would have allowed in hundreds of thousands, if not, millions of illegals into this country. And we can't do that anymore.\par \par \uc2 You look at what's driving up housing prices, you look at what's driving up migrant crime all across the country. We can't do this anymore, Bret. That's why, when President Trump gets back in, he has said that two things he's going to do on day one, secure the border and drill, baby, drill. We need someone to come in and turn it around.\par \par \uc2 The great thing is, with President Trump, since everyone was better off with Trump, we know he can do it on day one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, where is the IVF idea? Paying for IVF. That's very expensive, as you know, it could be tens of thousands of dollars. If the federal government is picking up that tab or forcing insurance companies to do it.\par \par \uc2 There are real concerns in GOP lawmakers already. The Hill had a write up from Senator Mullen, saying, "I haven't talked to him all the way through with it. I think there is going to be an issue on how you pay for it. There's always that issue."\par \par \uc2 So, where did that idea come from? And is there conservative backing of that?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, there are a lot of people who support IVF. And everybody has someone, whether they're a friend or family member, who's had trouble with starting a family. And this is something that is we want our country to continue to grow the right way.\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure Americans can go and actually create babies. That's a good thing to go and have. So, yes, obviously --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, but paying for it.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: But hold on. But when you look at where money is going already, I would much rather be spending our money here in the U.S. to make our family stronger and better, as opposed to sending it overseas. You look at all the foreign aid that gets sent out the door.\par \par \uc2 The other thing too is the Biden administration, just look at the student loan giveaways, for example.\par \par \uc2 There are much smarter, better things we can be doing, such as this IVF proposal from President Trump. Now, obviously the details will continue to get fleshed out on this as it moves along, but this is something that's really groundbreaking.\par \par \uc2 I think, Bret, this is something that really cuts through with President Trump, and that he doesn't come from the traditional political sense of what the politicians in Washington do.\par \par \uc2 He sees problems that people are going through, and he takes steps to try to help them. That's why he was able to bring such dramatic changes right away, and other politicians would take decades to try to do it.\par \par \uc2 When you take a look at, say, coming up with peace in the Middle East. People laughed when President Trump said he would bring peace to the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: And he did it. We actually had peace in the Middle East. Putin did not invade Ukraine. That did happen, though, under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so, people like the bold ideas, the vision, and I think this IVF proposal will be a big winner.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Obviously, it needs to be fleshed out, as you said.\par \par \uc2 I want to wrap this up with this week -- there was the controversy about the Arlington National Cemetery and the visit there. The \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  says the issue is closed, but they put out a rare statement defending their employee there, who was, they say, defending the rules and the law that you couldn't take pictures and video at this certain section -- Section 60 of the grave sites.\par \par \uc2 And specifically, there can't be anything in a campaign. Do you have a regrets now that, you know, you all put out this video on the TikTok campaign site of that visit, despite the fact that the president was there at the invite of these families?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: President Trump and the campaign team did not do anything wrong. Again, President Trump was invited by Gold Star family members, followed all of the rules as they were explained to us, and then everything was done in memory of these 13 fallen service members.\par \par \uc2 Service members who, quite frankly, would still be here with us today if it wasn't for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's incompetence. It's because of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  that these 13 service members are dead.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, Bret, where was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on Monday, on the third anniversary? He was at the beach. Where was \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? A no show. That was disgraceful, and the fact that they wouldn't even respect the names of those 13 service members was a shamed.\par \par \uc2 That's why so many Gold Star families, members of the active-duty military retirees, are so supportive of President Trump, because he is going to get us out of these endless wars, and he's going to be smart. He's not going to do stupid things like take our troops out first.\par \par \uc2 Bret, I can't believe that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  didn't address this in the sit- down interview last night. It's something that's going to come up in the debate on September 10th.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2  with the Trump campaign. Jason, thanks.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's preferred inflation indicator, the personal consumption expenditures price index, increased by two-tenths of a percent in July, 2.6 percent from a year ago. That news led to a rally on the stock market, predicting interest rate cuts to come.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the Dow gained 228, for a new record close. The S&P 500 added 56. The NASDAQ jumped 197.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was up nearly one percent. The S&P 500 added a quarter percent. The Nasdaq fell nearly one percent.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a delicate balancing act as Ukraine seeks to use additional U.S. artillery inside Russia. And U.S. officials look to avoid escalation.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight, we have fresh perspective on the foreign policy challenges facing the U.S. Former Secretary of State, former National Security Advisor Condoleezza Rice has a new essay out on this subject. Here's a preview of our conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Years ago, and it's hard to believe how many years ago, when I was covering the Bush administration, I sat down with you a number of times at the State Department talking about the issues of the day, the Middle East has changed since then. How do you see this, especially with Israel- Gaza, Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and where that is going right now?\par \par \uc2 CONDOLEEZZA RICE, FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR: What happened on October 7th to Israel was an act of barbarity and sophistication on the part of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  that I didn't think I would ever see. The Israelis will have to establish, reestablish deterrence. They will have to weaken \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to the degree that they can. Something by the way that most Arab states want to see.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Iran's fingerprints are all over October 7th and supporting \uc2 Hamas\uc2  financially. It's also moving quickly on its nuclear front, they are moving forward even as the supreme leader says, maybe it's time to renegotiate with the U.S. They're a bad actor. How do we deal with them?\par \par \uc2 RICE: They're a terribly bad actor. And anybody who keeps looking for moderates in Iran is also delusional. The real power brokers are the Revolutionary Guard and the \uc2 Quds Force\uc2  and those proxies that are operating, whether they're Houthis, who are challenging international shipping or proxy still trying to attack our troops in Iraq and Syria.\par \par \uc2 And so, the only answer with Iran is to recognize that never give another penny back to Iran from frozen assets, continue to put pressure on that regime, which is extremely unpopular with its people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will have the rest of my exclusive interview with Condoleezza Rice Monday on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  has fired the commander of the country's air force. That move comes four days after an F-16 fighter jet that Ukraine received from its western partners crashed during a Russian bombardment, killing the pilot. Zelenskyy says Ukraine needs to strengthen its military on the command level, U.S. experts have joined the Ukrainian investigation into that crash.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's military is pressing ahead with its ground counteroffensive inside Russia. This comes as high ranking Ukrainian officials met with U.S. Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  at the Pentagon. It's part of an effort to convince the U.S. to relax restrictions on using American weapons inside Russia.\par \par \uc2 Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin has that story from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Ukraine's defense minister arrived at the Pentagon to make his case for the U.S. to relax restrictions on the use of American weapons inside Russia.\par \par \uc2 RUSTEM UMEROV, UKRAINIAN DEFENSE MINISTER: The United States has set a powerful example to encourage other partners to stand by Ukraine in this critical time.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Umerov arrived with a list of Russian targets Ukraine wants to strike using long range missiles, some of the billions in military aid supplied by the U.S. The visit came a day after Ukraine's foreign minister made a similar plea to European allies.\par \par \uc2 DMYTRO KULEBA, UKRAINIAN FOREIGN MINISTER: If we are allowed to strike, we will significantly decrease the capacity of Russia to inflict damage on our critical infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The Biden administration has barred Ukraine from using American weapons to strike Russian air bases from which Moscow is launching glide bombs that are terrorizing Ukrainian civilians, like this girl in Kharkiv, whose apartment block was hit today, killing six, injuring 55 including kids on a playground.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's request has been rebuffed in the past by U.S. officials who argue such a move could be escalatory, perhaps provoking Putin to launch a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PAT RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: As it relates to long range strike, deep strikes into Russia, our policy has not changed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Concerns have mounted that with Ukraine diverting thousands of troops from eight brigades in the east, it has opened itself up to Russia taking Pokrovsk, a key logistics crossroads town in Donetsk.\par \par \uc2 So far, Putin has not taken the bait and diverted his forces to expel Ukrainian troops from inside Russia, preferring to push further into Ukraine, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, tragedy strikes the world of hockey. The latest on the investigation into the deaths of NHL star Johnny Gaudreau and his brother.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, an Israeli missile hits a convoy carrying medical supplies and fuel to a hospital in the Gaza Strip. The aid group says several people were killed. Israel claimed, without immediate evidence, that it opened fire after gunmen seized that convoy.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Minato, Japan, one of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 Japan's defense ministry\uc2  is seeking a record $59 billion budget to fortify its deterrence on Southwestern islands against China's increasing threat. Defense officials are also focusing on unmanned weapons and artificial intelligence to make up for the declining number of service members in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, a Brazilian Supreme Court justice has ordered Internet providers to stop access to social media company X, formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , in that country. The move comes after X owner \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  refused to name a legal representative inside Brazil and refused to suspend certain accounts there.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, here in the U.S. a federal judge in Texas is allowing a lawsuit filed by Musk's X against the group Media Matters to proceed to trial. The lawsuit was filed after the group published a report showing pro-Nazi posts and other hateful content on the platform appearing next to advertisements from major U.S. corporations. X alleges it was all a manufactured smear. Several companies paused their advertising in response to the Media Matters post and controversy. Now X and Media Matters are heading to trial.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, a suspect is in custody over the deaths of NHL player Johnny Gaudreau and his brother Matthew. Sean Higgins is suspected to be under the influence of alcohol when his vehicle struck the brothers while they were riding bicycles. This is a horrific story. Correspondent Griff Jenkins has the latest tonight. For example, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Just heartbreaking. Good evening, Bret. Hockey fans are calling it one of the darkest days in the sport's history. Both brothers were said to be groomsmen in their sister's wedding today, a wedding now put on hold. The Gaudreau family releasing this statement "Last night, we lost two husbands, two fathers, two sons, two brothers, two sons and brothers in law, two nephews, to cousins, two family members, two teammates, two friends, but truly two amazing humans."\par \par \uc2 The 31-year-old NHL star known as "Johnny Hockey" for his style of play, a blend of speed and skill, and his 29-year-old brother Matthew who played in the minor league, were killed after 8:00 p.m. last night. The brothers were biking on a two-lane rural road in New Jersey. Police say the driver of the jeep was attempting to pass a vehicle on the left-hand side. When the vehicle was passing, moved to the left side to avoid the brothers. At that point the jeep tried to