the vaulting of the Gothic cathedral. After the flames, a promise to rebuild. Roughly 340,000 people from 150 different countries donated, adding up to more than $960 million.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON, FRENCH PRESIDENT (through translator): We will call upon the greatest talents, and there are a lot of them that will contribute. And we will rebuild.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: That work would not be restoring it to its original state but instead matching the centuries of wear and tear the building had experienced.\par \par \uc2 WILL GUSAKOV, CARPENTER: We were rebuilding the timber frame of the nave roof for the cathedral starting with logs, oak logs that had been shipped to us from all around the country.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Among the workers mobilized from around the world was Will Gusakov, a carpenter from Vermont. They used hand axes just as medieval craftsmen would have. By 2021 the structure was secure and interior work could begin, like dismantling the grand organ where led dust settled from the fire, or cleaning the iconic stained glass by delicately rubbing off smoke stains with cotton soaked in water and ethanol. But there is still work to be done. By February of 2024, workers removed scaffolding to unveil the new spire. The holy site, an architectural marvel, is one of the most visited tourist attractions in the world with an estimated 12 million visitors every year.\par \par \uc2 GUSAKOV: An opportunity to rebuild a cathedral like this is just a once in a millennium opportunity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): Over the next few months, furnishing and artwork are expected to be brought back into the cathedral. The initial goal was to reopen ahead of the Olympic Games. The official reopening is now set for the end of the year. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the latest on the campaign trail and the reaction to Vice President Harris's interview. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There's no question I'm in favor of banning fracking.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Do you still want to ban fracking?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: No.\par \par \uc2 BASH: What made you change that position at the time?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Let's be clear. My values have not changed.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You don't change like that. Every single policy change and it doesn't happen. She is a no-fracker. She is always going to be a no-fracker.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No fracker. A lot of reaction to the vice president's first interview. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " opinion page, "A vague, vacuous TV interview didn't help \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ." This is in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ." "She struggled to give straight answers to her shifting positions. She evaded the question of why it took the Biden administration more than three years to gain better control of the border, which it ultimately did through an executive order that could have been in place years earlier. It also didn't answer the question of why she reversed her former policy positions or whether she has higher values other than political expediency."\par \par \uc2 With that, right now, the Real Clear Politics average of polls at the national level has this race essentially tied. All these are within the margin of error, and it matches our polling from a lot of the battleground states.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," and Jeff Mason, White House correspondent for Reuters. Jeff, I'll start with you. I guess one of the big things about that interview was the constant disconnect between turning the page and wanting to be a fresh start and getting out of the 