ven follow-up questions. And yet she still seemed to struggle.\par \par \uc2 And the problem here is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did get a bit of a bounce coming out of the convention. Those polls are not horrific for her right now. But when you look at where her Democrat predecessors were four and eight years ago, Clinton and Biden, she is trailing them by like five to seven points. And that's a problem. Clinton lost, and Biden barely won. And so she is going to want to be performing much better, but she just seemed to struggle so much with answering what her positions are, whether her values not changing mean she really is as radical as she's always claimed to be. And those are things that voters are going to want to know answers for.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. To be fair to Dana Bash, they were given 20 minutes, I think. She got 27, but 16 with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . But remember, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  was there. And she did have follow ups, Matthew, that tried to pin Vice President Harris down on some of those flip-flops and other aspects of it. The Walz answers also, after all of this build up to this interview, to say that the whole military dust-up and his history was because of grammar didn't seem like it met the moment. But that's just what a lot of people seemed to say one day later.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: Well, a lot of people are right. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had 39 days to prepare for this interview, Bret. And whatever preparation she did, did not show. She couldn't answer the first question, which is a gimme if you are a presidential candidate -- what will you do on day one? And as much as Dana Bash tried to provide her options to answer some of these questions, Harris seemed, I think, lacking confidence and uncertain in many of her answers.\par \par \uc2 But her fundamental problem is that she is running as a change candidate while still in office. And you can pretend that she's somehow going to chart a new path forward, but nothing she said in that interview suggests that a Harris administration would be any different than a Biden administration, unless it's a little bit more to the left than the four years under Biden.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff, I'm intrigued by the role of RFK Jr., \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  on the campaign trail. She just did a town hall with the former president. And RFK Jr. was on the "All In" podcast, talking about his interactions with former President Trump and what he thinks is second term would look like. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think if President Trump wins, that people are going to see very different President Trump than they did during the first term. I think he has changed as a person. And I have known him for, you know, 30 years. He had no idea he was going to win. He had no idea how to govern, and people descended on him the day that he got elected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's really interesting, for all the talk about being lambasted by Democrats, he may be a solid campaigner on the trail for the former president.\par \par \uc2 MASON: He may be. They certainly agree on some things and that's obviously one of the reasons why he endorsed him. That said, I think his comment suggesting that President Trump has changed, that was an issue in 2016 as well, or a topic that was discussed, that president, then candidate Trump would be a different person in office than he was on the trail. And that did not turn out to be true. President Trump is who he is. And I think anyone who is suggesting he is going to change now or was going to change the last time hasn't been watching closely how President Trump performs and how he acts.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. But, Mollie, he clearly knows more about the inside workings of Washington.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Right. I think the whole country knows so much more than we did in 2016 about how much the permanent D.C. bureaucracy controls things, how they have to be tackled. And that is something where RFK Jr. and Trump definitely agree about some of the corruption we have seen at federal agencies and in that permanent D.C. consensus.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, up next, the Friday Lightning Round. Trump's legal roadmap, Biden campaigning with Harris, where is President Biden? Plus, Winners and Losers. Then "Notable Quotables." It is Friday, after all.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Do we have a president? He just got back from California. He was supposed to do to the White House. He never got there. He went to Delaware, and he is laying on a beach sleeping all day long. No, who the hell wants to sleep? And who wants to sleep in public?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I promise I will be the best volunteer Harris and Walz's camps have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was President Biden at the DNC. He is on the second week of a vacation. We do know, we only have partial schedules as far as next week, but President Biden is scheduled to be with Vice President Harris in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, on Monday. And there you see a couple of the other campaign stops. We don't have the full schedules.\par \par \uc2 But again, "Biden continues vacation for second straight week despite saying he will work like hell until the end of the term. President Biden began his second straight week of vacation Monday, providing more fodder for critics to insist he's not finishing out his term with very much vigor. Sources differ on the exact number of days that Biden has spent on vacation during presidency. Data from the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  claims that after serving roughly two years and seven months as president, Biden spent about 40 percent of his time on vacation."\par \par \uc2 Back with the panel. Matthew, what about this? Is this legitimate, the criticism of all this?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I think it is considering the fact that Biden withdrew from the race for reelection. You have to ask yourself, Bret, with Biden kind of being out of the loop and Harris not seeming to know how to answer basic questions about what she would do on day one, we have a very powerful presidency, but a president and a possible future president who doesn't do anything with this power, leaving the government to the bureaucracy and to the regulators. It makes for a less free America and a more dangerous world.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff, you have been covering this White House. One of the questions that Vice President Harris had was, does she regret saying that he was vigorous and could run circles around other staff members? She said no, in that interview last night. Where do you think this stands and what the president is forecasting two weeks in a row?\par \par \uc2 MASON: Well, a couple things. I've covered three president now, and all of them have taken vacations, and all of them work on their vacations. I travel with President Trump to Mar-a-Lago and to Bedminster when he was in office. And he worked there. President Biden was on the beach this week. I know this because I was in Rehoboth for most of the week, for just a few hours one day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, the White House travels all of them.\par \par \uc2 MASON: The White House travels with you. People can make their own judgments about leaving the White House, that's fine. But the truth is they work on their vacations.\par \par \uc2 As far as your second question, I think that relationship between Harris and Biden is fascinating. It was fascinating to watch the two of them on the stage briefly at the Democratic National Convention. And the fact that they are campaigning together on Monday suggests that she does, and the campaign does think he can be of help, at least in a state like Pennsylvania. That said, from my reporting, I have spoken to Harris aides who are also waiting to see if Biden's approval ratings continue to tick up a little bit after he stepped aside, because if they do then he's more helpful. And if they don't, then he's not.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, a quick Winners and Losers. Winner first, Mollie?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: My winner is Brittany Mahomes, the wife of the \uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2  quarterback Patrick Mahomes. She had been receiving some criticism for some pro-Trump social media activity and other things. And she had said recently that she just does not care what other people she doesn't know think about her. So that's good advice for everyone.\par \par \uc2 My loser is \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  who admitted this week that he was complicit in the censorship industrial complex's draconian censorship of American speech related to COVID and interference in the 2020 election with suppression of stories about the Hunter Biden laptop.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Matt, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: My winner Dylan Crews. Welcome to the show. The Nationals new right fielder had his first hit, his first home run. He's going to hep bring the championship back to Washington.\par \par \uc2 And my loser Carlos Alcaraz, the great tennis player knocked out in the second round in an upset match. It just goes to show that even the best of us can have a bad day at the office.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That is true, 74th player upset him. OK. Jeff, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 MASON: OK, winners are the two astronauts who are staying in space a little bit longer after ride home got canceled and they're handling that with aplomb. And the loser is the CEO of Telegram who was arrested and is being investigated for alleged illegal activity on his app.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, guys have a great Labor Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 It is Friday. You know what that means. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN 