 Her Positions; CNN Fact Checks V.P. Harris' Stance On Fracking; Soon, Trump Pennsylvania Rally After First V.P. Harris Interview; NHL Star Johnny Gaudreau And Brother Killed In Crash; Doctor Charged In Perry's Death Arrives At Court; Soon, Doctor Expected To Plead Guilty In Perry's Death;' Trump Attorneys Try To Move New York Case To Federal Court; Venezuelan Gang Taking Over Apartment Buildings In Colorado\b0\par\par\uc2 Gillian Turner, Jacqui Heinrich, Joe Concha, Bryan Llenas, Joe  Concha, Robert Wolf, Martha MacCallum, David Spunt, William La Jeunesse \par \uc2 7,647 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [15:00:00]\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Good afternoon. I'm Gillian Turner in for Martha MacCallum. This is The Story.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris defending what critics view is a flip flop on two major issues for swing state voters who could well decide the election. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN ANCHOR AND CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: In 2019, you said, quote, there is no question I'm in favor of banning fracking.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT, DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: As president, I will not ban fracking.\par \par \uc2 BASH: In 2019, when you first ran, there was a debate. You raised your hand when asked whether or not the border should be decriminalized. Do you still believe that?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I believe there should be consequence. We have laws that have to be followed and enforced, that address and deal with people who cross our border illegally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: On fracking, she says her position may have changed, but insists that her values have not, a statement the Trump campaign is pouncing on.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: If you have the same values, why have you changed your mind on literally every topic that you previously held an opinion on?\par \par \uc2 She's going to do the same thing that she's been doing for the last few years. She's going to try to make it harder to get American energy from American territory. She's going to make it harder for us to drive down the cost of everything, from food to housing. She's going to make it harder for us to enforce our border laws.\par \par \uc2 It's really absurd for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to sit there and say, well, I don't actually believe in any of the thing that I told you, I believe, three years ago. She has governed as a person who believes those things.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We got a big lineup this hour for you. Clay Travis is here, Joe Concha, and the National Review's Noah Rothman, both standing by.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's go to Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich, who joins us from the North Lawn. Hey, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS SENIORE WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Gillian. Vice President Harris says her values have not changed, even though her campaign admits her positions have. And they characterize that as three years of effective governance with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, HARRIS CAMPAIGN ADVISER: She made it clear that some of her positions have evolved, which is very standard when you're in public service. When you learn more about the issues as things evolve, sometimes your positions do evolve. She leaned into her position on fracking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But Harris didn't exactly lean into her own position on fracking. She adopted \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's position after she became his running mate. In fact Harris' 2020 campaign website had this pitch. After immediately halting all new fossil fuel leases on federal lands and waters, Kamala will use existing authorities and work with Congress to phase out existing leases. The Biden administration did the opposite, outpacing the Trump White House and approving permits for drilling on public lands. So, Harris now claiming that she would govern as Biden did leaves a lot of open questions on the specifics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARKWAYNE MULLIN (R-OK): She can say all she wants about. She's not okay with outright banning the fracking, but she's also not saying what she's going to do through the regulatory environment. Is she going to actually embrace it?\par \par \uc2 If she's serious about bringing down inflation, she's got to be serious also then about drilling and making sure that we have all the energy that we need inside the United States without importing energy, like her state does from Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: This extends to other issues like the border. A \uc2 New York Times\uc2  opinion writer called her interview vague to the point of vacuous. Jillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Jacqui Heinrich at the White House for us this afternoon, thank you, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Let's go ahead and bring in Fox News Contributor Joe Concha and Noah Rothman, National Review senior writer. Thanks to you both for being here on set with me.\par \par \uc2 I guess, Joe, we are used to politicians flip flopping, AKA, changing their minds, AKA, evolving. I think watching the interview in full last night, what we did get from Harris for the first time was acknowledgement on some of these issues that she has changed her mind. What we didn't get was a clear explanation as to why.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Clear as mud.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: That was my takeaway. What was yours?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: My takeaway was we look at the style and the substance and you juxtapose it with the rallies, the coverage, the joy of the last five weeks of this campaign, which couldn't go any better for any political candidate in U.S. history. And then you look at that interview last night, and I think it wasn't that negative for the vice president for the reasons that you talked about. She lacked that energy. She often was looking down when she was giving her answers and it only lasted 18 minutes. That debate coming up in September, that will be 90 minutes and she seemed to be running out of gas almost at that point.\par \par \uc2 When you get to the substance, though, the biggest takeaway, like Jacqui just reported, like you've been talking about as well is the fracking and that she said that she was for fracking and she said it on a debate stage in 2020. That was her claim. I went back and looked at that transcript and that was with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  There is no mention whatsoever of fracking or her support for it.\par \par \uc2 [15:05:01]\par \par \uc2 And as Jacqui just reported on day one of the Biden-Harris administration, they banned fracking on public land.\par \par \uc2 So, you look at Pennsylvania, Nate Silver finds that whoever wins Pennsylvania, Nate Silver, obviously 538, whoever wins Pennsylvania has a 95 percent chance of winning the election. As Pennsylvania goes, so does this election. Her answer was clear as mud. And when you're talking to voters there and they don't know where you stand on the biggest issue, that's going to make it very hard to win that state, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Now that we have seen the interview, right, 