st looked odd. And to your point, why was \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  there? I guess he was the emotional support animal, but it looked like she needed a chaperone instead of saying, okay, this is my stage, this is my moment, I'll handle the questions.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Do you think he should have been -- meaning from the campaign's perspective, do you think he Served whatever purpose they wanted him to serve last night or it's still, you think, a big mistake that he --\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Net negative on both. And I don't think, we'll put it this way, you're not going to see another interview from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  or \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  before that debate on September 10th or even after anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 ROTHMAN: That is a huge liability if they think that this is all we get, because there's not very little to talk about, so we're going to parse this interview. And what do we get from this interview? \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  defending \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's record, taking ownership of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's record, saying that I have no regrets about essentially engaging in misleading comments regarding his condition, when, what you knew and when you knew it, and then offering this, you know, comea (ph) to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , that he's got the judgment and the character to be president. Well, then why did he leave the office? We're going to spend the next two weeks poring over these responses in which expose a glaring liability to the vice president, that she is tethered to an administration Americans don't like.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Real quick, you're each going to get five seconds.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: After watching last night, can you tell me what Harris' vision is, what she stands for, what motivates her to get out of bed every morning and do the hellacious campaign trail? What is it?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: It's clear as mud. And I think it's a huge problem when you can't define your own campaign. Four seconds.\par \par 