BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Gillian. Well, the latest Fox News poll shows that the race here in Pennsylvania is actually tied. And today at 4:30 here in Johnston, Pennsylvania, in Cambria County, which is a county Trump won by 37 points in 2020, we expect the former president to attack Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on her flip flopping on the issue of fracking and to react to that interview last night on that other network.\par \par \uc2 This is what he said yesterday specifically about the one preview clip he saw before he spoke to a crowd at a town hall in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He didn't look like a leader to me, I'll be honest. I don't see her negotiating with President Xi of China. I don't see her with Kim Jong-un, like we did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: This is his fourth visit to Pennsylvania in just the last month.\par \par \uc2 [15:15:01]\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, he went to Michigan and Wisconsin. And in Michigan, the former president making a surprise announcement, saying that if he's elected president, women would get free IVF, or in vitro fertilization. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I've been seeing a lot of IVF and I kept hearing that I'm against it. I'm actually very much for it.\par \par \uc2 The government is going to pay for it, or we're going to get, or mandate your insurance company to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: This new policy comes as Fox News polls show that Harris is up on Trump with women by 11 points in the Sun Belt states, and that abortion remains Harris' strongest issue, with plus 19 points over former President Trump on the issue of abortion and female reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 One last thing, Gillian, Trump is going to Washington, D.C. to speak to Moms for Liberty, a parental rights for education event in D.C. tonight. Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Bryan Llenis in Johnstown for us, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Clay Travis, OutKick founder, and Robert Wolf, former Obama economic adviser, now CEO of 32 Advisers and a Fox News contributor. Thanks to you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Clay, I want to get your general thoughts on last night and then in particular, let's drill down on fracking, the issue for which she has received the most criticism for supposedly flip flopping. A number of outlets have done a fact-check, including CNN, on what she said last night, which is that she made it very clear as far back as 2020 that she no longer supported a fracking ban. Take a quick listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will not end fracking. He has been very clear about that.\par \par \uc2 I will repeat, and the American people know, that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will not ban fracking.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL DALE, CNN REPORTER: So, it makes perfect sense that at the time she was speaking on behalf of Biden, the president, not the vice president, sets administration policy. But maybe other people feel differently. I certainly did not hear anywhere in there \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  saying that she personally had abandoned her previously expressed 2019 view.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: She did seem to acknowledge, Clay, last night that she had changed her mind. But as we talked about a moment ago, she, nowhere in last night's interview, explained why, even when pushed.\par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, that's why they haven't had her sitting for any interviews, Gillian. Thanks for me. I hope everybody has a happy Labor Day weekend as we roll in here. It's interesting Labor Day weekend because I felt that I watched that interview that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , who was sitting beside \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , was trying to serve as her lifeguard, except he's not a very good swimmer himself, and so both of them ended up drowning during the course of that interview.\par \par \uc2 And Dana Bash wasn't even at CNN pushing back very hard against her, but she pointed out quite clearly that Kamala has completely reversed her opinion on fracking. She said she would address it immediately if she were elected president in 2019. She said Biden didn't have the same opinion. She's also said that she believed in defunding the police. Now she wants to fund the police. She said that Trump wanting to build a border wall was racist. Now she wants to build a border wall. And she said that she believed in an electric vehicle mandate. Now she wants to end the electric vehicle mandate.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is actually presenting the best argument against \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  by taking the exact opposite sides. And then \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , the lifeguard who can't save somebody from drowning, was asked why he lied about carrying weapons of war in combat, and said, quote, my grammar isn't always correct. Well, he's a school teacher and he's been lying about that for 20 years. There's a picture of him in 2004 saying he's a veteran of Operation Enduring Freedom. He also didn't answer Dana Bash's question about why he lied about his DUI.\par \par \uc2 This is a combination of the two dumbest people to ever be nominated for president and vice president in history. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is a dim bulb, but I really believe that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is the dumbest person to theoretically ever be within a heartbeat of the presidency. The guy's a moron. Even on CNN, he couldn't answer questions well. I think it was a great night for Trump and that's why the gambling markets have made Trump even more of a favorite after \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  opened her mouth on CNN.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Wow. Okay. Robert, why didn't the governor answer the substantive questions put to him last night? To my ear, the only thing that he really spoke about at any length was his family. It's not really why he's there.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I hope I get the same amount of free time that you just gave Clay for that incredible advertisement. I know he's very good on his podcast. So, I hope I get to kind of rebut some of that.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: I'm good on podcast, T.V and national radio, to be fair, all three. I didn't interrupt. I didn't interrupt you once. So, I think what's only fair is I get to kind of same amount of --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Robert, you have the stage. Take it away.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Thank you. So, listen, I think it's all about what the vice president said. It's about giving opportunity the middle class.\par \par \uc2 [15:20:02]\par \par \uc2 Let's just state some facts here. When former President Trump was asked how he would rid inflation, he talked about drill, baby, drill. The good thing about the Harris perspective on energy is that she now has an all-in approach, which we know has had more energy production than former President Trump ever had.\par \par \uc2 Number two, when he was asked about housing, Vice President Harris has a very good plan to really go after the supply issue of 3 million homes. Former President Trump talked about mass deportation, completely irrelevant, okay, to the housing situation.\par \par \uc2 When we went into talking about pro-choice, no one has flip flopped more than former President Trump. He brags about ending Roe v. Wade continually. And what's interesting when he talks about his IVF policy last night, he must be talking about extending Obamacare, because that's the only way really insurance companies would ever be able to pay for that. That would actually be a new condition put on for insurance companies.\par \par \uc2 And the last thing, let's talk about the border. I could argue the biggest flip flopper on the border has been former President Trump, because why isn't he supporting a bipartisan deal that Border Patrol supports, adds more asylum workers to make sure that illegals don't cross the border, actually adds physical security and cybersecurity, and was actually put forward by a conservative Republican named Senator Lankford.\par \par \uc2 So, listen, I think this is a tight election. One poll has vice president up top. The other poll has former President Trump. My view today is, today, the vice president's the underdog. I think the former president has a little lead, but I think the momentum's with the current vice president.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Well, you both took all the time that we had for the segment, so we're going to have to leave it there with only one question to each. So, I hope you guys are happy with what we gave you the opportunity to lay out. Thanks so much for taking time with us and have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: Happy Labor Day, everybody.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You too. All right, one of the doctors charged in connection to Matthew Perry's ketamine overdose death is about to appear in court in California. We're going to take you there live, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN ESTRADA, U.S. ATTORNEY: This network included a live-in assistant, various go betweens, two medical doctors, and a major source of drug supply known as, quote, the ketamine queen.\par \par \uc2 ANNE MILGRAM, DEA ADMINISTRATOR: When clinic doctors refused to increase his dosage, he turned to unscrupulous doctors who saw Perry as a way to make quick money.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [15:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. An unimaginable tragedy, that's what the \uc2 Columbus Blue Jackets\uc2  are saying about the death of hockey player Johnny Gaudreau and his brother, Matt. Police say that they were killed by a suspected drunk driver while riding bikes last night in their home state of New Jersey. They were supposed to be, today, in their sister's wedding.\par \par \uc2 Johnny leaves behind a wife and two children under two years of age. He was 31 himself. His brother, Matt, just 29.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press reports the suspect, 43-year-old Sean Higgins, told police he had five or six beers before that crash, admitted drinking behind the wheel, and then failed, obviously, a sobriety test. He's facing charges now, including death by auto. He's going to be locked up until his next hearing, which is slated to take place next week.\par \par \uc2 Moments ago, one of the doctors charged in connection with Matthew Perry's overdose death, arriving at federal court out in L.A., he is expected to plead guilty there.\par \par \uc2 Chief Correspondent Jonathan Hunt joins us live from L.A. Hey, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHIEF CORRESPONDEONT: Hey, Gillian. That court appearance due to get underway in just about 30 minutes. According to prosecutors, Chavez, who, as you said, just arrived at the courthouse in downtown L.A., was one of a network of doctors and dealers who preyed on Matthew Perry's history of addiction, providing the actor with amounts of ketamine they knew were dangerous. Dr. Chavez is expected to plead guilty in that courtroom today, having reached a plea agreement with prosecutors earlier this month. He will be the third person to plead guilty following the death of the Friends star who was found face down in a hot tub at his home last October.\par \par \uc2 The medical examiner ruled ketamine was the primary cause of death. Perry had apparently been using ketamine in a legal but off label treatment for depression but he wanted more than his own doctor would give him and Dr. Mark Chavez was, according to prosecutors, one of those who helped supply him. Chavez was asked by another doctor, Salvador Plasencia, to help get the ketamine. Chavez has admitted he did so, getting it through submitting a fraudulent prescription and selling it to Plasencia for $4,500.\par \par \uc2 According to prosecutors, Placencia had texted Chavez saying, quote, I wonder how much this moron will pay.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Plasencia has pleaded not guilty, remains one of the main targets of the investigation, along with Jasveen Sangha, a dealer known as the ketamine queen. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Jonathan Hunt in L.A. for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump's making another play now to try and delay sentencing in his New York case where he's been convicted on 34 criminal counts. We've got former acting A.G. \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2  with us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [15:30:00]\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. Former President Trump's lawyers trying again to delay sentencing at his New York criminal trial. This time, they're suggesting the case be turned over to federal court. So far, no response from Manhattan DA \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The former President insists he did nothing wrong and plans to appeal the conviction for falsifying business records. The sentencing in that case is set for September 18, that is less than two months from Election Day just a couple weeks from now. Trump's lawyers write, "The ongoing proceedings will continue to cause direct and irreparable harm to President Trump, the leading candidate in the 2024 presidential election, and voters located far beyond Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Let's go ahead and bring in \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2 , former Acting Attorney General under President Trump. Matt, thanks for taking time with us today. What do you think about the latest play to try and get this sort of bumped up or remanded over to federal court? Is it a winning strategy you think?\par \par \uc2 MATT WHITAKER\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP ACTING ATTORNEY GENERAL: Yes. It's good to be with you, Gillian. This appeal to the federal court, I think is really important from a legal standpoint because, as you know, the Trump campaign and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  particularly doesn't feel like Judge Merchan has given them a fair shake.\par \par \uc2 And so, the hope is that by making this appeal to the Southern District New York, that they'll take the case and give fair hearing to, for example, the immunity question, which I think is probably the most compelling argument in this appeal.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Forgive me for not knowing this, but it strikes me as sort of unusual move. Do you see stuff like this happen with any regularity that a state court will bump it up to federal court for the sentencing portion only?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Yes. Gillian, typically removal to federal court is not unusual. You know, you see a lot in civil cases. Criminal cases is a little more unusual, but in this case, because the interests are federal and, you know, you're dealing with a federal candidate for federal office, then you would typically see a federal court with better jurisdiction and the appeal outlines, for example, the \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2  and their jurisdiction in this case.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Matt, do you think -- well, here's a point from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  real quick. They write, if the federal judge doesn't grant the request, they'll have further alienated Justice Merchan as he prepares to sentence their client. Trump faces up to four years in prison, though he could, as you well, know, you know, easily receive a much shorter sentence.\par \par \uc2 Are they kind of giving that potentiality up by doing this?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Yes, I don't think so. You know, I was in the courtroom with President Trump and I saw Judge Merchan and how he treated Trump. And so, I don't think that this will alienate him anymore. You know, they've tried to remove him three times from the case, and obviously they've tried to now remove this to federal court. I think this is the second attempt.\par \par \uc2 So this is not going to affect Judge Merchan. He's clearly made up his mind.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. Well, Matt, we got to leave it there. Thank you for taking time with us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: All right. Thanks, Gillian. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Meanwhile, another judge set a deadline of today to hear from former President Trump's lawyers and Special Counsel Jack Smith with their proposals for the next steps, plus an accompanying timeline for the election interference case. Justice correspondent David Spunt reports live from DC with the breaking details. Hey, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Gillian. Remember this was supposed to go to trial in DC in March but was delayed because of the Supreme Court ruling on immunity, that presidents are immune if it's involving an official act. Special Counsel Jack Smith and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's attorneys expected to submit a status report to Judge \uc2 Tanya Chutkan\uc2  within the next couple of hours. This will show how both sides plan to move forward.\par \par \uc2 Will Jack Smith, the man right there, the special counsel, try to take this to trial before the November 5th election? Sources close to both sides say it's possible but that window is almost closed. Earlier this week, Trump received a superseding indictment, essentially indicted again on the same four charges. But the new indictment focuses more on the former president in his unofficial role as a candidate. Think of Trump's tweets and speeches to supporters while he was a candidate.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, Gillian, Judge \uc2 Tanya Chutkan\uc2  will make a decision, both sides will be in court in person next Thursday. The former president not expected to be there but will get some sort of road map for where they plan to take this over the next few months. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. David Spunt live in DC for us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: When we come back the Aurora Colorado council member who says the alleged Venezuelan gang invasion of his Denver area apartment complex is not unique. It's not the only place where this is happening. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. Colorado's governor now responding to reports of a Venezuelan gang taking over apartment buildings in Aurora outside Denver. Police there have not confirmed that the men in the video we are showing you are gang members as of yet, but a spokesperson for Democratic Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  tells the New York Post, "According to police intelligence, this purported invasion is largely a feature of Danielle Jurinsky's imagination. If Jurinsky has knowledge of such activity, the governor's office is "ready to assist cops in taking them back."\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  there talking about \uc2 Aurora\uc2  Councilwoman Danielle Jurinsky who will join us in a moment to react, but first we go to senior national correspondent William La Jeunesse reporting live from LA. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Gillian number one, prosecutors disagree entirely with the governor. But there is a lot of finger pointing in Colorado, many saying the problem began when the Biden-Harris team opened the border to migrant apprehensions from Cuba, Haiti, Nicaragua and Venezuela, that jumping from 17,000 encounters in 2020 to 600,000 in 2022.\par \par \uc2 So the administration created a program providing migrants from those four countries humanitarian parole. But auditors found widespread fraud. Migrants lying in their application, sponsors using fraudulent identities, including dead people, traffickers demanding payment from those they sponsored.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ARTHUR, CENTER FOR IMMIGRATION STUDIES: They're literally just funneling people into the United States under this program with no expectation they're ever going to leave.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: And that's what sources say we are seeing now in Colorado, a mix of innocent migrants along with graduates of the Venezuelan prison gang Tren de Aragua extorting tenants for payment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE COFFMAN (R), AURORA, COLORADO MAYOR: There are several buildings actually under the same ownership, out of state ownership, that have fallen to these Venezuelan gangs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So DHS briefly suspended that program because of the fraud, but we started it this week, Gillian, saying they think they've worked out the kinks. It still means they're bringing in 30,000 migrants a month from Cuba, Haiti and Nicaragua, Venezuela. The fear is some have been infiltrated, if you will, by these gangs. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right. William La Jeunesse in LA for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's go ahead now and bring in Aurora, Colorado City Council Member Danielle Jurinsky, who found herself suddenly in the midst of all of this brouhaha. I want to ask you -- so first of all, you know, Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  said that this is all a figment of your imagination. We had John Kellner on earlier on our network today. He's the DA that represents Aurora, Colorado. Take a listen to what he said on "America's Newsroom."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KELLNER, COLORADO DA: I strongly disagree with the governor's comments about this. This isn't misinformation. Right now there's an active investigation to a massive shootout that occurred outside of one of these apartment complexes where men ended up dead. That's not misinformation, and these are real problems.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you, this is real, it's not imaginary, and it's certainly not misinformation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Danielle, do you feel vindicated at least partially by those comments?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE JURINSKY, AURORA, COLORADO CITY COUNCIL MEMBER: Yes. I mean, that's almost such a ridiculous comment that our governor made. It's almost too ridiculous to even respond to you that I've known since I found out that this was happening, since I found out that there are people still living like this, and went in for myself to move them out.\par \par \uc2 I know the truth. I've known the truth. \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  would as well if he would visit these apartment complexes. But you also had Cindy Romero on this channel earlier today, and she is one of the residents that I moved out. And in her interview, she said \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  wouldn't last five minutes in one of these complexes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes, I did hear her say that. What do you think, I mean, it's not your job to figure out the governor's world view, but why do you think he's so not even reluctant to address this but so eager to deny it in its entirety?\par \par \uc2 JURINSKY: Well, I think twofold. I think it's his way of taking a cheap shot at a local Republican. I also think politics are at play here. There is a very important election on the line in November, and I think politics are at play.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Some of your colleagues and other residents there have called out the Biden-Harris administration's immigration policies, and they are in particular citing the allowance for Venezuelan nationals coming into the United States essentially unvetted, without any intelligence or criminal law enforcement cooperation from the Venezuelan side. So, you end up with a whole lot of foreign nationals living in Colorado who the United States doesn't know anything about. What are your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 JURINSKY: Well, that's exactly what's going on. You know, there's not enough if any information being gathered at the border on these folks and how many of them are gotaways. We don't know -- we'll never know but this just absolutely shows the epic failure of the current policies in place at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 We How do you advise -- I mean, I know you've been involved in trying to help residents there, like you said, evacuate apartments. How are you telling them to sort of protect themselves against the possibility of this, or to keep their families safe and secure?\par \par \uc2 JURINSKY: Well, at this point, I haven't really offered any options of how they need to protect themselves. I've just been moving them out. They simply cannot stay. I don't know how exactly you protect yourself from something like this. This is a complete takeover of these complexes.\par \par \uc2 There is no staff, there's no property manager to call, there's no maintenance man to call. There's nobody to call, so --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Who would also be defenseless? I mean, against, you know, a dozen armed men.\par \par \uc2 JURINSKY: Right, that's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So yes, I guess it's hard to really do much in terms of preparation for an event like that. But, Danielle, thanks for laying it out for us. Thanks for bringing the issue to our attention on the network. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JURINSKY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Here's another live look as we await Trump in battleground Pennsylvania. The former president is launching a new effort there to counter VP Harris' big gains among black voters. We've got an exclusive with Janiyah Thomas. She's the director of Trump campaigns -- of the Trump campaign's Black Media arm. We've got her on board next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. Former President Trump's about to speak at a rally in battleground Pennsylvania, as his campaign there tries to win over more black women voters in particular. They put out this new video featuring women who say they support Trump not Vice President Harris, look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I am not with Kamala.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I am not with her because she is not with me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I support President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Let's go ahead and bring in Janiyah Thomas. She is the Black Media Director for the Trump campaign. Janiyah, thank you for taking time with us today. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JANIYAH THOMAS, TRUMP CAMPAIGN BLACK MEDIA DIRECTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You got a steep road it looks like ahead of you. Latest Wall Street Journal polling shows that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  nationwide right now has locked in 83 percent of black votes, support among black voters nationwide. That's obviously among men and women. It is certainly an uphill mountain you're going to have to climb.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Yes, for sure. I mean, President Trump is going to continue to go into deep blue cities and meet people where they are. You know, I think that black voters know that our message resonates with them because they know that President Trump is the strongest person on the issues that voters actually care about, like the economy, like crime, like immigration.\par \par \uc2 I think all these issues are very important to the black community, and President Trump is a stronger candidate on these issues.\par \par \uc2 Also I'll say that President Trump has done an excellent job of showing up in places where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has not been. For example, he went to the National Association of Black Journalist Conference. And he faced a, you know, a tough crowd. He answered the questions. But the most important thing is, he showed up in a place where he knew people were not going to be welcome to him.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, he did certainly face a tough crowd at the Black Journalist Conference, but he also didn't help himself out with his comments of questioning the vice president's ethnic and racial heritage. He said at the time, I didn't know she was black until a number of years ago when she happened to turn black, and now she wants to be known as black. He has gotten, Janiyah, a lot of pushback on that from black voters, white voters, Democrats, Republicans. Why did he go there?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: I'm going to say that President Trump didn't say anything that a lot of people in the black community have been saying for a long time. I think a lot of people have question her race --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: About the vice president?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Yes. Yes for sure. A lot of black people have been questioning her race before she was vice president. When she was running against Biden, she was not popular with black people then, and she's not that popular with black people now. I think that right now we're seeing a lot of hype, and a lot of, you know, a lot of joy and hope around them is like, what they like to call it, and I think that -- that, you know, fantasy world that they're living in right now isn't going to last for long.\par \par \uc2 I think we're still going to see an uptick in black voters voting for president Trump this year.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, again, it will have to be a very steep uptick, because the reality is that she is captured right now, 83 percent of public approval from black voters across the country.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Yes. I mean, I understand it still early. We still have plenty of work to do. You know, we have two-and-a-half months or so to get to November, and we're still going to continue to do the work that we've been doing, and showing up and speaking to the issues.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: What other sort of campaign strategies are on the agenda for you guys as you work towards November? Obviously, you know, aside from prioritizing black women voters, what other groups is the campaign looking at right now?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: I mean, also, of course, we want to gain more black men voters as well. But I think, you know, a bigger part of our strategy is getting our message out to the right communities. I think we can do better on that as Republicans in general, making sure we meet people where they are and are getting our message. Especially to Black Media audiences, which isn't always the easiest thing to do, so I think that's a huge step for us to keep doing that.\par \par \uc2 And especially going into non-traditional media spaces as well, like doing things like podcasts and working with influencers. I think that shows a lot of respect to the younger crowd. And I think that President Trump has a lot of momentum to move with the Gen Z audience.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And now they got you on their side as well tonight. Janiyah, thanks so much for coming on with us today. Great getting to know you a little bit interviewing for the first time.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You bet. We hope you'll come back. Take a look at this actor, Dennis Quaid, is getting candid now about the current presidential race. This is his new movie "Reagan" hits theaters today. He talked to Fox News Digital on the porch of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 's childhood home in Dixon, Illinois about what the former president would think of Vice President Harris' economic plan among other things. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DENNIS QUAID, ACTOR: I was very intimidating when I got offered it. I didn't say yes for a couple of months, because everybody knows Reagan, just like everybody in the world knows Muhammad Ali, in a way. And he was also hero of mine, my favorite president. And I didn't want to do it impersonation.\par \par \uc2 But I went to the Reagan Ranch, and that's where I really felt him. He was a very humble man, actually, there at the Reagan Ranch. And that's when I decided to take the role, not an easy role.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Not an east role to take on indeed. Quaid also talk to Martha right here on "The Story" back in June, that was a clip you were hearing with the incredibly, incredibly cute visual of his dog as well. Hope you guys will all go to Fox Nation, Fox Digital to check that out.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us. That is "The Story" of Friday, August 30th. As always, "The Story" will go on. I will see you on the five coming up at 5:00 PM. "Your World" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240831ek8u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Holds Rally in Pennsylvania; Interview With \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  General President Sean O'Brien; Tight Presidential Race; Busy Holiday Travel Weekend\b0\par\par\uc2 Bryan Llenas, Steve Bender, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 10,261 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: FOX on top of Americans taking to the roads and skies as the presidential nominees take to the trail, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  set to rally supporters in Pennsylvania at this hour, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gearing up for a Labor Day event in the same state, along with President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , come Monday.\par \par \uc2 First to the great exodus, as a record number of Americans say goodbye to summer with one last getaway. And it's a crowded one.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 How crowded is it?\par \par \uc2 Let's get to Jeff Flock. He's on the Pennsylvania Turnpike, where travelers are looking to escape, and we're just hoping that Jeff keeps his well eyes on the road -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Neil we're eastbound on the Pennsylvania Turnpike.\par \par \uc2 And maybe you see from a camera pointed out the window that, yes, it's moving. That's a good thing. Obviously, during the rush hour times, it's been tight, but we're moving right now. And, of course, there are more people on the road this year, more than a long time, a 9 percent increase, according to the AAA, in travelers, both air, land and sea.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, they're also being greeted by better prices for things like gasoline and airfare. Take a look at the numbers on gasoline. This time last year, we were at $3.81 a gallon. This year, you save about a half-a- dollar compared to that. Yes, not bad.\par \par \uc2 And where are we going? There's another question. Well, we're headed toward the Jersey Shore because I like the beach. But the most popular destinations, Seattle, Orlando, Anchorage, Alaska. Didn't see that one coming. New York City and Boston. That's where the AAA says people are going this year.\par \par \uc2 And the last thing I will leave you with is, well, if you didn't leave yet, the guidance is maybe wait until later. Worse travel times, 2:00 to 6:00 p.m. your time zone. It's clear sailing here, Neil. Good news for us -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. I'm wondering. You know, a lot of people are talking about roads packed. I mean, I guess it depends on the roadways and what have you.\par \par \uc2 Now, Seattle, I was told when you were mentioning popular destinations, Seattle is a gateway to go for Alaska cruises and the like. So maybe that is skewing these numbers. But, obviously, people are spending.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: They apparently have the money and saving a few dollars on gasoline, which isn't a bad thing either.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, Jeff, touche to you here, because you kept your eyes on the road. You were trying to ignore my questions, but you still gave us information-plus, which is what you're all about. And I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 Have a wonderful weekend. There he goes off into the blue yonder.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have got Steve Bender joining us right now, FOX Weather meteorologist, what he makes of all of this, because a lot of folks, especially those flying, could have some problems with weather, right, Steve?\par \par \uc2 STEVE BENDER, FOX WEATHER METEOROLOGIST: They could.\par \par \uc2 Jeff did a great job talking about the roads, right, Neil, 10:00 and 2:00, locked in.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Indeed. Indeed.\par \par \uc2 BENDER: I'm locked in on the forecast, right?\par \par \uc2 So we're looking here, 6,300 delays already today. You look at the worst locations. You got some big cities reflected, Las Vegas, New York, Chicago. That's going to be the concern. We have had severe weather all week long. So if you're trying to get out early, you probably got delayed. You got snarled in all of that mess of storms in the Midwest, leaking into the Northeast today.\par \par \uc2 Here's your FlightAware MiseryMap, slight dials of the red. JFK is one of those spots where they felt it, along with also DCA there in the nation's capital. Here's those lines of storms that kind of fight their way over the Appalachian Mountains. This is by Saturday afternoon into the evening time, then leaking into the tristate area.\par \par \uc2 Another thing that emerged today, Neil, is this, Disturbance No. 1, Northern Gulf. We knew it was an upper-level low. It was going to bring rain. Now it's looked at. Will it be organized enough? Why that's so crucial? We talk about those big hubs like Houston, out towards New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 If you're flying or driving, I-10, right through there, they could be getting easily a half-foot of rain in multiple locations through the holiday weekend. As we push into Sunday, it's the Southeast and up into the Northeast. Really, East Coast dominate with the storms.\par \par \uc2 Everything out West, you're West of the Mississippi, you're sitting pretty right now. On Monday, Labor Day, maybe you're doing return travel, lighting up the load there a little bit on the overall impact. But still, for Labor Day itself, Neil, we talked about this earlier in the week. Incredible. Montana had snow earlier. Now they could be the hottest portion of the country.\par \par \uc2 Who would have thought that when you enter into Labor Day?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Beautiful. Beautiful. Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 All right, thank you very much for all of that, Steve Bender. And he will keep us updated on this because the weather can change. We have discovered that, haven't we?\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I want to show you something from Newark International Airport. Obviously, a lot of fliers getting ready to hit the skies, but they're bumping into a lot of union workers who are hitting the picket line right now, mainly against \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , and this is part of a nationwide rally, series of rallies across the country.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2  is demanding more pay, better benefits, particularly for maintenance workers, aircraft maintenance workers.\par \par \uc2 With us now is Sean O'Brien, \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president.\par \par \uc2 Sean, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 What's the real problem, the dividing point for your members and United right now?\par \par \uc2 SEAN O'BRIEN, GENERAL PRESIDENT, TEAMSTERS: I think right now, it's like anything else. We are trying to demand what we're worth. United wants to be the leading airlines in the industry, and we want to make sure that they reward the people that have made them the success they are.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, our mechanics, we represent 10,000, and the most vital part of that operation, they're fighting for better health care, but more importantly, they're fighting for respect, outsourcing of their work, and many other issues. And so we want to make certain that everybody understands, the general public, how important these folks are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  put out a response when we called them about this, Sean.\par \par \uc2 And they say: "We're eager to reach an agreement with the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , which represents the United mechanics. We're actively talking with them as we work toward their next contract."\par \par \uc2 They seem, at least on the surface, amicable, or how would you define it?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, look, we want to get a -- we want to get the best contract for our members. And under the RLA process, similar to what our members in the railroad industry went through a couple of years ago, we want to get a deal where it's great for our members.\par \par \uc2 And the employer has the advantage here, because they can string us along. If they're truly sincere about what they want to do, they know what our demands are, they know what we need, and if they truly want to solve problems, the ball is in their court.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I have you, Sean. You have probably heard this dust-up. It wasn't a dust-up. It was a union worker at a Trump rally, and speaking on behalf of the president and saying he's doing a great job.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, this is what he said. And, of course, it flies in the face of comments you get from union chieftains. Anyway, just react to this, if you can, after you hear it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN PANNEBECKER, RETIRED AUTO WORKER: I'm doing a rally at a different auto plant every week. I started in June. We're going to go all the way to November.\par \par \uc2 And we're talking to the autoworkers as they come off their shift and go into work and 70 or 80 percent of them are voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because they know their jobs depend on it.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now as you know, Sean the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  is officially backing \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  right now. They had originally put their money on and their backing for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He's out of the race now. You know the rest of that.\par \par \uc2 I know it's a sticky time here and I don't want to belabor the point, no pun intended, but are you closer to a decision how to deal with all of that, whether the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  is going to make a backing at all this year?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, we're going to make a decision.\par \par \uc2 And the two decisions are, obviously, it's most important decisions we have to make. But the one thing that we're focused on is not what the leadership wants to do or thinks they should do. It's about the people we represent, the rank and file. That gentlemen, \uc2 UAW\uc2 , I don't know anything of what he's doing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: I don't know if there's been any polling by the leadership with the rank and file. But we are certainly polling. And we're going to release that data when we compile it. It's not just one poll. We're going to plant to plant.\par \par \uc2 We're pretty sophisticated in what we're doing. So I can't speak for any other union, but I will say this. There's a lot of talk out there about what direction rank-and-file members are going in general. And I think this would be a great opportunity for all the labor leaders in the country to put all their cards on the table.\par \par \uc2 And let's look at the polling data at some point say, what is wrong with this system? And why are our members feeling this way? Why are they feeling that they don't have any input in this? We're making certain the teams that union are going to be unique and different and include all of our members.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, you, of course, spoke to the Republican Convention. You wanted to do the same with the Democratic one and you didn't get that opportunity.\par \par \uc2 And I know you were drawn away from that convention the last day for the rail strike that looks like it hasn't been settled, but at least it's been contained. But that was maybe an excuse that was raised that you were never able to speak before them. Is that what they said?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, they haven't said much.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I went to do my job. And that's represent members that day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: They had several months to respond. And there's no doubt that certain people's noses were out of joint that I took a platform.\par \par \uc2 And I will take any platform to highlight how important the American worker is. If they're upset about it, they will get over it. I can't hide behind the fact that my members are the most important thing to me personally and professionally. And any time you get to highlight American workers and especially \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  and how valuable we are out of this country, I will do it at any and all venues.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sean, I am curious, though you mentioned it earlier there, that there might be a disconnect between union members the rank and file and leadership.\par \par \uc2 And maybe this one worker who was at this Trump rally the other day might have been a sign of that. I don't know where he stands. I don't know where actually the rank and file stand in that union. I do know that there are many in your union who are very fond of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , maybe a majority.\par \par \uc2 Do you have a sense of that?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Yes, I mean, I think the polling is suggesting that we know for a fact 50 percent of our members are registered Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is that right?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We know a lot of our members vote. Yes, we know a lot of our members vote based on a lot of social issues, whether it's guns or anything else.\par \par \uc2 And what we try and do is get the focus on who is going to be the best choice for workers. And, oftentimes, people, because of where they live and sometimes because of the great contracts they work under and the upper- middle-class lifestyle that we negotiate for them, they actually identify with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But our message has been clear is that we want to make sure we pick the best candidate for workers. The social issues are important. We talk about them all the time. But the most important thing is picking the people, and not just in the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: It's in every single election that are going to basically work for us.\par \par \uc2 And I tell people and I tell politicians all the time, I think you know, Neil, I call balls and strikes. We need to be the people we elect's conscience. We need to make certain they understand who they work for and how important we are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If the majority of your members, it turns out, do not want to support \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , would you then throw your support behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: We haven't got that data yet. It is very, very close so far.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: And that's a decision that we're going to consult with rank-and- file members and more importantly our general executive board.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would your being snubbed at the Democratic Convention weigh on you personally?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: I wasn't -- I mean, I don't take it personally.\par \par \uc2 Look, like I have states before in many opportunities to speak about this, it's their loss.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: I mean, if they don't think that they need the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 , it's not an attack on me. It's an insult to the 1.3 million members that they actually work for.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Any meetings scheduled with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: So we -- the Friday -- conveniently, the Friday before the Democratic Convention, they reached out to our union, our folks, and said they'd be willing to meet.\par \par \uc2 But as of today, we don't have a date.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: And so maybe they don't need us. Maybe they think they don't need us. Maybe they don't want to come talk to us.\par \par \uc2 But people have asked me the question over the last week, why don't you to just endorse \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? And I said, it's simple. I don't hire someone and then give them an interview. This is their interview to tell us what value they're going to bring to us, what support they're going to give to our issues.\par \par \uc2 And we need to hear it. Our rank-and-file members need to hear it. Our general executive board needs to hear it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Only \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been granted that interview, and he with you.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, that's not true, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , RFK, and every single candidate prior to the primaries.\par \par \uc2 So everybody accepted our interview.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But of the two big surviving ones right now, right?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Yes, so far, yes. You're right. Accurate.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came as well.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was in the race, he came as well, President Biden.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. Very interesting. Keep us posted, Sean. Have a safe weekend.\par \par \uc2 Sean O'Brien, the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  general president.\par \par \uc2 A couple other developments we're following here. This is the last trading day of August. Everyone is focused on, of course, Labor Day weekend. August is now in the books as being a pretty good month, considering its volatility a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 We were in the middle of a meltdown. But a key inflation gauge that is favored by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  looked pretty tame today. So, on the month, all the major averages up. In case you're keeping track, for the S&P 500, that's the fourth straight winning month, and for the Dow, the S&P and Nasdaq gains across the board.\par \par \uc2 Here comes September -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, the month of August still is another day to go. But for Wall Street, it's all wrapped up now, the final trading day of the year, a day of incredible gains, and, if you think about it, a month of incredible gains, considering where we were almost halfway through the month, when we had that great Monday meltdown.\par \par \uc2 Remember that? When we were sliding over 1,000 points on the dial, a lot of people were saying we were going into something very bad akin to, I think one analyst issued 1987. Others said this. We tried to step back and tried to give you the balanced picture on that, and that these type of events normally don't transpire into something like that. That was then, this is now.\par \par \uc2 What does Keith Fitz-Gerald think of the comeback in the markets and where we go for the next few months of the year? He is the Fitz-Gerald Group principal.\par \par \uc2 So, Keith, what did -- we had a stable inflation report today. One of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's favorite numbers came out more tame than thought. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 KEITH FITZ-GERALD, FITZ-GERALD GROUP: Well, I think that's good, and that's -- it doesn't necessarily match up to what people are feeling in their wallets, and that's the real challenge here.\par \par \uc2 But, statistically, from a trading standpoint, from an investing standpoint, this is absolutely what you want to see.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So where do you see things now? September is generally one of the more, I think, the most problematic month of the year. Typically, the S&P 500 has been -- that's on average falls about eight-tenths of a percent. I think that dates back to World War II.\par \par \uc2 You don't want to be spooked by that kind of stuff, but what do you think?\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: Well, you raise a very important point, right?\par \par \uc2 And the markets have changed considerably over the last 70, 80, 90 years. And so September may not be as bad as you expect because of the amount of money, Neil, that's chasing stocks like \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and many of the great names that are changing our world today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I wanted to speak to you because I didn't get the pleasure of chance on \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , which, of course, reported boffo earnings and revenues. Even guidance was pretty strong.\par \par \uc2 But this time, the stock was not treated well, today, a little bit better. The bottom line, since that report came out, it's about 4.5 percent lower. Now, it had been a lot lower. The immediate reaction was a 12 percent hit and a $200 billion hit to its market cap.\par \par \uc2 But what's interesting to me, Kevin, is that it was localized to \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 . And it did not really impact the other Magnificent Seven stocks that make up this widely acclaimed technology stock average, nor did it ding the Nasdaq when all was said and done.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that, and maybe a potential widening out here of investment alternatives?\par \par \uc2 FITZ-GERALD: Well, there's two things that work, right?\par \par \uc2 And it's important to forget -- to really talk about that, because most average investors don't understand what's being played here. This is Wall Street's gamesmanship at its finest. Now, we have a saying in our office, when in doubt, zoom out. The reason why all the shenanigans were confined to \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  is because it was the most anticipated early report in probably a decade.\par \par \uc2 But, number two, you had all kinds of leverage, options, expired -- all kinds of things playing there. So if you take a step back, you zoom out because you're in doubt, you look at the company. It reported a 100 percent year-over-year growth. You have got 80 percent profit margins, 70 percent profit ability, increase, increase, increase, increase.\par \par \uc2 So this is one of those instances where, as an individual investor, you can take a deep breath, you can chill out, because let Wall Street duke it out, you want to invest it out. And that's how we approach this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Kevin, thank you very much. Always good to have you on.\par \par \uc2 Kevin -- I'm sorry. I apologize. Keith Fitzgerald, thank you. It's been a long week, Keith. You know what I'm saying? It happens.\par \par \uc2 We have got Bob Cusack with us, The Hill editor in chief.\par \par \uc2 And, Bob, in a way, I do want to pick up with what I was raising with Keith there on the markets. We're waiting to hear from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at this big Pennsylvania event. Now, he, of course, has been saying the markets, when they were advancing were advancing because of the prospect of him returning to the Oval Office. And when we sunk that day, that fateful Monday, a couple of, three Mondays ago, it was on concerns that Biden and everyone else was destroying the markets and they were imploding and all that.\par \par \uc2 What do you think the markets mean to average voters? The lore is that they're not impacted by that because they say it's all rich guys playing that. We know for a fact that that's not the case. But how do you think this plays into the thinking of voters when they go to the polls?\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, EDITOR IN CHIEF, THE HILL: Well, Neil, I mean, I think a lot of voters think about their retirement. They think about their 401(k)s.\par \par \uc2 And, certainly, you're right. I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has made some predictions. Some have come true. Some have not. They said -- he said, if Biden were to be elected in 2020, the stock market would crash. So -- and, obviously, that didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 So it is one, though, the economy is in Trump's side, because a lot of people were more satisfied with the economy when he was president than Biden and Harris. So that's -- I think that's the rub here. And I think, honestly, both candidates would be wise to be talking about the economy nonstop in the month of September.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, it's interesting because now these battleground state polls have gotten pretty tight. And I do this thing every day where I look at who's ever leading in the polls. It could be just by a teeny bit or a 10th of a percent.\par \par \uc2 And then I start looking at those battlegrounds and in the seven that I watch -- and they represent 93 electoral votes, about a third, a little more than a third of what you need to be elected president. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has as a lead in two-thirds of those votes, two-thirds of those 93 electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 Now, you properly remind me, Bob, as you should, that's a snapshot in time. Once snapped, it's shot. Don't focus on it, Neil. But I only say that to say that represents a significant change. Is it a lasting one?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: That remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 But you're right. There's no doubt about it. I mean, you're talking about seven battleground states. A month ago, when Biden was struggling after that disastrous debate, there was more like 12, because Trump was playing in Virginia and New Hampshire and Minnesota. Those are all states that Democrats have to win.\par \par \uc2 And now we're talking about a state like North Carolina. That's a state that Trump has to win that's within the margin of error. So, right now, it's a toss-up race. But who has the momentum? Who has the steering wheel? I would say it's Harris.\par \par \uc2 And -- but Trump has an opportunity in September, especially with the debate upcoming, to take that steering wheel back.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, I don't know if you watched the Dana Bash interview with the Democratic ticket team, but she brought up an interesting question for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  regarding comments that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has made about her race. I want you to react to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN HOST: You have never interacted with him, met him face-to- face.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (D) AND U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BASH: That's going to change soon.\par \par \uc2 But what I want to ask you about is what he said last month. He suggested that you happened to turn black recently for political purposes, questioning a core part of your identity.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Any...\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Same old tired playbook.\par \par \uc2 Next question, please.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BASH: That's it?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: That's it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What did you think of that?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: It was a bit of an awkward moment. I thought there would be -- Dana would have a follow-up along the lines of, potentially, did that offend you or something along those lines?\par \par \uc2 But it's interesting. When Obama ran, he did not run as a black man. He said that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: He said told that to the Congressional Black Caucus.\par \par \uc2 So I thought it was it was an unusual moment. And I also thought there were some weird moments, other moments on fracking. She wasn't clear on why she changed her position or if she changed her position, which she did. Those are questions that are going to come back, obviously, hopefully during -- definitely during the debate, but hopefully in other media interviews.\par \par \uc2 And, honestly, she should do plenty, because people do deserve to know her policies and why they -- she picked those policies.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, it's interesting as well, Bob.\par \par \uc2 We talk a lot about the Republicans who are urging \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to get back to the issues, talk the issues, the issues are going your way, pound her on the border, pound her on the economy and inflation, and all of that. And he does.\par \par \uc2 But he oftentimes will mix it with personal attacks or I would even go so far as to say juvenile attacks. He was referring to her comments and the look of that interview when: "He said she was sitting behind that desk, this massive desk, and she didn't look like a leader."\par \par \uc2 And he was telling this town hall audience in Wisconsin: "I will be honest. I don't see her negotiating with President Xi of China. I don't see her with Kim Jong-un like we did with Kim Jong-un."\par \par \uc2 But, again, if we're talking about a substantive issue and getting back to the appearance of what it looked like at that table with her running mate and whether she looks up to the task, does it really matter whether you look physically, whatever you're surrounding, up to anything?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes. No, it's a good point, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I mean, listen, you can criticize Harris for her foreign policy stances or lack thereof as far as foreign policy experience. And, obviously, Trump has been on the job. He's been president. But -- so that's, I think, fair ground.\par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, these other side issues, there's a lot of independents out there that make the -- make their choices late, and they're going to make their choices this fall. And, honestly, Trump has to be concerned about the exhaustion, chaos criticism that \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  went after him.\par \par \uc2 They -- a lot of voters could be tired of that chaos, and that's something he's got to be concerned about and adjust to.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Does he recognize or feel he should recognize that saying that about a woman is going to alienate you among women? And, sure enough, polls come out to show that, overwhelmingly, women don't flip over him, but, overwhelmingly, men flip over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So it is a gender gap here, and it's a substantial one.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: It is.\par \par \uc2 Now Trump, to his credit, did very well in 2016, outperformed the polls, but he's got to be very careful, because Harris could be baiting him in the debate to say something outrageous.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: And if he takes that bait, it's going to be trouble.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you, my friend, very much.\par \par \uc2 Bob Cusack on that.\par \par \uc2 Waiting to hear from the president. We will be dipping into that. He is due to speak to this audience very soon in Pennsylvania. Very important state, 19 electoral votes up for grabs there.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  moments away from addressing yet another crowd, and a big one, in Pennsylvania, but not before stopping by our Bryan Llenas and saying some very interesting things to him.\par \par \uc2 Bryan and the former president -- coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the venue is Pennsylvania, in Johnstown, Pennsylvania, right now, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  expected to address that crowd. He has no trouble packing crowds in regardless of the venue, the venue size, whether it's indoors or outdoors.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania, depending on how -- what polls you want to track and follow, it shows it to be a fairly even race right now, but all within the margin of error. I believe six of the seven states are within the margin of error. And, again, as I was mentioning here with these battlegrounds, they represent 93 electoral votes, so more than a third of what you need to become president.\par \par \uc2 And judging by where we are right now, even including states where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  might lead by as little as hat's half-a-point, if you take that as a finished product, in other words, as a finished result, she would be grabbing a little bit more than 60 electoral votes of those 93.\par \par \uc2 Now, whether that means that she would capture those 270, it's anyone's guess. And, at this point, who would have thought back in 2016 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in the position he was when it appeared that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was leading in all those battleground states? We know how that materialized and the attention that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was affording those states.\par \par \uc2 So we're following that very, very closely here. Also following some other developments when he goes to various venues, but the presidential candidates themselves as they spread out more actively these days.\par \par \uc2 Danny Coulson is a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  deputy assistant director.\par \par \uc2 Danny, always good to have you, because I was mentioning this venue. It's an indoor venue. Most have been for both candidates since the assassination attempt, but not exclusively on the part of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who has preferred these larger venues, as long as everyone's safe.\par \par \uc2 I was curious to ask you, do you feel that we're ready for these candidates to be at outdoor venues, especially when we still don't know a lot about the assailant in Butler and what motivated him?\par \par \uc2 DANNY COULSON, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  AGENT: Well, Neil, frankly, I'm not comfortable with him being outside.\par \par \uc2 I have done his security before. I have done a lot of people's security. And you're much better inside, especially since we have all these questions about what happened at the first one. I do not understand the circumstances that occurred there, why they didn't post the roof of the building.\par \par \uc2 There's so many issues there that, until those are cured, until somebody comes in and analyzes it, I think