dn't you get a larger arena?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Somebody said, as we were riding by, oh, it's too bad the arena hasn't started filling up. I said, no, you don't understand. It's totally full, but the line goes back for miles. It's already full.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But we got to do that, fellows, you know? We got to do it.\par \par \uc2 Because I have been saying for a long time, I said, these people are coming in from prisons and jails. Nobody listened to me. They're murderers. They're drug dealers. If you take a look at Venezuela, as an example, their crime is down 72 percent, because they have taken their criminals from Caracas. They have taken their drug dealers.\par \par \uc2 They're emptying their prisons into our country. Their crime is down. All over the world, crime is down in the Congo, in Africa, 22 people deposited into our country. Where do you come from? The Congo? Where in the Congo? Jail. What did you do? We will not say that. You can imagine.\par \par \uc2 Listen, they come from Africa. They come from the Middle East, countries that are not friends of ours. And they're coming by the millions, by the millions. And these fools, Kamala -- these are Marxists.\par \par \uc2 These fools are letting them come into our country, destroy our country. And I said, did you ever see that -- I don't want to brag, but they do have a big-selling hat? You know what it says? Trump was right about everything, right?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Front-row Joes. Front-row Joes.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Right? No, but it's -- look, if I ran one of those countries I would be doing better than them at getting the people out.\par \par \uc2 You run a country, the country is inundated with really tough people that are criminals, I want to get them the hell out. If I was the head of a country, the president of a country, if I was in charge of anything having to do with a country, I would have moved them out even faster than they're doing.\par \par \uc2 They are moving people into our country at numbers that nobody's ever seen. And those countries are becoming safe countries. And wait until you see what's going to happen. They're just getting acclimented (sic). You know what that means, acclimented (sic)?\par \par \uc2 They're getting used to a society that really goes too far. The only thing they go after is guys like Trump, because he protests an election. That's the only thing they're good at going after. They don't go after criminals. They don't go after people that kill people. They don't go after drug dealers that kill on average 500 people during their lifetime.\par \par \uc2 So, a drug dealer kills 500 people. And the only thing they would understand would be the death penalty. If you had the death penalty for drug dealers, you wouldn't have any more drugs. You wouldn't have any more drugs.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Instead, we form blue-ribbon committees made up of our great first lady and lots of people like \uc2 Steve Witkoff\uc2 , great developer in New York.\par \par \uc2 But they don't do this for a living. The only thing they understand -- I went to China. I saw President Xi. Had a good relationship until COVID came in. Then I broke it off. It was -- that was too much.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But I said to him -- and I had a great relationship with him and I will again probably.\par \par \uc2 But I said do, you have a drug problem, 1.4 billion people. No, no, no, we have no drug problem. Why don't you have a drug problem? Because we have the death penalty, he said, for drug dealers.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : If they sell drugs, they die, he said.\par \par \uc2 So those people that sold drugs, they said, let's go to another country. This is a little bit tough. But they do in many other countries. In countries where you have that, you don't have a drug problem.\par \par \uc2 And we are becoming a drug-infested nation, whether you like it or not. We're becoming a drug-infested nation. So we better get our act together. Years ago, centuries ago, China was taken over in numerous times by smaller nations, much smaller nations, because they were all drugged out from the opium fields and all of that.\par \par \uc2 And then one of their very powerful leaders said no more. And they ended it. And we're going to have to end it too, because we're really a country that is a -- we're a failing nation in a lot of ways. What they have done to our country is incredible. What is Kamala -- who's a total lightweight.\par \par \uc2 Did you see her on television last night? This is going to be the president.\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This is going to be the president of our country? I don't think so, sitting propped up in a desk with this guy, this tampon Tim, tampon.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And that's the first interview she's done in like -- nobody's ever seen anything like it.\par \par \uc2 And if you're too weak to do a one-on-one interview with a person that was so soft -- I know Dana. She's always -- always nasty. She was so nice to the Democrats. It's much easier to be a Democrat. But we don't agree on their policies, because their policies will destroy our nation.\par \par \uc2 So we can't do that. We can't do that. But it was a very weak interview from the standpoint of CNN. I think CNN should be ashamed of themselves. If that were me, aren't the -- sir, I heard you want the death penalty for drug dealers. Why?\par \par \uc2 Well, I'd like to end the drug epidemic, if that's OK. Remember this. And I said it.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Every drug dealer, on average, kills 500.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240830ek8u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Kamala Tries To Cover Up Her Record; Kamala Admits Her Radical Values Haven't Changed; Aurora Apartment Residents Lived In Fear Of Gang; Middle-class erosion, how Bidenomics has crushed middle-income Americans; American unretire under Biden-Harris economy; Poll shows voters trust Trump most on the economy; Trump says IVF will be paid for in his second term; Trump leads Harris on top issue, the economy; Ohio State football players lead faith event\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Kayleigh McEnany, Garrett Tenney, Kayleigh McEnany,  Sean Duffy \par \uc2 7,655 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Whatever Happened To You series with an update on the efforts to restore the iconic Notre Dame Cathedral. We'll take you there. Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this Special Report. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. The Ingraham Angle with guest host, \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , starts right now.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Well, this is a Fox News alert. A lot of news to get to this evening. And I'm \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2  in for Laura Ingraham on the special edition of The Ingraham Angle. The terrifying consequences of Biden-Harris policies extend far beyond border states.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: And it's not just Kamala's border promises that no one is believing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE U.S., (D) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We fight for a future where we build what I call an opportunity economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, a man who had to unretire to make ends meet tells his story tonight. Plus, \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2  football players are here to share their extraordinary experience of faith.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I just encourage you to step out of in faith. Whatever you're going through in your life, where have you been through? We pray that you would see him. As he sees you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: But first, we are waiting for former President Trump to hit the stage at a town hall event in La Crosse, Wisconsin. Let's go live to the ground where Fox's Garrett Tenney is standing by. Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Kayleigh, officials here had to close the doors more than an hour and a half ago, because this arena was already at full capacity. Folks were lining up before sunrise to get in for this town hall tonight with former President Trump. His campaign has been spending a lot of time in Wisconsin, Michigan and Pennsylvania. States that he hopes will be a red wall for Republicans in November.\par \par \uc2 And they plan to do that by targeting blue collar workers in these Rust Belt states. The top issues for voters, by a huge margin is the economy, and that's what the campaign will be hammering on these next 10 weeks, asking folks if they are better off now than they were four years ago. One area, the former President is also trying to make headway in is with women voters, particularly suburban women on the issue of reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 And this afternoon in Michigan, Trump made a major announcement saying he will require insurance companies to cover IVF treatments and allow parents to take tax deductions for the major expenses involved with having a baby.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: As part of our efforts to help working families, I'm announcing today in a major statement that under the Trump administration, your government will pay for your insurance company will be mandated to pay for all costs associated with IVF treatment, fertilization for women. IVF treatment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: And we may hear more about that tonight at the town hall that will be moderated by former Democratic Congresswoman and Presidential Candidate, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , who endorsed President Trump earlier this week. Kayleigh.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: We'll just hop in there in La Crosse, Wisconsin. Keep us updated. Garrett. Thank you. All right. Well, on to this. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  set to give her first interview since being handed the Democratic presidential nomination, but not without her running mate, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . We are told, this is, quote, the most expansive explanation to-date on why she has changed some of her positions. But thus far, Kamala has given no explanation for her epiphanies, relying instead on anonymous aides to water down her radical views with reporters all done behind the scenes.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are big pressing, even confounding questions tonight for Harris, but will the liberal media ask them? We have some questions. We put them down. We want to pose them to you. First, on the border. The Harris campaign confirmed to Fox News, listen to this, that she has reversed several of her prior positions on immigration, including decriminalizing illegal crossings, closing ICE detention centers, and starting from scratch with ICE. But here is how she explained her flip flops in a clip released from her joint interview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think the most important and most significant aspect of my policy perspective and decisions is my values have not changed. My value around what we need to do to secure our border. That value has not changed. I spent two terms as the Attorney General of California prosecuting transnational criminal organizations, violations of American laws regarding the passage, illegal passage of guns, drugs, and human beings across our border. My values have not changed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Her values have not changed. She is insistent on that point. So, does she still believe this?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: This issue is about a vanity project for this President.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: The President's medieval vanity project is not going to stop them. Let me be very clear, I'm not going to vote for a wall under any circumstances.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: So, despite saying her values have not changed, Axios is reporting that Harris pledges to spend hundreds of millions of dollars on the wall along the southern border. So, did Dana Bash follow-up with Kamala, asking her to just explain her radical transformation? Did Dana Bash challenged her with her past statements like we just showed you, and did she ask her if it had anything to do with the polling that you're looking at, a majority of Americans now support a border wall. And on that same subject, why did Kamala say this?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We've got to critically reexamine ICE and its role and the way that it is being administered, and the work it is doing, and we need to probably think about starting from scratch.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Does she stand by these comments? And when Kamala is done struggling to answer those questions, she needs to be asked about the economy, it's the top issue to voters. Here she is. Look at this image. She's casting the tie-breaking vote for the so-called Inflation Reduction Act, which added nearly $740 billion to the national debt.\par \par \uc2 And then here she is, in this video, casting the tie-breaking vote that advanced the American Rescue Plan, which added nearly 2 trillion to the national debt. And here were the results, the 1.4 percent inflation rate that Trump left office with spiked to a 40-year high of 9.1 percent under Biden-Harris. This is Bidenomics. And now Harris supports widely panned socialist price control policies to fix the mess.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: A loaf of bread cost 50 percent more today than it did before the pandemic. Ground beef is up almost 50 percent. I will work to pass the first ever federal ban on price gaging (sic) [gouging] on food.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: But even the liberal \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  editorial board derided the idea. Quote, the times demand serious economic ideas. They wrote. Harris supplies gimmicks, price gouging is not causing inflation. So, what say you Vice President Harris, and would you take back those tie-breaking votes that caused inflation?\par \par \uc2 And then there's the most important question of them all. On August 15, 2021, Afghanistan fell to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  on the Biden-Harris watch. Just 11 days later, 13 heroic U.S. servicemembers lost their lives. But when the administration made the reckless decision to just pull the troops out, Kamala said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Biden always said that he wants you to be the last person in the room --\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- particularly for big decisions, he just made a really big decision, Afghanistan,\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Were you the last person in the room?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And you feel comfortable?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Now, that's what Kamala said publicly, but behind closed doors, we know that generals expressed their reservations with the Biden-Harris exit plan. Here's The Associated Press. Top former U.S. generals say that the failures of the Biden administration drove the chaotic fall of Kabul. Kamala, why didn't you listen to the generals? And if you won't listen to them, who will you listen to?\par \par \uc2 And tonight's interview, Kamala commits to appointing a Republican to her cabinet. But what is even more interesting is this Politico reporting. Politico reports, progressive activists are drafting a list to fill the National Security Council and the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2 . I mean who's on that list? Are we going to see Secretary of State, \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 , Secretary of Defense, Jamaal Bowman, who's on the list? Is China going to invade Taiwan? Will Iran get nuclear weapons on Harris' watch?\par \par \uc2 And this is before we get to all the questions for the interview partner, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . There are a lot, we need to know more about his misleading comments about his DUI and his military service, why he thinks tampons belong in the boys bathroom, and why he let Minneapolis burn during the 2020 George Floyd riots. All of those questions is a tall order for the liberal media. But will they ask any of them?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Speaker of the House Mike Johnson. Always a pleasure to have you, Speaker Johnson, and I want to start here with the border and the about face of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  tries to say, you know, I was for this bipartisan immigration deal that contained border wall funding, but Lankford, who negotiated that bill had this to say, this was in Axios.\par \par \uc2 Lankford said he was surprised with Harris' full embrace of the border bill this year. He told Axios that Harris wasn't involved in the months long negotiations. We never saw any Vice President staff here. She was a Johnny Come Never. The reason I bring this up to you is because you've been around President Biden. I don't know if you've met with Vice President Harris on this issue, but in your meetings with the White House, have you felt a keen readiness to go build Trump's wall like what they're saying now?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): Of course not. Absolutely not. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is running from her record and lying to the American people. The truth is, and we have the tape. Your opening monologue was a fantastic recitation of a lot of this. She is clearly on record a lifelong opponent to President Trump's ideas on the border. She called the wall un-American. She mocked it as a waste of money. And she has been the border czar. Let's not forget. She's been in charge of the open border. She helped engineer it.\par \par \uc2 So, to come forward now and try to pretend as though she somehow is now for border security is laughable. If she was Kayleigh, she should put H.R.2 in front of the Senate. She should tell \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  that he should bring that bill up for a vote immediately. We could secure the border, and they could use executive authority to do it, but that is not her agenda, and she's lying to the people right now.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, she could do that immediately. You know, we have another clip, I want to play for you. This is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in her interview this evening, and here's what she says about her cabinet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN ANCHOR: Will you appoint a Republican to your cabinet?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes, I would. I have spent my career inviting diversity of opinion. I think it's important to have people at the table when some of the most important decisions are being made that have different views, different experiences, and I think it would be to the benefit of the American public to have a member of my cabinet who was a Republican.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: So, she says she wants Republicans in her cabinet. She loves diversity of views. I mean, has Kamala brought you into the West Wing and asked about your conservative viewpoint, or any of your colleagues?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No, of course not. She's on the far opposite end of the spectrum. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has a record, a long record in public service. She was in the Senate. She has been Vice President. Everyone knows where she stands. She's a San Francisco radical, a big government socialist. She has been on record on the opposite side of every issue of where the American people are. You pointed out her position on the border, the economy, I mean things like banning fracking and banning gas-powered vehicles.\par \par \uc2 She was a co-sponsor of legislation to end trucks and cars as we know them. I mean, that's her values. That's who she is, and they're trying to run a campaign right now based on fantasy instead of the facts. So, our job is to make sure that the people know it, and the American media needs to do its job and ask tough questions. But of course, she's not submitting for any of those, Kayleigh, she's hiding. She's trying to run a campaign in a basement, like her mentor \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did. And I don't think it's going to work. I think that people see right through this,\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, except the teleprompter is now the basement it seems, you know. Speaker Johnson, the presidential race is obviously hugely important, but so too is the House. We just have a three member majority at the moment, and I know you've been traveling the country, raising money, doing everything you can to expand that majority.\par \par \uc2 Here's the RealClearPolitics polling average. It is neck and neck on the generic ballot. You see it here? Democrats 46.5, Republicans 45.8, I mean, essentially a tie. What are you seeing when you cross the country?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I'm seeing real energy on the ground, Kayleigh. I am very bullish about November. We're excited, and we believe we're going to win. And the reason is because we have extraordinary candidates that we recruited that are out there in the field running great races. I've been so far. I've done campaign events in 198 cities and 39 states as of today. We have many more to go.\par \par \uc2 And it doesn't matter whether we're in Montana down to Florida, from Maine to California. I've been in all the states, and it's the same thing. Kayleigh, people are so fed up about the cost of living and the rising crime rates and the wide open border and the catastrophe that's been. They're worried about instability on the world stage, and they realize that our adversaries, you know, Russia, China, Iran, North Korea, are looking at this moment as an opportunity.\par \par \uc2 We have to have strong principal leadership again. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will provide that in the White House, and we can win, and we will win, well, the House and the Senate. We're going to grow the House majority; I'm absolutely convinced of it.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: That -- yes, that's a big prediction. You know, we'll see what happens in November, and it's good to hear about that energy across the country. You mentioned cost of living. This is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' co- interviewer, also known as her running mate, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  on that very point.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN), VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: As Vice President, she cast the deciding vote on the American Rescue Plan, which helped keep workers on the job. Look, I'll keep saying it. Words are cheap. Actions is all you should care about. Make sure you're counting on what was delivered.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: So, this was yesterday. The American Rescue Plan, he's bragging about that tie-breaking vote, but it is a fact that there was 1.4 percent inflation, and one month after the American Rescue Plan, it shot up above 4 percent. Do you think bragging about that vote is something that's going to resonate with the voters you're talking to?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: It's not. They're absolutely tone deaf, and this is part of the fantasy campaign that they're running. We have to have record over rhetoric. And he's right. Words don't matter. Actions do. Look at what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has done. Look at what \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has done.\par \par \uc2 I was in Minneapolis over the weekend. On Saturday, we had a press conference out in front of the precinct that had burned, and he allowed Minneapolis to burn. These people have dangerous policies. They will dramatically harm the country. This is a serious election, and everybody has to get involved.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: No doubt. House Speaker Mike Johnson, thank you very much as always. Well, coming up, insane new Video shows an alleged migrant gang storming a Colorado apartment building. Look at that. Our next guest has a message for the city, just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: The border crisis is producing some wild and dangerous scenes across the country. First to San Diego County this week, where two days in a row, a group of migrants tried to board a school bus picking up young kids.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's just scary to that these kids are put into this situation. If those 20 people would have gotten onto the bus and tried to take over the bus like these kids and the bus driver could have been in real danger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: And it's not just border communities. Take a look at this terrifying video from just outside Denver. It shows a group of men, they're believed to be members of the gang Tren de Aragua, armed with handguns and a scope rifle, and they're just walking through an apartment complex. Now there's been allegations that these gangs have completely taken this building over, and while Fox hasn't confirmed that, the video does suggest a frightening example of gang activity, and the residents are terrified.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Danielle Jurinsky, Aurora City Council member. Danielle, this is a shocking video, and there are rumors that they have taken over several buildings. We haven't confirmed that here, but I want to know what you're hearing, and I specifically want to know why police or federal law enforcement, or someone cannot remove an alleged Venezuelan gang from a building.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE JURINSKY, CO-COUNCIL MEMBER, AURORA CITY: What was going on here Kayleigh is exactly what it looks like. This gang has taken over several complexes in \uc2 Aurora\uc2 . Our Mayor Mike Coffman was on a program earlier on Fox this afternoon, and he said definitively, three properties have been taken over by this gang. I do believe it's actually more than that, but this is what they're doing. They are controlling these complexes with guns. They go in, they beat up the staff. They hold guns to their heads. Staff flees.\par \par \uc2 And as far as local law enforcement, I believe at this point that this is far beyond any one local law enforcement agency taking this on, and we need our state and federal partners to step in at this point.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, I've read that these gangs are actually collecting rent. I mean, it's stunning what we're watching. But there's one resident, I couldn't believe this. This is how she has to live her life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Every day when we come home, we have to do this. Every time we go outside to take out the garbage, every time we try to go to bed at night, we have to keep it like this, so nobody can kick in the door.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Now, Danielle, you actually know this woman, right?\par \par \uc2 JURINSKY: I do. I assembled a crew in order for me to be able to get this video footage out yesterday. I had to make sure that this woman, her and her husband were moved out of this property safely. So, I went over there and physically helped move them yesterday out of there and into a safe place.\par \par \uc2 And then we were able to release this video footage. They have a lot more video footage, and I'm so thankful, Kayleigh, that you just showed that footage that you showed of Mrs. Romero locking all of her doors, because our own governor, \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , just within minutes ago, released a statement to The New York Post claiming that this is all, in fact, largely a feature of my imagination.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Oh, my goodness, a feature of your imagination. I can't even imagine this, you know, and I read that there is actually a state law that bars you from talking to federal law enforcement. It seems to me; you need to be able to coordinate with federal law enforcement here in order to stop this. Is the Governor stopping you?\par \par \uc2 JURINSKY: So, I have not heard from the Governor at all. I mean, it's interesting, Kayleigh, that the tone has really changed around town since this video footage was released, because up until yesterday, you were hard pressed to get anybody, most other elected officials, police chiefs, you were hard pressed to get anybody to admit that this was going on.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JURINSKY: Our police officer in the Aurora Police Department are talking. Our Aurora police officers, you know, want to go in. They want to police. They want to do the work. But they are. They're held back by state laws. There's actually a house bill in Colorado that precludes local law enforcement from working with ICE.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Wow. It's amazing. It's amazing. Stay safe. We will stay on it. We will keep covering the story. Danielle, thank you. Well, coming up, retirement is not all it's cracked up to be in a Biden-Harris economy. People are now having to un-retire just to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Prices are still too high. When I am elected president, I will make it a top priority to bring down costs.\par \par \uc2 As president, I will be laser focused on creating opportunities for the middle class.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Laser focused. Well, why hasn't she been laser focused the last three and a half years? Americans are struggling. People are actually postponing retirement because of the cost of living. Take a look at this new study from Live Career. It found that about half of middle-income Americans say they expect to work past age 65, and roughly 8 in 10 of those site financial pressures. And for those who have already retired, some of them are getting back into the workforce just to make ends meet. Like my next guest.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Greg Piazza, a retired police officer who now works as a full-time groundskeeper. Greg, you retired, you wanted to work here and there, you said you wanted some beer money, but now you have to have money for all kinds of groceries. And your wife, too, a retired teacher, had to un-retire, is that right?\par \par \uc2 GREG PIAZZA, UNRETIRED FULL-TIME GROUNDSKEEPER: Yes, thanks for having me. Sure, that is correct. I initially intended to work 20 to 30 hours a week. Like you said, for some beer money, some extra money in the pocket, but now it's a necessity to pay for everything that has gone upside down over the last three and a half, four years.\par \par \uc2 And my wife, she is a retired teacher. She's tutoring a couple nights a week to help supplement with everything. She's also babysitting for our three grandchildren, soon to be four, or four, it's going to be four grandchildren now, which saves our son and daughter-in-law hundreds of dollars, if not thousands a month, in child care.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a upside down -\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Are they struggling, too? Are those -- are they struggling as well?\par \par \uc2 PIAZZA: I wouldn't say struggling. Well, no, I wouldn't say struggling in the sense that they both have full-time jobs. But, you know, like anything, this economy is destroying our nation, the policies of this current administration is absolutely destroying our nation.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yeah, they are. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , meanwhile, had a message about retirement. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We fight for a future where every senior can retire with dignity. We know that while fighting for a brighter future may be hard work, hard work is good work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Do you feel, Greg, that you were able to retire with dignity in the Biden-Harris economy?\par \par \uc2 PIAZZA: Not under their economy. What I can tell you is this, I've been working, I'm going to be 59 tomorrow, by the way. I've been working since I was 16 years old.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Happy, almost, birthday.\par \par \uc2 PIAZZA: Oh, thank you. I've been working since I've been 16 years old and I don't mind working. And trust me, I want to say this. I'm not looking for sympathy. My family is not looking for sympathy. I'm not looking for a handout. What these people want to do, when I say these people, I mean, Biden and Harris and the liberals and the socialists, they want us peasants to be dependent upon government.\par \par \uc2 I don't want their handouts. I don't want their sympathy or empathy. I want them to get the hell out of the way and let the private sector take over like it was under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and let the capitalists, economy work the way it should.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to be a socialist. I did not grow up in a socialist country and I don't want to die in a socialist communist country. So, get out of the way, get out of office and let a man who knows what he's doing take over.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well said. Greg, happy, almost, birthday tomorrow. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 But when we hear stories like that from Americans, it is no surprise that the economy is still a top priority for voters. And they trust Trump to handle it more than Kamala. Brand new Fox News poll of the Sun Belt, Arizona, Georgia, Nevada, and North Carolina show Trump with a massive eight-point edge over \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Doug Schoen, pollster and former advisor to President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , and Sean Duffy, co-host of the Bottom Line on Fox Business. Sean, there was some news that's been breaking and it's on the issue of abortion this evening. And we're going to get to Trump and some of the comments that he made on the trail.\par \par \uc2 But I want to first play Sophia Bush from One Tree Hill, for all my millennials out there. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You can't say you're a pro-family party and then work to ban IVF. None of this makes any sense. It's about money, control, and power. And if you can't win on policy, you certainly shouldn't be trying to win on killing half the American population.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Sean, she is a surrogate for the Democrat. So, Republicans want to kill half the population and take away IVF, which by the way, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  literally just said he once paid for IVF this evening.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, CO-HOST, THE BOTTOM-LINE: Guaranteed coverage for IVF is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said. And again, to run on policy, we haven't heard any policy from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . What is she talking about? And by the way, she's talking about a Democrat Party that had vasectomies free at the DNC and abortions at the DNC. This is the most anti-family party that's ever existed, and she's trying to pretend like the Democrat Party sticks up for families.\par \par \uc2 You know, again, that's why these actors' surrogates are just absolute jokes and don't move anybody from one side to the other.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Doug, you know, my phone lit up and there was a new tweet from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . I want to pull it up. It's really shocking, this new tweet that she has here. So, we're going to put it on the screen. It is about the issue of life. Let's pull it up. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins, he will not only sign an abortion ban, he also intends to create a national anti-abortion coordinator forcing states to report on women's miscarriages and abortions. When exactly did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  say that?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, DEMOCRATIC POLLSTER AND STRATEGIST: Well, he never did. And he's made it clear, I think a number of times, that he favors the Dobbs decision giving states the power to legislate, as the court said. I think, though, the larger point that you've raised, Kayleigh, is that he has a big lead on the economy in the swing states, but the race in those states is effectively deadlocked.\par \par \uc2 I make the argument, he needs to move to the economy is the central theme of this campaign, along with the southern border, and point out what his positions are and what her flip-flops are. He's neutralized, I think, to the best he can the abortion issue, but the economy and the southern border are the issues that can elect him, and arguably will elect him if he runs a discipline campaign focusing on those two things.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Do you think, Doug, the economy is enough to get him over the edge? It is number one in every single poll, and way below that is abortion, somewhere in the teens, 14 percent.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: It's undeniable for suburban women, abortion is important. But as we have heard and seen in poll after poll, interview after interview, people are having a tough time making ends meet. They want tough policies at the border. They also want, as your earlier visitor showed, they want tough policies on law in order to take the lead with illegal immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Those three issues I believe can elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The question is, can he run a discipline campaign on those issues because Harris is running, I think, a very good campaign really obfuscating the real positions she has taken, and what the Democratic Party has traditionally stood for.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Sean, and the key to winning this election, I believe, is forcing \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  off script. So how does President Trump do that? You have to force her off script. September 10th is going to be a big moment to do just that on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: No, it is. But to Doug's point, you want to -- all candidates want to focus on the issues that they win on, and that's why \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  focuses on abortion. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can't take the bait. He has to come back to the issues that, to your point, can truly matter to the American people, and point out that you can never tax and regulate and redistribute your way to prosperity. It's never worked. It brings poverty to the people in which you try this with.\par \par \uc2 And again, I wish that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should call Kamala out and say, listen, if you disagree with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's policies, his economy is so bad, what do you disagree with? What would you change? And if you could change it, change it right now. Call \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  out if you disagree with him.\par \par \uc2 But she won't because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's policies are Kamala's policies, which means you're going to get more of the same. And to Doug's point, the border truly matters in these Southern states, even in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And it's important to go, listen, I'll secure your border. I'll bring safety and security back to your communities. Kamala won't fix on the issues that you win on, and that's what Trump has to do. And to Doug's point, if he does, I think he wins.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Good advice. Doug and Sean, thank you. Well, we are still waiting for Trump to come out on stage at any moment. We will bring you any news from that. But coming up, what he needs to do to win back the Keystone State. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Trump, today, continuing to campaign in battleground states, starting the day with events in Michigan, and ending in Wisconsin where we expect him to take the stage at any moment. We will take you there when he arrives. And tomorrow he's heading to Pennsylvania where polling guru Nate Silver says Kamala has a big problem. Writing this on X, it's been a while since we've even seen a poll showing Harris lead in Pennsylvania. A high- quality poll showing Harris ahead there would make a big difference right now in the forecast of who would win the election.\par \par \uc2 Remember, this is the state that pushed Trump over the threshold in 2016, and that he lost by just 1.2 percent in 2020. Joining me now is \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , the Republican candidate for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in Pennsylvania. Dave, fantastic to have you here.\par \par \uc2 I was looking at Politico playbook who laid out like 10 different paths to the White House, seven of which for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  included Pennsylvania. So, how do you win the great state of Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 DAVE MCCORMICK (R-PA) U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, listen, I think -- and we're blessed in Pennsylvania by the fact that people on the ground are looking for strong leadership and commonsense solutions. And right now, we have \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  running on a radical liberal agenda. In her own words, she wants to ban fracking, she wants to legalize illegal immigration, give amnesty, she wants to make sure they have federal benefits, and most recently she wants to spend $2 trillion, more money, and have that as well like price controls.\par \par \uc2 These positions are wildly out of step with the people of Pennsylvania. And so, this week liberal leadership is not going to fly, and I think if we can reveal to the voters of Pennsylvania that's what they get with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Bob Casey, that kind of weakness, that kind of radical liberal agenda, the answer will be clear on Election Day. And I think President Trump will win, I think I'll win.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yeah, Bob Casey, of course, your opponent there. I want to play a campaign ad. You put this out, I mean, like that. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  became the nominee, and you had this out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is inspiring and very capable.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You're considered the most liberal United States senator.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: There's no question, I'm in favor of banning fracking.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Would you ban offshore drilling?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Now that was your opponent Bob Casey there at the top. I mean, did you know Kamala was coming because it was like, she's the nominee and you put this out and they called it the model for how to attack \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, you know, it looked like he was going to endorse her, and it was clear right away that this is a San Francisco liberal agenda that's just out of touch with Pennsylvanians. We don't need to tell people that, we just need to let her say it in her own words.\par \par \uc2 And Bob Casey has been around for 18 years. He has not had the strength to resist the radicalization of the Democratic Party. And so, Bob Casey is a sure vote for whatever \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Walz come up with. And so, this is really an election that's going to determine the future of our country, and so we got to make sure the people in Pennsylvania understand the stakes and understand that they if they pick Harris, Casey, they're going to get an extreme agenda that's truly out of touch with Pennsylvania values.\par \par \uc2 And the key here is, we don't have to convince people, we just have to let them hear her beliefs, what she believes in, that's who she is. That's who she's been her whole life. People of Pennsylvania aren't going to buy that.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , the man to watch in Pennsylvania, thank you. Well, we're still waiting for the former president to come out on that stage in Wisconsin. We'll bring it to you when he comes.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2  football players leading a Christian revival on campus. They're here to talk about it, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: These Ohio State football players just did something on campus that I have never seen before. Each of the players went up to share their stories of how their relationship with Jesus has changed their lives and how much God loves each and every person in the crowd.\par \par \uc2 Late into the night, with everyone taking notice, person after person made the decision to get baptized and lives are going to be changed forever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, there is nothing more American than faith and football. And over the weekend, \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2  had both. What an amazing night. That event was put on by the players. It featured four tubs filled with water where an estimated 60 students were baptized. How beautiful.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now are three people who were there. Kamryn Babb, former Ohio State wide receiver, and Master Teague III, former OSU running back. They organized the entire event. And also joining me, Gee Scott Jr., tight end for the Buckeyes. He even performed some of the baptisms.\par \par \uc2 Master, this is incredible and I want to know what inspired this idea. Was it the Holy Spirit? What brought this about?\par \par \uc2 MASTER TEAGUE III, FORMER OHIO STATE RUNNING BACK: Of course, the Holy Spirit, he's our guide. Christ is our Savior and our Lord. And we just wanted to present this great gift that we've been given to everybody else. Listen, Kayleigh, I know you know that in this world, we have a serious issue, and it's a disease. It's a disease. And unlike other diseases that spread, and maybe not everybody gets a disease, this disease, every single person has and it's sin.\par \par \uc2 And it's sin brings about death. And there's but one cure and that cure is Jesus Christ. And so, we desire to share that cure with everybody and give them that hope that they can have.\par \par \uc2 Everybody's looking for peace. Everybody's looking for joy. Everybody's looking for meaning and purpose. Everybody wants to be held and loved and saved, but they're looking in the wrong places. Jesus is that answer and we desire to share that.\par \par \uc2 God has been moving tremendously on this team, on the football team. And God has given this platform to share it. And so, we want to share it. What better way to do that. People elevate football to the highest level. It's like a God to them. These players are like God to them. And so, when you see a player that is deflecting the glory and giving Jesus the praise and pointing to him, that's tremendous, the impact that it has.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yeah, and you're pointing out -- that is so beautifully said. You know, Kam, you did some of these baptisms, and I want to play a little clip. This is you baptizing one of the students.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Kam, what was that moment like for you?\par \par \uc2 KAMRYN BABB, FORMER OHIO STATE WIDE RECEIVER: Well, it was amazing. Just representing really what's going on the inside of his heart and just really being poured out on the outside. Going from death to life with Jesus Christ. And so, it's really just a representation of what Jesus is doing. And in his word, he says that he would draw all men and women and all people to himself if we would lift his name high. And so, our goal was just to lift his name high and give him all the glory.\par \par \uc2 And just tell people about this, like Master said, about this glorious God in Jesus Christ who saved our souls. And so many people, to Master's point, are looking in all these different areas and directions, but there's only one name under heaven and earth which men can be saved, and that's this God that we've got to know, and His name is Jesus Christ.\par \par \uc2 And so, that young man, as well as many others in ourselves, my great teammates, and so many people that were involved, we were truly just honored just to see the heart of God that pursues the ungodly, and not just pursues the ungodly, but has the power to save and to change us, making us holy as he is holy.\par \par \uc2 So, the goal is to be with him forever, for eternity, and it's only by his blood that we have access to the Father and to him, so, and to the Holy Spirit. So, we're just honored to be there and to be a witness, but this was truly unto God, this was for him, but he's the one that directed this. He was the starter of it and he's the finisher of it, and we're just glad to bring him the glory, so.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Amen. And Gee, you're currently on the team at \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2 . You said something that caught the eye of myself and my producers as well. People worship football players, you said. And when they hosted this event, we knew people would come, but they don't understand who the players' worship, and that's God.\par \par \uc2 So, people are pointing at you, Gee, but you're pointing up.\par \par \uc2 GEE SCOTT JR., OHIO STATE TIGHT END: Yeah. I mean, I think that's the goal of all of our hearts is to deflect that glory. And we understand that there's many people that may come out with the interest to be around an Ohio State football player, to hear the message that we might share as football players. But ultimately, we have the mindset that you might have came to hear something about football, but we want to leave you with the gospel.\par \par \uc2 Because, ultimately, this football game, it will come to an end. The career that you may have, it will come to an end. All of the things that we have passion about, these worldly passions about, it will come to an end at some point. And we believe that you'll be just at some point left with your faith.\par \par \uc2 And that's what we put our foundation in. It's our firm foundation and we stand firmly in it. And it was just an amazing event top to bottom just to see the fruit of it, the amount of people that came, and more importantly than the multitude of people that came but the hearts that were truly affected by it.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Gee, I only have about 15 seconds, but were students very receptive to this? It seemed they were.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT JR.: Absolutely. God was very evidently speaking directly to the hearts of many people. I'm talking people bawling, crying in front of the baptisms. It was just so powerful.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Wow. I love this. Gee, Kamryn, Master, you've inspired me. Thank you so much, and good luck on the game this weekend. We will be watching and rooting for OSU.\par \par \uc2 Well, that's it for tonight. I'm Kaylee McEnany in for Laura Ingraham. Thanks for watching the special edition of the Ingraham -\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240830ek8t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Administration Officials Meeting with Chinese Officials to Reduce Tensions in South China Sea; \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  Admits \uc2 Facebook\uc2  Suppressed "New York Post" Story about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's Laptop During 2020 Election Season; Democrat Presidential and Vice Presidential Candidates \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  Give Interview to CNN; Polling Shows Close Senate Races in Stats that Would Flip Control of Senate to Republicans; Harris And Walz Wrap Up Georgia Swing; Harris Gives First Sit-Down Interview As Nominee; Campaigns Spar Over Debate Audio Rules; Trump Makes Appeal To Rust Belt Voters; \uc2 FBI\uc2  Promised Policy Changes After Nassar Case; Hostage Families Attempt To Enter Gaza\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Howard Kurtz, Mark Meredith, Aishah  Hasnie, David Spunt, Alicia Acuna, Jacqui Heinrich, Jeff Paul \par \uc2 7,631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh. OK. That's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: All right, Judge. The mystery of the bread on the plane.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, to investigate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 A shocking watchdog audit brings to light recurring failures within the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to protect child sex abuse victims, years after promise policy changes. The migrant crisis reached far beyond the southern border as the threat of violence from a Venezuelan gang forces Colorado residents to pack up. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden's last stand to stand up to China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think the most important and most significant aspect of my policy perspective and decisions is my values have not changed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in an interview. Her first interview since becoming the Democratic nominee 40 days ago. That's an excerpt that came out from CNN.\par \par \uc2 She says her values have not changed, but many of her Republican critics say her policies sure have, sometimes 180 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris and running mate, Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  are now in southern Georgia, finishing up their bus tour as part of a wider emphasis on sunbelt states. That comes as the Harris and Trump teams work to find common ground on debate rules for September 10th.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith is in Savannah, traveling with the Harris-Walz ticket. Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. Tonight, the vice president is wrapping up a two-day swing through this battleground state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 You can see this area is already cleaned out in this arena because the rally is over, but it's what Harris did before the big event that's making news, sitting down for her first big interview since becoming the Democratic nominee, and it's an interview that could once again shake up this race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: My values have not changed.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): For the first time ever, Vice President Harris and Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  faced in depth questions about how they govern, if elected.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And we should meet certain standards for reduction of greenhouse gas emissions, as an example, that value has not changed.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): The CNN interview taped this afternoon comes amid heavy criticism, Harris has ducked the press for five weeks, all while raking in record cash.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump, among those watching tonight. He posted the interview will "expose her as being totally inept and ill-suited for the job of president." Tonight, both the Trump and Harris campaigns are fighting over how next month's debate will be conducted.\par \par \uc2 The Harris team says it's still in discussions with ABC as it wants both candidates' mics on at all times. Unlike the June debate between Trump and President Biden.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We're here to speak truth, and one of the things that we know this is going to be a tight race until the very end. OK? So, let's not pay too much attention to the polls, because we are running as the underdog.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): As Harris courts South Georgia's rural voters, a Quinnipiac poll out today, shows Harris trailing Trump on key issues, including immigration, the economy, and Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Georgia Democrats say people will be swayed as they learn more about Harris.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I do think the numbers will rise.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm very excited. The last rally I went to was for \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in '92. So, I'm very excited.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Democrats also insist they can keep their momentum alive, even as reports emerge of friction at Harris campaign headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Axios writing today, Harris' team has been wary of making the Biden people feel set aside. But that has led to some internal confusion about who is in charge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): We'll be looking for any of that friction next week as President Biden prepares to hit the campaign trail for his vice president. That will be happening at a dual rally in Pennsylvania on Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, we're also learning more about what kind of administration Harris would like to see if she were sent to the Oval Office. She says she would appoint a Republican to serve in her Cabinet. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, CNN: You had a lot of Republican speakers at the convention.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Will you appoint a Republican to your Cabinet?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes, I would. Yes, I would.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BASH: Anyone in mind?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: No. No one in particular mind. I got a - we have 68 days to go with this election. So, I'm not putting the cart before the horse, but I would. I think -- I think it's really important. I have spent my career inviting diversity of opinion. I think it's important to have people at the table when some of the most important decisions are being made that have different views, different experiences.\par \par \uc2 And I think it would be to the benefit of the American public to have a member of my Cabinet who was a Republican.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Harris, of course, is still struggling with some progressives. Even at this event tonight, Bret, she was interrupted twice by pro- Palestinian protesters, indicating they are not happy with her Middle East stance. So, there may still be some issues there for her. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. More on this with the panel. Mark Meredith in Savannah. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 As the Democratic ticket targets the Sun Belt, the Republican nominee is canvassing in the Rust Belt with the V.P. nominee, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will participate in a Wisconsin town hall tonight after delivering economic remarks in Potterville, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Aishah Hasnie is in Potterville with the latest. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. In a pretty direct appeal to women and growing families, the former president made a very big announcement here in Michigan today, saying that he is going to half the government, if he wins the White House.\par \par \uc2 Half the government pay for or force health insurance companies to pay for IVF treatments, connecting the reproductive rights issue to the economy, saying that for far too long, too many American families have been hurt very badly by inflation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Congratulations on 76 years of manufacturing in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Former President Trump back in Michigan for the eighth time this year, with Democrats deploying second gentleman, Doug Emhoff, to counterprogram in the battleground state.\par \par \uc2 Trump and his running mate barnstorming the Rust Belt this week, focusing on the economy and pitching themselves as the most pro-worker Republican ticket in history.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : With this administration, they won't even know what's happening. They will take every single job. You're not going to have any auto workers within two years, maybe three years, nobody is going to be making cars here.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): That message, however, getting mixed reviews at a pit stop in Boston, where Senator J.D. Vance got booed at the International Association of Firefighters Convention.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), VICE-PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I want to talk about why we're fighting for working people, why we're going to fight for unions.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Boo!\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  received a standing ovation at the same event on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Trump-Vance ticket is performing better on the economy, though, in brand new Fox News polling, in down ballot races in Arizona, Nevada, and North Carolina, the Democratic candidate leads by double digits, overperforming Harris by at least five points, while the Republican candidate trails Trump by seven or more.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): They are still introducing themselves to voters. It's totally normal to see them a little bit behind in the polls.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): In battleground Michigan, Republican \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2 , who faces Congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , thinks the Democrats momentum won't last, that Trump can give him a bump.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: What do you need from the Trump campaign?\par \par \uc2 MIKE ROGERS\uc2  (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, MICHIGAN: Run hard, focus on the economy. And did I mention the economy?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And the former president during this rally, took a few moments to also slam Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for doing this joint interview as her debut one-on-one sit-down interview. Of course, doing that with her running mate, saying that he does solo interviews all the time. Did one backstage right before he came out today, and adding this, if you can't do an interview, we got the wrong person. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today. The Dow gained 244, to finish a new record close at a new record close again. The S&P 500 dropped a fraction. The NASDAQ lost 40.\par \par \uc2 A scathing report from the justice department's internal watchdog says the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is still failing to protect children who have been sexually abused.\par \par \uc2 These findings come years after it promised policy overhauls over the scandal involving the bureau and U.S. gymnastics Dr Larry Nassar. Correspondent David Spunt has details from Washington.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A new report reveals little has changed at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , three years after the justice department's internal watchdog slammed the agency over its failure to properly investigate and report child sexual abuse cases.\par \par \uc2 In nearly half of the 327 cases reviewed, mandatory reporting requirements to local law enforcement of alleged abuse were not followed. 42 cases were considered so disturbing, the Inspector General's office contacted the \uc2 FBI\uc2  directly to urge immediate action.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say in one case, a 2-year-old was sexually abused, and agents added only one document to the case file in 21 months.\par \par \uc2 In another case, the report says the bureau sat on information so long an offender was able to sexually abuse another victim, following a damning 2021 I.G. report over the \uc2 FBI\uc2  probe into convicted sexual abuser, and former U.S. gymnastic team Doctor Larry Nassar, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  promised swift action.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, FEDERAL BUREAU OF INVESTIGATION: I'm especially sorry that there were people at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  who had their own chance to stop this monster back in 2015 and failed, and that is inexcusable. It never should have happened, and we're doing everything in our power to make sure it never happens again.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): In April of this year, the DOJ announced a nearly $139 million settlement with more than 100 people who accused the \uc2 FBI\uc2  of grossly mishandling the allegations against Nassar.\par \par \uc2 Critics say the Bureau has not learned from prior mistakes. Nassar victim Attorney John Manly told Fox News in a statement, "The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is simply not doing its job when it comes to protecting our children from the monsters among us who stalk them.\par \par \uc2 Senate Judiciary Chairman Dick Durbin is promising a congressional hearing this fall. Writing, "It's shameful that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is continuing to fail victims."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): This afternoon, on a conference call with reporters, a senior \uc2 FBI\uc2  official said new procedures have been put in place to protect children. The official said, "This is a no fail mission with the children at stake. Any mistake or deficiency is unacceptable." Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Residents of Denver area -- the Denver area, are on high alert tonight, as officials raised the alarm there that members of Venezuela's most notorious gangs are likely operating inside Colorado.\par \par \uc2 The fear of violence is forcing people in certain areas to pack up and move out. Senior correspondent Alicia Acuna is in Denver with the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Two disturbing doorbell videos from an apartment building in Aurora, Colorado. One shows a group of armed men in a stairwell and hall approaching a doorway. In another, two men forced their way into the unit.\par \par \uc2 MIKE COFFMAN, MAYOR OF AURORA, COLORADO: There are several buildings. I see under the same ownership, out of state ownership, that have fallen to these Venezuelan gangs.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (voice over): Mayor Mike Coffman says the gangs have pushed out management at three apartment complexes and are likely extorting residents. He could not confirm reports the gangs are part of the federally designated transnational criminal organization Tren de Aragua, or TdA, which originated in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 COFFMAN: I'm not going to surrender any part of this city to a criminal element.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: The hallway incident was the last straw for Edward and Cindy Romero, who decided to move out.\par \par \uc2 CINDY ROMERO, FORMER RESIDENT AT APARTMENT COMPLEX IN AURORA: Every time we try to go to bed at night, we have to keep it like this so nobody can kick in the door.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (voice over): The Romero say a gunfight this month left their car riddled with bullet holes. They say things changed when migrants moved in.\par \par \uc2 Aurora P.D. won't connect the video to the gang. Telling Fox News in a statement, "Based on our initial investigative work, we believe reports of TdA influence in \uc2 Aurora\uc2  are isolated."\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Aurora\uc2  is not alone. Last week, the U.S. attorney in Colorado announced a federal grand jury indicted four Venezuelan nationals for the armed robbery of a Denver jewelry store. Homeland Security investigation says at least one is affiliated with TdA.\par \par \uc2 And in nearby Douglas County, the sheriff's office this week made arrests in an auto theft ring. Three of the suspects are Venezuelan nationals.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (on camera): Back to the apartment complex, as Mayor Coffman told Fox News, he does not know how the gang members got into the buildings to take over, but he believes the federal government needs to step up. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alicia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we go live to Israel, where the large-scale incursion in the West Bank is complicating ceasefire negotiations.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our fox affiliates around this country are covering tonight. FOX13 in Tampa, as CDC data shows a listeria outbreak linked to Boar's Head deli meat is the cause of death in at least -- for at least nine people, and hospitalization of 57 others. The outbreak is the largest of its kind in the United States since 2011.\par \par \uc2 Fox 10 in Phoenix, where a series of water main breaks forces visitors out of hotels at Grand Canyon National Park. The restrictions will run throughout Labor Day holiday, when hotels are near or at capacity. And this is a live look at Oakland, California, from FOX2, our affiliate there. One of the big stories there tonight. New professional baseball team, the Oakland Ballers of the Pioneer League, raised more than $1 million in a crowdfunding campaign.\par \par \uc2 The investments came from more than 1,600 fans. Those fans now become shareholders in the team and will receive a say in how the Ballers run their business.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One of the handful of F-16 fighter jets Ukraine has received from its western partners has crashed. The war plane went down this week when Russia launched a major missile and drone barrage throughout Ukraine. The pilot did not survive. The Ukrainian defense ministry is investigating.\par \par \uc2 A confidential report from the United Nations nuclear watchdog finds Iran has further increased its stockpile of uranium enriched to near weapons grade levels in defiance of international demands. Uranium enriched up to 60 percent purity is just short a technical step away from weapons grade levels of 90 percent.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations working towards a ceasefire and hostage agreement between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  are underway in Qatar, but without any meaningful movement in the talks, families of Israeli hostages are becoming restless.\par \par \uc2 National correspondent Jeff Paul reports from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): carrying missing posters and wearing shirts stained with red handprints. Families of hostages, frustrated by the lack of progress over a cease fire deal, attempt to cross into Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Authorities near the border stopped them, but it doesn't hold back their anger.\par \par \uc2 GIL DICKMANN, COUSIN: We're recalling our prime minister. If you can't do this, we'll get inside and we'll bring them back ourselves. Bring them home.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): American Hersh Goldberg-Polin is one of the more than 100 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  abductees who's now spent 328 days in captivity. His mother Rachel sending this message towards Gaza.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL GOLDBERG, MOTHER: Hersh we are working day and night, and we will never stop.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): Talks over a deal between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  are ongoing. White House officials say they'll keep pushing until they get it across the line.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: It's in the nitty gritty, and that is a positive sign of progress. But at the end of the day, nothing is done until it's done.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): Complicating peace efforts, the major operation Israel launched in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 On the second day of raids, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  says it killed five more militants. The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  says the operation is deeply concerning and is fueling an already explosive situation. Palestinians there agree.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): They bulldoze streets, they destroyed parts of houses, they bombed places of worship, which affected us the most and made us cry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): Starting on Sunday, there's expected to be a series of pauses in the fighting in Gaza after the reemergence of polio there. For the first time in 25 years, both Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  have agreed to allow the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  to get in there and help vaccinate the more than 500,000 kids who call Gaza home, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff Paul live in Tel Aviv. Jeff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The suspects in the foiled plot to attack Taylor Swift concerts in Vienna earlier this month were seeking to kill tens of thousands of fans before the CIA discovered intelligence that disrupted that planning and led to arrests. That's according to the deputy director of the agency. The intelligence and subsequent arrests led to the cancelation of three sold out shows.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a lame duck president and an antagonistic adversary. How President Biden is dealing with China in his final days in office.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, a Hong Kong court convicts two former editors of a shuttered news outlet in a sedition case widely seen as a barometer for the future of media freedoms in a city once hailed as a bastion of free press in Asia, the trial was the first in Hong Kong involving the media since the former British colony returned to Chinese rule in 1997.\par \par \uc2 A Brazilian Supreme Court Justice is threatening to shut down the local operations of X, formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . The order from the Justice says X owner \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  must name a legal representative in Brazil within 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London, one of the big stories there tonight. British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  confirms his government is considering tougher anti-smoking measures, which could potentially see a ban on smoking in outdoor spaces, including at pubs and restaurants. The prospect was widely criticized by hospitality bosses, who say it would be an overreach by the state and another potential blow to their finances.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The matter involving an incident between an Arlington National Cemetery employee and a Trump staffer is considered closed, according to a statement put out by the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  today. Former President Trump went to the cemetery this week to mark the anniversary of the 2021 Kabul airport terrorist attack that killed 13 service members. The former president was invited by two of the grieving families.\par \par \uc2 The tense exchange, what the army called the altercation, occurred about where the Trump campaign the staffers could film. The army's statement says the worker was abruptly pushed aside by a Trump team member, but the employee does not want to press charges. Trump campaign staffers dispute that there was any altercation, and says it is considering releasing footage from the event.\par \par \uc2 It is against the rules and the law to use the cemetery and images at the grave sites for any political purposes. The families and the former president say they were honoring the fallen service members.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is keeping an open line of communication with China as tensions rise over global security and industrial policy. National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan met with Chinese leader Xi Jinping today.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has the story live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. This trip was in the works for months. The Chinese first sent their foreign minister to the U.S. and this was less a response to any single incident than a follow-through with plans that Biden and Xi made last year to recalibrate. Now, the two sides are finding a date to talk before a possible meeting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR: It is likely that both President Biden and President Xi will be at \uc2 APEC\uc2  and the G-20 later this year. And if they are, it would only be natural for them to have the change to sit down with one another.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan holding rare talks with China's central military commission vice chairman, the first at that level since 2018. Also meeting with China's foreign minister and President Xi Jinping. The visit comes amid continuing tensions in the Indo-Pacific. Right now, there's no U.S. naval aircraft carrier in the region as the buildup in the Middle East continues. And the leader level military communications between the U.S. and China were restored after Biden met with Xi last November. Contact at the theater level has not resumed, something Sullivan wanted to fix after China bristled at this.\par \par \uc2 ADMIRAL SAMUEL PAPARO, U.S. INDO-PACIFIC COMMAND: Escort of one vessel to the other is an entirely reasonable option within our mutual defense treaty.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The U.S. Indo-Pacific Command suggesting American forces could escort Philippine ships through the South China Sea after a month's long series of violent confrontations with China.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: Nobody is looking for a crisis. Not the Philippines and not the United States, and, we hope, not the PRC.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: There are broader reasons to stabilize U.S.-China relations.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GOLDBERG, FOUNDATION FOR DEFENSE OF DEMOCRACIES: They want to be able to take Taiwan by 2027. And right now, they're assessing us.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Especially before there is a new president in the White House.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ, (R-FL): Let me translate Sullivan's words in Beijing for you. We are desperate to find whatever concession we can to keep China and Taiwan and that area quiet while we deal with the fiasco in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: FOX is told, as a result of this trip there are now planned talks at the theater commander level, and there was also progress made cracking down on fentanyl precursors and streamlining rules for A.I. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live on the north lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we take a look at media conch at the Hunter Biden laptop story in the wake of those revelations from \uc2 Meta\uc2  CEO \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  about content censorship over government pressure. The Hunter Biden story was initially flagged as potential Russian disinformation and was treated that way by the media and on social media. But the story was factual. So how is it being covered now? Here is FOX News media analyst, host of FOX's "Media Buzz", Howard Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: It was at this Delaware computer shop that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's infamous laptop became a symbol, not only of the president's son's personal and legal problems, but of a major media embarrassment.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  founder \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  admitted in a letter to Republican Congressman Jim Jordan that his company suppressed a "New York Post" report on the computer shortly before the 2020 election. At the time, more than 50 former top intelligence officials, including \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2 , John Brennan, Michael Hayden, and \uc2 James Clapper\uc2  signed a letter saying the laptop story has all the classic earmarks of a Russian information operation. This generated headlines first in "Politico" and drew substantial TV coverage.\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MSNBC ANCHOR: This will expose you all as fools and useful idiots for the Russians.\par \par \uc2 WOLF BLITZER, CNN ANCHOR, THE SITUATION ROOM: Serious questions tonight about whether the Russians are using \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  to interfere in the U.S. presidential election.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We do know that it's a very active Russian campaign. That's according to the U.S. intelligence community.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took the same line in a debate with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Look, there are 50 former national intelligence folks who said that what he is accusing me of is a Russian plant.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Others, such as NPR, refused to cover the story. Then managing editor Terence Samuels saying, "We don't want to waste our time on stories that are not really stories." When "The New York times" and "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " confirmed the laptop's authenticity a year-and-a-half, Scarborough said he had just been raising questions, but that it doesn't let the media off the hook.\par \par \uc2 Zuckerberg now says \uc2 Facebook\uc2  was wrong and there was no Russian disinformation involving the laptop. When pressed, some of the former officials still defend signing the letter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ (on camera): In hindsight, most of the media badly blundered by dismissing the story based on ex-officials never examined the laptop. The question now is whether the news business has learned a lesson about avoiding propaganda. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Howie, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we go to the touch screen to dive into our new FOX polls on the issues in sunbelt states. And then the panel joins me to discuss those polls and the state of the presidential race, also, that interview, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOUG EMHOFF, SECOND GENTLEMAN OF U.S.: Very few people want what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and J.D. Vance are actually talking about.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE, (R) VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So I would ask you a question that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  asked America in 2016, what the hell do you have to lose?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 As we mentioned earlier, President Trump is campaigning in Wisconsin and Michigan today, telling voters about his goal for the U.S. economy. If we look at our latest FOX News polls across the sunbelt, Americans continue to say the economy is by far the most important issue, with immigration and abortion coming in a distant second and third.\par \par \uc2 Now, the former president leads Vice President Harris in all four sunbelt states when asked who would do a better job on the economy. North Carolina, he is ahead by nine points, his largest lead in that issue in all four states. The state saw a population increase in recent years as wealthy Americans from urban areas and surrounding states moved into North Carolina suburbs. The Tarheel state even gained an electoral vote because it expanded after the 2020 census, giving it a total of 16.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the issue of immigration, who does a better job on immigration and border security. Vice President Harris is making headway, outpacing where President Biden was, but voters still prefer the former president. He is ahead, again, in all four states. In the border state of Arizona, check this out, he leads by 15. The Hispanic vote there has been growing. While Republicans are pursuing those voters with some success, the majority still vote Democrat. Overall Hispanic voters in the sunbelt states prefer Harris to Trump 56 to 42. In 2020, Hispanics favored Biden by 19 points in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Nevada also has a large Hispanic population, a large working class population there. But on the issue of immigration, former President Trump, his platform is preferred in that state 55 to 42.\par \par \uc2 Now, here's the one that changes. Who does a better job on abortion? You can see these numbers. Voters in all the sunbelt states trust the vice president to do a better job on this issue. In Georgia, she is ahead on this issue by 20 points. Atlanta metro area has seen an increase in younger voters who typically prefer Harris and this abortion agenda, how she talks about it. This week she is campaigning alongside her running mate, Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , in southeast Georgia, focusing on abortion rights and economic issues.\par \par \uc2 The former president has said he wants to leave abortion policy up to the states. You can see he's trailing in all four of these. But on the big issues of the economy and immigration, he leads.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: How should voters look at some of the changes that you have made, that you have explained some of here, in your policy?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think the most important and most significant aspect of my policy perspective and decisions is my values have not changed. My value around what we need to do to secure our border, that value has not changed.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: People just want to see if she can get through the interview. Nobody knows what's going on. I've done so many interviews in the last couple of months. Every time I go out, I have an interview.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, that was a piece of that interview with CNN. We haven't seen the whole thing. We've only got a couple of excerpts.\par \par \uc2 But we have two new national polls out. "The Wall Street Journal" national poll has Vice President Harris up one on former President Trump, and the Suffolk-"USA Today" poll has Harris nearly five points, post DNC polling here. "The Wall Street Journal" matches some of our polling that we have had in the sunbelt and rustbelt states.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , Amy Walter, publisher and editor in chief of the "Cook Political Report," and "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " opinion columnist Ross Douthat. Thank you all for being here.\par \par \uc2 Amy, I just want to get a sense of where you think this race is. We get inundated with these polls, and right about this time everybody starts to glaze over on numbers. I think what you could definitively say is it is a very close race.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, NATIONAL EDITOR, "COOK POLITICAL REPORT": I think that's a very good way to think about it. I think if you go back where we were, say, six or seven weeks ago, it was pretty clear that Trump, Republicans had a decided edge in the presidential race, and even trickling all the way down ballot because, quite simply, the Democratic base, those voters who maybe they turned out for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020, maybe they're new but they want to vote for a Democrat, they just weren't enthusiastic. They didn't want to pull the lever for Biden or turn out to vote.\par \par \uc2 That has been -- that has changed tremendously with Harris on the ticket. And so now what you have is both candidates basically bringing their bases back into this game. And that means what we have is -- I think about it kind of like a football game. Both sides now have their full team on the field, and we're basically within three yards of the end zone either way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh, there's a lot of interest in this interview. We've only gotten, as I mentioned, a couple of excerpts. And I want to be fair. I assume Dana may have followed up about how the evolution on some of these big issues has changed. But that first answer, that my values haven't changed, being confronted about, for example, banning fracking or not, being confronted about some of these other terms, that's an interesting answer that didn't seem to reach a conclusion at least in that excerpt.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: You're right, Bret. It's a nonanswer answer. And I think going into this interview, I think Dana will do a fine job. We'll see the whole thing tonight and we'll be able to judge for ourselves. But the two clips that have been released have done nothing to resolve the essential challenge facing Vice President Harris. I want to draw from history going all the way back to Johnson. She has a credibility gap about whether or not she has the skill sets necessary to do this job. And to erase that credibility gap and somehow break away from what is essentially a tied race, you're going to have to get people to believe that you have command of facts and the ability to communicate in an effective way.\par \par \uc2 The other clip that's been released where she talks about deadlines around time is already much mocked on the Internet, and it's been less than an hour that it's been out there. If there's another faceplant like that in the course of however many minutes she talks to Dana or \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has to pull her out of a jam, that credibility gap about her capacity, her skill set to be president, is going to grow larger. And I think the polls show that the convention did not give her the bump that many people, including me, expected her to get.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ross, where do you see the race? What do you think we are not thinking about right now?\par \par \uc2 ROSS DOUTHAT, "NEW YORK TIMES": I think maybe the most important thing is whether we can really trust the polls. I think if you look overall, I agree with you that there hasn't been a big bounce so far post convention. I think you could make an argument that the Harris ticket already got that bounce when she became the nominee and the candidate, right, that it was sort of pulled forward earlier so we have been living in a bounce environment effectively for the last couple weeks.\par \par \uc2 But look, if you just take the polling averages right now, FOX's polls and other polls as well, you would say this looks like a replay of 2020, right, that Harris has a very narrow lead. She could win very narrowly the same way \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did by a few points nationally and less than that in swing states.\par \par \uc2 But in 2020, the actual polls showed Biden winning by five, six, seven, eight points. There were big pulling errors in midwestern states. And so the question hanging over the whole race really, has polling fixed the problem of how you reach a certain kind of Trump voter and how you deal with the enthusiasm for answering polls among a certain kind of liberal Democratic voter. And we won't know for a while, but if they have only fixed part of it, then the polling numbers actually look a lot better for former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's a great, great point, Ross. And Amy, we have been dealing with this for a few cycles now. And the last two cycles, going into the election, former President Trump has outperformed the polls going in by at least a couple points, sometimes greater depending on the state. And maybe that is the un-pollable Trump supporter? What do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: It could be. One thing we have been learning, though, is that if you look at voters who say that they have voted in, say, the last four elections in a row. These are what we consider consistent voters. They are, to Ross's point, the kinds of voters that may be more enthusiastic about answering polls. But they are -- they lean much more towards Harris and leaned much more to Biden.\par \par \uc2 If you look at voters who say -- they have been inconsistent voters. Those are the voters that Ross is talking about. Those are the ones that may not necessarily either, it's not just that they don't take polls. They just don't participate in every single election, whether it be a presidential or off year election. Trump does better with that set of voters. And so what pollsters are trying to do now is make sure ha is that they have a good balance of both of those consistent voters and those inconsistent voters.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I will note when I look at where the polls sit today, Trump is doing better in polling than he has in 2016 or 2020 at this point in the election. But the question in my mind is whether what this signifies isn't that the polls are off or on but that there is now a Trump base that he consolidated earlier than he ever has. And the question going forward is, can he build beyond that?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that is the question.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, panel, stand by. Swing state Senate races that could determine control of Congress. And then a small scale baby boom for a very close group of friends.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON, (R-AR): \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  in Pennsylvania, \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2  in Michigan, \uc2 Eric Hovde\uc2  in Wisconsin, these are all presidential battlegrounds and critical Senate races. President Trump is doing exactly what he needs to do, which is win his race and turn out as many voters as can he so we can elect people.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: We also have amazing candidates -- Ruben Gallego, Angela Alsobrooks, Andy Kim. More than half of our candidates are candidates of color. We're making the Senate look like America.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We spent a lot of time talking about the presidential race, but you look at our FOX power rankings map, and you've got a lot of Senate races that are up in the air. The yellow ones are really the toss-up states. You can see the lean and the Republicans. As you take a look at power rankings, Republicans have a favorable map. You look, they only need to pick up two, and you look at Montana, a place that Trump won by 16, and you look at West Virginia, it's kind of in the bag, Trump won by 38.9.\par \par \uc2 We're back with the panel. Hugh, despite all of that Democrats, are polling pretty well across the board in some of these races that they thought would be tighter.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: That takes us back to the issue of polling, and both Amy and Ross have forgotten more than I know about polling. But I'm familiar with the term polling response bias. A lot of people just don't talk to folks. I'm from Ohio. I think Bernie Moreno is going to win in Ohio, but the polls don't show that. I don't know about \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , who I talked to this morning on the program this morning in Pennsylvania, but boy, Vance and Trump are there a lot, and the three of them are helping each other out.\par \par \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  was talking to me two days ago. I think \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  is looking pretty good in Maryland. I just don't know that the polls are very useful on a state basis. I remind everyone every time I'm on, a week before the 2020 election, ABC-"Washington Post", and I worked for the post, had \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  winning that state by 17 points a week before the election. He won by less than two-thirds of a point.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Ross, on these Senate races, they usually track with the presidential. And some of these candidates have to be careful that they're not crossing waves with the presidential candidates. It's interesting to see the former president on the issue of abortion and IVF when he came out and talked about today, having insurance companies federal government paying for IVF. You know, in some places that helps candidates.\par \par \uc2 DOUTHAT: Yes. I mean, I think the assumption in the Trump campaign, clearly, is that pro-life voters don't have anywhere else to go, and that he can go a certain distance away from them and still assume that they will pull the lever for him in the end.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not that's true, I think one thing you can say just based on Senate polling and national polling is that there is different dynamic here versus 2016 and 2020. And this goes to Amy's point in the first segment about maybe Trump consolidating his base earlier. But, in those races it was normal to see more conventional Republicans doing better than Trump. And in these polls, again and again, admittedly sometimes they are up against Democratic incumbents, but Trump is more popular than a sort of, you know, a sort of average Republican Senate candidate. And that is itself an interesting dynamic this time around.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amy, you look at how she answers these questions about a change in position. She says she has the same values. We'll see what comes out of that interview. But \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  in Ohio, \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  in Montana, Bob Casey in Pennsylvania, I doubt they are going to be campaigning with this presidential candidate that much. Do you think?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: No. I think they will. I think they -- well, actually, the battleground states, so maybe a Pennsylvania, Bob Casey is in a very different position than in Ohio and Montana, where I completely agree with you.\par \par \uc2 Look, the ballgame as we all know is Montana and Ohio. If those two states flip to Republican, even if Harris -- if she wins the presidency, Republicans will have won the Senate. But I think that there is a broader thing going on, which is if you look at those battleground states, we could have a whole conversation about whether the polls are correct or not. But if the environment is, indeed, as bad for Democrats as theoretically it should be given the concern about inflation and how well Trump is doing on the economy, the fact that Democratic candidates down ballot are doing as well as they are suggests that the environment actually looks a lot like we saw in 2020, which is it's pretty even.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which is where we started this panel discussion on polls. It's pretty even. Thank you, guys. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The only one that was trying for a baby was me, so -- and none of this was planned. And they were all born the end of June and July.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Six Utah friends grew up together, all gave birth within a few days of each other. Somehow their babies just 20 hours apart. They now range in ages from four to eight weeks old. The women and their babies were offered a free photoshoot to celebrate the rare occurrence. A lot about babies today.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our "Whatever Happened To?" series with an update on the efforts to restore the iconic Notre Dame Cathedral. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE with guest host \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2  starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240830ek8t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC); \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Campaigns in Michigan; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  in Joint Interview\b0\par\par\uc2 Aishah Hasnie, Mark Meredith, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 11,099 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: You are looking live at Potterville, Michigan. That's where the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is set to speak at any moment.\par \par \uc2 He will reportedly be hitting Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on the economy and inflation before heading off to Wisconsin for a rally there tonight, this as Harris and her running mate, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , are wrapping up that bus tour and that earlier CNN sit-down, before heading to a rally in Georgia tonight.\par \par \uc2 We're all over all of this with Aishah Hasnie in Michigan, where the former president is rallying, and Mark Meredith in Georgia on that Harris-Walz interview and what we are learning.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. Great to have you with us on a busy NewsHour.\par \par \uc2 Let's go right to Aishah in Potterville, Michigan, ahead of the former president's remarks -- Aishah..\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We were expecting remarks to begin about 3:00, but, of course, the president, the former president has been delayed. We don't know exactly when those remarks will start. I can tell you he is wheels down in Michigan. He's on the ground and he is en route. It might take him a little while to get here, but his fans, his supporters behind me, they are in full anticipation, and they're ready to hear from him face-to-face.\par \par \uc2 I have been talking to a few folks here on the ground about what their number one issue is. Of course, we know in the Sun Belt states, it's the economy. It's really the economy everywhere you go in this country and here especially it's rising energy prices. It's the housing crisis. It's the grocery store bills.\par \par \uc2 They have a lot of complaints. They're super stressed out and they want someone to talk about this. The former president does well on these issues, and so that is exactly what he is going to talk about when he arrives here in Potterville.\par \par \uc2 This is the Trump/Vance strategy that we have been seeing them play across the Rust Belt states all week long, just focusing on the issues, trying to connect with those middle class, working-class voters, even those independents, trying to use the economy to woo them in, talking about really day-to-day struggles.\par \par \uc2 And voters here are watching that pretty closely too, Neil, because I was talking to one gentleman outside this venue about how he thinks the former president could bring in some of those folks that maybe haven't decided yet. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: How does he court those independents and those folks that haven't made up their mind yet in Michigan?\par \par \uc2 MARK CHMIELEWSKI, MICHIGAN VOTER: I think Kennedy just helped that out. So a lot of those independents, I think he's going to bring them all in. And they don't agree on all the items, but they have come together with the items they do. And it's necessary that we bring and unite people together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, we will see if it works because RFK Jr, of course, can't seem to get off the ballot here.\par \par \uc2 Now, new FOX News polling shows Harris has improved some upon Biden's numbers among women, among black voters, among young voters. And the Trump campaign is targeting those female black voters as well with this brand-new add out featuring some black women from battleground states denouncing Harris, saying, I'm not with her.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump is also getting a big helping hand tonight from Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  in that battleground state as he is going to be headlining a big fund-raiser for Trump tonight. And that, of course, follows a public quarrel that happened a couple weeks ago. Looks like they have mended things and now \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  along with his wife is going to fund-raise for him tonight, saying that Georgia is a must-win.\par \par \uc2 And the campaign says it's a must-win. You cannot achieve the White House without winning over the Peach State. Right now, Trump is headed here to Potterville, and the Democrats trying to counterprogram, Neil, by sending second gentleman Doug Emhoff to the state as well in Grand Rapids.\par \par \uc2 They are trying to strategize as well, watching what the Trump/Vance ticket is doing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, got it, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie following all of that.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Mark Meredith right now in Savannah, Georgia. This is where you're finding a big focus on the part of team Harris and Walz just finishing up a couple hours ago, I guess, that interview on CNN -- Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 The vice president has wrapped up that interview, the first time that she has sat down with the network to talk about how her and Governor Walz would govern if they were to be sent to Washington, certainly a high-stakes interview for them. And then later on this evening they're going to be right here behind me taking the podium to address the people down here in Savannah.\par \par \uc2 While the vice president has certainly been on the trail quite a bit visiting multiple battleground states, this would be the first time that she's really taken direct questions. She's really only spoken to the press corps a few times, the traveling press pool, when she is on the road.\par \par \uc2 And she's likely to face questions today about some of the change in positions that she's had, whether it be now embracing fracking or now embracing some border wall funding. Could be an attempt to win over voters in the Sun Belt states like Georgia, where former President Trump is leading on some issues, like immigration, the economy and the war in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with voters today who told us they think Harris will be able to overcome some of those shortcomings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I do think the numbers will rise. I hope that anybody who's maybe on the fence and who is unsure of where they want to place their vote will think from their heart also, as well as their head.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: We're also hearing from the \uc2 RNC\uc2  about this visit to say: "Georgians are uniting behind one thing. They know America cannot afford another four years of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' failed, weak and dishonest leadership."\par \par \uc2 So, not a very warm welcome for the vice president from the Republicans here in Georgia. Neil, we're also watching some new back-and-forth when it comes to next month's debate. That's the one that's going to be happening in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 We were under the belief that the determination was set up, that pretty much everybody had agreed to the rules, which were going to be the same ones as the CNN debate back in June with President Biden. Now, team Harris says that they have not agreed to that yet, that they are still in discussions with ABC, because they want the candidates to have their microphones on, both candidates at the same time.\par \par \uc2 You will remember back in June, Neil, only one candidate could speak at a time, and that was that President Biden's request -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That is interesting in and of itself, right? I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  agreed to all of that, and it looked like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  agreeing to this debate itself. Remember, there was resistance on the part of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  whether it should apply to all of a sudden the switch in nominees. Now she's the one balking, or possibly.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Well, this is such uncharted territory, as you would imagine.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Before, we had the \uc2 Commission on Presidential Debates\uc2 , and all of these rules were agreed to for years. They would go back.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: So, you can imagine there's going to be this tit for tat, Neil, and it's still not a done deal at this point.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it, my friend. Thank you. Great job, as always.\par \par \uc2 Mark Meredith following that.\par \par \uc2 When the former president takes to the microphone, of course, we will take you there to Michigan.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we got \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2  with us, GOP pollster, and Joey Garrison, USA Today White House correspondent, Bill McGurn, finally, Wall Street Journal editorial board member and a former Bush 43 speechwriter. He has a way with words.\par \par \uc2 So, Bill, end it with you.\par \par \uc2 Take your take on what's at stake right now. All of these states, the seven battleground states are sort of a jump ball right now. But the one thing we have seen is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has pulled even or is even money in essentially all of them. And I'm just wondering what you make of that. Is this a Democratic post-convention bounce, or how would you describe it?\par \par \uc2 BILL MCGURN, EDITORIAL BOARD MEMBER, THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: I think it's pre-convention bounce too.\par \par \uc2 Look, they're very relieved they no longer are stuck with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  weighing them down. So there is joy in the Democratic Party. I never would have thought she could go so long without answering a single question or sending surrogates out to answer questions about her policy.\par \par \uc2 It's amazing. I would have said before she can't get away with the whole campaign, but I think the press is being complicit. They're just letting her off the hook. If this had been a Republican, not just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , any Republican, there'd be editorials every day saying, why isn't she answering our questions? Where's her policy?\par \par \uc2 What I think this leads to -- you mentioned all these developments -- all are important because when you don't have information, little things assume great magnitude. I think it's all putting bigger pressure on the debate.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the press isn't going to hold \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to account. He has to do it. He has to be aggressive, but not personal, and call her to account for her policies, for her policy switches. Get her on the record.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we just hear from surrogates.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you're right about that.\par \par \uc2 Lee Carter\uc2 , I know we in the media tend to focus on, you have got to do media interviews, you have got to do media interviews, and then I discovered myself -- and I'm so old, I can remember -- criticism like that of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , that his venues would be large crowds and speaking to audiences, didn't do too many interviews.\par \par \uc2 Now, to be fair, we had really the three major networks, and that was it. I mean, obviously you spoke to the print press, which was much more viable at that time. But he chose to sort of bypass the media in his run to the nomination in 1980, and ultimately a landslide victory.\par \par \uc2 So maybe he's figuring -- or the campaign of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is figuring, it worked for him, maybe it can work for us. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: And it worked for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you're right.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Let's remember we talked about him campaigning from his basement.\par \par \uc2 So, there's something to be said about taking your message directly to the people, especially in the age of social media. But I think that the thing that is so important right now is, we had 90 days really to get to know what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' presidency was going to look like, and you would expect it to be all hands on deck.\par \par \uc2 It seems to be all hands on the road, all hands in advertising, all hands on everything but talking to the media. And I do think that that is going to be an interesting calculus that they have made, because she's going to have her first interview tonight with the vice president, her vice presidential pick. They're doing it together.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: She's not doing alone. She's chosen to do things in a way that makes every moment count.\par \par \uc2 So, all eyes are going to be on this interview. And if it's not perfect, there's going to be a lot of questions, the questions why she didn't do it alone. And just like we're talking about with a debate, there's going to be increased scrutiny on the debate. She's going to have to land and stick every landing from here on out in order to keep this momentum.\par \par \uc2 But the momentum is real. I mean, it's not just that she's winning people over. She's got the Democrats so excited that enthusiasm numbers -- what I'm looking at right now, the enthusiasm among Democrats is in the 80s. It was in the 30s when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the nominee.\par \par \uc2 So that's a huge shift and really explains what the difference is in the polls.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I wonder if those polls change, though, Joey, whether that enthusiasm lasts. You're only as good as your last poll sometimes and it reflects the mood within a campaign as well.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 JOEY GARRISON, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Well, right now, Vice President Harris has been able to run as a change candidate, even though she is the incumbent vice president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GARRISON: So if you're the Trump campaign and you're \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you're about to give this rally here in a few minutes, you need to remind voters that she has been in office for 3.5 years.\par \par \uc2 And that's what they have done in one of their recent ads. And so, like, if you look at the economy and inflation, right now, Trump has a lead on how - - over Harris in terms of who you trust more on the economy, but it's not as large of a lead that he enjoyed over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So if you're the Trump campaign, you have to be economy, economy, economy, and remind folks that, hey, Harris was right there step by step with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Otherwise, that -- it's hard to see how he starts improving on the economy to where he was earlier in this campaign when it was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We don't want to get bogged down in numbers, but looking at these polls we're showing you in the various battleground states, they represent, those seven states, 93 electoral votes, or about a third of what you need to become the next president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And breaking down, as I often do, who leads even by a point and how many electoral votes would that be if it was etched in stone on Election Day, she comes away with winning two-thirds of those votes, as things stand now. And I know smart people like you, like all of you remind me, Neil, it's within the margin of error. I get that.\par \par \uc2 But just looking at it now, that's a big turn in her favor, Bill. The question is, does it last?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Well, I don't know. It depends partly whether the press does their job or whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can enter that debate and do what he did last night, be disciplined, but be aggressive, not nasty, not personal, and hold her to account for the things she's not doing.\par \par \uc2 In her words, say -- explain her flip-flops, why she changed. And, also on economy, she -- we talk about inflation. It's really prices people understand. They're about 20 percent higher today. And ask her, does she take -- accept any responsibility for what happened and what would she do differently?\par \par \uc2 So I think he's going to have to really put all in on those debates. I don't think ads, as good as they are, can really do it. I think people have to see the candidates up close with each other, answering tough questions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You guys are the experts, but, as you know, and, Lee, particularly in your case, I read a prompter, so I believe I qualify.\par \par \uc2 And here's what I think is going to happen. Now, again, leave the room. It's the debate. That debate, especially if it's the one and only between these presidential candidates, that will decide it all. And I'm just wondering your thoughts on that, Lee, and how -- who bears the burden the most for that debate?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: It's really fascinating, because debates usually don't count that much. But when you're going to race that's this close and we have had this much time to decide, it actually could create enough of a difference to make the difference.\par \par \uc2 And the thing is, both \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  have incredible strengths in debating and they have incredible weaknesses. We saw \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  have an incredible debate in 2020, which really made her a superstar for a moment. And then she had another debate where she absolutely flopped and she came across as defensive and just a whole host of other things that really made her lose steam fast.\par \par \uc2 So if she has a night like that, it could be a real problem for her. Similarly, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has had some of his most epic moments during debates, but he's also had some moments where he's come off the rails. He's got to Neil it. He's got to stay on message. And I think the most important thing is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not become overly reactive to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's really, really hard, I think, right now in this moment to look at her and not react to everything she says, to look at her and not say, how can you not take responsibility for the economy? How can you not do this?\par \par \uc2 If he's reacting to her, he's not talking about his agenda. And his agenda is the one that was winning in the polls right before all this happened. His agenda on the economy is the one that people are looking for. His agenda on immigration is the winning one. So he needs to take the narrative back and take control of the narrative back.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it's really being set by her. And so I think the debate could be a really important night just to shift the momentum for him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We are getting some blurbs out of this interview that CNN conducted with the presidential and the vice presidential candidates of the Democratic Party, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and the governor.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that comes across here is Dana Bash asking about how voters would look at some of the changes that you have made, referring to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' reversal and some key positions. She goes on to say, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , on that, that: "The value is not what has changed. I spent two terms as the attorney general of California prosecuting transnational criminal organizations, violations of American laws regarding the passage, illegal passage of guns, drugs and human beings across the border."\par \par \uc2 So what she's saying, essentially, Joey Garrison, is: My values haven't changed. I think what she's saying is, maybe some of my exact positions have.\par \par \uc2 But is that going to do the job?\par \par \uc2 GARRISON: Well, yes, and we will see more on that when we see the full interview later tonight.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, she's going to have to explain some of these more liberal positions she took in 2019, when she was running in the Democratic presidential primary, whether it's on immigration, the border wall. Obviously, more recently, as a vice president, she supported the bipartisan border bill, which would have used unspent dollars to continue building the wall.\par \par \uc2 And so she -- that is one obvious question that needs to be asked. Hey, do you support a border wall similar to what Trump has long talked about? But, yes, more in terms of clarifying her positions on a number of things, particularly also fracking.\par \par \uc2 And that was something that she came out against as a presidential candidate. What's your position now? Because, right now, what we have right -- currently are statements, a lot of them from the campaign itself, not from the candidate. So that's the kind of things we're going to be looking at this evening with her long-awaited interview.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, now, again, they're not sharing much with us, in fact, none of video or audio of that interview, so what I'm reading to you is the quotes that CNN provided us from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But one of the things, I guess, that Dana Bash got into as well was the inconsistency on a number of positions, including not only what's going on at the border and the illegals coming into the drug issue, but also when it comes to her controversial final deciding vote on the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 And, in that regard, Bill McGurn, she stands by that, said: "We had set goals for the U.S. and, by extension, the globe around when we should meet certain standards for the reduction of greenhouse gas emissions, as an example. That value has not changed. My value around that, what we need to do remains."\par \par \uc2 So, again, back to: My values haven't changed. Some of my positions have, presumably, because we accomplish what we set out to do.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MCGURN: Yes, I don't think any American would agree they accomplished what they set out to do. I mean, inflation is still higher than when they took over and so forth.\par \par \uc2 It gets back to, I think that Trump's challenge is, as you say, to get his agenda out there. That's the hope for the American people. But I also think he has to -- when she does this, she's trying to have it all ways. He has to point out, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is not who she says she is. She says she's everything to everyone.\par \par \uc2 And he has to point that out. Who do you trust? Here's my agenda. This has worked before. It can work again and improve your lives. And, meanwhile, we can't get a straight answer out of her. So I think he has to prove that she's disingenuous and is being propped up.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Lee, if I can go back to you a second, because this involves -- I know you're a great pollster. And I wonder how, when I get back to this debate that I have properly said is going to be the decider of the selection, I base it on how men react versus how women react as well.\par \par \uc2 I only say that, Lee, because I cannot forget in the debate that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had with Vice President Pence at the time, he interrupted her or something and she stopped him, said, "I'm talking, I'm talking," and that was it. And then the fly was on his head. And it just went all downhill from there.\par \par \uc2 My only point was with that, and I mentioned it many times, men overwhelmingly hated that. I think 80 percent of men, polls, they said, how obnoxious, blah, blah, blah. Women, by about the same margin, 80 percent, said, fine, that was a great thing to do, shut him up.\par \par \uc2 So that's not exactly how they characterized it, but it was an extreme reaction the part of whether you're a male or a female. Do you think we will see a split like that and see that through the prism of whether you're a man or a woman watching this debate?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: I think it's impossible not to look through the world through the lens in which you see it.\par \par \uc2 And for whatever reason, it is true that men and women have very different reactions to these candidates. And you look at even among young people, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does very, very well with young men, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does very, very well with young women.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: There is a prism that we look through the world at, and we can't help it. It doesn't make us sexist. It doesn't make us anything. It makes us who we are. And it's not what people say that matters. It's what we hear and react to it.\par \par \uc2 And so I think there will be very different reactions among men and women to this debate. I think we imagine ourselves in a situation where focused on what we're focused on and we're going to have different reactions. And it's very, very clear that both of these candidates elicit very different emotional reactions among people.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, final word on that, guys. I apologize.\par \par \uc2 We are going to be going back to Michigan and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  speaking to this crowd. It's an economic address, we're told.\par \par \uc2 Should let that yet another poll has come out confirming that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is gaining some steam. This is from The Wall Street Journal, just released a short time ago that shows Harris supported 48 percent to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's at 47 percent in a head-to-head matchup. It's a little wider when independent third-party candidates are included.\par \par \uc2 She leads by two points there, but a reminder, this race remains as tight as a tick and getting tighter.\par \par \uc2 More after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Potterville, Michigan, this is where former President Trump is due to address a crowd, talk some of his economic ideas. I wonder how that will sort out there in a state right now that is running dead even, depending on what poll you look at.\par \par \uc2 But that really could be the case with all the seven battleground states and the 93 electoral votes they represent. You need 270 of them to become the next president of the United States. And a third of them are in states like Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Nancy Mace kind enough to join us from South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Good to see you, Neil, as always.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We were talking before, Congresswoman, about the role gender plays in watching a debate, in watching each candidate's demeanor toward the other. And there's been all sorts of advice from some of your fellow Republicans on how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should handle and treat \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and how far that can go, that she's not another \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , that it's a very different case.\par \par \uc2 It's eight years later. How do you feel about this whole gender thing? Because, again and again, I was raising with our last panel, Congresswoman, men do react differently than women, and vice versa, particularly if there are attack lines back and forth.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, I'm a woman who's broken a number of glass ceilings over my career.\par \par \uc2 And I understand firsthand what that means. But I'm voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because I am a woman. And nothing says I'm a woman, hear me roar, like having your first national interview sit-down with a man by your side. This is -- this is an interview for the leader of the free world. And so as a woman, I want to vote for someone who's going to protect women like me who are survivors of rape.\par \par \uc2 I want to vote for someone who's going to protect children who are victims of incest, like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will. I want to make sure that single moms like I am can afford their groceries and their gas when they're trying to put food on the table for their kids.\par \par \uc2 That person is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because of his economic policies, like lowering taxes for the middle class, expanding the childcare tax credit, those kinds of things, lowering -- looking at the spending of the federal government, et cetera, and the rise of inflation.\par \par \uc2 I'm voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because I want to be sure that biological men aren't in the locker room showering next to my underage daughter. I want to make sure men aren't stealing women's achievements in sports or academics or their scholarships. And that's why, as a woman, I support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I want to see him come out strong for women's issues, as he always has. We have had many conversations. He was one of the first Republicans to come out very strong for IVF and supporting women and families. He can go toe to toe with her on women's issues. I feel very confident about that. His economic policies are far better.\par \par \uc2 And, look, she's stealing many of his ideas like no taxes on tips, like building more border wall. These are all things that she has lied to the American people about her position. She's now flip-flopping.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's a way to show that her policies have changed, that she's flip-flopped without calling her a liar. And I think he will do a very good job of that at the debate.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, you were among the Republicans who said stick to the issues there. They're going your way and you're very good at them.\par \par \uc2 Maybe he has heeded that advice. This whole address that we're going to be going to shortly, Congresswoman, is on -- is addressing economic issues. You like that, right? I mean, that gets back to something you think is to his advantage?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I do.\par \par \uc2 Look at his policies and look, how many policies has she now stolen from his Web site? And she's going to continue to do that because she knows the policies of the Biden/Harris administration have failed the American people. This is a woman who was supposed to take care of Mexico and the Northern Triangle and the southern border and failed miserably.\par \par \uc2 This is someone who is OK with the changes to Title IX that would allow biological men to take away women's achievements in sports, to take away their scholarships. This is a woman who is OK with biological men getting in the shower next to underage girls.\par \par \uc2 Like, this -- these aren't things that I can support as a woman, as a mom. We go to economic policy, we know that tax cuts work, that they're desperately needed right now. We also know that we need to rein in federal spending. It will not happen under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I know that his policies are better for women everywhere.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congresswoman, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We're going to go to President Trump right now in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (JOINED IN PROGRESS)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: And I also want to thank \uc2 Alro Steel\uc2  CEO Randy Glick. Just met Randy and he's fantastic. The job the family has done, he's done, it's beautiful.\par \par \uc2 And all of the talented workers here at \uc2 Alro Steel\uc2 , I hope you're all happy. Are you happy with the company? I think, yes?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think? Great company.\par \par \uc2 And congratulations on 76 years of manufacturing in Michigan. And you're -- you had your best years four years ago, but you're going to have even better years coming up, I can tell you that.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You're going to have even better.\par \par \uc2 I'm here today with a simple message for the American autoworker and for the American worker. Your long economic nightmare will very soon be over. It's going to be over. Going to turn it around.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : With your vote, we will defeat comrade \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and we will bring back the American dream bigger, better, stronger and just better, bigger, better. We love the American dream. You don't hear about the American dream.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : When was the last time you heard about the American dream? They don't talk about it, although they copy everything else I do. So I guess that will be next. They will be copying. They will be saying, oh, the American dream. She doesn't know what the American dream is.\par \par \uc2 Over the past four years, Kamala and crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  have presided over an economic reign of terror, committing one financial atrocity after another. As vice president, Kamala cast the deciding votes that caused the worst inflation in American history. You're all victims of it. We all are. Everybody is a victim of it.\par \par \uc2 It's a nation-buster, costing the typical family $28,000. Under her policies, household wealth has plummeted by $33,000 per family. The cost of the typical monthly mortgage has really tripled and you can't get the money. So you will go from 2 percent to 10 percent, but you can't get the money at 10 percent. I guess they want more than that, but you just can't get the money.\par \par \uc2 Credit card debt has ballooned by 40 percent and they have the highest numbers in history right now, as about three days ago, the highest number in history. Now Kamala says she wants to talk about the future, but she's been there for 3.5 years. She only wants to talk about the future because the past has been so -- the job they have done.\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you, he's the worst president in the history of our country and she's the worst vice president and she's the worst border czar in the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And she's the one who gave us every single disaster that our country is facing right now. Look at Afghanistan. She said, I was the last person in the room.\par \par \uc2 What a horrible, horrible event that was. Kamala's made middle-class life unaffordable and unlivable. And I'm going to make America affordable again. In addition to make it a great again, we're going to make it affordable again. And that's going to be starting on day one. Isn't it amazing?\par \par \uc2 They go three years and now they say, oh, we're going to do this, we're going to do that. The only good thing that she's flip-flopping, she's the greatest flip-flopper, things that she never even thought of. She probably goes back to her room and gets sick to her stomach when she says what she has to say, because she's a Marxist, she's a fascist, and she never believed him.\par \par \uc2 And now she's saying, oh, we want to build a strong border. Where has she been for 3.5 years, as we took in 20 million people, many of them horrible criminals?\par \par \uc2 My vision is for a middle-class that is, once again, the envy of the entire world. It's going to happen. It's going to happen fast.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Workers like you in communities like this should be able to afford a nice house, a new car, and a growing family on a single income, all while enjoying the highest standard of living on Earth, right?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Americans deserve safe streets, secure borders, great schools, healthy children, bustling factories, and a nation that is confident, and a nation that is strong. That's the future that I intend to deliver, and I will deliver.\par \par \uc2 And you had the best economy and the history of our country just a short while ago, and now you have what they call inflation, what that has done to this country. And, frankly, they have done also worse, and you're going to end up in World War III with these people. I will tell you what, we have never been so close to World War III.\par \par \uc2 And with the power of weapons, I always say this isn't two army tanks running back and forth shooting at each other. The power of weapons today, very dangerous, very bad. We have people -- we don't even -- do we have a president? He just got back from California. He was supposed to go to the White House. He never got there. He went to Delaware, and he's laying on a beach, sleeping all day long.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, no, she -- who the hell wants to sleep, and who wants to sleep in public? He's sleeping.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Do you think President Xi of China is at a beach sleeping? Do you think Kim Jong-un is sleeping from North Korea with his nuclear weapons all over the place? They don't sleep so much.\par \par \uc2 And we have a guy sleeping. And she's -- look, she's incompetent. She can't do an interview? It's been months. She can't -- I just did an interview backstage with a very terrible person, but she was OK, actually.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: NBC fake news. It was NBC fake news. She asked me about all sorts of things. She asked me about abortion. And I handled it very nicely, because you know what? That's so overplayed.\par \par \uc2 We have abortion. We have the whole thing brought back into the states, where it belongs. That's where everybody wanted it for years and years and years.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And they're voting on it. And I happen to believe in the exceptions \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  did for life of the mother, rape, incest, the exceptions. Probably 90 percent of the people do.\par \par \uc2 But the -- I was just telling this reporter the real problem and the real radicals on that issue are the Democrats, where you can have an abortion in the ninth month. And in six states, you're allowed to kill the baby after the baby is born. And one of those states is Minnesota, where this tampon Tim comes from.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Kill the baby after the baby is born. So that's no longer abortion. That's called execution, right?\par \par \uc2 You're allowed to execute the baby after the baby -- they're the radical -- Kamala is promising communist-style price controls, free taxpayer-funded health care, not that you're going to want it -- nobody in this room is going to want it -- for all illegal aliens, mass amnesty and, citizenship for the millions and millions of migrants she lets in.\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She wants to give citizenship to people that have come here illegally. They didn't come through the system. We want people to come into our country, but they have to come in legally, right? They have to come in legally.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're taking people that are murderers and drug dealers, and she wants to give them amnesty. I tell you, I don't get it.\par \par \uc2 But all of this will destroy Medicare and will destroy your Social Security. She called for the abolition of ICE. You know, ICE, these are great patriots. These are tough people. I'm looking at some of these people right in the front row. They're tough as hell, but you don't want to be in ICE.\par \par \uc2 You don't want to walk into MS-13 gangs and start slugging it out. These guys are seriously tough, and they live our country. Somebody's got to do it. I took them out by the thousands. Now they don't want to take them out anymore. They want to let ICE (sic), the worst gang probably in the world, the meanest, the nastiest. They slice up people with knives.\par \par \uc2 They'd rather use a knife than a gun because it's more painful. They kill two young students walking to school, two beautiful young 16-year-old girls walking to school. They sliced them up in Long Island. And they want to let these people stay, and we're not going to let them stay. We want to get them the hell out of our country.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Kamala sponsored a bill to blow up the entire U.S. health care system and force every American into a socialist government-run program with rationing and deadly wait times.\par \par \uc2 You go there and they say, that's good. We're going to be all set for you. Come back in three months. She endorsed a 70 to 80 percent tax rate increase. Look at this. She thinks the rate, actually, the rate could be as much as 80 percent. She says, I will think about that.\par \par \uc2 Well, I won't think about it, OK? I won't think about anywhere near it. In fact, we're going for a tax cut. We gave you the biggest tax cut in history, and that gave us tremendous numbers of jobs. And we actually took in more income the following year after the cut because our country was booming like it had never boomed before.\par \par \uc2 She supports abolishing cash bail so that, when you murder somebody, oh, don't worry about it. You're free to go. No cash, no nothing. And then they go murder somebody else and, in some cases, much more than one person. And she endorsed the Marxist crusade to defund the police.\par \par \uc2 Do you know that she was the leader of the defund the police? Does anybody in Michigan -- you almost -- I said, why don't I just walk up? I don't have to waste a lot of time. You're all fans, right? Is anybody here not going to vote for Trump? I'm just kidding.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Raise your hand if you have the courage to do that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think pretty good. No, but I said, why do I have to make a speech? All I have to do is say she was the leader of the defund the police movement. And then I say, ladies and gentlemen, thank you very much, and leave.\par \par \uc2 Because, when you hear that -- no, she's a Marxist. Her father's a Marxist. He's a Marxist professor. It's very nice. This election is not a choice between Democrats and Republicans. It's a choice between communism and freedom. That's what it's about.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And nobody -- nobody really knows her. They were doing something, the man on the street, yes. They said, you know Harris? Everyone's saying, who the hell is Harris? Nobody knows who Harris is.\par \par \uc2 What is the vice president's name? They have no idea what the name is. Now the name Kamala is -- it's a little complex. There's about 19 different ways of pronouncing it, right?\par \par \uc2 But Kamala is -- at least it's a name you sort of remember. You can't use Harris, because nobody knows who the hell Harris is. Who's Harris? We want somebody they know. And one of the reasons they don't know, she did a lousy job. She was considered the worst vice president in history.\par \par \uc2 I don't know who does this stuff, but she was considered the worst. Kamala will turn America into a Third World country. Will happen. It's already getting there, I hate to say it.\par \par \uc2 When you look at our borders, when you look at our elections, our rigged elections, when you look at all the crap that's going on, we're turning into a Third World. When you look at how they go weaponized against me for doing absolutely nothing wrong. I got indicted, I think, nine different times, nine times.\par \par \uc2 I never even knew what an indictment was for my whole life. All of a sudden, the last year, I think I had nine indictments, far more than Alphonse Capone, who was quite well-known in Chicago and the environs. He liked killing people, but he had nothing like me.\par \par \uc2 Nine times, you know? And every time they see me going up in the polls -- and we're leading in the polls, and I think we're leading by a lot.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The polls are rigged too. The polls are rigged too. They say, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is tied, tied. We were up massively by Biden. How would you like to be me? I spent $100 million on beating Biden. We had a debate and everybody said, this guy's shot.\par \par \uc2 They never give me credit. They say he was shot. Maybe I did a good job. Somebody said that was the greatest debate performance I have ever said. And then you know what? That I have ever seen. Said -- it was a CNN person, and said, this is the single greatest debate performance I have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 Two days later, they forgot about that. They said he was terrible. But that's all right. Whatever it takes. But I spent $100 million on fighting him. We weren't fighting anyone else. We weren't fighting a vice president. We didn't even know who the hell she was.\par \par \uc2 And then all of a sudden, they say, Joe, you're losing badly. You got to get out. He was losing by a lot. But they went up to him. They said, get out. That's like you're in a fight. And you have a fighter, and he's doing great. And they say, listen, we're going to take the opponent out. We're going to throw another guy in to finish it off.\par \par \uc2 It's -- that's what they did, really. It's never been done before. And she was last online. And she came in last. She -- I think there were like 22 people. She was the first one to get out. And she got out. She had no votes. She had no votes. She went to -- was supposed to go to Iowa. She quit before Iowa. That was the first state.\par \par \uc2 She never made it to Iowa. And now she's running. And what about those other 20 or so people? They did much better than her. And they're saying -- they're sitting home watching television. But they're having a big discussion now because they don't understand why she refuses to sit down for just an interview.\par \par \uc2 And they have got a lot of friendly -- it's much easier being a Democrat because you see all the fake news back there? If you're a Democrat, they say he just gave the greatest speech that we have ever heard. Not since FDR has there been a speech.\par \par \uc2 I saw this with Biden. He made a State of the Union address. It was so bad. Oh, it was so bad. I saw her make a speech. It was so bad. And after they get off, this was one of the greatest performances we have ever seen in our country.\par \par \uc2 With me, I make a speech, I speak for two hours. Everybody loves it. I got thousands of people, by the way, outside trying to get in. I never -- they never said Trump's a great speaker. I don't even want that. But I must be a great speaker, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I must. We have got thousands of people.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, we have got thousands and thousands. Well, if I were a Democrat, they'd say he's the greatest that ever lived. He made Abraham Lincoln look like nothing.\par \par \uc2 But, no, I don't care about that. I care about winning for our country. I care about making America great again. And that's what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : So we're pleased to be joined today by some really great winners, champions, patriots.\par \par \uc2 And one of them is Lisa McClain, congresswoman. Where's Lisa?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Hi, Lisa. You are doing amazing.\par \par \uc2 Don't be fooled by her. She's a very nice person, but she's brutal.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : When it comes to your state, when it comes to jobs, right, Lisa? And she's proud of it too. Yes, she is. Thank you. Great job.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And another man that I picked out of a large group, I said, this guy's got what it takes. He's a highly respected person. He's running for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . And I think he's going to win. I just saw a poll where he's leading by a little bit. It's not an easy race. It's not an easy race.\par \par \uc2 But the other one is going to be just a disaster. She's going to vote everything that whoever it would be, except me. They won't. But we -- hopefully, we're not going to need it. But she's a disaster.\par \par \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2  is one of the most respected people in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And he will be, I believe, your next senator. Great guy.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He's one -- and he's actually one of the most respected people in Washington. And he came out of a large group of Republicans that all wanted the same job. And he really did. He ran a great race, won easily.\par \par \uc2 He had my endorsement. That helped.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But he did. He won easily. And he's got great gravitas.\par \par \uc2 Your next congressman, and you're going to have a great congressman, who I think is going to win, Tom Barrett. Tom.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you, Tom. Great job. I heard you're doing well, Tom. Good. Running against somebody that's not good, not good for your state, not good for our country.\par \par \uc2 Michigan House Republican Leader Matt Hall.\par \par \uc2 Are we going to win, Matt? That's -- he gave me a good summary. He said: "You're going to win, sir."\par \par \uc2 They have these fake polls. They're almost as bad as the fake writers, the fake news. We interviewed 49 Democrats for a poll. We interviewed 49 Democrats and 21 Republicans. It's a little thing on the bottom. I'm surprised they even put it. They probably -- must be a law.\par \par \uc2 But they put it 49 Democrats and 21 Republicans. And the race is tied. They said tied. How the hell can it be tied? So tied means I'm leading by like 14 or 15 points. I don't know. But it's fake news. It's just -- it's as bad -- it's as bad as they are with a pen. I'm telling you, it's really bad. They can make those polls sing. They can make them do whatever they want.\par \par \uc2 Michigan Senate Republican leader Aric Nesbitt. Aric, thank you. Great job. What a beautiful young daughter. What a beautiful young daughter. Great. Thanks, Aric. Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 We also have some people of great distinction. Justice David Viviano is here with us. David, thank you, David.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Highly respect justice. Thank you, David.\par \par \uc2 Judge Patrick O'Grady. Patrick is here someplace.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Where's Patrick? Another one highly respected. So whatever you're doing, keep it going. Good.\par \par \uc2 And Tudor Dixon, who really ran a good race.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Where is Tudor? Well, hi, Tudor. She's a great person. She loves this state and loves the workers. I knew her father. Her father was in the steel business, successful guy. I knew him as he was leaving our planet, right, leaving. He was very sick and leaving.\par \par \uc2 But he loved his daughter. He loved his -- he was so proud of you. So he's up there watching.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Tudor. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: And Brian Pannebecker, who's like a friend of mine, this guy. Where is he? Where's Brian? Where is he? You want to come up?\par \par \uc2 You -- come on up here, Brian. This guy, he thinks the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  have the worst leader and that they're crazy. They're going to be driven out of business. All the business is going to China. Within two, three years you're not going to have autoworkers in this. If they vote for Trump, we're going to -- we're going to bring in factories at levels that you have never seen before.\par \par \uc2 We're going to get the autoworkers' jobs back like it was 30 years ago and 40 years ago before everybody left. We're going to get it back at levels that you have never seen.\par \par \uc2 Brian, say hello, please. Hey, Brian.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN PANNEBECKER, RETIRED AUTO WORKER: Thank you Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 And the last time you called me up on stage in Grand Rapids, it was the first rally that you would held since the assassination attempt. And I -- honestly, I was speechless.\par \par \uc2 But, tonight, I want to say a couple things real briefly. I represent the Auto Workers for \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , which was a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  group. I started that group. I started that group -- I started that group...\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 PANNEBECKER: Thank you. There's some autoworkers here I'm sure. Lansing has got a lot of them. But, unfortunately, a lot of them have lost their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Listen, Chrysler and \uc2 General Motors\uc2  have already gone bankrupt once. But if we allow electric vehicle mandates to be put on this industry by the federal government, they're going out of business again. We got to fight against that. And this guy standing right here is the only person who will do it.\par \par \uc2 We cannot allow the federal government to put mandates on the auto industry to build electric vehicles when the consumers do not want them.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 PANNEBECKER: So I'm asking everybody. I'm asking autoworkers and I'm asking everybody that supplies the auto industry, get 10 or 15 of your friends, make sure they get out and vote.\par \par \uc2 Our very survival depends on it and we can't allow it to happen. So vote Trump and J.D. Vance, and we will turn this country around. He's done it once. Unfortunately, he's going to have to go back and do it again.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: As he's speaking, look at the arms on this guy. Wait a minute.\par \par \uc2 PANNEBECKER: Oh, cut it out. Cut it out.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 PANNEBECKER: Cut it out.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Now let me see.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He's really great and a real patriot. And it's true.\par \par \uc2 They're building, I don't know if you know, numerous of the biggest auto plants in the world right now. And that's near, right near the border in Mexico, owned by China. They think they're going to build the cars and send them in.\par \par \uc2 And with this administration, they won't even know what's happening. They will take every single job. You're not going to have any autoworkers within two years, maybe three years. Nobody's going to be making cars here. I told them, if you do that, we're going to put tariffs on at 200, 250 percent. You're never going to sell one car in this country and they didn't build them.\par \par \uc2 And then as soon as I left, because of a bad situation that happened, because we did great in this state too.\par \par \uc2 PANNEBECKER: Yes, you won it.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But a bad situation that happened, corrupt, bad situation, you have to keep your eyes open this time.\par \par \uc2 PANNEBECKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But they used COVID to cheat. Very simple. They used COVID to cheat. But I told them. I said, you do it, you're not going to sell any cars. As soon as I was gone, they started construction and they're almost finished and that's going to wipe you out.\par \par \uc2 They're the biggest plants anywhere in the world. A friend of mine builds plants. And I think he's building these. I said, I want to see some big plants. I want to go on tour some of the big auto plants. That's all he does. Honestly, he's a great guy. Can't walk across the street because he can build a plant like nobody.\par \par \uc2 He said, sir, you're going to have to come to Mexico. I said, no, I want to see one in the United States. He said, the big ones are being built in Mexico. That's no good. And we're not going to let it happen. We're not going to -- they can build their plant. They're not going to have any cars coming into this country. If they want to build a plant, they can.\par \par \uc2 They're going to build it in Michigan or at least some place in our country.\par \par \uc2 PANNEBECKER: Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 PANNEBECKER: The \uc2 UAW\uc2  members -- the \uc2 UAW\uc2  members realize this now. hand I'm doing a rally at a different auto plant every week. I started in June. We're going to go all the way to November.\par \par \uc2 And we're talking to the autoworkers as they come off their shift and go into work and 70 or 80 percent of them are voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because they know their jobs depend on it.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, that guy's -- he's a dynamic guy, isn't he, huh?\par \par \uc2 No, it's really -- look, I didn't know Brian. He just knows that we had four great years. But I got an award years ago in Michigan. I got man of the year a long time ago. In fact, the fake news said, I never got it, I never got it, I made it up. I didn't make it up, and then they found out, I actually did get it.\par \par \uc2 I was lucky they found out who it was. It was like a Chamber of Commerce someplace. And I gave a whole speech. This was probably 15 years ago, 17 years ago, I gave a speech on, why are you letting them steal your auto manufacturing business? And it only got worse and worse and worse, then I stopped it. And then as soon as I was gone, they let it happen.\par \par \uc2 And these plants that are being built all around the world, they are going to take all of your business. And I'm a big fan of electric cars, but they're limited. They don't go far, they're very expensive, and they're going to all be made in China because they have the material.\par \par \uc2 But we have more liquid gold under our feet than anybody else. It's called gasoline for what we talk about. And we want to build gasoline-propelled cars, but we also want to build hybrids, and we want to build electric cars.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , I think he's great, and he endorsed me very strongly. I said, listen, I'm all for electric cars, but not all of them, because they really don't go far yet, but they're incredible. The job he's done and the job that others have done are great. And it's called a market. You have to be able to buy what you want to buy. There will be another form of car. They talk about hydrogen now.\par \par \uc2 They talk about all sorts of things, but it's called a market. But we can't do that. They want to put, like, immediately all electric cars. But we don't have enough electricity to supply ourselves right now. You go out to California, which was destroyed by Kamala, destroyed.\par \par \uc2 She destroyed the city of San Francisco. It's -- and I own a big building there. It's no -- I shouldn't talk about this, but that's OK. I don't give a damn, because this is what I'm doing.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I should say it's the finest city in the world, sell and get the hell out of there, right? But I can't do that. I don't care, you know? I lost billions -- billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 You know, somebody said, what do you think he lost? I said, probably $2 billion, $3 billion. That's OK. I don't care. They said, do you think you would do it again? And that's the least of it. Nobody -- they always say that -- I don't know if you know, Lincoln was horribly treated. Jefferson was pretty horribly. Andrew Jackson, they say, was the worst of all, that he was treated worse than any other president.\par \par \uc2 And I said, do that study again, because I think there's nobody close to Trump. I even got shot. And who the hell knows where that came from, right? No, these are bad people. They're sick people. We have people on the outside, the countries, but if you have a smart president, the countries will be an asset, not a liability.\par \par \uc2 That includes China. That includes Russia, if you have a smart president. But we don't have a smart president right now, by the way. Going to end up in World War II. This period of time better happen fast, because we have nobody running our country. We have nobody at the Resolute Desk. There's nobody, the two of them.\par \par \uc2 She's no good, and he's sleeping. In fact, I'm going to go back to the name for him sleepy Joe, instead of crooked Joe. Did you see they found, last week, Brian, he took $28 million. They say he stole -- the family took $28 million, and nothing happened. That was the night he made his brilliant speech, which was all about Trump.\par \par \uc2 Everything they do, they talk about Trump. They talk about Trump, I think, 347 times. They talked about the border once. They talked about crime twice. They talked about all of the things that you should be talking about not at all, but they talk about Trump.\par \par \uc2 And, look, we did something that they're very angry about. We beat somebody that should have been beaten in an election that we weren't anticipated to win, but I thought we were going to win, because I came to Michigan the night before. We had 49,000 people. She came to Michigan the night before, because she was told she may lose it, and this was an upset so they had a fast tour.\par \par \uc2 She had like 300 people. They said, why would we lose? We had the crowd. You wouldn't have believed it. Half of you were probably there. But we're also joined by a very incredible service member. I presented him with a thing called the Congressional Medal of Honor, and he is a fantastic man, and he's a good-looking guy.\par \par \uc2 I don't know. He looks better than he did when I presented something good. He's doing good, Specialist 5 James McCloughan. Where's James? James?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you, James. You look good. Good head of hair. Look at him. Got that good Irish, Scottish, whatever, hair. Look at that guy. Stand up. Just stand up. Come here. I'm proud of him.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I wouldn't have done what he did. I know what he did. What he did. I always say, there are two awards. There's the Medal of Honor, and oftentimes there's posthumously where they're not there.\par \par \uc2 Oftentimes, they have died in battle and they're getting the award. And then you have the civilian version, the Presidential Medal. And I always say I'd rather get the Presidential Medal, because the guys that came in, other than you and a few others, oftentimes, they have suffered greatly, right? They have suffered greatly or they're not around.\par \par \uc2 But it's our highest award. And it's an honor to have you here. And I'm very proud that I was able to give you the award.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, James.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : In four short years under my leadership, we passed record tax cuts, record regulation cuts, and built the greatest economy in the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 I ended NAFTA, the worst trade deal ever made you. People had to suffer with it for years.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And replaced it with the USMCA. That's Mexico and Canada, which they say is the best trade deal ever made. Actually, the best deal I made probably was with China, but I don't talk about it because once COVID came in, that was the end of China, once they did that to the world, not to us, to the whole world, $59 trillion of damages and millions and millions of people.\par \par \uc2 And it should have been -- it came out of Wuhan, the lab. Came out of the labs. And that's where I said it came from. And it was incompetence. It was a mistake. I believe it was a mistake. A lot of people don't think so. A lot of people think they did it because I was getting hundreds of billions of dollars in tariffs from China.\par \par \uc2 No president had ever gotten anything from China. I got hundreds of billions of dollars in tariffs and saved a lot of jobs by doing that, because they ended up not being able to do what they were doing.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240829ek8t0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Israel And Hezbollah Exchange Strikes; Interview With Retired Lieutenant General Keith Kellogg; RFK Jr. Suspends Presidential Campaign, Endorses Trump; Interview With \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 ; Harris Proposes $25k Down Payment Assistance For First-Time Homebuyers; Growing Calls for Harris to Talk to Media; Patriotism A Major Theme At DNC; Harris-Walz Momentum Out Of Convention; Impact Of Abortion Policy Debate On Election; Investigations into Trump Assassination; Israel and Hezbollah Exchange Strikes; U.S. to Bolster Forces in the Middle East; Update on the Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Strikes\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Keith Kellogg, Lucas Tomlinson, Mollie Hemingway,  Richard Fowler, Trey Yingst \par \uc2 10,264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Israel launches what it calls a preemptive strike on Iran-backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militants in Lebanon, sparking fears of an all-out regional war in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Israel says it eliminated thousands of rockets, but \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  counters it still managed to launch hundreds of warheads and drones to avenge the killing of one of its top commanders last month. We'll take you live to Israel.\par \par \uc2 Then, shake up in the final weeks of the race as \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  suspends his long shot campaign and throws his support behind former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after a long conversation with him.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (I), SUSPENDED 2024 PRESIDENTIAL RUN: We talked about -- not about the things that separate us because we don't agree on everything, but on the values and the issues that bind us together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Could RFK Jr.'s supporters make the key difference for Trump in battleground states where the race is neck and neck against Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? We'll talk exclusively with Kennedy in his first interview since his big announcements.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Trump and Harris battle over two of the biggest issues for voters.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Every day brings another story about innocent Americans being tortured, raped, murdered and massacred by illegal aliens that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has set free in our country.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He doesn't actually fight for the middle class. Instead, he fights for himself and his billionaire friends.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we learned this week that the U.S. economy added nearly a million fewer jobs than previously reported. We'll get reaction from Democratic Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  of Colorado.\par \par \uc2 And our Sunday panel is here to give us their take on the next phase in the race for the White House.\par \par \uc2 All this week on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin in the Middle East where the \uc2 Israeli Defense Force\uc2  says 100 of its fighter jets hit more than 40 \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  launch areas in a pre-dawn strike today, ahead of what it called an extensive attack planned by Iran-backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militants in Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to FOX News chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, Israel launched preemptive strikes against the \uc2 Lebanese militant group Hezbollah\uc2 . The Israeli military says \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  was prepared to launch hundreds of rockets and missiles toward central and northern Israel when the decision was made to strike first. A senior Israeli defense official described the strikes as precise and meant to remove imminent threats as large explosions rock southern Lebanon, the Iran back group began attacking northern Israel. Sirens sounded in communities as deep as 20 miles from the border as Israel's missile defense system worked to intercept the incoming fire.\par \par \uc2 We are learning more this morning about the preemptive Israeli strikes. The \uc2 Israel Defense Forces\uc2  say more than 100 fighter jets took part in the air strikes and eliminated thousands of targets.\par \par \uc2 Initially, Israel's international airport was closed but has since reopened. Israel has now entered a special security situation for the next 48 hours. Now, despite the heightened tension along the northern border, today, ceasefire talks are set to resume in Cairo, Egypt. Both parties looking to work out the details of a possible agreement to end the war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Just last hour, a senior Israeli defense official telling FOX News they are hopeful still about the possibility of a deal -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Trey Yingst, we are going to check in with you later on in the hour. Thank you very much\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Lieutenant General Keith Kellogg.\par \par \uc2 General, welcome back. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of what we've seen over the last 24 hours? There's some say it's provocative. Others say it's a real push back to shut down what could have happened.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET), FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER TO TRUMP, PENCE: Yeah, Shannon. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 I thought it was a great move. Look, the preemptive strike was very, very important because if they had launched the strike -- they the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  -- into -- in Israel with thousands of missiles and they were talking thousands, what would have happened with the loss of lives of Israelis and also the facilities?\par \par \uc2 It would have pushed the Israelis into a doctrine they call Dahiya, and what Dahiya is called disproportionate response.\par \par \uc2 And you -- I think what they did, they prevented and the next or the third Israeli-Lebanese war, and that is smart. And because of that, I think it's kind of reset the battlefield there.\par \par \uc2 And it's -- it's important they did it. I think they did it quite well. The attacks will continue. They're just diminishing the ability of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to attack.\par \par \uc2 You know I have to go back when you think about what what's happened there is really the United States hasn't played a major role there in kind of de- escalating the situation because President Biden hasn't done a lot in talking to Netanyahu. Vice President Harris hasn't done very much. I mean she actually stiffed Netanyahu when he came here into the joint session of Congress when he spoke. And you've got Blinken going over there on his ninth trip no ceasefire. And you got the National Security Advisor Jake Sullivan headed to China instead of going to the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know CQ Brown's going to be there, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, but you kind of got to focus in on what's going to happen there and prevent something happening in into the future. And they haven't done it.\par \par \uc2 But the Israelis have done it. So the Israelis have done actually is reset deterrence in the region and I think it's smart. And I think it's put \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  on their -- on their back foot, and it's clearly put the Iranians on the back foot, that they haven't done anything at all.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well -- so we do have assets in the region. We beefed up our presence.\par \par \uc2 This administration keeps publicly saying Israel has every right to defend itself and we will back them in that, but where do we go from here? Because as you mentioned, we've still got the threat out there looming from Iran saying we're going to take action when we decide. You've got \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And never mind what's going on in Gaza -- I mean, Israel is stretched in a lot of different positions.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yeah, the problem you get is if something like this extends which it's done, it makes it harder to put back in the box to make it go -- put it in into a situation which is sustainable and peace in the region. And that is because technically (ph) we kind of backed away and we said, well, there's pox on both sides of the house.\par \par \uc2 Wrong. The way you handle that is you tell the entire region, you tell the world, we are behind the Israelis, full stop. There's a good side and a bad side and we need to do it.\par \par \uc2 Because we haven't done it, we've created a seam, a gap, and adversaries look for seams and gaps and they've basically done that.\par \par \uc2 And so, they say, okay, we can attack because the United States is not going to come back hard. And I think how you have to reset the fight, you have to tell everybody in the region who we're for and who we're not for and we really haven't done that.\par \par \uc2 So it's going to take a bit to put it back in the box because \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is going to keep attacking, Iran's going to keep supporting the attackers in \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  fight is virtually over. I think Israelis has kind of solved that problem. Look, when the leader of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has said, I want -- if we have peace discussions and we have a ceasefire, I want to make sure that I'm not a target -- Sinwar.\par \par \uc2 Nope, you started that fight. You're -- you know, you're at the risk -- at risk right now, but I think that fight is virtually over now. They -- Israelis can pivot to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and we know those ceasefire negotiations will continue in the coming days in Egypt, primarily.\par \par \uc2 General, thank you. Always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Let's get reaction to all of this from the White House where we find FOX News correspondent Lucas Tomlinson.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Officials say national security adviser Jake Sullivan kept President Biden updated throughout the night during the attack and \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , President Biden's defense secretary, spoke to his Israeli counterpart, offering continued support of those two aircraft carrier strike groups now on station in the region.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  says this is not the Democratic Party of his uncle and father.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: It have become the party of war, censorship, corruption, big pharma, big tech, big ag and big money.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): After endorsing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for president, the DNC responded: Like RFK Jr. Donald Trump is at a low point and acting out of desperation, embracing RFK Jr. now when he has nothing to offer but months of disqualifying revelations is not a decision a campaign makes when they're acting from a position of strength.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says the economy is strong.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Record 16 million new jobs.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Turns out that's about 1 million jobs too many, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  in a revised report that says there were $818,000 fewer jobs than previously estimated.\par \par \uc2 One number not in dispute, grocery prices up 20 percent since Biden and Harris came into office.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS HOST: Should she accept any blame for that?\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, HARRIS-WALZ CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: I -- no -- I think overwhelmingly what people are seeing is that the United States got through the recovery with greater jobs, greater growth.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: With the Democrats gathered in Chicago, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made another visit to the border, in the swing state of Arizona, flanked by members of the border patrol and families who have lost loved ones to illegal aliens, including the mother of a -year-old girl strangled to death in Houston.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We had a border czar who was -- the border czar, she loved the title but she didn't want to do the work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: RFK Jr. also said if the system was honest, he would have won this election -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas Tomlinson reporting from the White House -- Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy\uc2 , Jr.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 . (I), SUSPENDED 2024 PRESIDENTIAL RUN: Shannon, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So let's get this out of the way up front. You and President Trump not always fans of each other. He posted on social media earlier this year: He's one of the most liberal lunatics ever to run for office. A phony radical left fool whose poll numbers are terrible and getting worse.\par \par \uc2 "The New Yorker" said just a couple of weeks ago this: In a recent text exchange, Kennedy told one person that Trump was a terrible human being, the worst president ever and barely human. He is probably a sociopath.\par \par \uc2 And I haven't read your text so you can speak to that\par \par \uc2 But how did you get from that position to Friday night?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, you know, I -- it became clear to me that I did not have a path to victory. Sixteen months of censorship, of not being able to get on any network really except for FOX, and I had -- in fact, when -- when \uc2 Ross Perot\uc2  ran in the 10 months that he ran, he had 34 appearances on the networks. I had two appearances in months.\par \par \uc2 I was blocked out the networks. I was blocked out from the debate. I had no path to victory.\par \par \uc2 President Trump had been reaching out to me and I talked to him a few hours after the assassination attempt and we had a long conversation by phone. I then had two extensive meetings and there were issues -- the broad issues that were most important to me the ones that brought me into the campaign which was ending the Ukraine war, ending the censorship and protecting children's health. We're all into reforming our food supply, all the things that we need to do to make our children healthy again.\par \par \uc2 Those are all things that President Trump also wanted to work on, and he invited me to form a unity government. We agreed that we'd be able to continue to criticize each other on issues on which we don't agree. But these issues are so important and their way of unifying our country.\par \par \uc2 We need in this country to reach a point where we love our children more than we hate each other.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's a good, unifying theme that I think a lot of people could get around. It's how we get there that's always the devil is in the details.\par \par \uc2 Have you all negotiated over or talked about a cabinet position, another position within a Trump government in exchange for your endorsement?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: No, there was -- there's no commitments but I -- you know, I met with President Trump, with his family, with his closest advisors and we just made a general commitment that we were going to work together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What about the states where you're trying to get off of a number of swing state ballots to say, I'm going to stay on a number of state ballots but in these swing states, I'm going to try to withdraw myself and ask my supporters -- or at least you're telling them think about supporting President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Did you negotiate over which states you would try to withdraw from?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Yeah, I mean, we all knew which states they were. There basically is 10 swing states where my presence in the race would have helped Vice President Harris and would have harmed President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So I'm going to get off the ballot in those states and then we're going to stay on the ballot in 30 states and, you know, I'm encouraging people to vote for me in those states. Those are states with the votes -- they're either all red or all blue states where their votes are not going to change the outcome of the race.\par \par \uc2 And -- but in the -- in the red -- in the states where I would have been spoiler, I'm going to get out. It's about 10 states.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So there were reports a couple weeks ago citing an official from your camp and also a Democratic official saying that there had been outreach to the Harris camp as well to possibly discuss you working within the Harris administration in exchange for an endorsement of her.\par \par \uc2 Is that true? And what happened with that?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: It -- you know, I reached out to them on the same basis that I reached out to President Trump and I -- we actually talked to other presidential candidates, including Chase Oliver in the Libertarian Party about figuring out ways that we could end the polarization and the hatred and the vitriol, start talking about issues.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I -- and I would have -- I was welcome to listen to anything from the Democrats that they were going to do something about the war -- about the Ukraine war, about the censorship, about the child health crisis, this epidemic of chronic disease that is now disabled about 60 percent of our kids.\par \par \uc2 And you know, when my uncle is President Shannon, only 6 percent of Americans had chronic disease. Today, over 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it's hard to find a kid today that's not been damaged by it, and it's coming from our food supply, from contain -- from pollution in our environment, from toxics in our environment, and mainly from corruption in our government that allows that to happen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've talked about or there's been discussion that if you were to join the Trump administration in some health related position, that you have real interest in dismantling things like the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , the CDC, \uc2 NIH\uc2 . You would like to see focus away from infectious diseases to what you're talking about more chronic diseases.\par \par \uc2 Is it fair to say that you would try to dismantle some of those organizations?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: No, I wouldn't dismantle them. I would change the focus and I would end the corruption. I would -- right now, 75 percent of \uc2 FDA\uc2 's budget is coming from pharmaceutical companies. That is a perverse incentive.\par \par \uc2 In \uc2 NIH\uc2 , the -- if you -- scientists and officials in \uc2 NIH\uc2  who work on drug development, who incubate drugs for the pharmaceutical company, get to collect lifetime royalties from those products. These are regulators. They're supposed to be looking for problems in those products. We have these agencies that have become sock puppets for the industries they're supposed to regulate. They're not really interested in public health.\par \par \uc2 Everybody, the most profitable thing today in America is a sick child. Everybody is making money. The hospitals are making money. The pharmaceutical companies are making money. Even the insurance companies make money.\par \par \uc2 And we need to end those perverse incentives. We need to get the corruption out of \uc2 FDA\uc2 , out of \uc2 NIH\uc2 , out of the CDC and make them function as they're supposed to function, which is to protect public health and particularly children's health.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let me go back to your decision from Friday. Both you and Nicole Shanahan, your running mate, have talked about how you feel like the Democrats completely mistreated you, excluded you from the process. How much of your decision Friday is about revenge or motivated by punishing Democrats in some way?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Zero. I really -- I don't act in anything in my life. I don't act out of anger or revenge or resentment. It's a -- it's a bad motivation. It's like swallowing poison and hoping someone else will die. And so I don't do it.\par \par \uc2 I'm -- you know, I am very pragmatic and practical and I am focused on one thing, which is how do we restore health for our children? And that touches many, many other issues. How do we regenerate our soils in this country? How do we protect small family farmers? The incentives are all going to commodity agriculture and that's what is feeding this chronic disease epidemic. The processed foods that are created by commodity agriculture.\par \par \uc2 So we need to do all of these things at once. Protect small farmers, restore our soils, regenerative agriculture and to get the corruption out of the federal government and -- and make these agencies do what they were created to do, which is to protect our health and not serve the mercantile interests of the pharmaceutical companies in the processed food industry.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: This decision is not without personal cost for you. Your own family went to the White House on St. Patrick's Day, took a huge picture with President Biden, made clear who they were supporting. Friday night, your siblings issued this statement.\par \par \uc2 "We believe in Harris and Walz. Our brother Bobby's decision to endorse Trump today is a betrayal of the values that our father and our family hold most dear."\par \par \uc2 You posted on "X" Friday night saying, you're grateful to your amazing wife because the decision that you've made is one that she's uncomfortable with. So talk to us about the personal backlash you have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Yeah, I mean, you know, my family is at the center of the Democratic Party. I have five members of my family that are working for the Biden administration. President Biden has a -- a -- a bust of my father behind him at the Oval Office. He's been a family friend for many, many years.\par \par \uc2 And my family is -- I understand that they're troubled by my decisions, but you know, I love my family. I feel like we were raised in a milieu where we were encouraged to debate each other and debate ferociously and passionately about things, but just still love each other.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, they can -- they're free to take their positions on these issues. There are many, many members of my family who are working in my campaign, who are supporting me. I have a very big family. There's a few of them that are troubled, but you know, I think we all need to be able to disagree with each other and still love each other.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "Wall Street Journal" has this. They say it's a two -- a double- edged sword, essentially, that it may help President Trump to pick up some of your followers if they're willing to go over there, but they warn about what you would do in his administration. They say this, "RFK Jr., if he's anywhere near the health care or environmental agencies in a Trump administration, look out. Mr. Trump's best response is to thank RFK Jr. for his support, make no promises about the future, and by all means avoid joint campaign appearances."\par \par \uc2 You've already had one. Will you have others? Will you campaign for President Trump?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Yeah, I'm going to be campaigning actively. I think President Trump is going to make a series of announcements about other Democrats who are joining his campaign, and you know, I want to make America healthy again, and so does President Trump. So those are objectives.\par \par \uc2 My -- "The Wall Street Journal" may be worried about shareholder value for pharmaceutical companies, and I think, you know, I talked specifically to President Trump about that issue, and he said that he wanted to leave, as his legacy, healthy children, and that, you know, that he was unconcerned about the other implications.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , thank you for your time today. We'll see you on the campaign trail then.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Shannon, thanks so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, the economy takes center stage in the race for the White House, as we learn the U.S. created nearly a million fewer jobs than previously reported.\par \par \uc2 Colorado Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , the Harris-Walz campaign surrogate, joins us to talk about that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Fresh off the convention, we are still awaiting more details on Democratic presidential nominee Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' plans to turn around the economy. If elected, though, one of the things she says is Harris is proposing to give $25,000 to some first-time homebuyers. Not yet clear exactly how that would work and some experts actually feel it could inflate the market for homes.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Colorado Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , also a surrogate for the Harris-Walz campaign and somebody who has great concerns about the housing market.\par \par \uc2 Governor, good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D-CO), HARRIS-WALZ SURROGATE: Good to be back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's start with that proposal. There was some skepticism across the board about how it would work and get paid for. Here's \uc2 Kevin O'Leary\uc2 , Shark Tank fame, business investor. Here's his take.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN O'LEARY\uc2 , "SHARK TANK" INVESTOR: There are bad ideas, bad, bad ideas, and really bad ideas and that falls into this category. When you give $25,000 to anybody in a constrained market, you cause inflation. So if there's three houses for sale on the street and everybody bidding on it gets another $25,000, all of that attributes to the seller and you cause the price of the house to go up because there's no supply.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, knowing that this is an issue you've worked desperately on to try to get these things under control, what do you make of the criticism of this plan?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, look, if that was her entire housing plan, then some of that would be valid. That's a small piece of it that some of the opponents have glommed on to. The key piece of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's housing plan is removing barriers and red tape and regulations that prevent housing from being built, effectively streamlining the process of building homes, allowing the quantity of homes to increase, especially the kinds of starter homes that people can afford, $200,000, $300,000. That's the bulk of her plan. That $25,000, if it's a way to move renters into owners, that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 We live in an ownership society. That's how people build wealth. If people, instead of having their monthly check go every month to rent, if they can have a good mortgage and build equity, that's good. But of course, not alone. It needs to be coupled with, and it is under \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' detailed housing plan, increasing the quantity of housing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so she does want to get to, I think the goal is 300 -- excuse me, 3 million new homes in four years, but you talk about the barriers that exist to getting those homes built.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " says this, she's going to have to deal with regulations. The only real way to get there is via the one promise Harris will find hardest to keep, that is to make it much easier for builders to build. They go on to say actually getting local governments to expedite reviews is as much a management problem as a legal one, and her administration cannot micromanage every county seat and town hall. You alluded to that problem.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Yeah, and we have similar issues in Colorado getting rid of, you know, local red tape to get things built. We welcome the help of the federal government. Most Americans looking for housing affordability, they don't care if it's their mayor, their governor, or their president that does it, they just want it done, and President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is really speaking to that.\par \par \uc2 So I think you can look forward to how we can effectively tie in a wide variety of federal incentives to these kinds of land-use zoning reforms that effectively reduce red tape, reduce the size of local government, and make it easier to build homes. That's what's going to lead to the millions of new homes and more affordability for the American people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and I know that Colorado, we've talked with the Denver mayor and with others there, that -- that part of the issue with housing is this influx of a lot of new people showing up.\par \par \uc2 "The Daily Caller" says this, "The U.S. currently has an estimated shortage of 4 million to 7 million housing units. The recent influx of illegal immigrants is putting even more stress on the system by aggravating the current shortage of supply in the market at a rate that developers can't keep up with."\par \par \uc2 Under this administration, we have had millions of new people come to this country, and in a way that it is unregulated, it doesn't follow our laws, and it is impacting housing markets. Is that part of the problem in Colorado and beyond?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Look, there's no doubt we'd be in a better place today if former President Trump hadn't torpedoed the bipartisan border security plan that would have finally locked down the border to provide real security --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, but that comes three and a half years into this administration. You're repealing executive orders and doing all kinds of things that led us to these historically high numbers.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, look, I mean, Democrats have supported border security for decades. I supported it in Congress, sponsored bills to do it, you know, and again, we can't let politics stand in the way of solving this, and so while, again, there might be blame on both sides, the most recent attempt to fix it was clearly torpedoed by former President Trump, and that's why we have a chaotic border today. Very clear, that's the matter.\par \par \uc2 Of course, look --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, well you know that there are a number of senators we've had on this show and beyond who will say that they weren't going to vote for that bill regardless of whether President Trump liked it or not. They had real concerns with it in the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, the key question to ask them then is what amendments would you like to make to improve it? I mean, any idea that improves border security --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, they'll say they were allowed to make amendments.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: -- I'm sure Democrats are for. Well, then that's their problem, but look, that's what they're there for. We want ideas from the left and the right, but again, the key thing is the border would be locked down today if not for President Trump thwarting that effort, preventing that effort to provide border security.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wait, wait, wait. OK, but you --\par \par \uc2 POLIS: But again, there's plenty of blame on both sides for over a decade on this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is it fair to say that the bill was not going to lock down the whole border? I mean, in all honesty, I mean, that bipartisan plan was not going to completely stem the tide of what's happening at the border.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: It would have cut it by at least two-thirds, and look, if there's a way to do it a hundred percent, I'm sure that President Harris can deliver on that once she has the opportunity to serve. This is somebody who fought crime, she's a prosecutor, she's put criminals behind bars, she knows how to keep people safe, and that's exactly what we need here.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the dimension connecting it to the housing. Of course, that's a small part of it. I mean, housing costs have gone up in Colorado long before the recent influx here. The average home price in the Denver metro area, $600,000. Why? Because it's a great place to live. Why does it cost that much? Because we've artificially constrained the ability to build housing because of government interference. We need to remove that government interference, allow the housing to be built, and that's what Vice President Harris' plan is all about.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's talk about that because we would like to see more from her. We're all waiting for a sit-down interview, a press conference. We look forward to the debate on September 10th. We'd love to host additional ones. But in the meantime, we're sort of left to guess where she is on a number of things, where it appears she's changed positions on fracking and the Green New Deal, gun buybacks, mandatory gun buybacks, all kinds of things that we get a trickle from people within her campaign who, you know, off the record are giving us guidance about where she is now. Is it time for her to sit down, give us more information, put a policy primer on her website?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, I'm sure that she'll be doing a lot more interviews in the future. Of course, the debate is going to be very exciting. She's already taking questions at a number of different venues as she's moving in and out, but of course, the truth of the matter is, yes, she's come to the middle. She's pragmatic. She's a tough leader. She's the leader we need for the future.\par \par \uc2 So again, while she may have in the past ran on things just as President Trump ran on single-payer, supported single-payer health care years ago and has moved away from that, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did support things that she's now moderated. She's going to be a president for all the American people.\par \par \uc2 Look, whether you're conservative, whether you're moderate, whether you're liberal, I think you're going to see a lot of your priorities reflected in President Harris, who's really just out for the people, as she said, fighting to make housing more affordable, improve our schools, make education more affordable and strengthen the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, speaking of the jobs market, we talked about we got this downgrade in the year ending March 2024, 818,000 jobs that didn't actually happen.\par \par \uc2 There's been a revision there, Alfred Ortiz, the CEO of Job Creators Network says this, the labor market is far weaker than top-line numbers suggest and has been dominated by government and quasi government jobs. This fuller view of the jobs market nullifies a year's worth of exaggerated headlines that have helped the Biden-Harris administration present a false picture of economic strength.\par \par \uc2 It may be why in the polling, while the administration will say things are getting better, they are moving in the right direction, people say it's not their reality. I mean, they're paying 20 to 30 percent more for household items over January 2021.\par \par \uc2 Does president -- Vice President Harris plan to break with the Biden administration policies?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, right now, nationally, we have about 1.2 unemployed people for -- I'm sorry, 1.2 job openings for every unemployed person.\par \par \uc2 So there are more job openings than unemployed people. That doesn't mean that there's not a skills gap, meaning some of the unemployed people while there are jobs might need additional training. Three weeks, six weeks, whatever it is.\par \par \uc2 I know that President -- Vice President Harris has been a big fan of making sure the people have access to the training that they need to get the good jobs that are out there today.\par \par \uc2 I'm also excited, of course, by \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' middle class tax cut, putting more money back in the hands of middle class families, increasing their buying power. Very strong contrast to former President Trump's tariff tax plan that would increase the cost of household items by 10 to 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll wait to hear from both of them because we need details on both those things. These claims about the tariff situation and also the economic plans of Vice President. We look forward to more details and we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Governor Polis being with us.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Thank you. Take care.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OK. The 2024 showdown, Harris and Trump, both back on the campaign trail this week, making stops in crucial battleground states that will likely determine the outcome of the entire election.\par \par \uc2 Our panel is here to break it down as the campaigns gear up for that first Trump-Harris debate showdown, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I will be a president who unites us around our highest aspirations and always fights for the American people.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: When we win this November, we will end the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  border nightmare, once and for all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  accepting the democratic presidential nomination this week, as former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took his campaign to the border.\par \par \uc2 It's time now for our Sunday group. Francesca Chambers, "USA Today" White House correspondent. "The Federalist" editor-in-chief and Fox News contributor, Mollie Hemingway. Richard Fowler, Fox News contributor and Forbes contributing writer. And former \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  National Security official, \uc2 Michael Allen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right. Welcome, everybody. Everybody who made it back from the DNC. We all still need naps.\par \par \uc2 But let's talk about what happened there. There was a lot of messaging. It was interesting because it very much was the vice president is the challenger and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the incumbent. It was kind of one of the vibes we got.\par \par \uc2 We also have this. From "The Hill." Democrats try to take patriotism back from the GOP. It says USA chants, camo and American flag hats filled the convention in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 And when Vice President Harris wrapped up her speech, a balloon drop of red, white, and blue was met with dozens of giant American flags.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, was there -- in the United Center and they had this very organized group that would take out the signs and the flags, whatever they were going to be, for that particular night. And they had, I would say, thousands of flags in there.\par \par \uc2 Was this a purposeful shift in some way? Because at some points we've been told that the American flag was a symbol of not so positive things.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Well, I do think they focused on the theme of patriotism to your point. And, of course, people have commented on like the \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  camo hats as well.\par \par \uc2 With regards to her speech itself though, I think there were three main goals for the vice president that night, party, unity, reintroduce herself to voters and do no harm.\par \par \uc2 I say do no harm because if you pay attention to what she actually said in her speech, not a lot in there. That independence, the swing voters could be upset about.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the other things that she was saying in this speech in terms of the progressive policies, most of that was being done by those people who were speaking before her. They focused a lot that night on those anti-Trump Republicans as well.\par \par \uc2 So was her speech profound in some ways? I didn't hear that from Democrats. I'll let Richard speak to that, but she certainly met the bar that they were trying to set that evening.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, Richard, not a lot of substance. A lot of substance, we don't get that in convention speeches. We get broad themes. We get things that are hopeful and make people feel good. It's the minutiae that people really need to know about before they vote.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think a couple of things. I think -- I think Francesca is right when it comes to three principles that the Harris campaign wanted to get across.\par \par \uc2 And I think also to this happens in a moment where if this convention were happening in June or July, you probably wouldn't have seen party unity. You probably would have seen more, you know, ceasefire protests.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we were talking about that a couple of weeks ago on this show that you're going to see chaos in Chicago and that chaos did not result in any real form or fashion. I think that has lots to do with the work.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there were dozens of -- over at south side.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: No. There were dozens but there were --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But there was no --\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Thousands.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- organized, you know, inside.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Of course.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I heard some shouts of free Palestine in that case.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But nothing organized.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: And I think it has a lot to do with the work of the mayor of Chicago, Brandon Johnson.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, I think that all sorts to do with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , three weeks ago, really uniting this party in a new and invigorative way.\par \par \uc2 And I think her fundraising numbers today, over half a billion dollars raised, 200,000 volunteer shifts, people are signing up for today speaks to the fact that she has organized the party and united the party.\par \par \uc2 With 77 days to go in this election, you have a united Democratic Party. You pretty much have a united Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 And the question now becomes, for both of them, is who can turn out their base and who can talk to independent voters and voters who are likely not to vote in this election in a new way to get them out to vote, with ballots going out in a couple of weeks in some states.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, it is soon.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: There are a couple reasons why Democrats are really trying to push this message of unity. The first one is pretty obvious. They just ousted the President of the United States from the ticket and replaced him with someone who hadn't won a single electoral vote from any Democrat.\par \par \uc2 So it's really important to sort of pretend that that didn't take place, to pretend that there's unity. The first guest on your program today, RFK Jr., lifelong Democrat from a really important Democrat family shows that that unity is quite overblown.\par \par \uc2 But the bigger problem for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is that she's the incumbent vice president in a failed presidential administration.\par \par \uc2 And so that was the big takeaway I took from this convention, was how much they were trying to make this a referendum on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There are two problems with that. Again, one, she's the incumbent of what's going on right now. If she has these ideas that she claims she has, she's had three and a half years to implement them.\par \par \uc2 But the other problem is that the Trump presidency was very successful. And a lot of Americans view it far more favorably now than they did even when they were -- when they were finishing it and gave Biden a chance.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: But the net favorability of Trump himself are actually very negative. And I think that's why you see \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  having the bump in the polls that she currently has.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the policies though, when you ask people about how you were doing financially, did the Trump. We have recent polling on this too, where the Trump era policies better for you and your family?\par \par \uc2 It was like two to one over what they felt like. Many of them said was actually negative for them, that the Biden-Harris administration policies that actually hurt their families.\par \par \uc2 So do people, is it really still just about the economy stupid?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ALLEN, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  NATIONAL SECURITY OFFICIAL: It's not just about the economy. I think it's about all the issues that we know that President Trump has an advantage on.\par \par \uc2 But I think all of you were correct and that this was the second switch. The first switch was, let's put in \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And now let's switch out who the incumbent is.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that President Trump needs to be able to put her on the defensive and very, very soon.\par \par \uc2 The good news is, is that I think she hasn't done anything amazing yet. Bear with me for a second. She's riding a wave of relief that it's not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that's at the top of the ticket. I think she brought home soft Democrats. I think she performed with a teleprompter. She did fine. She didn't make any mistakes.\par \par \uc2 But the hard part starts now. She's got to be able to demonstrate that she has political dexterity and agility and a give-and-take campaign environment. And I don't think she's done that yet. And that's what we need to look forward to starting this week.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. So the debate prep is underway. We know that the Trump team is using \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , former congresswoman who really sparred with then Senator Harris when they were on the debate stage together. We'll see how that works.\par \par \uc2 But I want to bring this up too this note that the more that President Trump has tried to say, listen, no federal ban on abortion. Also this week talking about the Comstock Act that he will not use any federal action to block abortion polls going through the mail. There are many within the pro- life community who are starting to get a little frustrated with him.\par \par \uc2 "POLITICO" quotes, \uc2 Tony Perkins\uc2  of the \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2  saying this. It's not a pro-life position. It's not an acceptable position and it does not provide the contrast on this issue to the degree that we had in the past between him and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , what President Trump is doing is suppressing his own support.\par \par \uc2 Mollie, it's not necessarily the pro-lifers are going to go vote for President Biden, but -- or excuse, Vice President Harris, but do they stay home?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Right. Pro-lifers are very pleased with President Trump for how he ran his first administration. And they are very grateful for what he did with judges and other issues. And there's no question that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who just, I mean, pretty much the only policy that was talked about on stage at the Democratic Convention, was abortion through all nine months of pregnancy, up until the moment of birth, not a single protection for any child, no matter the circumstances. That is a strong contrast.\par \par \uc2 But President Trump should understand that his base of support includes these people who care about violence in the womb and they want to protect children and their mothers. And he should be mindful of how much -- how much help he gets from those people and not discourage them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Richard, I see you shaking your head. We're almost out of time.\par \par \uc2 But I didn't hear Democrats articulate anything short of what Mollie said.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Well, a couple of points here. I rarely agree with Mr. Perkins, but I do agree with him here.\par \par \uc2 I do think in Trump's pivot to try to get independence, he's losing right- leaning voters. I think beyond that, I think Republicans continue to make these arguments that Democrats believe in this abortion after birth, which is not a rule.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: No, no. You didn't -- you said up to.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Up to birth.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just to be clear, because, listen, we just had \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  on -- Governor Polis --\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- out of Colorado. He signed a measure, as \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  did in Minnesota that allows for abortion at any time during pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Sure. And I think those are very, very, very, very rare cases. In the case where a mother gets in a car accident, it's a life of the mother - - the life of the baby.\par \par \uc2 And I think doctors make them in very rare cases. I think the idea of these are happening every single day in hospitals across America is a total misnomer.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there are -- but no, I will say this. The American Institute, which is not a conservative group, says there are thousands of these every year.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Thousands in a country that has 300 million Americans is a very, very small number.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: But Americans would love some protection of children.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: But I think what you see in poll -- in poll after poll after poll and ballot issue after ballot issue after ballot issue is the American people stand on the side of a woman and her doctor making the best decision for them over the government making that decision.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And there are a number of those on key critical states.\par \par \uc2 We'll see how that plays out because they're on a lot of ballot initiatives in the fall.\par \par \uc2 OK. Do not go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 We've got this. Multiple Secret Service agents are now on leave following the assassination attempt on former President Trump last month. Our panel discusses new revelations as we preview two events on Monday that are aimed at getting more information about exactly how this happened, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): The \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Secret Service are moving at a snail's pace. They're acting like this is a one-time incident, that we can take our time to really unpack. No, these plots are happening right now as we speak. We have a foreign enemy trying to kill our former president. We need to be releasing information as we get it, and they need to move a lot faster in my view.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congressman Michael Waltz on "FOX AND FRIENDS" yesterday, pushing for a swifter, more transparent probe into the attempted assassination of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Now, five Secret Service members have been put on leave following the incident that left Trump wounded, Corey Comperatore dead and two others seriously injured.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with our panel.\par \par \uc2 Molly, you were pretty fired up about this during the commercial break.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST" AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, what happened with President Trump nearly being assassinated was traumatic, not just for him and his family, but the entire country. We came within centimeters of a horrific situation. You had, again, RFK Jr. on at the top of the hour. We all saw what happened with his father and uncle. And seeing that on live television is traumatic for people.\par \par \uc2 Washington, D.C., the permanent D.C. apparatus is not that interested in what happened, even as normal Americans are horrified by it.\par \par \uc2 And, obviously, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Secret Service can't investigate themselves on their own failings in this matter, but Congress also need - you know, Congress needs to do more. The media need to do far more and be far more interested in this situation and the political climate that leads to these types of situations.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, a couple of things that have surfaced. Senator Hawley has yet another whistleblower he says that's come forward. He sent a letter to the acting director of the Secret Service saying this, "a whistleblower with knowledge of the planning of the whole thing alleges that officials at Secret Service headquarters encouraged agents in charge of the trip not to request any additional security assets in its formal manpower request, effectively denying these assets through informal means." Hawley goes on to write that Roe (ph) had told him that no security assets had been denied. So, he says there - he needs an explanation. Somethings is in conflict.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, CONTRIBUTING WRITER, "FORBES" AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Listen, I think the American people deserve an explanation for this. If you look at what happened to former President Trump, and I think it's - it's heartbreaking, versus what happened to the last time we seen an attempted presidential assassination attempt which was in the `80s against \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , they're completely - the stark - the contrast can't be starker, right? And I say that in a world in which the heroic picture that many have celebrated is a picture that did not happen during the Reagan assassination because the Secret Service moved so swiftly to get \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  out of it that there wasn't time for a photo.\par \par \uc2 In this scene, what we saw three weeks ago, there were three or four, maybe five or six different photos that were taken that shouldn't have been allowed in a world when you're trying to get a protectee to a safe zone when there's an active shooter.\par \par \uc2 And so I think there's a lot of question that should be asked. I agree with Mollie, this is very disgusting. And I think Congress should move a lot faster and the Secret Service should move a lot faster to get the American people the answers they deserve because this was an utter failure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Congressman \uc2 Clay Higgins\uc2  has already released a little bit of what he found in this. He said, "the Secret Service did not retrieve radios that had been set aside for them by Butler County's tactical command, even though they had been reminded to do so by Butler County tactical command the day before and the morning of the shooting." That's according to Higgins.\par \par \uc2 We're going to have the bipartisan group from the House visit Butler tomorrow, but there's also this other forum that's happening with a couple of other members of Congress who are holding - you can't call it a hearing but something else. They say they're running in parallel. But hard to get anything done here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Well, right. It is an election year and right now Congress is out. They've been out for several weeks now. They'll be back in September. But there's also an appropriations process. And that is just, to your point, like, what is happening in this town heading into a November election.\par \par \uc2 The president of the United States has said he wants to see a thorough and transparent investigation because, to Richard's point, he believes that the American people need to know what happened here. So, certainly we'll - all of our eyes will be on this heading - heading into November as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And this morning we all woke up to - if you weren't up late last night, to this news on the international stage, Michael, that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and Israel have been trading fire. Israel had a commanding 100 of their jets apparently flew over \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , they said targeted at what they had been told was a preparation for some 6,000 potential missiles coming their way.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ALLEN, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  NATIONAL SECURITY OFFICIAL: That's right. So, Israel, of course, making a preemptive strike in part to take out all of this \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  capability. Imagine here in the United States if 10 percent of our territory were inaccessible because one of our neighbors was able to arrange it with rockets or missiles. Israel cannot live forever. They honestly can't live another year with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  able to attack their territory. This is why Netanyahu says what happened last night is just the beginning, not the end. Expect a regional situation to possibly escalates as Israel feels like they need to go more into Lebanon, to push \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  back.\par \par \uc2 By the way, this is the right way to do it. The way we put al Qaeda back on their heels was to keep a boot on their throat. And I think Israel knows that's the best solution for them moving forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How worried are you about that escalation you talk about, with the fact that Iran is still warning, oh, yes, we're still going to, you know, unleash our retribution on - or on Israel for the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader who was taken out. I mean there's a lot of threat flying back and forth here.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: I'm still concerned that 