ting, with all these multiple tiers of taxes that you were talking about, you'd pay about 80 percent, 80 cents out of every dollar in all these taxes. This is the opposite of what -- remember John F. Kennedy 60 years ago said tax rates are too high in America and tax revenues are too low. That's when even Democrats believed that you wanted to keep tax rates low.\par \par \uc2 This has blown the lid off that. These people know nothing about business and I agree with you it be a dose of cyanide for economy if this ever came to pass.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I mean how do you do unrealized capital gains? How would that go off? Okay, so you have -- you have an investment income. On paper, okay, you're -- you're making a profit, not a bad thing, in their minds I guess a bad thing.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so whatever profit at the end of the year they project you made, you got to pay taxes on that. Okay, now let's say when you finally sell off your stock or sell a piece of property, what they estimated it was worth isn't what it's worth anymore. Do you get your money back?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, you know, you paid -- you paid -- you don't get your money back, they just -- it just was -- you should have sold it when you had a chance, is that their message to you?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: You know, who's going to get really hammered by this tax is ranchers and farmers. So, you know, my favorite example, take a farmer who let's say you know years ago bought a piece of property, farm land, for, say, $250,000. And now, the government says, hey, you know that -- that land 20 years later is worth $5 million. So you're going to have to pay tax on that $5 million at 20 percent. You owe the government a million dollar.\par \par \uc2 Well, Sean, where is the farmer going to come up with the million dollars? So you're going to have a situation where literally -- not very -- literally, Americans are going to have to sell the farm, sell their businesses, sell