 We also want to have legal immigration.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We all agree on that.\par \par \uc2 WELCH: And we want to have justice and opportunity for a lot of these dreamers who came over here with kids.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How about Americans first, Senator? You're a smart Vermonter. I know, you're smart. You're going to go set \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  straight on this. I appreciate your coming on very much.\par \par \uc2 WELCH: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Thank you so much, great to see you. All right, for a party that says they want to bring down the temp, they are kind of bringing out their famous race hustler now, \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , no. He's not about love and joy, is he? Well, maybe, he just got off the stage.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Bill Hammer, co-host of America's Newsroom, Katie Pavlich, Fox News contributor. All right, Katie. Love and Joy and \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Love and Joy, \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , and the Democratic nominee tonight who's going to try to be born again. Everything that she's stood for in the past no longer applies. She wants to be new and fresh and undefined. Amazing how she was very defined in 2019 and 2020, but now wants to tell the American people nothing about her future plans for the country.\par \par \uc2 But it's been a fun week for Democrats. They are having a good time in the hall, but the big question is going to be whether what translates inside is going to work for working class voters on the outside who are really suffering as a result of the last three and a half years, which are trying to blame on Trump even though \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was in charge of the Biden- Harris Bidenomics agenda.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Bill? Is Bill with us down on the floor?\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, CO-HOST, AMERICA'S NEWSROOM: Sure. Hey, Laura. Good evening to you. Can you hear me, OK?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. Bill, what's the issue that you're finding that the folks on the convention floor are most excited about?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yeah, listen, they are excited to see whatever Democratic politician walks out on this stage. \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , the novice standing. This has been a politician parade, Laura. I mean, you look at this lineup, it's just one after the other. Members of Congress, senators. It's a long way from the everyday Americans that we saw in Milwaukee with the Republicans about a month ago.\par \par \uc2 Couple things tonight. There will be a focus tonight on gun violence. \uc2 Gabby Giffords\uc2  will make a speech in a couple hours from now. In a moment, we believe it's called the Representatives for the Central Part 5. I don't know what that is, but we're going to find out momentarily.\par \par \uc2 There's all kinds of rumors about Beyonce and Taylor Swift. The only thing I can tell you is that Pink is on the menu and she will perform a bit later tonight. There's also going to be a bio about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Remember the introduction? I mean, we still really haven't gotten there yet, right? And it's night four.\par \par \uc2 The schedule is long, Laura. There's a real good chance, based on the paper that I have here, that she could finish well after midnight in the East Coast, so we'll see how that goes.\par \par \uc2 One quick note here, Elizabeth Warren came out about ten minutes ago, huge reception for her. Likewise for \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  last night and AOC the night before that. So, some of the biggest applause reserved for some of the more progressive members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 But it has been a politician parade and not so much the everyday Americans. Back upstairs to you now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Just what Americans want to see, Katie, more politicians.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Where there're empty platitudes.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: I mentioned in the show earlier, Laura, about the term freedom they've been trying to use. And they have perverted the definition of freedom. They are arguing, and they will argue again tonight, I'm sure, with all these features from politicians and government, that they want more freedom for the government to interfere in your life.\par \par \uc2 The only freedom they're offering to individuals is freedom for abortion. Every other thing they want to control, whether it's the car that you drive, how much water comes out of your faucets, what kind of appliances you can use, whether you have a gas stove or an electric stove, where you travel, when you travel, it has nothing to do with the freedom of the individual.\par \par \uc2 They're arguing for freedom for government bureaucrats to control the lives of the people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, let's do the list. So, pot, porn, abortion, gambling, more illegals, that's kind of it. And then the freedom of the government to harass you, surveil you and put you in jail.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: And the freedom from illegal immigrants to break the law, freedom to be criminals, freedom to do all kinds of things that are a perversion of the definition.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So great to see you. Katie and Bill Hemmer on the floor, thank you. All right. I saw him earlier today. What's on your hand, Jesse?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, this is my injury from the training session that we did with \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  and his sensei. Laura did it too.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You are great.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I have to say, Laura is insane. She did more sit-ups than we did.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yeah, you did.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: First of all --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You did more sit-ups than Sean and I.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 's sensei, I'm sure that's relevant to the people, but he's so scary strong, and this guy is scary strong.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'm so afraid of this guy. I think he might have broken something. I have bruises all over my ribs. But Sean's an animal. You're just as crazy. I just barely survived.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All I know is that poor woman in the little room with the yoga mat, she just wanted to do her yoga, and there's like twisting your neck and like twisting your arm, and at one point I think you cried. But no, I'm just kidding.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That was not a cry, that was a high-pitched squeal.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And I say that with a strict record of masculinity.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was fun. That was prime time.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you so much, Laura.\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240823ek8m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Now: DNC Night 3 Underway In Chicago; Walz Set To Speak As DNC Night 3 Underway; Soon: Walz Takes Center Stage At DNC Night 3; Now: PA Gov Shapiro Speaks On Night 3 Of DNC; Now: \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  Speaks On Night 3 Of DNC; Now: MD Gov Moore Speaks On Night 3 Of DNC; Now: \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  Speaks On Night 3 Of DNC\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Bret Baier, Martha MacCallum, Brit Hume, Harold  Ford, Bill Hemmer \par \uc2 9,078 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: That's all the time we have left this evening. Set your DVR, so you never miss an episode. Stay with Fox News Channel for the continuing coverage of the DNC, Bret and Martha, let not your heart be troubled, next straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: A Midwestern moment, tonight, Minnesota Governor, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , gearing up for his big debut on the DNC stage after being tapped for the VP spot on the 2024 Democratic ticket just two weeks ago. Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. Welcome to Democracy '24 coverage of Night 3 of the Democratic National Convention. Live here at the United Center in Chicago. So, Vice President Harris' running mate, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , this is his big night. He's going to take the stage in what will be the biggest speech of his political career.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight, he introduces himself to the nation, highlighting his upbringing and experience. We don't anticipate seeing Vice President Harris here in the arena tonight. The White House calling a lid for her earlier this evening, tomorrow, obviously, is her big night.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, tonight's theme is, a fight for our freedoms, hight profile Democrats, including former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , we just saw moments ago, making the case for a Harris-Walz Administration.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And there's still more to come. Transportation Secretary, \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , and Senator Amy Klobuchar from Minnesota, previewing tonight's headliner.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we have our panel back with us. Brit Hume, Dana Perino, Harold Ford Jr., and Robert Wolf. Brit, I want to start with you, because last night, you said, you were looking forward to \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's speech. You said you guys sort of got your start together. You, at the White House, are on the campaign trail, and President Clinton, it was -- there were some moving moments from him in that speech.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, CHIEF POLITICAL ANALYST, FOX NEWS: Yeah. It was kind of nice to see him. I've always had a soft spot for him. I always liked him. He's had some health problems, and his voice is not what it used to be, but the style is still there. And I'm glad to see the old boy up there speaking. I enjoyed listening to him. It reminded me the old days.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, you came into office in the Clinton era, and I saw you listening to that speech intently.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I share a lot of Brit's thoughts. It's good to hear. I hope people who watch the night, who may be supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or may be supporting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  listen to Brit. It's nothing wrong with having a little soft spot for a guy that might be on the other side or a woman who might be on the other side.\par \par \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  inspired me and taught me how to reach across the aisle, so it was good to see him tonight, but the night belonged to \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , and he's got a big challenge in front of him. When you set it up, talking about his experiences, his upbringing, the accomplishments in Minnesota, will give people a greater sense of him and even a greater sense of this ticket. So, he's now what everybody's looking to hear from, and we hope he does a good job.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. We're also going to get to hear from Governor Shapiro, Pennsylvania. You always get the people who are in the running for Vice President get to have their moment up there to speak to everybody. Dana, your thoughts as we look ahead to this speech by \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , who has been, you know, sort of advertised as an extremely progressive Governor from Minnesota, he's got a job to do tonight.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, HOST OF AMERICA'S NEWSROOM AND THE FIVE: So, the feeling in the hall is different from last night, because they had Michelle and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  last night, and the anticipation was there, and then, they delivered. And so, tonight is a little bit, like, OK, but they're gearing up for tomorrow night, so it's like Kamala Eve here at the hall.\par \par \uc2 And I think that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , they're probably too fine. And you know what? That's all he has to do. He has to do fine, get through it, introduce himself a little bit to America. But really, what he has to do is, tee her up for a good night tomorrow night, because everything for them gets harder starting Friday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Robert, I had Governor Shapiro on special report today talking about some of the big issues, fracking in Pennsylvania energy. These are all things that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has to deal with at some point from as we've talked about her 2019 stance to where she is now. We're likely not going to hear any of that tonight. It's going to be probably more hope and change. But what are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, CHAIRMAN AND CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, UBS GROUP AMERICA: You know, it's interesting listening to what Harold and Brit said, because as my first 25 years in Wall Street, the two bookends were \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , and in 2012, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  changed the election in many ways for President Obama with his explainer in chief speech about the economy.\par \par \uc2 I would have liked to have seen that tonight. I think we've been on the panel for three days, and we want to hear she gave the economic speech. I think he could have delivered that punch back speech that we were probably expecting and didn't see. I love hearing him. I think like Brit, he brings a historic flavor to politics, but I think they could have used him differently.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it's interesting, because I remember that speech clearly, I was on the floor covering that speech, and he said, nobody could have saved this economy in four years, not even me. And he lifted \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , right? You're getting a kick out of that, but that's what he said. And the crowd went crazy. And he was giving everybody permission to give Obama another four years.\par \par \uc2 And one of the interesting things so far, to me, is the sort of glossing over of the Biden years. It's like, they're trying to sort of erase it, and go straight from \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  here, and Harold, maybe you can weigh in on this. You know, how successful is that? And is that a bit of a dangerous strategy in a way?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, he gave him a little -- he gave him some credit at the start of the speech President Clinton did, and talked about, gave him credit for his courage, compassion and for having the selflessness to not run again, but you're on to something. This is what -- this convention here, and what they're trying to do, what we're trying to do rhetorically, narratively, even historically, is to say, we've accomplished a lot, and we now got to look for the future. And don't forget, there's someone sending in the middle, someone sending in that breach.\par \par \uc2 His name is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, the contrast is there. Walz has to do a good job. Then you said it, well, fine, is all he needs to do. Then we looked at tomorrow to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  really laying out the predicate and telling us and explaining to us what she's going to do, and how we can get over the Gulf.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, the other big story today is the reporting that RFK Jr. may get out of this race on Friday in Arizona. There are some sources saying that he may endorse the former President, and what that means, a lot of analysis. How do you see that?\par \par \uc2 HUME: I think \uc2 Bill Daley\uc2  was right when he said to you earlier here on this set, that, it says a lot more about Kennedy and the state of his campaign than it does about the actual race itself. He's looking to find a place to be relevant to some extent, his campaign has been sinking since Dems -- since Biden got out really in order to search for an alternative for people who are well out there on the left that he may have answered, is diminished to some extent, by the emergence of this ticket.\par \par \uc2 So I, you know, I think in the end, it probably won't make much difference, but it might be a little bit of a boost for the Trump campaign. And they're already going --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, I mean, it's not a -- it's not a bad thing.\par \par \uc2 HUME: No, not a bad thing, and (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I mean, Kennedys have had an enormous influence at pivotal moments when the Kennedy family backed Obama over \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , that was a big moment. And I remember them saying, well, we sat around the table with our kids, Caroline Kennedy, and they wanted President.\par \par \uc2 They wanted a future President Obama. So, Kennedy is sort of detached from the rest of his family right now, but he could, that would be a big deal for any Kennedy to back a Republican.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I was the Vice Chairman of the Robert F. Kennedy Center. I was born in 62 named Robert. My brother was born in 60 named John. So, I'm obviously incredibly disappointed in where \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  went. That being said, you know, when we had this big double hater, I think it was one- fourth of the voters. \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  was right, they're playing a major game. It's not a nothing burger. It could be something we don't know.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah. Dana, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I do know this, that a Republican strategist I talked to quite a bit said that for Senate races on the Republican side, but also where Trump was in the battleground states, and in a tight race, with Biden, a little bit ahead, and then, tighter with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . The one thing they felt they could not do if RFK Jr. had a presence in that state, they could not lock down Trump getting that support.\par \par \uc2 So, now if RFK Jr. says, OK, fine, I'd be happy to come along with you. If I were Trump, I would not promise a cabinet position. I don't think that's necessary. And I also -- I think back to when RFK Jr. said that Nicole Shanahan is the most qualified person to be Vice President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And she never wants to interview, until, I think, last night with Trace Gallagher, which was a pretty good interview. And they -- she said, they would have won this election if they'd had a chance to debate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And it's just a lot of it to me, it sounds very far-fetched, but if you're Trump, you're looking for addition, and any small little piece of ground would help.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, that's all I'm saying, is that it's not a bad thing. Think about Jill Stein, the Green Party in 2016, that makes the difference in that race in a number of states. Think about \uc2 Ralph Nader\uc2  in 2000.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Do you know who else is thinking about that? \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Al Gore\uc2 ? Because that made a huge difference, especially where? Michigan.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Wolf -- Robert Wolf has it right. His last name is Kennedy. That's why it's -- that's why it's relevant. And it depends on how they use them. If he gets out in campaigns, if they use him in a way where they're putting him in the states where he may have one to 3, 4 percent, this could very well impact this race in a way that the candidate here and the vice presidential candidate here are impacted in November.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're getting the mind of former President Trump as he's watching this thing. Where do you think their mindset is? I know this is tough for you to do. Just take yourself to that place. Where do you think that campaign and that former President is watching this?\par \par \uc2 HUME: I'm sure he's got this, where did it all go? It was only a few weeks ago, he was riding high. It looked like his head, the victory was clear and probably inevitable. And now, only weeks later, you've got the -- we've got a democratic convention with equaling the enthusiasm that he enjoyed, and his party's fired up, excited, it's a candidate that he didn't think he was going to have to face, and he's got to figure out how to confront.\par \par \uc2 He's being asked to do things that don't really come that naturally to him. He's being asked to be the carrier of message, of a message loaded with specifics about her various positions on issues which are, in my view, vulnerabilities for her. But Trump is, that's not how he reacts. And so, we've heard him, Bret and Martha, recently, with speeches whose texts we had in front of us, who had those pointed comments in them.\par \par \uc2 And when he got to them, he kind of drones through them. And then, when he gets off the -- off the script, and onto his old material, that's when he comes to life. So, he's got a challenge ahead of him. I'm sure he's aware of it. I'm not sure he thinks is -- I think he's been told that he has a challenge ahead of him. I'm not sure he's aware of how important it will be, because he cannot depend on her record, and extraordinary flip flops that have occurred more recently with her to be exposed by the media. He's going to have to do it. And is he prepared to do that?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, he's been declared dead, how many times, right? And I think he's in sort of a reset moment. You had an assassination attempt, and now a new candidate. I think after Labor Day, you got to look at, at Trump and say, you know, is he gearing up again for a big fight? And I think we'll see signs of that coming after Labor Day, likely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, let's go to the floor. Our good friend Bill Hemmer is down there once again. Bill, what's happening?\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, CO-ANCHOR, AMERICA'S NEWSROOM: Yeah. Hey, good evening. To give you a little bit of flavor, guys, this USA sign you hear on both sides is in the hands of every single delegate in this entire hall, all 4,000 of them to begin the evening tonight. We'll see -- we ended the night last night with the signs about vote, and tonight we begin the night with the USA.\par \par \uc2 Couple of things going on over here over the past two hours. Kenan Thompson, an actor from SNL, best known from SNL, came out and did a skit about Project 2025, the delegates loved it. But, you know what the Trump team has said? They got nothing to do with it. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said repeatedly that he wants no part of it.\par \par \uc2 In fact, he wants it killed. You're going to be introduced tonight to \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , and just a moment ago, there was a video that ran about three minutes long. It was featuring \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as a DA in California, as an AG in California, and then, a clip as a U.S. Senator facing off with \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and that now infamous scene on Capitol Hill when he was going through his confirmation process.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how much more we will see of that, but that's been played for the delegates here in the hall. With regard to \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , we are told he will focus on working families. There will be a video that will be played about him tonight that will explain his bio.\par \par \uc2 Growing up in Nebraska, moving to Minnesota, several students will introduce him, students of his, when he was a high school teacher, and a high school assistant football coach, an assistant football coach that went on to win a state championship. He was elected twice as Governor of Minnesota, most recently in 2022, and that is very important for the biography of \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , because it was during that election that they got a majority in the House and the Senate, and as Governor.\par \par \uc2 And in 2023 they passed at least two dozen what Republicans will consider super liberal bills in the state of Minnesota. In fact, one Republican, back in Minnesota, said, they're going to make Minnesota California. It's that liberal record that Republicans will go after in a significant way. It was so liberal in fact, it got the attention of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who tweeted a year ago, hey, check out what's going on in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 You can see that online if you want to look it up. A moment ago, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was on stage. I think this was a snafu, Bret and Martha, I was checking out the prompter and listening to her speak, and she said, we need to re-elect \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , the Speaker of the House. You need to elect a Democratic Senate. And then, on the \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's name wasn't impromptu, and she didn't say his name. So, I don't know if she knows something that we don't, but I consider, for the moment, maybe just to be an omission of so or so. Listen, we got a long night, guys. We're at least 25 minutes behind, so I'll kick it back up you. You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's a common theme. At least, 25 minutes behind.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, exactly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Maybe if we're ready we might --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Maybe we're right on time, man, as far as things go here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In fact, in fact, there is so much time lost. What's that? Shapiro is up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There he is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Governor Shapiro of Pennsylvania, let's listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO, GOVERNOR OF PENNSYLVANIA: Two and half centuries ago, in Philadelphia, a band patriots declare their independence from a king and set ourselves on a path of self-determination. Generation after generation has embraced that responsibility. Ordinary Americans, rising up, demanding more, seeking justice, and in every chapter of our American story, we've made progress and advanced the cause of freedom.\par \par \uc2 Today, well today, we find ourselves writing that next chapter. Will we be a nation defined by chaos and extremism, or will we choose a path of decency, honor and continued progress? \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , while she has spent her entire career making progress. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a man with no guardrails, wants to take away our rights and our freedoms. And listen, while he cloaks himself in the blanket of freedom, what he's offering isn't freedom at all, because hear me on this.\par \par \uc2 It's not freedom to tell our children what books they're allowed to read. No, it's not, and it's not freedom to tell women what they can do with their bodies. And hear me on this. It sure as hell isn't freedom to say, you can go vote, but he gets to pick the winner, that's not freedom. But you know what? You know what Democrats? We, we are the party of real freedom.\par \par \uc2 That's right. The kind of real freedom that comes when that child has a great public school with an awesome teacher, because we believe in her future. Real freedom, real freedom that comes when we invest in the police and in the community so that child can walk to and from school and get home safely to her mama.\par \par \uc2 Real freedom, real freedom that comes when she can join a union, marry who she loves, start a family on her own terms, breathe clean air, drink pure water, worship how she wants, and live a life of purpose where she is respected for who she is. Real freedom, real freedom comes when she can look at Madam President and know that this is a nation where anything and everything is possible. That is real freedom, and that is what we are fighting for.\par \par \uc2 You know, Kamala and Tim's names may be on the ballot, but it's your rights. It's our rights, it's our future and freedoms that are on the line. And you have the power to shape the future of this country. Just like our ancestors, our ancestors who fought for freedom on the battlefield and sat in at lunch counters so our kids could stand up. Now, now it's on us, it's on us, my friends, to organize in our communities and on our for you pages, around three basic American principles.\par \par \uc2 We value our freedom. We cherish our Democracy, and we love this country. And listen, we love this country, and listen, despite our challenges, hear me on this, I want you to know I have never been more hopeful, because I see, in all of you, the enduring promise of America, e pluribus unum, out of many one, it's not merely a motto from the past, it's our direction for the future.\par \par \uc2 You see, you all give me hope, and you all have the power. So, let's use that power. Let's do the hard work necessary to win this election and write the next chapter in our American story. So, are you ready to protect our rights? Are you ready to secure our freedoms, and are you ready to defend our Democracy? And are you ready to elect \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 ? America, let's get to work. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Pennsylvania Governor, Josh Shapiro, you know, I had him on special report, he was not that that animated. He didn't have that same deal. But you know what? He had a little Barack Obama cadence to him. Didn't he?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, he was fired up and ready to go right there. And he is an up and coming star of this party. There is no doubt about it. Brit Hume, you got to look at that and say, he's somebody.\par \par \uc2 HUME: I wonder if he was thinking, will you hear this, you'll decide whether you should have taken me or not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, exactly. I think there was an element of that, Robert Wolf to, you know, sort of be like, look, I was -- I thought that I might be the Vice President speaking tonight. And since I'm not, I want to make everybody a little bit remorseful about that fact.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Well, Dana could tell you from her years in the White House, when your favorability is 65-plus percent in Pennsylvania, including rural areas, you're probably doing a pretty good job.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yeah. Well, not only that. I mean, I know that some people have suggested that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  did not want to pick him because he might overshadow her, but I'm thinking, if you're \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , pick the best person and have a great ticket. I'm not saying that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  isn't going to be fine speaker tonight. As I said, I think fine is fine, but I think that what you saw there does explain his very high rating in Pennsylvania. He also said something interesting, I believe it was on special report, that her flip flopping on fracking is a sign of strength.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And that was an --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was a tough one to swallow.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- interesting way to put it forward. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was a tough one to swallow.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah. But he was saying that she's changed her position. Harold, he was saying that this is a new thing. She's now not against fracking. And I played the sound bite from 2019 and the statement by the campaign in a press release that reversed that decision, she hasn't talked about any of that.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I'd say a couple of things. I think Governor Shapiro is right. Politicians, we live in an era where we think that people are just supposed to be static in politics. We seem to think that we should applaud them more, because they never change their positions. If you are the CEO of a company and your earnings come out, and Robert knows this better than anybody on the panel, and you don't have a good quarter, a good year, and you don't switch, you don't change, you don't adjust, you're going to get fired.\par \par \uc2 I think it's great when politicians are able to say, you know, I was wrong about something, I misjudged something, I miscalculated about something, and changed their mind. Voters are ultimately the arbiters of whether or not that is a motivated by ambition, or it's an authentic reason for doing it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I hear you. I think it's a great thing too, (CROSSTALK) but he's got them out in a press release. It has, has to be the person saying the change.\par \par \uc2 FORD: So, which is one of the reasons why I think President Trump wants to debate so badly, Vice President Harris. The greatest rate of return he can hope for is to have a face to face with her and have the country watch her and evaluate whether or not they believe its ambition or an authentic move.\par \par \uc2 And the authentic move would be defined, for me, defined -- defined in many terms, about whether or not she believes this is going to strengthen the country. But I'll tell you, listening to Governor Shapiro tonight, there's a Shapiro in our future.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. I think we all remember \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's speech in Boston, and it was quite clear in that moment that this man, I remember thinking, he'll -- he'll probably be the first Black President of United States. I mean, you know, it was so undeniable, the magnetism that he had over the room that night. But with regard to changing tack, the only question is, I think the most important question is, why didn't you do it while you were serving?\par \par \uc2 And one of the biggest issues on that is the border, I mean, why they haven't adapted to the border situation? Biden and Harris. I mean, they had a lot of opportunity to make exactly the kind of evolution that you're talking about because of the situation at hand.\par \par \uc2 HUME: You know, it just occurs to me that if she, in explaining these reverse of that is it articulates it in a way that shows knowledge of the issue, the reason for her changing online, and all that. That would make a big, go a long way, but she's got a lot of reversals to describe and a lot of knowledge required to make a case for doing so. It remains to be seen whether she has ever shown the ability to do that.\par \par \uc2 FORD: But in fairness, J.D. Vance had to make a change, right?\par \par \uc2 HUME: A change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to take this video and tune in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The word is called freedom. Can you tell me what it means?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Interesting about every second.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The spirit of freedom --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For me, oh yeah.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Freedom to me means choice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The ability to laugh with friends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Be who you want to be. Go where you want to go.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Living your life day to day. Having new chapters in that life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I believe in having your own words and being able to do what you want to.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The freedom to raise our family in healthy communities.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The idea that I can turn to my partner, my husband, and say, I love you with every being of my soul.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Being able to start a family when I want to, being able to support the missions that I want to, to go to the church that I want to.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Not everyone has the same opportunities, but the United States is of anywhere I've ever been, it's the one place where you can accomplish more.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The big fantastic thing about America is that it is the land of opportunity. I know it sounds trite, but it's the truth.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I grew up with a single mom, and she really imbued in me these qualities that you just really have to care about your neighbors and your family, and your friends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel like the American dream is having opportunities, having opportunity to go to college if you want to, having opportunity to get any job you can, no matter who you are or where you come from.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The American dream is about having the ability to make choices about your medical care and do whatever you want to do with your life without anybody telling you, which is good enough.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are in an incredible moment in history. We've seen women stripped of their most fundamental right to autonomy over their bodies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The freedom to make choices about your body, your life, your career, should be entirely in your control.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But I have found that good people want to do good things.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That you find a way to move forward under ugly and scary circumstances.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The work that lies ahead is to make America live up to its highest ideals. And you know, we're proud to go to work.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The work to build a better future really depends on all of us coming together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We have to bring back that respect for everyone.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think we're as divided as we seem.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Let's come together as one big nation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I feel like we are on the verge of something very amazing in this country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It makes me want to fight for our children and future generations to ensure they have just as many, if not more, freedoms than we had.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I believe in a future where my mom can feel safe no matter where she is in the country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A future that is filled with a healthy and vibrant planet where families are not separated by fences or jail bars.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A future in which we all get what we need.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want to say that for people who are younger than me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hopefully the kids I teach can chase their dreams and believe in what they want to be.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want a future for him that he knows that he's covered and protected.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everyone should think about what we can leave to the next generation. It's our responsibility. It's what we have to do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My name's Tyler.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My name's Yvette.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And we believe in love.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm Alex.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm Kiara.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My name is Steven.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My name is Charlie. I'm a proud Seventh Generation Texan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am from Waterford, Michigan, just north of Detroit.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm from East Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And we believe in the American dream.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We believe in a better world for our child, and we believe in this country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My name is Karen, and I believe in justice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My name is Nancy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My name is Thoreau.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My name is Jennings.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My name is Dana.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My name is Todd.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And we believe in freedom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My name is Gina, and I believe in freedom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My name is Pat. And I believe in the freedom to work together. (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please welcome \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2 . (SONG PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 , AMERICAN HOST AND TELEVISION PRODUCER: Good evening, everybody. Who says you can't go home again? After watching the Obamas last night, that was some epic fire, wasn't it? Some epic fire. We're now so fired up, we can't wait to leave here and do something. And what we're going to do is, elect \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as the next President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 I am so honored to have been asked to speak on tonight's theme about what matters most to me, to you, and all of us, Americans, freedom. There are people who want you to see our country as a nation of us against them, people who want to scare you, who want to rule you, people who'd have you believe that books are dangerous, and assault rifles are safe, that there's a right way to worship and a wrong way to love people, people who seek first to divide and then to conquer.\par \par \uc2 But here's the thing, when we stand together, it is impossible to conquer us. In the words of an extraordinary American, the late Congressman, John Lewis, he said, no matter what ship our ancestors arrived on, we are all in the same boat now. Congressman Lewis knew very well how far this country has come, because he was one of the brilliant Americans who helped to get us where we are. But, he also knew that the work is not done.\par \par \uc2 The work will never be done because freedom isn't free. America is an ongoing project. It requires commitment. It requires being open to the hard work and the heart work of Democracy. And every now and then, it requires standing up to life's bullies. I know this. I've lived in Mississippi, in Tennessee, in Wisconsin, Maryland, Indiana, Florida, Hawaii, Colorado, California, and California, and Sweet Home, Chicago, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 I have actually traveled this country from the redwood forest, love those redwoods, to the Gulf stream waters. I've seen racism and sexism and income inequality, and division. I've not only seen it, at times, I've been on the receiving end of it. But more often than not, what I witnessed and experienced are human beings, both conservative and liberal, who may not agree with each other, but who'd still help you in a heartbeat if you were in trouble.\par \par \uc2 These are the people who make me proud to say that I am an American. They are the best of America. And despite what some would have you think, we are not so different from our neighbors. When a house is on fire, we don't ask about the homeowner's race or religion. We don't wonder who their partner is or how they voted, no. We just try to do the best we can to save them.\par \par \uc2 And if the place happens to belong to a childless cat lady, well, we try to get that cat out too, because we are a country of people who work hard for the money. We wish our brothers and sisters well, and we pray for peace. We know all the old tricks and tropes that are designed to distract us from what actually matters, but we are beyond ridiculous tweets and lies, and foolery.\par \par \uc2 These are complicated times people and they require adult conversation. And I welcome those conversations because civilized debate is vital to Democracy, and it is the best of America. Now, over the last couple of nights, we have all seen brave people walk onto this stage and share their most private pain. Amanda and Josh, Caitlin, Hadley, they told us their stories of rape and incest, and near-death experiences from having the state deny them the abortion that their doctor explained was medically necessary.\par \par \uc2 And they've told us these things for one reason, and that is to keep what happened to them from happening to anybody else, because if you do not have autonomy over this, over this, if you cannot control when and how you choose to bring your children into this world, and how they are raised and supported, there is no American dream.\par \par \uc2 The women and men who are battling to keep us from going back to a time of desperation and shame, and stone-cold fear, they are the new freedom fighters, and make no mistake, they are the best of America. I want to talk now about somebody who's not with us tonight. Tessie Prevost Williams, was born in New Orleans, not long after the Supreme Court ruled that segregated public schools were unconstitutional. That was in 1954, same year I was born.\par \par \uc2 But, I didn't have to head to first grade at the all-White McDonogh 19 School with a U.S. Marshal by my side, like Tessie did. And when I got to school, the building wasn't empty, like it was for Tessie. You see, rather than allowing McDonogh to be integrated, parents pull their kids out of the school, leaving only Tessie and two other little Black girls, Gail Etienne and Leona Tate, to sit in a classroom with the windows papered over to block snipers from attacking their six-year-old bodies.\par \par \uc2 Tessie passed away six weeks ago. And I tell this story to honor her tonight, because she -- she, like Ruby Bridges and her friends, Leona and Gail, the New Orleans Four, they were called, they broke barriers, and they paid dearly for it, but it was the grace and guts, and courage of women like Tessie Prevost Williams that paved the way for another young girl who, nine years later, became part of the second class to integrate the public schools in Berkeley, California.\par \par \uc2 And it seems to me that at school and at home, somebody did a beautiful job of showing this young girl how to challenge the people at the top and empower the people at the bottom. They showed her how to look at the world and see not just what is, but what can be. They instilled in her a passion for justice, and freedom, and the glorious fighting spirit necessary to pursue that passion, and soon, and very soon.\par \par \uc2 Soon and very soon, we're going to be teaching our daughters and sons about how this child of an Indian mother and a Jamaican father, two idealistic energetic immigrants, immigrants, how this child grew up to become the 47th President of The United States. That is the best of America. You know, you know. Let me tell you this, this election isn't about us and them. It's about you and me, and what we want our futures to look like.\par \par \uc2 There are choices to be made when we cast our ballot. Now, there's a certain candidate that says if we just go to the polls this one time, that we'll never have to do it again. Well, you know what? You're looking at a registered independent who's proud to vote again and again, and again, because I'm an American, and that's what Americans do. Voting is the best of America.\par \par \uc2 And I have always, since I was eligible to vote, I've always voted my values, and that is what is needed in this election, now more than ever. So, I'm calling on all you independents and all you undecideds. You know this is true. You know I'm telling you the truth that values and character matter most of all, in leadership and in life. And more than anything, you know this is true, that decency and respect are on the ballot in 2024.\par \par \uc2 And just plain common sense, common sense tells you that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  can give us decency and respect. They're the ones that give it to us. So, we are Americans. We are Americans. Let us choose loyalty to the Constitution over loyalty to any individual, because -- because that's the best of America. And let us choose optimism over cynicism, because that's the best of America.\par \par \uc2 And let us choose inclusion over retribution. Let us choose common sense over nonsense, because that's the best of America. And let us choose the sweet promise of tomorrow over the bitter return to yesterday. We won't go back. We won't be set back, pushed back, bullied back, kicked back, we're not going back, not going back. We're not going back. So, let us choose, let us choose truth, let us choose honor, and let us choose joy, because that's the best of America.\par \par \uc2 But, more than anything else, let us choose freedom. Why? Because that's the best of America. We're all Americans, and together, let's all choose \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Thank you. Chicago. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  with a very fired up speech, numerous times saying that this is the best of America, and this is why the choice should be \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , saying it numerous times.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, many times. All right. Let's bring in Kellyanne Conway and Karl Rove. Kellyanne, you know, I'm struck by how much we're hearing about how freedom is being, you know, taken away that people don't have freedom in America. And the biggest topic there is the abortion issue. That's what comes up the most that we saw in these videos. You know, different veiled references to it. What do you make of that? And how persuasive do you think it will be for voters in November?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER COUNSELOR TO THE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Well, Martha, it's very persuasive in this hall where there isn't a single swing and undecided voter. I think they run the risk of having a big theme that includes all Americans like freedom, and ignoring what so many Americans are telling pollsters, of all backgrounds, what freedom means to them. Sure, abortion is important to many people, pro-life and pro-choice, but they're saying, you stripped me of my financial freedom.\par \par \uc2 You are against school choice, you're against parents, these single moms in the inner cities, of allowing our tax dollars to follow their children to a better school worthy of their humanity and dignity, they're all against that here. And so, I think they're missing even just the essential conversational freedom. But something really strikes me about this conversation -- this convention, everybody is casting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as an insurgent, as a challenger, not the incumbent.\par \par \uc2 Today marks one month since she displaced \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but it marks the 43rd month of her being his Vice President. This is the Biden-Harris Administration. I think it's incumbent upon the Trump campaign to make sure everybody knows she is the incumbent, he's the insurgent, he's the change maker, as sure, she's the fresh face, but not the way \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was in 2008, certainly not the way \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was in 1992.\par \par \uc2 They're doing a good job at this convention of wrapping freedom around marriage equality and reproductive freedom, but they're missing the essential conversation. What does everybody tell pollsters? It's the economy inflation, the lack of financial freedom, and it's the unfairness of the out-of-control border, the lack of freedom that you're taking from us, and giving to people who just got here illegally.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah. Karl, to Kellyanne's point, there are a lot of people who just say, listen, it costs me too much to live. It cost my family too much to live. There are a lot of people who say, immigration really makes me mad the way it's being run on the border. That's not being addressed specifically here. They're talking about freedom. Is this working? This messaging working?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, it's working to in this sense that the Democratic Party, fairly or not, for many years, has been seen as being, you know, left of center and really disparaging of America. They've covered themselves in red, white and blue. They have talked about -- Governor Shapiro, talked about the founding in Philadelphia. They've chanted, USA, USA. We -- I've been to Democratic conventions where that wouldn't have been very popular to do.\par \par \uc2 We've all seen that kind of thing in the San Francisco conventions, San Francisco Democrats, as Jeane Kirkpatrick once called them. So in that sense, it's worked. But I agree with Kellyanne. I mean, think about it, the idea of freedom, you're -- you're stuck, your child is stuck in a failing school, and what they say is -- yeah, they were talking about the freedom to get out of that failing school. They talked about, you want to have the freedom to join a union?\par \par \uc2 Well, you can join a union in America, but what about people who say, I don't want to, as a condition of having a job, be forced to join a union? That's -- that's taking away their freedom, and that's exactly what this convention has talked about time and time again with the so-called PRO Act. And again, they also have talked about freedom and reproductive rights as no limitations on it. That's not where the American people are.\par \par \uc2 The American people are reasonable people who say, a woman ought to have a right to an abortion up to a certain point, 14 weeks, 15 weeks, 16 weeks, and after that, rape, incest and life of the mother, but they're not in favor of an untrampled right to destroy a child in the seventh or eighth month or the second or third trimester. All the polls show that. So that America's history is a battle over the definition of what our freedoms ought to be.\par \par \uc2 And sometimes, you know, we're fighting about things where if there's a broad consensus ultimately emerges, the right of every American, regardless of color, to be treated the same, to have the right to vote, to have the right to job, to have a right to advance in life, but there are other things, in which, you know, it's not so clear. Parents, for example, the freedom to have their children not have age-inappropriate books in school libraries.\par \par \uc2 That's -- there's a big battle going on in America about the definition of untrampled freedom to have anything in there, and the right of parents to have the expectation in the library, there will be age-appropriate materials and not age-inappropriate material.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And right now, you've got the Maryland Governor, \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2 , speaking. Let's listen in just for a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 GOV. WES MOORE (D-MD): -- your companion and not your captor. And I'm asking that you join us in the work, because making America great doesn't mean telling people you're not wanted, and loving your country does not mean lying about its history. Making America Great means saying the ambitions of this country would be incomplete without your help. It's the legacy of those six workers who fixed potholes on a bridge while we slept, who were born in a different country, but who knew that America was big enough for them too.\par \par \uc2 It's the journey, it's the journey of a man raised by a remarkable immigrant single mom, a man who felt handcuffs on his wrist at 11 years old, who now stands before you as the 63rd Governor of Maryland and the first Black Governor in the history of our state. It's the story, it's the story of a prosecutor who defended our freedoms and had Maryland's back when we needed it most, and now, MVP, we've got your back as well.\par \par \uc2 It's right. It's my fellow veteran, my brother and the next Vice President of the United States, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . And now, Tim, Tim knows that in the military, you count the days towards mission completion. So guess what y'all, we have got 75 days and a wake-up until Election Day. 75 days and a wake-up for us to prove what Americans can do when the pressure is on. 75 days and a wake-up for us to show that true patriots do not whine and complain, we put our heads down and we get to work.\par \par \uc2 75 days and a wake-up to build a future that those who came after -- those who came before us hoped for, and those who come after us that they deserve, and 75 days and a wake-up to elect a leader who was willing to believe in the best of us, and that leader is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the next President of the United States. Thank you. God bless you, and let's leave no one behind.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please welcome former South Bend Indiana Mayor, \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, FORMER SOUTH BEND INDIANA MAYOR: Thank you. Thank you. Good evening, Democrats. Thank you, Chicago. Here is a sentence I never thought I'd hear myself saying, I'm \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , and you might recognize me from Fox News. I believe in going anywhere, anywhere, in service of a good cause. And friends, we gather in a very good cause, electing \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  the next President and Vice President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 The choice could not be clearer. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rants about law and order as if he wasn't a convicted criminal running against a prosecutor, as if we were going to forget that crime was higher on his watch. Talks about the forgotten man, hoping we'll forget that the only economic promise that he actually kept was to cut taxes for the rich.\par \par \uc2 And don't even get me started on his new running mate. At least \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was polite. J.D. Vance is one of those guys who thinks, if you don't live the life that he has in mind for you, then you don't count. Someone who said that, if you don't have kids, you have, quote, no physical commitment to the future of this country. You know, Senator, when I deployed to Afghanistan, I didn't have kids then.\par \par \uc2 Many of the men and women who went outside the wire with me didn't have kids either. But let me tell you, our commitment to the future of this country was pretty damn physical. Choosing -- choosing a guy like J.D. Vance to be America's next Vice President sends a message, and the message is that they are doubling down on negativity and grievance, committing to a concept of campaigning best summed up in one word, darkness.\par \par \uc2 Darkness is what they are selling. The thing is, I just don't believe that America today is in the market for darkness. I believe America is ready for a better kind of politics. Yes, politics at its worst can be ugly, crushing, demeaning, but it doesn't have to be. At its best, politics can be empowering, uplifting. It can even be a kind of soul craft. My faith teaches me that the world isn't made up of good people and bad people, but rather that each of us is capable of good and bad things.\par \par \uc2 And I believe leaders matter because of what they bring out in each of us, the good or the bad. Right now, the other side is appealing to what is smallest within you. They're telling you that greatness comes from going back to the past. They're telling you that anyone different from you is a threat. They're telling you that your neighbor or nephew or daughter who disagrees with you politically, isn't just wrong, but is now the enemy.\par \par \uc2 I believe, in a better politics, one that finds us at our most decent and open, and brave. The kind of politics that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  are offering, and as you have felt these many days, that kind of politics also just feels better to be part of. There is joy in it as well as power. And if all of that sounds naive, let me insist that I have come to this view, not by way of idealism, but by way of experience, not just the experience of my unlikely career.\par \par \uc2 Someone like me, serving in Indiana, serving in Washington, serving in uniform, I'm thinking of something much more basic. I'm thinking of dinner time at our house in Michigan. When the dog is barking and --\par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020240822ek8l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Obamas Go Mean Girls At DNC; Democrats Fake Toughness On Border; DHS Inspector General Says Thousand Of Unaccounted Migrant Children; DNC Black Caucus Speaker Implies Black Male Trump Voters Are Crazy; Walz's Credibility Crisis; Parents Of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  Hostage Speak At DNC; Pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  Protesters Outside The DNC; Will RFK Jr. Join Forces With Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Peter Doocy, Dagen McDowell, Robert Wolf, Karl  Rove, Harris Faulkner, Mike Tobin, Jesse Watters \par \uc2 8,493 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: All right, good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from day three of the Democratic National Convention here in Chicago. Tonight's theme, A fight For Our Freedoms. We've been listening so far, and it's basically just all about abortion. So much for the freedom of the infants in the womb. And we're going to hear from Biden backstabber \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . She's all about the children, as well as \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and finally, the man of the hour, Twisted \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And for all the details, let's go live to the convention floor where Peter Doocy, Fox News Senior White House Correspondent is standing-by. Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And the theme of the night is a night for freedoms. So far since the program started that has been mostly what they referred to as reproductive freedoms. We are going to hear from \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . We are also going to hear from \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , as you mentioned, and it'll -- her speaking on the third night kind of works with the fact that they are talking less and less about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And what exactly is she going to say about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at this point after she squeezed him out.\par \par \uc2 What state delegation is this? California? OK, so you guys have \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  tonight. Who are you more excited to hear from? Why not \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 ? What's wrong with \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 ? Is anybody here down on Nancy at all, because she squeezed President Biden out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, Fox News. We're not.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: OK. Well then, then they did the right thing by getting President Biden out and having \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , also from California, assigned to the top of the ticket. And as we continue just our -- are you one of the delegates here? Where are we, Washington, D.C.?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Washington, D.C. delegate?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, I'm not. Actually, I'm just taking some photos right now.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you live right now.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: We're live, yes. All right, well, that's it from the floor, we will bring you news as we have it, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Peter, sometimes people don't want to talk. All right, tonight, the Democratic National Convention continues with the Harris team trying to convince you that they are the real border hawks, because they added a Long Island Democrat Representative \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2  to their lineup. And remember, Suozzi pretended to care about the border to get elected, as if that's somehow changes the fact that Kamala waved in what 10 million migrants.\par \par \uc2 Now remember, the idiotic bipartisan border bill that we exposed from day one. They love to cite this in response to criticism, but it wasn't really a border bill at all. It would have, in the end, allowed 1.8 million migrants into America annually. But the real question that I think we're asking tonight is, how did we go from the party of hope and change to what often sounds like the party of hate and revenge.\par \par \uc2 Here at the DNC, Democrats are talking about some of their favorite topics. You know, how much they hate Trump, how much they love abortion, which is what they're doing right now. How much they hate Vance, how much they love girl bosses, and how much they love vibes and memes. They sometimes sound like a bunch of mean girls in the cafeteria.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You have a choice America between the man who left our country a total mess and the woman who has spent four years cleaning it up.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER FIRST LADY: Doubling down on ugly, misogynistic, racist lies as a substitute for real ideas and solutions.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (D) 44TH U.S. PRESIDENT: This weird obsession with crowd sizes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The Kamala crews unserious, and that now includes the Obama's, who stole the show with one liners trashing Trump, something we'd expect from snarky late night TV hosts, not necessarily from a President and a former First Lady.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 M. OBAMA: His limited narrow view of the world made him feel threatened by the existence of two hardworking, highly educated, successful people who happen to be black.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA\uc2 : We do not need four more years, a bluster and bumbling and chaos. We have seen that movie before, and we all know that the sequel is usually worse,\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They made a deliberate choice not to defend the Biden-Harris record on the big three, the economy, inflation, and immigration. And of course, Democrats tonight will avoid talking about the 818,000 jobs that just disappeared from April 2023 to March of this year. Well, the fact that wages have declined, the fact that many school children never recovered from the COVID lockdowns because they know they have nothing useful to say on these points, so they're not going to put forward any suggestions about how to overcome those challenges.\par \par \uc2 They've also gone from hope to nope, nope. You're not going to have a standard of living that improves. Nope, we're not going to talk about our plans. Nope, we don't care if illegals get treated better than the homeless vets. So, between the rise of China and their views on climate change, I really think the Democrats have pretty much given up on boosting our prosperity, and they don't really care in the end, because in their minds, with about half of all American voters supporting Trump. Why would this country, or at least a lot of it, be worth saving anyway?\par \par \uc2 Now remember, Obama's, you didn't build that. Well, implicit in that was you don't deserve that, plus you probably are living on stolen land and have relatives who fought in the south for the civil -- in the civil war, so time to pay up.\par \par \uc2 They've determined that for now, the safest path for Kamala is to just mercilessly mock Trump as they criticize him, by the way, for mocking her. Well, tonight, starring as the Kamala attack dog du jour is \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , and when they're not going to do that with the attack stuff, well, they'll just rely on vibes, memes, and celebrity endorsements.\par \par \uc2 But if Harris wins this election, a year from now, the Obama's, the Clinton's, the Biden's, the Pritzker's, the Pelosi's, they're all going to be hanging out at their various vacation homes, and most Americans will be looking at a weaker economy and lowering their expectations. That's all a pretty high price to pay so that a bunch of rich people who despise Trump can get their revenge.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 . Congressman, I ran into you on the \uc2 Navy Pier\uc2  yesterday. It was great to see you. It seems like there's a lot of negative stuff happening at this convention. I know it's like people talking about love and joy, but was there a lot of love and joy coming from the Obama's last night? Did you hear that?\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): Well, I know you played some of the one liners, but President Obama also spoke about extending grace to each other, respecting difference, having --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They want to lock Trump up. How is that extending grace?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: He didn't talk about that. He talked about having kindness. And we've got to figure out in this country how we're going to come together at some point. And that part of the President's speech, I think a lot of Americans could agree with even if they don't agree with the one liners. Have a common agenda, bringing manufacturing back, of helping working families that have been left behind because of globalization, of helping places like Johnstown, Pennsylvania, and I think that's what the Democrats should talk about.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Do you think that the question of manufacturing jobs, let's just stay with that. I mean, that's been a huge disappointment in the last three and a half years. The jobs numbers revised downward; we're going to get into that more deeply later in the show. That is a huge scandal, and if that had happened under Trump, would be hearing Holy hell from this crowd. But Is anyone going to talk about this tonight?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, Laura, look, the manufacturing numbers, twice the investment in manufacturing actually over the last three and a half years --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But the jobs aren't coming. I mean that's been exposed, just like the EV charging stations aren't coming.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, there are areas where we can have common ground. We don't want China dumping the steel. Let's have tariffs on China dumping steel as their steel is collapsing, we want to have new steel plants here in America. Let's invest in that. Let's make sure that we have new aluminum here in America. There are areas where we can work together. And the President has had a manufacturing agenda with twice the manufacturing investment than in Trump's years.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Do you think that people like Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  and obviously \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , others, Jacky, senator from --\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, Rosen, is not, not, not coming to this convention. What does that say about the ideological split in the Democrat Party?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: One, I think they're in their states. They're in tough election races. They're all up. They're all up five to seven to eight points, at least, \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  and \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2 . And that's a smart thing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But why aren't they confident? Why don't they come here? And then they got the Obama, this is royalty here. They've got the Obama's, the Clinton. I mean, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , that's the biggest thing ever. They're not very controversial.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Because, you know, Laura, all politics is local, and they're better served being in their states. But look, I'm excited to hear \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . I know you had your correspondent talking about people not being excited. He's the only Democrat who's carried in the last 20 years white working class and working class areas, and we need to put him out there. I would send him to factory towns.\par \par \uc2 I'd send him all over Pennsylvania. I think he still understands a lot of people who have been left out in the economy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Are you for banning fracking like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , or are you not for that?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I'm not for banning it on private land. On federal land, that's what President Biden ran on. That is the same position --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But you are not for that, really, are you?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: On federal land, on federal land, but not on private land. And on private land is where most of it is.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We're artificially keeping down our energy supply because of that ban on federal land. And you know, fracking is actually quite safe. I mean, if you land on private land, that can hurt water supplies too. Why not offer it on federal land?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, I think you need to have a proper regulation. But on private land, neither President Biden nor Vice President Harris is for banning it. And as you know, natural gas production is at record levels, as is oil production in this country. Just factually.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, you think the price of gas is -- that's good for most Americans where it is now?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: No, it should come down more. But the way to make the price of gas come down is to be tougher on Saudi Arabia, to make sure \uc2 OPEC\uc2  isn't restricting, to make sure we have more --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We're making Venezuela richer by -- we're like going to Venezuela. I mean we're helping these countries by keeping our supply artificial. We should just dominate, right?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: No, look, we have record production in this country. And by the way, if we diversify the economy and if we have more renewable energy, that's going to make us less dependent on Russia, on Venezuela, on Saudi. Why wouldn't we want to do it?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She's made us more dependent --\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: All of the above.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- on those countries. That's the problem. We're more dependent on them because of the regulations in place. Why would we be?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: But we're at record production on natural gas and on oil. So, why not diversify? Why not do all of the above? Why not also have solar, wind, geothermal --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I think Trump says he wants all those. I just think he wants to drill baby drill to get the price down for working and I know you care about working class people. It's great to have you on tonight.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Thank you so much as always.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: For joining us.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. Where am I going? If you doubted the Democrats have enough religion in their lives. Come on. Just watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: It was subtle and deep and thought provoking. A subtle, deep, original, interesting take. Deep, interesting speeches from both Mr. And Mrs. Obama.\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES, CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: It was a sacred task. I didn't realize I had been in a spiritual desert until they created that oasis on that stage, and they did a beautiful job.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. Van Jones is in a spiritual desert. I just saw him though, he was -- looked fine to me, but because the Obama spoke, we found an oasis. That's kind of another slap at Biden. Don't you think it's getting a little bit embarrassing?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, the man who knows \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  very well, \uc2 Rod Blagojevich\uc2 , former Illinois Governor. Governor, in the end, what did the Obama's really do for urban America? What?\par \par \uc2 ROD BLAGOJEVICH (D), FORMER ILLINOIS GOVERNOR: What did he do for America?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Urban America.\par \par \uc2 BLAGOJEVICH: For urban America, where they left the city of Chicago with more crime, higher taxes, and schools that are performing even worse than they were when I went to those schools, and they weren't so good when I went. In Chicago, we have crime that disproportionately impacts black people. That's the great irony. If you're a black person in the city of Chicago, you're 20 percent more likely to be a victim of a homicide than if you're a white person.\par \par \uc2 And if you were to walk down the street from this arena just about a block away. You know what the life expectancy is for a young child growing up, 61 years old. But if you went a little bit east and a little bit north in Streeterville, an upscale neighborhood in Chicago, you lived to be 91 years old, all of the hope and change that was promised, there was no change. Maybe there was some hope. But when you're desperate and facing despair. Of course, you embrace hope. We all do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Hope and pocket change maybe. Now, the Obama's jet in from Martha's Vineyard to trash Trump and so, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , it's kind of our national healer, Governor.\par \par \uc2 BLAGOJEVICH: Well, all I can say is that the Biden-Harris record and the Harris-Walz ticket, it's going to have to explain to the American people why the cost of groceries is up over 20 percent, why a gallon of milk cost 50 percent more than it did under Trump. A blow for bread, 54 percent more under the Democrats than under Trump, a carton of orange juice, 71 percent more and a dozen eggs, 126 percent more. They can't talk about high prices or the high cost of living which impacts --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The price controls, they can get into that. But do you think that Michelle's speech last night was really -- it was kind of just trying to bait Trump. To me, it sounded -- I mean she had some, Kamala is mom, some of the nice stuff at the beginning about Kamala and motherhood and so forth, as this is all about abortion tonight. So, interesting juxtaposition, but it was a lot of mean girl stuff, kind of in the end.\par \par \uc2 BLAGOJEVICH: That was another disappointment with President Obama's administration. When he was elected as the first black president, and I was there that night, it was magical. I was the first governor to endorse him. A black man winning the White House said to all black America, you can be somebody. But instead of bringing our country together, Obama chose political choices to divide our country and play to the anger and the disappointment that people have and blame other Americans. Frankly, who lived generations before for the difficulties and the challenges that the Democrats have failed to improve.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It almost seems like, and I said this in the top of the show, that they've almost given up on, on the idea that the average person's standard of living is really going to go up that much. That's not really -- this is all about a woman driving to California to get an abortion tonight. And, you know, LGBTQIA+ rights and so forth, and I understand they want to talk about that, but how is that going to help put a down payment on a first time for -- a first time homebuyer.\par \par \uc2 BLAGOJEVICH: It's a party that's controlled by subs (ph) and elitists who are very wealthy, who just run away from the working class and poor people that they claim to be for. If I'm a working guy, and I'm looking at this party and I'm thinking about the high cost of everything I'm saying, you know what, bring down the cost of the price of my gas, and I'll worry about my own vasectomy myself. Their priorities are upside down. It's a party I don't recognize.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, should President Trump respond to the Obama's? I think that would be, I wouldn't get -- he's not running against the Obama's. I would stick to just the economy and just the border.\par \par \uc2 BLAGOJEVICH: I totally agree. I think --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Obama is popular. So, you can't take -- you know, you can kind of have fun with them, but that -- if you'd go on a personal invective against the Obama's -- that's a loser argument.\par \par \uc2 BLAGOJEVICH: I think President Trump has great skill, and I think he has an opportunity to reach into the inner cities, Detroit, Philadelphia and other places, to talk to black people about how things have not improved for black people under Democratic leadership.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Rod, great to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 BLAGOJEVICH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Thanks so much. All right. Up next, I don't think you're going to hear much about this by the Democrats tonight, bombshell economi