 that's where the Israeli consulate is located.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the street here on Madison, you have this group they call themselves Behind Enemy Lines. They had their plan demonstration earlier today. They came out on the street and frankly the police and media outnumbered the demonstrators. And the initial conflict, I got to tell you was really contrived, very deliberate. They put their banner in front of themselves and marched directly into the police line and I should say a aggressively into the police line.\par \par \uc2 That caused the first conflict, a flurry of arrests. I saw personally about four arrests as probably going to be more when the -- when the smoke clears. Now, you see the line of police, the blue helmets, the different police, some of them with the yellow vest. Behind them, if you'll notice, you have some Israeli flags and you have American flags combined with Israeli flags. That's some of what was burned on the street.\par \par \uc2 Now, just a few moments ago, in fact, about minutes ago, the one of the white shirts got out there with a loudspeaker and gave the order to disperse. They said this is now an illegal assembly and you see the conflict set to take place right here on Madison Street in front of the Ogilvie Transportation Center. To give you what Ogilvie is, that's where most people commute to and from the city when they're coming particularly from the northwest.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Mike, I'm going to go to Minnesota in the roll call where we're going to hear from Minnesota and then later California. Let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And favorite teacher, we were excited to go to his classroom every day. If you've ever been in high school, you know that can be rare. He opened our eyes to the world, and taught us how to talk about global issues with respect, curiosity and kindness, even and especially when we disagreed.\par \par \uc2 But he wasn't just a great teacher. He was also a great neighbor and friend. In 