eigh-in day. And this is how they do it. They give them food or anything, it depends on the animal. So, with a squirrel, monkey, they put a blue ball on a stick, and it jumps on the scale. A penguin gets tricked by walking on the scales because they give it fish.\par \par \uc2 And I'll be on "HANNITY" tonight if you want to watch me.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Ten seconds. What have you got?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: The kids made it possible for disabled kids to surf. Thank you for young Mr. Clyde (ph) and all of his -- all of his classmates.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Surfing disabled kids, Harold. Well done.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us. Have a great one.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020240821ek8k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Now: DNC Night One Underway In Chicago; Hillary Clinton Addresses The DNC; Representative Jasmine Crockett And Pennsylvania Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  Speak At First Night Of Democratic National Convention In Chicago; Governor Beshear Discusses Abortion Rights; President Biden Slated to Speak Last On First Night of Democratic National Committee\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Martha MacCallum, Brit Hume, Harold Ford, Bill Hemmer,  Peter Doocy, Dana Perino, Karl Rove, Kellyanne Conway \par \uc2 10,519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: We both got our start as young lawyers, helping children who were abused and neglected. That kind of work changes a person. Those kids stay with you. Kamala carries, with her, the hopes of every child she protected, every family she helped, every community she served.\par \par \uc2 So as President, she will always have our backs, and she will be a fighter for us. She will fight to lower costs for hard working families, open the doors wide for good paying jobs, and yes, she will restore abortion rights nationwide.\par \par \uc2 And as a prosecutor, Kamala locked up murderers and drug traffickers, she will never rest in defense of our freedom and safety. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  fell asleep at his own trial, and when he woke up, he made his own kind of history, the first person to run for President with 34 felony convictions.\par \par \uc2 As Vice President -- as Vice President, Kamala sat in the Situation Room. (inaudible) as Vice President Kamala sat in the Situation Room and stood for America's values. I know what it takes, and I can tell you, as Commander in Chief, Kamala won't disrespect our military and our veterans.\par \par \uc2 She reveres our Medal of Honor recipients. She won't be sending love letters to dictators. She will defend Democracy and our Constitution, and will protect America from enemies, foreign and domestic. Think about it. The Constitution says, the President's job is to take care that the laws be faithfully executed. Those are the words of our founders. Take care.\par \par \uc2 Just look at the candidates. Kamala cares, cares about kids and families, cares about America. Donald only cares about himself. On her first day in court, Kamala said five words that still guide her. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for the people. That is something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will never understand.\par \par \uc2 So, it is no surprise, is it? That he is lying about Kamala's record. He's mocking her name and her laugh sounds familiar, but we have him on the run now. So, so, no matter what the polls say, we can't let up. We can't get driven down crazy conspiracy rabbit holes. We have to fight for the truth.\par \par \uc2 We have to fight for Kamala as she will fight for us, because you know what? It still takes a village to raise a family, heal a country, and win a campaign. And America needs every one of us, our energy, our talents, our dreams. We're not just electing a President, we're uplifting our nation. We're opening the promise of America wide enough for everyone.\par \par \uc2 Together, we put a lot of cracks in the highest, hardest, glass ceiling, and tonight, tonight, so close to breaking through once and for all. I want to tell you what I see through all those cracks and why it matters for each and every one of us.\par \par \uc2 What do I see? I see freedom. I see the freedom to make our own decisions about our health, our lives, our loves, our families, the freedom to work with dignity and prosper, to worship as we choose or not, to speak our minds freely and honestly. I see freedom from fear and intimidation from violence and injustice, from chaos and corruption.\par \par \uc2 I see the freedom to look our children in the eye, and say, in America, you can go as far as your hard work and talent will take you, and mean it. And you know what? On the other side of that glass ceiling is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  raising her hand and taking the oath of office as our 47th President of the United States, because my friends, when a barrier falls for one of us, it falls, it falls, and clears the way for all of us.\par \par \uc2 So, for the next 78 days, we need to work harder than we ever have. We need to beat back the dangers that Trump and his allies pose to the rule of law and our way of life. Don't get distracted or complacent. Talk to your friends and neighbors. Volunteer, be proud champions for the truth and for the country that we all love.\par \par \uc2 I want -- I want my grandchildren and their grandchildren to know I was here at this moment, that we were here, and that, we were with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  every step of the way. This is our time, America. This is when we stand up. This is when we break through. The future is here. It's in our grasp. Let's go win it. (SONG PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: 2016 Democratic Nominee, Former Secretary of State, Former Senator from the State of New York, former First Lady, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , talking about, again, the cracks in the glass ceiling, and she can see them. She says, this is our time talking about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , also said, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is distorting her record and mocking her laugh, something she finds familiar from 2016. Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. Welcome to Democracy '24 coverage of the Democratic National Convention. We are live here at the United Center in Chicago, where they are getting things off to a pretty lively start. We heard from AOC a short time ago. We just heard from \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . Obviously, she was the last woman who tried to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and she did not succeed in that quest.\par \par \uc2 She is here to cheer on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  tonight in hopes that she can do the same. At one point, the crowd started chanting, lock him up. I mean, that's also a familiar refrain that she remembers from last time around. We've got a great panel alongside us once again here, Brit Hume, Dana Perino and Harold Ford Jr. Brit, we just heard from the former First Lady, Former Secretary of State, former candidate for the presidency, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, CHIEF POLITICAL ANALYST, FOX NEWS: A familiar voice took me back to 2016 and beyond. Not a place I was mostly eager to go, but there it was. And it does establish one thing, that there are still places in America where Hillary Clinton can go and receive a rousing reception. I'm not sure there are very many left, but this is one of a woman. So, she got.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Earlier, Harold, he had \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the Vice President, come out a little surprise address to tee off the night. She said, this is a night about realizing and recognizing President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And said, there's a lot to do going forward.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: He will be -- President Biden will be the focus of the evening. I hope, in his message this evening, that he is less contentious and confrontational and is more cheerful, constructive about what he accomplished and why this race is so important. I think a lot of people have tried it tonight. I was struck, Bret, as I listened tonight.\par \par \uc2 Remember that Republican event? You had four nights with four different themes, each of those nights, and we've seen them really, really heated those themes. Tonight is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and we'll see here these next few speeches, if they really begin to set him up to come tonight to hand that baton off to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. We had themes, and the theme tonight is, what happened to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? I mean, tonight is about the extraordinary events that we have seen over the past four weeks. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should have been the big Thursday night speaker here Dana, but he's relegated to Monday night, and we're told he's going to be up and out of here right after he speaks.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, HOST OF AMERICA'S NEWSROOM AND THE FIVE: The morning after the debate in June, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  went on all the shows in the morning to defend \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and said, he had a bad night, but he is the best person to be the president for the next four years. Two weeks later, not even that. He was out.\par \par \uc2 He was out because \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said, you are not going to destroy this party on my watch. Apparently, now their lifelong friendship is that a bit of an impasse, as you can imagine. But what I thought was interesting about the Hillary Clinton speech is, there's a lot of women in this room tonight, maybe not watching, but a lot of people in this room tonight thought she should be the first woman president.\par \par \uc2 And that she was pretty gracious and enthusiastic, and saying, let's do this for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She's a good friend of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as well. But just think, six weeks ago, we had stories about how there were donors in the Democratic Party who did not think she should continue to be on the ticket if he wasn't going to be on the ticket. And now you have this entire room, all saying, we are for her, absolutely for her.\par \par \uc2 And I thought that her speech was good. AOC speech was good. There was so much of it. They was just like nonsensical to me, but for this crowd, for this room, getting them ready for the next 78 days, they're doing a good job tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, what is the purpose do you think of this week? You know, there is tight polls, independents and moderates, they really want to try to go after them, but there is a big pull from the progressive part of this party, AOC getting that speaking position, one of the \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio- Cortez\uc2 , we should say, Congresswoman, is one of those indications.\par \par \uc2 HUME: And I think it's reflected as well in the fact that we don't have any detailed policy position as a speaker from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We know, not from her, but from aides that she's apparently in the process of abandoning positions she's taken that were probably too far to the left for national consumption.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: From 2019.\par \par \uc2 HUME: From 2019 and maybe beyond. Whether she will -- whether she herself will do that in her remarks, remains to be seen. But what I think this night reflects in this event in the place where she now stands, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  now stands, is the Democrats are pretty good at making an emergency into an opportunity.\par \par \uc2 And as you heard me say earlier, Bret, they had to say -- they had an emergency in 2016 when \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  was about to run away from the nomination. And they turned to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who was lost everywhere in that primary season until the South Carolina came along.\par \par \uc2 He got -- they got him through South Carolina with a big boost from James Clyburn, as we as we speak here, and he went on party filling behind him, won the nomination, won the presidency against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Now, it's four years later, suddenly, we had an emergency when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  proved incapable of carrying on, it was pretty clear to anybody in their right mind that he couldn't possibly be -- couldn't, -- probably couldn't win, and certainly couldn't serve out another four years.\par \par \uc2 Sudden emergency, they turned to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and what happened both times? The Democratic Party has been described as a party when it comes to nominating presidents and falls in love. Republicans always serve as a party that falls in line. Well, these Democrats, both this year and four years ago, fell in line pretty quickly behind somebody who appeared nowhere near getting ready to be nominated. Both Biden didn't and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  certainly didn't. And now here we are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Harold, today, it was written that President Biden is still a little bit in shock and a little bit angry about what happened over the course of the last few weeks. And I guess, one of the questions is, what can he accomplish tonight to bring the party back together? Are there any voters who are saying, I'm so upset about what happened to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I'm going to stay home, I'm not going to vote.\par \par \uc2 FORD: You know, I don't think so. I think it's a -- I read some of those reports. I'm not persuaded by those reports. I think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  recognized a lot of what Brit just shared there, had he performed better in that debate we'd having a different conversation today. Don't get me wrong. I think he wanted to be on this stage Thursday night, accepting this nomination and battling in November.\par \par \uc2 But I think he understands that the things he's fought for and achieved over the last four years and throughout his career, a lot of that was at stake, and if he didn't, and he lost, he went forward and he lost, Democrats would likely blame him. I think, for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , Brit said something also about the substance.\par \par \uc2 There's not enough detail yet about what she wants to do and how she wants to do it. Tonight, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  saying what they have done together and how she's going to continue is an important first stop. He's got to be cheerful. He can't seem like he's upset about it, even if he is. And then, we got to get on with the business of populating what we're going to do as a party, what she's going to do as President, and how we're going to make the lives of everyday Americans better.\par \par \uc2 The only thing I'm struck a little bit about that, I wish we were talking more about people and talking more about how we're going to make lives better, and hopefully, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does a lot of that in the final speech tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is this a lot about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is one, not former President Trump, and a lot of this race is about running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the person. And two, she's not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and it's a fresh face. And the party is relieved to have somebody in the game.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, certainly, because if you saw that poll that 93 percent of Democrats are relieved that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is no longer the nominee. Think about that. You're coming into this hall tonight, and everyone has been told, you will clap, and you will give him a resounding send off. And I think that's one of the reasons that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  came here tonight.\par \par \uc2 She wasn't expected to be here this evening, but she came and what was her first message? She said, and I want to thank \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and we got -- and she just got the whole crowd going because she said, we have to do something for him. She understands -- I actually think that there's some affection there and respect, and gratitude, that she's had for him.\par \par \uc2 But now, she's going to have to figure out a way to say, and thank you sir, see you out the door. Now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a challenge in many ways, the media is not going to help him prosecute the case against her. She is figuring out a way to define herself in a way that is just amorphous, like, she wasn't there.\par \par \uc2 When AOC just gave her speech, she said, if you are a person who had to come out of retirement because you couldn't afford your grocery prices, and Kamala is the one for you. And I thought, but she's been there for three years. It's in the title, Biden-Harris Administration.\par \par \uc2 But, for some reason, partly because of the media attention, and partly because this whole crowd is in here, they're saying, she is different, she is unique, and she was not responsible for all of those things.\par \par \uc2 I'd just say one quick thing, you know, she has \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to thank for the fact that in the second breath after he dropped out, he endorsed her, and he shortened this process dramatically. A lot of people wanted it to go on.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I think this party likes \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I think people are going to be as genuine, the response he's going to get tonight. Remember, he's been in politics for 40-plus years. So, we know him. We like him. But Brit said best, he probably couldn't win him to fall. And there's an acknowledgement in this room, and he acknowledged it too by saying, I'm not going to run. So, I think it's a genuine affection for him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Let's check in with Bill Hemmer. He is down on the floor. Hey, Bill.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hey, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, CO-ANCHOR, AMERICA'S NEWSROOM: guys, good evening to you. We are on the far corner of the arena here in Chicago, stage left as you're looking at it, listening to the commentary on behalf of all of you. I'll tell you guys, when the Democrats turn the page, they turn it hard and they turn it fast.\par \par \uc2 To date, there are four times as many mentions of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and there are of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and at the current rate, the way this program is running, there's a chance now that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  may not be on this stage before the clock passes 11 o'clock on the East Coast, and you just wonder now, he landed at 3 o'clock this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 His schedule was to be wheels up by midnight, local time, in Chicago, which would keep him on the ground here for a grand total of nine hours. And that's it. And he's gone and he's out of town, and you wonder, by the end of this convention, how diminished this President might be, after his party is giving somewhat of a long goodbye.\par \par \uc2 And Frankly, it feels, to me, like they've got a hard time saying it. Couple observations from down here on the floor. I can tell you a lot of USA signs from time to time. What's not visible to me are protests over Gaza. Remember eight years ago, the last time Democrats got together at a convention?\par \par \uc2 It was Philadelphia, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was the candidate. She had a long battle with \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , and the big debate on the floor was over a trade deal called the transpacific partnership, that was later nullified by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when he won and became President January 2017.\par \par \uc2 Those were the Bernie's supporters in Philadelphia, men and women who did not want that trade deal passed. And you think about the conflict today within this party is far from a trade deal. It's about life and death in the Middle East in a war that is ongoing. So, we'll watch that throughout the night tonight and out over the course of the next four days.\par \par \uc2 Talked to a Democratic insider a bit earlier today. He said, we're back in the game now. We felt like we were back on our heels, but now, we feel like we are on our toes. Another insider told me a few days ago, he said, we are out with the old and in with the new. And I said, is it that easy that you can flip a switch that easily and turn your attention?\par \par \uc2 He said, man, we want to win in 2024. The debate was 53 days ago, Biden dropped out 29 days ago, the long goodbye continues, guys and we'll wait here in Chicago for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to be on stage, perhaps 30 minutes or 45 minutes from now. Back upstairs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wow, they're running about 25, 30 minutes behind. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's bring in the rest of our panel. Kellyanne Conway and Karl Rove Joining us now. Great to have both of you with us. Kellyanne, we just watched Hillary Clinton's speech. Jamie Raskin up there now. Your thoughts on what we've seen so far tonight, at night one of the DNC?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER COUNSELOR TO THE PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES: Well, of course, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should be the focus. I don't understand why he's speaking so late, if at all. Let's not forget when people give him ovation here, they're not applauding his legacy or service, or even his presidency, necessarily. They're applauding his exit.\par \par \uc2 And I think that, obviously, this whole process has been undemocratic, but for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  it's been fairly undignified as well. They've -- they've stripped him of his presidency, they stripped him of his honor. The way he was treated is pretty untenable.\par \par \uc2 And we were reminded earlier, that, I think it was Dana that mentioned, it was \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  that went on all the shows after the debate, and said, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had a bad night, but he's a great president, and said, there's somebody else who did that.\par \par \uc2 His name was \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . He came out with a tweet shortly after that debate and said, we all know, we all have bad nights, we all have bad debates. I, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , know something about that. So, this whiplash, I think it's a sugar high. It doesn't have staying power for the swing voters watching for the independents in those seven states, for the 21 or 22 counties.\par \par \uc2 They're going to decide who the next president is. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , somebody should tell her that she's not the nominee. It's not 2016. I think that it was part a little bit of regret and revival for her, and part looking at the future, but you can't help, but think, tonight, you're also looking at two political dynasties, the Bidens and the Clintons, that are all but gone.\par \par \uc2 These are relics of the past Democratic Party. One last thing, when Kamala speaks of late, she sounds like, a not -- she sounds like a challenger, making promises, not an incumbent, bragging about performance. And until she actually hooks herself into the better parts of this White House, as they say it, I don't think she's going to sway the people who she needs to sway.\par \par \uc2 She's got 98 percent of Democrats now, and Trump's still beating her in Arizona, Georgia, North Carolina, according to \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll, tide in Pennsylvania, Wisconsin. So, we have a lot of runway before us, but I think the fundamentals still slightly favor him. She'll get a big bump this week. She's got to do more than come out for 90 seconds, and rally the crowd.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That was just Luke Skywalker looking on there. Karl, you know, Politico has this write up about this speech tonight, saying this, the speech from Biden will be emotional, according to one senior Democrat, people will cry. But then, asked if anyone will feel regret for pushing Biden aside, the same Democrat said unequivocally, no,\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: Yeah, well, there's a palpable sense of relief among this crowd. In fact, you saw that in Hillary Clinton's speech, it was sort of low energy at the beginning, but the more she focused on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and what that represented for the future of the Democratic Party, the more energetic the crowd got.\par \par \uc2 I mean, now let's not kid ourselves. Tonight is going to be a bittersweet moment for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . They're going to treat him with respect, but they're going to be grateful that he came and spoke late in the evening, and that he's going to be gone. And that's going to determine -- that sense of relief is going to determine a lot about this convention.\par \par \uc2 For example, I don't think, we're going to see a lot of policies, because we're certainly not going to see any concern by the left of the Democratic Party about the flip flops that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been involved in, against fracking, and now I'm for fracking, I was for Medicare for all. Now, I'm against Medicare for all.\par \par \uc2 There's not going to be any of that concern, because again, they are relieved that they were facing certain disaster and an electoral defeat, and now they think they have a shot at it. And as a result, I think we're also going to see a big emphasis on the persona, and the personal values, and sort of vision of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We're not going to be sitting here with, you know, a lot of detailed big policy issues that are going to be driving the convention. It's going to be, she was a prosecutor, she was the attorney general. Here's her -- here are the skills that she brings to the fray. Here's the vision and values that she's got.\par \par \uc2 It's not going to be a lot on policy, because they don't care about policy. They care about winning. And Brit made the observation at the beginning that it used to be Republicans fall in line and Democrats fall in love. So, Democrats are both falling in love and falling in line tonight.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, Brit, back to you, tonight, one of the big blocks is for reproductive freedom. And it's interesting that the banner this evening is for the people. And we just heard \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  talking about freedom and what that means, and that translates in this room a lot to the freedom to have an abortion across this country.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yeah. And it uses, what I think is, one of the most annoying and obnoxious of all, the euthanasia abortion, which is reproductive freedom. Abortion is about a lot of things. It's not about reproduction. It's about avoiding reproduction. But that is a phrase that the Democrats have hit on, and have used it for years.\par \par \uc2 Abortion rights advocates do the same thing. I think, you know, people who want to justify what it can make, in some instances, arguably a necessary thing to have happened, but never, but never, something you -- you celebrate in this hall, on this night, to this week, it's celebrated as a form of freedom.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, (inaudible) around town. Performing abortions and vasectomies, that's also something that's been celebrated this week, so something different.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right, yeah. You wonder, though, Harold, as this continues to go longer and longer, they're now 35 minutes behind. Putting the President that late, isn't that kind of in the front to you?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I hope he comes on. I hope he comes on sooner than a 10:40, top 10:50, but just a couple observations, some points have been made. Winning is not a bad strategy to have in politics. So, if they win, it's a positive thing for the party. It is not lost on anyone that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is exiting, and it's not lost on him, the voters here, or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, we've gotten over it as a party. I'm a Democrat. The party has gotten over it for the singular reason, for the singular thing you said, Democrats want to win this election. Two, the issues are of a concern to me. I do think we're going to have to begin to talk about them at some level, in a serious way, because campaigns are one, the successful campaigns are ones that talk about the future, talk about everyday people, how their policies are going to make their lives better.\par \par \uc2 We got a few days to start getting to it. This week, we may not get to it. Karl may be right, but we're going to leave this convention on Thursday night if she gives a great speech with a lot of momentum, with a lot of energy, and hopefully, a preparedness to focus on how we're actually going to make people's lives better day to day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. Dana, they have, by my count, about 10 more things between now --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think they're shedding, I think they're shedding some speakers as we speak.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here, we're here right now, right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Representative Meng, I think just got -- just got the act. This is Representative Jasmine Crockett from Texas. What do you say? We listen in for just a couple seconds? Yeah.\par \par \uc2 REP. JASMINE CROCKETT (D-TX): And she is known for inciting violent mobs. Listen, y'all. He's only looked out for one person, himself. As women are dying, he is bragging about overturning roe. And y'all know I come from Texas, and right now, in Texas, come on, Texas.\par \par \uc2 But right now, in Texas, they want to institute the death penalty. That is a problem, while \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is fighting for our reproductive rights to be restored. She is also the leader we need on the global stage. She helped secure the release of Americans wrongfully detained in Russia.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, he cozies up to his role model, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , and MAGA holds legislation hostage here at home, critical resources to secure the border, military aid to Ukraine, and even the Farm Bill. She's lived the American dream while he's been America's nightmare. America, looking at the two choices before you, who would you hire? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No!\par \par \uc2 CROCKETT: Or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 CROCKETT: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has a resume. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a rap sheet.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 CROCKETT: She presides over the Senate, while he keeps our national secrets next to his thinking chair.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CROCKETT: You all know what I said that another time, at Mar-a-Lago. While \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to put our 1787 Constitution through his Project in 2025 paper shredder and make every day January 6th, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is fighting to fulfil the promise of America. In the real world, this wouldn't even be close. But this election is. Do not make a mistake, we are the underdogs in this fight, even though there was only one person qualified, only one person who has done the work, and who has delivered the results. And she needs you. She needs your one vote this November. Can we count on you?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, that's representative Jasmine Crockett from Texas. She is on the Judiciary Committee and she has some fiery hearings in that back and forth.\par \par \uc2 I think that Peter Doocy is live on the convention floor with a little color from down there. Peter?\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: A lot of color. We're actually next to the Washington state Democratic section because they have the best -- you guys are the only ones with hats that light up.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, I'm the chair.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Tell me about the hats. Washington state, I don't think of a blinking cowboy hat when I think Washington state.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, we are going from the Beyhive to the KHive to the W.H.-Hive, all the way to the White House. We are cowboy Kamala like Cowboy Carter. We are celebrating this cultural and political moment.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: All right, you heard it there. And you could tell, people are paying very close attention to everything that is being said on stage. You talk about the vibe shift, which is something the Democrats are hoping to keep going. You don't see people really on their phones except to take a picture right now because they are on TV, or take a picture of the stage. But it is a very engaged crowd, and everybody here is just waiting for the president who is backstage right now doing a photo line with the folks who set up this convention for him and had to, at the very, very last minute, change everything. Bret and Martha?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Peter, you just spoke the other day to President Biden outside. You asked him how he felt about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  potentially distancing herself from his Bidenomics and some other possibilities are. He said, oh, she's not going to do that. You have been around the president a lot on the trail at the White House. So what do you expect to hear from him tonight? It's a big night for him.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Well, they are saying that it's not going to be about his legacy, and that makes sense, because the policies that he was trying to bring into a second term were so unpopular against Trump that they decided to fold it up and tweak things a little bit.\par \par \uc2 What's going to be really interesting, though, is we know this from a practical perspective, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  cannot be tied to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  too much and win the election. Just because of those policies. And so it will be a very interesting watch to see how they salute President Biden. Biden aides are telling us that he wants to speak on the first night and hand the convention off to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , which seems like a nice way of them saying that he is just going to start to slowly fade into the background and let them come up with some new economic policies that might be more popular and keep the good vibes going. In this convention hall, you guys see it from the suite, it's a lot of good vibes for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's right. Hey, Peter, really quick, one more thing. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been an office, in politics for more than 50 years, five decades. He has never had a convention to be nominated in with all of these people. He had to do his convention with COVID, with nobody there. A lot of people talk about this moment being bittersweet. You're at that White House. What is the feeling there?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: I was standing at the foot of the stage when President Biden came out to do his walk-through. And you got a sense, I got a sense of from being the number one consumer of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  content in America for the last five years that as he looked out at all of this, there was some kind of a feeling like this is all supposed to be for me. And so, and so it's not going to happen for him ever.\par \par \uc2 And you can see in the clip right here, he looks kind of morose. He was speaking just barely above a whisper. That is not somebody pumping himself up for this big primetime speech. That is somebody who knows that this is the beginning of the end.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Peter, we'll check back in on the floor. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's get back to our panel and go around the horn again as we wait for President Biden to come out and speak. We understand he is doing some meeting and greeting on the line in there. Dana, when you look at the big issues of this election, immigration, economy, what, if anything, might he say about some of the actual persuasive elements in this campaign to voters?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: I think that it's interest. I don't because that he and the White House got the memo that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to distance herself from the Biden-Harris administration and the economy in particular. And I thought that when she did her first policy announcement, it flopped so badly. It reminded me that she starts off strong in campaigns. She was the first one out of the gate in 2020, February 2020, big event in California. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tweeted that day, wow, look at her, she's a contender. By December it was all over. So she starts strong.\par \par \uc2 And you know the game of Jenga. OK, it starts off real easy, and you're pulling those pieces going around the table. When she pulled the price gouging, greedy supermarket chains, let's put price controls on that from the government and try to put it on top, it got wobbly. And that was just the first one. The leftwing media panned it, but what I thought was interesting is that she didn't try to correct it. So to me, I don't know whether they're just going to rest and let the media try to do this for them, or if they're going to realize if they have some problems not just on the economy, on other issues you mentioned as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, we have invited her on our shows and hasn't taken any invites for any media interviews. She will, though, and there will be a lot of pressure on whoever sits down with her first to go over those 2019 positions to what they are now.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Let's hope that happens, Bret. There has been no sign of any appetite on the part of most mainstream media outlets to even raise these issues with her. After all, you don't have to get an interview to write a piece making the issue of the country as between what she said then and what she says now. What we see now is passing references to the fact that she no longer holds this position now or that position now. Of course, we haven't heard any of that from her as we have spoken of that.\par \par \uc2 I must say, I am reminded tonight, I was a long reporter in Washington watching the Democratic convention in 1972 when George McGovern was their nominee. He got his speech off at 2:00 in the morning. I thought the Democrats would never let that happen again. They seem to have learned a lesson to some extent, but not completely from what we're seeing tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They're about 40 minutes behind right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Karl, let's bring Karl Rove back in. Karl, we were on this afternoon when former President Trump was speaking in York, Pennsylvania. No doubt he is going to be taking this in this week. And he is in a tougher position now than he was a few weeks ago now for sure. Talk about what you think needs to happen on your side of the equation.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  SENIOR ADVISER: There's such a target rich environment that there needs to be a disciplined effort to take the advantage that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has on the issues of inflation, the economy, and immigration, and to go straight at \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on those issues, and to contrast their two records.\par \par \uc2 And he needs to hold her, explain why she has to be held responsible for it. For example, on the issue of inflation, to say she is the woman who cast the deciding vote that passed a $2 trillion American Rescue Plan that Democrat economists, including the former secretary of treasury \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  said would cause inflation. And then she cast three more votes adding trillions of dollars of spending to our economy and to the government's budget which blew up the deficit and gave us this inflation that we have.\par \par \uc2 And he's got to make that case. People, you can't be she's got bad idea. He's got to explain those bad ideas in a way that people can say, you know what, I understand better than I have before when she is responsible for it. Remember, the people who are up for grabs in this election, a bunch of them are low information, low propensity voters. One Democrat told me recently they have been doing a big study. Those people spend four minutes a week, according to their estimation, thinking about politics.\par \par \uc2 So he needs to get back to the essentials and say here's what she did wrong, and then say here is specifically what I'm going to do. And he gets a lot of applause when he says "drill, baby, drill" because that's something specific. I want energy independence, and the way to get it is to unleash our energy industry. But he needs to augment it with more so that people have a sense he has got a second act in him and that his answers will help solve those problems. Both parties are going to have to convince the American people that they will make their lives better, and to some degree that requires them to outline their visions, their values, and what they want to do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Kellyanne, you successfully were leading that campaign in 2016. Pennsylvania was a big part of that equation. How does somebody here move the needle in Pennsylvania? How does the Trump campaign move the needle in Pennsylvania with all those different kind of voters there?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER SENIOR COUNSELOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: So our strategy in Pennsylvania can be the strategy this time, Bret, which is do better than most Republican candidates in the five counties about Philadelphia. You may not win them, but you've got to mitigate the deficits that the usual Republican candidate would have. Then you run up the totals of the western part, the part of Pennsylvania that \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  visited together just a couple days ago.\par \par \uc2 Look, it's energy independence, it's fracking. Pennsylvania has more energy under its feet than many countries. It's also inflation. I think it's also school choice. You had a governor in \uc2 Tom Wolf\uc2  that vetoed school choice to charters a few times. I think that can bring a lot of these Hispanic and African American parents and grandparents back to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll says he has got 41 percent of Hispanics. That ain't nothing.\par \par \uc2 But I will say the Pennsylvania 2016 that I publicly said is our reach state, the 19 electoral votes it represents now, that's almost incidental to the fact that you can vote so early in Pennsylvania. Part of my expressed strategy of going after Pennsylvania in 2016 is that it had a rich tradition of day of voting. That's all been turned upside down because of COVID.\par \par \uc2 And ladies and gentlemen, I can't believe the only thing that we still do the same way that we did during COVID is the way we vote. It makes no sense. I think it benefits the Democrats. Republicans are catching up as much as they can by investing in the non-sexy parts of politics. But Bret, the Pennsylvania 2016 is winnable to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but if and only if they do the attack as well as the messaging. And the tactics here are early voting, legal ballot harvesting. And I think that President and Mrs. Trump can make a great example by voting early in Florida for him. If the lion roars, others will follow. I like the fact that he is tied in Pennsylvania. I think \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , the Senate candidate, is a strong one. He has got some of the best advocates against Kamala of any down ballot candidate in this certainly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's interesting.\par \par \uc2 Right now, they are going to the reproductive freedom bloc. We expect \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , the governor of Kentucky, to come on. He was at one time thought to be in the V.P. hunt but was taken out of that mix towards the end, leaving \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  from Pennsylvania, \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2  from Arizona, and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , obviously, from Minnesota, who is now in the hall and is sitting as the nominee.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There he is with his wife Gwen as they listen to these stories. Harold, this is one of the biggest issues for the Democrat Party. They believe that this is going to help them win in November. So right now we are listening to some stories of people's personal experiences after the change in the abortion law. But there are voters, Hispanic voters, black voters, lots of voters out there for whom this is not a good issue.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: I differ slightly. I will say this, the Democrats learn from the Republicans who did this well at the convention where they had everyday people talking about these issues that really mattered to them. You look at what has happened over the last two years since Roe was overturned, each state that has had an issue on the ballot to ensure that rights for women to have an abortion are protected, they all passed, even in red states including Virginia and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Two, we shouldn't kid ourselves. This race has been reset, and she has reset it. Her entry on the ticket has done that.\par \par \uc2 Three, a rate cut, a cease-fire in Gaza, and gas prices after Labor Day remain still staples in politics. We don't have any of those things yet. We'll see what happens here over the next few weeks. And I think she will be advantaged, she being \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , if indeed that is the case.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I am looking forward to hearing \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  tonight. I think he is one of the rising stars in the Democratic Party as well. He has got a great record in Kentucky. And he talks about something I haven't heard a whole lot here. You know what I hear a whole lot in Milwaukee? Growth. We've got to figure out how we grow things. Grow more jobs and better jobs, how we grow better schools, how we make better schools. So hopefully we will get a little bit of that from him tonight as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Before we hear from the Kentucky governor, I want to head down to Bill Hemmer. Bill, what do you got?\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, guys. Good evening again. We were just watching a video a moment ago about abortion. And we had a couple different couples some out and share their experience about having troubled pregnancies. That clearly is an issue they are going to ride and drive hard.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, I thought Karl made an interest point about maybe a lack of issues, maybe more of a biography debate, or a convention, I should say, as like an introduction of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . There is a video they played of her as a younger woman in Oakland who came to the rescue of a friend of hers who had been raped by a relative. So we're going to get into that over the next days and more of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's life.\par \par \uc2 I'll send it back up to you. Let me just mention this guys. You can't help but to get the sense that maybe this convention doesn't care if they push \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  closer toward midnight on the east coast.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wow. That's really late. That is really late\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: We'll see, we'll see.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's take a lesson into Kentucky Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2 , (D) KENTUCKY: -- must tear away any chance he can ever be president ever again.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: In 21 states, Trump's actions have resulted in extreme bans on abortion, in my state, even in cases of rape, incest, and nonviable pregnancies. That leaves 12 year old girls like Hadley (ph) with no options. That fails any test of humanity, any test of basic decency, any test of whether you have any underlying empathy.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, this extremism is being soundly rejected all over our country.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: In Kentucky, we put reproductive freedom on the ballot last November, and I beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's handpicked candidates by more than five percentage points.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: This November, we are going to beat him again!\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Elect \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , and protect reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Folks, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointed the Supreme Court justices who got us into this mess up. His Project 2025 goes even further. Here is the thing, Trump and J.D. Vance simply don't believe in your freedom. Trump says people are absolutely thrilled that women had their basic rights eliminated. J.D. Vance says women should stay in violent marriages, and that pregnancies resulting from rape are simply inconvenient. Their policies give rapists more rights. That's not inconvenient. It's just plain wrong.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: And a woman grieving a nonviable pregnancy shouldn't be required to carry it to term just to listen to her child die or to hear no sound at all. All women should have the freedom to make their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Freedom over their own bodies, freedom about whether to pursue IVF, freedom about whether to have children at all.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: How we treat people transcends party lines. It goes right to the heart of who we are. My faith teaches me the golden rule, that I am to love my neighbor as myself. And the parable of the Good Samaritan says we are all each other's neighbors. So I want anyone watching tonight, Republican, independent, Democrat, to know that you are welcome here.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: We believe in America where we live out our values. End anger politics once and for all and moved beyond this us versus them by remembering that we are all Americans.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: That is how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  lead. They both called to ask how they could help Kentucky in recovering from natural disasters. They helped us improve our roads, our bridges, and invested in our people. They didn't ask me who Kentuckians voted for. They asked me what Kentuckians needed, and, folks, they delivered.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gets it. She knows we must move beyond anger, extremism, and division, that everyone has dignity and deserves respect. America, we're going to win.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: And we're going to win by staying true to our values of compassion, empathy, and doing right by our neighbors. I'm so proud to be all in for the next president of the United States, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Thank you!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kentucky Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  there talking a lot about abortion after a number of different speakers about the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 Back with the panel. Brit, it's interesting that we are in this point.\par \par \uc2 HUME: It is. And what does it say about the modern state of the Democratic Party, that it could not ask these abortion speakers to stand aside to make room for the president of the United States to speak at a reasonable hour of the night. And they're still in it. He is not up yet. I think it's striking. This is the party of abortion. It is their biggest issue. And in their own minds I think they think it's their biggest advantage, which tells you a lot.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, Brit, what are they doing tomorrow? Outside of the convention, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is offering free abortions.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, and free vasectomies, yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So that aside, the DNC didn't say, OK, we are doing it inside the hall. They let them right outside the hall and they are proud of it. They are talking about it all over town. And it is just jarring.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There is a big inflatable IUD contraceptive device that people are taking pictures in front of. If the theme is for the people tonight, that's what it was supposed to be about, right, for the people. We have now spent over an hour, more than an hour talking about abortion, about abortion rights. And I think about, Harold, this message that we have been hearing that is joyful, a joyful patriotic embrace, even, of the future.\par \par \uc2 HUME: It's one thing to make abortion available in certain circumstances, but it is regrettable, I would think, most decent people would say, it is quite another to celebrate the way it is being celebrated here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's no longer about it being rare.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Right.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I feel like I am dating myself when I say I am used to going, I've been to a dozen or more conventions in my life, Democrat and Republican. And I like hearing America's best days are in front of us. I like hearing from governors who have great track records of creating jobs and improving schools and lowering drug rates and keeping people, keeping people safe.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There wasn't a lot of --\par \par \uc2 FORD: I was really looking at Governor Beshear who I think, again, is one of the stars in our party to give us a little bit of how did you win two times in a state that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is the sitting senator, where you have \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  as the two Republican? He said he won because, don't get me wrong, I think protecting abortion rights was part of it, but also because he has created jobs, because he's improved schools.\par \par \uc2 So I hope maybe tonight is going to focus on this. I hope the next few nights we also focus on some of these other things as well. But I am looking forward to hearing the president. I do hope he comes on.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, at this time, you've got one, two, three, four, five, five speakers to go.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Still?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Plus, a James Taylor "You've Got a Friend," performance.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I wonder if we can put that on 1.5 speed. That's a slow song.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And it's a go to. They keep going back to it, right. Election after election. I grew up listening to James Taylor "You've Got a Friend," but at some point, I think we're going to have some good musical surprises, though, later in the week.\par \par \uc2 Brit, if you are \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and you're sitting back there, you have been doing this for 52 years. This is supposed to be your big night. Can you say to Raphael Warnock or Chris Coons or a few of these folks, like, why don't you guys go on after me?\par \par \uc2 HUME: I would think that you could, or I think somebody --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know Brit Hume would.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You guys are out of here. I'm up next.\par \par \uc2 HUME: I could have done it if I were \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . We're the same age. I know who I feel. I'd be furious. And I'm just sitting here as an observer, and I find it somewhat infuriating.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HUME: It's not just because I don't think I would get a good night's sleep.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Can I say one thing, Bret? There was something done earlier in the evening, I just want to attribute, it was done to Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  from Chicago, someone who inspired a generation of politicians and pastors and other public leaders. He was a hero of mine. At the 84 convention, 88 convention, happened early in the evening. And I want to just salute to him and his family and the great work that he continues to do but the great work he has done on behalf of the country and our party. So I say thank you, Reverend Jackson.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is the 26th convention that has been held in the city of Chicago. Most conventions held in this city than any other in the U.S. And the last one was 1996 with \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . Remember, he accepts the nomination, they do the macarena, the whole dance.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Over and over again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want two back to Karl Roves and Kellyanne. Karl, is this going to work tonight, I mean, pushing Biden past 11:00 p.m. east coast time?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: It's going to work if you don't want to make the face of the convention \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and congratulations on them for deciding that that wouldn't be a wise thing to do. But it is very strange. We are sitting here approaching midnight on the east coast, and the audience for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to be small. People are going to hear about it tomorrow. I am sure it will be a warm, fulsome response from the crowd. But, again, it's an indication they want to put him in the rearview mirror as quickly as they possibly can.\par \par \uc2 Had enormous lines outside, it's been in years in terms of getting people inside and getting this whole thing going. Typically not to, typically it's a Thursday night, you may have a night that looks like it will have in norma's anticipation of the nominee and giving their big speech p., we all know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spoke for of a long time with that was a late night as well, usually Thursday night, noted Monday.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They had enormous lines outside, so a lot of this has been sort of clogged up in the gears in terms of getting people inside and getting this whole thing going. But typically, not to belabor the time thing, but typically Thursday night, you might have a night that looks like it's going to go this late, and enormous anticipation of the nominee coming out and giving their big speech. And we all know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  certainly spoke for a very long time, and that was a late night as well. But usually that's Thursday night, not Monday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and Kellyanne -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I was just going to say, if you look at the crowd, though, no one's leaving. Everyone seems to be very happy, very excited. They're enthusiastic. And I truly believe if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  were still the nominee, it would not be like this at all. So the enthusiasm gap that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been able to close, especially with the younger people, it is an impressive feat, I think all of our heads, our heads are a little bit spinning. Eight weeks you said she shouldn't be on the ticket but now you're all for her.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing is that she has basically said I will rise to this occasion. I'll take the baton and I will run with it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Has team Trump, Kellyanne, settled on their attack plan against \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 ? Has there been, do you think, a uniting of forces here, a change in how they are approaching this ticket? Because, obviously, it's a new ticket.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: It is new, and look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  beat a woman before, he can beat a woman again. But this woman is not defined the way \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was. She was seen as sour and dour. She had a lot of people telling pollsters I don't think she's honest or trustworthy, in fact, she's probably corrupt. And she wasn't seen as fresh and new. She wasn't seen as part of the old -- she really was part of the old girls' club to the extent there was one.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's a different race, a different set. And look, the struggle is not to find the best nickname for her or insult her. I want more insights, fewer insults. I think the way to beat her is very obvious. It's through policy. And in 2016 when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  beat \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , a much more formidable, much more experienced woman, the issue set was not as clean. He wanted border security and immigration to be a top issue and couldn't get it there. He's the one that introduced it and elevating it into the national consciousness, but to international derision, not to the number two issue in all seven swing states and nationwide where it is now.\par \par \uc2 He said he could get growth better and employment -- unemployment down and wages up. But there were promises from a business guy and a job creator. He did all that. He can point to what he did, performance versus promises. On policy he wins this hands down.\par \par \uc2 And I do want to say this, because we're going to be reminded of it if and when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  speaks some time tonight. Scranton Joe got in the way of our 2016 victory in 2020 in this way -- we had flipped 206 counties that voted for Obama twice. They voted for Trump in 2006 (ph). Biden won back five of those, two in Pennsylvania, two in Michigan, one in Wisconsin. Why is that important? because we all know that these elections come down to a handful of votes in about 22, 23 counties. The question is, if \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  runs too far away from of the Biden economic plan, the Biden legacy, will she be seen as too undefined, as too radical, as to San Francisco and not enough Scranton? So she's got to be careful.\par \par \uc2 I want to make one last point, because it bothers me tremendously. Here we have a woman running for president. Who is giving the advice to silence the woman? I find, not even as a woman even, I just find it as an American disgraceful and disingenuous, that we're so excited we have a woman, and nobody wants her to speak. They don't want her to speak to the press. They don't want her to speak under the wing of the plane. They don't want her to do softball interviews. It's a terrible look. Give Hillary Clinton her due.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020240820ek8j0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  To Address Crowd And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Releases Tax Proposal; Thousands Protest Israel Policies; Republicans Claim \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Committed Impeachable Offenses; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Speaks To Chicago Audience Tonight; Grappling With Dissent Within Party; High Costs Prompt Promises Of Change, Union Activism Led To Employer Funded Benefits; Republican Presidential and Vice Presidential Nominees \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and J.D. Vance Go On Battleground Campaign Tour During Democratic National Convention; Polling Shows Close Presidential Race between \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Despite \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Polling Lead on Economic Issues.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Mike Tobin, Chad Pergram, Grady Trimble,  Brit Hume, Harold Ford Jr. \par \uc2 7,959 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It's going to be great. All right, that's it for us. Have a great night. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It is going to be great. Hi, Dana, good evening, I'm Bret Baier. We are coming to you tonight from the United Center in Chicago, site of this week's Democratic National Convention.\par \par \uc2 You know, this was supposed to be President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's week in the sun. He was going to be nominated to try to win a second term as president, and the -- and the convention with a rousing speech on Thursday. Instead, he speaks on this, the opening night, and it's a speech that we're told will be an address focused on his legacy and at the same time, turning the page to a new generation of leadership.\par \par \uc2 One unusual item, delegates tonight are set to approve a party platform that still refers to President Biden's second term.\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, the Harris campaign announced as president, she wants to raise the corporate tax rate to 28 percent, another detail in the economic plan she started to unveil on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy is here in Chicago. He starts us off tonight live on the floor. Good evening. Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. A few minutes ago, we saw President Biden on stage doing a walk through ahead of his address tonight. And in a video that we have from this walk through, he appears kind of downtrodden, his mic wasn't turned on. He was barely speaking above a whisper.\par \par \uc2 But when somebody asked him if this is a bittersweet moment, he said, it is a memorable moment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, are you ready to pass the torch, sir? Are you ready to pass the torch, sir?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Biden aides tell us the president's pitch tonight will be with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , we must ensure democracy is preserved.\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN, SENIOR ADVISER, FUTURE FORWARD SUPER PAC: He's going to be giving a speech I think that will look very much forward as her campaign is into what comes next.\par \par \uc2 You know, this is not a time for legacy. This is a time for arguing why \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the best candidate.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Not according to the DNC's official platform, which was voted on July 16th, five days before Biden dropped out and still included 21 references to President Biden's second term when it was released with lines like this one on page three, President Biden, Vice President Harris, and Democrats are running to finish the job.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): You can tell right now by what very few proposals that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has put on the table. She doesn't have a clue.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The Harris campaign website still doesn't list any policy proposals, but officials tell us she wants to hike the corporate tax rate from 21 percent to 28 percent as a way to put money back in the pockets of working people and ensure billionaires and big corporations pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM MCGOVERN (D-MA): They will be fighting for a progressive agenda.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): In fighting has been a problem for the party for the last month in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Maureen Dowd writes, it's going to be a glorious coronation, except that everyone's mad at one another. The first lady's office now says otherwise.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don't see any hard feelings. I mean, I think that there's been way overblown.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Democrats concede intra party policy fights are unavoidable, but they can wait till Friday when the convention is over.\par \par \uc2 MCGOVERN: Look, I'll be the first to admit Democrats are not perfect, but you know what? They're nuts, and that's what this is all about here today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Biden's aides are telling us he's happy with the momentum the Harris-Walz ticket has been making since he dropped out, but -- and he is going to be the headliner tonight, but because his 2020 convention happened in a Delaware parking lot. He's never accepted a nomination for president in a crowded convention hall, and he never will, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter, another topic, what are we hearing about the attempted cyber attacks on the Harris and Trump campaigns?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: We have a new update in the last 30 minutes from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . They are saying Iran is responsible. They are telling us that the Iranians are trying to gain access to people with direct access to the campaign and to the staff using societal -- social engineering. And the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is warning that there has been increasingly aggressive activity in this way from the Iranians this cycle, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's one to watch. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 One of the big stories in this week's convention, the protests Democrats desperate to avoid a repeat of the 1968 Chicago Convention, which was disrupted by demonstrators. No indication that it's anywhere near that. The city's police department says it is prepared. There are actually police officers from departments around the country here, as well as the Secret Service and other federal officials, National Guard troops are on standby.\par \par \uc2 Senior Correspondent Mike Tobin has details tonight. Good evening, Mike, what are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Well, what I'm seeing, Bret, right now is kind of the diffusion of a situation here where the demonstrators had gotten down some three sections of the fence, and they got in within the inner perimeter of this anti-climb fence. They got in between the two fences there.\par \par \uc2 And what you see now is the aftermath. The police came down from both the east side and the west side, sort of pinched him in and forced him back outside of the fence.\par \par \uc2 This as we watch this coalition to march on the DNC and their big demonstration largely went off without a hitch, but there were -- there was a lot of pushing and shoving in the center of it, as you have some of these black clad anarchist or Antifa demonstrators trying to make a rush to the United Center and get into the DNC.\par \par \uc2 We've seen a lot of different people out here protesting for a lot of different causes, but I did find one man carrying a \uc2 Hamas\uc2  flag saying that he's not only here to support Palestinians, but he supports \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . In fact, he advocated October 7th.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: You support \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Every Palestinian support \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , not just me. Every Palestinian support \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: You support October 7th?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who?\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: October 7th?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: October 7? Yes, I do. What's wrong with October 7th? You tell me.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Women and children, well, it's a murder.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What is this (EXPLETIVE DELETED)?\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Now, with all the different causes getting in on the act, a very well-known wiener circle, known for rough language and good hot dogs, they got on it as well, offering free hot dogs to people who got abortions or vasectomies at a nearby \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic.\par \par \uc2 In fact, they posted that they've got a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  footlong special when in fact, the footlong is three inches.\par \par \uc2 But back out here live, the situation looked pretty tense. They got within the outer perimeter of the fencing surrounding the -- surrounding the DNC. Got to the softer fencing on the inside, there was a police presence here, a large police presence, but they kept it pretty light disposition.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): There was some riot gear, just in the form of helmets. I did see some things get thrown at the police officer. They get hit by sticks and signs and things of that nature, but they force people back out through.\par \par \uc2 In fact, I'm standing in one of the holes in the fencing right now. They force people back out through the fencing. They put that pieces, those pieces of fencing, back in place. The only one that remains open is the one I'm standing in right now, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hey, Mike, real quickly. I mean, they were saying tens of thousands, right now the numbers are pretty small, right? I mean, across the city so far?\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Well, as far as the number of demonstrators, I think that they are in the tens of thousands. As far as the agitators, yes, those numbers would be small.\par \par \uc2 And again, I'm not ready to call this violence in the protests, a lot of pushing and shoving. They did break the fence, and they did so throw some things at the police. We just saw them get hit in the helmets, with signs and sticks, things of that nature. But we've certainly seen more violent protests than this, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin, outside. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, House Republicans say President Biden committed impeachable offenses centering largely around the business activities of family members, including son \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , that final report out today.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram takes a look at that report tonight from Capitol Hill, good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. The GOP report says President Biden engaged in, "impeachable conduct". It comes as President Biden prepares to speak to the Democratic Convention.\par \par \uc2 Republicans accused the Biden family of using the president as a gateway for business.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republicans say the president violated the public trust. The GOP says bank records reveal his family scored $27 million from abroad since 2014, the report alleges that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and brother James Biden sold then Vice President Biden as a brand, access triggered payments to the Biden family.\par \par \uc2 Now, the top Democrat on the Oversight Committee, Jamie Raskin, says the report is full of, "pathetic innuendo".\par \par \uc2 One Democrat tells Fox that Republicans hope to close the Biden investigation now so they can turn to Vice President Harris and Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 . Republicans are wary of spending more time on the president.\par \par \uc2 Now, a senior GOP source says they lack the votes to impeach, plus they want to guard vulnerable Republicans from facing a tough vote, but hardliners could still compel an impeachment vote, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But bottom line, it's not going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Not so far.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Chad Pergam, on the Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It was a great honor and humility. I accept this nomination for president of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We have a chance to change the course of history. We're all in this fight. You, me and Joe, together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Much different convention back in 2020. Let's talk about what we can expect tonight from President Biden and the rest of the week here in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2 , is co-chair of the Harris-Walz campaign. Cedric, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND, CO-CHAIR OF THE HARRIS-WALZ CAMPAIGN: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you look at tonight? There's a lot of writing about what the president is going to say, and some call it bittersweet for him. How do you see it?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Well, look, if you know \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , you know that he's been through a lot of turmoil in his life. He's had a lot of serious losses in terms of his wife and his daughter and his son Beau. So, he's used to setbacks and he's used to real loss.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think he looks at this like this. I really believe that what he wanted to do was pass the baton so that we can put the American people first, he was -- he didn't want to fight Republicans and Democrats. I think he looked at the situation and said, the best thing I can do for the future this country is to exit the race, pass the baton, and put the issue and the focus back on the American people and not on me. And I think that's what he was thinking.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know there's a lot of Democrats, and I've talked to them that want to lift him up as a hero here making this choice. But there's a lot of people outside this building that think this was not a choice. This was a push out, and it's a tough thing to see for them.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: No, it was a choice. He still had the votes. We would still be here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You were out Saturday before the Sunday.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: I was.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He's the nominee.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: And we still had the votes. We still had the votes for President Biden to accept the nomination, and that would have happened, except he decided to bow out.\par \par \uc2 And so, when people -- when you hear people say that the president made the choice, he did, he made a real choice because we knew we whipped the votes. We knew he had the votes to get the nomination, but he decided to put people over party, people over politics and air on the side -- really focused on the side of purpose, and that purpose is the American people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me ask you about the website, the Harris-Walz website. There's some people pointing to the fact that there's just not a lot of specificity and policy.\par \par \uc2 You know, we heard Friday, some economic pitches some more today, but if you look at the website, there's really nothing there. Should there be?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Well, there will be, and let's just be fair about it, she got the nomination. Biden stepped out almost 30 days ago. She had to immediately start vetting and looking at running mate possibilities. I was on that team interviewing running mates, and that was a real process, and going through and interviewing a lot of great people, but figuring out who would be a great partner to deliver for the American people.\par \par \uc2 And since then, she prepared for the convention. So she's unveiled some economic policy, but you'll continue to see her roll out more policy as we go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not just running on the joy of this moment, because it seems like a lot of relief and joy that they're trying to kind of continue that momentum.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Well, it's a joyous time to be here, but this is not the victory party. This is the beginning of a race, and it is a close race.\par \par \uc2 You know and I know that polls oftentimes are wrong, and we know that this race is going to be tight, and that means both sides, Democrats and Republicans, but we know we have work to do to go out and talk about the issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking about the issues and polls, if you look at what -- who do you trust more to handle these issues and the top issues voters care about, the economy, inflation, immigration, Israel, the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war. You look at this, and the former president currently has big leads in all of these really important issues, yet the overall race nationally is tight. How do you read that?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Because the question should say, who do you believe cares about me the most? Who do you believe is actually invested in my wellbeing and will do the things necessary to uplift me and my friend, family, no matter if you're urban, rural, black, white, but who do you believe actually wants to see me succeed and not picking winners and losers? This is not about pitting black people against white people, immigrants against Americans. This is about the fact that we can all lift up together and tomorrow can be better than yesterday. That's the part I think most of the polls miss.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Are you concerned about protests? You saw that Mike Tobin piece. Are you concerned about that here in Chicago?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: No, I'm not concerned about protests. Look, I fundamentally believe people have a right to protest. People have a right to be mad. People have a right to do whatever they want to do, but they don't have a right to destroy property. They don't have a right to be violent and I believe that the mayor of this city has sent that message that we will respect your right to exercise your First Amendment right to free speech, but you will not destroy property, you will not hurt police, you will not engage in violence.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: These are live pictures outside. Obviously, you got a lot of people here to deal with this. I chatted with Governor Pritzker earlier. He says he's prepared.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you one other controversial question. Obviously, abortion is a big plank, and there'll be a lot of talk about that as an issue. What do you make of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  offering abortions and vasectomies outside the convention?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Well, I think that that was a store talking about, we'll give you free food.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, but there's a mobile unit, a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  unit.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Look, let me say something about this. The decision to have an abortion and terminate a pregnancy is a gut wrenching decision, and I don't think any either party should talk about it like it's an easy decision, or women or men come together and make that decision with their doctors or without their doctors, easy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's a gut-wrenching decision, and I think that's the part that we focus on as Democrats, is that we should respect a woman's right to make that decision. And then, there are a lot of people who need help to conceive, to have one of the best miracles in the world, which is to have a baby and to have someone who comes in the world, who looks like you and that you just want to nurture.\par \par \uc2 And IVF is important. But neither party should make light of the fact that it's a gut-wrenching decision. We believe that women should have that right to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lastly, you were on that V.P. vetting committee, and looking at those nominees. There have been a number of stories. We can go down the list about Governor Walz and they have been popping up. Do you think that vetting was full enough as you headed into this decision?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Oh, absolutely. I haven't been surprised about anything that I've seen yet just popped up.\par \par \uc2 I know Governor Walz. I served with him in Congress. He was the first baseman on the congressional baseball team, but he's a guy that truly cares about the country. And anybody who teaches our kids, coaches our kids, and volunteered to serve in this nation's army, I think you start off as a remarkable human being.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: By the way, Chad Pergram will say that Democrats lost after you left. I mean, they have not won that baseball game.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Well, that's true. They haven't. So, they are kind of --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Will you go and back?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: They are kind of in a rebuilding process.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Got it.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: But I do get -- take great joy in saying that Republicans never beat me while I was in Congress and pitching in the congressional baseball game.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: But on a serious note, I will say, after the shooting of Scalise and the Republicans at the practice, I saw unity, and I saw us come together. It's unfortunate that unity didn't stick.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: But it shows that we have it in us, and we should all search for those things that we have more in common than we have in different.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it sounds like common ground. Cedric, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Yes. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at some other headlines tonight. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  says Israel has accepted a proposal to bridge differences, holding up a cease fire and hostage release in Gaza. He is calling on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to do the same, without saying whether the agreement had addressed concerns cited by the terrorist group.\par \par \uc2 Former Congressman, Republican Congressman George Santos has pleaded guilty to committing wire fraud and aggravated identity theft in connection with the federal fraud case that led to his expulsion from Congress.\par \par \uc2 The New York Republican entered the plea in a courtroom in Long Island just weeks before his trial is set to begin.\par \par \uc2 Pioneering talk show host Phil Donahue has died. Donahue's daytime program, which originated from here in Chicago for 11 years, set the stage for \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  and others.\par \par \uc2 Donahue's show won 20 Emmys. He reportedly died Sunday after a long illness. Phil Donahue was 88.\par \par \uc2 And on stock market today, stocks bounced back. The roll on continues. The Dow gaining 237 the S&P 500 finished ahead 54. The NASDAQ jumped 245.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talk to Illinois Democrats about what issues are most important to them. And later, what the Republican ticket is doing to stay in the news during the Democrats convention week?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We brought you our latest Fox News Power Rankings, concentrating on the issues last night. Throughout the week, we'll delve into some of the issues, most important to Democratic voters, and how those come into play here in Illinois?\par \par \uc2 First, we look at health care. It is one of the top concerns for Democratic voters, according to our recent Fox News poll.\par \par \uc2 Health insurance was once a part of the workers' rights movements. Now, candidates are looking to lower costs and expand access.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We fight for the future!\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: A future with affordable health care!\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Over the past decade, health care costs have risen across the board.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We're going to bring down the cost of health care and housing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Prompting presidential candidates to promise changes to the nation's health care system.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How have unions influenced health care benefits over time here?\par \par \uc2 BOB REITER, PRESIDENT, CHICAGO FEDERATION OF LABOR: We negotiate with our employers to make sure our members get the best health care they can.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Bob Reiter\uc2  is president of the \uc2 Chicago Federation of Labor\uc2 , which represents more than 300 affiliated unions and 500,000 workers.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, the labor union has been really important for workers' rights and benefits here.\par \par \uc2 REITER: Oh, absolutely without a doubt. You know, I talk to members all the time about, if they didn't have their union, what that would mean for their retirement, for their health care, for them and for their children, and --\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Modern insurance for medical care began with actions from unions and organizers.\par \par \uc2 Following World War II, union activism and federal policy helped tie health coverage to the workplace. Health and welfare benefits were major factors in post war strikes, as unions began pushing for employer funded benefits.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Welfare of the worker. That's the magic of production.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): In the 1950s, the internal tax revenue service made employers contributions tax deductible. This eventually led to increasing support for employer-based insurance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And they are more likely to have those health benefits or retirement benefits today. Have you seen that change over time?\par \par \uc2 REITER: What I've seen change over time is the people, people looking at the union as the protector of those benefits. To make sure that they are still relevant in the workforce.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): In 2023, 96% of private industry union workers had access to medical care benefits, while just 69 percent of workers not in unions had access. For all workers, the uninsured rate has declined steadily with expanded access. But now, many candidates and voters say rising medical costs are the problem.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: With Union involvement, do costs come down?\par \par \uc2 REITER: You try to drive down costs, but make sure that we're maintaining high benefits.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Got it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Employer-based health care costs have risen in the past decade. The \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  says average worker contributions for single insurance coverage have increased by 40 percent. Out of pocket costs have also gone up by more than $600 annually.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JAY ROBERT PRITZKER (D-IL): I'm not willing to play political games with the health of Illinois families.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): In Illinois, health care costs have risen steadily, alongside the national average. But prescription drug costs in the state are among the highest in the nation.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: The pharmaceutical companies have been running the game for too long, jacking up the cost of prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Out of pocket expenditures have decreased over time as health insurance has expanded. In 1970 insurance covered just 42 percent of costs. It now covers around 72 percent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, do unions, and your unions that you represent still support employer-based insurance? And would they -- would they prefer kind of a Medicare for All plan?\par \par \uc2 REITER: We have unions that have really good and strong, robust health care funds. We also have unions that are incredible supporters of Medicare for All and making sure that there is always a strong public option, and those two things don't have to be exclusive to one another.\par \par \uc2 So, it varies based on the union.\par \par \uc2 It varies based on the Union, but I generally speaking, all unions support making sure that their members have health care options.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: The bottom line, and the most important, is that everyone have access to health care. That is the goal. That is the purpose for me, supporting the policy of Medicare for all.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Vice President Harris has since pulled back on that stance. A Harris campaign official recently told Fox, she will not push for single payer Medicare for all.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : She is flip flopping on every single thing she's ever espoused or believed in, the border, health care, crime.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): The Biden-Harris administration has recorded increased enrollment in Affordable Care Act marketplace plans and supported further legislation to close the coverage gap.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : There are many people in this country who spend a lot of money on private health care. It's the best health care in the world by the way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice over): Former President Trump has promised to repeal and replace the Affordable Care Act after an unsuccessful attempt in 2017. During his first term, the Trump administration implemented price transparency requirements in an effort to lower health care costs.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It's a very important thing that we've done here. I don't think it will be covered by you, but it will be in the years to come.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Up next, we'll check in on the Trump-Vance campaign. See what the Republicans are doing during the Democrats' big week here in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: DNC Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  taking the stage behind me. Former President Trump, though, not ceding the spotlight to Democrats this week. He was back in Pennsylvania this afternoon for a campaign event focusing on what he calls Vice President Harris's anti-American energy policies. FOX Business correspondent Grady Trimble reports tonight from York, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Former President Trump and his running mate Senator J.D. Vance are on the road providing counter programming to this week's DNC. Two weeks after Vice President Harris and Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  barnstormed battleground states, the Republican candidates are doing the same, hitting the rustbelt and east coast before a swing out west. After a rally in Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania, this weekend, Trump is back in the make or break keystone state at a manufacturing facility.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Our plan will massively cut taxes, unlock American energy, slash regulations, and bring back those beautiful words, "Made in the USA."\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The former president largely focused on economic policy in his roughly 50-minute speech, discussing plans to reintroduce tariffs to boost domestic manufacturing and increase U.S. energy production, a critical industry in Pennsylvania. Trump did at times deviate from prepared remarks, attacking Harris and Walz.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Between his movement and her laugh, there's a lot of craziness, I would say a step further than weird. "Weird" is a nice word by comparison.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Pennsylvania say a one-two punch from the GOP candidates with Vance visiting Philadelphia today. Both men criticizing Harris's newly unveiled plan to go after companies for price gouging and offer government assistance for new homeowners.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We don't need lectures on the economy from a candidate pushing communist price controls. Kamala has no idea what the hell she's doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: In an interview after the event, Trump told David Shepardson from Reuters that if he's elected he would tap \uc2 TESLA\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  for a cabinet position or an advisory role if Musk would do it. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Grady, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Our economic future never looked brighter. When Kamala and Biden came in, they quickly blew it all up.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL PALERMO, FORMER DEPUTY COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR AND ASSOCIATE COUNSEL TO VICE PRESIDENT HARRIS: He is going to of course distract from the convention this week. But the reality is that the momentum is so strong right now.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  SENIOR ADVISER: The Republican campaign has got to be disciplined and focused if it wants to drive home the message that she is responsible for inflation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, let's talk about where the race stands right now. Joining us tonight, political consultant Alex Castellanos, and Robert Wolf, CEO of 32 Advisors, former economic adviser to President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . Gentlemen, getting underway here, first night. Alex, let me start with you. These polls about who do you trust more to handle the economy, and every poll we see, at least so far, has the former president up significantly. The poll we have here is 46-37. And, yet, this race is very tight overall, if you look at the polls nationally. How do you explain that disconnect.\par \par \uc2 ALEX CASTELLANOS, POLITICAL CONSULTANT: As a consultant, I would be concerned, because I'd said, OK, people are aware of the differences on these important issues, the economy, inflation, the border. But it's only getting you to the 50-yard line, not across it. What is it that people are concerned about beyond that that you're not addressing that maybe \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is winning?\par \par \uc2 And I know we laugh at the joyous campaign, but people are so exhausted with both left and right, Republican and Democrat and the current chaos, maybe they hope she is something better, she's the future. Maybe the change will work. After all, she has proved herself entirely flexible. So maybe she will adopt. Unless I think the Trump folks address that, I don't know that can he offer a great vision for the future. But he needs to explain, she is kind of part of our current political illness, just another politician who will say anything.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, is that it, hope and change part two, Robert?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, 32 ADVISORS CEO: Actually, I think that's part of it. I think hope is always a good thing. But let's just talk about what former President Trump said. He talked about we want energy, an American energy boom. We have an American energy boom. We are more energy independent than ever before in the history of the United States. We actually supply over 14 million barrels a day, much more than under President Trump. We have more permits going out. Energy companies have more profits than ever. And they're investing, actually, in renewables because they see that as part of the future. When he talks about factories and manufacturing, there was a manufacturing recession under him. We've had 800,000 new jobs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In part because of COVID.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: OK. But let's talk about what happened post-COVID. We've had more factories being built, constructed under the Biden-Harris administration than in decades.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So why do people feel so bad about the economy?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I think that's a very fair question. And I think it begins and ends with inflation. Even though wages have outperformed inflation over the last 15 months, prices are still up north of 20 percent in the goods and services that we need. That being said, we need wages to continue and we need prices to continue to come down. I believe time is our friend, but it hasn't been easy, and I'm not saying it has been.\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: Robert, I think we are producing a lot of energy, but we could probably fix that with some good price controls. They do kind of empty the shelves and groceries. They do cause shortages and rationing. And if that's the policy that this administration is going forward with, I think this exposure that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is getting is not necessarily good for Kamala if we are seeing a lot of different positions on issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, quickly, President Biden, this dismount speech. It's kind of bittersweet. We heard Cedric Richardson on it before, Richards. There is a sad part of it because he's never had the big nomination night in a convention.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Listen, I've said this before, and you and I have talked about it. I love President Biden. When my dad passed away and he called me, it was probably the nicest call I have ever gotten. He's an empathetic man. I think is he a great job as the president. Unfortunately, when it came to candidate Biden the last few months, really it just didn't work. And I think we are at a situation today where he is ready to pass the torch.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And Alex, the same people, you know, were saying he could run circles around him and everything was fine. And now they are saying no, no, no, this is just passing the torch.\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: He is passing the baton tonight, but they do have to pry the baton from his cold, inert fingers. And that is not bittersweet. There's a little bitterness there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, guys, great having you.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, up next, the panel about President Biden's big speech and how progressives are flexing their muscles. But first, \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  has a lot going on besides being governor of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are at the Park Hyatt in Chicago. This was the first of the hotel chain's brand. It was built in 1980 and it has been partially owned by the Pritzker family since 1957. Donald Pritzker was the chain's president until his death when his son, \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 , was only seven years old. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 , of course, now is the Illinois governor, and his family fortune is part of what brought the DNC to Chicago. Pritzker is a noted philanthropist.\par \par \uc2 He's also spent a lot of money on politics, backed major campaigns in swing states like Michigan and Wisconsin. He also told President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  directly Chicago has the ability to fund the convention without incurring debt. So organizers have committed to raising at least $85 million to cover the costs under an agreement with the city of Chicago. Now, "The Chicago Sun Times" says, as of July, \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 , the governor has not contributed, although he is expected to.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have decided the best way forward is to pass the torch to a new generation.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has transformed this nation. We're going to give him his praise and his flowers tonight at the United Center.\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, FORMER BIDEN WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: You'll hear him tonight not so much talk about his legacy, but talk about the future, what America can be under Harris-Walz.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: He is angry. I think that may not manifest on stage tonight, but this was taken from him. He did not do this voluntarily.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight is President Biden's big speech as he hands the baton. This is "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ." "The Dems are delighted, but a coup is still a coup. Top Democrats are bristling with resentments even as they are about to try to put a united center on at the United Center in the Windy City. A coterie of powerful Democrats maneuvered behind the scenes to push an incumbent president out of the race. It wasn't exactly Julius Caesar in Rehoboth Beach, but it was a tectonic shift, and essentially, there are going to be serious reverberations."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume, Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com, Mary Katharine Ham, columnist for "Outkick", and Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE. Brit, what about that as we get away here?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, he did have to be pulled kicking and screaming away from the candidacy. But I think he did finally see the handwriting on the wall. He was going to lose. The whole senior hierarchy of the party thought he was going to lose. And there came a time when it began to look just absolutely inevitable that he would have to go. And in the end, he did.\par \par \uc2 It will be interesting to see how he frames it as he puts it here tonight. I think he will be gracious. I think he will reflect back, and I think the audience, so grateful now that he's gone, will reflect that in their response to him. It will be a pleasant night for him, I suspect. It could be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As we are officially getting underway, we'll listen to the spar tangled banner for a second.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I knew there was going to be a big finale, so we had to wait for that, Harold. These are, this is your folks here. What's going to happen here tonight.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: First of all, it's good to be here. Being in Milwaukee the first night when President Trump entered the arena after being shot, it was truly an American moment. We all had reason to be proud. I didn't think the turn of events would be as dramatic as we sit here tonight.\par \par \uc2 I think the baton handoff is going to be a successful one. He might have been dragged screaming and kicking to this point, as Brit, I think aptly raised. But if you look at the reverberations, which I think is how you led in, the down ballot pressures for Democrats have eased if not muted. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is running even in most states. It's going to be a tough, tough race.\par \par \uc2 I was interested to hear \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2 , the congressman say this isn't a celebration here, a victory celebration. This is an acknowledgment that we have a very, very tough race in front of us. We're going to have to enunciate and enumerate our ideas. And you talked about the lack of substance on the website. All of that has to come. But this convention here, this tonight is a very special night for Democrats, for us to thank a man who fought criminals, who fought dictators, who advanced good policy on the foreign policy front, and even advanced a lot of good domestic policies as well. And we'll do that tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Who Republicans today have 292-page report that says he should be impeached for a number of different things. Speaking of the quickness, to Harold's point, Mary Katharine, Democrats have this platform that they approve tonight that refers to Biden's second term several times.\par \par \uc2 MARY KATHARINE HAM, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Right. It belies the problem that Democrats have here. And although there will be happiness in the arena because they are pushing him off the stage and they're happy to be doing that, but we have gone from a cognitive decline issue to a cognitive dissidence issue. And the platform is reflective of that, because the reason it says President Biden's second term is because in actuality that's what this would look like. And it's something that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  does not want to be as associated with as she is even though she voted the tiebreaking vote for some of these signature pieces of legislation that voters are not happy with and did not improve things. So you've got this cognitive dissidence that will be part of this convention no matter what because voters are trying to figure out where you are and whether these things are separate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's interesting, Katie, is the effort to go after independents and moderates, yet, the strength, the power of the progressive side of this party, and they are flexing.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. Look, they have to speak outside of the rom tonight. Everybody here is trying to grapple with this plug and play with the new nomination, letting Biden go away, pretending that everything over the course of the last six weeks was perfectly normal in taking out the elected nominee and putting in a new one without a lot of transparency around why that actually happened or admissions.\par \par \uc2 There is still a lot of tension within the Democratic Party. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , for example, former speaker of the House, hasn't spoken to the former president ever since they pushed that campaign out to get him out of the top of the ticket and replace him for this convention.\par \par \uc2 But, in terms of what they are talking about this week and whether we will get any policy position, they have specifically been very vague on the vice president's agenda because, a, she is trying to run away from the unpopularity of the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  agenda. And, b, she can't fall back on her 2019, 2020 stances because they are very far left and unpopular with outside of the United Center here in Chicago. And those are the people she needs to win over.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're getting drips and drabs. Obviously, we had the economic rollout, Brit, on Friday. We have a 28 percent corporate tax rate today. Are we going to have little pieces of this along the way?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes. We're going to need more than pieces. I guess we will get some more of that.\par \par \uc2 But let's remember this about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She is -- this is the second presidential cycle in a row where Democrats have resorted to an emergency candidate. In 2020, it looked like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  was going to run away with the nomination, and the party elders in the hierarchy all thought he will drive us to a defeat. So, they turned the tide in Biden's favor in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 And the Democratic Party, which used to be the party that fell in love while the Republicans was the party that fell in line, fell in line. And that's what's happened here. Four years later, they had another candidate, another emergency, the candidate looked like he was a sure loser, as did \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  four years earlier, and they turned, in desperation, really, to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . It's no wonder they are still trying to figure out what the agenda will be, because they didn't want it to be her, and they are acting like they do now. That's only because of relief that Biden is gone.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They herded the cats very quickly, 48 hours to get that nomination. Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Please join Martha MacCallum and me tonight at 10:00 p.m. eastern for special coverage of the DNC. Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Arizona Democratic Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2  will join us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll take you on a very special trip. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT. We are cruising down the Chicago River thanks to Shoreline Sightseeing. It is a gorgeous day here. You know, the Windy City has played host to more conventions than any other U.S. city. This will be the 12th time that the Democrats have had their convention here, 26 overall. The Republicans have been here 14 times. The first, 1860 from President \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 . Most recently, President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  accepted the nomination here in Chicago. At that convention the original Broadway cast of "Rent" performed. A group of Chicago restaurants catered food for the press, and perhaps most memorable were the delegates doing the Macarena on the convention floor.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Maybe no Macarena this time. You can see the Trump hotel over the Chicago River. We didn't even plan that, but he will be watching, I'm sure, what comes out of the Democratic convention. And we have everything covered this week. We have got a special 10:00 p.m. eastern time prime time coverage. Martha McCallum and I will be anchoring that. We will bring you President Biden's speech as he says farewell to this nomination. This was supposed to be his convention, remember. He has been to almost every single Democratic convention since 1972.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we've got you covered and all week. And, of course, we will do it fair, balanced, and unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Tr