In Several Key States; A Look Back at the 1968 Democratic Convention; Chicago Bracing for Protests Outside DNC; Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  War Looms over Democratic Convention; Behind the Scenes at the 2024 Democratic National Convention\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Jacqui Heinrich, Trey Yingst, Robert Wolf, Jessica  Tarlov, Chad Pergram, Juan Williams, Brit Hume, Karl Rove \par \uc2 10,024 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 On the eve of the Democratic National Convention here at Chicago's United Center, the Harris-Walz ticket kicks off a bus tour through battleground Pennsylvania, a day after a fiery speech by President Trump at the heart of that state's energy sector.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: My plan will include new penalties for opportunistic companies that exploit crises. Prices are still too high.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Is Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  looking to distance herself from President Biden's economic policies?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Comrade Kamala announced that she wants to institute socialist price controls. This is the Maduro plan, Venezuela. Maduro plan of like the old Soviet Union.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: New FOX polls show it is still the top issue for voters across party lines.\par \par \uc2 We'll discuss the contrasting plans to help Americans beat rising prices with Harris-Walz national co-chair, Senator \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2 , and President Trump's running, Senator J.D. Vance. Both FOX News exclusives.\par \par \uc2 Then, new peace talks underway to finally end the war in Gaza as Iran refuses to back down from its promises to retaliate against Israel.\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel on the efforts underway to stop a wider Middle East war and how pro-Palestinian protests could disrupt this week's convention.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the parallels between 1968 and today. We'll take a look back at some of the darkest days of that turbulent year.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on a special edition of "FOX News Sunday" live from the DNC.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from the United Center in Chicago where Democrats will launch their national convention tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We have brand new FOX News power rankings out this hour and they show a tightening presidential race on key issues that matter most to voters. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still leads on the economy and immigration but \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has gained ground on those issues since rising to the top of the ticket and she's built up more voter support for her positions on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's lead on the economy is now just eight points, a seven-point shift in Harris's direction over where it was with President Biden. That tightening backed up in new polls from several key outlets.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will get reaction from Harris-Walz campaign national co- chair, Senator \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2 , and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's running mate, Senator J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to FOX News senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich here with us in the United Center.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Great to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 It is so hard to believe, it was just four weeks ago today that the president ended his bid for a second term and now he's at Camp David putting the final touches on a speech urging voters to bring the momentum behind his vice president to the finish line.\par \par \uc2 A "New York Times"/Siena College poll shows \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's candidacy put four Sunbelt swing states back in play for Democrats. Harris now leading Trump among likely voters in Arizona and North Carolina and also closing Trump's lead in Georgia and Nevada, consolidating support where Biden was slipping among young non-white and female voters.\par \par \uc2 But Trump still edges out Harris on the economy. In Pennsylvania, Saturday, he branded her plans as too liberal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This is communist. This is Marxist. This is fascist. But you know what, it's dangerous because she's saying that she's going to give away things that she'll never be able to get approved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The most controversial pillar of Harris's plan unveiled in North Carolina on Friday, expanding the authority of the \uc2 FTC\uc2  to crack down on corporate food price gouging while propping up smaller shops.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: As attorney general in California, I went after companies that illegally increased prices including wholesalers that inflated the price of prescription medication. I won more than $1 billion for consumers.\par \par \uc2 So believe me as president, I will go after the bad actors.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Critics though panned it as a poor solution to a problem not caused by corporate greed but big government spending and excessive regulation. But Trump veered from the issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Look, people say be nice. Have you heard her laugh? That is the laugh of a crazy person. You don't mind if I go off teleprompter for a second, do you?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  hates her. This was an overthrow of a president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: While Trump campaigns in Pennsylvania on Monday President Biden is not the main event here at the DNC. His speech is opening night set to frame the end of his race as a sacrifice for democracy under threat from Trump. But tributes around the convention center will make for a bittersweet handoff to the president whose own election was marred by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , DNC CHAIR: We didn't get to have a traditional style convention for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . It's like a big family reunion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So, Biden will speak on the opening day of the convention and then leave town. The White House is framing that as a symbolic changing of the guard, but there are also reports that he is not keen to thaw relationships with the party heavyweights who pushed him to end his race. Trump and Vance will spend the rest of the week stumping in Wisconsin, Michigan, North Carolina and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So we got a little bit of the economic plan from the vice president on Friday. Do you sense that we'll get sort of more meat on the bones policy stuff from her this week, or do you think we're going to stick to the themes that are positive and uplifting and stick with the momentum?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: I think this is going to be light on policy and heavy on navigating what's a really sensitive handoff from a convention that would have been Biden's four weeks ago, and trying to get that support, that united front and drive that energy through November.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. This summer is one for the history books.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: It is.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Glad we're along together to cover it for folks.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you, Jacqui. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 All right. Now to the Middle East where negotiators may finally be inching closer to a ceasefire deal after 10 long months of war in Gaza. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is there in Israel this weekend where we find FOX News chief foreign correspondent Trey Yingst, been covering it for months.\par \par \uc2 Hello to you, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning. After two days of talks, there is cautious optimism about the possibility of a ceasefire deal. We do know negotiators wrapped up meetings on Friday in Doha, Qatar. They will resume those talks again this week in Cairo, Egypt.\par \par \uc2 Now, during the conversations, the United States submitted a bridging proposal to bring Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  closer to a deal. A joint statement released by the Egyptians, the Qataris, and the Americans said the meetings were serious, constructive and conducted in a positive atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 Today, U.S. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  will arrive in Israel where he is slated to meet Monday with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 . And while President Biden said late last week that the parties were closer than ever to a ceasefire deal, initial leaks tell a different story. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is still upset about the status of the proposed agreement and has continuously said the Americans can't negotiate in good faith because they are supplying Israel with weapons.\par \par \uc2 Last week, $20 billion in weapon sales were approved by the Biden administration. But most of it will be used to deter and possibly fight Iran and its largest proxy \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . The Iran-backed group launched dozens of rockets into northern Israel this weekend as the Israelis continued extensive air strikes against \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , killing a top Radwan force commander.\par \par \uc2 For now, Iran itself appears to be backing off weeks of threats against Israel where officials in Tehran promised retaliation for the killing of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leader Ismael Haniyeh. This comes as the battle inside Gaza grinds on, many Palestinian civilians were killed over the weekend in Israeli airstrikes, and just yesterday, two Israeli soldiers were killed in an IED explosion -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Trey Yingst with the latest from Israel -- Trey, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, national co-chair of the Harris-Walz campaign, Senator \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2  of Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us. Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Thanks, Shannon. Great to be on with you. I look forward to seeing you in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will see you here.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let's talk about what we've gotten from the vice president so far, a bit of an economic rollout on Friday. Reaction, one of them post- speech was this, the times demand serious economic ideas but Harris supplied gimmicks.\par \par \uc2 They say her ideas would cost money but the campaign didn't specify in its offsets its economic plan rollout. Without them, her plan would cost $1.7 trillion in addition to the federal deficits over a decade.\par \par \uc2 They went on to say, even adjusting for the pandering standards of campaign economics, Ms. Harris's speech Friday ranks as a disappointment.\par \par \uc2 That's not a far-right appraisal. It's "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ". They're not buying it, Senator.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, Shannon, one of the most important pieces of Vice President Harris's economic policy rollout this week was the announcement that the 10 most expensive prescription drugs which have now had to negotiate with the federal government under Medicare because of a vote where she cast the deciding vote legislation that Biden-Harris passed and Trump would reverse, it's going to reduce $6 billion in federal expenditures, it's going to save seniors $1.5 billion in out of pocket costs, and there's more to come.\par \par \uc2 Her proposals included prescription drug price costs, incentives for housing production to make housing more affordable, support for the costs of child care -- Shannon, these are all things that middle class working Americans are concerned about and President Trump just this past week rolled out his proposal for essentially a national sales tax, a new 20 percent tariff on all imported goods. That would raise costs by thousands and thousands of dollars for every American.\par \par \uc2 Remember, a tariff is paid by consumers. It's the equivalent of a new Trump tax.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay.\par \par \uc2 COONS: And he didn't offer any offsets or how to pay for that either.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, we're going to talk to J.D. Vance about that coming up because it is something they do need to answer this issue about tariffs.\par \par \uc2 But on this issue of the prescription drugs, I think all Americans want to celebrate that, everybody needs relief in that -- in that place.\par \par \uc2 But the Committee For the Responsible Federal Budget says that is just a drop in the bucket to what the vice president proposed on Friday. It is not anywhere near an offset. And they talked about as well this issue of price gouging and of these, you know, housing $25,000 to a number of first-time home buyers. So let's walk through some of that.\par \par \uc2 On the issue -- issue of price gouging, there is a lot of pushback from economists across the board. One of them says this: when your opponent calls you communist, maybe don't propose price controls. They say it could lead to shortages, black markets and hoarding. At worst, it might accidentally raise prices.\par \par \uc2 And here's economist EJ Antoni on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EJ ANTONI, THE HERITAGE FOUNDATION ECONOMIST: Please explain to me how Ms. Harris's policies are not in fact aligned with the communist ideology when she's talking about trying to artificially set prices for, I don't care, if it's food or rent, you name it. If you employ the same policies as we have had in these other communist countries around the world, you will get the same results here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDOE CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Federal government tried it in the 1970s, Senator. You know it didn't end well and economists across the political spectrum say it's a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, let's be clear, I know \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  believes in the free market. What she put out in her proposals includes tax incentives for us to increase the production of housing, and yes, reductions in the prices that seniors pay for prescription drugs. I don't think there's anything communist about wanting to make housing more affordable and prescription drugs more affordable.\par \par \uc2 And as for an offset, former President Trump has made it clear --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But specifically to the issue of price gouging, if you're setting prices, a lot of people feel like that is communist in nature. The housing aside, let's talk about price gouging and price controls.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Shannon, presidents of both parties have tried to use the power of the \uc2 FTC\uc2  to rein in high prices at the pump, high prices at grocery stores. I think picking this one proposal of the many she's put out misses the broader point, which is that Vice President Harris is continuing the work of President Biden in reducing costs faced by working Americans.\par \par \uc2 And as for an offset, President Trump wants to make permanent the nearly $5 trillion in tax cuts for the wealthiest Americans and the most profitable companies that he passed on a party line vote in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Just last month --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let me --\par \par \uc2 COONS: -- Republicans in the Senate blocked a bill that came over from the House, the Republican-controlled House that would have expanded the child tax credit and small business credits.\par \par \uc2 We need to know why Republicans in the Senate want to block progress in reducing costs for small business and for families.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the good news is both tickets seem to agree they want to expand the child tax credit. So maybe --\par \par \uc2 COONS: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- there is a rare point of agreement between these two.\par \par \uc2 On the tax issue, because this comes up quite a bit, I want to point back to something in "The Hill". They cite government data and say this: Income data published by the \uc2 IRS\uc2  clearly show that on average, all income brackets benefited substantially from the Republicans' tax reform law with the biggest beneficiaries being working and middle income filers, not the top 1 percent as so many Democrats have argued. That's \uc2 IRS\uc2  data, Senator.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Yeah, and that's frankly just not true, that by dramatically --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is the \uc2 IRS\uc2  wrong?\par \par \uc2 COONS: -- cutting the corporate tax rate and by reducing taxes on the wealthiest Americans. The dollar benefits, they're talking percentages -- in terms of the dollar benefits, it is the wealthiest Americans who benefited enormously.\par \par \uc2 Here's the bigger point: it exploded our deficits. The national deficit and the increase in the national debt was greater under former President Trump than it has been under President Biden. And we need to find a path forward that pays for the tax incentives that we're talking about.\par \par \uc2 I would vote and I believe Vice President Harris would support for allowing some of those 2017 tax cuts to sunset as a way to increase things like the child tax credit incentives for the production of more affordable housing, and incentives for things like child care and a first child, which are a key piece of the Harris-Walz economic proposals that were rolled out this week.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's turn to foreign policy because I want to make sure we hit on this as well. We haven't heard much from the vice president about this. It clearly as commander-in-chief would be a very full table for her should she come into. It's something she's part of the administration handling now.\par \par \uc2 And there's been a lot of attention to the fact that, you know, when it came to Afghanistan the way and the timing and the way in which that withdrawal was done. She has stood by that decision.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something she said when asked about it in 2021.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN HOST: President Biden always said that he wants you to be the last person in the room --\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BASH: -- particularly for big decisions just as he was for President \uc2 Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BASH: He just made a really big decision, Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Were you the last person in the room?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BASH: And you feel comfortable?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So she stands by that. You know, of course, we lost tragically 13 young American service members and many, many more injured. We left tens of thousands of people behind that. We were told we were going to get out of that country. Why should American voters trust her as Commander-in-Chief if she stands by that foreign policy decision?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, two things. We have worked hard on a bipartisan basis to provide support for those who served in Afghanistan alongside us and to continue to get them out of the country. Tens of thousands have come to the United States over the past two years, and I think we need to continue to work to make sure that Afghans who come to the United States get the support that they deserve for the fight they were involved in alongside our brave troops for so many years.\par \par \uc2 Second, I was with Vice President Harris at the Munich Security Conference earlier this year and the previous year, where she gave strong, clear-eyed and forceful speeches about the importance of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , the importance of fighting for democracy, something that J.D. Vance does not agree with, that we need to continue to push back on Putin's aggression in Russia and to strengthen our alliances around the world.\par \par \uc2 The only time \uc2 NATO\uc2  deployed in defense of another \uc2 NATO\uc2  nation was to Afghanistan on behalf of the United States. That's the only time Article 5 was invoked and \uc2 NATO\uc2  troops deployed was after 9/11.\par \par \uc2 And I think the way that we do our best for the memory of those Americans and those \uc2 NATO\uc2  troops who served and made the ultimate sacrifice in Afghanistan was to continue to strengthen the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance, as President Biden has and as Vice President Harris will, and to not abandon our partnerships and our alliances with democracies, as former President Trump has threatened to do.\par \par \uc2 Look, Shannon, if you ask the former president's Secretary of Defense, National Security Advisor and Chief of Staff, a decorated Marine veteran himself, they are all not voting for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . There's a core reason for that. They don't think he should be anywhere near the Oval Office again. And I'd encourage you -- I'd encourage you to ask J.D. Vance about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said just this week --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 COONS: -- about the Congressional Medal of Honor, something that was denounced by the \uc2 VFW\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I have a lot of questions for the Senator coming up, but this is the visual we got this week. Three years out from Bagram Air Base, we've got the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  parading with our equipment, with our base that we left behind. That's a tough visual and one that people feel like this administration wholly owns, and that includes the vice president.\par \par \uc2 Senator, we'll get to all kinds of questions with Senator Vance, maybe some of yours. It's always good to see you, and we'll see you here in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, a close race in the battleground states, former President Trump's once dominant lead against Democrats fading as Vice President Harris tightens the key race in swing states.\par \par \uc2 We're going to bring in GOP Vice President Nominee J.D. Vance to discuss a lot of what we just talked about and the Trump campaign strategy. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY\uc2 , FORMER 2024 GOP PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is not an election for just the MAGA vote. Trust me, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has that. Republicans need to be fighting for suburban women, for college educated, for independents, for moderate Republicans and conservative Democrats. They want to be asked for that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former Presidential Primary Candidate \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  urging the Trump campaign and all Republicans to appeal to a broader group of voters than the already committed MAGA base.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Republican Vice Presidential Nominee and Ohio Senator J.D. Vance. Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH), REPUBLICAN VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: Thanks, Shannon. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's start there. This race is much different than what you were looking at a month ago. It is much tighter. You have said, "Republicans are going to need every vote they can get." You heard what \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  had to say. Here's what Karl Rove says echoing her. He's going to be on the show later.\par \par \uc2 He says, "Mr. Trump so far has largely focused on themes that appeal only to his true believers, childish insults and talk of stolen elections, January 6 political prisoners in a weaponized justice system. That won't win him a single vote. He doesn't already have."\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the faithful are with you. What more can and should your campaign be doing to reach beyond those boundaries?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, first of all, I think Ambassador Haley is right that we have to reach a broader group of voters, but I think that we're doing exactly that.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, if you look at what President Trump has been saying at his rallies, what I've been doing at my rallies and my press events is we've been making a very simple argument that when \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  became president or vice president, I should say, she caused policies that led to a rapid increase in inflation and a rise in chaos around the world. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, we had stable prices, we had prosperity, we had higher take home pay for American workers, and we had peace across the world.\par \par \uc2 The contrast is really remarkable. And importantly, Shannon, we actually have a vision for bringing down energy costs, for bringing down food costs, for making housing more affordable for American citizens, and we have been running on that. And of course, the media has been giving \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  a three-week love fest, but the unfortunate fact for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is her record is her record.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to prosecute the case that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  caused peace and prosperity, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  caused chaos and lower take home pay. I think that we get that message out there, Shannon. I feel very confident the American people are going to elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  president in November.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. I want to get to some of the policy, but first a little bit more on the campaign. Much has been made of the fact that \uc2 Corey Lewandowski\uc2  is returning to the campaign. President Trump is well known for doing some shakeups in the summer leading up to his elections in 2016 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 Here's an analysis from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." They say this, "With so many seeking his confidence, there's one thing that no one will tell him: What's most in need of fixing is the candidate himself."\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  says he's had these conversations with the is open, he's receptive to it. Who is the president listening to and are there going to be more changes at the top of the campaign?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, anybody who knows President Trump will tell you he listens to everybody. I think it's one of his great strengths as a leader is he solicits opinion from his campaign staff to the people who serve him his food at a restaurant. He's trying to get feedback from everybody because he actually wants to understand where the American people are on the issues of the day.\par \par \uc2 But look, you listen to one of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's rallies, go to his North Carolina rally from just a couple of days ago. He was talking about Americans suffering under higher prices. He was talking about the need to bring down housing prices so that young people can afford to get into home.\par \par \uc2 He was talking about the need to bring groceries down to the level that they were at when he was president, Shannon, because normal Americans could afford to put a nice meal on the table. These are very simple things. And I think that what a lot of the people who are giving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  advice would, I think, do well to understand is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is carrying the message out there to the American people every single day.\par \par \uc2 I'm doing it every single day, and we're just going to keep on doing it for the next 80 days. And that's how we persuade the American people to elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the next president of the United States. That's really what the campaign is all about.\par \par \uc2 I think we're very receptive to feedback. But at the end of the day, if you look at our messaging, we're focused on the issues that Americans care about, and we're going to keep on doing that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, our "Fox News" polling does show the economy is and that people do give you all the edge on that. And yet, this is what we have out from some new polling out of "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ." They say, "Ms. Harris is now leading Mr. Trump among likely voters in Arizona, 50 to 45, and is even edged ahead of Mr. Trump in North Carolina, a state Mr. Trump won four years ago, while narrowing his lead significantly in Georgia and Nevada."\par \par \uc2 What is the administration doing -- the campaign doing with that data as it comes in? I mean, these are critical states that you got to have to have a path to 270. Are there any pivots? Are there any, you know, reconfiguring of what you're doing in the strategy? Because you talk about your message, but is it not landing?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, let me say two things about this, Shannon. First of all, the polls tend to radically overstate Democrats. We certainly saw that during the polling of summer of 2020 and summer of 2016. And of course, a lot of those polls were wrong when it came to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 The second thing I'll say, Shannon, is what we have certainly seen is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  got a bit of a sugar high a couple of weeks ago. But what we've actually seen from our own internal data, Shannon, is that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has already leveled off.\par \par \uc2 If you talk to insiders in the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign, they're very worried about where they are, because the American people just don't buy the idea that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who has been vice president for three and a half years, is somehow going to tackle the inflation crisis in a way tomorrow that she hasn't for the past 1300 days.\par \par \uc2 Giving \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  control over inflation policy, Shannon, it's like giving \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2  control over human trafficking policy. The American people are much smarter than that. They don't buy the idea that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  represents a fresh start. She is more of the same. It is doubling down on the failed policies of the Harris administration to give \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  a promotion rather than defy her, which is what I think most Americans are going to do on November.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How does that not line up then with another poll we got out this morning? "Washington Post-ABC," they're giving the vice president nationally a four-to-five-point lead. I mean, those are new numbers. So if you think the momentum is not swinging or your internal polls are suggesting differently, every other poll that's been released has shown great momentum in her direction?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: You know, Shannon, I think there are a lot of polls that actually show her stagnating and leveling off. Of course, "ABC-Washington Post" was a wildly inaccurate pollster in the summer of 2020. And look, if you see the numbers that we're seeing and you actually talk to the American people, I feel extremely confident that we're going to be in the right place come November.\par \par \uc2 We can't worry about polls. We have to run through the finish line and encourage everybody to get out there and vote. But our message is going to be very simple. If you want to get back to the peace and prosperity, the rising take home pay, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  delivered it once and he can do it again. If you want to double down on the failed policies that have delivered higher food prices and higher housing prices, well, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been vice president for three and a half years, and I guarantee she's going to make the problem worse if the American people give for a promotion.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So let's get to this --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: The thing we have to remember -- one final point -- let me just say one final point on this, Shannon, because I think it's important. Consistently, what you've seen in 2016 and 2020 is that the media uses fake polls to drive down Republican turnout and to create dissension and conflict with Republican voters. I'm telling you, every single person who's watching this, the Trump campaign is in a very, very good spot. We're going to win this race. We just have to run through the finish line.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to give you a chance to respond to some of this back and forth on financial issues. This is what the Vice President had to say about what your ticket is planning for America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) U.S. VICE PRESIDENT & DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: He plans to give billionaires massive tax cuts year after year, and he plans to cut corporate taxes by over a trillion dollars even as they pull in record profits. And that's on top of the two trillion-dollar tax cut he already signed into law when he was president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: She also talked about the plans for tariffs, as you heard Senator Coons talk about. Other economists have said those tariffs, along with mass deportations that would impact the workforce, will also drive up prices for Americans.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, Shannon, I don't buy that argument because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was already president. He already secured the wall. He already implemented tariffs, which brought back 12,000 American factories were built during \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's administration and prices were low and take-home pay was high.\par \par \uc2 The entire argument of the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign boils down to forget how good things were when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president. Forget that we had rising take home pay. Forget that we had low inflation. Forget that we had peace all over the world. And somehow if you give \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  more power than she already has, she's somehow going to do something different than she's done for the past three and a half years, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 The most absurd thing that Kamala says at her rallies is on day one, I'm going to tackle the food and housing affordability crisis in this country. Shannon, day one for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was three and a half years ago, and everything that she's done has made the affordability problem worse. We cannot give her more power and more influence. She's just going to do more of the same, which has already made the affordability crisis for Americans very, very real.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll see if she presents something that's different or break with the Biden administration when she speaks here this week. But quickly before we go, I want to touch on foreign policy as well. Ukraine, you have been very vocal in saying, you know, the resources and the tax dollars that are going there.\par \par \uc2 You have serious questions about whether the American people are being told the truth about Ukraine's ability to actually advance there. Now we know there are all kinds of reports this week. They've actually advanced into Russia. They're taking prisoners. They're taking territory.\par \par \uc2 So, has your view on that changed at all? And what about Senator Coons' assertion that you and President Trump aren't going to be faithful to \uc2 NATO\uc2 ? You're not going to be faithful to foreign policy alliances and that allies shouldn't be able to count on you.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, what \uc2 Chris Coons\uc2  is saying -- look, I like and respect Chris, but he's just wrong here.\par \par \uc2 What \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I have said is that there shouldn't be a free lunch for Europe or anybody else. America is not the policeman of the world and \uc2 NATO\uc2  should be a military alliance where Germany, France and all these 