2 WILLIAMS: Yes. Well, I mean, to me, that's ridiculous. You think about the reality. She is saying to the American voter, I'm with you. I think a lot of these big corporations have huge profits. And I'm going to stand with you against price gouging. I'm going to stand with you in terms of childcare tax credits.\par \par \uc2 That's a message more than an economic policy. So you can attack the proposal and its minutia. But I think the message to the voters is pretty effective.\par \par \uc2 What we are really experiencing at this moment is a transformed race from when Biden was the candidate to when Harris is the candidate, and she is writing a huge wave.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's unbelievable. She pretty much has not made an error so far. And I think that's what we are dealing with.\par \par \uc2 And the question is then, where does the first stumble come? And can she recover from that stumble?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And, Jessica, it's hard to make a stumble if everything is scripted.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't think that that's fair for a candidate that's three and a half weeks old. I believe the story that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said, and the Biden campaign said, that this was his decision that he made on Saturday night. And by Sunday, he turned it over to her.\par \par \uc2 She worked the phones, right? She was calling hundreds and hundreds of people to get the endorsement. And now she's out on her own. And she agrees with a lot of what Biden, top of the ticket, had put out there.\par \par \uc2 But she is finding her own way. And she's changing the policies a little bit. And I know that she's been paying attention to the fact, especially on the economy, that she has room to change people's minds. They don't know her as well.\par \par \uc2 They associate the inflation with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . They don't associate it in the same way with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And we're seeing that in the Fox News tracker poll and the issues, right?\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is losing ground on the economy, on immigration. And I couldn't believe it that crime he only leads by five points. That's basically within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 Those are the bread and butter issues for him. And at the same time, she is adding to where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was.\par \par \uc2 Look at the abortion issue, which is the number one issue for a lot of Democrats. He was plus 12, she's plus 18. And it turns out listening to a woman talk about reproductive health makes a difference to people.\par \par \uc2 And then you have J.D. Vance on the other side with the childless cat lady stuff. And I'm feeling pretty good about where we are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So let me put this up. This is what we mentioned about the issues. So here's where President Trump has the advantage. It is double digits on border security, 19 points, immigration 14 points for policy, seven points economy, six points.\par \par \uc2 But the other side of that coin is that people are much more comfortable, and they trust president -- or excuse me, Vice President Harris on the issue of abortion, as you said.\par \par \uc2 I want to put this up, because this is a map of a number of states where abortion has been added to the ballot.\par \par \uc2 The green are -- listen, some of those very critical states, they already have ballot measures on abortion. The blue, they've got signatures and they might get it.\par \par \uc2 Brit, how dangerous is that for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I don't think it's big enough to surmount, under normal circumstances, the lead that the Republican Party and President Trump have on those other issues, particularly the economy, and border immigration, crime.\par \par \uc2 However, it's a big motivating issue for Democrats. And the one thing that's happened here is we got two factors in this race now that strongly matter to Democrats and encourage them.\par \par \uc2 One of them -- one of them is Trump. Trump has been the single most motivating factor for Democrats in the past two or three cycles, including the congressional cycles. So -- and that remains.\par \par \uc2 And the second thing is that they now have hope with Kamala for all her weaknesses, and they are manifold, and so far, being untouched by most of the media, a great blessing for her.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, it is a -- it's a source of hope. You know, people talk about this great Kamala boom and all the rest of it. I think it's a relief rally. Relieved that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who looked like a sure loser, is gone. And somebody, somebody, somebody with a strong voice, somebody who can smile, somebody who can read the teleprompter effectively, is up there, and that's enough to encourage the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And that's where we are. And the abortion issue is a part of that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Panel, do not go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 As the DNC braces for possible mass protests outside the perimeter here at the United Center, we look back at the tumultuous summer of 1968. And the historic clashes police had with the -- at that year of the DNC convention in Chicago. Will we get a repeat?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A live look here at the United Center in Chicago, the site of this week's Democratic National Convention.\par \par \uc2 Well, political observers were already making comparisons between 2024 and 1968 when Democrats announced their plans to hold the convention here in Chicago. Then there was the assassination attempt on former President Trump and the decision by President Biden to drop his bid for re-election.\par \par \uc2 Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram explores any kind of parallels between 1968 and this convention.\par \par \uc2 Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, it is said that history doesn't repeat itself, but it does rhyme. 2024 could rival the political tumble of 1968 as Democrats launch their convention in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): In 1968, President Lyndon Johnson bowed out of his re-election bid.\par \par \uc2 LYNDON JOHNSON, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT (March 31, 1068): I shall not seek, and I will not accept, the nomination of my party for another term as your president.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): President Biden's words this year echoed Johnson when he dropped out.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: So I decided the best way forward is to pass the torch to a new generation.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Political violence was a hallmark of 1968.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (April 4, 1968): \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  was shot and was killed tonight in Memphis, Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The assassination of \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr.\uc2  fueled riots. Palestinian Jordanian Sirhan Sirhan assassinated Democratic Presidential Candidate \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy\uc2  over his support for Israel in the 1967 Middle East War.\par \par \uc2 Anti-Israel demonstrations rocked the nation in 2024.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Free, free, free Palestine.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Free, free, free Palestine.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): And there's political violence.\par \par \uc2 Democrats pivoted to Vice President Harris after President Biden abandoned his re-election bid.\par \par \uc2 HUBERT HUMPHRY (D), FORMER VICE PRESIDENT (August 29, 1068): Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats switched to another vice president in 1968, Hubert Humphry. Harris is poised to secure the Democratic nomination in Chicago. The same city where Democrats convened the most volatile, political convention of all time.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM RIBICOFF (D), FORMER GOVERNOR OF CONNECTICUT (August 26, 1968): And with George McGovern as president of the United States, we wouldn't have to have Gestapo tactics in the streets of Chicago.\par \par \u