ier tonight, a guest on "The Ingraham Angle" show with Laura had this to say about Walz's time in the Guard. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yeah, he was in for 24 years and he quit, well, okay, so that's what you did. But don't try to make it look like you were a command sergeant major, don't make it try to make it look like you were going to some place that was in support of Operation Enduring Freedom. I mean, granted, you know, there's that -- that's just all embellishment and lies to try to make things look better coming up too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We've also uncovered a 2005 press release from Walz's congressional campaign which he is quoted as saying: As a command sergeant major, I have a responsibility not only to ready my battalion for Iraq but also to serve if I am called. I am dedicated to serving my country to the best of my ability, whether that is in Washington, D.C. or in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 And again, to reiterate, he instead chose to retire, got demoted from that rank as a result, never deployed. A statement from the Harris campaign also confirms that after 24 years of military service, Walz retired in 2005 and says he will continue to be a champion for veterans should he become vice president.\par \par \uc2 All right. Here with the full report analysis, John Solomon, justthenews.com. And by the way, National -- Minnesota national guard veteran himself, our friend, our colleague, "FOX & Friends" weekend co-host Pete Hegseth.\par \par \uc2 John, let's start with the news with you. What do we know? Because didn't he also say that 18 to 24 year -- oh that was Kamala who said that they were stupid and didn't he also say that 19 year olds in the guard are basically cooks? And didn't he once say that -- oh that's right, these are the weapons that I used when I was in war. Did he say that too?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SOLOMON, JUST THE NEWS EDITOPR IN CHIEF: He did. Yeah, listen, he's sometimes a comedian and but -- in this case, he's a charlatan. His own official biography on the state of Minnesota government website, the governor's website, falsely claimed that he retired as a command sergeant and he was not. He retired as a master sergeant, not as a command sergeant major. That's a really big difference.\par \par \uc2 The reason he retired at the lower rank is he was demoted, because he didn't complete his required training and other obligations when he left the military before being deployed to Iraq and instead he ran for Congress.\par \par \uc2 If you can't get your basic record right in the military, not only are you disserving the American people, you're dishonoring veterans who had that rank or who did stay in, who did go into combat. I think it's is going to become an issue that won't go away.\par \par \uc2 By the way, you were talking about the media. "The Hill" tonight is still reporting the old story. It's still reporting -- other newspapers still reporting he retired as a command sergeant major. He did not. And I think that that's important.\par \par \uc2 It's on the record from the National Guard. It isn't that hard for journalism to get it right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. Pete, is this a case of stolen valor as -- well, people he served with now claim and did he also lie when about being the head coach?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, CO-HOST OF "FOX & FRIENDS" WEEKEND: He did. He was an assistant coach. He was a linebacker's coach, a defensive coach. That's great. That's important, but not the head coach.\par \par \uc2 John Solomon is completely correct, this is a case of stolen valor, Sean. I don't say that lightly. This is a guy who ran for Congress in 2006 while his men were doing 22 months in Iraq, 22 months the Minnesota National Guard ended up doing in combat in Iraq. And you know what? They can't do when they're deployed, they can't speak. So they couldn't contradict the claims he was making that he was the highest serving enlisted member ever to be elected to Congress.\par \par \uc2 He retired when he was a command sergeant major, but he was actually just a sergeant major, and to keep that rank, you have to complete the sergeant major's academy, which he never did, which means when he quit he gets demoted to E8, was a master sergeant. He knew that when he retired. He knew it when he claimed to be a command sergeant major. It was all a lie and that has absolutely stolen valor.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, he likes to say he was in support of Operation Enduring Freedom. I deployed to Guantanamo Bay in 2004. We were not in combat. We were in support of what were they're doing in Afghanistan because they were sending the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  over. I never claimed to be in combat.\par \par \uc2 Yet, he all -- pull the tape, let's find him, Sean, they're all over the place. He's -- he's convoluting his words about whether he was in combat or not. This is all words though, Sean. Okay, I got more though go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. So, no, no, no, okay, I would normally serve anybody as -- I would normally praise anybody that served in the Guard. I normally would. But he referred to guardsmen as 19-year-old cooks. And then he said, we can make sure that uh those weapons of war I carried in war is the only place where those weapons are at.\par \par \uc2 This is what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALZ: We can do background checks. We can do CDC research. We can make sure we don't have reciprocal carry among states, and we can make sure that those weapons of war that I carried in war is the only place where those weapons are at.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay. Well, was he ever at war?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Never carried a weapon in war. Never, never, he was in Italy for six months, supported, that's fine, total lie and he knew it at the time the entire time.\par \par \uc2 He was -- he was the command sergeant major. He's in charge of the logistics, the training, the emotional support, the preparation for every man in his battalion, a field artillery battalion, a very important role if you're headed to Iraq.\par \par \uc2 And when he got the orders and the notification that his men were going to combat, he said, I quit, I'm out, I'm done.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And he had two years left?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: That's exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And he had two years left?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: He had two years left in his contract, but he was beyond his retirement so he could work his way to get out of his contract which he did.\par \par \uc2 You know what the real -- you know, what the real phrase is for guys that serve with \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is, no, it's Tim AWOLS. Tim AWOLS. When his men needed him, he left, he bailed, he did not deploy. He spent 24 years in the military, Sean. You go with your men to combat if you care about those men. But if you don't, you care about yourself in politics, you stay home and that's what \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  did, and that's why so many people are frustrated and angry about this. He's Tim AWOLS in my book.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It's interesting. The lie about \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  ended Dan Rather's career over a similar issue.\par \par \uc2 My question is, is this stolen valor, John Solomon?\par \par \uc2 SOLOMON: Listen, it's a lie to the American people and it's a dishonor to every person who served in the ranks and actually deployed and actually had the rank of a command sergeant major. He'll have to be held accountable, the American people have to hold him accountable. Let's get the media getting reported. Pete has it exactly right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Just exit yes or no, stolen valor, Pete?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Of course, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 When we come back as we continue our vetting of Governor Walz -- well, we'll take a look at his extreme positions on abortion, transgender kids issues with Elizabeth Hasselbeck, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And tonight, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 's radical stances on abortion and gender issues are under scrutiny. Just last year, he signed a bill allowing unlimited abortion access in the state of Minnesota which includes no limitations on when in the pregnancy a woman may have an abortion given the option of an abortion up until the moment of birth, radical, extreme, dangerous. I keep reminding you.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, just earlier this year, in a bizarre, unprecedented move, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  became the first sitting vice president to visit an abortion clinic when she stopped at a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic in Minnesota, alongside her future running mate, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And after a year of anger and fear-mongering from the left following the Dobbs decision, a new study found that the number of abortions has actually gone up, since Roe is overturned. But it's not just abortion, the new Democratic Party ticket has taken an extreme stance on.\par \par \uc2 In just March of last year, Walz signed an executive order, doing what? Protecting trans procedures on minors.\par \par \uc2 Here -- and by the way, without parental consent. Here with reaction, broadcaster, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestselling author, Elizabeth Hasselbeck.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth, let's get your take on all of it. Let's start with no parental notification on gender affirming surgery and treatments?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH HASSELBECK, NEW YORK TIMES BESTSELLING AUTHOR: Sean, thank you for having me tonight and hello to all your viewers there. Number one, I'd like to say if we learn anything from the Olympic, Sean it's that performance matters and since the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Walz ticket has no performance to run on because they let cities burn down and our border be open and fentanyl and illegal weapons come in and threaten our children, our teenagers, our college kids, why would we think they would protect our children when it comes to their God-given identity, or their right to life?\par \par \uc2 It's low expectation when it comes to their radical riptide. So that's what they're running on, a radical riptide, hoping to drown voters so that they can't see nor breathe properly to cast a vote for anything that resembles an ounce of integrity.\par \par \uc2 Here's my issue, Sean, I -- this is an attack on parenthood. I would normally, say, take a breath, swim to the side out of the riptide and don't get emotionally distracted. But this is an attack on the value of life and this is an -- this is an important matter to God. This -- this is God's creation, God's masterpiece, our children, pre-born children and children out of the womb to confuse them about their gender. God will handle that, he's sovereign.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to abortion and I do believe we need to keep an eye on the moderate Republican \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 -Vance ticket that right now just needs to kind of sit back and let crazy look crazy. They're doing that enough.\par \par \uc2 But I -- it is important to note that not only are they creating a force field behind more abortions in their state, and I read some research today that 5,000 women who went into have an abortion said they just didn't feel like having another child. They will highlight situations that seem extreme.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, Sean, what's deeply concerning is that they're defund funding pregnancy centers. These centers are ones that I've worked with on a national level that actually comfort women, don't cast shame on them, when they find themselves in a situation with an unplanned pregnancy, unexpected pregnancy, they provide them sustainable support and equipment to have an awesome peace filled life, with their child, because God cares about both the mom and the child and they have options and support.\par \par \uc2 I'm -- I'm deeply sadden that this is increasing and that -- but not surprised that these -- the radical agenda is running on this. Sean, they're just not supporting it, they're running on this. Actually, they should fear the day they meet their own maker. I would love to know of Walz's wife if she took prenatal vitamins at any point.\par \par \uc2 Do they value life in the womb as they should? It has a value. It's an amazing creation by God with the plan and a purpose.\par \par \uc2 And if there's a woman out there who needs to know that she's valued without shame, she should go to these pregnancy centers, and Americans watching -- if you feel like there's nothing you can do, go support them, go donate to the pregnancy centers that he's defunding in his state and put some -- put some money behind them because they're doing good things in supporting women.\par \par \uc2 Our family has decided to walk this out in a different way. We have taken in and it's been a blessing to us newborns who would have otherwise potentially been aborted but they found their moms found pregnancy centers that supported them and for the first month of life, that mom just needs to know that their support, their sustainability with the plan, that their loving arms to hold her baby, and maybe she's reunited in some cases, and maybe that baby is placed.\par \par \uc2 But there are options out there, that this radical rip tide once eliminated and we need to stand for our children.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, it's just sad. I mean, okay, up to the moment of birth, talk about radical abortion, no parental rights, something as serious as that. By the way, for the record, I think that was more interrupted talk time that you had than all your years combined on "The View". I'm just saying, without somebody jumping in and annoying you, and annoying anybody that was watching. We appreciate you being with us Elizabeth Hasselbeck.\par \par \uc2 All right. When we come back Virginia Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  is taking a major step to ensure election security in his state. I hope the other state governors paying close attention, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Earlier today, Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  of Virginia issued an executive order to codify Virginia's election security procedures that his administration put into place. The order will protect legal voters through what are stringent ballot security and voter list maintenance among other things. In a statement, the governor said, quote, this isn't a Democrat or Republican issue. It's an American issue and a Virginian issue.\par \par \uc2 Here with more, Virginia Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, I hope every governor in the country is watching.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN\uc2  (R-VA): Well, Sean, call me crazy, but I think American elections should be decided by American citizens and Virginia elections should be decided by Virginians. And that's why this executive order is so important because it does make sure we have clean voter roles. It makes sure that votes are counted accurately and it recognizes the fact that elections need to be trusted by voters and that's exactly what we're doing in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is going to be the most important election of our lifetime. We're going to see the future of America determined, and I want to make sure that Virginians know they can trust this election process, the majority in our house is at stake, the majority in our Senate is at stake and, of course, we've got a giant -- giant decision in front of voters.\par \par \uc2 Do they want strength in the White House or weakness? And, of course, what we've seen out of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is continued weakness -- weakness at the border which has unleashed chaos, weakness in economic policy which has unleashed inflation and, of course, weakness in national security which is at the root of the war we're seeing in Israel, and the war we're seeing in Ukraine, and, of course, China's threats against Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 So elections have consequences and I want to make sure that everyone trusts that in Virginia, when citizens vote, their votes will be counted accurately.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You mentioned energy, you mentioned the economy. You mentioned America's role in the world.\par \par \uc2 You know, I add two other issues to that and one would be law and order and the is other issue would be their war on energy. That scares me, Governor.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Yeah, it does. I mean, of course, what we have seen is the Biden-Harris administration do everything they can to undermine American energy dominance which we were energy independent coming out of the Trump administration. And now, they've done everything they can to put us at the -- at the beholden call of foreign actors and we have to get this back.\par \par \uc2 Listen, we have an all-American, all of the above energy policy in Virginia because we need it all. We need gas. We need clean coal. We need, of course, nuclear energy that's that will be the base load power of the future.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, what -- what the Biden-Harris administration has routinely done is undermine not only our energy policy but as a result America's strength around the world. This is a clear -- clear moment for Americans to choose a future which is based on America's capabilities and to stop exporting all of our strength to other nations, and take it back.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will do that. We can not only retain our majority in the House. We can flip the Senate and we can put strength back in the White House.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, if you have illegal immigrants given legal driver's licenses like Walz will give illegal immigrants in the sanctuary state that he loves in Minnesota, and free health care and free college education and you get a license, if you once you have a legal license can't you register to vote, oh, and a bit of trivia. You may not know \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , one supported paper ballots.\par \par \uc2 Wow. I wonder where she stands today. I bet that's another flip-flop.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Yeah. No, Sean, this is why this -- this executive order was so important. We have 100 percent paper ballots in Virginia and they are counted accurately and this next step that we have taken to have daily, daily scrubbing of our voter rolls to make sure that, in fact, if an illegal immigrant e either mistakenly or maliciously registers to vote, that person will be cleaned off the ballot immediately and turned over to our prosecuting attorneys to make sure that they are prosecuted to the full extent.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, the Democrats don't want proof of citizenship. They don't want voter ID. They don't want signature verification. They don't want partisan observers watching the voting all day, the counting all night.\par \par \uc2 Governor Youngkin, great job. I hope every swing state governor pays attention. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Squad member \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  lost her primary last night, and kind of lost her mind, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. The radical squad, they lost a second member last night. \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  was ousted in her primary and instead of accepting a loss with grace, well, she made this unhinged concession speech. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MO): I guess I don't have to worry about those strings attached is much as I love my job, but all they did was radicalized me. So, now, be afraid.\par \par \uc2 AIPAC\uc2 , I'm coming to tear your kingdom down. And let me put all these corporations on notice, I'm coming after you, too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I think she radicalized herself.\par \par \uc2 That's all we have left for this evening. Thank you for making their show possible. Please set your DVR, so you never missed an episode.\par \par \uc2 Please let not your heart be troubled. Greg Gutfeld is standing by. He will put a smile on your face. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020240808ek8700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Sen. Chris Coons (D-DE)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,551 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 July 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: A majority of Americans want to see this. They want to see some form of reform.\par \par \uc2 And the president is certainly -- want to make sure that he is where a majority of Americans -- I will say, and I talked about this a little bit yesterday when we were in a gaggle -- a range of conservative legal experts and Republican elected officials have voice support, for example, term limits, which was part of what the president laid out yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, well, a lot of people might support what the president wants to do with some change in the Supreme Court, but far from everyone, and certainly deemed dead on arrival in the House by the speaker there, and the Republican leader saying much the same thing in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We thought we`d touch base with Senator Chris Coons at Delaware, very instrumental in the Biden campaign, very instrumental in this campaign to revamp the court.\par \par \uc2 Senator, always great to have you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: First off, on this, it`s deemed to be a Herculean attempt that`s not going to succeed, no way, at least this year, with this president, right through next year. So what do you think?\par \par \uc2 COONS: So, the question of Supreme Court ethics and transparency is one that, just two years ago, I introduced a bipartisan bill in the Senate with my colleague and friend Senator Cornyn of Texas.\par \par \uc2 It passed unanimously, and it was signed into law by President Biden. That bill required all federal judges, including Supreme Court justices, to report on any stock transactions, any stock holdings in any businesses that might appear before them.\par \par \uc2 Here we are, just a few years later, and a similar bill on Supreme Court ethics and recusal and transparency passed the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  on a straight party-line vote.\par \par \uc2 That`s because there`s now been reporting that one justice, Justice Thomas, accepted hundreds of thousands of dollars of gifts, of travel, of hospitality, of physical assets, a trailer and a house, that he never disclosed or reported, and then didn`t recuse.\par \par \uc2 I do think that, more broadly...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But this measure is much bigger than that, right, Senator?\par \par \uc2 The ethics issue that you addressed is something worth addressing. You`re quite right. But this goes into term limits. This goes into dealing with decisions that have already been made and getting new laws and amendments in the Constitution to do it. This is much bigger than what you originally came up with. This goes way beyond ethics and things like that, right?\par \par \uc2 COONS: So, yesterday -- yes, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, President Biden spoke at the LBJ Library in Austin, Texas. He was commemorating the 60th anniversary of the Civil Rights Act. And, in that context, yes, he made several proposals about the Supreme Court and ethics and recusal, about term limits for justices, a practice that is widely adopted, in fact, uniformly, among other advanced democracies, and some other proposals.\par \par \uc2 I was focusing on one where I have recently moved legislation through the Senate and one where I think we could still take a vote this Congress and should take a vote this Congress.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So let me take a look at the bigger picture, your relationship with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . You were obviously concerned about this pile-on with him or what appeared to be a pile-on.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know what was presented to the president to say that he had no chance and should step down. We still don`t know the details. To be fair, Senator, the president himself really never explained it in the speech.\par \par \uc2 Was it that? Was it this issue where he was shown polls that, with him at the top of the ticket, the ticket and the downticket candidates would all lose, and that it had to do with his cognitive abilities, the people who thought that he wasn`t up to the job? Was that what undid him?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Look, Neil, I`m not going to get into private conversations I had with the president.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this. What unites all Democrats is clarity that J.D. Vance and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are not the best leaders for our country moving forward. In fact, in some critical ways, they pose a real threat to our standing in the world, to our opportunity and our prosperity and our security here at home, and that he endorsed his vice president, his trusted partner in everything that the Biden/Harris administration has gotten done in this term.\par \par \uc2 And he trusts that she is the one best position to finish the job and to move forward. We here in the Senate, Neil, in just two days are going to vote on an important piece of tax legislation. It passed the House overwhelmingly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 COONS: And it would continue some of those core Biden/Harris policies, a child tax credit that would help advantage families with children and a small business tax credit that would help with innovation, investment in manufacturing equipment, in research and development.\par \par \uc2 My hope is that, if Republicans here in the Senate are pro-family and pro- growth, that they will join Democrats in voting for this important piece of tax legislation and we won`t let electoral concerns get in the way.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I don`t know how that will go, Senator. You know the mechanics better than I do.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you did sound a little upset and bitter about the way many in your party treated the president.\par \par \uc2 To others, it looks like a quick switcheroo to do better in the general election, but that there`s not a lot of loyalty in your party, and that you have a bad poll, a bad debate -- I know this debate was particularly bad -- you can be thrown out on your heinie, and Democrats don`t have much loyalty toward each other.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, Neil, I would disagree.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I`m wondering if you felt the same way. Do you feel the same way, that the party was fair to the president? Because, literally hours before this move, the president was saying he was going to run. Something happened.\par \par \uc2 COONS: So, Neil, the outpouring of affection and support for our president, the number of members of Congress and longtime supporters, Delawareans and world leaders who`ve reached out to him, many of whom have reached out to me, to express gratitude for his service, his lifetime of service, and for the incredible difference that he`s made, has been heartwarming.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, was he bitter about it? Are you bitter about it, Senator?\par \par \uc2 COONS: No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Was he upset about it? Was he upset about it? Were you upset about it?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Neil -- Neil, I was with him yesterday.\par \par \uc2 He is looking forward. Part of the point of the speech he delivered in Austin yesterday was to lay the foundation for what he intends to do, both in the last six months of his presidency and after the presidency.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing he did yesterday is just a reminder of Joe Biden`s spirit and personality. After we went to Austin, he flew to Houston to pay his respects for a member of Congress who recently passed. Any other president, knowing he wasn`t running again, knowing there wasn`t any political upside, might have made a brief appearance.\par \par \uc2 President Biden went and spent 45 minutes with the family, even though it was 10:00 at night, and we didn`t get back to Washington until well after 1:00 a.m.\par \par \uc2 One of the things I have always admired about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is his compassion, his commitment to others, his grace. And so I spent the day with him yesterday. He is optimistic about the future. He is supporting \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  for president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think the party returned that -- do you think the party returned that compassion and grace? The rap is no. It looks kind of conniving and opportunistic.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, many of us who support \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as our president and who look forward to his leadership in our party at the convention this fall and next year and into the future...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 COONS: ... are looking forward to demonstrating just how much we respect and admire our accomplished president, who`s done so much on the world stage and here at home for America, for our middle class, and for our security.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Well, you have been a loyal friend, and that is an understatement.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Chris Coons, thank you very much. Good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240731ek7u0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Speculation Over \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Vice President Pick; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Proposes Major Changes To \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 ; Timeline Of Shooter Actions On Day Of Butler Rally; 12 Israeli Children Killed In Rocket Attack; Conservative Concerned Vance Struggles Will Continue; Vance Quips Blamed For Eclipsing Focus On Harris; Trump Campaign Reaffirms Support For Vance; Favorability Rating For Presumptive Democratic Presidential Nominee \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Increases In Recent Polling; Firefighters In Northern California Reporting Some Progress In Containing Wildfire That Has Now Burned Almost 400,000 Acres; President Biden Calls For Terms Limits On Tenure Of Supreme Court Justices; Republican Presidential Candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  And Republican Vice Presidential Candidate J.D. Vance Attack \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  On Campaign Trail\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Trey Yingst, David Spunt, Jeff Paul,  Mike Emanuel, Brit Hume, Bill Melugin, Juan Williams, Mark Penn, Katie  Pavlich \par \uc2 7,805 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And so do your civic duty. It's on InStyle magazine with Christina Perrier. She was a great interviewer and appreciated the opportunity there. Also, Perino on Politics with Michael Duncan just posted.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm sorry, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I like Michael Duncan.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, he's good.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We're just not going to be able to give you the last word. I apologize.\par \par \uc2 That's all for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'll wait, Jesse, if you want to wait.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No, please, I insist.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. I mean, I'll wait.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I insist.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we're covering two major stories, growing concern tonight that the conflict in the Middle East may expand and expand rapidly. Israel is warning of a severe response to a rocket attack from Lebanon that left 12 children dead.\par \par \uc2 Here in the U.S., we just heard from President Biden for the first time since he explained to the nation why he was dropping out of the 2024 campaign. Some people say he didn't explain enough.\par \par \uc2 But today, he did speak at the commemorative library of the last president to make such a move. President Biden using that forum to explain his plan for a massive overhaul of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 But first, Vice President Harris seconding the motion by her boss to overhaul the High Court as she gets ready to continue her campaign on the way to the Chicago convention for the DNC.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Jackie Heinrich has that part of the story tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The vice president remained in Washington today laying low with no public schedule as her boss made his first public remarks since dropping out of the race. Behind closed doors, though, her V.P. shortlist is said to be significantly narrowing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Vice President Harris backing President Biden's calls for sweeping changes to the Supreme Court Monday, her campaign saying its fairness has been called into question after numerous ethics scandals and decision after decision overturning long standing precedent. That is why President Biden and I are calling on Congress to pass important reforms.\par \par \uc2 Harris signaled openness to expanding the court during her 2020 campaign, but today indicated legislation is the preferred vehicle to restoring precedent reacting to Iowa's six week abortion ban taking effect.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What this means is that one in three women of reproductive age in America lives in a state with a Trump abortion ban. So, what we need to do is vote.\par \par \uc2 When I'm president of the United States I will sign into law the protections for reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Harris enjoying new energy within the Democratic base, her campaign taking in more than 200 million in donations and 170,000 new volunteers in its first week, but her biggest decision lies ahead. The shortlist for vice president said to be narrowed to three, Pennsylvania Governor \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , Minnesota Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  and Arizona Senator \uc2 Mark Kelly\uc2 . Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , who might have made for a two woman ticket not interested.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): The vice president has got a great group of people from whom she's going to pick her running mate. And I know that I can be the best ally on the ground in Michigan as a co-chair of this campaign as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Now, according to Politico, progressives are pushing walls, citing Kelly's soft support among labor groups, and Shapiro's condemnation of anti-Semitic protests on college campuses. He is a Jewish man by the way.\par \par \uc2 Notably, Kelly is now voicing support for pro-union legislation that he once resisted and Shapiro got the backing of two major Pennsylvania education groups, teachers unions, despite his past support for school vouchers, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All jockeying there, Jacqui, Vice President Harris has enjoyed some wave of support from Gen Z, it seems on people on TikTok, repurposing some of her most mocked moments into really viral support on those videos. But are there risks you know on that TikTok presence?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: There were there a lot, Bret. Harris's campaign joined TikTok a few days ago. Biden and Trump's campaigns also use it, the platform is banned for government employees over national security concerns.\par \par \uc2 But a new DOJ filing accuses TikTok of collecting its users political views across a range of areas amid an intense lobbying effort to prevent a national ban.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, U.S. intelligence officials are spotlighting risks on other platforms saying that Russia is trying to create a lot of verified accounts on X to have the appearance of credibility while they prop up some candidates and push down others and try to sow division and disbelief in the electoral process.\par \par \uc2 And also they say China is targeting down ballot candidates school they view as harmful to their own interests, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks. Let's go now to David Spunt, more on the details of the president's plan when it comes to the Supreme Court. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. No question the President Biden is promoting an election year wish list, which is long promoted by liberal advocates upset at some of the six to three conservative court recent rulings, he says his proposals are bold. But it's questionable, though, Bret, if they will become a reality.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have great respect for our institutions, whose separation of powers laid out in our Constitution. But what's happening now is not consistent with that doctrine of separation of powers, extremism.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Of the nation's highest court, the president endorsed three proposals. He wants a constitutional amendment to reverse the Supreme Court's landmark ruling earlier this month that presidents can be prosecuted for unofficial criminal acts, but granting them broad levels of immunity for core presidential duties. This after former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was charged with four felony counts related to 2020 election interference.\par \par \uc2 Biden also wants to replace lifetime tenure for the justices with 18 year term limits, as well as an enforceable binding ethics code for the nine justices.\par \par \uc2 The likelihood of any of President Biden's changes being adopted is low, since they would require broad majorities in Congress to act or amend the Constitution. Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  recently became the first on her bench to call for a new way to enforce an ethics code, including a disciplinary system.\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN\uc2 , SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: Both in terms of enforcing the rules against people who have violated them, but also in protecting people who haven't violated them. I think a system like that would make sense.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Critics say all three proposals won't go far. House Speaker Mike Johnson today wrote, it is telling that Democrats want to change the system that has guided our nation since its founding simply because they disagree with some of the Court's recent decisions.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As a press shout to me as I got off Air Force One, Republican Speaker of the House said, whatever he proposes is dead on arrival. (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): President Biden's that he thinks the thinking of House Speaker Mike Johnson is dead on arrival. One proposal not on the agenda, Bret, so called court packing, expanding the high court from nine to 13 or even 15 justices. Presumptive Democratic nominee Carolyn -- Kamala Harris backs the Biden plan overall, Bret, so she's the one that's going to have to try to see it through.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, as we saw with Jacqui Heinrich there at the White House, this clearly is an effort to create a campaign issue, though.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: To talk about something over the next few months, it's very unlikely conservative scholars, liberal scholars say that any of this will actually come to fruition, but it gives her something to talk about on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump meantime has agreed to be interviewed by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as part of the Bureau's investigation into the attempted assassination earlier this month in Pennsylvania. We have new information tonight about the days hours and minutes leading up to that shooting.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has obtained copies of text messages from a local law enforcement member about seeing that gunman, ways before that shooting. What we still do not have is emotive from authorities.\par \par \uc2 National Correspondent Jeff Paul is in Butler, Pennsylvania again tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Seconds of terror we're now learning from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was the work of careful and diligent planning in the attempted assassination of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Investigators say just days after the Butler, Pennsylvania rally was announced, Crooks started putting his plan into action.\par \par \uc2 On July 6th, Crooks registered for the event and did an online search for how far away Oswald was from Kennedy. The next day, Crooks surveilled the site. Then one day before the rally, Crooks practice at a gun range with the same AR-15 style rifle used in his attempt against the former president's life that killed one person.\par \par \uc2 Things accelerated the day of the rally where he purchased 50 rounds of ammo from a Butler gun store. He then went back home and he told his parents he was going to the shooting range but went back to Butler parked and flew his drone.\par \par \uc2 Later, local police officially identified him as a suspicious person.\par \par \uc2 Text between local law enforcement officers obtained by Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  appear to show a picture of Crooks, the local officer telling the group if you want to notify Secret Service snipers to look out, I lost sight of him.\par \par \uc2 Beaver County SWAT team member Gregory Nicol told ABC News he took the picture.\par \par \uc2 GREGORY NICOL, BEAVER COUNTY SWAT TEAM MEMBER: I assume that there would be somebody coming out to, you know, speak with this individual or find out what's going on.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): But according to the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , that Intel was never relayed to Trump's Secret Service detail, a likely communication problem that day. According to Beaver County SWAT member Jason Woods, noting their face to face briefing with Secret Service snipers never happened.\par \par \uc2 JASON WOODS, BEAVER COUNTY SWAT MEMBER: We were supposed to get a face to face briefing with the Secret Service snipers whenever they arrived and that never happened. So, I think that that was probably a pivotal point where I started thinking things were wrong because that never happened. And we had no communication with the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): So far, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has conducted roughly 450 interviews and plans to sit down with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the very near future. Trump himself agreeing to that interview and adding he'll be coming right back here to Butler, Pennsylvania for a future rally, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff Paul live in Butler. Jeff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The national debt for this nation surpassed $35 trillion for the first time in the nation's history today. That's according to new data from the Treasury Department. The milestone -- grim milestone comes just months after the U.S. eclipsed the $34 trillion threshold in early January of this year, while the 33 trillion mark was reached in September of last year. And as you can see, it's ticking right now.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed ahead of a week full of earnings reports from Wall Street's most influential companies and a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  meeting on interest rates, the Dow loss 49, the S&P 500 finished ahead four, the NASDAQ gained 12.\par \par \uc2 Now, for news overseas as mentioned at the top of the show, there are growing concerns tonight that the conflict in the Middle East could expand. This follows the death of a dozen children and teens in the Israeli controlled Golan Heights from a rocket fired by terrorists in Lebanon. Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is warning of a severe response.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. meantime cautioning Israel about a possible escalation. Correspondent Trey Yingst has the latest tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Small coffins are carried through the streets of Majdal Shams, the community is mourning the deaths of 12 children and teens killed in a \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  rocket attack.\par \par \uc2 KAHLONY MAGEED, MOURNER (through translator): At this point, if this thing continues in this way, I don't see the light at the end of the tunnel.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): Overnight, the Israeli security cabinet met to discuss the approval of operational plans to strike \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  targets in Lebanon. Israeli leadership is walking a tightrope with the Iran backed group in southern Lebanon trying to hit back hard while avoiding a broader regional war as the Middle East braces for the possibility of a widespread counter attack. Lebanon's caretaker foreign minister is echoing calls from the Biden administration for Israeli restraint.\par \par \uc2 ABDALLAH BOU HABIB, LEBANON CARETAKER FOREIGN MINISTER: That's what he was trying by diplomatic means not to have a regional war, not to have a war, not to have big escalation.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): Israel's Prime Minister and Defense Minister toured Northern Israel separately today to express their condolences for those killed in the rocket attack and to send a message of deterrence to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . This comes as reports indicate that the team traveling with Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  last week in Washington brought with them a list of weapon systems that Jerusalem once fast tracked to stockpile.\par \par \uc2 National Security Council Spokesman John Kirby assured Israel today that they'd be supported.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY COMMUNICATIONS ADVISOR: We continue to make sure that they have the tools they need to defend themselves. That's not going to change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Some airlines are canceling all flights to and from Beirut amid concerns about an Israeli counter attack, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Democrats are targeting the Republican vice presidential nominee, we'll tell you what word they're using to describe J.D. Vance, we'll have a report plus analysis from Brit Hume.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, the U.S. and foreign governments holding off on recognizing the results of Venezuela's presidential election. Both incumbent Nicolas Maduro and the Venezuelan opposition party claimed victory. Government officials announced Maduro as the winner with 51 percent of the vote, but they have delayed the release of an official vote tally.\par \par \uc2 At least two children are killed, nine others people injured in stabbings at a seaside town in northwestern England. Police say they have arrested a suspect and recovered a knife. The attacker occurring at a children's dance class.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at New South Wales, Australia. One of the big stories there tonight, diplomats from the U.S., Japan, Australia, Australia and India meet in Tokyo to discuss improved maritime safety. The four ministers noted that they are seriously concerned about tensions in the South China Sea but avoided identifying Beijing in their joint statement.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the honeymoon may be over for Republican vice-presidential nominee, J.D. Vance, it was pretty short, if there was one, he's getting fierce criticism for a comment made a long time ago that he says is being deliberately misconstrued, and Democrats are trying to define Vance by a single insult, hoping it will stick. Vance is trying to clean up.\par \par \uc2 Here is chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Trump running mate, Senator J.D. Vance is pushing back on Democrats for their latest attack on his 2021 \uc2 quip\uc2  about childless cat ladies running the country.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), VICE PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: If you look at the full context of what I said, it's very clear the Democrats have tried to take this thing out of context and blow it out of proportion, which is what they always do, Trey, because they don't have an agenda to run on themselves.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Vance went on to say, the left. Increasingly become anti-child and anti-family. But early polling suggests Vance is off to a rough start on the Trump ticket.\par \par \uc2 In the latest Fox poll, Vance's net favorability with Republican voters in Michigan, Minnesota, Pennsylvania, and Wisconsin, is lower than former President Trump's.\par \par \uc2 Despite the fact that part of the sales pitch for Vance was his ability to connect in the Rust Belt, Democrats are using a new line of attack against the Ohio senator.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): That is weird behavior, and I don't think you call it anything else. It is simply what we're observing.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Every day, Vance, it comes out, Vance has done something more extreme, more weird, more erratic.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Vance was asked by Fox if he minds Democrats calling him weird.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Look, the price of admission, meaning the price of getting to serve the people of this country is the Democrats are going to attack us with everything that they have.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): There are some prominent conservatives who worry his childless cat lady's comment will not be the end of it. They know Vance has said a number of things that they consider odd over the years, and are concerned he was not properly vetted. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: I know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He sits there and he watches the cable news. My guess is he is pulling his hair out saying, why the heck did I choose this guy? In fact, his choosing. J.D. Vance was the best decision he ever made for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, there you have it. Let's talk about the new challenges for the vice-presidential nominee, J.D. Vance. Fox News chief political analyst Brit Hume, joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Brit, the headlines are out there, the media is obviously jumping on this. Democrats are loving it. There you see, "Trump and Vance seemed ready to offend as many women voters as possible."\par \par \uc2 "J.D. Vance hits back at Jennifer Anderson, defending childless cat lady's remark."\par \par \uc2 "Trump's insult trap: Republicans hunt for elusive Harris attack line."\par \par \uc2 What do you make of this? And obviously, the media is covering this honeymoon already of the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  nomination before it happens, but the Vance stuff, he is clearly under attack.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: You know, Bret, it reminds me a little bit of 2008 when \uc2 John McCain\uc2  is a surprise of many, chose \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  to be his running mate, and she got off to what appeared to be a great start.\par \par \uc2 She had a fiery press conference, and then she made a convention speech that had the delegates absolutely roaring. She looked fine until the interest in her deepened. Facts About her record, strange things that she said came out, and the whole thing came tumbling down and ended up being thought as a mistake.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, Vance made a mistake with that stupid comment about childless cat ladies, which he's now having to defend. But the other side of this is what can happen when the media, if it ever does, starts digging into the background and the previous statements of one \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It is an absolute festival of exotic policy positions, very far left and strange and bizarre statements that appear to make no sense, that have been described in the past, at least as word salad.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the media are not interested in that, and they may not get interested in that, but one sense is that the advertisements and the statements of the Republican Party in this -- in this campaign, will bring them fully to light, and the picture on her may change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, yes. We have seen Democrats on Sunday shows, and over the weekend, they all sung -- were singing from the same sheet of music that J.D. Vance and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are weird. That was the use -- the word they used, and bizarre.\par \par \uc2 However, James Carville said the attack is coming. The tough stuff is coming. Get ready. And to your point, that's probably right.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I think Bret that it's pretty much impossible, even with the favorable media. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has certainly gotten favorable media, and will continue to get it. But even with that, it is difficult, if not impossible, to conceal someone's record and pass statements through the whole of panoply of a fall campaign for the presidency.\par \par \uc2 People will find out soon enough what \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been in the past, where she came from, you know, the kinds of policies she put in place as a prosecutor and attorney general in California and subsequently as a vice president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And I think when that all comes out, people may have a very different opinion over --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HUME: -- than this glorified notion that is standing, at least for the moment, in the polling and in the opinion of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But quickly, I mean, we're under 100 days, a vice president -- vice presidential nominee with a couple days of bad press in the big picture. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, vice presidents, vice presidential nominees, tend not to matter very much. At the moment, it appears that Mr. Vance -- Senator Vance, there's a drag on the on the ticket.\par \par \uc2 But in the end, it's -- people are going to vote for the top of the ticket, and the problems that Republicans have with Vance, he is not the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not as serious as it will be, in my view, when we begin to hear more and more about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who is, after all, at the top of that ticket.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Brit, as always, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, political experts \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  and Alex Castellanos on where the presidential race stands right now.\par \par \uc2 And later, a live report on Northern California's massive wildfire.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KEN PAXTON\uc2 , (R) TEXAS ATTORNEY GENERAL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the Republicans are going to tie her to what she was actually responsible for, which was the failed policies, the economic policies, the failed international policies, and the failed policies on the border. Nothing was done well for the American people, and she is responsible for that.\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM EMMER, (R-MN) MAJORITY HOUSE WHIP: Once you get past Labor Day, once people start using Kamala's own words, which are out there, as soon as she starts talking, after Labor Day, you watch that campaign drop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, Republicans are hoping that that's what happens, but looking at favorability ratings in a number of polls, not just our but other, the ABC-Ipsos poll one week ago had \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's favorability at 35 percent. You jump one week, that same poll has it now at 43 percent favorability. And if you look at enthusiasm, Democrats, these are Democrats, very enthusiastic with this nominee to be as she heads to Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Right now, let's take a look at the state of the presidential race, the Republican emphasis on Vice President's Harris's record. Joining us tonight, political consultant Alex Castellanos, along with pollster and political strategist \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 . Mark, you are just out in the field with some polling. And does that track what you are seeing there, favorability?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, our poll confirms that we think this race is back to June 26th, meaning Trump's ahead by, in our poll, three to four points, very much what it was like before the debate. Remember, Democrats had been shooting at President Biden, so that coalition was split. Democrats have come back together. The polls have stopped dropping. And her favorable in our polls moved from 38 to 47. And so she is well- positioned here to go toe to toe with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But, to those Republicans' points, there really hasn't been this air attack in these states, there hasn't been this mining of all the things she said on social media that would maybe throw off independents or suburban women, or --\par \par \uc2 ALEX CASTELLANOS, POLITICAL CONSULTANT: Right. It's just getting started. She's had a heck of a rollout, and she has proven in her first week that she has great benefit of not being \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And so hopes sore again. And she is not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , whom we know is very polarizing. So just being on the other side of that fills her tank.\par \par \uc2 Now I think she's going to try to create biggest gender gap in history. She is creating a campaign where it's about being the first woman president, a black woman. And that's going to be hope and change. Campaigns about issues and ideology are powerful, but they're even more powerful when they're wrapped around a personality, when they are something new. She is going to try to frame this election, I think, as old, white, bully party, that's Republican, has had its day. It's time for something fresh. And she's going to try to energize women in America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Women in America. Now, you look at our FOX polls on the battlegrounds, it really hasn't changed that much. Maybe a point or two seeing her shift up a little bit in our battleground polls. But when it comes to issues, the economy in all of these states overwhelming is a big issue, but abortion still holds in there. Immigration obviously is something the former president is hitting again and again.\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, I'm just surprised that neither candidate has a real economic plan when the economy is by far the number one issue. I think we had an interesting question on immigration. We said are you for or against open borders? Sixty-eight percent said they are against. We said, do you think Harris is for open borders? Seventy percent said they thought she was.\par \par \uc2 So this shows you that there is no traction with the issues with which they disagree with Harris yet, and the Trump campaign so far has not made any progress on that. Although I think she is at a tough ceiling here. Every easy point has been gained. It's the tough points from here on in.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. So how would you characterize that, that the former president has a high basement but a low ceiling? And what is her deal?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, her deal is she has consolidated the Democratic vote. She has definitely consolidated the black vote. I think the Latino vote is out because most Latinos are very upset about the economy. And if Trump is successful, he'll still be able to drive a wedge with the Latino vote, though I think he would have done better with a different vice president pick.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How does the former president change the dynamic? Obviously, J.D. Vance as I talked to with Brit Hume in Mike Emanuel's piece, he is under attack for things he said years ago and how he is dealing with them now. It fits what you are saying about going after women, because clearly, it fits that mold. How do they change the dynamic on their side?\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: I think it's a very clear path. I think they have to -- they understand that only half of the paint by numbers picture of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has been filled in. And they have got go out and prevent helper from being new and the future, right, from being change?\par \par \uc2 So, what do you do? You say there is nothing new about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . On the issues, boom. Immigration, open border. On crime, bailing out people who attack police and set cities on fire. She is more of the same. A fresh face, a fresh face on a car that's being wrecked and pulled by a tow truck. It's not going to work. So that's where I think they need to go.\par \par \uc2 And it's hard for Trump to move into the future because he is such an intense opponent of what has failed. That's what populism is all about. But if he can lift his eyes over the horizon just a little bit and say, tell me what making America great again means. Paint a picture of that for me. What's it going to be like? If his campaign can do that just a little bit, he could change the whole race.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And from the Democratic point of view, Mark, what do they need to do? Obviously, you couldn't have scripted a better launch than she has had with very little media scrutiny. There will be times that, to James Carville's point, that it comes. How do they deal with this?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, look, the best Democratic path here is she is within a couple of points. They've got the convention. Conventions are usually worth a couple of points, got a week of unimpeded message here. She is going to, I think, rally women, consolidate blacks, get young people back and not answer any questions about anything, if possible, in the last 100 days, because that worked for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and it would work for her, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But a debate, she would have to answer some questions.\par \par \uc2 PENN: Maybe there will be a debate. There aren't going to be three. And can \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pin her down the way that he was able to deal with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Well, she has gotten a lot more sophisticated if you saw her recent appearances than she was a couple years ago when she started.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And again, we have offered a debate here on FOX. We're going to see if both candidates do. Already the former president has. Last word?\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: She would be smart to take a debate on FOX. Go into the lion's den, go into red state, heartland America, and make her case that I'm not the way out there wacky liberal San Francisco Kamala. I'm somebody you can trust with America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Martha and I are tough but fair. There you go. Guys, thanks so much. You're the best.\par \par \uc2 Up next, firefighters battle a massive blaze in northern California. We'll get a live report. And later the panel with a look at the running mate decision, probably days away, for Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight in just the past few minutes, the special counsel has just responded to the Hunter Biden defense team's attempt to have the tax charges against the president's son dismissed. Biden's lawyers claim that the prosecution, the prosecutor, rather, David Weiss, was illegally appointed. Weiss's office says the new documents contain multiple misrepresentations and do not meet the requirements for a motion for reconsideration. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's new effort follows a judge's ruling in Florida dismissing charges against former President Trump on grounds the special counsel there in that case was illegally appointed by the Justice Department. The Hunter Biden case will continue.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, firefighters in northern California are reporting some progress and slight containment against a historic blaze that has now burned almost 400,000 acres. Correspondent Bill Melugin has an update from Chico, California, tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Destruction in northern California as the Park fire burning north of the city of Chico has now incinerated more than 360,000 acres, an area larger than the entire city of Los Angeles. It's now the sixth largest fire in state history. The fire has already destroyed more than 100 structures, including Susan (ph) Singleton's (ph) home, a local evacuee who loaded her seven dogs into her car and got them out just in time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Whatever we had can be replaced. But I wouldn't have left the animals at all.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: The park fire is only at 12 percent containment, and it's burning in dense, dry vegetation, which is acting as a constant supply of fuel. Thousands have been evacuated as nearly 5,000 fire personnel are battling the flames in largely remote and difficult terrain. The weather today is providing firefighters some relief, but during today's briefing, officials said they expect the favorable conditions to dissipate soon.\par \par \uc2 MARK BRUNTON\uc2 , CAL FIRE BATTALION CHIEF: If this thing gets up and wants to run like it's been doing this past week, then we'll be ready for that. We want to stay aggressive.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Authorities say the Park fire started on Wednesday after a two- strike felon was seen pushing a flaming vehicle into a gully, igniting the dry brush. Ronnie Dean Stout is now under arrest for arson and being held without bail. Prosecutors say he has prior convictions in California for lewd acts with a child and robbery causing great bodily injury.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And Bret, just a short time ago, that suspect did appear in a local courtroom out here where prosecutors formally charged him with arson. They allege he was responsible for starting what is now the largest wildfire here in California so far this year. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin live in Chico. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's wish list for a Supreme Court makeover and setting up a possible issue for his vice president.\par \par \uc2 First, though, what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX Rochester in upstate, New York, where police are searching for suspects after at least two people were killed, five others injured in a shooting at an afternoon barbecue. Hundreds of people gathered in a local park when a suspect opened fire. Police say multiple weapons were fired during that attack.\par \par \uc2 FOX 17 in Nashville, as Dollywood reopens after flash flooding forced the amusement park to close Sunday afternoon. Rising water covered walkways and trapped multiple vehicles. At least one person suffered minor injuries during that storm.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Providence, Rhode Island. One of the big stories there tonight from WPRI, our affiliate, dragonflies invade the beaches along the coast there. Videos show hundreds of insects flying around as sunbathers try to find shelter from the plug bugs. Scientists says dragonflies migrate from Canada as early as July and fly toward the Gulf Coast. It just happens to be there. The journey takes several weeks.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And I believe we should have term limits for Supreme Court justices in the United States. The 18-year term limit. That would help ensure the country would not have what it has now, an extreme court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sir, do you support limits for the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Does that mean you're open to term limits?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No. No, no, no, no. There is a question about whether or not -- it's a lifetime appointment. I'm not going to attempt to change that at all.\par \par \uc2 The last thing we need to do is turn the Supreme Court into just a political football, whoever has the most votes gets whatever they want. Presidents come and go. Supreme court justices stay for generations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, not if this president now wants to get through his recommendations today. That was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  then. The beginning was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  today. The president, and in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " he writes "What is happening now is not normal and it undermines the public's confidence in the court's decisions, including those impacting personal freedoms. We now stand in a breach. We can and must prevent the abuse of presidential power. We can and must restore the public's faith in the Supreme Court. We can and must strengthen the guards of democracy." But realists, some of them like Jonathan Turley, say this is just not realistic. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY LAW PROFESSOR: For over 50 years, Biden has refused to, quote, reform the Supreme Court. Then when his nomination was falling apart, he tried a Hail Mary pass to the far left. He said I will give you limits on the Supreme Court. It didn't work. But the pledge will now outlast his presidency.\par \par \uc2 But if you take a look at what this would do, it's no accident that the three justices who would be kicked off happen to be conservatives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So with that let's start there with our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , and Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com. Not only that, Katie, but it does give his vice president a key issue to run on, to run against the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, typically, the Supreme Court is an issue that gets conservatives out to vote. But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , as Jonathan Turley has just laid out so beautifully, says this is just a policy play. This is for political reasons only. And it would take a constitutional amendment for them to get this through. So that's how you know it's all about the politics.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  doesn't want to run on her Senate record, which was one of being the most far left senator on Capitol Hill and the least bipartisan. She's running away from her 2020 campaign because of her policies there that were so far left that she couldn't get any votes in the Democratic primary. So now \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is trying to give him an agenda moving forward, and she is trying to run on some of the accomplishments that they have had in his first term. So this is purely political and just in line with the Democrats ongoing onslaught against the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden in the past, Juan, as we showed, didn't want to change limits for Supreme Court justices. Now, if you put that question to voters, our latest poll, favorability of 18-year term limit for SCOTUS justices, 78 percent say sure. Democrats are at 89 percent. Republicans at 68 percent.\par \par \uc2 Trump campaign has said this tonight, "\uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is a leftwing radical who fully supports \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's dangerously liberal approach to politicize the Supreme Court, subvert the Constitution. Our nation's court system cannot be overturned to the radical left who seek to target their political opponents and disregard the will of the American people. President Trump has a proven record of nominating the most highly qualified justices and judges to the federal bench and will continue to do so during his next term in office." Where do you see it?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I just look at the numbers you put up there. If you stop and look at it, you see Republicans as well as Democrats think term limits just aren't that bad an idea. They don't see it as so purely political.\par \par \uc2 Now, the key here for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is, and I think the difference between Biden in 2020 and Biden today, is that he says it's not normal what's going on at the court. And that's not just an inside baseball argument among political peoples trying to fire up a campaign. At the moment, what you see is that, I think it's almost 55 percent of the American people say that they think -- they hold the court in disfavor, disapprove of the court, in large part because they disagree with two major decisions, by the way. One would be the abortion ruling. The other one the immunity.\par \par \uc2 The final point I would make to you on this is I'm listening to the conservative argument and say, why mess with the court? But you think about the founding fathers, the framers. The Supreme Court is an equal branch of government, it's a co-equal branch of government with the legislative branch and the executive branch.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But they did want to take the politics out.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right, but my point to you is both executive and legislative branch have ethics guidelines. Why should we be different --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, well, that's a different question. That's in the list of things. But it's separate but equal, separate but equal. Matthew?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: The plan is unconstitutional. It's unworkable. And because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a lame duck president facing a Republican House it's going to go nowhere. Katie is absolutely right. This is about politics.\par \par \uc2 And I think it hands the Trump campaign an issue, Bret. What's the big criticism of the Trump campaign that's coming from the Democrats? Oh, Project 2025, this radical agenda for government. What can be more radical than trying to reshape another branch of the federal government simply because you don't like two decisions they made in the past year?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: By the way, we don't cover. The decisions were all nine.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Just the majority.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They decide together, and there are a number of those.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: What's more radical than trying to phase out oil and gas in the next decade? What's more radical than reimposing not just Roe v. Wade but eliminating all restrictions on abortion throughout the country? I think there's an issue here that Biden has given the Trump campaign to use against \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of which, the campaign has -- the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign, as we've talked about all show, has had a great launch, and they've had a lot of support. They've had a lot of enthusiasm. There was pushback this weekend from the former president and his vice presidential nominee who is obviously under attack, especially in the media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Let's review Kamala's insanely liberal record. As a senator she was ranked the number one most radical left Democrat in the entire Senate. They say she made Bernie Sanders look like a moderate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE, (R) VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  questioned my loyalty to this country. That's the word she used, "loyalty." I served in the \uc2 United States Marine Corps\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I went to Iraq for this country.\par \par \uc2 My running mate took a bullet for this country.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 VANCE: so my question to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is, what the hell have you done to question our loyalty to the United States of America?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Katie, news cycles going to come and go, and there's going to be good ones and bad ones, and there's 99 left. It just depends on whether they are going to be able to fight back on some of these attacks.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, so far the polling isn't exactly where you would think the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaign would want it to be given her reboot and the media's help with that. I think looking at her V.P. search and the fact they are trying to moderate her and take her away from her previous positions shows that she knows she has to come somewhere closer to the middle.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Something tells me we will have a lot of time to talk about this.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Ninety-nine days.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, yes. All right, thank you, panel.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day for former Senator Elizabeth Dole who is celebrating her birthday. Happy to the former Labor and Transportation Secretary, former North Carolina senator, the wife of the late Senator \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 . She turns 88 today. Elizabeth Dole, there she is, happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our "Common Ground" segment deals with bipartisan efforts to make the Internet safer for children. Plus the latest on tomorrow's \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Secret Service hearing on the Trump assassination attempt. Both senators will talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. And thanks for tuning in, highest ratings since 2020. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240730ek7t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 New Details Emerge on Trump Assassination Attempt; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Continues V.P. Search; Interview With Sen. \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  (R-LA); President Biden Pushes Supreme Court Reform\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeff Paul, Bryan Llenas, Mike Emanuel, David Spunt, Jonathan  Turley, Trey Gowdy, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,922 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 July 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: You are looking live at Austin, Texas, President Biden is now set to appear at the LBJ Presidential Library moments from now as he makes a big Supreme Court gamble, some are calling it gambit, that could change the High Court forever.\par \par \uc2 Among the president's proposals, term limits for Supreme Court justices and a constitutional amendment limiting presidential immunity.\par \par \uc2 We're all over it with our legal eagles, including \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2  and Tom Dupree and Jonathan Turley, all here, and \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  member \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2  on where this maybe goes from here.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. Very glad to have you on a busy news day.\par \par \uc2 Let's go right to David Spunt in Washington with what this is all about -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is expected to make this announcement soon at the LBJ Library in Austin, Texas. But it's the vice president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who will attempt to see this through over the next few months, even after Election Day if she wins.\par \par \uc2 Three big changes the administration is proposing, no immunity for crimes a former president committed in office, term limits for Supreme Court justices, and a binding code of conduct for the Supreme Court. The first would require a change to the Constitution in the form of an amendment.\par \par \uc2 It comes after the Supreme Court earlier this month ruled former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was largely immune from prosecution in his federal trial for alleged attempts to overturn the election, as far as official acts are concerned. Biden calls that the No One Is Above the Law amendment.\par \par \uc2 The second proposed change, term limits for justices. Right now, justices enjoy lifetime tenure. President Biden wants to change that. The president supports a system in which they would appoint a two -- a justice every two years to spend 18 years active service on the Supreme Court, so essentially switching them out every two decades.\par \par \uc2 The third and final change, a binding code of conduct for Supreme Court justices, the key phrase, a code that is enforceable. The president said justices should not be exempt from an enforceable code of conduct that applies to every other judge.\par \par \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 , with the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 , today criticized the plan. He wrote: "If Democrats want to adopt an across-the-board ethics ban for all branches, I am in favor of that, no jets, no meals, no speaking honorariums, no gifts for anyone from anyone, for any reason in any branch starting with Congress. Until they support that, let's all be honest about what this is, a campaign to destroy a court that they disagree with."\par \par \uc2 Both House Speaker Mike Johnson and Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  said yesterday and today the legislation is dead on arrival in both the House and the Senate, as we wait, right now, 4:02 Eastern time, present by expecting to formally make those announcements on camera.\par \par \uc2 He did so in an op-ed earlier today, but on camera at the Lyndon Baines Johnson Library in Austin, Texas -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, David, just to be dotting the I's and the cross and the T's on this, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  completely on board with this, agrees with it 100 percent?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: One hundred percent. Her campaign came out today, said that she agrees with President Biden, will back him 100 percent all the way.\par \par \uc2 And, ultimately she's the one that's going to be dealing with the cheers and the jeers from this plan...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: ... as President Biden announced he's only in office another six months or so. So she's the one that's going to try and take it past the finish line. But as your next guests will, I guarantee to you, it is not an easy thing to do on any day, let alone try to cram this in before an election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, to put it mildly. So even if you put it off into the future, what are we looking at?\par \par \uc2 David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt.\par \par \uc2 The aforementioned brilliant legal eagles are with me right now, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , "Sunday Night in America" host, former South Carolina Republican Congressman, former federal prosecutor. You got the whole shebang there. Tom Dupree, the former deputy assistant attorney general, expert on all of these matters, and constitutional expert Jonathan Turley, \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law professor, "Indispensable Right" author, much, much more.\par \par \uc2 So, Jonathan, end it with you, begin with you on whether this is an indispensable just-don't-touch-it issue. Should a president even be toying with this?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No, he should not be toying with this.\par \par \uc2 And the interesting thing about the book is, it's subtitled "An Age of Rage." This is not our only age of rage, but throughout those different periods of intense hatred, fear, where people went after each other, presidents have never done this.\par \par \uc2 For over two centuries, even though they have had terrible disputes with the Supreme Court, they have never sought to deny justices their life tenure appointments on the court. And it really, I think, does mark this age for what it is.\par \par \uc2 What is really disappointing is that this was one of the principles still remaining for President Biden. He's long been criticized for following the polls, rather than principles. But on this, for 50 years, he's refused to go after the court.\par \par \uc2 And that changed when he was trying to resurrect his nomination, the final few days before he had to withdraw.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , I believe the president's position is something akin to, I didn't change the court change. It went wacky. It went far right. It went ahead with decisions that were against the grain of even common sense.\par \par \uc2 That's what he's going to be pounding, especially on some of this presidential immunity, criminal immunity, that's been debated widely on both sides. But what do you make, Trey, of that, that that is how he's going to position this, that I do this with great reserve and regret, but I'm going to do it anyway?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, I'm doing it when I have months left and Congress is gone in August and October.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: It's just -- it's not a serious proposal.\par \par \uc2 Neil, my first name is not professor, but you don't have to be a professor to know that it was \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  that saved Obamacare, a GOP nominee. It was Blackmun, a GOP nominee, who wrote Roe v. Wade. This court, this Republican court, preserved Elizabeth Warren's beloved \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, look, term limits, we got them for the president. Unfortunately for Biden, the Constitution says for the period of your good behavior. That's been interpreted to mean life. I'm not going to say it's dead on arrival, because I have got two smart lawyers with me. I'm going to say it's dead ab initio.\par \par \uc2 So they know that I know some Latin.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I thought that was an Italian dish myself, but I will go with that.\par \par \uc2 Tom, et me ask you about this because the 18-year term limiting is proposing, you're right, there are term limits right now for presidents, but there are not for senators and House members. There are certainly for a smattering the governors across the country, and some reduced to just a single term, as in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 So should we apply the same to Supreme Court justices? And I don't know where and when and how this deals with some of the more controversies we have dealt around this court, but it's usually coming from Democrats who don't like this court. So what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER JUSTICE DEPARTMENT OFFICIAL: Well, that's exactly right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Look, let's not make any mistake here. What is going on is Biden is trying to transform the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  because he and his liberal allies disagree with their decisions. That's the bottom line of what's going on here.\par \par \uc2 And from someone who practices in front of this court on a regular basis, it's frustrating and, frankly, it's a bit heartbreaking to think that the president would make an effort. I agree this proposal is done on arrival. It's not going to go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 But he's nonetheless making an effort to transform one of the signature institutions, one of the fundamental bedrock institutions in our form of government that has served the nation admirably for almost 250 years, and to do it in this context, in the context of a political campaign, making these proposals for what are transparently political purposes.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is not a toy or something to be played with. And so approaching these proposals in the spirit of political opportunism is exactly the wrong way to go.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Jonathan, I'm also thinking how difficult it is, regardless of your point of view, to add a constitutional amendment, period, and is the weight and the gravity that's significantly more than what's being proposed here?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you can separate out these proposals.\par \par \uc2 On the immunity issue, many of these proponents are misrepresenting what the Supreme Court actually did. The court did, similarly to earlier decisions, in dividing up cases, like the Youngstown Steel case, dividing up cases to address a different standard.\par \par \uc2 So they said, look, if this is a core presidential function, you have got absolute immunity. If you grant someone a pardon, you can't be criminally charged for that. But there's a lot of areas where you may be doing something officially, and they say that you're doing it for a corrupt or criminal or personal purpose.\par \par \uc2 And they said, for that, we will give you a presumption, but it could be overcome. And then for a wide array of other things where you're acting just for personal purposes, you have no protection.\par \par \uc2 Now, that's the middle ground. That was rejecting the Trump position and the lower court position. And what really puts the lie to what's being said by the president and his supporters is, if you look at the remands in these cases, these judges are doing exactly that.\par \par \uc2 They are not saying he has absolute immunity. So the question is, why do we need a constitutional amendment on immunity, given this balanced approach and before we actually see how the courts can work with this sort of tripartite standard?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. I want to thank you all, gentlemen. We're waiting to hear from the president. He's running a little bit behind for this Austin event at the LBJ Presidential Library, no less.\par \par \uc2 Just again, to wrap up some of the key points of this, they will include 18-year term, a binding code of conduct, a constitutional amendment saying that a president does not have total criminal immunity.\par \par \uc2 Speaker Mike Johnson in the House has already said this thing is dead on arrival in the House. The political fallout from all of this -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Speaker Mike Johnson has already said that this thing to overhaul the entire court is dead on arrival in the House.\par \par \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the minority leader in the Senate, is kind of saying the same thing, saying that -- and I quote here in his op-ed -- "The president says he wants term limits on justice, never mind what the Constitution says. Never mind the advice and consent role of the Senate. President Biden and his leftist allies don't like the current composition of the court. So they want to shred the Constitution to change it. He wants what he calls an ethics code that already exists.\par \par \uc2 "What the president is actually proposing is a stealth process for people other than the justices to decide cases, again, Constitution be damned."\par \par \uc2 So I'm taking a leap here, folks, and assuming that Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is a maybe on all of this.\par \par \uc2 Mike Emanuel on the political fallout from all of this -- Michael.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign has pointed me to something the former president wrote earlier this month. Former President Trump called President Biden criticizing the Supreme Court like working the referee in sports, writing - - quote -- "The Democrats are attempting to interfere in the presidential election and destroy our justice system by attacking their political opponent, me, and our honorable Supreme Court. We have to fight for our fair and independent courts and protect our country."\par \par \uc2 Of course, the former president nominated three conservative justices to the Supreme Court during his time in office, \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , three of the nine justices on the High Court.\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail in St. Cloud, Minnesota, on Saturday, former President Trump praised all the conservatives on the court on abortion for overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: You have to remember that we have to get elected. And I want to thank the six Supreme Court justices, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: ... for the wisdom and courage they showed on this long-term, very contentious issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Some Republicans have chuckled that the suggestion of term limits for Supreme Court justices is rich coming from President Biden, who has spent more than 50 years in national politics -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's a very good point.\par \par \uc2 Mike Emanuel, thank you very much on that.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Bob Cusack, The Hill editor in chief.\par \par \uc2 A lot to talk to you about, and I do want to get to the campaign, but this is actually interwoven with the campaign, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, EDITOR IN CHIEF, THE HILL: It is, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And this is definitely firing up the liberal base. And as you discussed, it's not going anywhere on Capitol Hill or anywhere for that matter. And I do this -- I think this is going to be part of a one-two step. Over the weekend, we reported that Harris has pivoted and said she doesn't ban fracking anymore.\par \par \uc2 So that's one way to move to the middle. Here, it's appeasing the base, which is, of course, furious with the Supreme Court. At the same time, I think Democrats, with this, all this stuff and all the term limits, they get away from something they're really upset about and could be a political winner, is the ethics code, more stringent for.\par \par \uc2 That gets lost in the headlines when you're proposing to do so much.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would any of this be going on right now if not for that Supreme Court reversal of Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: No, no, no, I think and certainly other decisions that have come from the court, some which have been majority decided, that Democrats actually have praised.\par \par \uc2 And, certainly, we remember Justice Roberts saving Obamacare many years ago.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: True.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: But this is -- really stems to abortion. That's the one issue Democrats have a clear advantage on, other issues, not so much.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, we talk about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  entering the race. She's narrowed the polls. The enthusiasm is building for her, the goodwill certainly among the party. She stopped the sort of air coming out of the tires for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and supporting the party. Most were disinterested.\par \par \uc2 They probably would not have voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but they might not have voted at all. She might have changed that. Do you think that's the case?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: I do. I really do.\par \par \uc2 I know a fair amount of people who were leaning toward Biden going to that first debate who then said subsequently, I can't vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  after that disastrous debate performance. That doesn't mean they love Harris, but I do think that they are going to get off the couch and vote for Harris, at least most of them, where I know a fair amount who voted for Biden in 2020 that were going to sit this one out.\par \par \uc2 So I do think this helps turn out, but it helps turnout on both sides. Without a doubt, Trump is still the favorite, but this is a very, very different type of race. Biden couldn't win it. Can Harris? Yes, she can win.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, again, this has been a delicate balance for a lot of Democrats I have had on to talk about this above and how it looks to be once loyal to the president and then toss him out.\par \par \uc2 They are a lot more nuanced in that. I have caught up with more than a couple, including the Massachusetts Democratic Congressman Jake Auchincloss, who put this in a different perspective. I want to you react to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We rushed this, that, yes, I mean, there is a great deal more enthusiasm for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  than -- among the base than there was for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and maybe that's a sign it's working, but others are still angry about it.\par \par \uc2 Are you?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAKE AUCHINCLOSS (D-MA): She can't apologize for winning fast, right? I mean, this is politics and everyone's going to compete hard. And the reason that she won is because she has closed the enthusiasm gap that was existing over the last year within the Democratic base relative to the Republican base.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that is true. She has closed that gap, to your point, Bob, but at what cost?\par \par \uc2 I mean, there are a lot of older voters who tend to vote in greater numbers who don't like the way an older president was treated. And I'm wondering what you make of that and whether it could boomerang here.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Well, yes, I think that's a factor that the Harris campaign has to consider.\par \par \uc2 And, also, where does she stand on policies? Is she going to continue the Biden/Harris policies, or is she going back to her presidential bid in 2019, where she didn't even make it to Iowa in 2020, whether that's a 35 percent corporate tax rate, fracking, as I mentioned earlier, a lot of Medicare for all, transitioning to that? What's the role of private insurance?\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of people need to know where she stands on policies. And she's got to do that relatively soon. And I think that's with -- dealing with some type of media outlet that is going to challenge her, and that she's got to do well, because we know as vice president, when she got -- started out of the gate, there were some bad interviews, and that's why her numbers went down.\par \par \uc2 They have rebounded, though, recently, and that's why this is a close race.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do you think some of the Republican focus -- leaving the J.D. Vance controversies aside, the cat lady remarks and all this other stuff I'm sure even he would wish he could dial back a little bit,and then some of the other comments about her in general and the cackling, a lot of that seems kind of silly to me.\par \par \uc2 It might resonate with some who are had a pre kind of a view of her.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But isn't there greater concern for how she might prosecute \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a one-on-one, that she's not to be taken for granted or dismissed easily, and they're presenting or raising a race with her that might be just plain insulting?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes, I think that when I -- when Biden was resisting calls for him to step aside, a big question I was asking Democrats, who do you want in the next debate against Trump? Do you want Harris or Biden? And the answer, for the most part, was Harris.\par \par \uc2 I think