ave you. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, a couple of thoughts. Number one, when President Biden's Supreme Court proposal gets to Capitol Hill, in my opinion, it's as dead as \uc2 Woodrow Wilson\uc2 . I don't think the House will even bring it up. I don't know of anyone who's taking it that seriously.\par \par \uc2 Number two, I don't think President Biden's proposal is really President Biden's proposal. I think it's Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' proposal. When the president announced it, she immediately endorsed it enthusiastically. I\par \par \uc2 think she's asked him to take the lead, so, in case there's blowback, it doesn't get on her. Number three, the Biden/Harris proposal is not about the Supreme Court, in my opinion. It's about abortion. Particularly, Vice President Harris is very angry at the Supreme Court for saying abortion's not in the Constitution and sending the issue back to the people and the states.\par \par \uc2 She supports a national rule which basically says abortion on demand, a woman can have an abortion at any point, even up until the moment before birth. The vice president -- I think most Americans see abortion as trying -- the issue of abortion as trying to balance the rights of a woman and an unborn child.\par \par \uc2 I think the vice president sees it -- sees -- or thinks that the unborn baby has no rights, none whatsoever. She's entitled to her opinion, but I don't think that's mainstream.\par \par \uc2 And the final point I'd make, Neil, this is really the vice president's first major policy proposal. And I just think it's very strange that she decided to lead with this, given what the polls show about the vice president.\par \par \uc2 Alex Castellanos is one of the brightest political commentators I know, and he's made the point that the burden on the vice president is to prove to the American people that she's a serious person. \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2  didn't giggle. Golda Meir didn't giggle.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the polls, fair or not, many Americans -- and, again, this may not be fair, but I'm just telling you what the polls show. Many Americans think that the vice president is a little bit of a ding-dong, that she's not serious.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, this ding-dong, Senator, has risen in the polls.\par \par \uc2 And I'm wondering if the strategy to focus on her laugh or the former president calling her nasty and crazy really promotes that push to show she's not up to the job. Does it look petty? And does it say to judge her on this level looks like a strategy that could backfire on Republicans?\par \par \uc2 By all means, get her on the issues that matter, like you have talked about abortion, to clarify what this Supreme Court push might be about, but to focus on this other stuff just looks dumb.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, what I was going to say, Neil, if I could finish my thought, when you look at the polls, fair or not, what the polls, the crosstab show, is that those Americans who have an opinion of the vice president think that, number one, she's a bit of a ding-dong, and, number two, that she is a member of the loon wing of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 She's a San Francisco Democrat.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, that member of the loon wing, Senator, that -- I'm not casting aspersions either way. That member of the loon wing is now dead even with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in one battle state -- ground after another.\par \par \uc2 Now, I'm not saying that might last. I am saying that this loon is surging. And if the Republican approach to her is to treat her like a loon and a cackle and she is just an empty suit, I wonder how far that will go.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, I'm sorry if that offends you, Neil, but I'm just telling you what the people of America think.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just telling you what the polls are showing right now.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you think they're 