ho were using it as a staging ground. Still, the Secret Service is under increasing pressure to explain.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Director Kimberly Cheatle is expected to meet virtually with lawmakers to answer for the deadly mistake. Cheatle, in a new statement, assured the public the agency welcomes scrutiny, writing, "The Secret Service is working with all involved, federal, state, and local agencies to understand what happened, how it happened, and how we can prevent an incident like this from ever taking place again."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Secret Service director, Cheatle will appear before the House Oversight Committee in person next Monday. Expect intense questions from both sides of the aisle. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the justice department. David, thank you. While President Biden is calling for Americans to turn the temperature down on political rhetoric, he is also facing his own political crisis in the Democratic Party, the effort by some in his own party to try to get him off the November ballot.\par \par \uc2 The president spoke with Lester Holt of NBC News this afternoon for an interview to be shown this evening. We have a brief excerpt to show you tonight. Senior White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich joins us tonight, live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening. Bret.\par \par \uc2 This interview was supposed to revitalize President Biden's campaign, amid his party's calls to replace him. Now, he is navigating new messaging while trying to maintain confidence in his administration's ability to investigate the attempt on his opponent's life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NEWS ANCHOR, NBC: Well, you said it's time to put Trump in the bull's eye.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Well, I guess I was talking about at the time was, there is very little focus on Trump's agenda. I didn't say crosshairs, I mean, bullseye. I mean, focus on it. Focus on what he's doing. Focus on one's policies.\par \par \uc2 How do you talk about the threat to democracy, which is real, when a president says things like he says.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Now, a subtle shift in the president's language.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What do you think of J.D. Vance pick?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He's a clone of Trump on the issue.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The Biden campaign is sticking to its message that Trump's agenda is extreme, but White House officials appear to be struggling to convey their full view without stoking hostilities.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Does the president view Trump's agenda as a threat to democracy?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Look, what I will say is, right now, we're not going to politicize what happened on Saturday. What happened on Saturday was horrific. It was. It was sick, as the president said.\par \par \uc2 And we got to move forward in the way that we respect each other, that we don't have -- that we don't have this type of discourse.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Just one day before, a gunman tried to take Trump's life, this was Biden's framing.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Trump is a threat to this nation.\par \par \uc2 Trump said, if he wins, he'll be a dictator on day one. He means it, folks. We're not going to let that happen.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: No!\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Over my dead body will happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): As to how Biden is going to lead his own call to turn down the temperature, White House officials pointed to his actions in the immediate aftermath, calling Trump personally, giving multiple updates on the status of the investigation and delivering an Oval Office address to call for unity.\par \par \uc2 But where this all goes from here, Bret, is an open question. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER