d, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: By miracle, she was found 37 hours later, drifting in an incredible swing ring.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: What?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: -- more than 50 miles offshore in the Pacific Ocean. She was spotted by a cargo ship, and she was struggling in six-foot waves, and they saved her. So, good job, everybody.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Right before she was eaten by cannibals. That's what a close call.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, true. That's some --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right. That's it for us. Have a great night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to Washington, I'm John Roberts, in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 And "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Biden's political cris deepens, as big donors reportedly withhold nearly $100 million, and more congressional Democrats urged the president to leave the race.\par \par \uc2 There are now 19 of his party colleagues on that list. One of them told the president that to his face -- well, to his ear, at least, during a conference call.\par \par \uc2 The president will speak at a campaign rally in Detroit any minute from now, specifically, Dearborn, where he's got a lot of trouble.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House correspondent Peter Doocy starts us out tonight. He is at the White House. Good evening. Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, John.\par \par \uc2 Aboard Air Force One tonight on the way to Michigan, a Biden campaign official said President Biden's been making gaffes for 40 years. He made a couple last night, and he will likely make some more down the line. That is a Biden campaign official.\par \par \uc2 The party's ultimate power brokers, though, remain unconvinced, as both \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  kept quiet today,\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I promise you, I am -- I'm OK.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Biden's campaign claims donations exploded during last night's press conference, but super PAC money is drying up. $90 million pledged dollars to the main pro-Biden super PAC future forward will be frozen until he leaves the race, according to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JAMES CARVILLE, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: And not only they shut down contributions to the Biden campaign, they shut down contributions to a Senate committee, to the House Committee. They shut their wallet. Going to make a lot -- a lot of people unhappy.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Influential Democrats, according to an Axios report, have become, "The unofficial Committee to Unelect the President. The mission: Pushed Biden out of the presidential race -- the sooner, the better." Not if the Biden brain trust has anything to do with it.\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: This is like some soccer game where some shadow group of people on the sidelines get to run new players out on the field of last minute.\par \par \uc2 You know, the donors don't get to decide. The voters decided.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): But doubts remain about Biden's viability.\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, SENIOR POLITICAL COMMENTATOR, CNN: He really needs, like a royal flush to win this race. The implication is that he may have made an unequivocal statement, but he ought to reconsider it.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He won't, unless --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Unless they came back and said, there is no way you can win.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden is daring delegates to challenge him at the DNC.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If all of a sudden, I show up at the convention, everybody says, we want somebody else, that's democratic process. It's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Something that could happen, a Gaza cease fire deal that frees Israeli hostages. The president announces, "that framework is now agreed to by both Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . My team is making progress, and I'm determined to get this done."\par \par \uc2 That would add to a foreign policy record, President Biden believes is winning hearts and minds worldwide.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And by the way, look at the numbers in Israel. I mean, I -- my numbers are better than Israel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): As President Biden tries to prove to members of his own party that he has got another four years in him, he apparently does not feel like he needs to do that every day. And he does not feel like he needs to do that from here at the White House. Because when he is done in the Detroit area, he is going to fly to Delaware for a weekend at the beach house. John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, it's been a busy week for him. I think there is no question about that. Peter Doocy for us in 1600 Pennsylvania. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democrats are turning up the pressure on the president to consider the down ballot implications of his increasingly threatened candidacy. Our senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram, has that part of the story tonight. He is on Capitol Hill. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: John, good evening. There are now 19 Democrats who want the president out. Mike Levin of California is one of them. He told the president to bow out during a conference call with the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Brittany Pettersen of Colorado also asked Mr. Biden to stand down. Levin, Pettersen, and Brad Schneider of Illinois used the same language when calling on the president to step aside. They said it was time to "pass the torch".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. BRAD SCHNEIDER (D-IL): It's not about a single press conference or a debate or a campaign event. It's about where are we going and who's best positioned to lead us. It's time for the next generation to step up.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): House minority leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  met with President Biden last night. Jeffries told Mr. Biden what members of his caucus hoped the president would do. Republicans say it's too late for Democrats to worry.\par \par \uc2 REP. SCOTT PERRY (R-PA): All these people that are complaining about that he is not ready, he can't do it, he is unable, he is unfit, they have known for years what many of us have known, and so, they have been complicit in the cover up.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The president has conference calls tomorrow with the progressive caucus and the new Dems coalition. The president is trying to stop a revolt.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 GERRY CONNOLLY\uc2  (D-VA): What you risked was the atomization of the House. What did you think was going to happen if you weren't reaching out to them and communicating with them?\par \par \uc2 So, I'm not surprised --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Now, 13 of the 19 who want to bounce Biden come from the new Dems. People predicted a wave of Democrats might call for the president to quit the race today, but that never happened. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Chad Pergram for us with the latest from Capitol Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We got a "FOX NEWS ALERT", because we've got some dramatic news out of Santa Fe, New Mexico, where the trial of Alec Baldwin has been going on. The judge just dismissed the charges against him.\par \par \uc2 Our Jonathan Hunt has been following this case. Jonathan, something suddenly happened in the courtroom this afternoon regarding ammunition. And now, here we are with the charges dismissed. What transpired?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: John, an extraordinary end to what has been an extraordinary day.\par \par \uc2 Alec Baldwin arrived here this morning, and we all expected that the usual day full of testimony from witnesses. Instead, his legal team filed a motion to dismiss. They alleged that the prosecution had a box of ammunition, which was connected to the set of the movie rust, but they never revealed that evidence to the defense, and they said that was cause for the case to be dismissed entirely.\par \par \uc2 The judge sent the jury home, said, come back on Monday. And then, she listened to testimony without the jury, in which the defense again made their case that the case should be dismissed entirely.\par \par \uc2 The prosecutor, the lead prosecutor, Kari Morrissey, then, took the stand herself and said that they believed that, that box of bullets was not relevant to the case because it was never on the set of the movie, Rust.\par \par \uc2 The judge then, just in the last few minutes, said she disagreed entirely with the prosecution. They should have revealed that evidence. So, she said the case is dismissed without prejudice, because of what is known as a Brady violation.\par \par \uc2 At that point, I can tell you, John, that Alec Baldwin broke down in tears, sobbing as he sat in the courtroom. Hilaria Baldwin, his wife, also began to cry.\par \par \uc2 We expect them to emerge from the courthouse behind me any moment now. But the bottom line, John, this case has been dismissed with prejudice. In other words, there cannot be a retrial. Alec Baldwin, tonight is a free man. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Wow, stunning developments there from Santa Fe, Jonathan Hunt, for us. With that, John, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, back now to politics, and we're just three days away from the start of the Republican National Convention in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 The big question is, who will former President Trump choose as his running mate? The big mission is to take advantage of a seriously weakened incumbent. Our correspondent Bill Melugin is in Milwaukee tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The stage is officially set here in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 Decorations are prepped, security is tight, political ads are flying, and Republicans say they are ready for their first full scale convention since 2016. United under their candidate, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG BURGUM (R-ND): He has got the Republican Party more united than they've ever been. Going into this convention next week, everybody is aligned.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): The \uc2 RNC\uc2  hasn't released the official schedule yet, but Fox can confirm a handful of speakers set to take the stage, including Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , UFC President Dana White, \uc2 Donald Trump Jr.\uc2 , and East Palestine, Ohio Mayor Trent Conaway.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans to do list includes voting to approve the new agenda they released earlier this week, which notably leaves abortion to states rather than call for a national ban, as the party has done for decades.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans are feeling bullish after weeks of questions swirling around President Biden's health and cognitive ability. Trump challenged the president to take a cognitive test, posting on Truth Social, "Joe should immediately take a cognitive test, and I will go with him and take one also. For the first time, we'll be a team, and do it for the good of the country.\par \par \uc2 The big question heading into the convention, who will Trump select as V.P.? He confirmed in a podcast today, he's narrowed his list down to "four or five" finalists, including familiar names, \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , Marco, Rubio, Tim Scott and J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And John, former President Trump is set to hold a rally in Butler, Pennsylvania tomorrow. That's just about an hour away from Senator J.D. Vance's hometown, State of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 It remains to be seen if Senator Vance is going to be at that rally, or what it could potentially mean for his potential V.P. prospects. But you'll remember, earlier this week, Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  was at a rally with President Trump in Florida.\par \par \uc2 So, the bottom line is, when it comes to these Trump rallies and the veepstakes, they could mean something or they could mean absolutely nothing whatsoever. We're going to find out soon. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I love that. He's narrowed it down to four or five finalists, which means we're exactly where we were two months ago. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes, yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I mean this to complete the job I started.\par \par \uc2 AXELROD: I don't know what his aides are telling him, but he is in a very tough spot right now to win this election.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): If he decides to change his mind later on, then, we will respond to that. We have until the 19th of August to open our convention.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, CHIEF POLITICAL ANALYST, NBC NEWS: If they are going to make a move against Biden, though, they got to do it this weekend. They can't keep dragging this out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Oh, is it going to be the weekend of the Long Knives? Let's bring in our panel, Fox News senior political analyst, Juan Williams, \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  columnist and \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  Senior fellow, Marc Thiessen, and Fox News state department correspondent Gillian Turner.\par \par \uc2 Let's first of all, Juan, start with you, because the president will be in Dearborn, Michigan, the same high school he was at four years ago for a campaign event. At the same time, the mayor of Dearborn, Abdullah