s are looking dire for Biden in his blue wall.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes. No, it is. So he was already behind before the debate disaster but not within the margin of error in most of those states. And now he's only dropped a couple of points, which is why there hasn't been a drumbeat to really to push him out. But he's now outside the margin of error in a lot of those states.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes. And if Biden, Gillian, cannot count on his blue wall, what's he got to back him up?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes. Well, why he's -- you know, he's here in Michigan for at least the third time, maybe the fourth time this year, he keeps going back. This is particularly good timing for him today in terms of optics because, you know, if you look who's in the audience, you have black activist leaders, you have union leaders, this squares perfectly with his whole MO right now, which is complaining about how the elites have turned against him, but the ordinary Americans and voters are still with him. So this kind of --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Juan, when you take a look at those numbers now, particularly compared to 2020, if the election were held today, it's President Trump.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It's not being held today.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So you're wiping the sweat off your brow here.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, that's right. But, I mean, here's the --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Here's the tissue.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Thank you, my friend. But the thing about this is, you know, you stop and think about what people are saying --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- and you say, you know what, remember Yogi Berra's thing? It's getting late early.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And as the fall comes and the days grow shorter, I think that it's not only a concern for Democrats. Right now it's a concern, like the Democrats, it's a concern for the donors. And the question is, if you cut Biden off now, are you saying in September, oh yes, I'm willing to live with Trump.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's a difficult choice.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, winners and losers. Marc, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: My winner is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He's been in a role for a couple of weeks now. Polls are growing. Is it leading? The polls are growing, debate success, and he was staying on the sidelines and letting Biden twist in the wind. \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders, including on your show, are crediting him with the success of the alliance and spending. And he's going into the convention with his party united in the Democrats in disarray.\par \par \uc2 My loser is the media establishment which spent three years pushing this lie that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was cognitively declined. They have egg on their face and they didn't redeem themselves with that press conference.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: My winner is Congressman \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2 , because you may remember a few months ago, he basically got left out of town for primarying Biden. Well, it turns out that now his colleagues apparently on Capitol Hill are all coming up to him in the hallways and being like, we're really sorry. We're really sorry -- you know, acknowledging how short sighted --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And if they wish for two.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: -- they were. So here you go. He's maybe a visionary --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And your loser?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Loser is \uc2 AT&T\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Allowed their data to be -- they say almost every single one of their customers was comfortable.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, Juan, finished his off.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Ukraine President Zelenskyy on his visit to the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit here in Washington. He came away with a pathway for Ukraine to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  and got anti-missile protection. My loser, I would say Hungary's \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , who was embraced by Trump. I don't understand that, but also was visiting with Putin who bombed the children's hospital.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I mean, it's just terrible.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes. It is. There's no question about that. That was horrible.\par \par \uc2 Juan, Gillian, Marc, thank you all much. We will see you next week. Big week coming up.\par \par \uc2 It is Friday, which means it's time for Notable Quotables.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN; Look, I wouldn't have picked Vice President Trump to be vice president. I think she's not qualified to be president. So I start there.\par \par \uc2 I am running. I am running.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don't care if he's pooped his pants. I don't care if he can't put a sentence together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's up to the president to decide if he is going to run.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you believe he should run for re-election?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Am I speaking English to you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We miffed around here about how information's been shared with the press corps.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everybody knew that this man is basically a vegetable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How do you see him behind the scenes?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The commander-in-chief that I know is not what people saw at that debate, on that debate stage.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And now I want to hand it over to the president of Ukraine, who has as much courage as he has determination. Ladies and gentlemen, President Putin.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm also officially challenging Cricket Joe to an 18-hole golf match.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Tomorrow, if all of a sudden I show up at the convention and everybody says, we want somebody else, that's a democratic process. It's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How closely are you following the day to day politics here in Washington?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: I think sometimes we are closer than you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't know about that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, we will have preview coverage of the Republican Convention coming up Sunday on Fox News. That will include a special edition of this program live from Milwaukee at 6:00 Eastern. Please join Shannon Bream for Fox News Sunday. She is going to debut new Fox News polls, and her guests will be \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairman Michael Whatley, Milwaukee Democratic Mayor Cavalier Johnson, and \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  Senior Fellow Elaine Kamarck.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR 6:00 Eastern, 3:00 p.m. on the West Coast. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  about to take the stage in Detroit.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching Special Report. I'm John Roberts in Washington.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240713ek7c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Completes NATO Speech Amid Mental Acuity Concerns; Some Democrats Appear More Anti-Trump Than Pro-Biden; Softened Abortion Language In GOP Platform; \uc2 NATO\uc2  Summit Amid Debate Over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Leadership; Biden Delivers High Stakes Speech During NATO Summit; Concerns About Biden Acuity As NATO Leaders Visit United States; White House Not Changing Ukraine Strike Policy; Jury Selection Underway In Manslaughter Trial Of Actor Alec Baldwin For Accidental Shooting Death On Movie Set; Democratic Strategist James Carville Says President Biden Will Not Be Democratic Presidential Nominee For 2024; President Trump Reportedly Still Considering His Vice Presidential Nominee\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Jennifer Griffin, Bryan Llenas, Chad  Pergram, Jonathan Hunt, Ben Domenech \par \uc2 8,111 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A visionary statesman and ceaseless defender of democracy. Secretary General \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2  has guided the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance through the most consequential decade for European security since World War II.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  launched his brutal assault on Ukraine, betting that \uc2 NATO\uc2  would break, Secretary General Stoltenberg proved him wrong.\par \par \uc2 Under his stewardship, \uc2 NATO\uc2  has become stronger and more united than ever, and Americans for generations to come will benefit from the safer world he helped create. He demonstrates that the core truth of the alliance is as powerful now as it was 75 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Together, we are stronger.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. You're just watching President Biden with \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders delivering a speech. Welcoming them here to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has just completed those remarks to diplomats and officials in Washington. That's to mark the \uc2 NATO\uc2 's 75th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 The president, however, at this time, still remains under intense scrutiny over his mental acuity amid increasing calls from within his own party for him to get out of the presidential race. A lot of them.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. And the remarks we just heard are a perfect example of what President Biden is trying to do right now, to stabilize his shaky standing in the presidential race by focusing less on stuff that's happening right now and more on things that have happened over the last 3-1/2 years, like strengthening \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: You're going to see the president engaging with 32 leaders of this alliance. I think that's really important.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Blocks away, his team continued, insisting the president is fit to serve.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: It was being incorrectly assumed and insinuated that the president was being treated for Parkinson's.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The president has not been screened since February because according to his doctor, Kevin O'Connor, during a winter physical, there were no findings which would be consistent with any Cerebellar or central neurological disorder such as a stroke, multiple sclerosis, Parkinson's, or ascending lateral sclerosis.\par \par \uc2 That explanation is lacking to a former White House physician.\par \par \uc2 REP. RONNY JACKSON (R-TX): If you saw someone besides the president, just tell us that, OK? Have Dr. O'Connor come down to the podium and say that. This is our commander in chief, this is a national security issue, and if this man is not leading our country, and he's not capable of doing so, the world and the country needs to know that.\par \par \uc2 The president and his campaign co-chairs insist there will be no changes at the top of the ticket and down ballot Democrats will not be dragged down because of it.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND, BIDEN-HARRIS CAMPAIGN CO-CHAIR: Well, I don't believe that to be true. Your constituents vote for you.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Officials are pointing to policies in place to ensure national security in times of emergency, even though President Biden told a group of governors that moving forward he plans to get more sleep by working less after 8:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Say that the Pentagon at some point picks up an incoming nuke, it's 11:00 p.m. Who do you call, the first lady?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: He has a team that lets him know of any -- of any news that is pertinent and important to the American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE (on camera): The president has been leaning more on family lately as he waits to see which elected Democrats are going to be with him moving forward.\par \par \uc2 And we learned two new things about this new dynamic. Number one, when \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  sits in on White House meetings. We are told he does not have access to classified information. We are also told that if President Biden winds up winning reelection, he is committing as of today to serving a full second term, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democrats are divided over what to do about the president, and more importantly, what to say about it right now.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram has that part of the story tonight. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, there are now seven congressional Democrats calling on President Biden to step aside. The latest is moderate Democrat \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  of New Jersey. She asks for a new nominee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats divided. There are the president's cheerleaders.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Go Biden all the way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are riding with Biden.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): There's one person in this country that's kicked Trump's ass in 2020.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Others harbor grave concerns.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He just has to step down because he can't win.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): But the silence of some evasive Democrats speaks louder than any soundbite.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Should the president step aside?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Senator Warren, do you support Biden? Do you support Biden?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): However, even Democrats who support the president make the case more against former President Trump, rather in favor of Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR (D-CA): We're unified that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cannot win.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: It's more about Trump and not so much about Biden, isn't that the issue here?\par \par \uc2 AGUILAR: Right now, President Biden is the nominee, and we support the Democratic nominee that will beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats facing competitive re-election bids struggle to distance themselves from the president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm hearing discussions and legitimate questions about the president, and I continue to listen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Should he step down in your opinion?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I've said what I'm going to say.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The House could vote Wednesday on a measure by Republican Anna Paulina Luna to fine \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  10,000 a day for not coughing up the audio tapes of a special counsel interview with President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Republicans believe the tapes could reveal the president's possible cognitive issues, but even top Republicans are skeptical of Luna's gambit.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): If it's brought to the floor, I'll vote for it, but as a constitutional law litigator, a former constitutional law litigator, I'm frankly have a little pause.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): A senior House Democratic leadership source says President Biden will not meet with Democrats in person this week. That's because they would say things he'd rather not hear, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump hosts his first major rally since the day after the debate. In about an hour, Florida Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is scheduled to attend that event in Doral, Florida, adding to speculation about the former president's running mate choice.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in Doral tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, former President Trump is expected to attack President Biden, the White House Democratic Party, and the media, accusing them of covering up Biden's, "cognitive decline."\par \par \uc2 Trump's expected to sharpen attacks against Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the likeliest alternative Democratic nominee, as being more extreme than Biden.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, meantime, warn Trump will usher in the end of American democracy. The DNC purchasing five billboards in Miami warning voters about Trump's ties to Project '25, a controversial playbook for the next Republican administration crafted by \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign has distanced itself from the project and points to the new GOP platform approved by the \uc2 RNC\uc2  Monday as its road map, including ending inflation, preventing World War III and deporting pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  radicals from college campuses.\par \par \uc2 But the platform makes only one mention of gun rights, far fewer than eight years ago.\par \par \uc2 And for the first time in 40 years, does not include a call for a constitutional amendment banning abortion. This year's platform only says the party opposes late term abortions.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life delegates blasting the party's platform process. The president of \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2 , \uc2 Tony Perkins\uc2 , writing, Delegates were given only a few minutes of discussion before debate was ended and a vote taken.\par \par \uc2 Six anti-abortion organizations signed on to a letter supporting the platform. Some voting delegates say it does not go far enough.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE RUZICKA, \uc2 RNC\uc2  PLATFORM COMMITTEE MEMBER: I want something that says that the unborn baby has a fundamental right to life. That can't be infringed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): And Florida Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , a potential vice presidential pick for former President Trump just wrapped up his comments. He spoke in English and in Spanish and he said President Biden is not calling the shots. He says Biden is the head of a shadow government and he said, "I don't want a president who is open less than our banks are open."\par \par \uc2 Of course, speaking to the fact that the president is only alert during limited hours during the day, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas at that rally. We'll monitor that for news. Bryan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  says the U. S. is no longer an overheated economy. Powell telling congressional lawmakers today the job market has cooled from its pandemic era extremes.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  chair also says he does not want to send signals about the timing of any future actions of -- on interest rates. The Dow lost 53 on that news. The S&P 500 gained four, the NASDAQ was up 26. Both of those again record closes.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, you saw at the top of the show, and we told you earlier, \uc2 NATO\uc2  ministers are here in Washington D.C. this week for their 75th anniversary conference.\par \par \uc2 The alliance finds itself at a pivotal point with that possibility -- with the possibility that former President Trump could return to office and bring with him some attitudes about the allies and \uc2 NATO\uc2  specifically.\par \par \uc2 Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin takes a look tonight from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The leader of the world's largest democracy, India's Prime Minister, warmly embraced Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  today in Moscow, just hours after Russian missiles targeted a children's hospital in Kyiv, killing dozens of children.\par \par \uc2 A senior \uc2 NATO\uc2  official warned that Russia plans more of these high profile attacks against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The timing of the Moscow meeting by a key U.S. ally and affront to \uc2 NATO\uc2  as world leaders arrive in Washington, D.C. to celebrate 75 years of the Western alliance.\par \par \uc2 All eyes now on President Biden after a rocky debate performance and the future of the alliance in question. European allies are moving quickly to Trump proof the alliance with former President Trump continuing to threat to stop funding the war in Ukraine and pull out of \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. currently deploys 90,000 of \uc2 NATO\uc2 's 500,000 armed forces in Europe. \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders plan to sign a defense industrial pledge to strengthen the allied industrial base on Wednesday to enable arms makers across Europe and North America to produce at a larger, faster scale.\par \par \uc2 Allies signed a multi-nation contract for nearly $700 million of Stinger missiles. Those stingers are produced by \uc2 Raytheon\uc2  here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 JENS STOLTENBERG, SECRETARY GENERAL, \uc2 NATO\uc2 : 23 allies are now spending at least two percent of GDP on defense, up from only three allies when we made the pledge back in 2014.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): When Biden became president, only nine of the 32 \uc2 NATO\uc2  members were spending two percent or more of their GDP on defense.\par \par \uc2 Now, 23 NATO allies are doing so, an achievement President Biden highlighted to \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies as he welcomed them tonight to the summit. Those still not pulling their weight include Canada, Spain, Italy, and Belgium, home to \uc2 NATO\uc2  headquarters, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you. A process started by former President Trump to get them to pay more.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk with National Security Council Strategic Communications Coordinator John Kirby about the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit and the presidential race in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, heavy Israeli bombardment shakes Gaza City as thousands fleeing Palestinians search for shelter and medical facilities. They're forced to shut down those medical facilities. Israel's new ground assault in Gaza's largest city is its latest effort to battle \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists, regrouping in areas the army previously said had been largely cleared.\par \par \uc2 Rescue workers dig through tons of mud and rubble as they search for dozens of people still missing after a landslide hit an unauthorized gold mine area in Indonesia. At least 23 people killed so far. 66 villagers managed to escape that landslide.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at France. One of the big stories there tonight, newly elected lawmakers arrive at the France's National Assembly for talks seeking to cobble together a government majority.\par \par \uc2 This follows, as we told you, a chaotic election result that left the legislature split among left, center, and far right parties, all of this coming less than three weeks before the start of the Paris Olympics.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As we showed you, President Biden has just finished his remarks to open the NATO Summit here in Washington. Joining us tonight, \uc2 U.S. National Security Council\uc2  Coordinator for Strategic Communications John Kirby.\par \par \uc2 John, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY COMMUNICATIONS ADVISOR: Good to be with you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to talk about \uc2 NATO\uc2 , what you're hoping out of this week? I obviously have to deal with the news of the week and all that's been happening with the questions.\par \par \uc2 For the second time in two weeks, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  editorial board, not a conservative outlet, is calling for President Biden to withdraw from the Democratic race, saying, "The Democratic Party must speak the plain truth to the president: They need to tell him that his defiance threatens to hand victory to Mr. Trump. They need to tell him that he is embarrassing himself and endangering his legacy. He needs to hear plain and clear that he is no longer an effective spokesman for his own priorities."\par \par \uc2 They're not alone. There are other big wigs, and president called them elitists. But James Carville pushes back on that. He is saying the same thing. What do you -- what do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Oh, the president has, I think, spoken very clearly to that himself, Bret. He says he is not going anywhere. That he is going to run, that he is running, and he is the nominee for the Democratic Party. And that was --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not technically, yet, but --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, not technically yet, but I mean, he is. And I got to be careful here, because (INAUDIBLE) I can't get into politics, but --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I know, I see (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: But there was a process that put him in that position, Bret, and it wouldn't be right to the people that voted for him to in that process, for him to explain his mind.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sure. But obviously those people didn't see his debate performance.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I just want to talk to -- you can talk to this. We've heard for weeks and months how sharp President Biden was behind the scenes. Now, we have these anonymous stories popping up, citing White House insiders that are different.\par \par \uc2 And congressional Democrats, both publicly and privately, are saying that they see something different, that it doesn't explain what happened on the debate stage with jet lag 12 days after he gets home, or the cold, or his performance since, and interview since. How do you see him behind the scenes?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, I do see him quite a bit. I was with him today. I was with him yesterday. I'll tell you, Bret, the guy -- the guy that I see, the guy that I work with, the commander in chief that I know, is not what people saw at that debate -- on that debate stage, just not.\par \par \uc2 He is sharp, he probes. He asked me questions yesterday about Europe that I couldn't answer, and I had to go back and do the homework and come back to him on it. He takes seriously his job as commander in chief, and I'm telling you, he is -- he's got the context, he's got the relationships. That's the guy I know. That's the president that I'm working for.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you know that there is such a disconnect between what you're saying there, what was seen on the debate stage and other things. Even in performance since, in interviews that have raised questions.\par \par \uc2 And you saw how \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , you were standing next to her said, you know, this is a conservative manipulation of cheap fakes and videos that are being played. Now, in retrospect, what does that look like?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, let's go back to the debate. I mean, the president himself talked about how he could have done a better job, and he knows that, so, I hear him out. He is not defending his performance at the debate, Bret.\par \par \uc2 All I can do is tell you the guy I've known now for 2-1/2 years. And I didn't know the president before I came to work at the White House -- never really met him.\par \par \uc2 And the man that I see every day, including today, is in command of the facts and the context, he asks good questions, he is tough on us, on the staff in the room, and, you know, he's really looking forward to this week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. About the \uc2 G7\uc2 . This is from Wall Street Journal. "German officials, aware of Biden's fatigue at night, sought to accommodate the president by planning a June 22 event with German Chancellor Olaf Schultz early in the evening. The informal event, a soiree at the Alpine resort -- during the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  summit, was arranged as a confidential meeting on Ukraine in a relaxed setting."\par \par \uc2 That, "Biden didn't show, surprising, the chancellor and his aides, officials said. Instead, Secretary Blinken arrived and announced that Biden had to go to bed, according to two people who were there."\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I can't verify those reports, these anonymous reports. I mean, I would point to you to what got done at the \uc2 G7\uc2 . And one of the things that got done was a press conference with President Zelenskyy at the end of a very, very long day, at the very end of that trip, where he signed a bilateral security agreement with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there was an awful lot of work done at the \uc2 G7\uc2 , and the president was right in the middle of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How is the world safer with President Biden?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Because he prioritizes alliances and partnerships? That is a great question, and glad you asked it. This week will show that, unlike others, he doesn't demean them, he doesn't insult them, he doesn't cast them aside. He is investing in alliances and partnerships.\par \par \uc2 This week at the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit, Bret, four countries from the Indo Pacific are going to be there. Four countries. New Zealand, Australia, Japan, South Korea. Why? Because they know that American leadership on the world stage matters.\par \par \uc2 They know that \uc2 NATO\uc2  matters, and they are watching really closely what's happening in Ukraine because of what might happen in their own neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, a couple elections ago, there was the 3:00 a.m. call. Now, there is real concern about, can he handle some -- something, you know, that doesn't happen between 10:00 and 4:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: He gets calls in the middle of the night all the time. And, you know, a couple of G7s ago, Bret, you might remember, there was a missile that fell in Poland. There was a concern that it could have been a Russian missile. That call came in to him in the middle of the night. He called together the \uc2 G7\uc2  at a snap meeting by 8:00 that morning. He gets calls all hours.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. This is the president, as we just said, just finished speaking. Here is what he told ABC just a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS, CHIEF ANCHOR, ABC NEWS: The question on so many people's minds right now is, can you serve effectively for the next four years?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: George, I'm the guy that put \uc2 NATO\uc2  together. The future. No one thought I could expand it. I'm the guy that shut Putin down. No one thought could happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Does he really think that he shut \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  down?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: He certainly has held Mr. Putin and Russia accountable for what they tried to do to Ukraine. I mean --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yu just saw what happened with the \uc2 Children's Hospital\uc2  being hit the other day.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes. Well, Bret, nobody is saying they are not still capable of attacking --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But he's definitely didn't shut them down.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Nobody is saying they are not still capable of attacking Ukraine, as you saw over the weekend, and what you're going to see tonight, tonight is it. We're going to announce more air defense capabilities for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But you can't tell me by looking at what Mr. Putin said he was going to do two years ago, that he's accomplished any of his strategic goals, and that's what the president was talking about. Everything Putin --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. But you can concede that he didn't shut \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  down.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Everything Putin said he wanted to do to Ukraine, he hasn't done.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Ukraine is still an independent, sovereign country, and that's not by accident, Bret. That's because of our leadership.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is the president and your administration tying the hands of Ukraine to be able to go after Russian bases, including the one that was responsible for that attack on the children's hospital in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We have loosened up guidelines to allow them to strike over the border, particularly when they are facing imminent threats. We have not changed any other guidelines. We are in constant communication with the Ukrainians about what they need, capabilities, the guidance, the support, and we'll continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 But what's really important to your last question is to avoid a situation where the war escalates to the point where it really is the West and \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the United States versus Russia, where the cat -- there could be truly catastrophic consequences. That's not good for anybody, certainly not good for the people of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. There is pushback to that. Here is General Jack Keane.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: These rules of engagement, they are unprecedented. From anything I am familiar with dealing with warfare. This would stop Russia from having any offensive success, but also enable Ukraine, itself, to have offensive success, because they are depleting Russia's ability to support their defensive positions. Makes no sense, this policy restriction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Like I -- still has a lot of respect inside the Pentagon and elsewhere, as you well know, but --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I have a lot of respect for him as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You give them the weapons, but they can't use them. Is that fair what is --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No, that's not fair at all. Even in the weapons, and they are using them. And they are able to strike --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But not in those places.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: They are able to strike across the border for imminent threats. But it is about defending Ukrainian territory and Ukrainian sovereignty, and that's what the focus ought to be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is the goal still to get all Russian forces out of the country?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Absolutely, they have no business being there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And do you think --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: This war could end tomorrow if Putin did exactly that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And do you think it's a stalemate right now?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: It's hard to say a stalemate. I mean, I don't think it'd be fair to put a label on it right now, because it's very static across that front. The Russians, just in the last few days, started pulling back from four -- from emplacements they had north of Kharkiv. They failed to get into Zaporizhzhia. They failed to achieve other objectives in the East. And it's because once we got the supplemental and we got the funding back, the Ukrainians started gaining some ground back, and they are still doing that, even as you and I are speaking, Bret.\par \par \uc2 So, no, I would not call it a stalemate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing. Marc Thiessen in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . "President Biden has put the United States on track to spend the lowest percent of GDP on defense since 1999, when President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  claimed a misguided post- Cold War peace dividend." How is it safer to be in that financial state with defense?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: You mean with the two percent pledge? Well, for --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, no, no. With the U.S. on defense spending. This is coming from your budget about defense here.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The president is confident that our defense budget will meet the needs for our national security interests all around the world, and obviously we're working with Congress on that every single year.\par \par \uc2 But the United -- nobody spends more on defense than the United States of America. We are the strongest, most powerful military on the planet. In fact, I'd say in human history, and the president is not going to let that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And let end it with this. There was the Medal of Freedom provided to John Stoltenberg.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He did seem to get a little emotional there.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: He did.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Of course, a surprise today?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: It was a surprise. He had no idea that was coming in. You know him as well as I do, Bret. He is a pretty stoic character. He doesn't really show a lot of emotion. But you're right. I think -- I think it got to him, and it was a -- it was really important for President Biden to bestow that honor, that Presidential Medal of Freedom. Not only does he deserve it, but it says a lot about, again, how much we value him, his leadership, and the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you're looking for tangibles out of this week.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: There will be tangibles. In fact, as I said, tonight, we're announcing additional air defense capabilities, five Patriot systems, four are long range systems, some of them are Patriots for Ukraine. We're announcing that tonight, and there will be more announcements coming in the next few days.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We appreciate you coming in.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: My pleasure. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the manslaughter trial begins for actor Alec Baldwin.\par \par \uc2 As we go to break, former Oklahoma Republican Senator \uc2 Jim Inhofe\uc2  has died. He was the state's longest serving senator from 1994 to 2023. Inhofe, an Army veteran, a major support of national defense. He chaired the Armed Services Committee in the Senate. He was also a climate change skeptic. His family says he suffered a stroke over the Fourth of July holiday.\par \par \uc2 An aide says Inhofe died peacefully this morning, surrounded by his wife, children, and other family members. \uc2 Jim Inhofe\uc2  was 89 years old.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, jury selection is underway in the manslaughter trial of actor Alec Baldwin. He's charged in the death of a cinematographer killed when a gun discharged on a movie set. Chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt is in Santa Fe, New Mexico, with the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Baldwin, are you feeling confident today?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Alec Baldwin arrived for day one of his trial, saying nothing to reporters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you going to be a free man in two weeks, Mr. Baldwin?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Inside the Santa Fe County courthouse, jury selection got underway in what is expected to be a two-week trial. Baldwin faces one count of involuntary manslaughter in the death of cinematographer Halyna Hutchins on the set of the movie "Rust." Hutchins was killed when a gun Baldwin was holding and pointing in the cinematographer's direction fired a live round. Baldwin says he never pulled the trigger and couldn't possibly have foreseen a live round had somehow been put in the weapon.\par \par \uc2 LUKE NIKAS BALDWIN ATTORNEY: We do intend to argue that Mr. Baldwin didn't have subjective knowledge of a substantial risk to Ms. Hutchins life because there wasn't a live bullet. In his mind he didn't think there was a live bullet in the gun.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Prosecutors say testing proves Baldwin must have pulled the trigger and that his actions were needlessly dangerous.\par \par \uc2 ERLINDA JOHNSON, SPECIAL PROSECUTOR: He should have been aware that his conduct of pointing a firearm at another human being, cocking that hammer, and pulling that trigger is reckless conduct.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Baldwin's attorneys won a pretrial battle Monday when Judge Mary Marlowe Sommer ruled prosecutors could not bring up Baldwin's role as a producer of the movie and therefore could not accuse him of overseeing what some witnesses were claimed were lax safety standards on set.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): And we have heard in the last few minutes that a jury has been selected, so we will begin tomorrow morning with opening statements. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the latest movement surrounding President Biden's political future. First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 26 in Houston where officials say six deaths are being blamed on what had been hurricane Beryl as its remnants tore through southeast Texas. More than 2 million people lost power. Officials say most of those were still without electricity earlier today.\par \par \uc2 FOX 5 in San Diego as more than 1,000 firefighters battle a blaze in the mountains of Santa Barbara County. The wildfire has grown to more than 34 square miles. That blaze just eight percent contained so far.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Phoenix from our affiliate, FOX 10. One of the big stories there tonight, surgeons at the \uc2 Mayo Clinic\uc2  in Arizona remove a patient's cancerous larynx and replace it with a donated one. Transplants of the so-called voice box are extremely rare and normally are not an option for people with active cancer. The man has now regained his voice. He's only the third person in the U.S. to undergo a total larynx transplant.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES CARVILLE, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Everybody knows what's going on here. We have all been through it. We have seen our parents go through it, our grandparents, our aunts and uncles and neighbors and relatives. This is all a matter of time.\par \par \uc2 He's going to come to the conclusion that this is just not a good idea. And he is going to resist it. And he is going to listen to his family. It's inevitable. He will come to the conclusion. People will get the message to him. He will understand. His family will understand. They'll pray on it, and they'll make the right decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: James Carville, Democratic strategist, saying President Biden will not be the Democratic nominee, and it's only a matter of time. That's counter to what we see on Capitol Hill today. "Axios," "Biden rebels in Congress see their revolt crumbling. Just before House Democrats Tuesday morning "come-to-Jesus" meeting on President Biden's path forward, a smaller group of swing district Democrats held what sources described as a despondent gathering with actual tears." But yet, the White House citing the Congressional Black Caucus members and others rallying to the president's side.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Ben Domenech, editor at large for "The Spectator" and host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, New Hampshire talk radio show Christopher Ryan, and Morgan Ortagus, host of "The Morgan Ortagus Show" on \uc2 Sirius XM\uc2 , founder of Polaris National Security. Morgan, had John Kirby on there and obviously spent some time talking about a number of the editorials and some of what he sees behind the scenes. This disconnect between the president is really sharp behind the scenes and he's on it. And then what everybody sees not only on that debate stage but since is pretty wide.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: You know, if there had been proper scrutiny by the press corps over the past, not even just the past three-and-a-half years of his presidency but in the campaign as well, if there had been proper scrutiny, a lot of this could have come to light earlier, and the Democrats could not be in the pickle that they're in now, Bret, which is that they are trying to figure out in a chaotic summer leading up to a convention how they replace their nominee. It's the dirty laundry all airing and happening in public.\par \par \uc2 This is not the position you wanted to be in. They wanted to in be a position where they were, quote-unquote, running against MAGA extremists, running on abortion and these types of issues. Now they are in the defensive position. And I think at some point Democrats are going to accept that whether it's Biden or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  that they're going to have to probably take the loss. And Trump only has four years, but maybe better just to