uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, those swing states for the RCP average virtually everywhere except for Michigan, Trump is really stacking up his lead.\par \par \uc2 But here's the thing to keep in mind. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  went into that debate as the underdog. He was losing this election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which is why they said they did it, really.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Yes. It was clear that they wanted to do this early to do a reset. The whole goal was to try to change the conversation away from the trajectory it was already on. So I actually think that this process is not over, despite what maybe people are saying in Washington about we're just not going to be able to do it. It's up to him, that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 Look, the Democratic Party has in its own bylaws the ability for delegates to make a different decision, to say in all good conscience they can't support the nominee that they were assigned to vote for. There's no legal requirement for them to do so. If we get further along in this and he has more senior moments and we see these polls continue to creep up and up and up in terms of Trump's victory, there are a lot of Democrats who are going to be completely scared about losing their own seats. There are a lot of competitive elections this cycle. And I think at that point they make a shift to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of what's happening, we have got the \uc2 RNC\uc2  convention starting next week. The former president hasn't done much. He did \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  last night, called into that. We have today \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  has released her 97 delegates, urging them to vote for the former president. Her spokesman said that she was not invited to the convention. Seemed a little interesting. What about the Republicans' play to let the Biden news cycle flourish in this environment, Morgan?\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: One of the smartest things that the campaign has done. Susie Wiles and Chris LaCivita are running a very tight, disciplined campaign. That's been said about them for the past year, even during the primary. And it's been especially pertinent and an example of running that disciplined campaign this week.\par \par \uc2 So I think you're going to see a lot of enthusiasm next week at the GOP. You certainly won't see the infighting. In the 2016 convention, that's when Ted Cruz famously gave the speech. There was a little bit of chaos among the Republicans. You're going to see a very united Republican Party next week no matter who the VP nominee is. And I think that will drum up some excitement, certainly, next week.\par \par \uc2 I think there's going to be curtains. Everyone is going to have to stand behind a curtain, Bret. And then one of the curtains comes up if you're the VP nominee.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. We're going to talk about that next panel.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Remember how much people, Republicans, even those who support him, have said if only \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be more disciplined. What we just saw is unprecedented, 12 days of discipline. I mean, it's incredible. It's not just the campaign. It's the man himself, and he has pulled it off.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chris, when we were heading to New Hampshire ahead of the primary, did you ever think this is where we would be right now?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Yes, I thought there was definitely an opportunity given Biden's age, and he didn't partake in the primary.\par \par \uc2 Just one thing on the delegates for a second. Republicans tried to do this behind the scenes in 2016. You have to have a candidate. You can't just free the delegates and anticipate that there is going to be somebody magically comes about. There has to be a Democrat that steps forward.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right. We will see. We'll have it covered.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Trump veepstakes, as promised, and Candidate Casino, and then the world's oldest tennis tournament.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO, (R-FL): We can't afford another four years like the last four, and I'm willing to help in any way I can.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2 , (R-ND) FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It doesn't really matter who Trump's V.P. is. The guy is so strong across every demographic.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT, (R-SC): The only thing I'm talking to President Trump about is how to make sure he gets four more years.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE, (R-OH): Could I stand ultimately in the big chair? And look, if he asks me, I'll think about it. But I think the answer is yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Those guys are, it appears, on the short list for VP. And the former president says he is close, maybe days away.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I haven't made a final decision, but I have some ideas as to where we're going. And a little bit, you know, we wanted to see what they're doing, to be honest, because it might make a difference. I don't know. I'm not sure it would. But there are those who say Trump is waiting until he finds out what's going to happen with crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It could be. We're back with the panel. Chris, it's interesting. I think there is probably this feeling that this is "Apprentice" like, and you are going to drag it maybe all the way into Monday of the convention week.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Yes. It comes down to who makes the most sense for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in hitting core constituencies and also the person who is most comfortable with him. To me, that's \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 . Burgum is a person that is very easy to get along with. He's relatable. He knows how to have good conversations with people. And I spent a ton of time with him during the New Hampshire primary. And Burgum is the individual that he has the type of gravitas where he can be trusted and also he can be listened to. And I think that Trump is looking for that kind of adult in the room type of figure, despite the fact that Trump is 10 years older than Burgum. And Burgum provides \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 -esque type of a feel when it comes to showing voters there is somebody there that will kind of make sure that Trump doesn't go off the rails. And I think Burgum provides that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, well, since you are already there on your prediction, we're going to take this journey down a place we call Candidate Casino. This is the Trump VP version. And Chris has always put his chips on the table. So what is the breakdown here?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: We're going to do $60 for Trump (ph), $35 for Vance, and five percent for \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 . I think that Burgum provides that type of a stability that Trump knows he needs in order to win independent voters and folks that are still undecided.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Morgan, bets are open.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: I'm going to go a little bit more, a little bit unconventional. I am also going to do $60 on J.D. Vance. I'm going to give $40 to Ben Carson. I think, all of this talk about who helps with the electoral map, it's silly. None of that stuff matters. The VP never matters. The VP could probably hurt. The VP pick, what really matters who is spending every single day on the campaign trail and then every day in the White House with the president. Who does he want to have lunch with?\par \par \uc2 When I -- when COVID was happening and I was in the Oval Office and I was in the White House. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was in there. President Trump trusted him to lead that task force. It comes down to personality. I think that President Trump -- former President Trump gets along with J.D. Vance and Ben Carson the best just one-on-one. They like each other.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, what do you got, 100 bucks?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: My theory is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn't really like senators. He doesn't really like politicians that much. So he prefers to have people who have been in other worlds first. And so because of that, I'm putting, and I didn't coordinate, I promise, with Chris, I'm putting half my chips on \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , another portion on Ben Carson. But then I also have $25 on a wild card choice. I think that the president, given everything that has played out in the last 10 days, could feel like he has the ability to just pick someone who is kind of off the table or hasn't gotten as much --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, there is a little scuttlebutt about the governor of Virginia, \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 . The pre-debate poll in our FOX poll was a dead heat in Virginia, someplace that if they won, that would be a big, big deal. But if you look at that picture, he is super tall. I'm not sure.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Right, he is very tall.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm not sure that goes to central casting. We'll see. Days away.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, "Today's Throwback." On this day in 1877, the All-England Croquet and Lawn Tennis Club began its first lawn tennis tournament in the London suburb of Wimbledon. Twenty-one amateurs competed in the gentleman's singles tournament. Twenty-seven-year-old Spencer Gore was the first champion. The world's oldest tournament is in its 147th year going on right now. Among those still in contention, singles, 24-time Grand Slam champion Novak Djokovic and Spanish phenom, defending champion Carlos Alcaraz. The only top five ranked women's player still alive in singles is Elena Rybakina.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we begin the series that looks at the efforts to boost cybersecurity in the private sector. Big topic.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for t