LC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Harold, go.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: What about --\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: So, I can see now that action movie. This 17-year-old jumped from a jet ski on to this boat that had capsized and got controlled the vessel, saved people's lives, saved his and thank God, he's going to the Navy in the fall. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And he's going to the Naval Academy.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Stuntman.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's it for us, have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It was a scintillating podcast this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It was.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of juicy detail to that, Greg won't hear it. OK. Thanks, Dana.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the pressure intensifies on President Biden to drop out of the race. Today, the president defiantly insisting he's not going anywhere, both in a phone interview and in a letter to congressional Democrats.\par \par \uc2 This comes as the president prepares for a major international event, a \uc2 NATO\uc2  conference right here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. White House officials are claiming they're not aware of any examples of national security officials withholding information from President Biden late at night, even though the president now says he doesn't work that well after 8:00 p.m. And President Biden is ignoring all calls from Democratic colleagues to exit the race on his own.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Why? Why? I'm getting so frustrated.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The president daring democratic officials to try to remove him with a letter to elected Democrats, the voters and the voters alone decide the nominee of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: Joe has made it clear that he's all in, that's the decision that he's made.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The Biden campaign is trying to change the subject wrapping buses with pro-Biden slogans in the \uc2 RNC\uc2  host city, Milwaukee, but questions about his health persist.\par \par \uc2 MIKA BRZEZINSKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Have you been tested for any age related illnesses, pre-Parkinson's or anything like that?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They tested me before, I thought maybe I had COVID, maybe there was something wrong, I had an infection or something. They tested me, they gave me those tests.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): White House visitor logs show a Parkinson's specialist at the White House eight times in eight months.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Has his physician seen a reason to reevaluate him for Parkinson's since the February physical?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: No.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): A radio host in Philadelphia parted ways with her station days after admitting she used questions provided by the Biden campaign to interview him. In a phone interview today, the president could be heard shuffling papers.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Posts out there in the local, you know, race. I'm reading from a list of lies.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Officials insisted it was just a list and his answers were from the heart.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The president was very clear there was no script at all.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Questions about Biden cheat sheets and teleprompters date back years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Has \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ever used a teleprompter during local interviews or to answer Q&A with supporters?\par \par \uc2 T.J. DUCKLO, JOE BIDEN CAMPAIGN PRESS SECRETARY: Bret, we're not going to engage -- this is -- this is straight from the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, yes, they're using it.\par \par \uc2 DUCKLO: And what it does -- and what it does, Bret, is it's trying to distract the American people.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): And there's no plan to change anything because this has been a winning strategy.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So, I'm not lost. I haven't lost. I beat him last time and I'll beat him this time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): White House officials are claiming they can't tell us anything about the Parkinson's specialist who has made all those visits here to the White House because they're trying to protect the specialists privacy even though his name and title appear in publicly searchable visitors logs. One of those logs shows that one of the visits in January was with the president's personal physician, who also remains unavailable for Q and A, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More on this with Britt Hume and Dr. Siegel in just a bit. Peter, another topic. Why did the White House urge lawmakers to vote against a bill that requires voters to prove their U.S. citizens?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Because they're insisting in a rare official statement of administration policy that there are already laws that prevent non-citizens from voting and in their estimation, it is rare for non-citizens to try to vote in U.S. elections, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Additional high ranking House Democrats have joined that group asking President Biden to step aside. Several Democratic senators are also casting doubt on the president's candidacy.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie has that part of the story live from Capitol Hill. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  today said that he is for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  but his Senate Democratic colleagues are not on the same page and that includes Intel Chair Mark Warner, who just last week was reported to be rounding up a group of Senate Democrats to call on President Biden to step aside. And here's what he told our camera just moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Asking questions, at least in my case, made me a better campaigner. And I think a lot of folks who are raising some questions they need to get asked.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): So, vulnerable Democrats have also been cagey about their support for President Biden. Ohio Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  reportedly said today that his voters have some legitimate questions about Biden's future. New Mexico Senator \uc2 Martin Heinrich\uc2  says the president must demonstrate that it was just really one bad night. And Montana Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  saying that Biden has to prove that he really is up for another four years.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): The senators are up for election, they're going to have a lot of input. I'm anxious to hear from them and anxious to hear from their conversation with the president, they've had that and we'll just see what happens.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Today, the president sent a letter to congressional Democrats in an effort to stem any more defectors that came after four senior House Democrats joined five ranking members -- excuse me, rank and file members in abandoning him.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Mike Quigley\uc2  who's among those members predicts that more people will follow as the week goes on, as Biden supporters push back.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE QUIGLEY\uc2  (D-IL): I know individuals that I've talked to who have been processing this, thinking about it, talking to their constituents, most importantly, looking at polls. So, it's going to be an amazing, unfortunately horrible week. But I do think the numbers will grow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, as the Senate Democrats are walking in for votes, behind me right now, a lot of us are shouting at them, do you support President Biden and many of them are not answering that question.\par \par \uc2 As of right now, the Congressional Black Caucus is meeting with the president tonight. The chair endorsed him earlier today, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we'll follow all angles. Aishah Hasnie, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks mixed ahead of new inflation data, the Dow lost 31, the S&P 500 gained six, the NASDAQ was up 51. Both are new record closes.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a major Russian missile attack across Ukraine has killed at least 31 people and injured 154 others. Officials say one weapon struck a large children's hospital in the capital city of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill Melugin has details tonight on that. Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. This is believed to be Russia's biggest bombardment of Ukraine in at least four months with dozens of these missiles targeting several cities all across the country. And it comes just before the NATO Summit happens in Washington this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): In the Ukrainian capital of Kyiv, the country's largest children's hospital was hit by a Russian missile that interrupted heart surgeries, it forced child cancer patients to have to take their treatments outdoors. At least two staff members were reportedly killed there while five others died in other strikes across Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 This daytime Russian barrage targeted five Ukrainian cities with more than 40 missiles of different types. That's according Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . At least 31 people were killed. More than 150 people were hurt all across the country.\par \par \uc2 Russia claims they did not target the children's hospital and that all of their targets were military infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Today, the White House was asked if this attack was Putin's way of somehow sending a message to \uc2 NATO\uc2  days before the alliance meets in Washington.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, WHITE HOUSE NATIONAL SECURITY COMMUNICATIONS ADVISOR: I can't draw the line that this is some sort of message. But as I said and what you're going to see over the course of the week is a very set of strong signals and messages to Mr. Putin that he can't wait \uc2 NATO\uc2  out. Can't wait the United States out that we're going to continue to support Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, as a result of these Russian missile attacks, the \uc2 United Nations Security Council\uc2  scheduled an emergency meeting for tomorrow. That is at the request of France and Ecuador.\par \par \uc2 But Russia holds that council's rotating presidency this month. So, Russia will be the ones presiding over that meeting tomorrow. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Bill, thank you. Also overseas, a surprise surge from left wing voters has left the French government without a clear majority party.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  facing a big challenge now heading into a very important that next few weeks. Correspondent Alex Hogan has the latest on France tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Massive celebrations and protests Sunday in the second and final stage of France's parliamentary elections. Rioters launched fireworks and police fired tear gas to break up the crowds.\par \par \uc2 In a shocking twist, the country's right-wing national rally which came in first place last weekend dropped to third place. French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  centrist alliance came in second and a left wing coalition, the new Popular Front won big, securing the most seats in the National Assembly.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-LUC MELENCHON\uc2 , LA FRANCE INSOUMISE PARTY FOUNDER (through translator): People have voted, voted with their conscience. We won, a result we were told was impossible.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): Polls predicted the national rally which campaigned on immigration crime and the economy would win the second round.\par \par \uc2 Still, it's not a total loss. This is the R.N.'s (ph) largest number of seats in parliament to date.\par \par \uc2 MARINE LE PEN, FRENCH NATIONAL RALLY PARTY (through translator): We will lose another year another year of illegal immigration. But if that's what it takes, well, then that's what it takes.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): In a gamble calling the elections early, Macron's position is now weaker. Today, his Prime Minister Gabriel Attal resigned although the president has asked him to stay on temporarily.\par \par \uc2 With none of the parties winning an absolute majority, its political deadlock.\par \par \uc2 FRANCOIS HEISBOURG\uc2 , INTERNATIONAL INSTITUTE FOR STRATEGIC STUDIES: The situation is indeed one of parliamentary confusion.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (voice over): There is no deadline by which Macron will need to appoint a new prime minister and no defined terms of how he'll do so. Among potential options, he could ask someone within the left wing coalition, the largest bloc to take the job.\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2 , FRENCH PRESIDENT (through translator): The governance of this country is going to be extraordinarily difficult within the next weeks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): A period of uncertainty at a time when the country is also on the world stage. France will host the Olympics which kickoff the week after next and already strikes are expected, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex Hogan in London. Alex, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, reading President Biden's political and medical tea leaves, Brit Hume and Dr. Marc Siegel are here.\par \par \uc2 First Beyond our Borders, Israeli forces advanced deeper into the Gaza strips largest city in pursuit of militant terrorists who had regrouped there. It has sent thousands of Palestinians fleeing from an area ravaged in the early weeks of the nine month long war.\par \par \uc2 The Gaza City incursion comes as Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  draw closer to bridging gaps and indirect talks over a possible ceasefire and hostage release.\par \par \uc2 Reformist candidate Masoud Pezeshkian wins Iran's runoff presidential election, he defeats a hardliner \uc2 Saeed Jalili\uc2  by promising to reach out to the West and ease enforcement on the country's mandatory headscarf law after years of sanctions and protests squeezing the Islamic Republic. Pezeshkian promised no radical changes to Iran Shiite theocracy.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Chiba, Japan. One of the big stories there tonight, Japan and the Philippines signed a key defense pact. It allows the deployment of Japanese forces for joint drills in the Southeast Asian nation that came under brutal Japanese occupation in World War II, but is now building an alliance with Tokyo as both face and increasingly assertive and aggressive China.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking right now, you're looking live, First Lady Jill Biden, appearing at an event in Columbus, Georgia. We're going to monitor that event for news. Turn it around, maybe dip in a little bit as the cheers get, "four more years" here. Let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Four more years! Four more years! Four more years! Four more years!\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Four more years!\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Four more years!\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN: Gloria. Gloria, you have such a powerful story, and I appreciate you and your families and may year -- many years of service to our country, in and out of your uniform. And I'm so glad to have many members of the Georgia State legislature with us today. Thank you all for being here Georgia.\par \par \uc2 I was at Fort Moore a couple years back to meet with Army families. So, I'm glad to be back to visit with this community that so many military families and veterans call home.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN: So, this is a beautiful space, by the way, it's really -- so, four years ago, Joe went from being a military dad to a commander in chief.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN: It's a responsibility he stepped into with pride, because, as Joe reminds everyone, any chance he gets, our U.S., military is the finest fighting force in the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN: Of all that Joe has shouldered since he took the oath of office, he believes that we have only one sacred obligation in preparing those we send into harm's way and caring for them and their families when they come home.\par \par \uc2 So, anyone can tell you what they want to do, but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can tell you what he's done. For our military and their families, he's lowered the cost of childcare and mental health services.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Yes!\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN: He signed the first and only executive order to help military spouses keep their careers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jill Biden, the first lady, speaking Columbus Georgia, want to dip in and just see if she had anything, at the beginning, to talk about. We'll monitor this for news. That in Georgia, the spot of the now infamous debate.\par \par \uc2 Standby.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's estimated we could save over $100 billion a year in Medicare -- in Medicare if we've made all these modernizations and changes.\par \par \uc2 Dealing with everything we have to do with -- look, if we finally beat Medicare.\par \par \uc2 This is the first president in history of United States of America that's anybody seeking asylum has to do it in another country.\par \par \uc2 And I'm going to continue to move until we get the total ban on -- the total initiative relative to what we can do with more Border Patrol and more asylum officers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Then and now, debates of the past and the one that just passed. Let's bring in Fox News chief political analyst, Brit Hume. And Dr Marc Siegel, professor of medicine at \uc2 New York University\uc2 , Langone Medical Center,\par \par \uc2 Dr. Siegel, I want to start with you. There is this New York Post piece. A lot of media picked it up. "Biden's physician met with Parkinson's disease specialist in the White House: A top Washington, D.C. neurologist had a meeting with President Biden's personal doctor at the White House earlier this year, visitor logs reviewed by The Post show."\par \par \uc2 We've gone on to confirm there have been numerous visits by that specialist. The White House would not go down that road. But there are people, Dr Siegel who look at different elements of President Biden, including that interview with George Stephanopoulos, where he holds this pen throughout the interview, he's not writing on a pad or he's not taking notes, but he's holding this pen. Some to say it means something else about his current condition.\par \par \uc2 How do you see things as things have developed since the debate with his physical standing?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARC SIEGEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Bret, first of all, I'm concerned about something called cognitive reserve, which is, you know, all these excuses under stress, haven't slept enough late at night. All of that speaks to cognitive reserve. Is there enough? And we need a lot of it in the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, and also, any kind of cognitive problems wax and wane. So, you can't say, oh, here's a good day, here is a bad day, because what if something, God forbid catastrophic happens to our country on a bad day.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, we need to show compassion and empathy for the president, because if he's being managed the way it looks like he is, you just saw that from the first lady, seeming to be speaking for him, he may not realize the extent to which his problems exist here. And then, I feel bad for him.\par \par \uc2 And then the other thing you brought up is about Parkinson's disease, you know, looking at it from a neurological point of view. And I've talked to many neurologists, yes, they had him there to kind of rule that out, but the way he walks looks like something else, like it might be insufficient blood flow from his atrial fibrillation or from his aneurysms years ago. That's called vascular problems.\par \par \uc2 Here is my point. Whatever it is, it could be diagnosed by an MRI, probably, which nobody is talking about, whether he is had. And as far as a neurologist diagnosing Parkinson's, that's a clinical diagnosis. You test somebody for rigidity, you look at their gait, how they are walking. It wouldn't be something that would require 20 visits to the White House.\par \par \uc2 And most importantly, if the president's doctor, Kevin O'Connor, puts on two physicals, Bret, that he doesn't have Parkinson's, he better be telling the truth, because that's part of the medical record.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, we've both been in White Houses before. When you get a sense that the White House press corps has turned or is jumping on something because they sense they are not being told the full story that happened today, there was a lot of frustration in that room with \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And it comes after these efforts by President Biden to go on media to say he is OK and he is staying in the fight. Here is one with "Morning Joe, a couple of different clips from that this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm getting so frustrated, by the elites, no, I'm not talking about you guys, but by the elites in the party who they know so much more.\par \par \uc2 I have terrible night, and I really regret it happened. But the fact to the matter is, how can you assure you're going to be on, you know, face back and intervene on your way to go to, you know, work tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 Age, age wasn't, you know, the idea I'm too old.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, age wasn't, you know, the idea I'm too old. A lot of those sentences ended sort of like that. Your thoughts on all this, well.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, that kind of stuff. I don't think Bret, despite the president's hopes, are very reassuring to people who looked at that debate, and said, oh my God, this man is too old to be president, and he is not -- his mental acuity is gone.\par \par \uc2 The problem I think the White House has now is that, a lot of the press corps overlooked this, and they should not have, and they have only themselves to blame.\par \par \uc2 And, but they are mad, and that scene in the White House today where they were peppering \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  with questions about the neurologist and his apparent eight visits over a period of time and all that is a sign that it's going to be very difficult for the White House, the president, and his campaign to change the subject. This matter has got to resolve itself, one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 The voices from the Hill and elsewhere is saying, Mr. President, you need to step aside are growing, not massively, but growing nonetheless. And the president has dug in today with the letter that was reported earlier and with his own statements publicly.\par \par \uc2 And so, they are in an impasse, and as long as that's the case, this thing goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HUME: And there is no ready way if he doesn't step aside to get him out of the race. So, I don't -- there is one thing that could change this, Bret, and that is the person they are depending on anyway to help Biden get elected, and that's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He's got a convention coming up next week.\par \par \uc2 A lot will depend on the tone of that convention, and how he, Trump, acts during it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Brit, let me -- let me just play one more sound bite that they - - he -- the president went on black radio, a couple of different stations, and we were told that the host was given a list of questions to pick from, to choose from for the president that came out afterwards.\par \par \uc2 Here is another clip from that. This is again, post-debate in the days where he is trying to salvage some of this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I am proud to be, as I said, the first vice president, the first black woman, to serve with a black president. I'm proud of the -- we have long of first black woman Supreme Court. There is just so much that we can do, because together, we -- there is nothing. Look, it's is the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Brit, what would it take -- do you think there'll be some other major incident that changes, the dam breaking? Or do you think where we are, where we are politically?\par \par \uc2 HUME: I think we kind of are where we are for now, Bret. You know, he is going to do, apparently, a full-blown news conference later, that might, you know, if he has a good day and is in good form, that might help a little bit.\par \par \uc2 But I think the impression that has been created over time and the public, I think, was way ahead of the media on this that he is not all there anymore. It is now nearly indelible, and that I can't really think of an event that could happen that would change that, as I suggest, Bret, the only thing could happen is if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  used his, you know, instead of using his convention to be a reassuring president, used his convention to remind all the people don't like him, why they don't like him.\par \par \uc2 Short of that, I don't see any way for the Democrats to get out of this anytime soon, unless Biden, which he says he won't do, agrees to step aside.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, Brit, Dr. Siegel, go ahead, real quick. I got to go.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Well, I just want to say, I completely agree with Brit. But I want to say that, believe it or not, to the American public out there, this president is in denial. He doesn't realize how bad he is, and he is not going to step aside. They have to actually get him to do that.\par \par \uc2 He doesn't understand. That's my view of this kind of cognitive problem, if it's dementia.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Dr. Siegel, Brit, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 By the way, former President Trump will be on \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  tonight.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a shift in the GOP's platform on the issue of abortion and pushback on a conservative think tank's blueprint for a possible second Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we get a live report from South Texas as Tropical Storm Beryl pounds the region.\par \par \uc2 And later, closing arguments begin in the corruption and bribery trial of New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are just a week away from the start of the Republican National Convention in Milwaukee, Wisconsin. The big lingering question tonight, who will former President Trump choose to be his running mate? There's also a big change in the party's position on a few key issues for the GOP's platform, and a battle over a think tank blueprint for a possible way forward. Here is chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Former President Trump has been keeping a very low profile ever since the debate. Behind the scenes, however, he's reportedly responsible for a major shift in GOP policy. For the first time in 40 years the \uc2 RNC\uc2  has dramatically revised its language on abortion in a new platform that omits mention of a national ban and is in step with Mr. Trump's position allowing states to set limits.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE, (R-OH): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the pragmatic leader here. He's saying most abortion policy is going to be decided by the states.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: There are signs some pro-life groups are changing their messaging to try win over voters, profiling women with difficult pregnancies who carried their babies to term. Meantime, Mr. Trump and his surrogates are distancing themselves from Project 25, a controversial roadmap for the next Republican administration crafted by \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO, (R-FL): Think tanks do think tank stuff.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Controversial with some because it would overhaul the federal government, firing as many as 50,000 government employees and expanding presidential powers.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , (D-PA): It's like, do we want the kind of a president that is all about Project 25? And anyone can read on that because that's what Trump is all about.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The former president could name his running mate any time ahead of the next Monday's opening day of the convention. Some names mentioned, Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , Senators \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , J.D. Vance, and Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 DOUG BURGUM\uc2 , (R-ND) FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He's a great selection of people for V.P., and he's going to have