ajor storm for a Cat one because it hit directly on a huge population center and it's done a lot of wind, rain, and damage, and we've also this some tornadoes reported on the ground.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Before hitting Texas, Beryl grew to a category five, tearing its way through the Caribbean, destroying homes and wreaking havoc on islands in the Gulf of Mexico. While it's now been downgraded to a tropical storm, it's still causing life-threatening conditions as it moves inland.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: So you have waters meeting each other. The bayous will overflow in Houston.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Rivers around Houston are rising above their banks. High waters are quickly rushing through streets and highways. One man sat on top of his truck to escape the water as he waited to be rescued.\par \par \uc2 LINA HIDALGO, (D) HARRIS COUNTY, TEXAS, JUDGE: We don't want people to have first responders put themselves in danger simply because they didn't heed the advice to stay indoors while the hurricane passes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): And Bret, this evening Texas officials now estimate nearly 3 million people lost power. It could take days to restore. You could see the swollen Buffalo Bayou behind me. People have been stopping to take pictures. And Port Houston, which describes itself as the country's largest for waterborne tonnage, says it will be closed tomorrow. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matt Finn live in Houston. Matt, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Closing arguments in the corruption and bribery trial of a New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 . We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 And later, a homecoming after mission of more than a year. You'll want to see this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Closing arguments are underway in the bribery and corruption trial of New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 . We have been following it. Senior correspondent Eric Shawn is in New York with the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: New Jersey Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  leaving court after hearing the government accuse him of selling his office. Prosecutors giving their closing arguments in his bribery conspiracy trial, telling the jury that Menendez allegedly accepted hundreds of thousands of dollars in gold and cash from 2018 through 2022 in exchange for using his power in the Senate to deliver political favors, benefiting Egyptian officials and New Jersey businessmen.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the trial, the jury saw hundreds of photos showing the $486,000 in cash and $100,000 worth of gold bars the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it found hidden around the senator's New Jersey home. Assistant U.S. Attorney Paul Monteleoni telling the jury, quote, "\uc2 Robert Menendez\uc2  put his power up for sale. It wasn't enough for him to be one of the most powerful people in Washington. You saw again and again a clear pattern of corruption." Menendez declined to testify in his own defense last week.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ, (D-NJ): The government is intoxicated with their own rhetoric. They spent two hours on charts, not witnesses, that came before the court, on charts. Two hours.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: The 70-year-old senator's attorneys argued that his practice of keeping large amounts of cash at home was part of his Cuban culture, for emergencies, even though Menendez was born in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Menendez was charged in a similar federal corruption case in 2017. That ended in a mistrial. But if he is convicted of the 16 counts against him here, he could face up to 200 years in prison if he is sentenced consecutively. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Eric Shawn following the trial in New York. Eric, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on whether President Biden can survive these increasing calls for him to step aside and what comes next.\par \par \uc2 First, here's 