sident Biden is so far not taking any of the advice he has been given from Democrats to demonstrate he can handle unscripted moments in public. Instead, he has written a letter to congressional Democrats and called into a friendly cable news program, and close listeners could here pages turning on the broadcast while he spoke. Here's a piece of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The bottom line here is that we're not going anywhere. I am not going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 I wouldn't be running if I didn't absolutely believe that I am the best can to the to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2024. We had a democratic nominating process where the voters spoke clearly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So with that, let's bring in our panel, Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com, Julia Manchester, national politics reporter for "The Hill," and Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 . Matthew, your take of where we stand and where we're going?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: It's a complete train wreck for the Democratic Party. There are a lot of competing if-then clauses. If Biden shouldn't be the nominee in 24 because he can't serve another four years, then why is he president now? But conversely, if Biden is right by saying that, well, to George Stephanopoulos, if I lose, at the end of the day I will still feel good if I gave it my best shot, if I gave it my all. Then why have the Democrats or have Biden framed this election as a referendum on the state of American democracy?\par \par \uc2 None of these statements can be true at the same time. And so what would have now is a stubborn man who may not know the extent of his condition in denial, not just about his condition, but also did about the state of his electoral campaign against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and he is on a course to lose, perhaps bigly, as the former president might say.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, right. On Capitol Hill, five Democratic members of Congress now openly calling for President Biden to step aside, and we've got those images of who they are. They have been speaking out. But it seems like there are more besides these who are on the fence. Take a listen to some of those words.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE QUIGLEY\uc2 , (D-IL): I think we have to be honest with ourselves. The situation is not getting better. Frankly, it's getting worse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Democrats are showing that we are a much more vibrant party. We are willing to question ourselves. We are willing to change strategy if that's what it takes to win.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They are not calling for the president to step aside because they think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a terrible person, because they think that he is a failure. They are calling for him to step aside because they are scared.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm getting so frustrated by the elites. Run against me. Go ahead, announce for president. Challenge me at the convention.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He says challenge me at the convention, Julia. There's a strange thing in Democratic politics. August 7th was going to be this virtual nomination process because Ohio passed a law that essentially the DNC had to adhere to get Ohio's delegates. They changed that law and they moved it to September 1st. But the DNC is keeping that virtual lock-in the nomination August 7th. It seems, to some political watchers, that that's an effort to hurry up and lock him in.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER, "THE HILL": Yes, definitely. I think there is definitely that sense among Democrats that the DNC is trying to move this along, to put this story to bed finally. But look, the party is not unified on this. And there are number of Democrats on 