 to run against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if their issue is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can't beat him? The polling shows Trump beating \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , and on and on and on. So they are in this position where they actually don't have a very deep bench to go against the former president given all the issue sets like inflation and the border that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is good on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One of the people who is definitely pushing for President Biden to stay is his wife, Jill Biden, who, obviously was on the front cover of "Vogue" magazine. Got a lot of attention in the days after the debate for that. Now she is on the campaign trail. We said we'd monitor this in Columbus. Here's a piece of that in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, U.S. FIRST LADY: He disparages those who sacrifice for our country. His own chief of staff said he called POWs and those who died in war losers and suckers. He's evil.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talking about former President Trump. Obviously, the former president pushed back against that in the debate very vigorously. Matthew, she is out campaigning, and, obviously, in it to stick in it.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Well, when the 3:00 a.m. phone call comes about the international crisis, we can only hope or think that it will be Dr. Jill picking up the phone. And I don't know whether that leaves many Americans reassured.\par \par \uc2 The real problem for me, Bret, here is people are talking about Biden, can he win this election? That's the main concern among Democrats and the media. They are not actually talking about Biden's condition, which is troubling for him and for people around him. But also, most importantly, it's troubling for the country. We are at the foothills of a major global crisis here, and there are adversaries of the United States watching very carefully, and they can be ready to move between now and Election Day and the next inauguration.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think it will be fascinating to see what these \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders say and how they say it even behind the scenes, Julia. I wouldn't be surprised if the administration tries to do some big shiny thing on foreign policy in the \uc2 RNC\uc2  week to try to take some of the oxygen out of Milwaukee. But can this president survive this if it is bumping along like we see it now?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: It's hard to say. This isn't going away for the president, but at the same time, he is saying is he not going anywhere. He's clearly stubborn. He clearly has a team around him telling him to stay in. So we haven't been here before. So I can't really predict this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And by the way, the former president will be on with \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  tonight. That will be one to watch. We haven't really heard much from him. We'll see. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you going to come out? Hey!\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Get me out of there. Four crew members who spent more than a year inside a 3-D printed habitat similar to what may be used on Mars have emerged. The volunteers shared the 1,700 square foot facility, again, for more than a year. Carried out tasks and experiments that may be done on the red planet. Current \uc2 NASA\uc2  plans aimed to have humans on Mars as soon as the late 2030s. Do you get more than 1,700 square feet? I hope so.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT. House and Senate Democrats meet separately to talk about the future in their party and about President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. in the west.\par \par \uc2 Fair, balanced, and unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240709ek7800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Holds Meeting As Calls Grow To Suspend Campaign; Democrats Assess \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Viability; Panic Inside Democratic Party; Calls For Biden To Step Down; Biden Attacks Trump For Supreme Court Abortion Decision; Hurricane Beryl Hits Jamaica As Category Four Storm; Israel Reportedly Annexing Part Of West Bank For Future Israeli Settlers; Biden Administration Enlisting Help Of Mexico And Central American Countries Is Stemming Flow Of Migrants To U.S.; Some Democrats Calling For Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  To Replace \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  And 2024 Democratic Presidential Nominee\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Robert Ray, Trey Yingst, William La Jeunesse, Brit  Hume, Jacqui Heinrich, Eric Shawn, Marc Thiessen \par \uc2 7,874 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That is so well done.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN POLICY CORRESPONDENT: Do they get salmonella?\par \par \uc2 TOM SHILLUE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Keep it away from your mouth, don't -- you don't want the raw eggs going near your mouth.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY JOEY JONES, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: It's in the U.K., not a Waffle House. No salmonella.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, all right. And by the way, we found out the sea lions and walruses are part of the same family, so that's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 SHILLUE: Happy Fourth.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, Judge and keep your dogs out of Washington, D.C., you never know. All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, President Biden is about to meet with Democratic governors at the White House, a key constituency in his effort to remain on the presidential ticket.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, the president told his campaign staff, no one is pushing him out, and he's in the campaign for the long haul, but more Democrats today and major newspaper editorial boards are calling for President Biden to suspend his campaign, and some lawmakers, albeit Republicans, are even urging the vice president and the cabinet to initiate the 25th Amendment to remove President Biden from power now because of the serious national security threats facing our country, the story is obviously changing by the hour, it has for the past couple of days.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight with what this hour brings live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 President Biden abruptly departed the East Room last hour, not stopping to talk to reporters after giving prepared remarks at a Medal of Honor ceremony. It was one of the fastest exits that we have seen him make.\par \par \uc2 In the room was his son, Hunter, awaiting sentencing on felony of gun conviction, and reportedly one of the voices the president is listening to the most, amid calls for him to drop out and denials that he is considering it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , president OF THE UNITED STATES: Please be seated. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The president himself trying to quiet the tailspin of internal concern he may not be able to salvage his candidacy, reportedly saying on an all staff call, "Let me say this as clearly as I possibly can, as simply and straightforward as I can, I am running. No one is pushing me out. I'm not leaving. I'm in this race to the end, and we're going to win."\par \par \uc2 The vice president also reportedly on the phone after saying this.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is our nominee. We beat Trump once, and we're going to beat him again. Period.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you ready to lead the country if necessary?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I am proud to be \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's running mate.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): A second Democratic lawmaker, Arizona Congressman Raul Grijalva, calling on Biden to step down, saying what he needs to do is shoulder the responsibility for keeping that seat, and part of the responsibility is to get out of this race.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2  editorial board echoing, with the nation's future at stake, this is not a decision that should be made by one family alone. This is a moment when the Democratic Party itself never particularly good at behaving like a party must step into the fray. Democrats increasingly worried about the timeline for a swap.\par \par \uc2 REP. SUMMER LEE (D-PA): It's not going to be time for, you know, a primary. The optics of pushing aside a black woman, they're not good.\par \par \uc2 REP. LLOYD DOGGETT (R-TX): There's no reason we can't have a true, clear, open Democratic process, not making the decisions behind closed doors with a smoke filled room where ultimately the convention decides who our nominee is.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Democratic governors want to evaluate the situation for themselves.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): Want to make sure he's doing OK, and he says he is, and we take him at his word. But it's always good to see somebody in person.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): As details about the president's fight against father time continue to spill out.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Have an afternoon nap every day?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Let me be very clear about this. This is a president that wakes up every morning and puts the American people first. I am not going to speak to sources out there, unnamed sources out there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): 25 House Democrats are reportedly preparing a letter calling for the president to step aside if he has a shaky next few days, and a second letter reportedly demands it.\par \par \uc2 Now, neither letter has been produced so far, with members seriously weighing their next moves.\par \par \uc2 House Democratic leaders were holding a conference call last hour after the president connected with top leaders from both chambers in the last 24 hours. We're waiting what comes out of that and word out of the governor's meeting he is holding in just a little bit here, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): I think we're going to have a mini primary leading in to the convention. And I think that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  would acquit herself very well in that kind of a process, but then it would be fair to everybody.\par \par \uc2 So, all of the other governors who may be interested, and there are some that I would be interested in hearing from as well, because if she were to be the nominee, we need to have a running mate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Representative \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , one of President Biden's staunchest allies from South Carolina, predicting a possible mini primary before the Democratic Convention, adding his name to talking about at least the possibility of replacing President Biden as the nominee.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight, New York Democratic Congressman Dan Goldman, one of President Biden's most vocal supporters. Congressman Goldman, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 Where do you see this tonight as the president gets ready to meet with Democratic governors at the White House?\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN GOLDMAN (D-NY): Well, look, I think this is actually a very healthy democratic process that has not really existed in the Republican Party, and there are lots of discussions.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , as president, has said himself that he had an off night, but he indicated it was an aberration, and the American people want to see that it was an aberration.\par \par \uc2 And the president has scheduled several opportunities over the next several days to get out into the country to have an interview on television, to meet and speak with folks in Wisconsin and Pennsylvania and perhaps elsewhere, to show them that it was an aberration, that that was just simply a really off night.\par \par \uc2 And that he is certainly up to the job, and is far more up to the job than his opponent, who on that same debate stage lied right, left and center, made it very clear that he was putting himself over the country, whereas \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , as we all know, has spent 50 years of his life and his career putting this country first.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, tonight, you would say that you believe President Biden will be your party's nominee?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: As of right now, I absolutely do believe it, and I think he knows that he needs to get out there and to show everyone, not just tell everyone, but show everyone that this debate performance was an anomaly. It was an aberration, and he has committed to doing that. He's out speaking with George Stephanopoulos on Friday, and he will be meeting with a number of people.\par \par \uc2 And what he will be emphasizing, Bret, and I think this is really important, is he will be emphasizing not only his fitness, but the difference between a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  presidency and a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  presidency.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is working for the American people. He is working to lower costs for the middle class. He is working to lower prescription drug prices. He is rebuilding an economy that he inherited from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that was decimated.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , admittedly is on a revenge and retribution tour. He says he wants to be a dictator, and after the Supreme Court case on Monday, Bret, he has a road map to being a dictatorship, because he is immune from criminal liability if he becomes the president. The stakes of this election are so high.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I've seen your comments on that -- yes, I've seen your comments on that, on acts and the president obviously emphasized that essentially, not only running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but also the Supreme Court after that immunity ruling.\par \par \uc2 This is how the White House today explained the debate performance, at least in part, jet lag and a cold. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He arrives back in the United States 12 or 13 days before the debate. So, his explanation for a poor debate performance is jet lag.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So, what I want to say is it's the jet lag and also the cold.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And obviously, he had been back for a number of days, 10 plus, and then had all of that time at Camp David.\par \par \uc2 Everyone had been seeing this for a while. There were accused -- the White House and the campaign accused some outlets of running cheap fakes what they called them out of context, they said videos which were not edited.\par \par \uc2 And now, the American people are seeing this. Now, Congressman, you have editorial boards of major papers that have supported President Biden in the past. You have congressmen stepping out, reportedly, 25 of your colleagues with a letter we have yet to see yet, but reportedly it's coming, and real concern inside the party, at least privately, that this is going to be a drag on the ticket all the way down the line.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Look, I think there's no question \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had a really off night. I personally had never seen him like that. When I've spoken to him in the past, it was nothing like that.\par \par \uc2 So, but I'm not with him every day and the people who are with him every day have indicated as well that it was an aberration and it is an anomaly.\par \par \uc2 And if that is the case, then we will see that, and we will see that over the next week, and he will restore our faith and confidence in his fitness. And then everything else between the two of them cuts in favor of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, people are right to have some concerns after that performance, and I don't think there's any excuse that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  offered. I think he was trying to explain how he had such a poor performance, but he's accepted it, he's owned it. He's acknowledged it, as we all have.\par \par \uc2 And what we need to see from him is that vibrant and whip smart president that we have had for the last 3-1/2 years. One debate does not make a presidency, and we have a long, long record of historic legislation of restoring democracy at home and abroad, of leading us on the international, global stage in defending democracy in Ukraine and in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has been a historically successful president, and people are now starting to see all the benefits of the infrastructure bill, of the semiconductor and CHIPS act of the climate --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you have plenty of time to run on all of that. I'm just asking specifically about President Biden. There's serious concern. You know this in your party, there are some Democrats who believe the White House and campaign has been gaslighting because they've been saying he's as sharp as ever. He's as smart as ever.\par \par \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  said that today, there are concerns now mainly from Republicans, but others who say all of these people calling for him to step aside, they should really be looking at more than this. Here's Congressman Chip Roy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY (R-TX): They want to put a cadre of radical progressive Democrats around the president to advance their radical agenda, using him as a puppet to advance that agenda. The American people want something different. I think the 25th Amendment should be invoked.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, you feel differently.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Yes, look, I -- you can -- you can put up Chip Roy. I'm sure Chip has said more or less the same thing for the last 3-1/2 years.\par \par \uc2 The reality is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has surrounded himself with an incredibly capable team with almost no turnover.\par \par \uc2 On the flip side, 40 of 44 Cabinet members and senior officials in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's administration refused to endorse him. 91 percent including the vice president, do not think that he is fit for office.\par \par \uc2 So, let's not just focus on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  here. Let's focus on the people around him, the administration, the policies and most importantly, the appreciation and protection for the rule of law in our democracy that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , every single day, has vowed to take down.\par \par \uc2 He has said he will be a dictator. He has said that he will tear our democracy down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And I've heard you say that. When the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  says and quotes this anonymous ally saying that the president has, in fact, considered getting out of the race, you just don't believe it?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: No, I didn't say I don't believe it. I think that there -- everybody is talking about how are we going to go forward? We're having a robust conversation within the Democratic Party, within our family. Many of these conversations are private, and where I think everyone, including the president as the leader of the party, comes down is he is going to show the American people that last Thursday night was an aberration.\par \par \uc2 He is going to do it extemporaneously off the cuff without -- unscripted and he is going to get out there and show the American people.\par \par \uc2 And the American people deserve to see that, and I think that the president will do that, recognizes that he has to do that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Congressman Goldman, we really appreciate your time. We, by the way, have put in a request to interview President Biden, if you can put in a good word for us, we'd appreciate it. Thanks again.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: All right, Greg, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOGGETT: I think we have talented governors. I think we have some talented senators. Certainly the vice president would want to be in the mix. The time is short, but there's no reason we can't have a true, clear, open democratic process.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: He has the most historic record administration, the most in modern politics, and that should matter, and he wants to continue to do that work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've heard from Congressman Goldman, there are others Congressman Doggett from Texas, Grijalva now from Arizona, others saying there should be a change, and there should be some kind of change before the convention.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Chief Political Analyst Brit Hume. Brit, you've been digesting this, the interview we just did, all that's happened over the past couple of days, we haven't really seen anything like this in recent memory. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I don't think this has reached critical mass against the president running again, but it's closer than it was even a day or two ago, and it seems to be building.\par \par \uc2 And I think that the politicians in Washington who have gone home for -- or in touch with their constituents are getting a full sense of just what a bad performance that was, and not just a performance. What it said about President Biden is quite simply that he is senile and too old for this job, and he may be able to mount an interview with friendly journalist. George Stephanopoulos is a longtime Democrat. He was \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's top aide. He was at one time, his press secretary. That doesn't mean he's a bad guy. He's a rather nice guy.\par \par \uc2 But I must tell you, that's not the toughest challenge he would face. It was nothing like the debate and going out and talking to people.\par \par \uc2 And so, well, Biden's good at that. You know, he has a certain appealing bonhomie about him. He always has, makes people like him. But the idea that he is whip smart, that's -- look, I've known \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  since he came to the Senate. He's got a lot of experience, but whip smart, he's not.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that's where it is now and you're going to need to watch the next few days. The polling that's coming out is discouraging. And the Wall Street Journal poll will lead to Trump has, he's up to six points now. 80 percent of those polled said that he's too old to be president. So, there you are, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. New York Times Siena seeing the same kind of shift. The campaign, Biden campaign is saying it's not significant after a debate, but it is showing the former president ahead of President Biden by about eight points in that. And internal polls we're hearing Brit, in some states like Virginia, New Mexico, New Hampshire, shifting towards the former president.\par \par \uc2 I want to -- on this private call, this is from Semafor, Democrats weigh what they said a comatose or dead Biden and alternatives.\par \par \uc2 This is Clinton campaign manager, James Carville. "Maybe we look back on this thing and say, 'This is the best thing that ever happened to us.' If this would have happened to us on October 5th, we'd be more than bruised, screwed, and tattooed. Maybe this will set into -- it into motion something different."\par \par \uc2 And you just heard \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , Brit, you know, one of President Biden's staunchest allies suggest that there maybe should be a mini primary before the convention.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, you know, even around the edges of what Dan Goldman was saying there, you could -- you could hear him being increasingly open to a change. You know, he is saying the president is going to go out and he's going to reassure the American public that all is right with him and so on.\par \par \uc2 But he talked about how healthy this is for the party and so on, and he is a hardcore partisan, Dan Goldman is. And a hardcore Biden supporter.\par \par \uc2 So, even in around the edges of his remarks, which were, you know, pretty strongly pro-Biden, you could hear that the ground may be shifting. And I think all these things that you cite, James Carville being a kind of a truth teller, in democratic politics these days, tell you that the sentiment is pretty strong, and the politicians are resistant to it, at least publicly, but privately, I'm sure all of them were afraid, particularly the House members who are up for reelection this year, all of them. All the Democrats in the House, that Biden at the top of the ticket could mean that they lose their jobs. That's the kind of thing he gets the attention.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. It does, indeed, and it's changing and seems by the hour. Brit, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Other political news up next, why some Republicans are upset with former President Trump over his abortion stance ahead of the Republican convention?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stocks were mixed today. The Dow lost 24. The S&P 500 gained 28. The NASDAQ jumped 160. Both of those, again, record closes.\par \par \uc2 We talked about this a lot. The Biden campaign seeking to make abortion a major issue in the election, as the president tries to rebound from last week's debate.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, former President Trump is getting some criticism inside the Republican Party over his latest stance ahead of the \uc2 RNC\uc2  convention. Explain that senior correspondent Eric Shawn.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I believe in the exceptions.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The post- debate focus has been on President Biden. But former President Trump's stance on abortion could spark a fight at the Republican National Convention. Reports say the Trump campaign forced to hardline abortion opponents off the platform committee, prompting concern from evangelicals and social conservatives that the party's anti-abortion platform could be weakened.\par \par \uc2 Trump appeared to offer a moderate stance on the issue.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I believe in the exceptions for rape, incest, and the life of the mother. I think it's very important.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN (voice over): Though Democrats remind, the appointed three of the Supreme Court justices who helped overturn Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's been a terrible thing what you've done. The fact is that the vast majority of constitutional scholars supported Roe when it was decided.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN (voice over): The Trump campaign says final abortion language has not been set. But in a change of tradition, the platform will be finalized behind closed doors before the convention opens later this month.\par \par \uc2 But abortion will likely be on the ballot in Arizona voters there could add it as a right to the state constitution. And Nebraska, where voters could cement a ban after 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Trump's daughter, Ivanka, is speaking out about her father's New York trial and conviction.\par \par \uc2 IVANKA TRUMP, DAUGHTER OF \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : On a human level, it's my father, and I love him very much. So, it's painful to experience. But ultimately, I wish it didn't have to be this way.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN (voice over): The former adviser to the president also says she is staying away from politics.\par \par \uc2 I. TRUMP: It's a pretty dark world. Like there is a lot of darkness a lot of negativity, and it's just really at odds with what feels good for me as a human being. And, you know, it is -- it is a really -- it's a really rough business.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN (on camera): Well, Miss Trump did not attend the trial unlike two of her brothers. And while she has not been on the campaign trail so far, she will attend the convention. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Eric, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll get a live report from Jamaica, as Hurricane Beryl zeros in there.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX5 in New York, as the city expands the program providing migrant families with debit cards to buy food and baby essentials.\par \par \uc2 City council member David Carr, says migrants are living for free at the expense of New York taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 FOX40 in Sacramento, as a wildfire triggers evacuation orders for residents in \uc2 Butte County\uc2 . Authorities say the blaze has burned more than 2100 acres so far. Evacuation and animal shelters have been established. Air tankers from \uc2 Cal Fire\uc2  are dropping fire retardant on the perimeter of that blaze.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Las Vegas from FOX5, our affiliate, as you look at the sphere there. One of the big stories there tonight, the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  approves it proposed private use airport. It would be located about 31 miles from \uc2 Harry Reid International Airport\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The CEO and developer of the project says he plans on filing an application to build a spaceport as well. Construction on the single runway facility is expected to begin in the next six months.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look, "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: FOX weather alert now. Hurricane Beryl pounding Jamaica as category four storm. At least six people have been killed so far as the system rolls through the Caribbean. Robert Ray of FOX Weather is in Jamaica tonight, a precarious spot where you are, Robert. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT RAY, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Good evening, Bret. And everyone watching here from negative gradual jo make can a this is the northwest tip of this island. These are the toughest conditions we've experienced all day long. It was tropical paradise this morning. Blue skies. You wouldn't even know that hurricane Beryl, that category four historic system, was coming ashore.\par \par \uc2 And here we go heavy winds, sideways rain, and storm surge. Kingston to the southeast of where I'm at is taking it in the chin. Up to 80 mile-an-hour wind gusts. Right now, we are 65 to 75 mile-an-hour wind gust. Beryl, which is 45 miles to the south, Bret, of the coastline of Jamaica is rolling at 150 miles per hour with those winds. The counter twist making its way above the high ranges of Jamaica, over 7,000 feet in some spots. And we're still experiencing conditions like this. Even Cuba to the north experiencing it, a monster.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister has told everyone here, nearly 3 million residents, to shelter in place. A curfew is in force tonight, a state of emergency for days ahead. The infrastructure, as we know in Jamaica, is not great. We have experienced power outages already. Some of the roadways are beginning to fall apart, and the structures that are on the shorelines are not made well, and they are breaking apart. Literally thousands of tourists from around the world are hunkering down in these hotels, as we are in for a very long night here, Bret. The system will exit Jamaica at some point in the middle of the night, head to the Cayman Islands, then the Yucatan Peninsula where Cancun is, then on to mainland Mexico, and, unfortunately, up no southern Texas. Bret, hurricane season 2024 is here, and it's already historic. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Robert Ray, be safe out there. Thank you. Hurricane Beryl, we'll watch it.\par \par \uc2 Overseas now. Israel has reportedly annexed additional land in the West Bank. This comes as Palestinians are fleeing their homes in southern Gaza while Israel presses on with a major offensive there. And in the north, exchanges intensify with the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terror group. Correspondent Trey Yingst has the latest tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: More than 100 \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  rockets rained down on northern Israel in the span of an hour. The barrage in response to Israel eliminating a senior \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  commander, one of the highest ranking since the conflict began.\par \par \uc2 YOAV GALLANT, ISRAELI DEFENSE MINISTER (through translator): We are striking \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  very hard every day. And we will also reach a state of full readiness to take any action required in Lebanon or to reach an arrangement from a position of strength.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Israel's Defense Minister Yoav Gallant made those remarks after meeting with soldiers today along the Gaza border. This week Israel urged thousands of Palestinians to evacuate southern Gaza for a humanitarian zone along the Mediterranean Sea, raising new questions about Israel's plan for the day after the war.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANE DUJARRIC\uc2 , UNITED NATIONS SPOKESMAN: People are left with the impossible choice of having to relocate, some most likely for the second or even the third time, to areas that have barely any space or services, or stay in an area where they know the fighting will take place.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Amid questions about land in Gaza, Israel is reportedly seizing new land in the West Bank. A settlement tracking group says around five square miles of territory will be taking over in the Jordan Valley to build thousands of settler homes, a move that further chips away at Palestinian claimed land in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL, FORMER DEFENSE DEPARTMENT CHIEF OF STAFF UNDER TRUMP: It should come as no surprise to you that we view the expansion of settlements as an outpost as inconsistent with international law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): The United States has urged Israel against expanding settlements in the West Bank over tension it causes in the Middle East. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst in Tel Aviv. Trey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's delegation to the third United Nations led Doha meeting on increasing engagement with Afghanistan, discussing with U.S. officials two Americans imprisoned in Afghanistan. The pair believed to have been held by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  for nearly two years. It was the first time the representatives of the Afghan Taliban administration attended the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  sponsored meeting. However, a \uc2 U.N.\uc2  official saying that does not translate into a recognition of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  government.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we take a look of the effects of President Biden's recent executive action to address the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden's latest action on the southern border appears to be having an effect in some areas, a significant change. His critics say it's too little, too late. But it comes as diplomatic efforts to deal with the crisis intensify. National correspondent William La Jeunesse takes a look tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is downright Trumpian.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: In a policy reversal for President Biden, the administration Monday struck a deal with Panama to intercept and deport migrants before they reach the U.S. border.\par \par \uc2 BEATRIZ NICE, WILSON CENTER: What they are hoping is that by the end of the year, in the next few months, migrants will say, you know what? It's just not worth me doing this.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: The intent is to plug the Darien Gap, a dangerous 60-mile stretch of jungle used by migrants on the way to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They have been passing through Panama for years now.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Thousands of Chinese nationals are among the half-million migrants who used the gap last year. Most fly in to South America before walking through the gap, eventually jumping on trains or buses in Mexico. The U.S. is now taking several steps to stop them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Where are you from? China? China?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ecuador.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ecuador.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Ecuador this month began requiring visas for Chinese migrants. Mexico upped troop levels and began policing the rail yards. And this weekend, for the first time in six years, the U.S. deported 116 Chinese nationals back to China.\par \par \uc2 Since the president's crackdown on asylum claims, the number of illegal entries has dropped to its lowest level in three years. However, many of those otherwise illegal migrants are now being admitted through legal channels.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I led the development of the root causes strategy.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: These moves mark a major departure from Biden's attempt to deter migrants by improving economic conditions in their home countries.\par \par \uc2 NICE: The root causes of migration are not enough. What we have been doing is not necessarily having the results that we want. So, yes, let's tighten the grip.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Restricting asylum and enlisting Latin America to help curb illegal immigration is not unlike President Trump asking Guatemala and Mexico to tighten their borders to strengthen ours when Congress refused to cooperate. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's fight to stay on the November ballot, and, for that matter, in the White House.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. A Russian missile and drone attack on eastern Ukraine kills five civilians, injures 47 others. Authorities say blasts blew out some windows of a shopping mall, raining shards of glass onto the street. Debris struck the intensive care unit of a children's hospital Ukraine and fire broke out another hospital.\par \par \uc2 Egypt's new cabinet is sworn in as the country faces an ailing economy and raging conflicts in neighboring nations. The new cabinet includes major changes, particularly in the Egyptian defense and economy related portfolios.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Japan. One of the big stories there tonight, Japan issues its first new bank notes in two decades. The new yen features 3-D hologram technology to fight counterfeiting. Japan's prime minister calls the changes historic.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There's a cold. There's a jetlag. You combine that, he continues to work for the American people day in and day out, around the clock. Things happen.\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": The game is up. They have been exposed. That debate last week exposed all of the untruths and misinformation that they have been pumping out for the last couple years.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL, (D-MI): He has to show that he can do the job. And he has to do that on a sustained basis.\par \par \uc2 I got in trouble for saying one news interview isn't going to do it. It's not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's a lot of talk about what's next for President Biden, whether he will be the nominee of the party and going forward.\par \par \uc2 As you look live outside the White House, the West Wing there, you can see the Marine standing guard. That usually means the president is in the West Wing. We don't know where he's meetings with Democratic governors, which room it's happening. Now we're getting word actually that it is happening in the Roosevelt Room inside the West Wing. That's just off the Oval Office with Democratic governors. We don't have a list yet of which governors are there in person and which are meeting over Zoom.\par \par \uc2 However, we do know the editorial boards around the country, there are now eight editorial boards calling for the president to step down as the nominee, "The New York times," "The New Yorker," "The Economist," "Atlanta Journal-Constitution," "\uc2 Chicago Tribune\uc2 ," "Wall Street Journal," "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," and "\uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 And then there's the shocking story out of "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " today, "Biden told ally that he is weighing whether to continue in the race. He is also continuing to reach out to people he has long trusted and has told at least one person that he is open to the possibility that his plans to move on from his debate performance and flip the focus back to his challenger, Mr. Trump, may not work." A lot of pushback from the White House and the campaign on that today.\par \par \uc2 But let's bring in our panel, Julia Manchester, national politics reporter for "The Hill," "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist, \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  senior fellow Marc Thiessen, and "Axios" political reporter Stef Kight.\par \par \uc2 Marc, your thoughts of where we are in this moment and what comes next?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, well, if this was, as \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  says, he had a cold, he had jetlag, he just had a bad night, I assume that means he's going to go ahead and debate again in September, right? Because that's already scheduled. He would have to pull out of that debate. And I'm sure he'll have a much better night. I think Democrats are really worried about the prospect of putting him out on the debate stage again with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But I'm sure they would love to replace him. But it's not as easy as all that. First of all, there's not a plethora of candidates available. If there was a better candidate, they would be president right now. The only reason he is president is because \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  was about to win the nomination, and everybody else had flamed out. So it's not like they have a deep bench.\par \par \uc2 And then they've got to get past the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . How does the part of identity politics kick the first black woman to the curb in favor of a white male? I don't think that's going to happen. And her poll numbers are even worse than his. He's at 39 percent in the RCP average. She's at 36. So they've got some real hurdles to get over. It's not just as simple as him just stepping down and everything is hunky-dory.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Speaking of polls, a number of them out today, "New York Times"-Siena poll has the race at 49-41 for former President Trump. If you look at independents, 47-37. And then the question, should Biden remain the Democratic nominee, no is at 60 percent. We add those, Stef, to what we're hearing, our internal polls, places like New Mexico, Virginia, New Hampshire, where the former president has now taken over a lead. That would be jarring to a number of people in Congress, and Democrats maybe rallying around those numbers.\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": Yes, the polls are really confirming some of the concerns that we have heard, especially on background from numerous Democratic lawmakers and people close to Biden. At this point we are really hearing concerns throughout the Democratic Party right now, within the White House, within the Biden campaign, among Democratic lawmakers, and even Democratic donors. That's another huge issue that we have been covering and watching, whether we start to see these major Democratic donors move their money elsewhere, again, indicating a lack of faith in Biden moving forward.\par \par \uc2 And again, these polls, maybe they could have been worse, but they are certainly looking like there has been an impact, even if it's small, from the debate performance of President Biden. And right now the White House is acting out loud that they are planning to stick this out, they are planning to keep Biden as the candidate moving forward. But we know the conversations behind closed doors are quite serious and that there is quite real concern.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And Julia, there are more and more stories from anonymous sources that Vice President Harris is doing great things, and a number of positive reflections on Vice President Harris. You heard \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  earlier in the show saying there may be a mini-primary, and he hopes that Vice President Harris would be leading that pack. Here is Representative Summer Lee talking about the vice president as well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. SUMMER LEE (D-PA): If our president decides that this is not the pathway forward for him, then it means that we are going to have to move very quickly. The vice president is the obvious choice because she's sitting right there as somebody who has been in the White House, as somebody who already has the name recognition.\par \par \uc2 And everybody has feelings about these folks. But the reality is the optics of pushing aside a black woman is -- they are not good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Biden campaign just sent out this text to supporters. "Let me be clear, I'm running. I'm the Democratic Party's nominee. No one is pushing me out. I'm not leaving. I'm in this race to the end, and we are going to win this election." But at what point does that all shift? Because there are so many things that have happened over the last day-and-a-half.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER, "THE HILL": Yes, absolutely. I think it happens, or the shift starts to happen when more of these anonymous sources, these lawmakers that are speaking on background or behind closed doors, once they start coming out in public. And we're starting to see that very slowly. Obviously, Raul Grijalva was the second Democratic lawmaker to come out publicly and say this.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that Congressman Clyburn, someone who played such an instrumental role in Biden's primary victory in 2020, in his general election victory, is even talking about the possibility of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the vice president, assuming the top of the ticket is telling in itself, that this is very well a possibility. I think when you hear comments from Congressman Clyburn and Congresswoman Summer Lee, it shows that they are already in a way jockeying for what could be somewhat of a contested fight for the top of that Democratic nomination.\par \par \uc2 We know that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is seen as the heir apparent. She is the vice president. She gets the campaign infrastructure. If she were to take over that fundraising money would go directly to her. However, like Marc said, there are clearly some very clear concerns about her polling, though I will note that a CNN poll out from yesterday showed her running better against Trump than Biden. But still, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  beats her.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I think we have that, 47-45. Still within the margin of error on that CNN poll.\par \par \uc2 But Julia, to your other point, on the campaign cash, to be clear, she would get, if he decided to step aside since he is on the ticket current, she would be the beneficiary of that $200 plus million campaign war chest. But if someone was chosen, they would not. Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: They would not initially. I think there would have to be some sort of a process. Once they were to become the nominee, I think there could be transfers made through super PACs. There are loopholes that I understand. But with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , logistically, it appears on paper easier for her to be that beneficiary.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. To your point, everybody's point here about the focus on Vice President Harris, the National Republican Campaign Committee, Marc, thinks there is some rich territory here. They have already got an ad out. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have been privileged and proud to serve as vice president of the United States with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 We have a fighter, a leader with skill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And it goes on to essentially make the case that if anybody knew there were issues here cognitively with the president, Vice President Harris would be number one.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Absolutely. The big lie that we've heard is that the White House has been telling us that he's fine, and she has been technology he's fine. So she starts out already with her under the water in the polls and having lied to the American people about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's fitness. If he can't be the Democratic nominee and she has to assume the Democratic nomination, then she has to assume the presidency, too. You can't be too mentally unfit to be the Democratic nominee and be mentally fit enough to serve as the commander-in-chief for the next seven months. So I think there's a lot of complications here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, which brings us back, Stef, to Special Counsel Hur who writes that the president in his interview was an elderly man who couldn't remember things -- I'm paraphrasing here -- but wouldn't be worth going after in court because of that fact. Now there's this effort to get the actual audiotapes here. But in retrospect from Thursday's debate, it all takes on a different context.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: This is certainly not the first time that there have been questions around Biden's age and ability to be in this role, ability to be commander- in-chief and fitness for president of the United States. We have seen this in the polls. This was already a concern that many Americans have. And it's one reason why I don't think we have seen a bigger shift in the polls, because some of this was baked in. And many outlets have covered this issue, including "Axios." But there is a sense of this really being a moment where all of that is in front of all of Americans' eyes, and there's really no way for anyone to hide those concerns anymore.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Panel, thanks so much. Just getting a list of governors, Minnesota, Kansas, Arizona, California, Governor Newsom, Colorado, Connecticut, there's a lot of them in person -- Hawaii, Illinois, Kentucky, Maine, Maryland, Massachusetts, Michigan, North Carolina, New Jersey, New Mexico, New York, Oregon, Pennsylvania, Puerto Rico, Rhode Island, U.S. Virgin Islands, and Washington state, all meeting right now with the president and the vice president.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Remember the nation that George and Philip fought for and died for, the United States of America. That's who we are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Earlier today President Biden posthumously awarded the Medal of Honor to two Union soldiers who hijacked a train deep in Confederate territory during the Civil War. Philip Shadrach and George Wilson drove that locomotive north 87 miles, got that award, the family did, today. Quite something.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. We have got you covered. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid.\par \par \uc2 THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is next with all kinds of breaking news.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240704ek7300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Biden Campaign Questions Whether Trump Will Show Up For Second Debate on 9/10; Sen. Vance on Trump's Plans for U.S. Role in \uc2 NATO\uc2 ; Supreme Court Curbs Authority of Federal Agencies; Examining the Supreme Court's Big Decisions as the Term Wraps Up; Supreme Court Ruling Makes It Harder to Charge Jan. 6 Riot Defendants with Obstruction; Mayorkas Disputes Reports That ISIS-Connected Group Smuggled Hundreds Of People Across Border; Bide: We're Providing Israel With All The Weapons They Need; Interview With Sen. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  (D-PA); Interview With Sen. JD Vance (R-OH)\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Jonathan Turley, Lucas Tomlinson, Mary Katharine  Ham, Richard Fowler \par \uc2 10,478 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Three days after the first debate, President Biden fights back against growing calls for him to leave the ticket, as viral moments like this light up social media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And I'm going to continue to move until we get the total ban on the -- the total initiative relative to what we can do with more Border Patrol and more asylum officers.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, DEBATE MODERATOR: President Trump?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I really don't know what he said at the end of that sentence. I don't think he knows what he said either.\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES\uc2 , CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: That was painful. I love \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I worked for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He didn't do well at all.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): President Biden is in full damage control mode amid growing calls within his party for him to step down.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't debate as well as I used to, but I know what I do know, I know how to tell the truth!\par \par \uc2 BREAM: While President Trump tries to ride the wave of momentum.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The question every voter should be asking themselves today is not whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can survive a 90-minute debate performance, but whether America can survive four more years of crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll get reaction from Pennsylvania Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , a staunch defender of President Biden, and Ohio Republican Senator JD Vance, who is on President Trump's VP short list.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: The court agrees that a number of the defendants in the January 6 proceedings have been overcharged.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2  (R-MS): We are a good country. We're a nation of laws and somewhere, there's a law that applies to people who break into the United States Capitol.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Major decisions from the Supreme Court this week on January convictions and limiting the power of the federal bureaucracy, with a decision on presidential immunity slated for tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Our legal panel is here to take a closer look at the contentious cases.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and the first lady are at Camp David today with their children and grandchildren after attending fundraisers at New York's wealthy enclave in the Hamptons. That follows calls from major newspapers from across the country for him to leave the race after his debate performance.\par \par \uc2 In a front page editorial, "The Atlanta Journal's" board writing that Biden should withdraw from the race for the good of the nation. It's all happening as former President Trump awaits the Supreme Court's ruling on presidential immunity tomorrow, and his sentencing in New York the following week for his hush money felony convictions.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll get reaction to all of the latest headlines from Biden supporter Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Trump supporter Senator JD Vance, but first, we begin with Lucas Tomlinson at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 That's right. President Biden is huddling with his family at Camp David to answer the question many are asking, should he drop out of the race?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Mr. President, did you alleviate donor concerns today?\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Biden ignored the question as he left the Hamptons, but with this banner seen flying overhead, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appeared to have an answer.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD CHANTING "FOUR MORE YEARS!")\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: After the debate, Biden vowed to press on.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I know like millions of Americans know, when you get knocked down, you get back up!\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: With pressure mounting on Biden, his opponent appeared to be taking it easy. Trump played a round of golf at his club outside the nation's capital after posting, quote: His speech on Friday was better and he seems to be coming out of his trance, but America must ask itself, with all the many dangers around, do we really want a president who chokes? I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 At a rally in battleground Virginia, Friday, Trump offered his assessment of the debate.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: He studied so hard that he didn't know what the hell he was doing.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has always had our back, and we're going to have his. So we're still riding with Biden.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Not all Democrats agree.\par \par \uc2 DON PEEBLES, FORMER OBAMA FUNDRAISER: Biden should be an elder statesman. He promised us all and -- that he would be a transitional president. He broke his word.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Would you close this door, please?\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Biden was forced to drop out of his first presidential run nearly 40 years ago, after he was accused of plagiarism.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And although it's awfully clear to me what choice I have to make, I have to tell you honestly, I do it with incredible reluctance and it makes me angry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): The Biden campaign says it's raised over $33 million since the debate. So far, there's been no calls from Democratic lawmakers for the president to drop out of the race -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Lucas Tomlinson, at the White House -- Lucas, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Pennsylvania Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Hey, it's great to be here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. You know what it's like to have a tough debate. You posted about this and said for people --\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- who were freaking out, you said, chill the blank out.\par \par \uc2 But that's -- see, you know, the worries have only grown. You heard Lucas's reporting. We now have "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ", "The Atlanta Journal Constitution", a number of pundits on the left saying it's got to be done for the good of the country, for the president to step away.\par \par \uc2 Have you changed your position at all?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Oh, no, oh, no. It's -- it was the -- I mean, I don't put any kinds of -- there's no value in any of those things.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there was that same kind of a freak out after my debate. And in fact, I might even say that I had a more difficult evening than the president did, and here I am right now having this conversation and I really like to remind everybody watching that right now, Biden is one and Trump is still zero, and he's the only person that's ever beaten Trump.\par \par \uc2 And I really believe that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will do that again, despite all of the Democrats wetting the bed over that kind of thing. I'm -- I'm right here there (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Senator, your position obviously is that we've been able to watch and see this incredible recovery for you. You've -- you know, gotten consistently better and better, but a lot of folks will point to President Biden and say, if this is a matter of age, it's -- if it's a matter of ability, that's not going to reverse. So, different than your situation?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Well, no, it's, of course, we both had -- we had a difficult debate and yet we still managed to go on to win. Now, everybody was calling that -- that was the end of my career, that was the end of this race and everyone was predicting that I was going to lose actually by two points or more. And I smoked Oz by five points right now.\par \par \uc2 And that's what I'm saying. It -- it's like one debate is not a career any more than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's 38 convictions for felonies aren't really going to define, you know, his reputation and his presidency. And that's why they're both here again.\par \par \uc2 And it's that same year -- year conversation, and I said this in 2016 that it's going to be very close. I've said this in 2020, and I'm saying it consistently through this race now. It's going to be very close.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And just for clarity, 34, and he is awaiting sentencing. We'll be covering that on July 11th, ahead of the convention.\par \par \uc2 You talk about the president being able to win, but is that the only measure here? Do you have any worries that he can actually govern if he does win, for four more years?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I don't because I've watched him for the last four years and he's done incredibly good things. I mean, he got us through that pandemic - - that pandemic that over a million Americans cost their lives through that.\par \par \uc2 And look at our economy. Ours is the world's envy economic kinds of power. And look at the stock market, look at all these other kinds of metrics. People were calling that it was going to be a recession and here we are now booming on all of those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 So the president has done a really good job and he deserves a second term. But I really do want to be clear: it's going to be very close. I've said this before and it's going to -- that's not going to change.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, in our latest polling that we did in Pennsylvania specifically because it is such a bellwether important state for any candidate who wants to try to get to the White House showed it was a tie. Our last polling had it at even for both of these men head-to-head. There's a slightly different look when you do bring in third party candidates.\par \par \uc2 But you mentioned the economy and so, I want to bring this up. In Pennsylvania, when asked who would do a better job on foreign policy on the economy on immigration and the border, they're specifically in Pennsylvania, they gave wide margins to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So how do you see that playing out in your critical state?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I see polls, the only poll that's really going to matter is election day and the polls changed and they inverted in my race as well and here I am as United States senator. And I just got back from Israel just a couple days ago, and that's why I want to remind everybody that one debate does not define a person's record or the kind of person or the situation. It's certainly not about an election that is four and a half months ago (ph).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. Let's talk about your trip to Israel. You met with a lot of top leaders across the political spectrum there, but I want to start with Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 . You spent time together. He had great praise for your unwavering support of Israel.\par \par \uc2 I wonder what your assessment is, if you talked about his perception of support from the Biden administration, clearly there have been very public tensions and some differences, what was your conversation with him on that point about?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Well, it was an honor to spend time with him as well, and he's the democratic elected leader of our key, key ally.\par \par \uc2 And I had the opportunity to literally meet everybody in leadership. I've met \uc2 IDF\uc2  soldiers. I've met university students, again across the spectrum. And I really wanted to really make sure everybody -- we stand with Israel throughout all of this.\par \par \uc2 And I really I've -- I've disagreed with the president through that and I've shared that. And that's why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- well, yeah, there's the -- there's the pictures.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I've had the opportunity to meet, you know, everybody, and remind everybody that we need to stand with Israel throughout all of this -- this, and it's going to get more and more difficult. But we also want to make sure we bring back all of the hostages (ph) and I can't possibly support any kind of a deal unless -- for peace, as long as \uc2 Hamas\uc2  -- can't be in power on any of that. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  needs to be effectively neutralized before there's any kinds of peace.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And there's a leftwing of your party, more progressive, that differs with you on that. They're asking for totally different things and pointing the blame at different places. They've been very vocal.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried about how that might impact President Biden's ability to shore up the Democrats and to win reelection knowing those other voices are calling -- some of them, you know, they've got a movement called Abandon Biden. They've supported him in the past and now they're actively campaigning against him.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: And that that whole abandoned Biden thing, it's the -- that's the dumbest shit I've ever heard. I mean, if you are more inclined to vote for a Democrat or be a Democrat, if you're willing to walk away from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , you're by defecting helping Trump, and that's really it is.\par \par \uc2 And if any of that kind of a movement has that kinds of move, then -- and they want to play with that kind of fire, they better earn that burn. And now, especially because people now have turned their backs on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , now it's going to mean, sure, that everyone, it's going to be an all hands on the deck.\par \par \uc2 And that's -- can continue to talk about this is a very stark choice, what kind of president do you want for the next four years, what do you want to talk about -- the things that these men are obsessed with. One is rebuilding America and taking us in a better direction or a republic -- I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who is obsessed with revenge, and has been very clear about he is here to be your retribution.\par \par \uc2 And I don't believe that the president's office should never be weaponized against the people in this America that just happened to disagree with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we know that you are in the middle of a lot of diverse, divisive, heated conversation.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for making time for us. Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Well, thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, joining me now, Ohio Republican Senator JD Vance.\par \par \uc2 All right. Senator, you heard your colleague there saying that President Trump is about division and he's about revenge. Your response?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JD VANCE (R-OH): Well, if you actually watch the performance of President Trump in the debate that's not what it's all -- that's not what it's about at all, Shannon. It's about getting the country back on track, bringing prosperity and peace back to the world stage, and actually having a leader who can sit in a room with other world leaders and do the job as president.\par \par \uc2 If you look, Shannon,