 likable, and you've got a new opponent to get ready for?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: You know, Shannon, I actually don't care if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the Democratic nominee because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the Republican nominee. And the contrast between Republican policies under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and whoever the Democrats ultimately end up with is very powerful.\par \par \uc2 What I do think, Shannon, is it would be an incredible insult to Democratic voters to have this primary process that was effectively rigged in favor of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And then at the last minute, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has a disastrous debate performance, try to switch him out. But who are they going to switch him out with? And how can they possibly offer a good contrast to the guy who brought prosperity to his own country and peace on the world stage?\par \par \uc2 The problem is not that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had a bad debate. The problem, Shannon, is that Democratic leadership over the last three and a half years has led to a war in Europe, wars in Africa, a war in the Middle East and a potential war in East Asia, not to mention sky high inflation that every single one of the possible Democrats who could run for president, they all supported the policies that led to that inflation.\par \par \uc2 So I'm really not worried about that -- their party. I'm focused on our own party. And I think that we have the best candidate to make the case that if you elect us, if you give \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a chance at the presidency, he is going to bring back that peace and prosperity. That's all we want is an America that's back on track. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  delivered it once. He's going to deliver it again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's talk foreign policy, because you talk about that and how important it is to any commander-in-chief. President Biden said at the debate that President Trump would withdraw from \uc2 NATO\uc2 . He says that he would also tell \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He says he has told him, "Just do what you want." That's the quote when it comes to attacking \uc2 NATO\uc2  members. Despite Article Five obligations, what is President Trump's position on staying in \uc2 NATO\uc2  in coming to the defense as agreed under Article Five, should someone be attacked?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, Shannon, first of all, the Democrats' argument here is so inconsistent with reality. Russia took territory under the Bush administration. It took territory under the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration. It took territory under the Biden administration. It took zero territory under the Trump administration because \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  actually respected \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he hasn't respected any of these other clowns. That's the most important thing when it comes to Putin's aggression in Europe. And the Democrats' arguments are absurd.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the question of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , what Trump has said very clearly is that Europe has to step up. America cannot be the policeman of the world. We want \uc2 NATO\uc2  to be a strong alliance. We don't want \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries to be welfare clients of the United States.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is a significant threat to Europe, then the Germans have to step up. The French have to step up. They need to do more for their own security. And I think that argument actually lands not just with a lot of Americans, but with a lot of Europeans. They are not our clients. We want \uc2 NATO\uc2  to be a strong alliance. But for that to be the case, the Europeans have got to step up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But is there any scenario in which there's an attack on a \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally and we don't show up?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Look, Shannon, we're going to honor our treaty obligations, and I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been very clear about that. But what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has also been very clear about is that the Europeans have to take a bigger role in their own security.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, Shannon, that's already had an effect. The Europeans have already stepped up substantially compared to the Trump administration because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  effectively applied pressure to them. And I think if you see another Trump administration, which obviously I'm working hard to make sure happens, we're just going to see more and more Europeans taking a bigger role because they respect Trump's leadership.\par \par \uc2 And they also recognize Trump is saying very clearly, you guys have got to carry your fair share here. It's fair to America. It's better for Europe. It's better for the whole world for the Europeans to step up. That is the Republican position.\par \par \uc2 And it is different from the Democrats' position where they've effectively told the Europeans, American taxpayers will float your security indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 America has other things to focus on. China, our own domestic problems. Europe's got to step up. And that's a very important distinction between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we're going to go. But Senator, any announcements you'd like to make before you go?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Not for me, Shannon. As you know, I've said I want to support the president however I can. He's winning this race. The debate was just the latest big, big moment for the campaign to make \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the president. I'm going to keep on working however I can and in whatever way I can and as hard as I can to make sure it happens, because that's what's in the best interest of our country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We'll see you out there on the trail. Senator, thank you.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's been a blockbuster week of historical rulings from the Supreme Court. The final decision day is tomorrow. We will finally learn where the justices stand on presidential immunity.\par \par \uc2 Our legal panel takes a look at the contentious decisions we got this week and that landmark opinion about to make history.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It has been a heated week at the Supreme Court with the justices divided on decisions about everything from abortion to the power of federal regulations. But it all comes down to tomorrow when the court will release its opinion on presidential immunity, which could have a direct impact on the federal criminal cases now pending against President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Let's break it down with our legal panel, Fox News Contributor and George Washington University Law Professor Jonathan Turley, author of the new book, "The Indispensable Right: Free Speech in an Age of Rage."\par \par \uc2 Also on our panel, Constitutional Accountability Center President Elizabeth Wydra. And Judicial Crisis Network President Carrie Severino, also clerked for Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you. We thought we might be sort of celebrating the end of the term by now, but we're going into overtime.\par \par \uc2 So let's talk about a little bit about what we got this week, this idea of federal regulation and how far regulators can go. It came to the court in the context of these fisheries that said, if you're going to put monitors on our boats and make us pay for them, that's just too much regulation.\par \par \uc2 Here is Meghan Lapp from one of the fisheries. They won their case.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN LAPP, SEAFREEZE FISHERIES: I'm ecstatic. We finally have a level playing field. American citizens finally have the same rights in court as the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . And that's huge. And that's going to make a huge difference in our industry and in every, every industry going forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Jonathan, kind of wonky. But every industry this -- this does have major impact.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: This is a huge impact on citizens across the country. We live in the so-called age of regulation. And this does level the playing field.\par \par \uc2 And this came after the court ruled that citizens deserve a jury trial when they are facing fines. And so this really changes everything. \uc2 Chevron\uc2  is as dead as Dillinger now.\par \par \uc2 And it means that judges will go back to doing what judges do and interpret whether this is a reasonable approach to of an agency to this law.\par \par \uc2 But it's going to have a huge impact. There are going to be more lawsuits. And more importantly, citizens are going to have a real chance now to say, I think that this interpretation is just simply wrong. And courts will have to look at that freshly.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They will. OK, so Justice Kagan, writing for the dissenter, said this. "In every sphere of current or future federal regulation, expect courts from now on to play a commanding role. It is not a role Congress has given to them." Carrie?\par \par \uc2 CARRIE SEVERINO, JUDICIAL CRISIS NETWORK PRESIDENT: Yeah, actually, not at all. What it just says is the courts, the real experts in interpreting the law, are going to go back to interpreting the laws. So when it comes to how to enact their regulations, the regulators can still do that. That's their expertise.\par \par \uc2 But when the question is, what does this law allow you to do? That's the court. We don't get to let the regulators get to say, oh, yeah, actually, this law that your representatives passed gives us way more power than the -- than -- than the words say. The courts get to read the laws and then -- then the regulators can -- can do their job within the realm that was given to them by Congress. They can't go outside that now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Elizabeth, a lot of this gets back to Congress. If you pass laws that are ambiguous and say, we'll just let the regulatory state take care of it, how much work does it now mean? More work that maybe they got to do on Capitol Hill?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WYDRA, CONSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY CENTER PRESIDENT: Well, yeah, exactly. That, you know, the point was that, of course, if Congress enacted a law, the agency was acting directly contrary to that clear congressional enactment. That has always been impermissible.\par \par \uc2 But Congress writes legislation that has to cover a lot of different things for a long period of time. Like the environment is a perfect example. And so they write broadly worded statutes at times that are filled with gaps and ambiguities that have been filled by experts in the agencies which are politically accountable because they are appointed by the executive, the elected executive, the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And so it's a massive shift of power from these politically accountable actors to the court. And it affects Americans on everything from the air we breathe, the food we eat, the safety of our workplaces, civil rights, health care, you name it. This affects every aspect of Americans lives.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I agree. More lawsuits. Well, we're all lawyers, so I know that's a good or bad thing for our industry. But more lawsuits are coming.\par \par \uc2 I want to touch on the Fischer case, too. This is the January 6th defendant who said this obstruction statute that was applied to me. It came in the wake of \uc2 Enron\uc2 . It's meant for financial crimes. It wasn't rightly applied to those who showed up at the Capitol on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 The court said, Carrie, that there wasn't a proper application. It was flawed. And this could potentially impact hundreds of cases, including President Trump.\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: Yeah, this fits squarely within the court's precedent. On things like this, they said, if you've got a list of a type of thing and then you say and otherwise do that, it has to mean along the same lines. They said it's like if you had a football regulation that said you can't pull or twist their helmets or otherwise harm them. It doesn't cover trash talking or stabbing them or something. It's talking about things you do to their equipment, you know.\par \par \uc2 So in this case, they're saying the Sarbanes-Oxley Act, which is about in this whole section, is about destroying evidence and blocking evidence and things doesn't have to do with, broadly speaking, anything you could do to interfere with the proceeding. It just has to be something to do with evidence.\par \par \uc2 It's possible that will cover some of the January 6th defendants. We don't know. But the government can't use that to just --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Shoehorn in.\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: -- shoehorn in a million other things. And I think that's what it said in other contexts as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've got a disagreement, Elizabeth?\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: Well, you know, it was an interesting lineup. We had Justice Barrett in dissent with two of her more liberal colleagues. And she said, look, the text is the text.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, especially these textualists and originalist judges should follow the text where it goes. And yes, it's broad, but clearly Congress intended it to be broad. And when you corruptly obstruct an official proceeding, as is alleged in the January 6th indictments, then that applies.\par \par \uc2 But also, Justice Jackson, again, this interesting lineup in her concurrence with majority said, you know, because the official proceeding we're talking about on January 6th has to do with the certification of the election, that does have records and documents. So even under the majority's view of the law, these January 6th defendants charges could still go forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let's talk immunity is the big one. It's coming tomorrow. We all heard the arguments. We've read the briefs and now it comes down to decision day. Professor?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yeah, well, if you follow the oral arguments, the expectation many of us had was that the court was clearly uncomfortable with the extreme views of either the Trump team or the lower court, because the Trump team was saying he is sweeping virtually absolute immunity. And then the lower courts gave very little protection for presidents going forward. They were bothered by both of those.\par \par \uc2 And one of the justices said, look, we're writing for the ages here. They have not clearly established where this line has been. So everyone's trying to do this bums rush on the court saying, where's the opinion? Where's the opinion?\par \par \uc2 This line is critical with -- in determining where Article Two begins and ends on some of these issues.\par \par \uc2 And so the expectation is they may come up with a nuanced view and send this back. Now that's important because if they send it back, it may be for the trial court determine, what here was part of an official function? That will take time and the clock is ticking. And it's -- many of us doubt that the court can do that and still have a trial before the election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Quick comments from both of you. Where do you think we get?\par \par \uc2 WYDRA: I mean, I think that -- I think the timing is really key here. You know, Trump might lose on the merits, but because of the delay in issuing the opinion essentially be handed a win by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: Yes. I think exactly as Jonathan said, this is -- they're going to come somewhere between the absolute immunity for anything and no immunity for anything.\par \par \uc2 And that's actually kind of where the Trump team moderated its position to by the time it got to the Supreme Court as well. So maybe personal acts don't get immunity for official acts you do.\par \par \uc2 And that's frankly good news for President Biden, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  -- President Biden, President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , other presidents as well to know that, you know, I'm not going to get sued for every single decision I make as president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's going to take us a minute, I think today just when we get it tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Jonathan, I want to talk about your book a little bit because you say this. And I think it's so relevant to where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 You say, we have to ask difficult questions about the limits of our tolerance for the speech of others including those we find hateful and or harmful. How do we do that?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, it's interesting because, you know, in 1800, we had an election where \uc2 Thomas Jefferson\uc2  ran against \uc2 John Adams\uc2  on free speech, because \uc2 John Adams\uc2  had prosecuted his political opponents.\par \par \uc2 Some of the opponents of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  are clearly ready to do that now to have a possible repeat. Adams lost that election, in part, because of his crackdown on free speech.\par \par \uc2 Many of us view \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as the most anti-free speech president since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  because of this elaborate censorship system that we have today.\par \par \uc2 And it will be interesting how history repeats itself and whether this will become an issue for citizens.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. The book is "The Indispensable Right: Free Speech in an Age of Rage."\par \par \uc2 OK. Thank you all. See you tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 Up next, just weeks after an executive order that will allow an estimated half a million people in the country now illegally to stay here, the Biden administration is reportedly about to announce yet another move impacting hundreds of thousands more.\par \par \uc2 We'll get reaction from the Chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee, Michael McCaul, live, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No one thinks we're weak. No one wants to screw around with us. Nobody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 