ork, Philadelphia, and L.A.\par \par \uc2 When I chaired Homeland, this is my worst nightmare. My worst fear is to have the southern border exploited, terrorists getting into the country.\par \par \uc2 We never saw that. We saw -- no unexpected terrorist detained, but never get in.\par \par \uc2 These individuals got in and they were not here on a goodwill mission. They were here to do us harm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Well, knowing that, knowing the danger, \uc2 FBI\uc2  director Wray has talked about this, all the lights flashing, leading up to a 9/11 again, potentially.\par \par \uc2 The president said in the debate the other night, he needs more money for border agents for, you know, getting the enormous backlog at the immigration courts undone.\par \par \uc2 Why not meet him somewhere, halfway on some kind of compromise just to get that funding to where it needs to go when we have this level of threat?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Because throwing money at this problem aren't going to fix it. It's a policy change that needs to be effectuated.\par \par \uc2 And that's the Remain-in-Mexico policy. I marked it up out of my committee. It worked very well in the -- in the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 And on day one, he rescinded this policy. It means that your political asylum claims had to be adjudicated outside the United States.\par \par \uc2 So when you're not released into the United States, that's culminated in about 10 million people known rapists.\par \par \uc2 You know, Mayorkas said, hey, you don't have to detain aggravated felons. Those are rapists, child predators, you know, murders. This is a reckless policy.\par \par \uc2 So when you have a weak foreign policy after Afghanistan, and they got a wide open back door into the United States, and they know it, they're going to exploit it. And now we have evidence that they have.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about some other foreign policy hotspots. Israel. We talked to Senator Fetterman just back there from meeting with folks who are very worded.\par \par \uc2 And I had -- I have a friend who's visiting in town from Israel this week, had lunch with them, very worried about the \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  threat to the north.\par \par \uc2 He says, people in Israel know that that is substantially much worse for them than what's happening in Gaza now.\par \par \uc2 I thought it's interesting that the chairman of the Joint Chiefs, General C.Q. Brown said, according to the "AP," that the U.S. won't likely be able to help Israel defend itself against a broader \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  war as it was able to help Israel fight off the Iranian barrage of missiles and drones in April.\par \par \uc2 Are we able to assist Israel if that turns into a whole other front for them with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , which is much better equipped and armed? And will we?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, yes, we can. We have the capacity. We did so when Iran launched their rockets into Israel.\par \par \uc2 Remember, that 99 percent were taken out. They were not effective.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But what about General Brown though saying, he doesn't think we can give that same level of help if \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  starts?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, I respect him. General Brown, I know him.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is, we're not helping them. In fact, this is what is the most disturbing to me is that we're withholding weapons systems that I have signed off on in Congress as appropriate with the intent of sending those weapons to Israel.\par \par \uc2 Remember the supplemental?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: They are effectively withholding seven weapons systems. I can't get into the details. That is not helping Israel.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, same thing in Ukraine. You know, I wrote in, must provide ATACMS to Ukraine to use against the Russians. They said, OK. We'll change the policy you can hit inside, but then they restrict them from using ATACMS.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, in fact, we've got 