ng time. And those are the only people who are really going to persuade \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to take this step.\par \par \uc2 I don't think he's going to take that step. There are no indications he will. But if he's going to, it's going to be in a setting like this where it's with the people he trusted, his family members. We know how much his family means to him. And those are really the only ones who are going to have a say in this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll see if we get any readout from that Camp David get-together.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, don't go far.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to focus on two key issues voters say will impact their decisions and who they think is currently better equipped to handle those issues, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The combination of what was (ph) left with incorporate (ph) greed is the reason why we're in this problem right now.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We had given them back a - a county where the stock market actually was higher than pre-Covid, and nobody thought that was even possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, President Biden and former President Trump debating the economy. It's at the top of nearly every poll when voters are asked, what do you care most about.\par \par \uc2 So, let's talk about that with our panel.\par \par \uc2 OK, so, coming on the heels of some polling that is not good for the White House, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " polling has Trump up 48-44. \uc2 Quinnipiac\uc2  has him up also 49-45. But they dug down in that "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll also to ask about the economy. And they say this, "inflation and the economy continue to be the top issues for voters and they overwhelmingly thought Mr. Trump would better handle the crucial economic concerns."\par \par \uc2 Richard, that's - where does 