he last couple of weeks, Nevada, Georgia, yesterday Arizona, it is the economy and it is 12 million illegal aliens. Those two issues are very connected to each other that are driving the center right motivation. And I think ultimately that may offset the Democrats' attempt to use abortion to drive their turnout.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How worried, Stef, is the White House, is the campaign, about that, know if those two issues are the big ones, and people in every poll give the edge to President Trump on that, and given the debate performance the other night, can they overcome those things?\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: I mean the Biden campaign is very concerned about both of those issues. And we've seen the White House try to find better messaging, try to prove to the American people that their economic policies are working. And they try to focus on the job numbers, which have been better for them. But really, when it comes to the economy, it's inflation that people are most concerned about, paying more than they feel like they did. Maybe it's not always exactly true, but more than they feel like they did in the past.\par \par \uc2 And also, you know, the border is and other issues that the White House has struggled with from day one. And we see Biden changing his tactic on that issue as well. Deciding to move forward with a very harsh restriction at the border. Taking essentially the exact same legal means that Trump used to - to shut down the border to deciding to adopt that for himself in his own kind of program to reject asylum-seekers. So, all that indicates that they know these are serious problems they need to get right before November.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and as we've talked about abortion, clearly a big issue. And it has worked really well for Democrats in most of these special elections they've had.\par \par \uc2 I want to put up a map that we put together about where there is an abortion issue on the ballot. In green, they're there. And that - that includes, you know, critical states like Nevada, swing states. They're also - the blue states, signatures, they've gotten enough there. They're trying to get officially get on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Efforts underway in the yellow states and the orange states. But, Mary Katherine, it only takes a couple of states with something that really turns out one side or the other that could flip this whole thing.\par \par \uc2 HAM: A couple states, not that many voters, and a very specific demo that this reaches out to. So that can be really important.\par \par \uc2 This goes back to the debate, though, and this performance and why it was important, depending on how long this hangover lasts.\par \par \uc2 Biden had a moment where he was garbled and unclear about his number one issue. Trump, on the other hand, surprisingly to me, hit a couple of important points, the liberalization of abortion rules in various states post Roe - post Dobbs. He talked about exceptions that he supported. He looked like the moderate that he should look like to appeal to this and to quell some of those concerns. Not exactly a simple pitch, like his economy pitch, which his very simple and appealing to voters. But he sort of managed that. And it might make a difference moving forward.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we have a lot of those states.\par \par \uc2 OK, before we go, just a few seconds left. Who thinks that President Biden stays on the ticket?\par \par \uc2 HAM: I do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You do?\par \par \uc2 Kevin, you think he doesn't, possibly?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I've been saying for a year that he won't. In fact, I first said that on this show.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I don't exist to say I told you so, I lament this, but I don't think it's going to.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll see.\par \par \uc2 OK, critical times, critical meeting at Camp David again this weekend with family. We'll see if anything comes of that.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream here in Washington. Tomorrow morning we'll see you at the Supreme Court for that immunity decision. It is the last of the term and one that could last for decades and generations moving forward.\par \par \uc2 Have a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020240630ek6u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Fallout Over Biden Debate Performance Against Trump; Biden And Trump Make Misleading Claims; Trump Chooses Virginia For First Post--Debate Rally; Trump Supporters Rally In Virginia; Court Weakens Federal Agency Regulatory Authority; Lawmakers Grappling With \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Debate Performance; Potential Replacements If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Quits Race; President Biden's And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Points About Foreign Policy Made During Debate Examined; "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " Editorial Board Calls For President Biden To Leave Presidential Race; Panelists Discuss Possibilities For Democratic Party If President Does Not Seek Reelection; Possible Candidates For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Vice President Reviewed\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Lucas Tomlinson, Shannon Bream, Chad  Pergram, Gillian Turner, Mark Penn, Kimberley Strassel, Trey Gowdy \par \uc2 7,684 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Apparently, he said all the Democrats are really mad. That's who I saw in there.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, they did know the good -- the very bad night.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, it is --\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Happy birthday to your mother.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Happy birthday to my mom. Let's do that one more time because we have ten seconds.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Was she a teen mom? Because she looks so young.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, wow. She -- now you really charmed her.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us. Have a great night, everyone.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Bye!\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Happy Birthday, Dana's mom.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Great to be with you today.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: See you, teen mom.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT" we are covering two major stories. Reaction to a major \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  ruling today with far reaching consequences. But we begin with major damage control from Democrats and the Biden campaign, following last night's performance by President Biden in his debate with former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 The current president, stumbling, losing his train of thought, freezing up at numerous times during the event. Many Democrats said to be in a state of panic. There reportedly is significant talk about trying to replace the president on the November ballot. Whether he is capable to serve another four years or four months, President Biden, however, was given a lifeline from two previous Democratic presidents by mid-afternoon with posts of support for him.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump and President Biden were both back out on the campaign trail today. The current president in Raleigh, North Carolina, where he did his own fact check of his predecessor, but admitted, he does not debate as well as he used to.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 A campaign official is telling us that President Biden will debate Trump again in September, that none of the people involved in prepping President Biden for last night's -- last night's debate will be fired.\par \par \uc2 And that there are no discussions about President Biden dropping out whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don't walk as easy as I used to. I don't speak as smoothly as I used to. I don't debate as well as I used to.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): That is now widely understood to be true.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Everything we have to do with -- look, if we finally beat Medicare.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Today, an attempt to redirect.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But I know what I do know. I know how to tell the truth.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): He didn't, with this mischaracterization of the Abbey Gate bombing, which killed 13 U.S. service members.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The ruth is, I'm the only president this century that doesn't have any -- this decade that done any troops dying anywhere in the world, like he did.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): A fact disputed by the defense department.\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SINGH, DEPUTY PENTAGON SPOKESPERSON: We have -- certainly, had service members pass during our -- during this administration.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Democrats today want the focus to be on what Trump said.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told lie, after lie, after lie.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The current president says this was the biggest one about January 6th.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: And as \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said, it was her responsibility, not mine. She said that loud and clear.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  writes, "Bad debate nights happen. Trust me, I know." And President Biden explained during a midnight waffle house run, why he thinks debating Trump is so hard.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's really hard to debate a liar. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  pointed out, he did lied 26 times. Big lies.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): But \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , also posted op-eds with headlines, "Biden cannot go on like this." "\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a good man and a good president." "He must bow out of the race."\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  could win this election. Let her."\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It was a slow start. That's obvious to everyone. I'm not going to debate that point.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): On teleprompter today, the president was sharper, and testing out new lines as he tries to change the subject.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I never said big lies.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  isn't just a convicted felon. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a one man crime wave.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Despite last night's struggles, President Biden is keeping his schedule. So, ahead of some Hamptons fundraisers this weekend, the president just appeared at an event with Elton John, as if to say, I'm still standing. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Very nicely done. Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump in Chesapeake, Virginia today for a rally that ended just a short time ago. Lucas Tomlinson is in \uc2 Chesapeake\uc2  tonight has the latest. Good evening, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Fresh off the debate last night former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  arrived here in Virginia to fire up a large crowd and talk about his assessment last night of President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Despite the fact that crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  spent the entire week at Camp David resting, working, studying, he studied very hard, he studied so hard that he didn't know what the hell he was doing.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): Now, beginning at 7:00 this morning, hundreds of cars lined up for miles to get in here. Law enforcement officials say 10,000 people attended the rally today. We spoke to some of them earlier.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If they need any more proof that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not fit to run the country, that's it right there.\par \par \uc2 KARL KUNDRAT, TRUMP SUPPORTER: We need to get take our country back in under control. This is bad, prices going up. Illegals come across the border.\par \par \uc2 LINDA LABAS, TRUMP SUPPORTER: I absolutely think Trump will win Virginia. He's winning Virginia.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: It's been a few decades.\par \par \uc2 LABAS: Well, it's time.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): It's been two decades since a Republican presidential candidate won Virginia. A recent Fox News poll has the race tied right now.\par \par \uc2 Biden won Virginia by 10 points four years ago, and Virginia's Republican governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  spoke earlier at the rally ahead of Trump.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN\uc2  (R-VA): We have seen the weak America that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has created, and we all lived through the strong America that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  built.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): Trump's campaign tells Fox, it holding $8 million in new fundraising yesterday, on the day of the first debate.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's problem is not as age, it's not his -- anything. Really, it's -- he got no problem other than it's his competence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He is grossly incompetent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): At his rally today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made no mention about any of his potential V.P. picks. That's going to have to wait, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lucas Tomlinson in Virginia. Lucas, thanks.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", we now know that Monday will be the day we learn of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision on former President Trump's bid for immunity from prosecution for acts during his presidency.\par \par \uc2 But there were a pair of major rulings today. One of which is a big development for some people arrested and jailed after the January 6th, 2021 Capitol riot. The other, involves a major shift in how government regulations on environment, public health, several other areas are handled and challenged.\par \par \uc2 We get details tonight from Fox News chief legal correspondent, anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY", Shannon Breen. Good evening, Shannon?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 So, there are two cases. That January 16, one of them that will have direct impact on the federal criminal charges against President Trump. This weekend, the justices will put the final touch on the other one, presidential immunity, something that Justice Gorsuch characterized as writing a rule for the ages.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Today, the Supreme Court dealt a major blow to the authority of federal agencies and their ability to regulate every facet of American life, relatively unchecked, areas like the environment, health care, workplace safety, and consumer protections.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Roberts penned the majority opinion, writing, "Courts need not and may not defer to an agency interpretation of the law simply because a statute is ambiguous."\par \par \uc2 A win for the fishery groups that had challenged the federal regulation requiring them to bear the cost of monitors on their boats.\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN LAPP, SEAFREEZE FISHERIES LIAISON: American citizens finally have the same rights in Court as the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . And that's huge.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Justice Kagan, writing for the dissenters, accused the court of taking what she argued should have been left to the agencies. "The majority disdains restraint and grasps for power."\par \par \uc2 The Court also sided with communities using ordinances, like bans on camping and sleeping in certain public spaces as a way of trying to manage the issue of homeless encampments.\par \par \uc2 Critics had challenged the measures as cruel and unusual punishment in violation of the Eighth Amendment, and said they don't solve the underlying problem.\par \par \uc2 JESSE RABINOWITZ, CAMPAIGN AND COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR, NATIONAL HOMELESS LAW CENTER: Can you imagine anything more cruel and unusual than throwing somebody in jail for using a blanket in the middle of the freezing winter?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a decision from the justices on Friday could impact hundreds of cases involving January 6th defendants. Charged under a federal obstruction statute crafted in the wake of the Enron scandal.\par \par \uc2 In a 6-3 opinion, the Court found the justice department's application of that statute flawed, giving those defendants, including President Trump the opportunity to fight to get the charges tossed altogether.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  called the decision disappointing. And said the justice department will comply, but also will use every remaining tool possible to go after January 6th defendants.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): And late today, the Court also weighed in on an emergency request from \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 . He asked the justices to allow him to remain free while his appeal was playing out. The Court denied that request. He is due to report to prison by Monday, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Monday, a big day. Shannon Bream, outside the Court. Shannon, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Here is some other headlines tonight. The Iowa Supreme Court has reversed a lower court ruling that put a temporary block on the state strict abortion law. Justices told the lower court to dissolve the temporary injunction and continue with further proceedings.\par \par \uc2 The new law bans most abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy and before many women know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The Texas Supreme Court has upheld the state's ban on gender affirming care for minors. Justices rejected pleas from parents that it violates their right to seek medical care for their transgender children.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors have rested their case in the bribery trial of New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Today, prosecutors elicited details about the senator's financial records by questioning an \uc2 FBI\uc2  forensic accountant. The defense begins its case Monday. And an indictment unsealed today says the police chief for schools in Uvalde, Texas failed to identify an active shooting did not follow his training and made critical decisions that slowed law enforcement response there.\par \par \uc2 Last night, \uc2 Pete Arredondo\uc2  was arrested and briefly booked into jail on 10 state jail felony counts of abandoning or endangering a child. 19 students two teachers were murdered in the 2022 attack. The shooter was killed by police.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were down today after new economic data. The DOW loss 45. The S&P 500 was off 22. The Nasdaq fell 126. For the week, The Dow and the S&P lost eight hundredths of a percentage point. The NASDAQ gained a quarter point.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we get reaction to President Biden's debate performance from House Democrats and how would it work if Democrats wanted to change nominees?\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX9 in the Twin Cities where officials are still working on responding to ongoing needs from that flooding in Minnesota. Governor \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  says the state is in a dangerous situation that floodwaters are still rising in certain areas. The Mississippi River is several feet above major flood stage in St. Paul.\par \par \uc2 Fox 45 in Baltimore with the sixth anniversary of the mass shooting at the capital Gazette newspaper.\par \par \uc2 The 2018 attack left five employees dead. Shooter Jarrod Ramos received six life prison terms.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at San Diego, courtesy of EarthCam. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX5. Two giant pandas complete their long voyage from China, making it safely to their new home at the San Diego Zoo. Officials said the pair will spend the next several weeks, acclimating to their new home in a private habitat. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: House Democrats are being careful with their public reactions to last night's debate. There are concerns the president's performance could have a negative trickledown effect on congressional races in November.\par \par \uc2 Here's Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Democrats dodging reporters after President Biden bombed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have no comments, whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: You have no comment on that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm staying with papa (ph).\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have a 12:00 flight. I have a 12:00 flight.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Is your flight -- is your flight more important than the president, the decision whether or not he could stay in the ballot?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, he had a tough night. But I don't think we need to overreact to that.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: What is overreaction in your mind?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People wanting him to remove himself from the ticket. Yes, that's a -- that's a big leap.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The top Democrat in the House sticking by the president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Leader Jeffries, do you think that President Biden should step down after the debate performance last night?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): No.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): It takes a lot for the party to unwind itself from the president. Biden delegates are pledged to him but become bound on August 7th more than a month before the next debate in September.\par \par \uc2 So, if President Biden stumbles again, Democrats are stuck. The party can only replace the nominee after August 7th due to death, resignation or disability.\par \par \uc2 Most Democrats believe the president deserves another chance.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): Well, that was strike one. If this were a ballgame, he's got two more swings.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: This isn't baseball. This is politics.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: That's what I'm saying, I know -- I don't know what you do in this game.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): House Speaker Mike Johnson discussed the 25th Amendment and its mechanism to sidetrack an unfit president.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): Since the cabinet that makes that decision, I would. I would ask the Cabinet members to search their hearts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): A senior House Democratic leadership source says people close to President Biden, "Did not serve him well." The source added, "This is not sustainable." Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram, live on Capitol Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WALLING\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: The Democrats love to wet the bed. That's what we're famous for. We were wetting the bed before last night -- last night's performance. And that's only intensified over the last couple of hours. But again, he's got to turn this around.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I know how to do this job. I know how to get things done. I know like millions of Americans know, when you get knocked down, you get back up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden today, let's get some analysis of the debate and where things go from here possibly. We're going to take a closer look at the race.\par \par \uc2 Joining us, Republican media consultant, Alex Castellanos and Democratic strategist, \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 . Gentlemen, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Mark, I want to start with you. You know, I turned the channel, I looked at some other things after we were finished our coverage at other places. And there were Democrats saying it is over. One after the other, it was pretty remarkable. What's your thought? What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I've never seen anything quite like it, to be honest. No matter how bad usually your candidate is. Usually you say he did a good job.\par \par \uc2 And in this case, it was a universal reaction to this debate to you know what, you know, how really President Trump -- President Biden rather, is really executing the presidency. And was he fit for another term? I think that was universal. It was across all ages.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's a little turbulence today. I think democratic congressmen want to see what do these polls show over the next two weeks. Is there real damage from this debate. They're the only ones who are going to put pressure on the president to step aside because they fear that they're going to be wiped out if they don't have a strong leader come November.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, there's a lot of talk about how this happens. You know, the people who the 16th aides who worked with him at Camp David for seven plus days, Dr. Jill Biden, other family members, they had to know -- they had to know that that was a possibility on that stage that last night.\par \par \uc2 ALEX CASTELLANOS, REPUBLICAN MEDIA CONSULTANT: I don't see any other way around that. But I think a lot of --- you know, you drink the juice. You buy in. Let's assume these folks love \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , that Dr. Jill does. She knows this as his life as the only life he knows and if you end his career, you end \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, I don't -- they're going to fight all the way through this no matter what, they're not going to -- she's -- Jill. Biden is going to lock the White House doors and not let anybody in who wants to talk about replacing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Though I do think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  found a good substitute today. He gave a great speech in North Carolina when he used that teleprompter. Give that teleprompter a raise and a promotion, make him the Democratic nominee, that might do it. But he didn't have one last night.\par \par \uc2 And what America saw was a -- this group of advisers sent \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  into the jungle to kill a lion, naked and without a spear. And that scared the country. Moments like that last a long time, they define elections. And last night was a defining moment for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Back to last night and the reaction to it, Mark. You know, when people talk about replacements, obviously you have Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  there. But the most common names that we hear are Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  from California and Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  from Michigan.\par \par \uc2 If -- you know, and it doesn't seem like it's heading down this road, but if it happened, how would it happen?\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, this party is not set up for a replacement. We're past the primary. The delegates are all \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , delegates pledged to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . The only person who could really change things is whether the president changes his mind about running. And then we have no selection process beyond a delicate vote. And I suppose whoever the president endorses, and that by nature has to be the vice president.\par \par \uc2 I don't see how this party couldn't nominate the vice president. It traditionally nominates vice president, that's how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got the nomination in the first place. And I think that's probably what would happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Alex, the -- you know, her -- \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's ratings are oftentimes lower than President Biden's in approval.\par \par \uc2 CASTELLANOS: She's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's insurance policy. She's got to keep her on the ticket.\par \par \uc2 This decision was made a long time ago, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to be the Democratic nominee. And that's set in cement.\par \par \uc2 He needs 1,976 Democratic votes at that convention to nail down his nomination. He's got 3,894 delegates pledged to vote for him now on the first ballot, the Washington super delegates can't come until the second ballot.\par \par \uc2 Mark is right. It's up to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Jill Biden and the family. And he's going to die with his boots on.\par \par \uc2 But look, the polls aren't going to move that much. I think you're going to see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's intensity of his support go down. But a lot of voters who said, look, I can't vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but I'm still not sure I'm going to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It might move three or four points, which is a lot in this race. But it may not be more than that, this thing may remain tight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Mark, traditionally, Democrats especially in Washington listen to the editorial board of the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . And there's a new editorial board -- editorial about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for the good of the country should step down from this race.\par \par \uc2 You have Tom Friedman, you have other people with big written things where they are on the record saying, I'm a friend of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I like the man. But he needs to get out of this race. It's it seems to be building.\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, yes, I've also never seen anything like this, where core Democrats call for the president to step down after he has effectively won the nomination, because they're going to be on the record, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be able to use those editorials in his advertisements.\par \par \uc2 So, look, the better thing for party regulars, frankly, is to -- if they really feel this way to go under the radar, and whether or not there's going to be a groundswell out of Congress. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his advisors say, hey, it's time to pack it in. I don't think that's happening.\par \par \uc2 You saw him come out swinging. If in fact, Alex is right, the polls don't move all that much. The conventional wisdom will move back to, he's going to get a convention, that convention will restore his numbers. Democrats still have a great argument against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and they're going to make it even if their leader isn't as strong as people thought he was.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, the DNC in Chicago was going to be bumpy. It may have gotten a little bit bumpier.\par \par \uc2 Alex and Mark, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how the world views the U.S. depends on whether you agree with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , we'll explain.\par \par \uc2 And then the panel on the Trump vice presidential sweepstakes and we'll talk about the possible replacement thing on the ticket, candidate casino (ph) tonight.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Time for Beyond our Borders as you take a live look at London. One of the big stories there tonight, Emperor Naruhito and Empress Masako of Japan greet an awaiting crowd during their trip to the English city of Oxford. It's part of a state visit that began Tuesday. It included a banquet hosted by King Charles Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In Iran, voters participated in a snap election to replace the late hardline president \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2 . He was killed in a helicopter crash last month. Iranians face a choice between hardline candidates and a little- known reformist. Women and those calling for radical change are barred from running. The vote itself will have no oversight from internationally recognized monitors.\par \par \uc2 The pier built by the U.S. military to bring aid to Gaza is being removed due to weather. U.S. officials now say they are considering not reinstalling it unless aid begins flowing into the population again. The vast majority of aid still sitting in the adjacent storage yard because of the difficulty agencies have had moving it to the areas in Gaza where it's most needed.\par \par \uc2 And Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  calls for resuming production of intermediate range missiles that were banned under a now scrapped treaty with the U.S. Putin says the U.S. is producing the missiles and has already brought them to Europe for exercises.\par \par \uc2 The way the U.S. responds to international events was a much more integral part of last night's debate than usual. A lot of foreign policy. State Department correspondent Gillian Turner shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: We are very, very close to World War III, and he is driving us there.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You want a World War III, let him follow and win.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Foreign policy rarely gets a feature role in U.S. presidential debates, but last night it did.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: When he was president, they were still killing people in Afghanistan. He didn't do anything like that.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : He got out, it was the most embarrassing day in the history of our country's life.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This is a guy who wants to get out of the \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Are you going to stay in \uc2 NATO\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I said no, I'm not going to support \uc2 NATO\uc2  if you don't pay.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Neither candidate offered policy solutions to America's most urgent problems like the wars in Gaza and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We continue to send our experts and our intelligence people as to how they can get \uc2 Hamas\uc2  like we did Bin Laden.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Or rescuing Americans like \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  held captive by enemy regimes.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I will have him out very quickly, as soon as I take office, before I take office.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Despair over the stage spectacle's lack of substance led one of former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 's national security advisers to ding both candidates' performances. Just think about what that debate looked like to people and leaders and people around the world. The Kremlin, seven hours ahead of Washington, says Putin snoozed right through it. Quote, "I don't think you can expect the Russian president to set his alarm. This isn't a main event for us." A Russian parliamentary leader says, quote, "Now it's clear to everyone that Biden is infirm and it's time for him to leave." Poland's foreign minister implied a comparison between Biden's performance and the fall of Roman Empire ruler Marcus Aurelius. Quote, "It's important to manage one's ride into the sunset."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Most of America's allies are staying tight-lipped today. This is very little for them to gain by publicly commenting. My favorite headline today about the international community came from "Politico." They say, "The world watched the U.S. presidential debate and it cringed." Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian Turner at the State Department. Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KING, CNN CHIEF NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Right now as we speak, there is a deep, a wide, and a very aggressive panic in the Democratic Party. It started minutes into the debate and it continues right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think the conversations range from whether he should be in this race tomorrow morning to what was wrong with him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The panic that I am hearing from Democrats is not like anything that I have heard in this campaign so far. Biden's answers were, in a lot of cases, not coherent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, and as we mentioned during this show, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " editorial board coming forward with this, for the good of the country, "To serve his country, President Biden should leave the race." As you lead leave that up, it goes into a very detailed editorial in which it says the clearest path for Democrats to defeat a candidate defined by his lies is to deal truthfully with the American public, acknowledge that Mr. President Biden can't continue his race, and create a process to select someone more capable to stand in his place to defeat Mr. Trump. It is the best chance to protect the soul of the nation." And there you have it, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " calling officially for President Biden to leave the race.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal," \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina, and Meridith McGraw, "Politico" national political correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Trey, to \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 's point, we have never seen this. We have never seen anything like this where "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " editorial board, which Democrats revere, comes out and says the presumptive nominee, the sitting president should not run.\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, I have been saying for a long time, Bret, that there may be Democrats that like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but what they really don't like is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They are not going to sit idly by and watch Biden lose this race. What their options are, I don't know. I have never been to a Democrat National Convention. But they are not going to sit idly by. What we saw last night is a candidate that may not make it to November, but he's certainly not going to prevail if he does.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's not just the editorial broadly. It is all the columnists. A list of columnists in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," Kimberley. Tom Friedman, "President Biden is my friend. He must bow out of the race." Frank Bruni, "Biden cannot go on like this." Patrick Healy, "I'm hearing high anxiety over Democrats over Biden's debate performance." Nicholas Kristof, "President Biden, it's time to drop out." Kimberley?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The media is there now, OK? Democratic activists are there now. People that are outside of elected positions, they are all there now. And you've even had some Democrats giving a pretty frank -- those sitting Democrats giving a pretty frank appraisal of his performance.\par \par \uc2 The problem Democrats have here is what has held them back from doing this all along, is that there are so many complications and uncertainties of changing nominees as well, too. Nobody is thrilled about the idea of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and she is still would be the frontrunner. There is obviously the possibility of a party split and people fighting over that. And then the unknown of how they would fare against Trump. So that's causing a certain paralysis among the elected class.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meridith, here is the president, the former president today out on the stump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don't walk as easy as I used to. I don't speak as smoothly as I used to. I don't debate as well as I used to. But I know what I do know. I know how to tell the truth.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: The question every voter should be asking themselves today is not whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can survive a 90-minute debate performance, but whether America can survive four more years of crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the White House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's this video of after the debate the first lady walking President Biden off of the stage that was in the CNN feed. We didn't see that. But, Meridith, the question comes, and I posed it to the other guys, how, if it is an existential threat to have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  back in office, do all of these aides and all of these family members and friends get to the point where they don't see this, that they allow this to move forward? They had a choice before, but now we are where we are.\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW, "POLITICO": I think President Biden's debate performance raised a lot of questions about what was going on behind the scenes during his five-day preparation at Camp David where he was out of the public spotlight. And we have seen stories over and over again from the press. I know stories have been written in "Politico," "The Wall Street Journal" had a big story, raising questions about President Biden's age, his mental fitness, that were widely criticized --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meridith, I don't want to interrupt you, but we were criticized, all of us who did these stories. And we have been doing them. We were told that we were running videos that were cheap fakes, somehow taken out of context, and that it was journalistically improper. And we never edited any video. It was just that. Then you had "The Wall Street Journal" with 46 sources, and people saying it is a political hit job. And now suddenly they have this epiphany after 90 minutes in a debate.\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: You're right. And they are asking the public look at what they saw last night during the debate, and we all saw it. We all saw the president, how he faltered, how he stumbled, how he zigzagged from talking about abortion to immigration. And it has set off real serious alarm bells in the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 But the question now is, what can they do about it? You know, 95 percent of the Democratic delegates, the 4,000, are pledged to President Biden. He would have to make the decision to step aside. And then it would set off a really complicated and just unprecedented situation at the DNC this summer.\par \par \uc2 And the president has said he will debate in that second debate. But that is still 11 weeks away. And so in that time period, they are going to have to really work to prove that he is up to the task.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , former President Clinton both sent out posts, Trey, seeming to provide a lifeline to President Biden after Democrat after Democrat after pundit after pundit said it's time to pull the ripcord here. And I don't know. Where do you see it going?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Well, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is lucky that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is his vice president. I think some people would prefer the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  we saw last night than a lucid \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . So I don't know where they go. But the guy I saw last night, Bret, is not capable of running anything. And it's a mixture of sadness and being infuriated. I take no delight in the conversation that we are having. It was sad to watch that last night. He was never Cicero. I mean, he was never a brilliant public speaker. But what I saw last night was the guy from "Rain Man," not the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Trey, doesn't that raise the question who is running the country?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: I will say this, not to be fair, because I know that's out of vogue, but my own -- Strom Thurman, people asked that question in South Carolina. Thad Cochran, people were running. Staff is running the country. And that's who is enabling him and telling him, hey, look, no, you are doing fine. You had a sundowner's moment. Stay in this race. It is staff that is empowering him. If you really love \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , let him go quietly into that good night and do not make him run for office.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, up next, we're going to check in on the Supreme Court. We'll have more of this. And later, "Notable Quotables." Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- making sure that we are able to make every single, solitary person eligible for what I have been able to do with the COVID -- excuse me, with -- dealing with everything we have to do with, uh -- look, if -- we finally beat Medicare.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN ANCHOR: Thank you, President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That moment and others have some wondering whether he may take the advice of "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " editorial board, which brings us to a place we call Candidate Casino, as you listen to the ball going around the roulette table. You get $100 in chips. Where are we on this? Is there a replacement? Is there not? If it is, who would it be? A hundred bucks, Kimberley?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: So I still think they have a hard time getting around the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  problem. I also think that even if they were, for this party in particular weird to replace her with a white guy. So I'm going $50 on Harris, $30 on Whitmer, and $20 on \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Trey?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Fifty on \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , $30 on \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , and $20 that "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " calls for the repealing of the 22nd amendment, and let's bring back Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Meridith?\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: Despite all these calls for President Biden to step aside, I really just don't see it happening. I was there in Atlanta last night, and there was a real circling the wagons for President Biden. And it's hard to imagine any scenario where they wouldn't push forward, in that case, V.P. Harris.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. So, we're still at the table. Bets are still open. You got another $100. And the V.P. choice for former President Trump is coming up sooner. Kimberley, another $100, where are you going?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Yes. I mean, this is a real bet. Who knows? But, $40 on Burgum, $30 on Vance, and $15 each on Rubio and Scott just because they are the most in the news. But he could go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Trey?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: You know, Tim Scott's name was mentioned last night. But I don't think he is seriously under consideration, which breaks my heart to say that -- $50 on J.D. Vance, I would say $20 on \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2 , and the rest on \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  because I'm not good with math. So whatever is left on Burgum.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Wine, women and song. OK, Meridith?\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: I'm split between Governor Burgum and J.D. Vance. But this being \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who really knows? Although Trey putting some money on Youngkin, that's an interesting move, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The former president was with Governor Youngkin today in Virginia, which is a blue state, but seems like its' getting pretty purple.\par \par \uc2 OK, up next, we've got Winners and Losers and then "Notable Quotables.". It's Friday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Winners and Losers. Let's start with Meridith. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: Well, honestly, I just wanted an excuse to bring up the pandas and panda diplomacy. So it's sort of a stretch, but I said China for losing the pandas. But it is part of panda diplomacy. And the Olympic trials are going on, Olympic teams are being selected this week. And it's fun to watch all of those winners, although some of the choices for some of the teams have been a little controversial, whether it's Caitlin Clark being left off the women's basketball team or, this week, Bryson Dechambeau with the men's golf team.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, good point. Trey, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: My winner, the 12th and 25th Amendments which pretty much haven't been read since they were ratified, but now they are in the news. One, of course, deals with whether the president and vice president can be from the same state, and the other deals with an incapacitated president.\par \par \uc2 My loser is Alejandro Mayorkas because American continue to be killed due to his border policy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Kimberley, winner then loser.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: OK, my winners are the lucky voters in the 16th district in New York who get to say goodbye to the scandal-laden squad member \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  after he lost his primary. And my loser is the administrative state which suffered some crushing though much needed new restraints on their powers after a string of Supreme Court rulings, including the end of \uc2 Chevron\uc2  defense -- deference.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen -- there you go. And we have the big Supreme Court ruling coming Monday with the immunity case that will follow.\par \par \uc2 My winners, Jake Tapper and Dana Bash. I think they did a nice job on that debate, moderating. It's a lot of pressure. And it's a lot of focus, and they got through it. And it was definitely interesting to watch.\par \par \uc2 Panel have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday. It is Friday. It's been a long week. But time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Come on, you know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is on top of it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: He and his team very, very, very much wanted to defy the debating incumbent curse.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If -- we finally beat Medicare.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I really don't know what he said at the end of that sentence. I don't think he knows what he said either.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The White House is now telling reporters that the president has a cold.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, U.S. FIRST LADY: Joe, you did such a great job, you answered every question, you know all the --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Trump killed it. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  killed himself.\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES\uc2 , CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: We are still far from our convention, and there is time for this party to figure out a different way forward.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This president, who has had our back, you don't turn your back. You go home with the one that brought you to the dance.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It was a slow start, there's no question about that. But I thought it was a strong finish.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT, (R-SC): The vice president must feel like a deer in headlights.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We've been told for weeks I've seen \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  do cartwheels and handstands while doing trigonometry.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And by the way, I told you I'm happy to play golf if you can carry your own bag. Do you think you can do it?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That's the biggest lie, that he's a six handicap, of all.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I was an eight handicap, eight --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I've seen your swing. I know your swing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, how did you think you performed tonight?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think we did well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The golf back and forth was really amazing. In fact, just a minute ago Phil Mickelson posted, "After watching the debate last night I feel it never got personal until they started talking golf handicaps and who kits hits the longest drive. As a golfer, I get it." There you go. Phil, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, the highly anticipated Supreme Court decision on presidential immunity, what that means for the former president.\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests are Pennsylvania Democratic Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , Republican Congressman Michael McCaul, and Ohio Republican Senator J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, please set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east coast, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 We've had a busy, busy week, and been on FOX air a lot. SPECIAL REPORT has you covered all things new.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is coming up in just seconds.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240629ek6s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 2024 Presidential Debate\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Martha MacCallum \par \uc2 11,583 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: And so much has changed in our country since then, on every single issue in this country and around the world.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS HOST: Yeah, one thing that hasn't changed --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is a moment --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- the same two people up there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Four years later, right? Stunning.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is going to be a stunning moment.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is time now to look live in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 It's go time in Atlanta. President Biden and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about to hit the debate stage. It is their first face-off as we mentioned in four years and a pivotal moment in the race for the presidency.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we are going to watch this entire thing together, all of us here on the panel and \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  in Atlanta and you can stay with us from the beginning to the end on FOX News Channel, and afterwards, we'll have the post-debate analysis. And Sean will take over at 11:00 o'clock Eastern.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's going to be fascinating to watch this. You can hear their hearts palpitating probably on both sides of this stage at this point.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Both men will walk out onto the stage. They will face each other for the first time in four years. They've talked past each other. Their commercials have run. The campaigns have hurled insults. Now, the two men are on the stage and the White House is on the line. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see if there's a handshake here and we'll watch this open right now.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN HOST: We're live from Georgia, a key battleground state in the race for the White House. In just moments, the current U.S. president will debate the former U.S. president as their parties' presumptive nominees, a first in American history.\par \par \uc2 We want to welcome our viewers in the United States and around the world to our studios in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 This is the CNN presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN HOST: This debate is being produced by CNN and it's coming to you live on CNN, CNN International, CNN.com, CNN Max, and CNN Espanol.\par \par \uc2 This is a pivotal moment between President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in their rematch for the nation's highest office. Each will make his case to the American people with just over four months until Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Dana Bash, anchor of CNN's "INSIDE POLITICS" and co- anchor of "STATE OF THE UNION".\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: I'm Jake Tapper, anchor of CNN's "THE LEAD" and co-anchor of 'STATE OF THE UNION".\par \par \uc2 Dana and I will co-moderate this evening. Our job he is to facilitate a debate between the two candidates tonight.\par \par \uc2 Before we introduce them, we wanted to share the rules of the debate with the audience at home.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump will be on the left side of the screen. President Biden will be appearing on the right. A coin toss determined their positions. Each candidate will have two minutes to answer the question, and one minute each for responses and rebuttals and additional minute for follow-up clarification or response is at the moderator's discretion.\par \par \uc2 BASH: When it's time for our candidate to speak, his microphone will be turned on and his opponent's microphone will be turned off, so if a candidate interrupt when his microphone is muted, he will be difficult to understand for viewers at home.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the debate, each candidate will get two minutes for closing statements. There is no studio audience tonight.\par \par \uc2 Pre-written notes, props, or contact with campaign staff are not permitted during the debate. By accepting our invitation to debate, both candidates and their campaigns agreed to accept these rules.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Now, please welcome the 46th of the United States, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Good to be here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: And please welcome the 45th president of the United States, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Gentlemen, thanks so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 Let's begin the debate. And let's start with the issue that voters consistently say is their top concern, the economy.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, inflation has slowed, but prices remain high. Since you took office, the price of essentials has increased. For example, a basket of groceries that cost $100, then, now costs more than $120. And typical home prices have jumped more than 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to voters who feel they are worse off under your presidency than they were under President Trump?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're going to take a look at what I was left when I became president, what Mr. Trump left me.\par \par \uc2 We had an economy that was in freefall. The pandemic are so badly handled. Many people were dying. All he said was it's not that serious, just inject a little bleach in your arm. It'd be all right.\par \par \uc2 The economy collapsed. There were no jobs. Unemployment rate rose to 15 percent. It was terrible.\par \par \uc2 And so, what we had to do is try to put things back together again. That's exactly what we began to do, and created 15,000 new jobs. We brought out in a position where we have 800,000 new manufacturing jobs.\par \par \uc2 But there's more to be done. There's more to be done. Working class people are still in trouble.\par \par \uc2 I come from Scranton, Pennsylvania. I come from household where the kitchen table -- if things weren't able to be met during the month, was a problem. Price of eggs, the price of gas, the price of housing, the price of a whole range of things.\par \par \uc2 That's why I'm working so hard to make sure I deal with those problems, and we're going to make sure that we have reduced the price of housing. We're going to make sure we build to 2 million new units. We're going to make sure we cap rents, so corporate greed can't take over.\par \par \uc2 The combination of what I was left and a corporate greed were the reason why we're in this problem right now.\par \par \uc2 In addition to that, we're in a situation where if you had to take a look at all that was done at his administration, he didn't do much at all. By the time he left, there's -- things have been in chaos, literally chaos.\par \par \uc2 And so we put things back together. We created, as I said, those jobs. We made sure we had a situation where we now -- we brought down the price of prescription drugs, which is a major issue for many people, to $15 for -- for an insulin shot, as opposed to $400. No senior has to pay more than $200 for any drug, all the drugs they can (inaudible) beginning next year.\par \par \uc2 And the situation is making -- and we're going to make that available to everybody, to all Americans. So we're working to bring down the price of - - around the kitchen table. And that's what we're going to get done.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Trump?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We had the greatest economy in the history of our country. We have never done so well. Every -- everybody was amazed by it. Other countries were copying us. We got hit with COVID. And when we did, we spent the money necessary so we wouldn't end up in a Great Depression, the likes of which we had in 1929. By the time we finished -- so we did a great job. We got a lot of credit for the economy, a lot of credit for the military, and no wars and so many other things. Everything was rocking good.\par \par \uc2 But the thing we never got the credit for and we should have is getting us out of that COVID mess. He created mandates that was a disaster for our country. But other than that, we had -- we had given them back a -- a country where the stock market actually was higher than pre-COVID. And nobody thought that was even possible. The only jobs he created are for illegal immigrants and bounceback jobs, they're bounced back from the COVID.\par \par \uc2 He has not done a good job. He has done a poor job. And inflation's killing our country. It is absolutely killing us.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, look, the greatest economy in the world, he's the only one who thinks that, I think. I don't know anybody else who thinks it was great -- he had the greatest economy in the world.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, the fact of the matter is that we found ourselves in a situation where his economy, he rewarded the wealthy. He had the largest tax cut in American history, $2 trillion. He raised the deficit larger than any president has in any one term. He's the only president other than Herbert Hoover who has lost more jobs than he had when he began, since Herbert Hoover. The idea that he did something that was significant.\par \par \uc2 And the military, you know, when he was president, they were still killing people in Afghanistan. And he didn't do anything about that. When he was president, we were still finding ourselves in a position where you had a notion that we were this safe country, the truth is, I'm the only president this century that doesn't have any this -- this decade, any troops dying anywhere in the world, like he did.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Trump, I want to follow up, if I can, you want him --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Am I allowed to respond to him?\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Well, I'm going to ask you a follow-up. You can do whatever you want with the minute that we give you. I want to follow up. You want to impose a 10 percent tariff on all goods coming into the U.S. How will you ensure that that doesn't drive prices even higher?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Not going to drive them higher. It's just going to cause countries that have been ripping us off for years like China and many others, in all fairness to China. It's going to just force them to pay us a lot of money, reduce our deficit tremendously, and give us a lot of power for other things.\par \par \uc2 But he -- he made a statement. The only thing he was right about is I gave you the largest tax cut in history. I also gave you the largest regulation cut in history. And that's why we had all the jobs. And the jobs went down and then they bounced back and he's taking credit for bounceback jobs. You can't do that.\par \par \uc2 He also said he inherited 9 percent inflation. No, he inherited almost no inflation and it stayed that way for 14 months. And then it blew up under his leadership because they spent money like a bunch of people that didn't know what they were doing. And they don't know what they were doing. It was the worst -- probably the worst administration in history. There's never been.\par \par \uc2 And as far as Afghanistan is concerned, I was getting out of Afghanistan, but we were getting out with dignity, with strength, with power. He got out, it was the most embarrassing day in the history of our country's life.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Trump, over the last eight years, under both of your administrations, the national debt soared to record highs. And according to a new non-partisan analysis, President Trump, your administration approved $8.4 trillion in new debt.\par \par \uc2 While so far, President Biden, you've approved $4.3 trillion in new debt.\par \par \uc2 So former President Trump, many of the tax cuts that you signed into law are set to expire next year. You want to extend them and go even further, you say. With the U.S. facing trillion dollar deficits and record debt, why should top earners and corporations pay even less in taxes than they do now?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because the tax cuts spurred the greatest economy that we've ever seen just prior to COVID, and even after COVID. It was so strong that we were able to get through COVID much better than just about any other country. But we spurring -- that tax spurred. Now, when we cut the taxes, as example, the corporate tax was cut down to 21 percent from 39 percent, plus beyond that, we took in more revenue with much less tax and companies were bringing back trillions of dollars back into our country.\par \par \uc2 The country was going like never before. And we were ready to start paying down debt. We were ready start using the liquid gold right under our feet, the oil and gas right under our feet. We were going to have something that nobody else has had. We got hit with COVID. We did a lot to fix it. I gave him an unbelievable situation with all of the therapeutics and all of the things that we came up with, we -- we gave him something great.\par \par \uc2 Remember, more people died under his administration, even though we had largely fixed it. More people died under his administration than our administration, and we were right in the middle of it.\par \par \uc2 Something which a lot of people don't like to talk about, but he had far more people dying in his administration.\par \par \uc2 He did the mandate, which is a disaster, mandating it. The vaccine went out. He did a mandate on the vaccine, which is the thing that people most objected to about the vaccine. And he did a very poor job, just a very poor job.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you, not only poor there, but throughout the entire world, we're no longer respected as a country. They don't respect our leadership. They don't respect the United States anymore.\par \par \uc2 We're like a Third World nation between weaponization of his election, trying to go after his political opponent. All of the things he's done. We've become like a Third World nation, and it's a shame.\par \par \uc2 The damage he's done to our country, and I'd love to ask him, and will, why he allowed millions of people to come in here from prisons, jails, and mental institutions to come into our country and destroy our country.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Trump, we will get to immigration later in this block. President Biden, I want to give you an opportunity to respond to this question about the national debt.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He had the largest national debt of any president, four-your period, number one. Number two, he got two trillion dollar tax code, benefited the very wealthy. What I'm going to do is fix the taxes.\par \par \uc2 For example, we have a thousand trillionaires in America. I mean, billionaires in America. And what's happening? They're in a situation where they, in fact, pay 8.2 percent in taxes. If they just paid 24 percent or 25 percent, either one of those numbers, they'd raised $500 million, billion dollars, I should say, in a 10-year period.\par \par \uc2 We'd be able to right wipe out his debt. We'd be able to help make sure that all those things we need to do, childcare, elder care, making sure that we continue to strengthen our healthcare system, making sure that we're able to make every single solitary person eligible for what I've been able to do with the COVID. Excuse me, dealing with everything we have to do with more.\par \par \uc2 If -- we finally beat Medicare.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you, President Biden. President Trump.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, he's right. He did beat Medicare. He beat it to death. And he's destroying Medicare because all of these people are coming in. They're putting them on Medicare. They're putting them on Social Security. They're going to destroy Social Security.\par \par \uc2 This man is going to single-handedly destroy Social Security. These millions and millions of people coming in, they're trying to put them on Social Security. He will wipe out Social Security. He will wipe out Medicare. So he was right in the way he finished that sentence. And it's a shame.\par \par \uc2 What's happened to our country in the last four years is not to be believed. Foreign countries, I'm friends with a lot of people. They cannot believe what happened to the United States of America. We're no longer respected. They don't like us.\par \par \uc2 We give them everything they want, and they think we're stupid. They think we're very stupid people.\par \par \uc2 What we're doing for other countries, and they do nothing for us. What this man has done is absolutely criminal.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you, President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Dana?\par \par \uc2 BASH: This is the first presidential election since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. This morning, the court ruled on yet another abortion case, temporarily allowing emergency abortions to continue in Idaho, despite that state's restrictive ban.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump, you take credit for the decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, which returned the issue of abortion to the states.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Correct.\par \par \uc2 BASH: However, the federal government still plays a role in whether or not women have access to abortion pills. They're used in about two-thirds of all abortions.\par \par \uc2 As president, would you block abortion medication?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: First of all, the Supreme Court just approved the abortion pill. And I agree with their decision to have done that, and I will not block it.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at this whole question that you're asking, a complex, but not really complex.\par \par \uc2 Fifty-one years ago, you had Roe v. Wade, and everybody wanted to get it back to the states, everybody, without exception, Democrats, Republicans, liberals, conservatives. Everybody wanted it back. Religious leaders.\par \par \uc2 And what I did is I put three great Supreme Court justices on the court, and they happened to vote in favor of killing Roe v. Wade and moving it back to the states. This is something that everybody wanted.\par \par \uc2 Now, 10 years ago or so, they started talking about how many weeks and how many of this are getting into other things, but every legal scholar, throughout the world, the most respected, wanted it brought back to the states. I did that.\par \par \uc2 Now, the states are working it out. If you look at Ohio, it was a decision that was -- it was an end result. That was a little bit more liberal than you would have thought. Kansas, I would say the same thing. Texas is different. Florida is different.\par \par \uc2 But they're all making their own decisions right now. And right now, the states control it. That's the vote of the people.\par \par \uc2 Like \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , I believe in the exceptions, I am a person that believes. And frankly, I think it's important to believe in the exceptions. Some people, you have to follow your heart, some people don't believe in that.\par \par \uc2 But I believe in the exceptions for rape, incest, and the life of the mother. I think it's very important. Some people don't. Follow your heart.\par \par \uc2 But you have to get elected also and -- because that has to do with other things. You've got to get elected.\par \par \uc2 The problem they have is they're radical because they will take the life of a child in the eighth month, the ninth month, and even after birth -- after birth.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the former governor of Virginia, he was willing to do this. He said, we'll put the baby aside and well determine what we do with the baby. Meaning, we'll kill the baby.\par \par \uc2 What happened is we brought it back to the states and the country is now coming together on this issue. It's been a great thing.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's been a terrible thing what you've done. The fact is that the vast majority of constitutional scholars supported Roe when it was decided, supported Roe. I was -- this idea that they were all against it is just ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 And this is the guy who says the states should be able to have it. We're (ph) in a state where in six weeks, you don't even know whether you're pregnant or not, but you cannot see the doctor, have your -- and have him decide on what your circumstances are, whether you need help.\par \par \uc2 The idea that states are able to do this is a little like saying, we're going to turn civil rights back to the states. Let each state have a different rule.\par \par \uc2 Look, there's so many young women who have been -- including a young woman who just was murdered and he went to the funeral. The idea that she was murdered by -- by --by an immigrant coming in and he talked about that, but here's the deal, there's a lot of young women who are being raped by their -- by their in-laws, by their -- by their spouse's (ph) brothers and sisters, by -- just -- it's just -- it's just ridiculous. And they can do nothing about it.\par \par \uc2 And they tried to arrest them when they across state lines.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : There have been many young women murdered by the same people he allows to come across our border. We have a border that's the most dangerous place anywhere in the world, considered the most dangerous place anywhere in the world. And he opened it up, and these killers are coming into our country, and they are raping and killing women, and it's a terrible thing.\par \par \uc2 As far as the abortion is concerned, it is now back with the states. The states are voting and in many cases, they -- it's frankly a very liberal decision. In many cases, it's the opposite.\par \par \uc2 But they're voting and it's bringing it back to the vote of the people, which is what everybody wanted, including the Founders, if they knew about this issue, which frankly they didn't, but they would have -- everybody want it brought back.\par \par \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  wanted it brought back. He wasn't able to get it.\par \par \uc2 Everybody wanted it brought back and many presidents had tried to get it back. I was the one to do it.\par \par \uc2 And again, this gives it the vote of the people. And that's where they wanted it. Every legal scholar wanted it that way.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Staying on the topic of abortion, President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Seven states -- I'll let you do that. This is the same topic.\par \par \uc2 Seven states have no legal restrictions on how far into a pregnancy a woman can obtain an abortion. Do you support any legal limits on how late a woman should be able to terminate a pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I supported Roe v. Wade, which had three trimesters. First time is between a woman and a doctor. Second time is between the doctor and an extreme situation. And a third time is between the doctor -- I mean, it'd be between the woman and the state.\par \par \uc2 The idea that the politicians -- that the Founders wanted the politicians to be the ones making decisions about women's health is ridiculous. That's the last -- no politician should be making that decision.\par \par \uc2 A doctor should be making those decisions. That's how it should be run. That's what you're going to do.\par \par \uc2 And if I'm elected, I'm going to restore Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : So that means he can take the life of the baby in the ninth month and even after birth, because some states, Democrat-run, take it after birth. Again, the governor -- former governor of Virginia: put the baby down, then, we decide what to do with it.\par \par \uc2 So he's in -- he's willing to, as we say, rip the baby out of the womb in the ninth month and kill the baby.\par \par \uc2 Nobody wants that to happen. Democrat or Republican, nobody wants it to happen.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He's lying (ph). That is simply not true.\par \par \uc2 That Roe v. Wade does not provide for that. That's not the circumstance. Only when the woman's life is in danger, she's going to die, that's the only circumstance in which that can happen.\par \par \uc2 But we are not for late term abortion, period -- period, period.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Under Roe v. Wade, you have late term abortion. You can do whatever you want depending on the state, you can do whatever you want. We don't think that's a good thing. We think it's a radical thing.\par \par \uc2 We think the Democrats are the radicals, not the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: For 51 years, that was the law. 51 years, Constitutional Scholarship said it was the right way to go. 51 years, and it was taken away because this guy put very conservative members on the Supreme Court. He takes credit for taking it away.\par \par \uc2 What's he going to do? What's he going to do, in fact, if -- if the MAGA Republicans -- he gets elected, and the MAGA Republicans control the Congress and they pass a universal ban on abortion, period, across the board at six weeks or seven or eight or ten weeks. Something very, very conservative. Is he going to sign that bill? I'll veto it. He'll sign it.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Let's turn now to the issue of immigration and border security. President Biden, a record number of migrants have illegally crossed the southern border on your watch, overwhelming border states and overburdening cities such as New York and Chicago, and in some cases causing real safety and security concerns. Given that, why should voters trust you to solve this crisis?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because we worked very hard to get a bipartisan agreement that not only changed all of that, it made sure that we are in a situation where you had no circumstance where they could come across the border with the number of border police there are now. We significantly increased the number of asylum officers. Significantly -- by the way, the Border Patrol endorsed me, endorsed my position.\par \par \uc2 In addition to that, we found ourselves in a situation where, when he was president, he was taking -- separating babies from their mothers, putting them in cages, making sure the families were separated. That's not the right way to go. What I've done since I've changed the law, what's happened?\par \par \uc2 I've changed it in a way that now you're in a situation where there are 40% fewer people coming across the border illegally. That's better than when he left office. And I'm going to continue to move until we get the total ban on the -- the total initiative relative to what we're going to do with more Border Patrol and more asylum officers.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Trump?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I really don't know what he said at the end of that sentence. I don't think he knows what he said either.\par \par \uc2 Look, we had the safest border in the history of our country. The border -- all he had to do was leave it. All he had to do was leave it. He decided to open up our border, open up our country to people that are from prisons, people that are from mental institutions, insane asylum, terrorists.\par \par \uc2 We have the largest number of terrorists coming into our country right now. All terrorists all over the world, not just in South America, all over the world. They come from the Middle East, everywhere, all over the world. They're pouring in. And this guy just left it open.\par \par \uc2 And he didn't need legislation because I didn't have legislation. I said close the border. We had the safest border in history. In that final couple of months of my presidency, we had, according to Border Patrol, who is great and, by the way, who endorsed me for president. But I won't say that. But they endorsed me for president.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, just speak to him. But, look, we had the safest border in history. Now we have the worst border in history. There's never been anything like it. And people are dying all over the place, including the people that are coming up in caravans.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you, President Trump. President Biden?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The only terrorist who has done anything crossing the border is one who came along and killed three in his administration, killed an al-Qaida person in his administration, killed three American soldiers, killed three American soldiers. That's the only terrorist that's there. I'm not saying no terrorist ever got through with the idea. They're emptying their prisons. We're welcoming these people. That's simply not true. There's no data to support what he said. Once again, he's exaggerating. He's lying.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Trump, staying on the topic of immigration, you've said that you're going to carry out, quote, "the largest domestic deportation operation in American history," unquote. Does that mean that you will deport every undocumented immigrant in America, including those who have jobs, including those whose spouses are citizens, and including those who have lived here for decades? And if so, how will you do it?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Just one second. He said we killed three people. The people we killed are al-Baghdadi and Salamani, the two greatest terrorists, biggest terrorists anywhere in the world, and it had a huge impact on everything, not just border, on everything.\par \par \uc2 He's the one that killed people with the bad water, including hundreds of thousands of people dying and also killing our citizens when they come in. We -- we are living right now in a rat's nest. They're killing our people in New York, in California, in every state in the union because we don't have borders anymore. Every state is now a border.\par \par \uc2 And because of his ridiculous, insane and very stupid policies, people are coming in and they're killing our citizens at a level that we've never seen. We call it migrant crime. I call it Biden migrant crime. They're killing our citizens at a level that we've never seen before. And you're reading it like these three incredible young girls over the last few days.\par \par \uc2 One of them, I just spoke to the mother, and he just had the funeral for this girl, 12 years old. This is horrible What's taken place in our country, we're literally an uncivilized country now. He doesn't want it to be. He just doesn't know.\par \par \uc2 He opened the borders, nobody's ever seen anything like. And we have to get a lot of these people out and we have to get them out fast, because they're going to destroy our country.\par \par \uc2 Just take a look at where they're living. They're living in luxury hotels in New York City and other places. Our veterans are on the street. They're dying, because he doesn't care about our veterans. He doesn't care. He doesn't like the military, at all. And he doesn't care about our veterans.\par \par \uc2 Nobody had been worse. I had the highest approval rating for veterans, taking care of the V.A. He has the worst. He's gotten rid of all the things that I approved, choice, that I got through Congress. All of the different things I approved, they abandoned.\par \par \uc2 We had, by far, the highest, and now it's down in less than half because he's done -- all these great things that we did -- and I think he did it just because I approved it, which is crazy. But he has killed so many people at our border by allowing...\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you, President Trump.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: ... all of these people to come in.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Biden...\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And it's a very sad day in America.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Biden, you have the mic.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Every single thing he said is a lie, every single one. For example, veterans are a hell of a lot better off since I past the PACT Act. One million of them now have insurance, and their families have it. And their families have it, because what happened, whether was Agent Orange or burn pits, they're all being covered now. And he -- his group opposed that.\par \par \uc2 We're also in a situation where we have great respect for veterans. My -- my son spent a year in Iraq, living next to one of those burn pits, came back with stage four glioblastoma. I was recently in -- in -- in France for D-Day, and I spoke to all -- about those heroes that died. I went to the World War II cemetery -- World War I cemetery he refused to go to. He was standing with his four-star general, and he told him -- he said, "I don't want to go in there because they're a bunch of losers and suckers."\par \par \uc2 My son was not a loser. He was not a sucker. You're the sucker. You're the loser.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Trump?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: First of all, that was a made-up quote, "suckers and losers." They made it up. It was in a third-rate magazine that's failing, like many of these magazines. He made that up. He put it in commercials. We've notified 'em.\par \par \uc2 We had 19 people that said I didn't say it. And think of this, who would say -- "I'm at a cemetery," or "I'm talking about our veterans" -- because nobody's taken better care -- I'm so glad this came up, and he brought it up. There's nobody that's taken better care of our soldiers than I have.\par \par \uc2 To think that I would, in front of generals and others, say "suckers and losers" -- we have 19 people that said it was never said by me. It was made up by him, just like "Russia, Russia, Russia" was made up, just like the 51 intelligence agents are made up, just like the new thing with the 16 economists are talking.\par \par \uc2 It's the same thing. Fifty-one intelligence agents said that the laptop was Russia disinformation. It wasn't. That came from his son, Hunter. It wasn't Russia disinformation. He made up the "suckers and losers," so he should apologize to me right now.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You had a four-star general stand at your side, who was on your staff, who said you said it, period. That's number one.\par \par \uc2 And, number two, the idea -- the idea that I have to apologize to you for anything along the lines. We've done more for veterans than any president has in American history -- American history. And they now -- and their family. The only sacred obligation we have as a country is to care for our veterans when they come home, and their families, and equip them when they go to war.\par \par \uc2 That's what we're doing. That's what the V.A. is doing now. They're doing more for veterans than ever before in our history.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: All right. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Let's move to the topic of foreign policy. I want to begin with Russia's war against Ukraine, which is now in its third year.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says he'll only end this war if Russia keeps the Ukrainian territory it has already claimed and Ukraine abandons its bid to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Are Putin's terms acceptable to you?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: First of all, our veterans and our soldiers can't stand this guy. They can't stand him. They think he's the worst commander in chief, if that's what you call him, that we've ever had. They can't stand him. So let's get that straight. And they like me more than just about any of them. And that's based on every single bit of information.\par \par \uc2 As far as Russia and Ukraine, if we had a real president, a president that knew -- that was respected by Putin, he would have never -- he would have never invaded Ukraine. A lot of people are dead right now, much more than people know.\par \par \uc2 You know, they talk about numbers. You can double those numbers, maybe triple those numbers. He did nothing to stop it. In fact, I think he encouraged Russia from going in.\par \par \uc2 I'll tell you what happened. He was so bad with Afghanistan; it was such a horrible embarrassment, most embarrassing moment in the history of our country, that when Putin watched that and he saw the incompetence that he should -- he should have fired those generals like I fired the one that you mentioned. And so he's got no love lost. But he should have fired those generals.\par \par \uc2 No general got fired for the most embarrassing moment in the history of our country, Afghanistan, where we left billions of dollars of equipment behind. We lost 13 beautiful soldiers and 38 soldiers were obliterated. And by the way, we left people behind too. We left American citizens behind.\par \par \uc2 When Putin saw that, he said, you know what, I think we're going to go in and maybe take my -- this was his dream. I talked to him about it, his dream. The difference is he never would have invaded Ukraine. Never. Just like Israel would have never been invaded in a million years by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . You know why? Because Iran was broke with me. I wouldn't let anybody do business with them. They ran out of money. They were broke. They had no money for \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . They had no money for anything. No money for terror.\par \par \uc2 That's why you had no terror at all during my administration. This place, the whole world is blowing up under him.\par \par \uc2 BASH: President Biden.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I've never heard so much malarkey in my whole life. Look, the fact of the matter is that we're in a situation where -- let's take the last point first, Iran attacked American troops, killed, caused brain damage for a number of these troops, and he did nothing about it. Recently, when he was president, they attacked. He said, they're just having headaches. That's all it is. We didn't do a thing when the attack took place. Number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, we got over 100,000 Americans and others out of Afghanistan during that airlift. Number three, we found ourselves in a situation where, if you take a look at what Trump did in Ukraine, he's -- this guy told Ukraine -- told Trump, do whatever you want and do whatever you want. And that's exactly what Trump did to Putin, encouraged him, do whatever you want. And he went in.\par \par \uc2 And listen to what he said when he went in, he was going to take Kyiv in five days, remember? Because it's part of the old Soviet Union. That's what he wanted to re-establish Kyiv. And he in fact, didn't do it at all. He didn't -- wasn't able to get it done. And they've lost over -- they've lost thousands and thousands of troops, 500,000 troops.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Thank you. President Trump --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I never said that.\par \par \uc2 BASH: -- come back to you for one minute. I just want to go back to my original question, which is, are Putin's terms acceptable to you, keeping the territory in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, they're not acceptable. No, they're not acceptable. But look, this is a war that never should have started. If we had a leader in this war, he led everybody along. He's given $200 billion now or more to Ukraine. He's given $200 billion. That's a lot of money. I don't think there's ever been anything like it. Every time that Zelenskyy comes to this country, he walks away with $60 billion dollars, he's the greatest salesman ever.\par \par \uc2 And I'm not knocking him, I'm not knocking anything. I'm only saying, the money that we're spending on this war, and we shouldn't be spending, it should have never happened. I will have that war settled between Putin and Zelenskyy as president-elect before I take office on January 20th. I'll have that war settled.\par \par \uc2 People being killed so needlessly, so stupidly, and I will get it settled and I'll get it settled fast before I take office.\par \par \uc2 BASH: President Biden, you have a minute.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The fact is that Putin is a war criminal. He's killed thousands and thousands of people. And he has made one thing clear, he wants to re- establish what was part of the Soviet Empire, not just a piece, he wants all of Ukraine. That's what he wants. And then do you think he'll stop there? Do you think he'll stop when he -- if he takes Ukraine? What do you think happens to Poland? What do you think of Belarus? What do you think happens to those \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries?\par \par \uc2 And so, if you want a war, you ought to find out what he's going to do. Because if, in fact, he does what he says and walks away -- by the way, all that money we give Ukraine and from weapons we make here in the United States, we give them the weapons, not the money at this point. And our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies have produced as much funding for Ukraine as we have. That's why it's -- that's why we're strong.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Thank you. Moving on to the Middle East. In October, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacked Israel, killing more than a thousand people and taking hundreds of hostages. Among those held and thought to still be alive are five Americans. Israel's response has killed thousands of Palestinians and created a humanitarian crisis in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, you've put forward a proposal to resolve this conflict. But so far, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has not released the remaining hostages and Israel is continuing its military offensive in Gaza. So, what additional leverage will you use to get \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Israel to end the war? You have two minutes.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Number one, everyone from the \uc2 United Nations Security Council\uc2  straight through to the \uc2 G7\uc2  to the Israelis and Netanyahu himself have endorsed the plan I put forward, endorsed the plan I put forward, which has three stages to it.\par \par \uc2 The first stage is to treat the hostages for a ceasefire. Second phase is a ceasefire with additional conditions. The third phase is know the end of the war. The only one who wants the war to continue is \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Number one.\par \par \uc2 We're still pushing hard from -- to get them to accept. In the meantime, what's happened in Israel? We're finally -- the only thing I've denied Israel was 2,000-pound bombs. They don't work very well in populated areas. They kill a lot of innocent people. We are providing Israel with all the weapons they need and when they need them. And by the way, I'm the guy that organized the world against Iran when they had a full-blown kind of ballistic missile attack on Israel. No one was hurt. No one Israeli was accidentally killed. And it stopped. We saved Israel. We are the biggest producer of support for Israel than anyone in the world. And so, that's -- they are two different things.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  cannot be allowed to be continued. We continue to send our experts and our intelligence people to how they can get \uc2 Hamas\uc2  like we did Bin Laden. You don't have to do it. And by the way, they've been greatly weakened, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , greatly weakened, and they should be. They should be eliminated. But, you got to be careful for what you use these certain weapons among population centers.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Just going back to Ukraine for one second. We have an ocean separating us. The European nations together have spent $100 billion or maybe more than that, less than us. Why doesn't he call them so you got to put up your money like I did with \uc2 NATO\uc2 ? I got them to put up hundreds of billions of dollars. The Secretary General of \uc2 NATO\uc2  said Trump did the most incredible job I've ever seen. You wouldn't -- they wouldn't have any -- they were going out of business. We were spending -- almost 100 percent of the money was paid by us. He didn't do that. He is getting all -- you got to ask these people to put up the money. We're over $100 billion more spent, and it has a bigger impact on them, because of location, because we have an ocean in between.\par \par \uc2 You got to ask him, as far as Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , Israel is the one that wants to go. He said the only one who wants to keep going is \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Actually, Israel is the one, and you should them go and let them finish the job. He doesn't want to do it. He has become like a Palestinian. But, they don't like him because he is a very bad Palestinian. He is a weak one.\par \par \uc2 BASH: President Biden, you have a minute.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I've never heard so much foolishness. This a guy who wants to get out of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . You're going to stay in \uc2 NATO\uc2  or you're going to pull out of \uc2 NATO\uc2 ? The idea that we have -- our strength lies in our alliances as well. It may be a big ocean, but we're ever able to avoid a war in Europe, a major war in Europe? What happens if in fact you have Putin continue to go into \uc2 NATO\uc2 ? We have an Article Five agreement, attack on one is attack on all. You want to start the nuclear war he keeps talking about. Go ahead. Let Putin go in and control Ukraine and then move on to Poland and other places. See what happens then.\par \par \uc2 He has no idea what the hell he is talking about. And by the way, I got 50 other nations around the world to support Ukraine, including Japan and South Korea, because they understand that this was this -- this kind of dislocation has a serious threat to the whole world peace. No major war in Europe has ever been able to be contained just to Europe.\par \par \uc2 BASH: President Trump, just a follow-up, would you support the creation of an independent Palestinian state in order to achieve peace in the region?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I'd have to see. But, before we do that, the problem we have is that we spend all the money. So, they kill us on trade. I made great trade deals with the European nations, because if you add them up, they're about the same size economically. Their economy is about the same size as the United States. And they were with (ph) no cars. No. They don't want anything that we have. But, we're supposed to take their cars, their food, their everything, their agriculture. I changed that.\par \par \uc2 But, the big thing I changed is they don't want to pay. And the only reason that he can play games with \uc2 NATO\uc2  is because I got them to put up hundreds of billions of dollars. I said, and he is right about this, I said, no, I'm not going to support \uc2 NATO\uc2  if you don't pay. They asked me that question. Would you guard us against Russia at a very secret meeting of the 28 states at that time, nations at that time? And they said, no, if you don't pay, I won't do that. And you know what happened? Billions and billions of dollars came flowing in the next day and the next months. But now, we're in the same position. We're paying everybody's bills.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Let's turn to the issue of democracy. Former President Trump, I want to ask you about January 6, 2021. After you rallied your supporters that day, some of them stormed the Capitol to stop the constitutionally mandated counting of electoral votes. As President, you swore an oath to quote, "preserve, protect and defend", unquote, the Constitution. What do you say to voters who believe that you violated that oath through your actions and inaction on January 6 and worry that you'll do it again?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I don't think too many believe that. And let me tell you about January 6. On January 6, we had a great border, nobody coming through, very few. On January 6, we were energy independent. On January 6, we had the lowest taxes ever. We had the lowest regulations ever. On January 6, we were respected all over the world, all over the world we were respected, and then he comes in, and we're now laughed at.\par \par \uc2 We're like a bunch of stupid people that -- what happened to the United States's reputation under this man's leadership is horrible, including weaponization, which I'm sure at some point you'll be talking about where he goes after his political opponent because he can't beat them fair and square.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: You have 80 seconds left. My question was, what do you say to those voters who believe that you violated your constitutional oath through your actions, inaction on January 6th, 2021, and worried that you'll do it again?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I didn't say that to anybody. I said peacefully and patriotically. And \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , if you just watched the news from two days ago, on tape to her daughter, who's a documentary filmmaker, they say, what she's saying, oh, no, it's my responsibility, I was responsible for this. Because I offered them 10,000 soldiers or National Guard. And she turned them down. And the mayor of -- in writing, by the way, the mayor, in writing turned it down, the mayor of D.C., they turned it down. I offered 10,000 because I could see -- I had virtually nothing to do.\par \par \uc2 They asked me to go make a speech. I could see what was happening. Everybody was saying they're going to be there on January 6th. They're going to be there. And I said, you know what, that's a lot of people coming, you could feel it. You could feel it too. And you could feel it. And I said, they ought to have some National Guard or whatever. And I offered it to her. And she now admits that she turned it down. And it was the same day. She was -- I don't know, you can't be very happy with her daughter because it made her into a liar. She said, I take full responsibility for January 6th.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Biden?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, he encouraged those folks to go up on Capitol Hill, number one. I sat in that dining room off the Oval Office -- he sat there for three hours, three hours watching, begging, being begged by his vice president and a number of his colleagues and Republicans as well to do something, to call for a stop, to end it. Instead, he talked -- they've talked about these people being patriots and -- and great patrons of America.\par \par \uc2 In fact, he says he'll not forgive them for what they've done. They've been convicted. He says he wants to commute their sentences and say that, no, he went to every single court in the nation, I don't know how many cases, scores of cases, including the Supreme Court. And they said they said -- they said, no, no, this guy, this guy is responsible for doing what is being -- was done. He didn't do a damn thing. And these people should be in jail. And they should be the ones who are being held accountable. And he wants to let them all out.\par \par \uc2 And now he says if he loses again, such a whiner that he is, that there could be a bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you, President Biden.\par \par \uc2 President Trump?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : What they've done to some people that are so innocent, you ought to be ashamed of yourself, what you have done, how you've destroyed the lives of so many people. When they ripped down Portland, when they ripped down many other cities, you go to Minnesota, Minneapolis, what they've done there with the fires all over the city, if I didn't bring in the National Guard, that city would have been destroyed.\par \par \uc2 When you look at all of the -- they took over big chunks of Seattle. I was all set to bring in the National Guard. They heard that. They saw them coming and they left immediately. What he said about this whole subject is so off. Peacefully patriotic.\par \par \uc2 One other thing, the "unselect committee," which is basically two horrible Republicans that are all gone now, out of office, and Democrats, all Democrats, they destroyed and deleted all of the information they found because they found out we were right. We were right. And they deleted and destroyed all of the information. They should go to jail for that.\par \par \uc2 If a Republican did that, they'd go to jail.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you, President Trump.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, I want to give you a minute.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The only person on this stage that is a convicted felon is the man I'm looking at right now. And the fact of the matter is he is -- what he's telling you is simply not true. The fact is that there was no effort on his part to stop what was going on up on Capitol Hill. And all those people, every one of those who were convicted deserves to be convicted. The idea that they didn't kill somebody, just went in and broke down doors, broke the windows, occupied offices, turned over desks, turned them over, statues, the idea that those people are patriots? Come on.\par \par \uc2 When I asked him about first of two debates we had -- debates we had the first time around, I said, will you denounce the Proud Boys. He said, no, I'll tell them stand by. The idea he's refusing -- will you denounce these guys? Will you denounce the people we're talking about now? You denounce the people who attacked that Capitol? What are you going to do?\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: I'm going to give you a -- a minute, President Trump, for a follow- up question I have. After a jury convicted you of 34 felonies last month, you said if re-elected, you would, quote, "have every right to go after," unquote, your political opponents. You just talked about members of the Select Committee on January 6th going to jail.\par \par \uc2 Your main political opponent is standing on stage with you tonight. Can you clarify exactly what it means about you feeling you have every right to go after your political opponents?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I said my retribution is going to be success. We're going to make this country successful again because right now it's a failing nation.\par \par \uc2 My retribution is going to be success. But when he talks about a convicted felon, his son is a convicted felon at a very high level. His son is convicted. Going to be convicted probably numerous other times.\par \par \uc2 He should have been convicted before, but his Justice Department let the statute of limitations lapse on the most important things. But he could be a convicted felon as soon as he gets out of office.\par \par \uc2 Joe could be a convicted felon with all of the things that he's done. He's done horrible things.\par \par \uc2 All of the death caused at the border, telling the Ukrainian people that, we're going to want a billion dollars or you change the prosecutor. Otherwise, you're not getting a billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 If I ever said that, that's quid pro quo. That -- we're not going to do anything. We're not going to give you a billion dollars unless you change your prosecutor having to do with his son.\par \par \uc2 This man is a criminal. This man -- you're lucky. You're lucky. I did nothing wrong. We'd have a system that was rigged and disgusting. I did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you, President Trump.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, you have said, I'm coming right to you, sir. You -- well, you want to respond? Go ahead. I'll give you a minute to respond.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The idea that I did anything wrong relative to what you're talking about is outrageous. It's simply a lie, number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, the idea that you have a right to seek retribution against any American, just because you're a president, is wrong, is simply wrong. No president's ever spoken like that before. No president in our history has spoken like that before.\par \par \uc2 Number three, the crimes that you are still charged with. And think of all the civil penalties you have. How many billions of dollars do you owe in civil penalties for molesting a woman in public, for doing a whole range of things, of having sex with a porn star on the night -- and while your wife was pregnant?\par \par \uc2 I mean, what are you talking about? You have the morals of an alley cat.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Give you a minute, sir.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I didn't have sex with a porn star, number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, that was a case that was started and moved. They moved a high- ranking official, a DOJ, into the Manhattan D.A.'s office to start that case. That case is going to be appealed in one.\par \par \uc2 We had a very terrible judge, horrible judge, Democrat. The prosecutor were all high-ranking Democrats appointed people and the -- both the civil and a criminal.\par \par \uc2 He basically went after his political opponent because he thought it was going to damage me. But when the public found out about these cases, because they understand it better than he does, he has no idea what these cases are, but when he -- they -- when they found out about these cases, you know what they did? My poll numbers went up way up. You know that because you're reporting it. And we took in more money in the last two weeks than we've ever taken in the history of any campaign.\par \par \uc2 I don't think any campaign has ever taken hundreds of millions of dollars came pouring in because the public knows it's a scam and it's a guy that's after his political opponent because he can't win fair and square.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you, President Trump.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, you have said, quote, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his MAGA Republicans are determined to destroy American democracy.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe that the tens of millions of Americans who are likely to vote for President Trump will be voting against American democracy?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The more they know about what he's done, yes. The more they know about what he's done.\par \par \uc2 And there's a lot more coming. He's got a lot of cases around the road coming around. He's got -- he's got a whole range of issues he has to face. I don't know what the juries will do, but I do know -- I do know he has a real problem.\par \par \uc2 And so the fact that -- could you ever think you're hearing any president say that, I'm going to seek retribution? Do you ever hear any president say that, I thought it would write some good ideas?\par \par \uc2 What got me involved to run in the first place after my son had died, I decided -- in Iraq -- because of Iraq. I said, I wasn't going to run again, until I saw what happened in Charlottesville, Virginia. People coming out of the woods carrying swastikas on torches and singing the same anti- Semitic bile they sang back in Germany.\par \par \uc2 And what did -- and the young woman got killed, they spoke to the mother. And she -- they asked him, they said, what -- well, what do you think of those people? The people who wanted to get killed, the ones who tried to stop it, and the ones who said, I think they're fine people on both sides.\par \par \uc2 What American president would ever say, Nazis coming out of fields, carrying torches, singing the same anti-Semitic bile, carrying swastikas, were fine people.\par \par \uc2 This is a guy who says Hitler's done some good things. I'd like to know what they are. The good things Hitler's done, that's what he said. This guy has no sense of American democracy.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Trump?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Jake, both of you know that story has been totally wiped out because when you see the sentence, it said 100 percent exoneration on there. So he just keeps it going.\par \par \uc2 He says he ran because of Charlottesville. He didn't run because of Charlottesville. He ran because it was his last chance at -- he's not equipped to be president. You know it and I know it.\par \par \uc2 It's ridiculous. We have a debate. We're trying to justify his presidency.\par \par \uc2 His presidency, his -- without question, the worst president, the worst presidency in the history of our country. We shouldn't be having a debate about it. There's nothing to debate.\par \par \uc2 He made up the Charlottesville story and you'll see it's debunked all over the place. Every anchor has -- every reasonable actor has debunked it.\par \par \uc2 And just the other day it came out where it was fully debunked. It's a nonsense story. He knows that.\par \par \uc2 And he didn't run because of Charlottesville. He used that as an excuse to run.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: President Biden?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And debunk. It happened. All you have to do is listen to what was said at the time.\par \par \uc2 And the idea that somehow that's the only reason I ran. I ran because I was worried a guy like this guy can get elected.\par \par \uc2 If he thought they were good people coming out of that all -- that for us, carrying those -- those woods, carrying those torches, then he didn't deserve to be president, didn't deserve to be president at all.\par \par \uc2 And the idea that he's talking about all of this being fabricated, we saw it with our own eyes. We saw what happened on January 6. We saw the people breaking to the windows. We saw people occupying there.\par \par \uc2 His own vice president -- look, there's a reason why 40 of his 44 top cabinet officers refused endorsing this time. His vice president hasn't endorsed him this time.\par \par \uc2 So, why? Why? They know him well. They serve with them. Why are they not endorsing him?\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you, President Biden.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be right back with more from the CNN presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BASH: Welcome back to the CNN Presidential Debate live from Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about persistent challenges you both faced in your first terms, and you'd certainly face again in a second term. President Biden, while black unemployment dropped to a record low under your presidency, black families still earn far less than white families.\par \par \uc2 Black mothers are still three times more likely to die for pregnancy related causes. And black Americans are imprisoned at five times the rate of white Americans. What do you say to black voters who are disappointed that you haven't made more progress?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They acknowledge he made a lot of progress, number one. The facts of the matter is more small black businesses that have been started in any time in history. Number two, the wages of black -- black unemployment is the lowest level of spend in a long, long time. Number three, we find them -- they're trying to provide housing for black Americans and dealing with segregation that exists among these corporate -- these corporate operations that collude to keep people out of their houses.\par \par \uc2 And in addition to that, we find that the impact of, on the -- the choice that black families have to make relative to childcare is incredibly difficult. When we did the first major piece of legislation in the past, I was able to reduce black childcare costs. I cut them in half, in half. We've got to make sure we provide for childcare costs. We've got to make sure -- because when you provide that childcare protections, you increase economic growth because more people can be in the -- in the job market.\par \par \uc2 So there's more to be done, considerably more to be done, but we've done a great deal so far and I'm not letting up and they know it.\par \par \uc2 BASH: You have 49 seconds left. What do you say to black voters who are disappointed with the progress so far?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I say, I don't blame them for being disappointed. Inflation is still hurting them badly. For example, I provided for the idea that any black family, first time home buyer should get a $10,000 tax credit to be able to buy their first home so they can get started.\par \par \uc2 I made sure that we're in a situation where all those black families and those black individuals who provided had to take out student loans that were ballooning, that if they were engaged in nursing and anything having to do with volunteerism, if they paid their bills for 10 years on their student debt, all the rest was forgiven after 10 years. Millions have benefited from that and we're going to do a whole lot more for black families.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Thank you. President Trump?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And he caused the inflation. He's blaming inflation. And he's right, it's been very bad. He caused the inflation and it's killing black families and Hispanic families and just about everybody. It's killing people. They can't buy groceries anymore. They can't.\par \par \uc2 You look at the cost of food where it's doubled and tripled and quadrupled. They can't live. They're not living anymore. He caused this inflation.\par \par \uc2 I gave him a country with no, essentially no inflation. It was perfect. It was so good. All he had to do is leave it alone. He destroyed it with his green news scam and all of the other -- all this money that's being thrown out the window.\par \par \uc2 He caused inflation. As sure as you're sitting there, the fact is that his big kill on the black people is the millions of people that he's allowed to come in through the border. They're taking black jobs now and it could be 18. It could be 19 and even 20 million people. They're taking black jobs and they're taking Hispanic jobs and you haven't seen it yet, but you're going to see something that's going to be the worst in our history.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Thank you. President Biden?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There was no inflation when I became president. You know why? The economy was flat on its back. 15% unemployment, he decimated the economy, absolutely decimated the economy. That's why there was no inflation at the time.\par \par \uc2 There were no jobs. We provided thousands of millions of jobs for individuals who were involved communities, including minority communities. We made sure that they have health insurance. We have covered with the ACA has increased. I made sure that they're $8,000 per person in the family to get written off in health care, but this guy wants to eliminate that. They tried 50 times. He wants to get rid of the ACA again, and they're going to try again if they win.\par \par \uc2 You find ourselves in a position where the idea that we're not doing it. I put more -- we put more police on the street than any administration has. He wants to cut the cops.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020240628ek6r0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House Reporters Push For Access, Biden Campaign Uses Q.R. Code To Attack Trump Record; Trump Says V.P. Pick Will Be At Debate; Justices Avoid Deciding Merits Of Idaho Restrictions; Voters To Assess President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Debate Performance; Funeral For 12-Year-Old Jocelyn Nungaray Allegedly Killed By Migrants; President Biden And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Prepare For Their First Presidential Debate Of 2024; Memorable Moments From Previous U.S. Presidential Debates Reviewed; President Biden And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Likely To Criticize Each Other On Foreign During Debate; Biden Campaign Releases Ad On Abortion Access While Trump Campaign Release Ad On President Biden Physical And Mental Competency\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Aishah Hasnie, Shannon Bream, Larry  Kudlow, Brit Hume, Nate Foy, Chad Pergram, Gillian Turner, Martha  MacCallum, Harold Ford Jr., Harris Faulkner \par \uc2 8,480 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Got to respect it, it is the perfect con.\par \par \uc2 Run on terrible policies, bet on yourself losing, and just clean up. Cha- ching.\par \par \uc2 "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" special edition. It's 7:00. And then, I'll be on at 8:00 and at 10:30.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It's going to be great. All right. That's it for us, everybody. Don't miss us tonight.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Us, all of us.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All of us. All night long.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's exiting. All right, Dana, see you in a bit. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: What are you doing? It's funny that they --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We are coming to you tonight from Fox News World Headquarters in New York.\par \par \uc2 And breaking tonight, you've heard it all day. We are three hours from the start of what figures to be the most important event of the 2024 presidential campaign, so far. President Biden will face former President Trump in a debate in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, both camps are setting expectations and getting in some pre-event spin.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there is still no decision from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  on the former president's claim of immunity from prosecution. However, they have come down with some important decisions. We'll get an update from Shannon Bream on that in a minute.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE" tonight. Fox News chief legal correspondent, anchor of "\uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS SUNDAY", Shannon Bream, with the latest from the court.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie in Atlanta with a report on the new rules for the debate and how they may impact what we see in here tonight. But we begin with Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn of the White House, with something the two sides are actually agreeing upon ahead of the debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 President Biden hasn't been here his office for the last seven days. But we have now seen him for the first time in a week on the ground in Atlanta, as aides are hoping that all that time away from official responsibilities and out of swing states helps them turn around a series of poor polls.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): The Biden campaign and the Trump campaign uniting behind the \uc2 White House Correspondents Association\uc2  letter to CNN, advocating for a pool reporter in the debate Hall the whole time, not just during breaks.\par \par \uc2 WHCA President Kelly O'Donnell, writes the host network. "Tonight's debate will have no audience present and includes format rules that can silence candidates' microphones. We don't know how this will play out in real time."\par \par \uc2 The headliners on the Biden side of tonight's spin room, California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  and Mary Trump, the former president's niece.\par \par \uc2 It's unclear if they'll go into more detail in the press secretary about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's claims that Biden will use Adderall to stay focused.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What is the response to former President Trump's claims that President Biden may be taking something tonight to boost his performance?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I don't think that I should dignify that with an answer.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president's debate prep was guided by 16 aides over a week, but ultimately, he is the one with all the experience in cycles 2020, 2008, and 1988.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Senator Biden?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I guess that's the camera. I was going to speak to the audience, but I'll speak to the camera.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): 37 years later, a Biden campaign Q.R. code will be projected on buildings in Atlanta, taking smartphone users to a site claiming \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to ban abortion and cut Social Security.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL TYLER, COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR, BIDEN 2024 CAMPAIGN: This is going to come down to tens of thousands of votes in a handful of states. And so, that's why, tonight is an opportunity for President Biden to communicate directly to those voters that are going to decide this election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The trend here at the Biden White House for the last year is for aides to put the president out less and less for shorter scripted speeches. Any Q&A is almost always guided by note cards, prepared by staff. None of that is going to be possible tonight.\par \par \uc2 Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 It's hard to overstate the importance of tonight for the 2024 race. We're about three hours away from the first presidential debate. Let's look at where things stand in this race with our Fox News Power Rankings forecast.\par \par \uc2 Four toss-up states in yellow; Arizona, Nevada, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania. Then, you have lean Democrat pretty close, but lean Democrat, Minnesota, Michigan, lean Republican, the pink ones, North Carolina, Georgia, ultimately, you're talking about electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we have 251 to 241 favoring Trump. 46 in the toss-up. 270 is the magic number to get across the threshold to the White House.\par \par \uc2 National polls, the Fox poll, you see 50-48 for Biden. Here, our most recent poll, plus or minus three is the margin of error, but you could look at the months. It's been very tight on a national level.\par \par \uc2 Now, you go to where the debate is tonight. The new Atlanta Journal Constitution poll has the former president up five here over President Biden, among likely Georgia voters.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons we're seeing that is a slippage in black support from his core supporters in black voters.\par \par \uc2 This a Fox News poll. This is from the 2020 results that we saw 91 percent for President Biden. There you see, October 2020, 79 percent. The slippage here, June 2024, 73 percent.\par \par \uc2 Now, the percentage of black vote in Georgia is actually double what it is nationwide. And if you remember how tight Georgia was, it was actually 11,779 votes that separated the two candidates.\par \par \uc2 Now, if there was a slippage of that much in the black vote, and they don't come home before the election, this state would clearly go to the former president.\par \par \uc2 The debate will be the first time in four years the current and former president will be face to face. The Trump campaign says he is ready. Biden campaigns putting out when he calls lies from the former president.\par \par \uc2 The rules are much different than previous encounters. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is in Atlanta with the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Sources tell Fox News, former President Trump is heading into the debate confident and prepared, calling his opponent this morning, a threat to the survival and existence of our country.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign sounds the former president will focus on fixing the economy, restoring peace around the world, and securing the southern border, after the recent assaults and murders of Americans at the hands of illegal immigrants.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, TRUMP 2024 NATIONAL PRESS SECRETARY: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has blood on his hands and he needs to answer for that tonight.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): The campaign also says Trump is ready to take on any questions about January 6, and election integrity, by focusing on the next four years. But all eyes are on which Trump takes the stage tonight. The combative candidate of the past --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Make sure you, in fact, let people know, your Senators.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He doesn't want to answer the question.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not going to answer, the people know, you're a senator.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: He doesn't want to answer the question.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not going to answer the question, because --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Why would you answer that question?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because the court --\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Or the more disciplined unifier seen in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): The personal attacks are going to come from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , because he's got nothing else to stand on.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): New rules this year include muting the mic of the candidate when the other one is speaking, an effort to prevent any talking over each other. And Team Trump says he is not fazed.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL WHATLEY, CHAIRMAN, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE: I don't think it's going to have a big impact. You know, look, this is not about whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can stand up for 90 minutes. This is about defending a record.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Bret, all eyes will also be on the spin room floor right behind me. Everyone's going to be on the lookout for who is here to support the former president. And that's because Trump has said previously that his V.P. choice, his pick will be here in Atlanta at the debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 Although, I don't know how much that helps us right in our search for this V.P. candidate, if every single person on that shortlist is here tonight. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. It doesn't -- didn't take anybody away. Aishah Hasnie, live in the spin room, where \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  would be later. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Well, what could have been a major topic of discussion for the debate? We'll have to wait. Fox News chief legal correspondent, anchor of "\uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS SUNDAY" Shannon Bream with the latest on the decisions coming out of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Good evening, Bret. So, the Court is making headlines tonight for the cases it didn't decide in the hours leading up to that big showdown, critical cases, a couple of them to President Trump's legal fights and to his run for the White House.\par \par \uc2 And apparently, it's so contentious and tough to wrap these up. The Court made it official today, they will not wrap his schedule this week, this goes into overtime next week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): We still do not know where the Court will land on the issue of presidential immunity. Behind closed doors, the justices are working on this question, "whether, and if so, to what extent does a former president enjoy presidential immunity from criminal prosecution for conduct alleged to involve official acts during his tenure in office."\par \par \uc2 Special Counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  alleges Trump tried to overturn the results of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are coming in!\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And directed his supporters to the Capitol on January 6th, Trump argues he has absolute immunity from criminal prosecution for acts taken within the outer perimeter of his official responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 The events of January 6th also figure in another decision we are awaiting. Whether the justice department wrongfully applied and obstruction statute passed in the wake of the Enron scandal against hundreds of January 6th defendants, including President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Court did release its official opinion in an abortion case after a document which appeared to be a draft of that decision was inadvertently and temporarily posted to the court's web-site, Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Court ultimately decided not to address the merits of a conflict between Idaho's restrictive state abortion law. And a federal law, the Biden administration is using depress for wider abortion access, sending it back to a lower court, instead, both sides say the fight is not over.\par \par \uc2 KATIE MAHONEY, PREGNANCY COACH: So, the emergency room is there to provide health care, abortion is not healthcare.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): We will not rest until reproductive freedom is the law of the land for every single woman in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's entirely possible that case ultimately ends up back here at the Supreme Court in some matter of time. Today, the justice is also blocking massive bankruptcy settlement, related to the opioid epidemic. It's a deal that would have shielded the family behind pharma -- Purdue pharma, the makers of OxyContin from a big deal of liability.\par \par \uc2 Well, we know this, the justices will give us decisions again, 10:00 a.m., Eastern on Friday.\par \par \uc2 And Bret, as I said, they made it official. Expect them on Monday too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, first time that that's happened in a long time. Shannon, really quickly. Everybody is focused on this immunity case. But that \uc2 Chevron\uc2  case, the so-called \uc2 Chevron\uc2  case, that's a really big deal. Big picture.\par \par \uc2 It is for federal regulations. Up until now, there's a lot of deference given to federal agencies that they get to decide how to interpret the laws that Congress passes when there's an ambiguity. A lot of folks worry about that say it's an unelected branch. They are not accountable directly to the people. Nobody votes for them. We'll see if the Court reigns in that agency power\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Shannon Bream, outside the Court. Shannon, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY CARTER\uc2 , 39TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I noticed that Governor Reagan recently mentioned that Reagan-Kemp-Roth proposal, which his own running mate, George Bush described as voodoo economics, and said that it would result in a 30 percent inflation rate.\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , 40TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes, you can lick inflation by increasing productivity, and by decreasing the cost of government to the place that we have balanced budgets, and are no longer running, grinding out printing press money flooding the market with it. We don't have inflation because the people are living too well. We have inflation because the government is living too well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , \uc2 FOX\uc2  BUSINESS NETWORK HOST Yes. Whoa! Whoa!\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go. \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  is getting very excited.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News chief political analyst Brit Hume. And aforementioned, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  of Fox Business. That's like music to your ears. Reagan --\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Oh, my gosh. You just -- I just -- this is the most wonderful thing to end the day. Thank you, Bret. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Economy, inflation, Brit. This is going to be, I think, a big deal tonight. We'll see where the moderators choose to go. But they can answer whoever they want to. And the former president is probably going to take it there.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, it's the overarching issue in almost any presidential election, certainly this one. And the Biden economic record, yes, there is job growth in the economy in his in his job growth and all the rest of it.\par \par \uc2 But all the public sentiment about that is completely smothered by the inflation. And there is no way around it. I mean, even though inflation is not -- is not happening at the rate that it did, but it is still happening. People feel it, everybody feels it. Nobody likes it. And it's, I think it's the insoluble issue for the Biden campaign this year.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Larry, you look at that inflation chart that started when President Biden took over year over year, and how it, you know, makes its mark into where it is now. The president has talked about this numerous times. He's had to be corrected numerous times about where it started and where it is.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, people feel it no matter what the administration says about the good numbers in the economy.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Yes. Well, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 Look, I agree. I mean, I agree with you. I agree with Brit on this. I mean, it started about 1-1/2 percent. And then, of course, it peaked at nine percent. But now more importantly, in terms of sentiment in voters and, you know, you say not feeling it. The cumulative impact of higher prices is about 20 percent on the Consumer Price Index.\par \par \uc2 20 percent is a big number. 20 percent, I might add is higher than the cumulative rise in weekly wages.\par \par \uc2 So, Biden is in the unenviable position of having to deal with negative declining weekly wages in real terms. That's a -- that's a kitchen table issue. It's a pocketbook issue. It's become, I might add one of the affordability crisis is in the homebuilding area.\par \par \uc2 You can't afford, house prices are 30-year highs, sales are crashing, mortgage rates are still sticky at seven percent. So, I expect Mr. Trump's going to take that to Mr. Biden on a number of occasions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Question, Brit, is whether we get down into the weeds, or it's not going to be that weedy. And one of the other questions on the economy is the deficit and debt.\par \par \uc2 You know, we remember just a few cycles ago, you had candidates and \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 , campaigning around the country in front of debt clocks, about how important the problem was with the debt and deficit.\par \par \uc2 Now, neither party talks about that issue.\par \par \uc2 HUME: The problem and the threat it poses is immeasurably worse now than it was then, and neither candidate, and a major candidate, in either party is talking about it. The reckless spending that occurred after COVID. There was -- there was a whole lot of spending during COVID that was -- that was going to probably trigger some inflation.\par \par \uc2 But the post-COVID spending that occurred in the Biden administration was reckless. It was extravagant. It is what produced the inflation and II don't --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is the debt today ticking as we speak.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thee you go. But the -- but the size of the spending and dumping that much money into the economy was going to produce inflation. It did. Economists on the Democratic side somehow believed it wouldn't, they were wrong, and the country, and I think the Democratic Party are paying the price for that now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Larry, you were in the Trump administration, this was not a sweet spot for the -- for the former president, President Trump as far as dealing with deficit and debt. He doesn't talk about it a lot.\par \par \uc2 But, eventually, we as a country are going to have to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Well, we will have to deal with it. And it is difficult. The reckless spending came after Mr. Trump and I might add, it came after the COVID emergency.\par \par \uc2 We had a bipartisan bill between the White House and the Republican Senate and the Democratic House Speaker Pelosi, I don't think there was anything else we could have done at the time.\par \par \uc2 I'm not a big spender. I'm a free market capitalist, and I'm a supply side. But at that moment, when large major chunks of the economy was closing down, we just had to provide government income to support families in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Now, that stuff was supposed to expire next year. And therein lies the problem with the reckless spending.\par \par \uc2 I think you'll hear Mr. Trump say tonight briefly, two ways, number one, keep tax rates low to grow the economy. And number two, he will talk about restraining spending, he has focused on it much more in this campaign than he has before.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What were you going to say?\par \par \uc2 HUME: No, I was going to say, what Larry is saying here emphasizes once again why some of us thought that the COVID shutdowns of the economy really, and much else was the worst public policy decision. Certainly I could ever remember happening. And it's what led to the spending during COVID. And does not account for the spending, as Larry points out, doesn't account for the spending.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We've seen a lot of these debates and well, obviously, we played that Reagan-Carter debate. How much of this debate do you think is looking backwards? And how much is looking forwards? If it's successful, it's looking forward?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I think the thing, the question on most people's minds tonight, the priority question is the present question of what kind of shape this President Biden is now in?\par \par \uc2 I think when people look at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and they say, yeah, he's old, and I wish he weren't so old and all that, but he seems vibrant. And you know, he makes mistakes. And maybe he's showing some of the effects of age.\par \par \uc2 We have seen the signs with President Biden that have become a Hector grip (ph), and then the people quite alarming in the way he moves away, he walks away, he talks to things he forgets and all the rest of it.\par \par \uc2 So, the question tonight, I think the threshold question is, can he get through this and seem lucid and sharp? His proponents all say that, o h, he's on his game. And Lord knows he's had enough time to prepare this week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, Larry, this whole thing about the muted mics, I mean, it's not like they're miles apart. If you mute the -- mute the mics, you're still going to hear the other guy if he pipes up. It's going to be fascinating to watch.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: It's a very interesting idea. As I said earlier in the day, I think to you, it may save Mr. Trump from himself sometimes, it may save Biden. I don't know. I don't understand any of that stuff.\par \par \uc2 I just wanted to say one quick political point. I think a lot of people are going to watch this today. They're going to see Mr. Biden, but they're actually going to see \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  behind Mr. Biden, a lot of people do not believe that President Biden, whatever happens in the outcome of this race, will be the president for the next four years. And I think that's one of the unseen, invisible hands in this whole story.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, gentlemen, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thanks. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here's some other headlines tonight, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is being sanctioned for sharing information about a federal investigation of a door plug blow out that left a gaping hole in a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  737 Max 9 aircraft. \uc2 The National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  says the company blatantly violated the agency's investigative regulations. More on that story tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 The judge in the trial of accused Idaho student killer Bryan Kohberger has scheduled a June 2nd, 2025 start for that trial. 29-year-old former criminology PhD student is accused of killing four University of Idaho students in 2022. The judge entered a not guilty plea on Kohberger's behalf last year.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up today ahead of key inflation data, the Dow gained 36, S&P 500 finished ahead five, the NASDAQ rose 54.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the funeral of a 12-year-old Texas girl who authorities say was murdered by two illegal immigrants. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 And later, we revisit some of the high points from past presidential campaign debates.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight a heart wrenching day in Houston, the funeral for a 12-year-old girl who police say was murdered by two illegal immigrants. It's a crime that has increased awareness of the southern border crisis and crimes stemming from it.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Nate Foy is in Houston with the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JULIE GONZALES, GREAT AUNT: And I was with her the day before she took her last breath, and now she's forever taken from us.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): 12-year-old Jocelyn Nungaray now laid to rest 10 days after prosecutors say these two illegal Venezuelan migrants strangled her to death.\par \par \uc2 ED GONZALES, HARRIS COUNTY, TEXAS SHERIFF: Our heartbreaks, first of all, as a Houstonian, as a father also of a teen girl. I can only imagine the heartbreak that a parent could feel.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Hundreds came out to support Jocelyn's family and her mother Alexis. Elected officials promised her death will inspire change.\par \par \uc2 JOHN WHITMIRE, MAYOR OF HOUSTON, TEXAS: Jocelyn (INAUDIBLE) will make a difference in the entirety of our life.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): The first lady of Texas Cecilia Abbott attended today's service as too Texas Lieutenant Governor \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2 , who announced new legislation called Jocelyn's law that would prevent accused murderers from receiving bail in Texas. The suspects in this case received a $10 million bail.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 DAN PATRICK\uc2 , TEXAS: I promised her mother.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Prosecutors now await test results to determine if a sexual assault happened which would make the case death penalty eligible.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: These two individuals deserve the death penalty for murdering an innocent little girl.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Lieutenant Governor Patrick has this message about the crisis at our southern border.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: These two alleged killers strangled the breath out of her. If that doesn't make people want to rise up in America and say enough is enough and secure that border, I don't know what will.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Moments ago, at a celebration of life for 12-year-old Jocelyn, Houston Mayor John Whitmire said, "There is unity in Houston tonight." Referencing not only this community shared grief, but also its desire to hold those who murdered Jocelyn accountable, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy in Houston. Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the resonating moments from debates in the past and setting the record straight on one piece of debate folklore.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 61 in Hartford, Connecticut, with at least one person killed Wednesday night when a line of powerful thunderstorms barreled across the Northeast, hundreds of thousands lost power. Numerous flights at some of the nation's busiest airports canceled.\par \par \uc2 Fox 32 in Chicago as a freight train derails in Madison, prompting nearby residents to evacuate their homes temporarily. The train consisting of about 25 rail cars was carrying various substances when it derailed. One railcar containing liquefied petroleum gas leaked a limited amount of product that was contained. And official said there was no danger to the public.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at New York here at Fox News studios. One of the big stories from Fox 5, the second round of the \uc2 NBA\uc2  draft is underway right here in New York. One notable pick, the Lakers take Bronny James, son of LeBron James to set up what could become the first father-son same team duo in league history.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back outside the Beltway too.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're looking live at the spin room in Atlanta, just outside where the debate will take place. We have complete coverage. JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME starts at 7:00 p.m. eastern time. \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  will broadcast after our debate coverage at 11:00 p.m. with analysis from the spin room tonight. We've got you covered all night long.\par \par \uc2 There has been plenty of memorable moments during past presidential debates. Tonight's senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram takes a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The debates change television.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The question is, the question is, the question --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: -- radical left.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Will you shut up, man?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: And television changed politics, underscoring the importance of live performance. Reality TV mixed with politics long before reality TV was a thing.\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I am not going to exploit for political purposes my opponent's youth and inexperience.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY, (R-UT): They brought us whole binders full of women.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: What we want to do is to replenish --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Such a nasty woman.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : -- the Social Security trust fund.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Debates amplify gaffes.\par \par \uc2 GERALD FORD\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: There is no Soviet domination of eastern Europe.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: They deliver unexpected humor.\par \par \uc2 ROSS PEROT\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I'm all ears.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Sometimes what's said isn't even what most people remember.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 : I'm going to fix it.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Viewers might recall what a candidate does.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I've had a record of appointing judges in the state of Texas. That's what a governor gets to do. The man is practicing fuzzy math again. There's differences.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: In 1992, President George H.W. Bush time in office was up. Tonight's debate is the first without a studio audience since the initial forum in 1960.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The candidates need no introduction.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: It was unclear whether \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2  or \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  won that first debate. Nixon sweated, sporting a 5:00 shadow. Kennedy, cool and confident. It's said that those listening to the radio believed Nixon won. People watching TV thought Kennedy prevailed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): That's been taught for decades. However, it's folklore. There is no study to prove it. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go, our resident historian Chad Pergram. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we conclude our preview of big debate issues by looking at foreign policy. It could play a larger than usual role in tonight's event considering what's going on around the world. Here is State Department correspondent Gillian Turner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I will prevent World War III.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CORRESPONDENT: President Biden and former President Trump go on offense against each other and America's enemies tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let me say again to any country thinking of taking advantage of the situation, I have one word -- don't.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Both candidates will face tough questions about America's role in major foreign wars.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: If I was president, the Afghanistan disaster would never have happened. Ukraine would never have happened, would never have been invaded by Russia. And the October 7th attack on Israel would never, ever, ever have happened.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Biden in recent weeks has doubled down on support for Ukraine with $225 million security assistance, air defense interceptors, and antitank weapons.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Everything we have is going to go Ukraine until their needs are met.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Trump insists all the money in the world isn't enough.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Putin has no respect for him. No foreign leader has respect for him.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And 1,200 miles south, the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  say they are wrapping up the deadly Gaza campaign.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I made it clear to the Israelis I think it's a big mistake for them to think they are going to occupy Gaza.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Israel's attention now increasingly on Lebanon where an even bloodier regional fight with Iran backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  looms. Trump says he will focus on protecting America no matter what.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We will build the great iron dome over our country.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Elsewhere in the Middle East, Iran is dramatically expanding its most prolific uranium enrichment facility in the hopes of fast tracking a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Iran must never acquire a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Iran was broke under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, the State Department today announced new sanctions targeting -- nuclear sanctions targeting Iran's petrochemical sector. That will certainly give President Biden a talking point tonight should he need one, should Trump accuse him of being soft on Iran, as he usually does. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian Turner live at the State Department. Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel setting the stage for tonight's CNN presidential debate that you can catch right here on \uc2 FOX\uc2 . The countdown is on to one of the most consequential moments in the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm absolutely going to watch the debate. I think it's one of the most pivotal debates in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have an hour at the end of the day to myself and that's not how I'm going to spend it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, I guess so. We'll be watching it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: More than likely not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, I just wait object soundbites.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I will probably tune in just to see what has to be said. It's not going to influence my vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know they said 70 percent of Americans may watch this debate in some way, shape, or form, either live, you can see it right here on FOX News Channel, or clips of it. And that's a lot of people, if you think 330 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel tonight, special panel, Martha MacCallum, anchor and executive editor of THE STORY, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE, and Harris Faulkner, anchor of THE FAULKNER FOCUS, co-host of OUTNUMBERED. We build this up, Harris. We've talked about all kind of different ways to look at it. But the bottom line, it's just a really big moment for this election.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, I think there are going to be a lot of things decided. Quinnipiac had an interesting stat this morning of 16 percent of voters who may change their minds today. That seemed like a big number to me. We have been told that everything is all baked. Most people know either they love Trump, they love Biden, they don't like either one of them, whatever. But when you come to the issues, that number, 16 percent, is a big deal. They ever watching specifically to see who will solve some of their problems, whether it's economy, it's security and crime in their areas where they are living, and the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the tune in factor that you mentioned, I do believe tonight it's up to 73 percent in some of the polling. The tune-in factor is huge. But the takeaways, we have got to figure out what that is really going to look like.\par \par \uc2 So I don't like the idea of the muted mic, by the way. I will just come clean on that. I think it's kind of chicken. I think that the moderators, you guys have done it so beautifully. You can handle that room. But by cutting a mic, you might miss a little something, because this is a human being making that decision. I want to hear everything they have to say.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. I was just saying that the room is pretty small. So even the muted mic, the other candidate is probably going to hear something.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Oh, they will, yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But we won't.\par \par \uc2 Harold, around this debate, obviously the campaigns are dropping ads on their biggest issues. The Biden focus is an ad about abortion. The Trump campaign focus is about mental competency. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ask yourself a question. Do you think the guy who was defeated by the stairs, got taken down by his bike, lost a fight with his jacket, and regularly gets lost, makes it four more years in the White House?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: These laws are truly barbaric. They are putting us back decades, if not centuries.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did this. He put women's lives in danger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you can kind of see by those ads maybe where their sweet spot is in some of the talking points.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Maybe. I think tonight, to Harris's point, and thanks for having me, is the first time, as you listen to some of those people who led in the show who were talking about what they were going to do this evening, we heard the numbers, three quarters of America may watch this or consume this somehow, this is the first time the American people are going to see them standing side by side.\par \par \uc2 They stood side by side in 2020. That was a long time ago. COVID, people's lives have changed. I love politics, and I don't remember those debates. So this is the first time you are going to see them head to head and answer questions about the border, answer questions about inflation, answer questions about abortion -- about the border and abortion and the economy and inflation, things that happened overseas.\par \par \uc2 And I think a lot of people, to your point, I think that number could be higher, Harris. We hear the poll numbers say 16 percent. I think upwards of a quarter or a third of America may say, you know, I had a thought about one of them. But I watched tonight, and I now for the first time I have seen the two of them together and I think differently. And I think Dana and Jake are going to be tough, but they are going to be fair. And I hope they are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I hope so. I hope so, too. This is the first, also, outside of the \uc2 Commission on Presidential Debates\uc2 . CNN is doing this, moderating it, but it's simulcast, and you can watch it here on FOX News Channel.\par \par \uc2 One of the controversies about all the new rules is that we don't get a journalist in the room, besides a CNN person. The \uc2 White House Correspondents' Association\uc2  put out a letter, Kelly O'Donnell with NBC, the president, saying that the "WHCA is deeply concerned that CNN has rejected our repeated requests to include the White House travel pool inside the studio." In other words, one person that can say here is what happened during the commercial break, or here is something you don't see on the TV screen.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS ANCHOR: So, people are -- one group who is going to watch this very closely is the Presidential Commission on Debates tonight, because they are ready to say I told you so. We're better at this. We know how to set the rules for this. We're not going to leave people unhappy that there is not a presence in the room.\par \par \uc2 The other part of it, and I remember in 2020 when you and I were in that room, it was a very tense night, it was in the middle of COVID.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: COVID.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It was a feisty debate. And you could see the ins and outs, right? You could watch people walk into the room. You could see the body language. You can see what happened during the breaks. Do they look at each other, do they talk to each other? This is what we call it color in journalism, and it adds to the story. So I don't know why CNN is forbidding the pool to add that kind of color to this event. And I think it's a loss.\par \par \uc2 I would also say that in 2020, 66 percent of America said they were dissatisfied with way things were going in the country. And that's understandable. We were in the middle of COVID. It was a nightmare. Now we are back to that same number, 66 percent again dissatisfied for different reasons this time. But this matchup isn't what a lot of people wanted to see in this process. But there's a reason that this is what we have, and we'll see it tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harris, that is a focus of some of the pollsters. They say this is about the double haters, the people who are angry and frustrated that they don't even want this choice. But they have to make a decision which one is worse.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: So I point out that many of the double haters whom we have talked with, with voters voices on my show, are young. And what they don't like, that's when the age and acuity comes in, and they don't like the fact that their only choices include someone that they have watched fall and stumble for the last three years, and also someone else who has been in office before. They are looking for someone who perhaps generationally speaks to them. So they will be watching tonight, too. They may not always vote. The youth vote may not always be something you can always count on. But they will be watching tonight.\par \par \uc2 There was something out of that \uc2 White House Correspondents' Association\uc2  letter that really stuck out to me that I wanted to share. So, without the pool camera there with that White House press corps, they are not there for the what ifs in a world where the unexpected does happen. And they say this sets up precedent that matters for future debates. I'm reading from that \uc2 White House Correspondents' Association\uc2  letter.\par \par \uc2 Look, to your point, Martha, this really is a dangerous template, if it doesn't work. Yes. And I would imagine the debate commission is watching and saying, well, we don't want it to fail. But if it has to, I'm sure they could point to the reasons why it would.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Really quickly, I've got to take a break. We're going to talk more about this on the other side. And as you look live at the spin room, the countdown is on. Complete coverage here on FOX News Channel. We've already got the clock up.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I know, I love it.\par \par \uc2 FORD: It's like the Super Bowl.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Let's do it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL TYLER, BIDEN-HARRIS 2024 CAMPAIGN: -- between himself, who is fighting for the American people every single day. And he's going to be debating against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who is now running his campaign as a convicted felon.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, TRUMP 2024 NATIONAL PRESS SECRETARY: President Trump has met with policy advisers to focus on the issues, again, that voters care about. We haven't seen \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in an entire week. So we expect him to be prepared tonight, I would hope, after a whole week hiding away with his advisers. He will be ready to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, we have heard from the Biden campaign that one of the topics is likely to be the threat to democracy, January 6th, the former president's role in all of that.\par \par \uc2 Meantime. A "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " poll out on swing states on the issue of who do you trust to handle threats to democracy has former President Trump at 38 percent, President Biden at 29 percent. Meantime, you have got a Gallup poll now of favorability with the former president at 46, President Biden at 37.\par \par \uc2 Harold, you see all these polls, do you think that one of the reasons this debate was chosen to be now and that's the Biden campaign's choice was because they saw this slippage that we are seeing across the country.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I think they agreed to this several weeks ago. I think both candidates -- remember, President Trump didn't participate in any of the Republican primary debates. It was probably smart not to, he had such a commanding lead. You generally want debates when you are falling behind or you think that things are getting tight.\par \par \uc2 Two, the six states that are most important, where the voters are most coveted in those swing states and those swing voters, that's who they are really appealing to tonight. They have got to figure out how do you gain an edge in Nevada? How do you gain an edge in Wisconsin and Pennsylvania and Michigan? So I'm going to be watching closely to see -- because they are not going to dictate the questions. CNN is going to dictate that. But how they answer those questions and even how they pivot to the issues that they think are most important.\par \par \uc2 The most surprising thing to me in that poll was the threat to democracy. I've said for a while in my party threat to democracy doesn't necessarily favor Democrats.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: That's right.\par \par \uc2 FORD: There are Republicans who believe that Democrats are a threat to democracy as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about what I showed earlier in the slippage in the black vote? Do you believe that that's real or that it's going to return to the mean of Democrats kind of getting black voters back?\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I believe it's real. I don't think that you can automatically say that those votes go to Trump. I don't think we know where those votes go, and I think the big crime here would be if those people sat home, because we want everybody voting in America, particularly those people who may feel disenfranchised or left out of the conversation. And Biden has left them out of the conversation. He's left us out of the conversation as people of color by saying things that are offensive and not working on the issues that lift all Americans.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the optimal color isn't brown, it's not tan, it's not black. It's green. And when the economy is hurting for people and economies that are not on Wall Street, then he hasn't done enough. And to keep saying Bidenomics, it's so -- I think your guy got it right. At least he stopped saying that after a while, that they were working, he has got to tonight, if he wants to get any people and independents in those six battleground states, he has got to admit that his policies have put people in some harm's way in the last three-and-a-half years.\par \par \uc2 And on the issue of democracy, how can you have a strong democracy if you don't have a strong sovereign border? And that's where you see that difference between Trump with 38 and Biden with 29.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's a good point.\par \par \uc2 Martha, we are going to be looking for other things besides the substance. I talked to Brit and Larry, and they were talking down in the weeds and economic numbers. But I bet it will be a moment outside of all of that that catches the viral clip that goes around the world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, you think about them in 20. It got very feisty. Biden called Trump a racist at one point. I think that he will want to go for the jugular at some point in this. He will probably pick his moments on both sides. These are men who do not like each other. I think about the Reagan moment when he talked about Mondale's youth and inexperience. Mondale cracked up. That was a warmer environment. We don't have that at this stage. So, we will see what lands.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Seven days at Camp David. There has got to be a lot of canned lines in seven days.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FORD: You would think so. I think that's the point. The threat to democracy, I think they open with the border tonight. The thing that should concern Democrats the most is what is our answer? I think we have a decent one. But you've got to be sustained with it.\par \par \uc2 And two, I love the fact that black voters are being paid attention to, both sides. It's normally an election where you only have Democrats. I don't know if President Trump is going to win a big number of black voters, but I like the fact that black voters are being engaged and spoken to in serious ways.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I've got to run.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: And Trump is changing the game for courting those black voters. He is going to places where they live, and --\par \par \uc2 FORD: Democrats have done that too. But it will be interesting to see how it all works out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we've got a lot to talk about tonight. The countdown is on. The clock is on the side of the screen. Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll have complete reaction to whatever happens tonight with analysis from strategist Alex Castellanos and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , both sides. Please plan to watch the debate right here on FOX News Channel. Again, starts 9:00 p.m. eastern time. We'll have all the debate.\par \par \uc2 And then look at that. You have got Jesse coming up, then you've got the debate coverage, the pre-debate coverage, the debate. Afterwards, \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  in the spin room. We have you covered. More Americans have turned to FOX News media for coverage of political events than any other source. Tonight is no exception. We'll have complete coverage starting 8:00 p.m. eastern time.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west for SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us, at least on SPECIAL REPORT, but not tonight -- fair, balanced, and unafraid. A special edition of JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME --\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240628ek6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Watch President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Versus \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  On Fox; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Arrives In Atlanta For CNN Debate; \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  Not Campaigning For Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters, Greg Gutfeld, Dana  Perino \par \uc2 9,137 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER\uc2 , GOP POLLSTER: Expectations of you, it's an easier place to be. So, if their expectations are low, it's easier to exceed them.\par \par \uc2 But I think he's going to have a hard time. It's a long time that he's got to speak. It's 90 minutes, no audience and people are watching to see if he's as sharp as they want him to be.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Got it. We shall see, the telltale event tonight. THE FIVE now.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Dana Perino along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford Jr., Jesse Watters and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 It's finally here, folks, the blockbuster moment that could change everything in the razor-tight presidential race. The candidates finally come face to face tonight at the CNN presidential debate that you can watch right here on the Fox News Channel. We hope you will.\par \par \uc2 The pressure is on for both \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the president emerging from seven days of seclusion at Camp David, where he crammed with 16 advisors.\par \par \uc2 Biden's own campaign acknowledging to Politico that tonight will be a "major turning point, for better or worse."\par \par \uc2 His opponent \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be arriving in Atlanta this hour and it looks like some eager fans have gathered to greet the former president on the tarmac once he gets there.\par \par \uc2 A new batch of poll shows Trump is either beating or about tied with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Trump's campaign tells Fox that he is "ready and confident."\par \par \uc2 And both sides are clashing before the candidates take the stage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has no defense for what he has done to America.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Trump team is playing games that we've always seen them do, right? They like to use debates as entertainment.\par \par \uc2 They play these games because they know on the issues that matter most of the American people, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a loser.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was preparing for this debate by practicing standing for 90 minutes. He was -- he was practicing the art of standing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Remember what Trump said about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 ? Everything he say would be disqualifying for her. He is now guilty of.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The leader of the free world, the president of this country cannot debate prep and also govern at the same time? We haven't seen \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in an entire week.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Should President Biden shake \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's hand. Does he deserve that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, that's up to the president. But I wouldn't shake his hand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: The answer to that is yes. Always. So, you can bet that President Biden will try to make the debate about democracy and not his record.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What in your view constitutes the primary threat to freedom and democracy at home?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Trump is an existential urgent threat to our democracy. He promises to rule as a dictator on day one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who's the threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Trump and his movement, they're not running against Democrats. They're running against democracy.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a great threat to our democracy.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He's willing to sacrifice our democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But a new poll from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  just blew up that key talking point, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trusted more than Biden on democracy among key swing state voters. Hardest hit, Greg, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I love how they keep saying like they're, you know, the strategy is to find a way to trigger Trump. It's like, where have you been living?\par \par \uc2 Who -- what leader has been more triggered than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , right? Triggering is defined as a sudden flash of anger or frustration. That's pure Biden. I mean, from questions going back 30 years about his plagiarism to yelling at people in the audience. Come on, man.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I've noticed that Trump does -- Trump doesn't snap, he trash talks, sometimes at length, but he knows that he has to roll with the punches, almost as much as he throws the punches.\par \par \uc2 I think again, you're seeing Democrats projecting their flaws on the opposition. I think the Dems are also tripping on their own acid.\par \par \uc2 The -- remember when Trump sat in courtrooms and slept, the same people that say that Trump is an agent of chaos were telling us that Trump was so relaxed in court, he was dozing during one of the most stressful moments of his life when he's on trial.\par \par \uc2 So, which one is Trump? The super relaxed guy or the guy that could snap at any moment? Forget the candidates, focus on the hosts. Right?\par \par \uc2 The questions for Trump are going to be pointed direct about abortion, January 6th, the court cases but for Joe, the questions are going to be pretend tough. You know, there'll be -- they there'll be like tough questions allowing for accessible handicap access, you know?\par \par \uc2 They'll bring up -- at some point, they're going to bring up Joe's, you know, age, or whether he's unfit to serve, but they won't bring up the obvious physical and mental decline. They will talk about age specifically, that way Joe can respond in a casual volley.\par \par \uc2 Well, neither of us are spring chickens. But that's not what we're talking about. We're talking about with Hur report. I think that's what Trump should bring up, it's like, look, you know, I did stand trial, but the Hur report concluded that the president wasn't fit to stand trial.\par \par \uc2 And shouldn't the American public, you know, be concerned about a president's obvious cognitive decline independent of age? I think that thing that, you know -- you know, people on T.V. giving advice, you know, everybody's doing it.\par \par \uc2 My advice to Trump is don't sound like cable news hosts. The kill shot isn't going to be bombastic. It's a casual sincere matter of fact, question in a way, Trump should be the moderator.\par \par \uc2 You know, just ask basic questions about the past four years, you know, you say inflation is down, but the prices of food are up, do you realize that inflation is additive month by month? So, even if the rate is declined?\par \par \uc2 I mean, if the -- if the inflation rate declines, the prices still keep going up? Do you understand that President Biden, it's a simple concept? I think he have to be really basic and relaxed. I just haven't seen what -- I think, again, I think the Democrats are high on their own stash.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Harold, this morning's Gallup's favorability ratings showed Biden at an all-time low, and Trump at an all-time high. So, that's kind of a tough way to go into a debate.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Good to be back.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Where were you, Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I think that the polls are fluid. Jesse, we had a conversation.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: A lot of things could go fluid.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: A couple of weeks ago, maybe a week and a half ago, we talked about how the polls may move a bit. And we -- our own poll, Fox poll showed that Biden had gained a little bit in the polls, I think I'm going to go back and forth.\par \par \uc2 Three quick points -- four quick points. First, this is the most important 90 minutes of the campaign and I would argue the most important 90 minutes.\par \par \uc2 You think about the last presidential elections, we've not had a debate, we've not had 90 minutes they're going to be this relevant, that's important -- this important, this pivotal, and is determined of two.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting, both candidates need this. I remember during the Republican primary, Mr. Trump decided not to debate any of the candidates who probably was right to do from a political standpoint, because he was so far ahead.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden, you can recall maybe just a couple of months ago said that he and his team didn't think they would even debate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What's happened to polls, what's happened to two or 300,000 voters in these six states that are so critical, this tight, tight race? So, both of them have been spurred to do this by the politics and the realities in their campaign.\par \par \uc2 Four, anyone that thinks that neither Trump or Biden are going to be prepared for this effort, a bunch of pundits and others on different networks and hours, even hours, speculating about how they prepared if they prepared. The American people should be assured of one thing. They have unorthodox and unique and different ways of preparing but both \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be uniquely and supremely prepared. And I think Mr. Biden will be the same.\par \par \uc2 Five, I think that Dana Bash and Jake Tapper are going to be tough, but I think they're going to be fair. I think they know the thing that you said Greg, you gave the best advice to both candidates tonight, but particularly President Trump, no need to try to kill with one punch.\par \par \uc2 He's got enough information and there are some things I agree with, some things I don't know, he should just lay it all out. And I hope that President Biden does the same thing.\par \par \uc2 And finally, the real winners are us, the voters, because if they do these things, this will be the first time. Most voters have not seen them stand side by side, most voters don't remember the 2020 debates, I barely remember them. And I follow this and steeped in this business of politics.\par \par \uc2 People are going to want to see where they stand on the border, where they -- where they stand on the economy, inflation, abortion, where they stand on Ukraine in the Middle East. And I think it's great.\par \par \uc2 Unlike a big sporting event, we have a lot of analysis leading up to it. The vote, the participants, the fans don't get a real opportunity to play in the game, we can bet on the game but not playing it. We are the game as voters, so we have an opportunity tonight whether you have a favorite candidate or not. I hope everyone's listened.\par \par \uc2 Because regardless of who wins, we should all have an understanding where these two come out on the issues and tonight is that first 90 minutes. So, I'm looking forward to it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Judge, I've been fascinated by the Biden team's reluctance to admit that they have some problems. They act like they're five points ahead instead of five points behind in some of these polls.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: You know, I wonder about that. And I wonder about the whole concept of how that got this thing. You know the preparation with 16 people in a hangar, people going in and out with golf carts.\par \par \uc2 But now I understand that the setting tonight is a very big setting. It'll be an empty setting. It's almost an auditorium-like setting. So, it's like they were getting \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ready for this kind of -- it sounds like a vacuous hall.\par \par \uc2 But to me, I mean, in response to what Greg is saying, and Harold as well, tonight's a prize fight. It is estimated Harold, that 73 percent of Americans are going to watch this fight tonight. It is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I think the president who has more at stake here is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is behind and I get what you're saying that polls change and all that.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is behind even though after the conviction, there was some question about the movement and the numbers. But the truth is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  risks being over-prepared, as I've said, I mean, I've done debates. You got 16 people coming at you, who's in your head right now? And you're so over-prepared, that when you're there, it's a split moment that cameras on you, you've got to be able to respond instinctively, not like saying, is this the bad Trump or the good Trump? Or do I bait him? Or don't I bait him? If you have to reach back into your mind, Dana, that takes a second and the audience gets it immediately.\par \par \uc2 Now, my question is, will they be able to ask each other questions? And also, will -- where is the camera going to be?\par \par \uc2 To me, the most important part of this debate is the control room. So, they can turn that monitor off and say, you know what, we're going to mute the other guy.\par \par \uc2 But the question is, is the other guy who may still be talking in the talker's ears still? And will the other guy be shown? So, is the camera then just on the guy who's talking? Or will the camera be on the guy who's not talking?\par \par \uc2 Because there's a lot of nonverbal communications that the public in -- also get some feedback on.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Those are usually the big moments.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They are the big moments. And you know, we saw it in the last debate and you cannot deny that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has that quick wit, where Hillary's walking around talking about taxes, and you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's, you know, if I weren't, you'd be in jail. I mean, he is that guy.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I don't think (INAUDIBLE) to that tonight.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You don't know. You don't know. The question is, will they see him? Will they see his faces last night, I hosted Laura's show, and they had a whole collage of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's faces during one of the debates. It was hysterical. Every face you could read.\par \par \uc2 But just the last thing I want to say is that the idea that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's being told there's got to be high drama clashes on popular economic issues, which is in one of those memos. The American people don't want that. They want to know, can I afford things? And will I be able to survive? Will you survive?\par \par \uc2 And by the way, are you going to be the president? Or is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  going to be the one?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jesse, what's it like to be so over-prepared?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I wouldn't know, Dana. Maybe Judge Jeanine knows the answer to this. I know the guys don't know.\par \par \uc2 If you have milk in the refrigerator. And it's open, but it's in the refrigerator. Does it spoil?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: No.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Even if the top is off?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The top is off.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: No, you think you're OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It'll spoil -- no, because --\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: It'll spoil.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: OK, because I'm either nervous or I had spoiled milk. And I don't know which one, it could be both. But I'm freaking out.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think you're OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'm freaking out, Dana. This is a big moment. And I don't know what to expect. And anybody that says they know what to expect, they don't know what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 Joe could have a great night, he could come out, mispronounced someone's name and be up-tempo and the media calls him a winner. He could also drool, and the media would say that's the greatest drool I've ever seen in my entire life.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I agree, just got to be calm. And a little dismissive. This guy has no common sense.\par \par \uc2 I almost feel pity for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I'd say, listen, I'm sure you don't remember. But I handed you a vaccine, a recovering economy and a secure border and you blow it. Let me take it from here.\par \par \uc2 Don't worry, Joe. I'm not going to get revenge. It's time for you to retire. Not worth it. You left me a big enough mess to clean up. I'll be a little busy. Just be like that. OK, if they mentioned Jan. 6 here, there's other days in the calendar. Joe, what are you going to make? Make it a federal holiday again? Be my guest, make it a federal holiday.\par \par \uc2 If they mentioned abortion, you pivot to the economy. Every time they talk about anything you don't like, because you know the first question from CNN is going to be will you accept the results of the election?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Second question could be a global warming. I mean, you don't know what they're going to ask.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You do -- you do and then it'll be abortion.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Abortion, global warming and threats to democracy. Again, Trump just has to shape and he has to absorb the question. And then shape it into something about your cost of living and your quality of life.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: But he didn't answer the abortion question, it's back at the states. He said it's in the rightful spot that states are deciding.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Done.\par \par \uc