e the anonymously sourced stories about Trump, you shouldn't pay any attention to this stupid story.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I strongly disagree I love this story. I think it's great and it's -- I think it's true too.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And nobody -- and nobody denied it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. Yes, that's true. And you know, the whole --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's not -- I have -- I have a higher criteria for my --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But you know what, Dana, you are so right. I mean, the fact that everyone keeps saying, you know, Michelle is coming back, she's coming back, she doesn't even want to be seen.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: No.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Anyway, ahead, will \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  bring up cannibals eating his uncle? Debate bingo is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Welcome back. Check this out. Check this out. We put together a bingo card, the Judge and I did for tonight's CNN Presidential Debate. So, watch out if President Biden says cannibals ate Uncle Bosie are cheap fake. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may bring up the Biden crime family or say his iconic you're fired.\par \par \uc2 Judge, you know some of the prep going on here. What -- as you look at this bingo card here, what's your take on how people may bet -- what they should bet on if they're -- if they're trying to do it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, you can bet on anything. Apparently, you can bet on who takes the first sip of water. I think that it's going to be Joe. And then there are other things like how many times you think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will say folks. You know, the odds are four. Or how many times is Trump going to say fake news. The odds are one and a half. You know, so how many times is Trump going to say MAGA. Are you getting any of this?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I am. I am. So, one of the -- one of the wackiest prop bets apparently over the weekend was who will mispronounce a politician's name first. And over-under on how many times \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's name will be mentioned and who will interrupt the moderators first. I think that one actually is -- because you know, you think -- I could -- I don't know who it might be. I mean, you think it could be President Trump? Everybody -- the bet is probably President Trump does it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, that's probably the house.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What do you think the first question is going to be?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Will you accept the results of the -- of the next election?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Either it'll be that or it'll be about abortion.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: So, you said -- so, let's make sure we got -- so, we think it's you'll accept the results of the election and or about abortion?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't -- I don't know abortion.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: No, that's what -- that's what --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. I just -- I just think --\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I think the border is the first question.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think that for sure one of the things you have on here, Harold, is elderly man with poor memory. If President Trump has, you know, memorized that phrase, I bet he will use it. And what's interesting is that for people who watch this show or are into the news, you know a lot about the Robert Hur report and how the Trump -- excuse me -- the Biden Justice Department won't release the transcript because they don't want -- I'm sorry, the audio, because they don't want us all to hear it. And that --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: If 72 percent of Americans are going to watch this tonight, that might be the first time that they hear that the special prosecutor said that.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But what is that tell you? They don't want us to hear the audio, but tonight we're going to be hearing the audio because Joe can't -- is he going to slow down, is he going to pause, is he not going to finish?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: You think it's a live audio? Jesse, what are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, well, Dana and I are going to be covering this at 7:00, then at 8:00, and also after the debate at 10:30. So, we actually are going to be drinking during the game.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's going to be really great. It's going to be fun to be there with you. Cheers, mate.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Cheers.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I'm going to be with you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: This is vodka.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, Harold, you go too?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, you're coming?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You don't want to --\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: So, first question, Jesse, you think it's going to be abortion?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No, it's not. It's about the economy.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: The economy. What do you think the first question is going to be?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's going to be accept the results of the election.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Results is you. I think it's the border. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think it will be something about how um 80 percent of the country wants a different choice between the two of you.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's a smart question, Dana.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: 80 percent don't want either of you.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We should -- we should actually ask the Biden White House what the questions are going to be.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. They know more, Donna Brazile.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Can somebody call Donna Brazile?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: He's just kidding. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Let Donna --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, I wasn't.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Time now for "ONE MORE THING." Greg?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, no show tonight. I wonder why. But I'll be back on tomorrow night with Adam Carolla and it's going to rock. Let's do this. Greg's Workout Tips. You know that I love to go to the gym. Check out this fellow. Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This little flamingo likes to get its legs in shape by rapidly pumping the water and mud to its beaks, straining everything just to get its food. That's at the Philly Zoo. It's good its legs are skinny, it can't get shots.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's kind of look like your legs when you wear shorts at the Jersey Shore.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: At least I wore shorts. What did you -- you dress like a --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I wore pants.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I wear long pants like a big boy.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You dress --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I dress like a big boy.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You dress like an online creep.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Or a creep to be online.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You're next, Jesse. All right, creepy.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, this politician in the United Kingdom, that's U.K. for some of you out there. Greg was supposed to have his big election next week but got suspended because officials discovered he was gambling against himself. He was literally betting that he would lose the election.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Why?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You got to respect it. It is the perfect con. Run on terrible policies and bet on yourself losing and just clean up. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" special edition at 7:00. And then I'll be on at 8:00 and at 10:30.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's going to be great. All right, that's it for us, everybody. Don't miss us tonight.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020240628ek6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hours Away From Biden-Trump Showdown; Biden Arrives In Georgia Ahead Of Debate With Trump; Now: Biden Arrives In Georgia Ahead Of Debate With Trump; Biden Backers Paint Trump As Threat To Democracy; Funeral Held For Jocelyn Nungaray\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Peter Doocy, Howard Kurtz, Robert Wolf, Leo  Terrell, Shannon Bream, Marc Thiessen, Nate Foy \par \uc2 9,810 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, Larry and Sandra. That was fun. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. And this is THE STORY. A live look at Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 We are now just hours away from the first 2024 faceoff between President Biden and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Any moment now, President Biden is expected to arrive in Atlanta. We saw Marine One take off about 1:50, somewhere in that area. So, we're going to watch for him to arrive. Obviously, it's a very big night for both of these men, former president and current president. You can watch tonight, the CNN debate right here, live on Fox from beginning to end, the coverage from 8:00 to 11:00 p.m. with that fantastic crew of all of your Fox favorites.\par \par \uc2 So, the Trump campaign says that he is, today, feeling ready, feeling confident. The Biden team is already fact-checking the former president before he said a word. They debated four years ago, of course, we all remember that night. But there are some big differences in the way this is going to look and sound tonight, including mute buttons to cut off the candidates when their time is up. In moments, we'll talk to Karoline Leavitt, Trump Campaign National Press Secretary; and Dick Harpootlian, South Carolina State Senator and Biden supporter. First, to congressional correspondent on the scene, Aishah Hasnie. Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 I'm not hearing Aishah. Are you guys hearing Aishah? All right. We've got a little mic problem there. So, we'll come back to Aishah in just a moment, but let's bring Dick Harpootlian in, a South Carolina Democratic State Senator who's on President Biden's National Finance Committee. He was also, as you may remember, one of Alec Murdaugh's lawyers at the murder trial, but he's been involved in South Carolina politics for a very long time and we're glad to have him back on. Dick, good to see you. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR DICK HARPOOTLIAN (D-SC): Good, good to see you. Thank you for having me on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I want to start with this. Obviously, the border is one of the big three issues. You've got economy, you've got inflation, you've got the border. Probably what voters say are the most important things on their mind. This is from earlier today. This is a dad whose son was killed at the hands of someone who was here illegally. And this is the question that he wants to be posed to President Biden. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why are you allowing this to happen? Why are you helping illegal aliens come into this country, disperse into this country knowing damn well that they will never leave. That's what I want to hear from Biden.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you say to that, Dick?\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: Well, I mean, it's a couple of points. Number one, Trump had a series of executive actions he took that were struck down by the court. But more importantly, President Biden negotiated a bipartisan deal to resolve these immigration issues legally. Trump urged the Congress not to pass it because it would hurt him in the election. I mean, that's the problem with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It's about, it's me, me, me, me, me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But let me ask you this, Dick, because just logically, you know, everyone knows that the things that you just mentioned didn't happen until deep into the Biden presidency. And here's what President Biden said at the ABC debate on this issue when he wanted to encourage people to vote for him. Here's what he said, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: I would, in fact, make sure that there is, we immediately surge to the border. All those people are seeking asylum. They deserve to be heard. That's who we are. We're a nation that says if you want to flee and you're fleeing oppression, you should come.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He's going to have to answer to this, Dick. I mean, there's a -- Jocelyn Nungaray's funeral is today, 12 years old. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: Answer to what? I mean, answer to the fact that we have people that are political refugees who are being persecuted, and he wants to let them into this country? I mean, we're all immigrants. I understand you don't want MI6 people, and you don't want criminals coming in.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Seven million people flood the southern border. I mean, we all, have always had immigration in this country. We never had anything like what we have now. Nothing like this.\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: This is much more complicated than a phrase, than a catchphrase or a bumper sticker. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not do --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't think these people who lost their children are going to feel like it's a catchphrase or a bumper sticker, sir.\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: Well, what you just said is sort of a catchphrase or a bumper sticker. I mean, it's --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't know what you're talking about.\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: The fact of the matter is, you've got Trump, you've got \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a guy who was born on third base, claims he hit a triple, convicted felon, lies, alternative facts, and by the way, most people who will be watching this debate tonight, for the same reason people here go to NASCAR races. They're going to watch for the spectacular wreck.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: And in my mind, they're going to --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think they're going because they want to hear about the issues that are on people's minds and they want to know who can solve their problems. You don't think that Americans want someone to solve their problems on that stage tonight?\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: I think people are, this is going to be blood sport tonight and that's what people are watching. And by the way, I like our odds because you know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at a NASCAR race is obviously going to smash into the wall several times as erratic as he is. We're going to hear about sharks. We're going to hear about electric cars. We're going to hear about all kinds of stuff that's not relevant to what people really want to hear about. We're not going to hear about issues tonight. We're going to hear blather, blather, blather, blather, blather.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I certainly hope you're wrong, and I think most American people hope you're wrong, too. I hope the questions are sharp and pointed. I hope they don't let people off the hook on both sides, and I hope that we get some clarity as to what either of these individuals would want to bring to the future of this country. If we don't get anything like that, that's a failure all around. So, I certainly hope that we do. Dick Harpootlian, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: You'll get it from President Biden. You will get it from President Biden.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will see. That's why we watch. And that's why we cover it fairly on both sides. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HARPOOTLIAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, joining me now is Karoline Leavitt, National Press Secretary for the 2024 Trump campaign. Karoline, I'll let you jump right off of that. Do you expect to see car wrecks and lots of talk about sharks?\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, NATIONAL PRESS SECRETARY FOR 2024 TRUMP CAMPAIGN: Absolutely not. And what we just heard from your former guest is exactly what we've been hearing for the Biden campaign and the Biden White House for the last three and a half years, refusing to take responsibility for their policies that have created unmitigated chaos, death and destruction across this country.\par \par \uc2 And to hear that man refer to the deaths of American citizens at the hands of illegal immigrants as a bumper sticker slogan is honestly infuriating. Tell that to the family of Laken Riley and her mourning mother who President Trump embraced at his rally in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Tell that to the mourning mother of Jocelyn, the 12-year-old in Texas who was just found strangled in a creek, her mother is at her funeral today, or the father of the 13-year-old in New York who was brutally raped in broad daylight and said on the record that it is because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's open border policies. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has blood on his hands, and he needs to answer for that tonight.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I mean, we've covered these stories in detail and we certainly hope that there are some questions about it because there's, I think, immigration, inflation, economy, that's what people want to hear about. I should point out on the right-hand side of your screen, the President of the United States has landed on Air Force One in Marietta, Georgia just moments ago to get ready for tonight's big face-off.\par \par \uc2 Now, we know that President Trump in some of the polling has lost some ground with independents since the conviction in a New York court. We're going to put those numbers up on the screen so that people can see what the independent vote looks like right now. It's Biden plus-nine according to, to this one measure. What can we expect from President Trump tonight to try to reach out to those voters who are still undecided about whom they should choose? I think about 16 percent say they're very open to changing their mind tonight.\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: Yes, I will say the poll you point to is one of many. There are far more that show that President Trump is actually leading with independent voters across this country. He's winning a large majority, or a large margin of the black vote that no Republican has ever received. And it is because voters view this as a binary choice between a former president who created unprecedented economic success.\par \par \uc2 We had record low unemployment, mortgage rates, interest rates in President Trump's first term. Now Americans are suffering from the worst inflation crisis we've had in a generation. Under President Trump we had a secure border, now we have a wide-open border that is causing crime all over the country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, those are all great general issues, Karoline, but I was asking you specifically about outreach to independent voters and you know and some of the voters that he didn't get during the course of the primaries. Dan Hanager at the Wall Street Journal wrote an editorial today and you know going through a number of issues but at one point in it he says if he were to pick \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  he would win this race.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't look like that's the way he's going, but I think some of those independent voters might bridge the gap if it were a pick that were someone along those lines. Is that a focus for him tonight, reaching out to those voters who maybe didn't vote for him or stayed home during the primaries?\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: As I was saying, he will relay his vision to make this country great again, to return to the days of a booming economy, peace around this world, and a secure border. At the end of the day, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's policies have been disastrous for all Americans from all backgrounds, no matter how much money they make, the color of their skin, or what state they come from.\par \par \uc2 And that's why President Trump, in our campaign, has been making a tangible effort to not only reach out to Republicans and independents, but also disillusion Democrats. President Trump campaigning in the Bronx, in Detroit, in Philadelphia. We just announced we're opening eight offices in Minnesota, 11 in the Commonwealth of Virginia. These are blue states. President Trump is currently tied with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, those are very interesting moves. You always watch where the candidates go to see where they think there is territory that they can pick up. In terms of, you know, do you expect that President Trump will pivot if he wants to get to a different topic or if he feels like these moderators aren't being fair to him? I know you've spoken out about this. You know, how is he going to navigate that if he feels like it's three-on- one as some have said?\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: Well as President Trump himself has said, a debate is like a boxing match. You have to adjust as you go throughout the fight. We do know that the moderators and CNN have a history of anti-Trump statements. With that said, we do hope that tonight will be a truly fair debate.\par \par \uc2 We hope these moderators will ask tough questions of both President Trump and President Biden on the issues that matter to the millions and millions of Americans that will be watching at home. And if that is the case, Martha, President Trump has a great opportunity to highlight his strength with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's weakness and his success with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's failures.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think about the fact that they're going to mute the other candidate while one candidate is speaking? And how do you think -- what has the Trump team done to discuss that, to talk about how to navigate that? Do you try to talk when your mic is off or do you just stay silent? What do you expect?\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: Well, that was a requirement that was requested by the Biden campaign in a clear effort to protect him. However, President Trump readily agreed to that to deliver on his promise to debate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  anytime, anywhere, any place. So, he will do his best tonight, regardless of some of these ridiculous rules to get his message and his vision for the next four years across to the voters watching at home.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I mean, there obviously last time around in 2020, it was a very different moment. We were in the middle of COVID and President Trump was diagnosed with it a couple of days after it was a very, very heated debate.\par \par \uc2 He has had, you know, appearances recently, and I think it kind of goes back to the Iowa town hall that he did with Bret and I that were very direct, very focused on issues.\par \par \uc2 You know, is that his goal tonight? And does he want, what if someone, you know, says, you know, we want to talk about January 6th, like pounding questions about January 6th. What's he going to say? What do you expect him to do?\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: Well, we do expect questions like that to come up at the end of the day, this is CNN. However, he's going to stay focused on 2024, and the next four years and what he's going to do to secure the border, to end the wars that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has got us into, and to bring down the cost of rent and gasoline and groceries for Americans at home. He has real plans and solutions to do it. I don't think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does. That's why you'll hear him talk about issues that voters don't care about, but President Trump will.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's a very unique, unprecedented opportunity for Americans to watch a presidential debate between a former president and a current president. And we're going to have that opportunity tonight. Karoline, thank you very much. Good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet.\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: You too. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the door that you're looking at, of course, is Air Force One, and we're waiting for President Biden to disembark just moments from now. So, we'll watch for that because this is obviously a very, very important day for him. It is an unusually early debate, June 27th. These nominees are not even officially nominated at this point.\par \par \uc2 And yet we have our first presidential debate on our hands this evening which I think we know from polling that a majority of Americans will be watching very closely, and we know you will too. In the meantime, as we watch this live shot, we're not going to go off that, the \uc2 White House Correspondents Association\uc2  has concerns because they say that CNN is rejecting their repeated requests to allow reporters to be inside the studio, a few reporters, for tonight's debate, except during a commercial break. So, you can see this face-off obviously live on Fox tonight.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy reporting live from the North Lawn. We're going to stay on this shot on the right-hand side waiting for President Biden to, to get off of the plane. But Peter, tell us what's going on with this and why it's so important that some reporters are able to be in that room tonight.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Martha, it's not just the \uc2 White House Correspondents Association\uc2 . They have the Biden campaign and the Trump campaign with them in telling CNN that CNN is doing the wrong thing by only letting a print pool reporter in the debate hall during commercial breaks.\par \par \uc2 And we've got part of this letter that the Correspondents Association sent to CNN. Part of it says the WHCA is deeply concerned that CNN has rejected our repeated requests to include the White House travel pool inside the studio.\par \par \uc2 It goes on, a pool reporter is present to provide context and insight by direct observation and not through the lens of the television production. It goes on, tonight's debate will have no audience present and includes format rules that can silent candidates' microphones. Silence.\par \par \uc2 We don't know how this policy will play out in real time. A pool reporter is there to observe what is said and done when microphones are off or when either candidate is not seen on camera but may speak, gesture, move, or engage in some way.\par \par \uc2 This is a White House, who in the run up to the debate, has been warning a lot about cheap fake videos, things that can be manipulated slightly so that the clip that a viewer sees or hears is not exactly as it appears.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 White House Correspondents' Association\uc2 , and both campaigns are trying to get out ahead of that. So that, if somebody says something off mic or during a commercial break, it can be cleanly observed, and then, reported out to everybody who is not actually in the room.\par \par \uc2 But unless CNN changes their policy in the next four hours and 44 minutes and 42 seconds, that's not going to happen. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's hope they do.\par \par \uc2 Peter, I was watching, as President Biden was getting off Air Force One. He is, you know, doing some meet and greet there on the tarmac. And I missed one sentence of your report with regard to whether or not the cameras will be on both people. So, will we see the reactions from Biden to what Trump is saying and vice versa?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That is going to be up to the people in the control room from CNN.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The control room. Very interesting\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And that's WHCA and both campaigns want to have a reporter in there the whole time.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. They should.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Not just during the commercial breaks, because there is probably going to be a lot more muttering over a turned off mic during the actual program, as opposed to during the commercial.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: 100 percent. 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 And you -- and you have the, you know, for them, I think that they would not want it to fall on them at the network hosting this event to make editorial decisions about when to put the camera on one person and another, and not have outsiders, you know, be privy to the process and see how all of that is working.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But they've had -- they've been asked. I mean, I'm looking at this letter from the WHCA.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Its repeated requests that CNN -- this is an issue that has been flagged to the debate hosts tonight and they have decided not to go along with it, at least not yet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you know, who is saying, I told you so right now, Peter?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Who?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The Commission -- the \uc2 Commission on Presidential Debates\uc2 , they are saying this is why it's a good idea to have an outside entity run these events and get all sides to agree on the parameters and the press and how it all works. That's how it's worked since 1987.\par \par \uc2 So, no doubt they are kind of shaking their heads when they watch this back and forth. This is the kind of stuff that they like to handle and have for many years. So, Peter, thank you as always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 So, former President Trump expanding his lead in one new poll, now up four points over President Biden in the New York Times Siena College Poll. The former president was up by just one in April. Now, at the end of June, he's up by four. So, the numbers getting better for him after his conviction in a New York criminal trial.\par \par \uc2 He says he did nothing wrong, and that he plans to appeal that conviction. Let's bring in "MEDIA BUZZ" host, Howie Kurtz, civil rights attorney Leo Terrell, and former Obama economic adviser Robert Wolf, founder and CEO of 32 Advisors. Robert and Leo are also Fox News contributors.\par \par \uc2 Great to have you all with us, gentlemen. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Howie, as someone who covers the media and political coverage very closely, I wanted to get your reaction on this, as the \uc2 White House Correspondents' Association\uc2  pushes CNN to allow just a few a handful of White House reporters to be in the room and watch and report on this thing.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS CHANNEL MEDIA ANALYST: Well, I think, of course, they should be in there. And I don't understand why CNN is digging in its heels. But it seems to me that those polls that you just read, underscore why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wanted this early debate.\par \par \uc2 Now, he'll come out energetic, and the media will declare him the winner, because he's got a very low bar, and just has to prove he can string two sentences together after all the Trumpian attacks.\par \par \uc2 But can he convince enough viewers, it's be a massive audience, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: That he is up to the job for the next four years. Where -- the challenge for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which I think is overlooked, is to be disciplined. When I interviewed the former president at Mar-a-Lago for an hour, he was serious and (INAUDIBLE) about the issues. You and Bret have had that experience as well. He can do that when he wants to, and that might reassure some people maybe aren't crazy, but some of the things that Trump says and does to feel like -- and put aside their doubts and feel comfortable voting for him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we see live right now. President Biden has landed in Marietta, Georgia. He is shaking a lot of hands before he gets into his presidential vehicle. And we'll see him in a few hours.\par \par \uc2 But no doubt some final prep and probably a little rest on both parts, before they get ready for tonight.\par \par \uc2 I want to take the history dial back here for just a moment and play some of the defining moments that we have seen in prior presidential debates -- now, the president has gotten into the car. So, we can cut away for just a moment and take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 These are some of the memorable moments of prior presidential debates. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , 40TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I am not going to exploit for political purposes my opponent's youth and inexperience.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BENTSEN (D), FORMER VICE PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Senator, you're no Jack Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. How has now national debt --\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , 43rd PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's not only what's your philosophy and what's your position on issues. But can you get things done? And I believe I can.\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, you're entitled, as a president, your own airplane and your own house, but not your own facts. All right?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: It's just awfully good that someone with the temperament of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not in charge of the law in our country.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Yes, because you would be in jail.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Secretary Clinton --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Robert Wolf, you know, when you look at that, and you look back at some of those memorable moments, really, you know, there is only a few that stand out, we put -- you know, obviously, there's so much emphasis on these nights. But when you go back to really land something that makes a difference in the trajectory and a campaign, and one of my favorites, actually, is that \uc2 Al Gore\uc2 -George Bush moment, because he got right into his face. And obviously, they had talked to the debate prep.\par \par \uc2 You know, when he says something, you should go right over to him, right? It seemed like a good idea at the time. But it -- but it was spectacularly bad. So, Robert, what stands out? And what are you looking at for when you look at tonight?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: To me, I think well, first of all, I loved what Howie said that Biden is going to be the winner. So, that's always a good start, when you have someone who knows media well.\par \par \uc2 I think it's going to be policy driven. I think that President Biden has the goods. And he is going to ask to explain to the people why those goods are working now and why they are going to work in the future.\par \par \uc2 I thought today, there was a great report came out that said simply put, America's workers are doing better than ever before on record.\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs decision was two years ago, Democrats have outperformed every poll by 68 points, winning in referendums in Kansas and Wisconsin, winning elections in Kentucky. And I think the most important thing is, you know, facts matter. We've passed the bipartisan infrastructure deal. President Trump could not pass that.\par \par \uc2 We passed the CHIPS Act. You now have Intel doing a big plant in Arizona. You have \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  doing a big plant in Wisconsin. I think state of the facts, I hope it's not one of these attack dogs back and forth. I think the people want to hear who has a vision for the future and who can make it happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, Leo, let me ask you, since Howie weighed-in and Robert Weighed-in, who do you think is going to win tonight?\par \par \uc2 LEO TERRELL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think it's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not going to give in -- let me -- excuse me, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to win. I'll tell you what's the problem is.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: With (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: If you listen to what Robert just -- if you -- if you listen to what Robert just said, he didn't address the issues, Martha that is on the minds of every American: the economy, immigration, and inflation.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can't run on those three items. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can do a compare and contrast. And you asked your two previous guests issues about independent voters.\par \par \uc2 Why is President Trump resonating with blacks and people of color, Hispanics? Because his message is colorblind. Every American is hurting from inflation.\par \par \uc2 You had on your show, Martha, victims of immigration crime. President Trump calls it migrant crime.\par \par \uc2 So, in summary, if President Trump hits the three issues that are on their minds of all Americans, immigration, inflation, and the economy, he is going to win hands down tonight.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: By the way, I said the media would declare, by the way.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So --\par \par \uc2 I know -- I know. I understood.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes. I know, Howie. I was just playing there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that -- and I think, can you said, if he can string two sentences together, the media will immediately say that he won. So, we'll see if that happens tonight.\par \par \uc2 Howie, June 27th. Very early in this process, why are we here today at this point?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: We are here today because all the polls show that Biden is on a trajectory to lose his campaign and his people know that. So, he wanted the early debate. He wanted the muted microphones and the no audience because he feared some sort of repeat of the interrupt the fun from 2020 as everybody well remembers.\par \par \uc2 And he needs to change the dynamic and the narrative of this campaign. You don't get many chances to do that. Now, in a normal campaign, three, or four-point lead not that much. But this campaign has seemed frozen for a long time, and it's hard to make up ground. And that's why Biden came out and said, let's debate starting so early. And that's why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  accepted, even though he had quarrels with the rules, which is going to be really weird and quiet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Because he didn't want to get into a debate about debates.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I think, speaking about a debate about debates, one of the things that we're going to be watching for in our coverage tonight is how this works, Robert. I mean, this is weird. Yes, to mute someone at a debate. The whole idea is that they are debating. That they are jumping in, you know, and they are -- they are challenging each other. And so, I think, you know, I really hope this is a great debate tonight, but I -- and I hope at the end of it, we are not saying, wow, that was weird. You know? Watching them stand there and you know, be -- have their microphone turned off. I mean, since when is that a debate?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes, Martha. I was hoping it was you and Bret. But tonight, it's two other people.\par \par \uc2 But listen, I totally I agree with you. We need to debate. We need to debate the policies. I think the last time around with them, it was a boxing match. It didn't help anyone. I mean, I think Biden won only because Trump, all he did was yell on screen. I think today --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But your candidate insisted on the muting. He insisted on the muting.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes. Yes, I don't love that personally. But no one asked me. I've done to actually debate prep, both with the Obama and Clinton campaign.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: But listen, rules are set. And I think they are set so the audience can hear each other talk. I hope they allow some back and forth. That would be the best thing. The only thing I need to say to Leo and Howie is it's amazing we didn't bring up Fox's poll that had Biden up and independents up for Biden nine points.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I did bring that up. I showed it.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: So, that's a great poll.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: She did bring it up. She did bring it up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I showed it with my last guest. I just showed that. We showed it 30 seconds ago, Biden plus nine with independent voters. That was the subject of our last interview.\par \par \uc2 So, yes. No audience, by the way. So, there is no audience in the room. So, that too is odd.\par \par \uc2 But anyway, thank you very much, you guys. Great to have all of you with us. And we look forward to hearing what you think about the big night.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, it is a very special big night here. And we look forward to our coverage tonight on Fox. And we continue with our coverage right here with Shannon Bream, and Marc Thiessen, and \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, you're planning on watching the debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A hundred percent, I can't wait. I'm amped up. It's like, it's going to be one of the greatest boxing matches of all time. It's like going to a UFC fight or a boxing match. This is like Leonard -- Hearns-Sugar Ray.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President Biden, praising today's Supreme Court ruling on abortion, ahead of tonight's face-off with former President Trump. You can see it live on Fox News here tonight.\par \par \uc2 There's also an update on when we might get the High Court decision on the former president's claim of immunity. That is a big one and we have been waiting for it day after day. Fox News Sunday anchor and Chief Legal Correspondent Shannon Bream reporting live from Washington. Hi, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. So yes, we are awaiting it. It seems like there's a lot of tension here among the justices. As we went on those final we believe pretty contentious decisions coming out of the core. Two of them could have a direct impact on President Trump, his legal battles, his run for the White House, and we could get an answer on that as early as tomorrow morning 10:00 AM Eastern.\par \par \uc2 Behind closed doors, the justices for weeks have been wrestling with this question of whether the president, former President Trump has immunity in regard to the allegations against him by Special Counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 , that he conspired to overturn the 2020 election, that he directed his supporters here to Capitol Hill on January 6 to obstruct proceedings. President Trump says he's got absolute immunity against criminal liability for acts undertaken during his presidency.\par \par \uc2 But here's we're going to look to see, if the court does, is that they say yes, there is some level of immunity but only for official acts and then potentially tell us how we're supposed to define that and moving forward.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's also the case of the January 6 defendant who says the Justice Department wrongfully applied this federal obstruction statute in his case. If he wins, that could impact hundreds of other defendants charged under that same statute, and yes, that includes President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So we got a bit of a non-decision in that abortion case out of Idaho this morning. The justice is sending it back to a lower court so it may end up back here. But in the meantime, there'll be more access to abortion in Idaho, at least temporarily, decisions and again today and Friday, and they've added Monday as well. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We'll be watching. Shannon, thank you very much. So Biden allies lineup to feat Trump as a threat to democracy ahead of tonight's debate here on Fox. But key voters in swing states seem to be differing in some of these polls. Marc Thiessen breaks it down for us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, everybody. They are on the move. We just watched President Biden land in Georgia and moments ago, we saw Foreign President Trump board the Trump Air Force, the Trump plane, I should say, to take him to Atlanta for tonight's big debate.\par \par \uc2 It was kind of hard to see him getting on the plane. He was on the other side. The car pulled up and he got in, and I saw him go up the stairs. He looked like it was just him going up. We don't know who else will be attending within this evening, so we'll be watching for that.\par \par \uc2 But there's the wide shot at Palm Beach International Airport where that plane is a fixture not far from Mar-a-Lago as the president gets ready for a very, the former president, for a very, very big night. So we'll watch all of that activity.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, listen to some pre debate spin from Biden allies this time, warning of the threat former President Trump poses to democracy. This is a big theme for their campaign, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R), FORMER ILLINOIS CONGRESSMAN: There is one man running for re-election of two men running for re-election that cares about the future of this country and cares about democracy, and that's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Are y'all as offended as I am about the fact that it's possible for this person, who is a felon and an adjudicated sexual assaulter, to stand next to the actual president?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL TYLER, BIDEN CAMPAIGN SPOKESPERSON: Going to have to answer for the threat that he continues to pose to American democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So that is out of step in many ways with a new \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  poll that finds a plurality of swing voters in key battleground states, so called deciders, because we know in prior elections, you know, several thousands, several tens of thousands voters have made these decisions. So this group believes that Trump, not Biden, would do a better job handling threats to democracy by 11 points.\par \par \uc2 So let's bring in Marc Thiessen, former Chief Speechwriter for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , AEI Senior Fellow and Fox News Contributor. Marc, it's always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Especially on these big days. You have advised presidents in the past in these moments, but that is clearly going to be a big theme. It seems like, you know, save yourselves from the fall of democracy is one of the big thrusts of the Biden campaign.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: We'll see only thrust, because the only play he has, he is the most unpopular president since World War II. Sixty-one percent of Americans say that his presidency is a failure. He's got double digit disapproval on everything. Twenty percent inflation, cumulative inflation on people, wars raging on two continents, a border that's in chaos. What else does he have to run on?\par \par \uc2 The only way he can win is by attacking \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and say, hey, we'll look at the alternative. But now we find with a swing state poll, that voters -- that one thing that does show is that voters think threats to democracy are very important, but they think Biden is a bigger threat to democracy than --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Then why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Well, because think about this. The Democrats -- what's one of the interesting findings in that poll was that the Americans said that -- the voters said that they believe the guardrails of our system, system of checks and balances that we have would protect any one Democrat -- dictator from ever getting power in this country.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats are threatening the guardrails. So, you know, they go and they wage law fair against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , 91 indictments at the federal state and local level. They tried to bankrupt him with civil suits. They've submitted -- some Democrats tried to use the Constitution to keep kick them off the ballot.\par \par \uc2 And oh, by the way, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  runs around boasting that he circumvented the Supreme Court to do student loans, and then they wonder why Democratic people think that he's a bigger threat to democracy than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is. They're attacking the guardrails that protect our democracy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's so fascinating, you know, when you look at Trump's courtroom appearances, when he will come out into the hall and use those moments, right? I talked to Dennis Quaid recently about a very -- about a Reagan movie, actually, that he's starring in. And he had recently said that he was going to vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 So I asked him about it, because he said that he had voted for Democrats in the past and, you know, was -- had voted for different people, depending on what he thought was important. But this is what he said when I asked him about it, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DENNIS QUAID, ACTOR: I am supporting Trump this time around. But, you know, for a number of reasons, but probably the most is just the weaponization of our judicial system. And, you know, I see it as a constitutional issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, when the Biden teams look at these numbers, they've got -- they have to get to a point where they think they overshot their mark on this.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes. Well, the lawfare has backfired. I mean, but think about -- so I was very critical of January 6, and as you were everybody else was on the air. On January 6, all the guardrails held, right? The electors did their jobs, the states did their jobs, all the Trump judges voted against him on January 6.\par \par \uc2 The courts did their jobs. The vice president did his job, Congress did his job. Our system work and checked what happened--\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Under tremendous pressure.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: They are trying to dismantle the guardrails. They are weaponizing the justice system. They are taking things that used to be nonpartisan and turning them into partisan tools. And people -- whether you like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or dislike \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that's offensive to a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 And it's taking swing voters, people who are not necessarily pro-Trump, and moving them into Trump's camp because they see it as a threat.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I thought it was interesting this week, Andrew Cuomo, the former governor of New York, came out and said that neither of the real estate trial and the Stormy Daniels trial he's had he's never won. They're such small like, you know, legal issues. They never would have been brought if it weren't brought against him.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: He's 100 percent right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: From Governor Cuomo, so interesting. All right. Marc, thank you.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you twice today, hopefully see you again today. All right.\par \par \uc2 So the issue of immigration is also expected to play front and center tonight in the wake of a recent slew of heinous crimes that have been committed against young girls and a mother of five, by people who came here illegally. That's just a fact. Former New York Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And, Biden, I would actually say, start taking care of our people. Um, you're here taking care of everything outside, taking care of foreign affairs. I'm like, I know foreign affairs are important, but you have to look at us from the inside. You're helping other countries but we are basically drowning -- our own boat is sinking. We can't save other people and other boats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So there is Atlanta. You see President Biden hugging some supporters in the crowd there, people chanting four more years. Good-sized crowd outside and they are cheering loudly. No doubt this is encouraging for the President as he gets ready for his debate tonight against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's going to be a very intense matchup, a very important night in the process for this election. You know, we today we have polls that show 16 percent say they will make up their mind tonight. Those independent voters, those people who still haven't decided will be looking for an argument that is compelling to them on both sides of this equation.\par \par \uc2 So an important night for America as this election really gets underway, full throttle tonight in this very important showdown between foreign President Trump and current President Biden. A unique experience for Americans to watch a president that they know and lived under against a president that they know and lived under. So very important high stakes for both tonight as we continue to watch this.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, while we keep on this live shot, let's talk a little bit about the migrant crisis at the southern border, which is clearly very important to voters. And so, we hope that it will be a big issue this evening in this debate, which you can watch right here on Fox live from beginning to end. We will have full team coverage for you.\par \par \uc2 So this all happens at this same moment right now, there is a funeral happening, as the mother and the family, and the grandparents of Jocelyn Nungaray, a 12-year-old child. The suspects in her murder are two Venezuelan men who were here illegally. Former GOP Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is standing by. We'll keep this shot open on one side, but let's bring in Nate Foy, who is covering this heartbreaking funeral.\par \par \uc2 Will her name come up tonight, is one of the questions. Will it be brought up by the Trump, by President Trump, perhaps? He has spoken to the family? What will President Biden say if he is confronted on the issue of the funeral that is happening today for 12-year-old Jocelyn Nungaray.\par \par \uc2 Let's go live to Nate Foy who is there on the scene. Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. Just a devastating day here, the service has concluded, and you can see a horse drawn carriage carrying the casket of 12-year-old Jocelyn Nungaray is arriving at her burial site about a half mile down the road behind me right now. The service again has finished, people just filed out. There's a lot of anger and sadness here today.\par \par \uc2 And I spoke to specifically Texas Lieutenant Governor \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2 , who just announced to me that Texas lawmakers will enter produce new legislation in honor of Jocelyn. They're going to call it Jocelyn Law that will require people charged with murder in the state of Texas to be held without bail. And he said that he promised Jocelyn's mother that he would get it done in her honor.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you mentioned, both of the illegal migrants from Venezuela charged with capital murder in Jocelyn's death are being held on $10 million bail right now. Here's Houston Mayor John Whitmire speaking at the service that just ended.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN WHITMIRE, MAYOR OF HOUSTON: -- that hold you responsible. They're not listening to us. We (inaudible) want to do this. (Inaudible) and make a difference in the entirety of our lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Right now, as you look live, you see the mother, Alexis Nungaray preparing to bury the daughter that she had when she was just 15 years old. She's now 27. Hundreds of people showed up today including the first lady of Texas Cecilia Abbott. Texas Governor Greg Abbott said today that Jocelyn would still be alive, if not for President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's open border policies.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol arrested and released both of the migrant suspects in El Paso this year. And one of them, as you know, Martha was wearing an ICE provided GPS ankle bracelet during the murder. Here's Texas Congressman Tony Gonzales reacting to this tragic story and the others across the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): Our hearts are broken in Texas for Jocelyn's family and this can happen anywhere. Today we're talking about Jocelyn and Texas, but very soon it is already happening in your community. I guarantee it. Wherever you are, it is already happening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: absolutely heartbreaking to see the pictures on your screen right now, as Jocelyn's family and the other mourners today prepared to say their final goodbyes. After this wraps up, local business owner Jim McIngvale will host a celebration of life where people in this community will honor the 12-year-old girl taking far too soon. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is a horrific, horrific crime. And these two people, these suspects should never have been in this country and able to carry out this crime. This is Jocelyn's body, 12 years old, this child, left her home and was accosted by these two individuals, these two suspects in this case.\par \par \uc2 She as her mother has said should have had a long life ahead of her. She wants like playing the cello. She had braces, she was 12.\par \par \uc2 So this is the situation that we're watching in this country, as we see millions of people pour across the border. Most of them do come here for a better life. But the problem is that it's so chaotic, so unorganized, open crossings all over the place. You heard Jocelyn's mother, who you see right in the middle of that screen, talking about it, saying this can't happen. We can't lose our children like this. We have to have a safe country for the children of America to grow up in.\par \par \uc2 And now this young mother has lost her 12-year-old child. It is just absolutely heartbreaking. And as a string, I could name five, six other cases that also did not need to happen because the people who committed them should not have been in the country.\par \par \uc2 One of these individuals who allegedly did this to Jocelyn said that he was fleeing Venezuela because he feared for his life. Think about that. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is with me as well. He's a former New York Republican Congressman and former candidate for governor.\par \par \uc2 Lee, this is an important topic, and I really hope that we hear about this tonight. It's not covered much on the other networks, so I hope it was on their list of questions because it is absolutely heartbreaking. And people do feel unsafe.\par \par \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R), FORMER NEW YORK CONGRESSMAN: Martha, when you say that you could think of five or six other cases, the craziest part is you're just talking about the last week or two.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: I mean, Jocelyn, and Rachel -- talking about from 12-year-old to 13-year-old, to single mom, stories breaking today out of Syracuse, New York, story a few days ago out of Albany, New York. It's happening all across the entire country. And Americans want to honor the lives of these victims. They're praying for their family, and they want action.\par \par \uc2 But right now for President Biden and this White House, and for his campaign, they don't want to talk about it. And to the extent it comes up today, the game plan on the Biden side is to talk about how they're taking action to secure the border, that the screening and vetting has never been better, and that border crossings are down. You know their spin.\par \par \uc2 But the fact remains that you have these victims like Jocelyn whose families right now are mourning the loss of their loved ones. I don't expect President Biden to bring up any of their names. I think President Trump should and quite frankly, the moderators should know better to bring it up proactively. And we should talk about it.\par \par \uc2 And from a policy standpoint, President Trump has a record to talk about his four years of his action that he took to secure the border, building a wall, Remain in Mexico, Title 42, ending catch and release to what we had from the first moment President Biden comes into office to roll these policies back.\par \par \uc2 And anything that President Biden says that he would do if he was given another term. President Trump should hold his feet to the fire, I'm sure he will, to the fact that if you wanted to do it, he could do a lot of this stuff right now with a stroke of a pen. So I think this is an issue that has to be discussed. And for a lot of voters, it's their number one issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, this is the kind of thing that President Trump could pivot from a question and -- and talk about. You know, it's going to be interesting to see how this plays out tonight with these muted microphones and everything.\par \par \uc2 I mean, on debate, you should be able to hear people on both sides. They should, you know, occasionally be able to jump in, and -- and challenge. So I'm very curious to see how this plays out with this muted mic situation.\par \par \uc2 But we have on the right hand side of your screen, former President Trump about to take off from Palm Beach, Florida. And he will fly to Atlanta, where he will prepare for the last few hours before this debate, which gets underway tonight at 9:00 PM Eastern. And on the right hand side of your screen, you have a woman who he called and spoke to about the death of her young daughter Jocelyn, whose funeral is now underway in Houston, Texas.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think about what Alejandro Mayorkas said when he was asked about these situations, these deaths of these young children, and also Rachel Morin, the mother of five. And he said basically, it's the criminal who's to blame for the crime, you know, not the policy, essentially.\par \par \uc2 Is that a response that gets us anywhere, Lee?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: No. And the fact is, if President Trump was in office, these murderers, these rapes, these kidnappings, we're reading about a 12-year olds of 13-year olds of young mothers, none of them would be happening. These people are coming in because of this open border that was opened up by design.\par \par \uc2 And this plan to bring in all these millions of people over the course last few years, their plans to bring in millions more over the course of the years to come, have the secondary impacts on quality of life, of rising crime. These criminals are going to continue to organize and commit gang activity, impacts on budgets that have to get cut on education, on health care, on law enforcement, to pay for the increased cost, and all the goodies that are given out by the federal government, by sanctuary states, by sanctuary cities.\par \par \uc2 And the last thing I'll point out is, on top of it all, they want to give these illegals the right to vote. And this isn't some theory, it's happening right now in Washington, DC. They passed a law inside New York City that fortunately was overturned, but the Democrats are challenging it to the highest level.\par \par \uc2 So what they're trying to do by design is give these incentives rewards for coming in illegally, giving money that's hard working taxpayer dollars, and Americans who have to see a story like this every single day now with different victims, are wondering when are you, the person sitting in the office right now, President Biden, going to fight for me and my family.\par \par \uc2 It's a message to the entire Democratic Party to blue state governors and blue state mayors. They all need to step up. And they're not, voters should kick them out in November.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I've heard people on other networks say, oh, this is fear mongering and even chuckle about these situations that it's, you know, everyone's imagination. And you hear Joe Manchin today on CNBC basically say, this isn't the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that I knew, you know? I want the old \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He's gone too far to the left, we have to fix the border.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, pragmatic politicians understand that that this is this is a losing proposition.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Yeah. And Americans are filled with fear, and to try to just tell Americans that their fears are something that they just need to -- it's just a perception. They need to get over it. Everything's fine. That is not the solution that Americans who are tuning in tonight undecided are looking for from these two candidates.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , former congressman and former gubernatorial candidate, good to have you with us today. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Obviously, we got a big night ahead and we very much look forward to it. We're going to have complete stern coverage of the debate this evening. And we will join you for our special coverage tonight. I'll see you on "Special Report." And then, Bret and I will join you at 8:00 PM Eastern with a great team that you see across the board there, wonderful group that's going to be gathered to watch this, give analysis out of it. And all of that happens on the simulcast at 9:00 Eastern.\par \par \uc2 So stick around just stay here all day long, and into the night. We've got a lot of coverage for you this evening. So we're very excited. Look forward to seeing you later. Have a good afternoon. We'll see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240628ek6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Document On Abortion Inadvertently Issued; Possible Migrant Terror Ties And Alejandro Mayorkas Visit; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  And President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Differing Debate Preparations; Campaigns Push Economic Plans Ahead Of First Debate; Republican Report On Hunter Biden Laptop; House Committee Accuses CIA Of Collusion With Biden; Republicans Tee Up Resolution To Arrest Garland; Anticipation Builds For Biden-Trump Showdown; Chinese Government Constructing Massive New Seaport In Peru; \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2 , American Imprisoned In Russia, To Stand Trial For Espionage Charges; President Biden And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Prepare For Their First Presidential Debate Of 2024; New Poll Shows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Leading President Biden In Five Of Seven Swing States; Two Illegal Immigrants Arrested For Strangulation And Murder Of 12-Year-Old Girl In Texas\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Shannon Bream, Matt Finn, Grady  Trimble, Brit Hume, Gillian Turner, Alex Hogan, Mollie Hemingway, Howard  Kurtz \par \uc2 8,105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And thoughtful.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You are insane.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I am so proud and honored.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You earned it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: This is the greatest untold story of all.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Don't say it. Don't say it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: So basically, Greg, Jesse has to get an award every time he speaks?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, you called it.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I just wanted to finish the sentence.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, for the second time in a little more than two years. A major \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  ruling on abortion appears to have been leaked to the public ahead of its official release. This time, the High Court is apparently poised to allow emergency abortions in Idaho when a pregnant patient's health is at serious risk.\par \par \uc2 This happens as we await a major decision on presidential immunity from the justices who did issue some rulings today.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" Shannon Bream takes a look tonight. Good evening, Shannon. What do we know about this abortion case online posting?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes. So, the court says today there was briefly a document that was posted on the website. But a spokesman for the court says it was an inadvertent uploading of a document to the opinion itself out of Idaho's case has not been released yet.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg News did capture the document, it appears to show that the court will not actually rule on the merits of the case, but send it back to a lower court for now.\par \par \uc2 Now, if, big if, that winds up mirroring the actual opinion. It avoids a ruling on a major hot topic in an election year but gives the Biden administration a temporary win on procedural grounds because, Bret, it will broaden access to emergency abortion, at least in the short term.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We did get a decision today on how the Biden administration is allowed to interact with social media companies and it's really a big win for the Biden White House.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is again though on a technicality. Here's what happens. The court said that plaintiffs who said the federal government was unduly influencing social media company on things like postings like about COVID-19 and the 2020 election. The court said they simply didn't have a legal right or the standing to sue.\par \par \uc2 Justice Barrett wrote for the majority essentially saying there wasn't enough of a connection between the government's conversations with various social media platforms and harm to the plaintiffs moving forward.\par \par \uc2 Now, Justice Alito wrote for the dissenters. He said, that high government officials placed unrelenting pressure on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  in particular, to suppress Americans free speech. He chided the court the majority for not addressing what he called a threat to the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  praised the decision. She says it'll allow the administration to continue working with tech companies to flag content they deem a threat to safety saying this "important work will continue."\par \par \uc2 Now, numerous conservative groups, Bret, as you would imagine, are continuing to vow that they're going to fight they say. It's dangerous to allow any presidential administration to decide what it deems miss or disinformation and then to be able to pressure social media companies.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, obviously, we have other cases to come. But the biggest is the Trump immunity case.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're still waiting. We could get it tomorrow as early as 10: a.m. Eastern, we will get opinions 10:00 a.m. Eastern Thursday and Friday. It's possible this term bleeds over into next week, improbable but possible. But it's also possible we get that decision, Bret, on debates day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's crazy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And that would really make things interesting.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And just back to the first story. We never really got to the bottom of the leak pre-Dobbs decision. And now the question is this happening again?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, more than two years later, we are still waiting for a decision there about who may have been responsible. The court decided to handle that internally. They didn't bring in the DOJ, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . They tell us it's an ongoing investigation. If there were updates, they'll let us know.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Shannon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, new concerns over illegal immigrants who may have ties to terrorism. More than 400 migrants from Central Asia and elsewhere have been flagged because they were brought in by a network affiliated with the \uc2 ISIS\uc2  terror group. This comes on a day when Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas visits a key border sector.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Matt Finn is in Tucson right now and it was with the Secretary today. Good evening, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. We asked Secretary Mayorkas about that reporting, the 400 migrants who were smuggled into this country via an \uc2 ISIS\uc2  linked group.\par \par \uc2 However, today, DHS and Secretary Mayorkas are disputing that those migrants have any ties to \uc2 ISIS\uc2 . Here's our exchange.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: DHS confirms that at least 400 migrants with potential \uc2 ISIS\uc2  ties recently crossed into the United States.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY, HOMELAND SECURITY: That is incorrect.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice over): Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas, shooting down his own department's information during a border visit today.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  senior official tells Fox News it's identified more than 400 subjects of concern, who came to the U.S. through an \uc2 ISIS\uc2  affiliated human smuggling network.\par \par \uc2 DHS takes issue with the initial reporting saying the vast majority were not subjects of concern. More than 150 people from Eastern Europe and Central Asia were arrested but 50 remain at large.\par \par \uc2 Mayorkas disputes the question of whether they have potential \uc2 ISIS\uc2  ties.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: We do not have identified 400 people with potential \uc2 ISIS\uc2  ties. And let me again assure you that individuals who are identified to have those ties would pose a concern to us from a public safety and security perspective, and they would be priorities for detained -- detention and removal.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice over): However, Biden administration critics still believe the current vetting system is weak and a threat to national security.\par \par \uc2 CHAD WOLF\uc2 , FORMER ACTING HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: We don't know who they are. We don't know where they came from. And we don't know their intentions.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice over): During his first trip to a border state since February, Mayorkas, says there's been a 40 percent drop in migrant encounters here over the past three weeks to about 2,400 a day. He says the President's recent executive order limiting migrant asylum is working. But Congress needs to pass comprehensive immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: These actions are changing the calculus for those considering crossing our border.\par \par \uc2 Still, as I will continue to make clear, they are no substitute for congressional action.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): Now, at least one Border Patrol agent here on the ground tells us that it's too early to tell what type of impact President Biden's executive orders on immigration are having.\par \par \uc2 Also, I followed up and asked Secretary Mayorkas if he understands that many Americans just aren't buying that this border is secure. He punted that question, said he's answered it before and would not answer it to me, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matt Finn in Arizona. Matt, thanks. That issue of immigration will likely come up in this first debate. We're just a day away from the first debate of the presidential election cycle, which will be simulcast right here on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we look at the differing preparation styles for President Biden and former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has that storyline from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Former President Trump says he got good reviews during debates last cycle when he was more tempered so he's going for that again tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 And Biden's team has reportedly been teeing up jabs to get him angry about his 2020 election loss and January 6th, but Biden also wants to appear as the adult in the room. So, some of the psychological warfare is playing out ahead of time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The DNC says it put up billboards around Atlanta welcoming former President Trump as a convicted felon as a sign of southern hospitality, finding ways to get under Trump's skin, a key goal for Team Biden staffers, according to an NBC report, but if past is prologue, the President will also have to mind his own temper.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- radical left --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Would you shut up, men?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Listen, who is on your list, Joe? Who's on your list?\par 