from House Republicans finds that CIA contractors and the 2020 Biden campaign conspired to discredit the Hunter Biden laptop story.\par \par \uc2 Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram is here with details tonight. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 The House Committee on the weaponization of the government says that -- says there was collusion by former government or Intel officials to influence the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 They claimed the Hunter Biden laptop was Russian disinformation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): 15 days before an election. 51 former Intel officials signed a statement with their former titles by their name, a statement that was prompted by the Biden campaign, a statement signed by some who were on contract with the CIA.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): House Speaker Mike Johnson says the officials, "betrayed" the public trust. But Democrats dismissed the allegations.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM HIMES (D-CT): That like so much of the Jim Jordan productions is a high decibel, zero calorie, nothing burger. If you read that report, what they are saying is the CIA should have stopped these 51 people from expressing their political beliefs.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The CIA says the officers did not speak for the agency. Republicans don't trust the government.\par \par \uc2 REP. TROY NEHLS (R-TX): They are providing cover for this guy. Maybe that's their job, because they work for the guy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Now, Republicans are looking at legislation to revoke security clearances. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad, where do we stand with the House voting to hold Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  in inherent contempt of Congress?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Well, a vote could come this week. Now, inherent contempt is where the House exercises inherent power. It would authorize the sergeant at arms to arrest \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  for not providing information. But some GOP members are skeptical.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JOHN DUARTE\uc2  (R-CA): Just visualize what we're talking about going over and arresting the attorney general in his office, and bringing back to a small holding cell, but you can't understand how this would actually work if executed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now, Republicans want the house to arrest Garland for not complying with a subpoena. The House demands audio tapes from President Biden's interview in the classified documents case. The House has not used inherent contempt since the 1930s. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Watch that, Chad. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Progressive Squad member \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  was defeated in last night's primary election in New York. Moderate George Latimer is calling for more civility, following the contentious election.\par \par \uc2 Colorado Congresswoman \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  won the primary in the fourth District of Colorado, just after deciding not to seek re-election in her current more competitive district.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE LATIMER (D-NY), CONGRESSIONAL NOMINEE: We have to look at the arguments of the far right and the far left, and say you cannot destroy this country with your rhetoric and your arguments.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2  (D-NY): I want to make an apology -- public apology for, you know, sometimes, using foul language. I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LAUREN BOEBERT\uc2  (R-CO): The people have spoken. They do not want the UNA Party (PH) representing them. But what you do want is a unified Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's digest all of that. We bring in Fox News Chief Political Analyst, Brit Hume. Brit, good evening, you know, we don't often read tons in the primaries. But that \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  loss as incumbent Congress -- congressman, in a primary, where \uc2 AIPAC\uc2 , the pack tied to Israel is a part of that. And he really argued that it was the part that made him lose was an interesting moment.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: It was, Bret. And I think that it's a danger of overstating \uc2 AIPAC\uc2 , which is, of course, a political action committee, American Israel, America-Israel political action committee, because he was down in the polls before \uc2 AIPAC\uc2  really got involved. The \uc2 AIPAC\uc2  spent some money left something on the order $14 million. And obviously, it didn't help him.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, this is a Democratic district in which he'd won before what twice. And he went down by what, 17-18 points. That's big. And it suggests that voters may not be in a mood anymore as much as they might want to been for these very noisy, somewhat radical politicians, who has he admitted use foul language and so on. That there may be a hope, maybe a sense here that what is -- what the guy that beat him, George Latimer is talking about more civility is something that people are starting to think about and care about.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, the \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 , one of his fellow Squad members, in the House of Representatives, is in some trouble in her race out in Missouri, which is thought to be neck and neck. And she is, you know, she is one out there several times before.\par \par \uc2 And I remember, this is within the Democratic Party. It's not as if, you know, Republicans are coming in here and making a difference that comes later if it comes at all.\par \par \uc2 But something is happening out there for sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HUME: And it appears a turn toward moderation, and civility, may be coming. To that point, some Trump backed candidates in South Carolina, in Utah, in Colorado, they did not win. And the more traditional candidates backed by, if you will, the establishment in those places, all won the primaries, which also seems like a shift in some of those dates.\par \par \uc2 HUME: I think so, Bret. And I thinking also that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gives signs that he's beginning to take notice of all this, you know, he is -- he is -- he is -- he is been moderate -- moderating his views on a number of issues on a number of candidates. And he is backing the establishment candidate in a number of places, including, as you've heard me say, Bret, most conspicuously his support for \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  to be the new Republican senator from the very, very blue state of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Trump clearly seems to recognize that he's better off with \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  than he would be from a Democrat, even though \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  has said he will not vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HUME: So, there is change in the air. Luminously to see how it all plays out, but I think we are seeing signs of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, I have covered a lot of elections. But you've covered many more than I have. And have you ever seen a president getting ready for a first debate, being prepping like this president is for so long, kind of hold away at Camp David?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, normally, you have -- you know, you do have to bone up to some extent to remember details. But what this president is going through is quite unusual. Because the president by the very nature of his or her job, his job so far, is cognizant of the details of these major issues.\par \par \uc2 You'd have to deal with them all the time. So, this level of preparation is unusual. And they probably know something other fact that, you know, Biden is pretty old and his memory is not so good. And people have seen that and they see how awkwardly he walks and all the rest of it.\par \par \uc2 So, they are obviously trying to get him mega prepped in the hope that he can get through this encounter with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and look like a -- you know, look like a vibrant man. But it will be able to do that. Of course, is I think one of the big things we're going to be watching for tomorrow night, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And you'll be part of that coverage, big coverage tomorrow here on Fox. Brit, as always, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, new worries over China's expanding influence close to the U.S. We'll have a report from the State Department.\par \par \uc2 And then, the trial of Wall Street Journal reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  begins in Russia.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sections of the Midwest are dealing with flooding from torrential rain since last week. Residents are also experiencing a heatwave now. Images show water overflowing farmland from eastern Nebraska and South Dakota to Iowa and Minnesota. The National Weather Service says some places have received up to 18 inches of rain. Hundreds of people were rescued, homes damaged. At least two people died after driving in flooded areas.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, outside of the U.S. and outside of this world, two \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronauts stuck on the International Space Station because of troubles with their Boeing Starliner spacecraft have an approximate return by date of July 20th. That's 45 days from their June 5th liftoff, which is how long Starliner can spend docked to the ISS. Astronauts Sunny Williams and Butch Wilmore's original stay was scheduled for only a week. A series of issues including recent helium leaks and thruster problems have pushed their homecoming back multiple times.\par \par \uc2 There are new signs tonight of China's growing influence in the western hemisphere. The communist government is constructing a massive facility in Peru. State Department correspondent Gillian Turner explains what this means tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: China's communist government is constructing a massive new $3.5 billion mega port in America's backyard. It's slated to launch later this year.\par \par \uc2 REP. GARY PALMER, (R-AL): In November, Xi Jinping will be in Peru to cut the ribbon on a major seaport that is built and designed to compete with our west coast seaports.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The port will be 60 feet deep and give China a foothold 4,500 miles from San Francisco. China's main goal, establish a direct trade route from Peru to Shanghai. But the State Department warns countries doing business with China should keep their eyes, quote, "wide open."\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER\uc2 , STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESMAN: If they enter into agreements with China, they ought to ensure that those agreements have transparency, that they are in the best interests of their governments, and most importantly in the people of their countries.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Biden administration officials have warned about China also using this mega port to plant military operatives, something they've already done at 100 or so other foreign ports according to "The Wall Street Journal". The Peruvian port is an extension of Chinese President Xi Jinping \uc2 Belt and Road Initiative\uc2 , known as BRI, an attempt to link his regime with the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 REP. RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI, (D-IL): The BRI fits squarely within the CCP's military. As we'll see, many BRI projects, especially deep water ports, are tailored for military use.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: This port will allow China to bypass other large ports already in existence in the U.S. and Mexico and bring them a step closer to their ambition of dominating the Panama Canal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Well, Secretary Blinken has said that the U.S. relationship with China is the most consequential in the world, and his actions lately do reflect that. He last met with Xi Jinping back in April, and we do anticipate him traveling to the region again over the coming weeks. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian Turner live at the State Department. Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The trial of "Wall Street Journal" reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  is underway in Russia. He's accused of spying for the U.S. Correspondent Alex Hogan reports from London.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Jailed American journalist \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  on the first day of his espionage trial appearing in court in a glass cage with a shaved head. It's been 15 months since "The Wall Street Journal" reporter's arrest while on assignment in Yekaterinburg, Russia. The White House once again today calling this a sham trial.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: Evan has never been employed by the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . Evan is not a spy.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Russia accuses the 32-year-old of working for the CIA and gathering secret information about a company building tanks for Russia's war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MIKAEL OZDOYEV, RUSSIAN PROSECUTOR: His illegal activities were committed in a highly discreet manner.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: With no proof provided, the U.S. embassy in Moscow today stated, "His case is not about evidence, procedural norms, or the rule of law. It is about the Kremlin using American citizens to achieve its political objectives." The closed-door trial means that aside from the media's brief chance to see Gershkovich before this morning's proceedings, journalists and family are barred from the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 SAM SILVERMAN, FRIEND: We already know how this is going to go. He's going to be convicted. This is hopefully leading towards a prisoner swap, but they are going to convict him of these bogus charges first.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Gershkovich faces up to 20 years behind bars if found guilty, which is likely given that Russian courts convict more than 99 percent of defendants. Russian president \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  said late last year, we want to make an agreement. That has not materialized yet.\par \par \uc2 DAN HOFFMAN, FORMER CIA STATION CHIEF: I have no doubt that there is a backchannel discussion going on now as we have in the past with Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): Gershkovich's next day in court is August 13th. The White House today also protesting the continued attention of former marine Paul Whelan who was arrested on espionage charges five-and-a-half years ago and sentenced to 16 years behind bars. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The former president of Honduras has been sentenced to 45 years in prison for drug crimes. \uc2 Juan Orlando Hernandez\uc2  was accused of conspiring with some of the largest traffickers in the world to send, get this, more than 400 tons of cocaine through his country to the U.S. He was convicted in March following a three-week jury trial.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the countdown to the debate tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Armored vehicles ram into the doors of Bolivia's government palace as President \uc2 Luis Arce\uc2  says the country is facing an attempted coup. Bolivian television tonight showed Arce confronting the general commander of the army who appears to be leading that rebellion in the palace hallway. The president named a number of new officials and a new army commander who then ordered troops to stand down. Watching that tonight.\par \par \uc2 Kenya's president says he will not sign into law a finance bill proposing new taxes. This comes a day after protesters stormed the parliament there in Kenya. Several people were fatally shot. It was the biggest assault on Kenya's government in decades. Yesterday, about 200 Kenyan police officers arrived in Haiti as part of a United Nations backed security force. The country has been dealing with deadly gang violence in Haiti.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Amsterdam, courtesy of Earth Cam. One of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 NATO\uc2 's ambassadors appoint outgoing Dutch Prime Minister \uc2 Mark Rutte\uc2  as the new Secretary General of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . He will be congratulated by President Biden and his \uc2 NATO\uc2  counterparts at a summit here in Washington, D.C., next month.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON, (R-WI): It doesn't matter if he drinks a whole gallon of energy drinks, he is not going to be able to match the acumen and the readiness of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2 , (R) FORMER ILLINOIS REPRESENTATIVE: How many families have been torn apart by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Everybody knows somebody. Maybe you're in that family, too, where your parents can't talk to you or you can't talk to them because there is a different governing philosophy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That is all the pregame, Speaker of the House. There you saw Republican Representative \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  who endorsed President Biden today.\par \par \uc2 This as some new polls are out. Interesting, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " out with swing state polls, and the former president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leading in five of the seven swing state polls, several of them outside the margin of error. If you look at people who look at the debates and to make a difference and how they will vote, all voters, about 30 percent say it will be extremely important to their vote. You see the breakdown with Democrats and Republicans there. And then A.P. out with a new poll, are you going to listen to the debate or watch the debate or some part of it? Extremely or very likely, 37 percent, somewhat likely 27 percent. Six in ten, roughly, say they will watch some piece of this debate tomorrow night. That's a lot of folks if we are at 330 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," "Axios" political reporter Stef Kight, and FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz. Howie, I can't remember a time where one debate was this anticipated at this crucial time even though it's so early in the process.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes. No, it's absolutely unprecedented. And you know, Biden is doing the traditional mock debates. Trump's people don't want to overprepare him. They feel like his prep is doing interviews. Of course, Biden shielded from the press.\par \par \uc2 I have a contrarian take, which is Biden will come out reasonably cogent, energetic, and will clear the bar set by months of Trump and allies saying he can't string two sentences together, he's confused, he's senile. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if he comes out and is disciplined, and he was when I sat down with him, and you've had this experience, too. He can be very serious and substantive when he is not taking shots at the other person. And for those Republicans, independents who feel, like, I don't like when he says crazy stuff, or I remember the chaos, that might enable them to put aside their doubts and vote for him when perhaps they were not going to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's interesting. It is a reverse take. And I heard that today also, this format, Mollie, with the mics being muted after a two-minute and one-minute rebuttal with no audience in a studio actually may go to Trump's benefit somehow in that he is not going to be like he was in the first debate in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Right. You get the feeling these were things requested by the Biden team.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thinking they were going to benefit from it.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: And we have reporting that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he just kind of agreed to whatever they put out there. But it might actually help him. We have a really unique situation in that we have never before had two previous presidents on this kind of stage at this point in the process. So we can just compare them. We can compare their policy records. That's something that's a huge advantage for Trump according to polls and the situation with the economy, the border, foreign policy, on pretty much every metric Americans seem to support the policies of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and they don't like the policies.\par \par \uc2 But we also have something interesting and different happening with the personality. So in 2020, Trump was much less light than Biden. But Biden had had a really big drop in his favorability ratings with many more people not finding him in a favorable way and many more people -- many fewer people saying that they don't find him favorable. And then with Trump he is actually improved a little bit with this new way of not being totally in everybody's faces all the time. So even on the personality issue, it might be to Trump's benefit.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stef?\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": One thing I'm really interested in is how many people actually tune in and how that changes the race moving forward. In the past there has been a question around how much debates actually change how American voters perceive the candidates, how they vote. But one thing that I'm interested in, obviously, people like us have been paying very close attention for a while now. We are in the middle of the 2024 cycle where we stand. But there are many Americans who have yet to tune in yet. And whether we see more American voters tune in and actually start thinking about how they are going to vote earlier on in the process and how that changes the dynamic of the campaigns and how we see the polling potentially fluctuate after that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Maher continuing his tour. He wrote that book. He has been on "GUTFELD!" and other places. He was at the \uc2 Aspen Institute\uc2  today saying that the left essentially has lost its mind and can't communicate with other Republicans. Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL MAHER, COMEDIAN: \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , you know, not my favorite Republican or person, but did the right thing. That's as good as it gets. If you can't talk to those people, you're just abandoning the country to half the people, who are even worse, who are going to vote for Trump this next time, and they are, and he very likely will win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He is obviously not a Trump fan, but saying that, Howie, this dynamic has changed in the country. And you look at the \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  race, you look at other races that may be canaries in the coal mine about a shift in the country.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: One of the reasons that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won in 2020, beside the pandemic, is that he was perceived as a more moderate Democrat. He had been around forever, seemed like a safe character. The far left in his party has made it seem like at times they have lost their mind, that they are more interested in battles over pronouns, and you mentioned \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2 . And it, I think, has put the president in kind of a box, and particularly the opposition to his handling of the wars that he didn't start but is trying to preside over, particularly the situation with Israel and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 And that's the problem with being an incumbent is you get to fly around on the fancy plane, but anything that's going wrong in the world can be played laid at your doorstep.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It's not just the fringe, though. You also have a situation where a lot of people in the Democrat Party are engaged in lawfare against their political opponents. Bill Maher has also talked about that and how dangerous that is to prosecute political opponents rather than work through the ballot box. And the fringe is a problem, but so is just the average Democrat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Another Republican who has backed President Biden is former Georgia Lieutenant Governor Geoff Duncan, saying it's through the prism term of how you look at the economy, how you perceive it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF DUNCAN, (R) FORMER GEORGIA LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR: There is two sides. Some people wake up today and it's the worst economy they have ever faced. They can't afford rent or houses or groceries. Some are waking up in the greatest economy ever because their houses are worth more and their 401(k)s are worth more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is a feel thing. And so far in the polls we haven't seen people feeling great about this economy.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: That's the biggest hurdle for Biden and his campaign. And that's what they have been trying to change the narrative around for months now. They feel like they have good economic data to present to voters. But every poll seems to show, again, that people aren't feeling it personally. And you can point to all kinds of job growth numbers, but at the end of the day it's inflation and how much just living day-to-day is costing average Americans that is going to make or break this election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. I think the big thing will be how much is this substance in this debate, specific substance, and how much is digging at each other and try to tweak the other guy.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: I think there will be a healthy mixture, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Of substance, hopefully there will be.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, the diverging media coverage of the border crisis, and then a heroic rescue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So what do these people do? What are they doing now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Which people?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The folks, the 11 million, 20 million, whatever you want to deport.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A lot of them are committing crimes like murdering the 12-year-old girl in Houston.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In Texas, undocumented immigrants were 37.1 percent less likely to be convicted of a crime.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Tell that to the survivors of the young leader killed and --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm just giving the numbers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What do you tell the parents?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: Our message to the families is, of course, our hearts are with them. The safety and security of the American public is indeed our highest priority.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, we have seen a number of these high profile cases and we have covered them here on FOX. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " covered it this way, "Killing a Texas girl becomes new immigration flash point. The killing of 12-year-old Jocelyn Nungaray in Houston has become the latest crime seized on by Republicans to attack President Biden over his immigration policies." Here is A.P., "Two men arrested in strangulation of 12-year-old Houston girl whose body was found in a creek." Not noted that they were illegal immigrants who entered the U.S. in March.\par \par \uc2 We're back with the panel. Howie, we see this a lot, but ahead of a debate and the framing of this story that Republicans are pouncing on it, it's happening.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: It can't be terrible on its own. They said Republicans are exploiting it. Well, these high profile crimes are really troubling. It talks about a porous border. And look, everybody sees through it, Bret, when the White House a day before the debate trots out Mayorkas who says 40 percent reduction since Biden closed the border. That still leaves 2,400 migrants coming across a day, which is appalling.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, what I don't understand is, why didn't \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , knowing this is an albatross for him and his party, do this a year ago? We kept hearing, he doesn't have the authority, he doesn't have the authority. Well, then a magic wand, I have the authority. And it looks blatantly political five months before the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I should point out, Mollie, that was the only story about this that "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " did. The only one.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: You might remember back in 2020 around this time when shortly after the George Floyd murder, the killing of George Floyd, that was super big news for the media for the remainder of the summer and going into the election. You have here all these Americans who are being killed by illegal immigrants, and you just don't see the same lack of -- or you don't see the same concern from a lot of people in the media. But it is one of these things that has become a really big issue for voters.\par \par \uc2 And the big problem, I think, for the Biden administration is it's directly a result of Biden administration policies. He ran in 2020 on a pledge to open things up, to reverse all of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's border policies. And he did it. And it's kind of similar to what's happening with the economy. People know the economy is bad in part because after all of the COVID relief, the Biden administration pushed for $2 trillion in more stimulus, which led to inflation. So there are problems that are directly related to Biden and his governance that he is going to have to deal with in this campaign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And clearly Stef, the Biden administration realizes the vulnerability on immigration. They've looked at the polls. Our latest poll has him going up slightly on approval on immigration to 35, but still really underwater on the disapprove-approve. Trying to do something will probably hang a lot on the Senate negotiation on the bipartisan bill that never came to be. But it's still a vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Absolutely. It's been really interesting. I covered immigration with Trump and this entire time under Biden, and it's been fascinating to see the way the White House has shifted its strategy when it comes to the border. When you go back and remember the things that Mayorkas and Biden was saying about immigration when they started the administration, it's a very long way from where they are now.\par \par \uc2 And the most recent policy that Biden did announce using an executive order, using the exact same legal authority that Trump used in some of his most high-profile immigration rules, it's clear that Biden know that's needs to do something about this now. I think the timing is intentional as well. The timing was before the debate, that he has something to point to on stage. It's before the convention. It also is before the summertime where we typically see large numbers of migrants coming across the border. And it also gives time for the actual policy to be worked out on the border and hopefully to keep those numbers low through the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I just want to play this one soundbite from our interpret report Hillary Vaughn. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We know migrants oftentimes, according to data, according to police departments, are often less likely to commit crime than natural born citizens.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Documented immigrants commit far fewer crimes than anyone else.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JERRY NADLER\uc2 , (D-NY): Immigrants, legal or illegal, commit crimes at a rate far lower than native born Americans. Those are the facts.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Aren't the crimes preventable, though, if these people don't have a right to be here in the first place?\par \par \uc2 NADLER: I said what I'm going to say.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's all I have on that, so anyway, we keep on asking the questions. Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How are you going to replace a house but not a life?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mesa, Arizona, real estate agent Nick Reeves being called a hero after rescuing a dog from a house on fire. You can see the flames there. Reeves was showing another house. Clients nearby, they saw this other house and then saw the smoke, and flames jumped into action. He realized there was a dog inside. After jumping the fence, seeing a pet relief area. Reeves says he thinks a lot of people in that situation would have done the same thing. I'm not so sure. Nice job rescuing the dog.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, you bet. Complete coverage of the hours before the CNN presidential debate, plus analysis, everybody you want to hear from.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. We're going to have the countdown clock pretty soon, I'm sure. Here comes Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240627ek6q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump Gag Order Modified And Classified Materials Hearing; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ...\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Jeff Paul, Aishah Hasnie, Kelly  Saberi, Garrett Tenney \par \uc2 7,955 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Donald Trump Gag Order Modified And Classified Materials Hearing; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Campaign Ad Criticizes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Economic Policy; Hillary Clinton Warns Against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Strategy On Abortion; Vehicle Sales Disrupted After Cyberattack; Sen. Roger Marshall (R-KS), Is Being Interviewed About The Need To Secure The Border, Citing Drug, And People Smuggling; Sen. Michael Bennet (D-CO), Is Being Interviewed About Immigration, Focusing On Maintaining A Well-Maintained Border, And A Lawful Immigration System; Derailment Likely Caused By Overheated Wheel Bearing; Defense Leadership Concerned Over Weapons Deliveries; Courts Rule Ultra- Orthodox Required To Serve; Sen. Michael Bennet (D-CO) And Sen. Roger Marshall (R-KS) Interviewed On Bipartisan Efforts To Address Drought In U.S. And Chinese Purchase Of U.S.\par \par \uc2  Farmland Near Military Bases; \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  Agrees To Settle Lawsuit Over Leaking Of Confidential Tax Records Belonging To Former President Trump, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , And Other Wealthy Americans; Justice Department Reaches Plea Deal With Julian Assange; President Biden And President Trump Prepare For Their First Presidential Debate Of 2024\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Here's a good one, the late host of "Jeopardy" and the American treasurer Alex Trebek is getting his own stamp. It happened this past Friday. The stamp says this, this naturalized U.S. citizen is the host of the quiz show "Jeopardy."\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's cute.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. Well, letters that were mailed in, that'll be really good. That's it for us. Have a great night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, Jesse. Just to clarify, can you tell Greg that we don't do editorial meetings at Hooters?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Exactly, not at Hooters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But the wings are great. Thanks, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 All right. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Two days to go. And \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  is offering President Biden advice on dealing with former President Trump during this week's debate.\par \par \uc2 Government investigators issued a scathing report on the disastrous train derailment in East Palestine, Ohio, and our Common Ground segment deals with finding a bipartisan solution to food costs, water woes and security issues.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is now free to comment on certain aspects of his New York criminal trial in which he was convicted on 34 felony counts. The judge in that case has just modified former President Trump's gag order ahead of the July 11th sentencing in that case, this comes on the third day of efforts by the former president's legal team to get rid of the classified documents case against him in Florida.\par \par \uc2 National Correspondent Jeff Paul is following that effort in Fort Pierce, Florida tonight. Good evening, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, good evening, Bret. Special Counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  was back in court today as Trump's defense team post Judge Aileen Cannon to throw out the evidence that was collected at Mar-a- Lago during that search back in 2022.\par \par \uc2 But most of the day, at least a good part portion of that early hearing, Judge Aileen Cannon spent a lot of time questioning Trump's defense team and really almost seemed skeptical at times of her argument.\par \par \uc2 Now they say basically, the search of Mar-a-Lago was unlawful on two main points. They say there was crucial information that wasn't given to the judge who signed off on the original search warrant. And we're now getting new images out of court documents showing what agents found during that search.\par \par \uc2 Trump's defense also argued the search itself was impermissibly large and that \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents didn't have a clear grasp of what they were looking for at Mar-a-Lago. They accused \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents of taking pictures of rooms that were not permitted to be in, like the first lady's room.\par \par \uc2 But Judge Cannon challenged paper is something that could be found anywhere in the house.\par \par \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 's team argued the search warrant covered the entire property because they knew the classified documents were there and that those documents had already been moved at least once.\par \par \uc2 On previous hearings, Trump's defense argued \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 's appointment was unconstitutional. And just yesterday, prosecutors pushed for a limited gag order saying that Trump had said things that put both law enforcement and \uc2 FBI\uc2  witnesses at risk.\par \par \uc2 However, today we're learning the Trump gag order in his New York hush money trial has been partially lifted, referencing the end of the trial. Judge Juan Merchan is allowing Trump to speak freely about the trial as long as it doesn't involve prosecutors, court staff or any of their family members.\par \par \uc2 Now back here in Florida, no decisions in any of these series of hearings that have happened over the last couple of days. The judge in this case doesn't normally rule from the bench so you could expect some sort of written order to be released in the coming days, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff Paul in Florida. Jeff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 As mentioned, we're a little more than two days away from the first scheduled debate of the presidential election cycle. It's a big one. President Biden continues his preparation at Camp David. The latest polling indicates the presumptive Republican nominee former President Trump is leading in several key battleground states. Advantages that could if the election were held today give him enough electoral votes to win.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Today, the Trump campaign said President Biden will probably be filled with Adderall during the debate, alleging he was also on it at the State of the Union. They also suggested that Biden might get a boost from CNN moderators.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Biden's allies put out word that Trump's claims cannot be trusted or even fact checked in real time, evidence that both sides are leery the other might have a good night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Team Trump appears to recognize the pitfalls of setting a low bar for bar Biden.\par \par \uc2 JASON MILLER\uc2 , DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: Many in the media are already prepared to give \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a participation trophy if he can simply stand upright for 90 minutes. We know that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that after taking an entire week off, he's going to be ready for this.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden allies also setting expectations that Trump might have a good night. Trump's former debate opponent \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  writing in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  op-ed, Mr. Biden starts from a disadvantage, being president isn't just a day job. It's an everything everywhere all at once job, Historically, that has led to weaker first debate performances for the incumbent.\par \par \uc2 Clinton predicts Trump will seize on voter unhappiness about the economy, offering a counterpoint from \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics that Trump's economic plan would exacerbate inflation.\par \par \uc2 But it appears Biden's strategy may focus less on refuting Trump's claims than on his character.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Because he's focused on revenge, and he has no plan to help the middle class.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Republicans seeing that as proof of Biden's vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ (R-FL): It doesn't surprise me that Biden's changing strategy because Bidenomics has clearly been a disaster.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , no fan of the former or current president predicts a Biden loss this November will come down to two unforced errors.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): One, $1.6 (ph) trillion, creating inflation and the other, basically, opening the border.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): As yet Team Biden's response amounts to a warning that Trump would be worse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: More voters think that Trump did a better job in office than Biden. Does that concern you?\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR (D-TX): It does. I do think that people have amnesia, they won't want to see tax increases that a Trump economy we'll bring.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The Biden campaign has been leaning into messaging that Trump is a convicted felon, but such a line during the debate could draw a personal counter punch now that the president's son also shares that title.\par \par \uc2 Today, Hunter's law license was suspended in Washington D.C. following his guilty verdict on gun charges, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight we continue to look at the major issues likely to play a major role in Thursday night's debate. Democrats hope abortion will mobilize the president's base to show up in November. Former President Trump is seen by many as either the hero or the villain in overturning Roe v. Wade two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Here's Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the extremist Republicans are coming for access to abortion and it is up to us to stop them.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President Biden surrogates are attacking Republicans across battleground states over abortion, hoping to mobilize voters as they did in 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is going to be one of the most decisive issues in the campaign.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Democrats claim former President Trump and Republicans want a nationwide abortion ban. Republicans allege the left supports abortion up until the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 The issue is sure to be a flashpoint at the first presidential debate since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Someone who debated Trump in 2016, former Secretary of State \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  warned in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  this week, Mr. Trump will most likely say he wants to leave abortion to the States. He hopes that sounds moderate. But it really means he's endorsing the most extreme abortion bans already imposed by many states.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: I want to see President Trump ask Biden, when is abortion not appropriate? The day before birth? The week before? The month before? And really point out a lot of extreme measures that are out there.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Critics say abortion doesn't move the needle as much as Biden would like.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE MCGHEE WHITE, SENIOR FELLOW, INDEPENDENT WOMEN'S FORUM: Many of these swing states that he really needs to drum up support in have already made their decision about abortion in their own statewide ballots.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Plus, new Fox News polling shows abortion ranks sixth in issues voters consider extremely important, ranking far more urgent for Democrats than Independents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And some speculation today that Trump could announce his V.P. pick this week with the Trump campaign swiftly pouring cold water on that. Although the former president did say his vice president will be at the debate with him, Bret, and we know that \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  will be there in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we'll see who else is there as well. Suspense. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Killing us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stocks were mixed today. The Dow lost 299, the S&P 500 gained 21, the NASDAQ jumped 221 today.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a cyber-attack targeting the automobile industry is having a major impact on vehicle dealerships tonight and it is continuing. The perpetrators are reportedly demanding tens of millions of dollars in ransom and that's creating serious problems for the industry.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Kelly Saberi is joining us tonight from Chicago. Good evening, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY SABERI, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Bret. CDK global a dealer management system says it's the victim of a ransom attack and it's impacting nearly 15,000 auto dealerships around the country.\par \par \uc2 Their software is used for different tasks that's car repair orders financing and accessing customer records with some of the biggest automakers like G.M., Ford, \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2 , and \uc2 BMW\uc2  relying on CDK.\par \par \uc2 Now, the company has not revealed what it knows about the hackers. This is typical for a company in negotiation. With a group like this, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reports through an unnamed source that the group is based somewhere in Eastern Europe, they're demanding tens of millions of dollars to relinquish their hold on their systems.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, the company told their customers, it anticipates restoration won't be resolved until at least Sunday, June 30th.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, they said, "Our sales and customer success teams are conducting one to one outreach with dealers in their territories to provide alternative ways to support their sales and service efforts in the interim."\par \par \uc2 John Crane is one of those customers that I've spoken to. Crane is dealer principal, at Hawk Auto Group, they have locations around the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN CRANE, CEO, HAWK AUTO GROUP: We process over 5,000 parts invoices a day at this -- at our group. So, that's now being done by hand. And, you know, it's a little more time and a little more work.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, we have enough guys who've been around long enough for anybody with gray hair and knows how to do the math and we get it right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SABERI: The systems went down early in the morning on June 19th. And some services were restored later that day, but then the company was hit with a second attack.\par \par \uc2 The company now facing litigation over the possibility of personal information being compromised. This goes to show no one is safe from ransomware attacks. Even a company as big as this. CDK claims 2.6 percent of all U.S. gross domestic product is transacted through their softwares, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I've talked to smaller dealers who are trying to do workarounds. It's a big deal, Kelly, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the government's report on the disastrous train derailment in East Palestine, Ohio. What happens next?\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 9 in Minneapolis says the partial failure of a dam in South Central Minnesota has officials monitoring that situation very carefully. Monday high water flows breached the dam, destroying a power substation.\par \par \uc2 County officials say riparian is a low risk dam and that online posts have exaggerated the danger. If there was a colossal failure there, we'll monitor it.\par \par \uc2 Fox 17 in Des Moines, Iowa as the city of Rock Rapids is cleaning up after a break of a levee along the Rock River Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Rock Rapids is one of the communities that Iowa Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  toured Monday. She's promising to get state and federal help to cities and counties as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Fort Lauderdale. One of the big stories there tonight from WSVN, \uc2 Florida Panthers\uc2  fans celebrate the team's first Stanley Cup Championship. It was quite a game.\par \par \uc2 Panthers defeating the \uc2 Edmonton Oilers\uc2  2-1 in game seven Monday night. Franchise's first championship in its 30 year history.\par \par \uc2 A championship parade will be held Sunday in Fort Lauderdale. Congratulations Panthers.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Residents of East Palestine, Ohio, are participating right now in a town hall meeting. You see it live there on the left side of your screen, to react to fundings in a government report about last year's train derailment. That report, we are told was damning.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Garrett Tenney is in East Palestine tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOMENDY, CHAIR, \uc2 NATIONAL TRANSPORTATION SAFETY BOARD\uc2 : It is not our role to defend \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 . We are here to protect the American people and the traveling public.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): \uc2 The National Transportation Safety Board\uc2 , going after \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  today, as it laid out the findings of its investigation into the railways toxic train derailment last year in East Palestine, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are here to ensure that the lessons learned from this derailment will lead to meaningful change.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): The agency found the derailment was likely caused by an overheated wheel bearing, which eventually forced 38 train cars off the tracks, including five carrying hazardous materials.\par \par \uc2 Officials made the controversial decision to vent and burn hundreds of thousands of gallons of those toxic chemicals based on the advice of \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 's contractors who warned an even deadlier explosion was imminent.\par \par \uc2 Investigators now say that wasn't the case.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Was the vent and burn necessary to prevent a polymerization induced tank rupture?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As I stated in my presentation, the answer is not. No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): A federal judge has signed off on a $600 million settlement in a class action lawsuit against \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  by folks who live in the area. Some of whom say they are still struggling with health issues from the derailment, and they feel abandoned by their own government.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My family are still experiencing health impacts that our doctors don't have explanations for --\par \par \uc2 Like, I never in a million years thought that we would be sitting here talking about the types of health illnesses that I'm seeing in my community. And we still have the \uc2 EPA\uc2  telling us everything is fine. It's not fine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): The Attention now shifts to Congress, where Republicans have been holding off on any major reforms to rail safety, until the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  releases its full final report. And that should happen within the next few weeks. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney, in East Palestine. Garrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  is pleading with Israel's government to avoid escalating its conflicts with terrorists. Austin, meeting with Israel's defense minister today, as fighting along the northern border with Lebanon increases.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Trey Yingst is in Tel Aviv tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Smoke billows from Southern Lebanon after an Israeli strike. Hours later, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  drones are launched into northern Israel. It's another day of cross border fire and another day closer to a larger war.\par \par \uc2 TZACHI HANEGBI, ISRAELI NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER (through translator): If there will not be an arrangement through diplomatic means, everyone understands that there must be an arrangement through other means.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Israeli officials are becoming increasingly impatient with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 's continued rocket, mortar, and drone attacks into their country. A staggering 5,000 such strikes have killed nearly two dozen Israelis, according to the foundation for defense of democracies.\par \par \uc2 Israel's Defense Minister Yoav Gallant, met this morning with his counterpart, Secretary of Defense \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , to discuss a path forward for countering the Iran-backed group with American support.\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN (RET), UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: The United States will always support Israel's right to defend itself. And the United States will always ensure that Israel has what it needs to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): Austin's words don't directly align with the policy of the Biden administration, which has abandoned emergency procedures meant to accelerate weapons shipments to Israel.\par \par \uc2 When similar actions were taken for weapons deliveries to Ukraine, it left the country low on ammunition and fighting at a disadvantage. Israeli leadership worries they could suffer a similar fate against Iran and its largest proxy.\par \par \uc2 YOAV GALLANT, MINISTER OF DEFENSE, ISRAEL (through translator): The greatest threat to the future of the world, and the future of our region is Iran. And time is running out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): While Israel braces for the possibility of war with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , the Israeli Supreme Court ruled today that ultra-Orthodox citizens will now be required to serve in the military. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 WikiLeaks\uc2  founder, Julian Assange is now in U.S. territory. He will enter a plea any minute in an American court in the Northern Mariana Islands. The deal is expected to free him and resolve years of legal fighting over his web site's publication of classified documents. The DOJ making that deal.\par \par \uc2 Assange has spent years holed up and imprisoned, while fighting extradition to the U.S. More on this with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Up next, one Democrat, one Republican. We seek "COMMON GROUND" about food security, water woes, and inflation.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TRACEY MANN (R-KS): As farm bills are filled in every corner of America, in every field and pasture, in every grocery store and agribusiness, the legislation we pass today will have ripple effects for years to come.\par \par \uc2 TOM VILSACK, UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF AGRICULTURE: I don't think we are close to getting a farm bill done, until the folks who are negotiating the farm bill are realistic about what's doable within a constrained resource environment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's the agriculture secretary there.\par \par \uc2 It's time now for our "COMMON GROUND" segment. Joining us tonight, Colorado Democratic Senator Michael Bennet and Kansas Republican senator, Roger Marshall.\par \par \uc2 Senators, thanks for being here. I know you're at a field hearing in Burlington, Colorado about costs and the effects of permanent drought on agriculture. I want to talk about the farm bill and what you hope to see develop there. But I do want to ask you about a couple of other things.\par \par \uc2 First, the border. It's going to be a big issue. Immigration border security at the debate on Thursday. Both of you have talked at different times about the border in different ways.\par \par \uc2 Senator Marshall, first to you. How do you see it and the concerns you have on the issue?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER MARSHALL (R-KS): Well, look, you know, back home, even in Kansas, we're not a border state, but we feel like one. We lose a Kansan every day to fentanyl poisoning. This is a tough one or two issue as the open southern border. Unfortunately, we had the Chinese triad, we had the cartel smuggling drugs, smuggling people through Kansas. So, this is a huge problem. We need to secure the border.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, we have more jobs, and we have people here as well. But we have to secure the border first, and then, work on a broken immigration system.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Bennett, Democrats, and I'm sure the president is going to point to the Senate bipartisan negotiation bill that never came to be -- you voted for that effort. How do you get to "COMMON GROUND" on this issue even around the edges?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET (D-CO): Well, I think the "COMMON GROUND" around this issue across this country is fairly straightforward, which is the American people don't want cast at the border. They want to make sure that the border is well maintained. And we've got a system of lawful immigration in this country that makes unlawful immigration unnecessary or less desirable on the part of the people that are coming here.\par \par \uc2 And that recognizes that our rural economy and our urban economy desperately need us to have a functional immigration system.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Marshall, what are you doing out there at that field hearing? What are you trying to get? One of the things is rising costs that every farmer feels, really every consumer feels.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Well, in addition to Colorado sharing a border with Kansas, we look very much alike. We have shared this same drought for 10 of the last 15 years. Then we had this aquifer underneath us, this aquifer of water. It's 200 to 300 deep of fresh water. It goes all the way from South Dakota through Nebraska, Kansas, all the way down to Oklahoma and Texas and New Mexico, Colorado, as well as Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 So we're trying to figure out how do we conserve this water. Because of the drought, we are using the water faster than we can replenish it. We use this water to grow our crops as well as nourish our animals. So we're trying to figure out the best conservation practices. As we put the final touches on a farm bill, we want to make sure we are spending that money wisely and capture what's working good and get rid of the bad. So we're out here talking to the producers and seeing what they are doing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Bennet, there's a drought report. There was severe to extreme drought in about six percent of the contiguous U.S. at the end of May, 13 percent moderate to extreme drought. Water is a major issue for your region.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: It is a major issue. And I have been very pleased to have the opportunity to work with Senator Marshall across the political aisle now for years on legislation that recognizes that nobody is making any more water. Our farmers and ranchers, who are the best husbands of the land, have to be the ones that are leading the way on things like how we still promote soil health in a world where we are facing this kind of drought, how we produce the kind of crops that are necessary for us to keep food prices low across the country -- across the world, and make sure that America is a leading exporter.\par \par \uc2 And I think as Senator Marshall said, there's not a lot that divides us on these issues. This border between Kansas and Colorado, drought does not recognize that border. Wildfire does not recognize our borders. It's critical for us to find ways to work together to support farmers and ranchers, by the way, just like we did years and years ago when the heart of this part of our country was subjected to the dust bowl. We have got deal with it as seriously as people dealt with it back then.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One other issue of common ground is concern about Chinese purchase of farmland in the U.S. Here is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director and General Jack Keane.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: It's not that is anything inherently unlawful about foreign purchase of land. But we get concerned when the purchaser may have ties back to a government that doesn't share our values.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: China is conducting one of the most comprehensive penetrations of American society in our history. Buying the farmland close to military bases is part of that strategy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Agricultural landownership now is at 349,442 acres. "New York Post" says it's next to 19 U.S. military bases in an alarming threat, and if you look at the map, it says under the guise of farming the Chinese landowners could set up a reconnaissance sites, install tracking technology, use radar, et cetera. You both seem concerned about this. Senator Bennet and then Senator Marshall?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Thanks. I'm deeply concerned about it. I introduced a bipartisan bill with \uc2 James Lankford\uc2 , who was one of the people that negotiated the immigration bill you talked about a couple years ago to get our Treasury Department and our Commerce Department to take seriously the Chinese purchase of our land. I have deep concern about it, both in terms of its proximity to military bases, but also its integration into our economy as well. As a member of the intelligence committee. This is something that we're focused on and worried about. And I think we're going to find Republicans and Democrats who can support -- who can support this effort together and make sure that the American people at least understand the degree to which Beijing is trying to own our land in a strategic that helps them but may not help us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Marshall?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Look, food security is national security. We take it for granted as Americans, but it is vitally important to our security. That's why we want to elevate the secretary of agriculture onto the \uc2 CFIUS\uc2  committee which evaluates foreign investments. And it goes way beyond the land, though, and agriculture. I'm probably more concerned about the food production. China owns about a fourth of our pork production, Brazil about a fourth of our beef production as well, the processing part of that food chain. So it goes way beyond just the land. Very concerned about all this. Food security is national security.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, we appreciate you both coming on common ground. We will continue to talk about the things you guys are working on. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can see all of the "Common Ground" segments on my podcast "Common Ground." Find that and the "All-Star Panel" podcast under the Bret Baier podcasts at FOXNewspodcast.com, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , or wherever you download podcasts. You can also see this discussion on the FOX News \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  settles a lawsuit over its failure to protect confidential tax data of some very wealthy people. We'll bring you that.\par \par \uc2 And later, the panel on the presidential race, the former president's hearings in courtrooms, and debate advice from \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Surgeon General is declaring gun violence a public health crisis. The advisory issued by Dr. Vivek Murthy comes after the nation endures another summer weekend marked by mass shootings that left dozens of people dead or wounded. Murthy calls on the U.S. to ban automatic rifles, introduce universal background checks for purchasing guns, and pass laws that would restrict their use in public spaces. No action by Congress on those fronts as of yet.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma's Supreme Court is ruling the state's approval of what would be the nation's first publicly funded religious school is unconstitutional and must be rescinded. Justices say last year's school board vote to approve the application by the Catholic Archdiocese of Oklahoma for the Saint Isidore of Seville virtual charter school violates both the state and U.S. constitutions. There has been no comment so far from the archdiocese.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  has agreed to settle a lawsuit over the leaking of confidential tax records belonging to former President Trump, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , and other wealthy Americans. Hedge Fund manager \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2  accused the agency of failing to protect his information from a contractor who stole it and leaked it to reporters.\par \par \uc2 Joining to us talk about it is Brooke Cucinella, global head of litigation and regulatory inquiries at \uc2 Citadel\uc2  and \uc2 Citadel Securities\uc2 . Brooke, thanks for being here. This has to deal with the "\uc2 ProPublica\uc2 " piece, "America's top 15 earners and what they reveal about the U.S. tax system." This is back in April. Why does \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2  go forward with this against the \uc2 IRS\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BROOKE CUCINELLA, \uc2 CITADEL SECURITIES\uc2 : Thanks, Bret. I think the answer to that is pretty straightforward and simple. For Ken, this case has always been about accountability. From the time that he and the thousands of other Americans found out that their information had been leaked, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  took the position that it wasn't them. It wasn't their issue. It wasn't their fault. And there was simply nothing that they could do about it. And that was an incredibly frustrating response to hear from a government agency that's entrusted with Americans' confidential information year after year. It's troubling, I think, for all of us that have to file tax returns. And so Ken took that on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And, well, thanks to him, the lawsuit went forward. And this federal contractor, Charles Littlejohn, 38, pleaded guilty to one count of unauthorized disclosure of tax returns, return information last October after he admitted leaking the former president's information to "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " and that of other wealthy Americans to "\uc2 ProPublica\uc2 ." And this the deposition is truly amazing. Question, "So you could theoretically access anyone's tax return in that job?" Answer from Littlejohn, "Yes." "Were you able to access anyone's tax return?" Answer, "I was able to access tax returns at will, yes." "And unlike before, you actually did target certain people?" Answer, "That's correct."\par \par \uc2 We should point out that there is an apology issued today by the \uc2 IRS\uc2  to \uc2 Ken Griffin\uc2 , and I will read from it. "The \uc2 IRS\uc2  sincerely apologizes to Mr. Griffin and the thousands of other Americans whose personal information was leaked to the press. The \uc2 IRS\uc2  takes its responsibilities seriously and acknowledges that it failed to prevent Mr. Littlejohn's criminal conduct and unlawful disclosure of Mr. Griffin's confidential data. Accordingly, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  assures Mr. Griffin and other victims of Mr. Littlejohn's actions that it has made substantial investments in its data security to strengthen its safeguarding of taxpayer information." Are you happy with that? And in this settlement, it says a third party outside monitor. Is that what the \uc2 IRS\uc2  needs?\par \par \uc2 CUCINELLA: Yes. I will say that this is, at the end of the day, the exact result that we were looking for. Ken brought this case with the hope of getting the \uc2 IRS\uc2  to do what was right by all Americans and really protect people's information. And it was pretty stunning throughout this case how they said, again, this isn't our issue. Sue Charles Littlejohn. Take this to Charles Littlejohn. Well, Charles Littlejohn is in jail, and the \uc2 IRS\uc2  has been told year after year that they have security vulnerabilities and they have done nothing.\par \par \uc2 So it took someone in Ken's position, and, you know, I think we can all acknowledge, with Ken's resources to actually be able to pursue a case like this and get them to the point where they admitted publicly finally we need to do something, and they committed to all Americans today that they are going to do that. So, yes, I think this was the result that we were hoping for when we filed this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's really interesting. I am glad we brought it to light, and thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 CUCINELLA: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel on the presidential race and debate, plus the Julian Assange plea deal. Then "Tuesday Xtras." Bring it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you have a response to Australia's request that you end Julian Assange's prosecution?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're considering it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, FORMER JUSTICE DEPARTMENT OFFICIAL: This justice department just reached a plea deal to let Julian Assange out. His actual leaks of classified information might have led to the deaths of hundreds of American servicemen at the same time they're pouring resources going after a former president for how he stored classified documents.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ, (R-FL) HOUSE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE: We can't ever forget Hillary Clinton's highly classified documents on her server. It's two- tiered justice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, DOJ is -- has reached a deal with Julian Assange. He tonight has landed Saipan and he is in the middle of this hearing. We are understanding this deal is going forward. If you take a look at the headlines, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", "Assange agrees to plead guilty in exchange for release, ending standoff with the U.S." "Julian Assange's plea deal, a suitable end to a grubby saga." "Australia's most senior diplomat in U.K. flying with Julian Assange to Saipan."\par \par \uc2 With that, let's start there with our panel, "Axios" political reporter Stef Kight, "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers, and Josh Holmes, co-host of "The Ruthless" podcast and former chief of staff to Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Josh, you can hear John Yoo and Mike Waltz, the irony here at the same time the administration is pushing forward with this case against former President Trump about Mar-a-Lago's raid. They make this deal today. Obviously, years in the making with Julian Assange. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, "RUTHLESS" PODCAST CO-HOST: I think it's extremely troubling. And I think John Yoo hit it right on the head. People forget because of the length of time, this has been going on for what seems to be forever. But people forget what the \uc2 WikiLeaks\uc2  exposed was real-time American assets, intelligence assets, in a field of war while they were there. I can't imagine a more dangerous act of espionage than that. And I don't really understand or even hope to understand how people can make a martyr out of Julian Assange. I think it is -- if you have any interest in protecting the well-being of men and women wearing the American flag on their shirt in a field of war, you have got to have a huge problem with this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is an argument, Stef, about whether it was journalism and equating it to "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " and the Pentagon Papers. But the leaker, \uc2 Chelsea Manning\uc2 , was actually pardoned by President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  put out a post today, "He endangered the lives of our troops. Julian Assange endangered the lives of our troops in a time of war and should have been prosecuted to the full extent of the law. The Biden administration's plea deal with Julian Assange is a miscarriage of justice and dishonors the service and sacrifice of the men and women of our armed forces and their families. There should be no plea deals to avoid prison for anyone that endangers the security of our military or the national security of the United States ever." So weighing in on that. But it looks like this deal is done.\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": Yes, I think it's been pretty interesting to see how some of the more conservative voices and further right voices have actually been praising this decision and celebrating the fact that Julian Assange will finally be free and able to move past this after so many years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is a split. There's a definite split.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Exactly, there has been a split. And I feel like it is a marker of how the Republican Party has maybe shifted on this issue slightly. It used to be everyone was saying things like \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  tweeted, saying that it was clearly, Julian Assange was a traitor, that he had betrayed his country and betrayed the U.S. But now we have seen more and more of these conservative voices like \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , like \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  coming out and saying that this was an important deal to have been made.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just interesting timing, I guess, Francesca, about this administration, the choice now to makes this deal and what it means big picture.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": And because there hasn't been a White House press briefing this week because the president has been in debate prep. We have not had a chance yet to press the White House on this in particular.\par \par \uc2 But with respect to the comparison to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it is noteworthy at this point that they are pushing back, essentially, on the obtaining the boxes, they are trying to get as much as they can thrown out in court at this point. That's largely what we expected to do, by the way, is be quite litigious on this.\par \par \uc2 But the reason that they were going after Trump on this is because he was not, as they said, forthcoming with the documents. He did not turn them over, and that is what is at the heart of the classified documents case.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, 100 percent, after the subpoena. But we should note that the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision on immunity and how that comes down could affect all of that this week.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn to the debate two days away. We have a "New York Magazine" headline, "What \uc2 Obama\uc2  is whispering to Biden. The president's plan to save their legacy from Trump," that there is concern in the \uc2 Obama\uc2  camp and with the former president. Here is Karl Rove.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  SENIOR ADVISER: My view of the \uc2 Obama\uc2  wing of the Democratic Party is they had their doubts about him in 2015. Remember, he was a sitting vice president, and the president of the United States said I'm not going to support him in the 2016 election. I'm going to support \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There was tension already that created more tension. But they have always had their doubts about his ability to win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's hard to imagine a more of a tense run-up to one event than we are having to Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: No question about it. What I find so fascinating is now the reemergence of \uc2 Obama\uc2 , for one, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  for another, basically taking on a role that you would assign to a deputy press secretary to lower exceptions for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in this performance.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think that they know that he has got a huge problem, and I think they are very concerned about his ability to deliver on that. Now, I think exceptions are appropriately low for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Most Americans are asking themselves whether he can make it through a 90-minute on-air debate in and of itself. And he gets through that, there is going to be sort of a residual media fanfare for that. So I think he's got exceptions where they need to be despite \uc2 Obama\uc2  and Hillary working on all of that. But he also has an awful lot to lose here, because he has lost ground in the last three months. He is going to have to try reestablish himself as a competent president who is able to do the job for four more years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " national average now tied at 46, the new poll out today, 46-46. And as you look at the polling averages in the critical battleground states, again, this is "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ." It has shifted a bit, but still, the former president with leads -- Wisconsin. Michigan, Pennsylvania, Nevada, Arizona, Georgia, and North Carolina. Some of those, Stef, are a little bit narrower than the previous "New York Times" poll, but they are still leads.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Yes, it's certainly neck and neck. And it's probably a good sign for many people in the Biden world that it is getting closer than some of these past polls have shown. But it does go to show that really anything could shake this up between now and November. And I think it's interesting and points to some of the strategy likely behind trying to have this debate so early in the election year. No matter what happens on Thursday, both sides will have some time to try to change those polls and get back in the lead before November.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And people forget that it was the Biden team, Francesca, that moved forward with this early debate, kind of taking control of this debate argument, and laid down the marker. I don't know if they thought that the former president was going to say yes. He immediately said yes. And now we are where we are, but it is a risky strategy. If it doesn't work well, you've got a long time until the next debate, which is September, first, second week of September.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Right. And the president's allies see an upside for them here. They believe that the contrast that would be shown on the debate stage between him and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be in their favor. Obviously, they would not have agreed to do this if they did not think that. By the way, both campaigns seem to think that the contrast will work for them. We will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of coming out of the debate and how much Americans will stay tuned into it, the Fourth of July is coming up. It's summer --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, no, no. I'm just telling you this will be the most watched event politically that we have seen since -- we had, what, 26 million in August of that 2015, that debate in Cleveland. I'm telling you this is going to be crazy, Francesca.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: But in terms, though, I'm saying of how it affects the candidates and the polls long-term in these battleground states, even if someone, whoever it is, does really well, in terms of capitalizing off of that and carrying that momentum all the way through to September, October, and November, it is a long summer, as we were talking about. The conventions are coming up. A lot can change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's true.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . She is giving advice. In this open guest essay, "It's a waste of time to try to refute Mr. Trump's arguments like a normal debate. He starts with nonsense then digresses into blather," she writes. "Exceptions for him are so low," this is Trump, "that if he doesn't literally light himself on fire on Thursday evening, some will say he is downright presidential." It is something.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: If anybody wants to give a tutorial how to lose a debate to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I think \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  is an excellent candidate for that. This is -- again, this is something that you have a press secretary out to do, to lower expectations. It's now Hillary Clinton's role in the Democratic Party. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will see.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 OK, it's time for "Tuesday Xtras." Haven't done them in a while. Thought we'd do them today. First up, Indie asked, "Do you enjoy being a debate moderator?" Actually, I really do. It's a tense situation, but you really study for it. We have a great team here. Look forward to doing it again.\par \par \uc2 Next question is for Jim. "What was your toughest interview and why?" There is a lot of them, but there was one interview with President \uc2 Obama\uc2  three days before Obamacare. Because of the timing and the long answers, it became a very tough interview, but worked out. But did ask whether you get to keep your doctor, which ended up being a good question.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, Ken asks, "What is the first set of golf clubs you remember playing?" I didn't see this one. Chi-Chi Rodriguez was the first set of golf clubs I ever remembered playing. OK, there you go.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, a House Republican lawmaker escalating her campaign to make Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  accountable for releasing to release that audio of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240626ek6p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Campaign Official Cut Off Mid-Interview; CNN Presidential Debate Prep; Bowman Raps, Curses In Weekend Campaign Event; Migrant Charged In 12-Year-Old Girl's Death In Court; Democrats Pretending They Want Secure Borders; New York Times Reports The Biden Camp Thinks CNN Presidential Debate Will Give Biden A Chance To Go On Offense On The Border; Trump Hones In On Biden's Failed Border Policies; \uc2 U.S. Government\uc2  Enters Into Pleas With Julian Assange; Trump Says He Has Picked His Running Mate; Team Biden Loses Financial Edge Over Trump; Governor Burgum Calls On CNN To Be Tough On Both Candidates; Former Sheriff's Deputy Warns Future Law Enforcement; White House Official Compared Police To Slave Patrols\b0\par\par\uc2 Kayleigh McEnany, Nate Foy, Kevin Corke, Tom Homan \par \uc2 7,579 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Democratic Senator Michael Bennet, Republican Senator Roger Marshall. Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6 PM in the East, 3 PM on the West Coast. Don't remember -- don't forget, Thursday. We've got complete coverage with that debate. It's big time. Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: I'm \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , in for Laura Ingraham and this is a special edition of The Ingraham Angle from New York City. Tonight, it's official Trump has selected his vice president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you decided who your vice president is?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: In my mind, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do they know?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No. Nobody knows.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Big news, possible Trump VP picks Congressman Byron Donalds, Senator Tim Scott and Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  are all here to weigh in. Also, tonight a liberal anti-Israel mob beats a Jewish woman outside of Los Angeles synagogue.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Plus, this one's wild, AOC and \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2 's cringeworthy New York City rally.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2  (D-NY): We're going to show them who the (inaudible) we are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yikes. More on all those stories and more. But first, we have just three days away from the CNN presidential debate and President Trump, he is testing out his attack lines on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Should I be tough and nasty and just say, you're the worst president in history? Or should I be nice and calm and let him speak? Be tough, be tough.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: And despite the relentless smears from the liberal media or the targeted lawfare by the DOJ and local Left-wing prosecutors, Trump is having a blast on the campaign trail. Look at him there. It's no wonder this happy warrior is leading in most swing states. And still ahead in the RealClearPolitics averages that you're looking at right now.\par \par \uc2 The people know he has their back and they're enjoying his presence everywhere he goes Philly residents, that's right in Philadelphia, even got to see Trump's own personal style of debate prep, when he stopped by a local cheese steak diner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : There's a lot of interest, tremendous interest in the debate. And you know, when you say prep, I think this is prepping. These people know better than anybody what they want. Right?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Trump is out, he's meeting voters face-to-face, learning what their needs are and what they believe we must do to turn this country around. Biden, meanwhile, it is the exact opposite approach. He's hunkered down in Camp David surrounded not by ordinary Americans, but by 16 of his advisors. You see him right there. And the ones who aren't in attendance, they're running around TV desperately trying to prop up their faltering candidate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCH LANDRIEU, BIDEN CAMPAIGN NATIONAL CO-CHAIR: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't have any concerns about his mental fitness nor do I. I have spent an incredible amount of time with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That's just the fade that the other side is pushing. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has taken this country from being in a cataclysmic downfall to actually leading the world once again.\par \par \uc2 JON MEACHAM, HISTORIAN AND INFORMAL BIDEN ADVISOR: What he has to do is show people that he is fully in command, which I believe him to be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Let's be real, the only thing Biden is in command of is