vice. I'm so glad you're OK. You're a great mother and a great American.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us tonight. I'm \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , in for Laura Ingraham. You can catch me weekdays at noon on Outnumbered. Thanks for watching this special edition of the Ingraham Angle. Jesse Watters' Prime Time takes it over from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240625ek6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Top Voter Issues And Presidential Debate Preparations; Special Counsel Defends \uc2 FBI\uc2  Search Warrant Language; Record Crossings During \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Presidency; \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  Rejects U.S.-Backed Deal To End War; Netanyahu Clarifies Comments On Cease-Fire Rejection; Disturbing Rise In Antisemitic Attacks; New York Democratic Congressman And Squad Member \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  Trails Primary Challenger In Polls; Sales Of Electric Vehicles Drops In First Quarter Of 2024; Return Home For Astronauts From International Space Station Delayed Due To Issues With \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's Starliner Spacecraft; Democrats Criticize President Trump And Supreme Court's Overturning Of Roe Versus Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Trey Yingst, Aishah Hasnie, Bill Melugin, Brit Hume,  Eric Shawn, William La Jeunesse, Madison Scarpino, Gillian Turner \par \uc2 7,883 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: And together, the pair cooked up empanadas. They cooked up crab cakes, roasted potatoes, chocolate chip cookies for the whole family to enjoy and it was beautifully done.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: You know what, I think that is a wonderful thing. I think kids don't learn enough about cooking but that's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 Do you cook, Bret?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I do cook just a little, Judge. How about you?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: A lot.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I look forward to a meal sometime.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Anytime.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. Former President Trump's legal team tries to get his classified documents case thrown out. We'll bring you down there.\par \par \uc2 A New York Squad member tosses out some F bombs during a weekend rally, drawing rebuke from his own party as he fights for a primary win.\par \par \uc2 And why electric vehicle owners aren't all that charged up about their cars.\par \par \uc2 But first, as Psresident Biden is sequestered at Camp David tonight preparing for Thursday's debate. His vice president is focusing on what Democrats think will be a big issue this election, abortion. On this, the second anniversary of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision, Dobbs that overturned Roe v Wade. That ruling was the subject of an emotional rally outside the High Court today.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Trump team is trying to shape the outcome of the debate Thursday night before it's even started. Aishah Hasnie starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn of the White House. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. Well, Biden surrogate \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  tells Fox today that the president is sharp engaging on the campaign trail and even members by their names and he believes that that is a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that you will see on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In the case of the stealing of reproductive freedom from the women of America, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is guilty.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Second in command \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  throwing shade on the campaign trail as the commander in chief is holed up at Camp David surrounded by a whopping 16 debate preppers, getting the 81-year-old ready to go toe to toe with former President Trump for 90 minutes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You will see a very energized President Biden, I can assure you of that.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Biden does not have any public events until Thursday's debate. Instead, the campaign is flooding the airwaves with new ads, marking the anniversary of the Dobb's decision to send the abortion issue back to the States.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Decades of progress shattered just because the last guy got his four years in the White House.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Trump and his surrogates are on the offense too going after the moderators.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Trump is knowingly going into a hostile environment on this very network.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): And trying to shape debate topics, offering a few questions of their own for Biden, like, why do you repeatedly lie about your record on inflation and whether he still thinks a Hunter Biden laptop is Russian disinformation.\par \par \uc2 Trump and Republicans are also alleging Biden will receive injections before the debate. Democrats lashing back.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JASON CROW\uc2  (D-CO): He lies and he lies and he lies. So, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  needs to do what he always does. Remain calm. Tell the truth. Be consistent and be steady.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): The administration is also swatting away criticism this week for promoting Staffer Tyler Cherry, who in the past called police a direct evolution of slave patrols and lynch mobs, now attempting to clean it all up. He writes, past social media posts from when I was younger do not reflect my current views, period.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And back on the debate issue, Bret Trump just posted on Truth Social that he would be willing to take a drug test immediately if President Biden takes one as well. Both of these men will be back on the campaign trail right after the big debate on Friday, Trump heads to Virginia and then Biden heads to North Carolina in New York City on the same day, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Take the under on that drug test. Aishah, what about the White House, so how are they responding to the Supreme Court decision to weigh in, take the case of whether states can ban transgender care for kids?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, this is a win for them. We reached out for a comment from the White House and we haven't heard anything back. They've actually told us they're not going to comment as of yet.\par \par \uc2 The administration, Bret, challenged Tennessee's ban on puberty blockers and transgender surgeries for minors on the basis of equal protection. It's actually the first time the High Court is taking this up.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee's A.G. says that he looks forward to finishing the fight in front of \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  and that it will bring much needed clarity to or whether the Constitution really does afford special protections for gender identity. So, more to come on that, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live on the North Lawn. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, former President Trump's legal team is trying to get a dismissal for his classified documents case in Florida. They're insisting the appointment of Special Counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  was illegal.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, prosecutors quoting Smith want to keep the former president quiet again.\par \par \uc2 National Correspondent Jeff Paul is in Fort Pierce, Florida tonight with the latest. Good evening, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Well, good evening, Bret. And that hearing just wrapped up here at the federal courthouse in Fort Pierce, Florida with DOJ prosecutors arguing in favor of that limited gag order on the former president whereas Trump's legal team saying it would be an extraordinary move given that he has a debate coming up this week and lots of campaign events leading up to November's election.\par \par \uc2 Now, this all stems back from some language that Trump had used after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  conducted that search warrant at Mar-a-Lago back in August of 2022.\par \par \uc2 The former president made comments on social media this past day that the DOJ authorized the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to use deadly lethal force. Trump on that same day said in a fundraising e-mail that Biden's DOJ was, "locked and loaded, ready to take me out and put my family in danger."\par \par \uc2 But Smith's initial motion argues the language in the court authorized search warrant was standard \uc2 FBI\uc2  procedure, prohibiting use of deadly force unless an officer is under imminent danger or death or serious injury.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say statements made by Trump about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are significant, dangerous, and they pose a potential threat to \uc2 FBI\uc2  witnesses. But Trump's defense argued the language in the limited gag order is very vague. They insisted the statements made by Trump were directed at President Biden and not at law enforcement adding that in their minds, it's political speech.\par \par \uc2 Now, things got a little heated between Judge Cannon and DOJ prosecutor David Harbach. At one point, Judge Cannon reprimanded the prosecutor for his tone in court. She said her court required decorum and if Harbach was unable to do that, one of his colleagues could take over, Harbach later apologized.\par \par \uc2 Now, with no decision coming from the bench today, we are expected a written order to be issued in the coming days from Judge Aileen Cannon. Tomorrow wraps up this three day spree of hearings involving Trump's classified documents case, tomorrow it deals with the actual seizure and the going inside Mar-a-Lago and the legality of that, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff Paul in Florida, we'll follow that as well. Jeff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Immigration likely to be a major topic of Thursday's debate. Much of the public considers President Biden's policies not working as far as border security but former President Trump is also taking hits for his recent suggestion about providing green cards for college graduates from other countries, at least taking hits from those who are on the extreme side of border security and immigration.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill Melugin is in Los Angeles with all of this right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  invasion.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I will never demonize immigrants, I will never refer to immigrants as poisoning the blood of a country.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President Biden and former President Trump are on a collision course likely to clash on immigration on the debate stage Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And on day one, we will begin the largest domestic deportation operation in American history.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And now he's proposing to rip spouses and children from their families and homes and communities and place them in detention camps.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): For President Biden, the border has been a rocky road politically, earning him criticism from conservatives and progressives alike. His administration has overseen three years in a row of the highest illegal crossings ever recorded after boasting about reversing Trump's policies.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY, HOMELAND SECURITY: We have rescinded so many Trump immigration policies, it would take so much time to list them.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Now in response to the bipartisan criticism, the president has issued a flurry of executive orders in recent weeks banning asylum to some illegal crossers and offering a pathway to citizenship for half a million illegal immigrants married to U.S. citizens.\par \par \uc2 And he points the finger at Trump and Republicans for killing a bipartisan border deal negotiated in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Every nation must secure its borders. It's that simple. And Trump in the Republicans wouldn't do it working with me, and I would do it on my own. And I did.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): The debate comes after a week of high profile migrant crimes with illegal immigrants charged in the rape and murder of Maryland mother Rachel Morin, the rape and kidnapping of a 13-year-old girl in New York and the strangulation murder of 12-year-old Jocelyn Nungaray in Houston.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Day after day, week after week, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is releasing illegal criminals into our communities to rape, pillage, plunder and to kill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, Trump is also taking some heat from conservatives regarding a pitch he made last week where he said he'd like to give green cards to international students who graduate from college here in the U.S. His idea being he'd like to keep the talent here in America.\par \par \uc2 Well, his campaign is now clarifying saying they would fully vet those students and that, "\uc2 Hamas\uc2  supporters and America haters would be excluded." We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin in Los Angeles. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Another slide for Wall Street heavyweight \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  kept the U.S. indices mixed today. The Dow surging 261, the S&P 500 lost 17, NASDAQ dropped 193.\par \par \uc2 Israel's prime minister says he will accept only a partial ceasefire deal that would not end the war in Gaza. But Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  is already hedging on that statement a bit. He also says the current phase of fighting in Gaza is winding down, that would free up troops to confront \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorists in the north.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Trey Yingst reports tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): All eyes are on Northern Israel as the country braces for another large war. The Iran backed \uc2 Lebanese militant group Hezbollah\uc2  continues to launch daily drone and rocket attacks. Israeli officials say they're wrapping up offensive operations in Gaza, something that will allow them to shift their focus to the Northern Front.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): After the intense phase is finished, we will have the possibility to move part of the forces north. And we'll do this first and foremost for defensive purposes.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): Israel's Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  overnight said he would not accept a complete ceasefire in Gaza, a position at odds with the American backed plan to get some of the remaining 120 hostages freed from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity. He later walked back those remarks.\par \par \uc2 As the war in Gaza winds down, Israel is still launching large airstrikes in heavily populated areas, killing dozens of Palestinians this weekend. According to a local hospital director.\par \par \uc2 Netanyahu has clashed with the Biden administration in recent days about the Americans withholding weapons shipments over concerns about the rising civilian death toll in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Israeli officials say those weapons will be needed in case of a larger regional war. A source with knowledge of ongoing talks to avoid such a conflict tells Fox News that Qatar has been engaged in Israel and Lebanon to deescalate. Those attempts have been coupled with American efforts to do the same as Israel's defense minister visits Washington D.C.\par \par \uc2 YOAV GALLANT, ISRAELI MINISTER OF DEFENSE (through translator): In areas where we have disagreements, we discuss everything in detail and reach agreements and solve issues.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Fox News has learned that before meeting with Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , Israeli Defense Minister Yoav Gallant signed the State Department guestbook with a message for the hostages and fallen Israeli soldiers, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst in Tel Aviv. Trey, thanks. A Russian nuclear powered submarine is heading home tonight following military drills with Cuba earlier this month. U.S. official telling Fox News U.S. Northern Command is tracking that sub as it travels north in international waters.\par \par \uc2 Three other Russian ships are now in international waters in the Caribbean Sea.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Brit Hume on \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  throwing cold water on President Biden's ceasefire proposal and what's coming out of that.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, Princess Anne sustains minor injuries and a concussion following what's been described as an incident in the state in southwest England. The exact cause is not been confirmed, but there were horses in the area and her medical team is saying the injuries were consistent with the potential impact from a horse's head or legs.\par \par \uc2 Russia's southern region of Dagestan holds the first of three days of mourning, following a rampage by Islamic terrorists. Authorities say 20 people were killed, most of them police. They say the terrorist attack houses of worship in two cities during what were apparently coordinated the assaults.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Australia. One of the big stories there tonight, Australia plans to outlaw the sale of vapes outside pharmacies in what would be one of the world's toughest restrictions on electronic cigarettes. The country's health minister says it will be unlawful to supply, manufacture, import or sell a vape outside of pharmacy. The ban applies to all vapes regardless of whether they contain nicotine.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER\uc2 , SPOKESMAN, \uc2 UNITED STATES STATE DEPARTMENT\uc2 : I saw the comments the prime minister made yesterday, and then, I saw the statement his office put out, clarifying that they wanted to secure the release of all hostages. And then, I saw the further statement the prime minister said today, where he said made clear he supports the proposal that Israel put on the table and the president laid out.\par \par \uc2 So, I will just say, I think all of us that speak publicly at times make mistakes and misspeak. And when we do so, we have an obligation to come clarify and we're glad he did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: State department, trying to interpret some confusion tonight after Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  of Israel's initial reaction to a U.S.- backed ceasefire proposal, when it comes to Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News chief political analyst Brit Hume.\par \par \uc2 You know, Brit, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Reading through the tea leaves there. I think state department wanted to believe one thing. It doesn't seem like that much has changed from Netanyahu's original decision on all of this.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. I agree with you, Bret. I don't think much has changed at all. I think Netanyahu is in a situation where he has two goals and not always entirely compatible.\par \par \uc2 One of them obviously, is he wants to finish the job of defeating \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militarily, and he hopes as a political force as well, and the second object, of course, and this is something he's under tremendous domestic pressure about is to get the hostages out.\par \par \uc2 So, he is willing to go along with ceasefire proposals that entail the release of hostages. But he is not prepared to say that he is going to go along with a permanent ceasefire, which would leave \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in place and still, to whatever extent it can be in power in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Let us also bear in mind, Bret, that when this hideous attack on October 7th occurred, there was supposedly a ceasefire in place, which \uc2 Hamas\uc2  roundly broke. And also, there is this. They have been what? Eight ceasefire proposals bandied about in recent times, recent weeks, and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has not accepted one of them. So, I don't know what all to do is about Israel. What's the point in making a ceasefire proposals or blaming Israel for their failure, when \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has not accepted any of them?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. And there still hostages there? Americans as well. We don't know how many of them are still alive. And we haven't seen much evidence that there is movement.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Or not on that front. And, of course, the Biden administration likes to talk about Israel and ceasefires. It never says anything that we can hear about the American hostages. Nor hasn't said anything about what it's doing or trying to do to get them out. So I think that is where we are.\par \par \uc2 But I think pressure on Israel by the administration is something you'd find useful for domestic political reasons, because so many people in the Democratic Party do not support Israel.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of which, we have seen more evidence. This L.A. synagogue beating as someone, it appeared, worship or trying to go to synagogue, and it gets beaten outside of Los Angeles synagogue, we've seen incidents in New York and other incidents of antisemitism, pure and simple. And it is striking still, as we've seen these protests and these incidents increase.\par \par \uc2 HUME: You know, you and I, Bret have talked about this rise in antisemitism or, at least, visible antisemitism in the United States, since almost from October 7th. And I think we both been kind of stunned by it. And I continue to be, I mean, there is no way that you can say that, that episode outside, that synagogue in L.A. over the weekend, had anything to do with, you know, anti-Zionism.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that was playing, and so, you are blocking somebody from going into -- going into worship and then beating him up on the street.\par \par \uc2 That is not anti-Zionism. That's anti-Semitism, pure and simple. As you suggest. It continues to surprise me. And I, you know, I kind of think that the effective it is going to be if the administration does not appear and I -- and the president did issue a very strong statement about this.\par \par \uc2 Is this administration on the Democratic Party do not appear to be stronger on the issue of antisemitism and they have been so far. I have to think of the political consequences for them in the fall.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Be fascinating to see. As always, Brit. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, an X-rated tirade from a Squad member on the issue of Israel. That squad member facing difficult primary election tomorrow in New York. We'll bring you there. First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 KHON in Honolulu, as a professional lifeguard dies after being attacked by a shark, while surfing off the island of Oahu, Sunday. Tamayo Perry is a former professional surfer. He is the second such -- it's the second such death there in that region this month.\par \par \uc2 Fox 32 in Chicago, as the longest serving Alderman in Chicago history is sentenced to two years in prison and fined $2 million. 80-year-old \uc2 Ed Burke\uc2  was convicted on 13 political corruption charges. Last December. He served for more than 50 years in the \uc2 Chicago City Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at the Maryland Zoo in Baltimore. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX45, the ship that crashed into the Francis Scott Key Bridge in late March, transits to the Port of Virginia. The Coast Guard oversaw that journey. The Dali will have roughly 1,500 cargo containers offloaded at Virginia international gateway. It will then go to Norfolk for additional salvage and repairs.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look, "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are one day away from voting in an increasingly bitter primary election in New York. Saturday, progressive Squad member, incumbent Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  on a profanity-fueled tirade against his detractors at an event in the Bronx. Senior correspondent Eric Shawn shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2 , (D-NY): South South Bronx, South Bronx. South South Bronx, South Bronx.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: He riled up the crowd in a t-shirt, shouting profanities during a political rally.\par \par \uc2 BOWMAN: We are going to show -- \uc2 AIPAC\uc2  the power of the mother -- South Bronx.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Two-term congressman and progressive \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  launched an X- rated rant against \uc2 AIPAC\uc2 , the pro-Israel lobby supporting his Democratic primary opponent, dropping the f-bomb about half a dozen times.\par \par \uc2 BOWMAN: We are going to show them who the -- we are!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Fellow squad member, Representative \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  ran out on stage to hype up the crowd.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): Let go, Bronx!\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Today, Bowman campaigned with Cortez but refused to comment about cussing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you want to apologize for any profanity over the weekend?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Bowman could lose tomorrow's primary to Westchester County executive \uc2 George Latimer\uc2 , a moderate who says Bowman is too irresponsible to be in Congress and should watch his mouth.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE LATIMER\uc2 , (D-NY) U.S. CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: I have used bad language from time to time. But you've got to know what you can and can't say in a public setting, and that was inappropriate.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Bowman has accused Israel of committing genocide, did not vote for a congressional resolution condemning \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and called for defunding the police. Last fall he pled guilty to a misdemeanor for falsely pulling a fire alarm during a congressional vote. Latimer promises a different tone.\par \par \uc2 LATIMER: We have a responsibility not only to unify the party but to unify the nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN (on camera): Latimer is leading in the polls, and if he wins, he will be the first person to depose a member of the squad. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it here. Eric Shawn in New York. Eric, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Some of the other primary contests we're following tomorrow include Utah Congresswoman Celeste Maloy against combat veteran Colby Jenkins. Maloy is endorsed by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  while Utah Republican Senator Mike Lee has endorsed Jenkins.\par \par \uc2 Controversial Colorado Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  is trying to get elected in a new district in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Virginia challenger John McGuire is holding a 372-vote lead over incumbent Congressman Bob Good with 99 percent of the vote in for the fifth district Republican nomination, that primary days ago.\par \par \uc2 There may be big trouble ahead in the electric car industry. A new survey out today indicates nearly half of E.V. owners want to switch back to traditional vehicles. National correspondent William La Jeunesse tells us why and what that could mean tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Come on, jump in. I'll give you a ride to Washington.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Even a sales pitch from President Biden and massive subsidies can't rescue the E.V. market.\par \par \uc2 KARL BRAUER, SEECARS.COM EXECUTIVE ANALYST: E.V. values and E.V. market as dropped substantially, like you could use the word "collapse" here.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: E.V. sales jumped from two percent of new cars sold in 2021 to eight percent last year, but nosedived in the first quarter of 2024 for a variety of reasons, including buyers watching E.V.s die in freezing temperatures.\par \par \uc2 SHON HIATT, UNIVERSITY OF SOUTHERN CALIFORNIA: Once you take a car off the lot, an electric vehicle is depreciating 50 percent of its price compared to six percent depreciation of an internal combustion engine vehicle.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: A recent \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  Report says nearly half of U.S. owners would likely switch back to gasoline powered car if given the chance.\par \par \uc2 HIATT: For electric vehicle owners, it may cost just as much or more to charge their vehicles than it would be to purchase gasoline.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: That's especially true for fast chargers when you can find one.\par \par \uc2 JASON MIYARES\uc2 , (R) VIRGINIA ATTORNEY GENERAL: You see a lot of consumers have price and range anxiety.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: AAA also found declining interest with two-thirds of buyers saying the next car they buy will not be electric.\par \par \uc2 MARK HANCHETT\uc2 , \uc2 NXU\uc2 , CEO: We just need a couple minor little milestone improvements in battery technology and efficiency. Combine that with the infrastructure, and that comes together, and it will take off like a rocket.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Another question, how long can manufacturers hold on? \uc2 Fisker\uc2  last week declared bankruptcy with \uc2 Rivian\uc2 , Polestar, and \uc2 Lucid\uc2  burning money. Ford says it's currently losing $100,000 on each E.V. sold.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Dealers tell me there are buyers for E.V.s -- a second car, big cities, short commutes. But for others, families, suburbs, rural areas, older buyers, even government handouts can't overcome their skepticism. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William, thanks.\par \par \uc2 There are new delays tonight for the crew of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's Starliner spacecraft. The two astronauts are getting an extended stay on the International Space Station while their ride home is being checked out. Correspondent Madison Scarpino has the latest tonight. Good evening, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON SCARPINO, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Glitches with the spacecraft are keeping the astronauts docked in space for weeks longer than expected. The Starliner's first manned mission began on June 5th and was supposed to last a little over a week. Now \uc2 NASA\uc2  is saying the Starliner won't return to earth until at least July. \uc2 NASA\uc2  is blaming small helium leaks and problems were thrusters for the delays. Engineers are really using the extra time to study the spacecraft's issues and to prevent them from happening again during future missions. But in an emergency, the Starliner would be cleared to return to earth. So \uc2 NASA\uc2  says the spacecraft is doing well overall in orbit. Former \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronauts not too concerned.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSE HERNANDEZ, FORMER ASTRONAUT: You remember when we first came out with the space shuttle. Tiles were falling left and right. We had a lot of problems. And that's what happens when you first introduce a new space vehicle, you get the kinks out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO: But one expert in the space field says the delays don't look good for \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE HANLON, AIR AND SPACE LAWYER: Here we have China returning a mission with samples from the far side of the moon, a first in human history, and it looks like \uc2 NASA\uc2  and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  can't get their own people back from low earth orbit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO: \uc2 NASA\uc2  is also having some setbacks. A spacewalk scheduled for today was scrubbed because of a water leak in the cooling unit on one of the astronaut's spacesuit. It is the second time issues with spacesuits led to canceled spacewalks in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's a lot of water flowing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is it still coming out?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don't think we can do it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's literally water everywhere.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are going to terminate for the day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO: And if the Starliner's leaks are not repaired, it could put future missions at risk. \uc2 NASA\uc2  says that the leaks could push back another Starliner launch that is scheduled for early next year. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Madison, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the presidential race, the abortion issue, and fundraising, when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARA TRUMP, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE CO-CHAIR: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , obviously, nominated three Supreme Court justices to the bench who made right decision.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a healthcare crisis. And we all know who is to blame. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have seen the impact of these Trump abortion bans, the horrific, heartbreaking reality. A second Trump term would be even worse.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: We have gotten abortion out of the federal government and back to the states, the way everybody and all legal scholars always said it should be.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK: All he can say about abortion is I took the woman's right to choose. That is his talking point.\par \par \uc2 LARA TRUMP, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE CO-CHAIR: This is just about the only thing Democrats think they can run on this election cycle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, on this, the anniversary of the Dobbs decision overturning Roe v. Wade, June 24th, 2022, this is a big issue. And it's going to be a big issue politically. If you look at the latest Gallup poll, there is a record number of people saying they will only vote for a candidate who shares their view on abortion. There you see now at 32 percent. And clearly Democrats and the Biden campaign is banking on that, especially in swing states.\par \par \uc2 Let's start there with our panel, FOX News State Department correspondent Gillian Turner, Julia Manchester, national politics reporter for "The Hill," and former \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  communications director Douglas Heye. Doug, obviously, Democrats really see fertile ground here politically. Do Republicans have a pushback that deals with this issue that is effective so far?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE, FORMER REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: So far, no. And it's interesting to see what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to do something he doesn't do very often, which is be nuanced. He gets that politically this has been a loser for Republicans. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is usually all blunt force trauma all the time, but he has seen what happened in midterm elections, and he sees what's happening in states.\par \par \uc2 And I think the problem for Republicans is we can't define this as a party in Washington, D.C. We see legislators that are sort of legislating out loud. So Arizona puts a restrictive law in or South Carolina, that defines it nationally and makes it harder for Republicans to campaign on this. And this is where Democrats see opportunity.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Twenty-one states, Julia have had serious restrictions or banning of abortion since the overturning of Roe v. Wade. And the question is, how big an issue does that becomes as we head to November?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER, "THE HILL": Look, we saw, obviously, Democrats after the Dobbs decision in 2022 really seize on this with the threat of these bans in these various states. And it worked at the state level, and it worked in statewide races in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The problem, though, is I think this issue for Democrats, potentially, could be coming a bit stale. And that's why you continue to see them really push it out front and center. You know a few weeks ago after the mifepristone ruling from the Supreme Court, I think there was sort of this fear that Democratic voters or voters who might be leaning Democrat on the abortion issue could become apathetic. And I think that's why you saw the vice president come out and label this the Trump abortion ban. They don't want the economy or the border to eclipse this, so they are zeroing in on it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, this week we have the debate Thursday. Thursday.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: We do?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think we do, yes. Seen right here on FOX. We will have the simulcast. It's going to be really fascinating to see, 90 minutes of these two together. And President Biden clearly is going to use this issue, at least in one of those exchanges, whether it's from a question from CNN or on his own.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Absolutely. That's one of the things that sources from the White House have been telling our reporters this week, one of the issues that he is really beefing up on while he is holed up at Camp David, something for which he has kind of taken flak from a lot of critics. They are accusing him of staying home, going back in the bunker as he is approaching big moment rather than getting out there.\par \par \uc2 I will say, if I was going to be in the presidential debate three days from now, I would be at home studying too. There is not necessarily a problem with that. I would also point out to folks that the Biden campaign is not in the bunker right now. They are hosting 200 events this week in Georgia ahead of this thing on Thursday. So they are trying to get the message out there even if the president himself is not the face of it this week.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: And Bret, there is a reason that they're emphasizing this issue, Democrats are, and Biden certainly will. They are on offense here, but everywhere else they are on defense. And if you look at polling on issue after issue, obviously, the border and prices and what things cost being one and two, Biden is under water in all of those. So he's trying to get to some terra firma, because he in quicksand everywhere else.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is President Trump asking supporters what he should do, how he should act in the debate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: How should I handle him? Should I be tough and nasty? Or should I be --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Should I be -- she said no. Should I be tough and nasty and just say you are the worst president in history, or should I be nice and calm and let him speak?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So the latest polling for who is going to win, you have got this 50- 45 over Biden. But the expectations game is really fascinating to watch in these days ahead of Thursday, Julia.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes, it's been interesting to see a lot of Trump surrogates including North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  come out and say, well, look, Biden is actually very much on his game. He could very much rise to the occasion. And I would say that \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  is right. Going back to the State of the Union, Biden very much rose to that occasion. I would say it was a good night for the president, a good night for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  needs to be careful of here is not doing a repeat of first debate in 2020 when he came in way too hot, was way too on the offense. He needs to stick to policy as much as he can. Whether he will do that, who knows?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Trump folks have been very critical of the moderators, particularly Jake Tapper more than Dana Bash, but of what they have said about the former president before. Their spokesperson went on CNN today and went down this road that got cut off. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, TRUMP 2024 NATIONAL PRESS SECRETARY: It takes someone five minutes to \uc2 Google\uc2  "Jake Tapper, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 " to see that Jake Tapper has consistently --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Ma'am, we are going to stop this interview if you are going to keep attacking my colleagues. Ma'am, I'm going to stop this interview if you continue to attack my colleagues. I would like to talk about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who you work for. If you are here to speak on his behalf, I would love to have this conversation.\par \par \uc2 LEAVITT: I am stating facts that your colleagues have stated in the past.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm sorry, guys.\par \par \uc2 She is welcome to come back and speak about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She did not come back, at least today. It was an awkward moment.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The invitation is in the mail.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: CNN put out a statement defending Jake Tapper and Dana Bash ahead of this moderating job, which they will be in the focus of a lot of attention as well.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Absolutely. It's odd to me that maybe this anchor was not entirely prepared for that to go down the way that it did. These are standard Trump campaign talking points. It's not as if she proposed something that blew everybody's minds we hadn't heard of before. This is what he himself says over and over every single day. He believes CNN is biased. I would have thought they would have had a response.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But he took the debate. They agreed to the debate, they agreed to the moderators. That decision was kind of --\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Bret, I talked to Kasie Hunt earlier today. She is a good friend. And I know that if I came on the show and gave out all the things that I find objectionable about Shannon Bream -- there is zero, by the way. You wouldn't put up with it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: yes, evil Shannon Bream?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Exactly You wouldn't put up with that. And I think Kasie was very right to defend her colleagues. That's what you do. She did her job, and I think she should be commended for it. She's obviously -- you're going to have a tough time on your hands when you're getting campaign surrogates on your show. That happens everywhere. But you're going to stand up for your colleagues. That's formal.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. She could have moved on and come back to a different topic. But I get it.\par \par \uc2 One last thing -- fundraising. The fund raising in May for the Trump campaign is pretty strong. I mean, if you look at the numbers, they have closed the gap and surpassed the Biden campaign in May, specifically.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes, and what happened that month also was the guilty verdict. And it has clearly, in terms of fundraising at least, helped the Trump campaign. It was interesting, in the lead up to this verdict during the trial, there was so much chatter about how Trump was going to be able to afford all of his legal bills and fundraise and run for president and such. But at least on the fundraising part, he is very much closing the gap with Biden, which is obviously very important.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, up next, we'll look at some of the big House races coming up. And then the world's ugliest dog? Are we giving it away? There he is. Oh, the tongue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 AYANNA PRESSLEY\uc2 , (D-MA): In \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  in word and deed, I see a servant, someone who leads with love.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2 , (D-NY): Get -- big money out of politics. We're going to show them who the -- we are.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE LATIMER\uc2 , (D-NY) U.S. CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: I think there's been a lot of criticism lobbed at me by my opponent. But I don't think I get a right to lose my temper. I don't think I get the right to curse in public.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, leads with love. "Spectrum News" New York One, "Record breaking ad spending in New York 16." That's that race we are talking about, the primary. This is tomorrow. "So far, $24 million has been spent on ads, including TV, radio, satellite, and digital in New York 16 district Democratic primary. That outpaces nearly $18 million spent in the 2022 contest in Oregon's sixth district which now ranks second."\par \par \uc2 Back with the panel. I'll tell you, Julia, this is hot and heavy, but the latest poll shows Latimer leading big.\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: Yes, absolutely. And I think if you zoom out, this sort of takes us back to 2016 in a way and how it's a bit of a proxy battle, if you will. You've \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , who has endorsed \uc2 George Latimer\uc2 , and you've had \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  endorse \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2 . This is clearly a battle, I think, between progressives and more moderate Democrats playing out in this district with the Israel-Gaza war at the center of it. So, I think going forward, I mean, \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  is in quite a bit of trouble, though.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And when they are out raises funds, firing people up. AOC went out there this weekend, Doug, that really doesn't help the Biden campaign, especially on the issue of Israel.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: No, not at all. And this is part of the real split that Democrats have on this issue. And it puts Biden in a very tough spot. You look at say, Dearborn, Michigan, disproportionately high Muslim representation in that community. And if Biden goes too far to help them, he risks where his standing is with Jewish voters. So he's really caught in a tight spot.\par \par \uc2 And the montage you showed sort of was like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's speech the other night. Which one do you want me to be? Do you want me to be in nice guy who focuses on issue or do you want me to be the tough, bombastic guy. And this is what the Democratic voters in New York are going to have to decide.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I will say that AOC, Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , was very critical of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the former president, showing up in the Bronx, saying that all these people were from other places and she was alleging all kinds of things. He got thousands and thousands of people to show up, and there were only about 300 at that event this weekend.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I think at most. I saw some photos on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  of the stage with AOC and \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2 , and some of them looked like there were maybe 50 people in that crowd.\par \par \uc2 But as the to the behavior, I guess the best thing to say is that this is the "you do you" congressional generation, right? If you want to wear basketball shorts and a hoodie on the Senate floor, fine. If you want to get on the stage and act like a rage rapper at a rally, that's fine. But the context here is important, because while this is going on and he's cursing our \uc2 AIPAC\uc2 , a Jewish rights organization, Jews in L.A. are being attacked, congregants outside their synagogue are being attacked. Overseas in Russia, southern Russia, Jews are being killed while they're worshiping inside a synagogue. Antisemitism is up 400 percent since October 7th. You might want to tone it down a little.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and the balance of power, the balance of power in the House, and you just look at it, 218-213, four vacancies, it really is on a razor's edge, both the House and the Senate, control of both chambers, Doug, and obviously the presidency.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: It's going to go the way of the presidency. And what you highlighted there I don't think is getting enough attention. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is campaigning in very strong Democratic areas -- Philadelphia, Richmond, the Bronx. Is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  going to go ruby red districts in rural areas? He needs to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Panel, thanks. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The world's ugliest dog is --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we gave it away. There it is. I think it's the tongue. It took five tries, but Wild Thang, an eight-year-old Pekingese has been named the world's ugliest dog. Wild Thang battled a virus at a young age that left him without teeth, causing his tongue to always hang out. He was three-time runner-up in the competition from California, but this is his first win. Congratulations. I'm sure his family loves him.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our "Common Ground" features Democratic Senator Michael Bennet, Republican Senator Roger Marshall.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Don't forget, Thursday, we've got complete coverage of that debate. It's big time.\par \par \uc2 Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240625ek6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Biden: Supreme Court "Out of Kilter"; Biden's New "Parole in Place" Executive Action; Tensions Rise Between Biden and Netanyahu; Israel PM Netanyahu to Give Speech to Congress on July 24th; Awaiting Supreme Court Ruling on Presidential Immunity; Interview With Sen. Chris Coons (D-DE); Interview With Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R-SC); Migrant Suspects in Violent Crimes Make Headlines; Former Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  on His Time on the Supreme Court.\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Jonathan Turley, Mike Emanuel, Chad Pergram, Katie  Pavlich \par \uc2 10,002 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: "Equal justice under law", it's the foundational motto chiseled on the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  building here in Washington. As we await some of the biggest decisions of the term on things like presidential immunity and abortion, we'll take a deep dive into the cases and take a look at why there's such partisan heat on the high court.\par \par \uc2 I'm Shannon Bream. This is a special edition of "FOX News Sunday" live from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The Supreme Court has never been as out of kilter as it is today. I mean, never.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): They are setting up to gut American democracy towards the end of this season.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: They're in there trying to make much ado about nothing.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY (R-TX): The bottom line is they just don't like Alito. They just don't like Thomas. They don't like conservative justice.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Democrats, including President Biden, target the Supreme Court ahead of the announcement of key opinions that could have a seismic impact on the race for the White House. We'll get reaction from a top ally of President Biden, Senator Chris Coons, and the lead Republican on the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then, we are just days away from the CNN presidential debates. President Biden and former President Trump gearing up for the big nights.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Nobody's going to be watching the debate on Thursday night, right? Nobody?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Our Sunday panel on the key issues sure to make news at the head- to-head showdown from the border to inflation, foreign policy to fitness for office.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday", live from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from outside the Supreme Court on Capitol Hill here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Every June, it comes down to this -- the final opinions from the Supreme Court as they prepare to wrap up the term. June is where most of the justices' landmark decisions fall, many of them have changed the course of our country's future.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, for example, is the 2-year anniversary of the Dobbs decision. That overturned the court's 1973 ruling in Roe v. Wade and said the Constitution does not guarantee a right to abortion, sending the power to regulate the procedure back to lawmakers. This year, it's possible that what the court decides could alter the course of the 2024 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll talk with Senators Chris Coons and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , both of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  about that, and much more.\par \par \uc2 But first, we turn to Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Mike.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: The White House is closely watching for the next Supreme Court rulings, particularly the decision over whether former President Trump should have immunity from prosecution. Mr. Trump's chance of having a pre-election trial in D.C. depends on the court's ruling. It's been a busy week for President Biden who remains at Camp David this week, focused on preparing for Thursday's CNN presidential debate against his predecessor, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The work does not stop and plenty of problems continue to pile on his desk, including, the tightening relationship between Russia and North Korea. The administration concerned over the show of force between Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , on an official visit to North Korea to visit the isolated Kim Jong-un. The visit to establish a stronger support system to share weapons between the two countries.\par \par \uc2 Also on the foreign policy front, increased tensions between the White House and Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 . He's expected to address Congress in Washington at the end of next month.\par \par \uc2 While the U.S. continues to support Israel publicly behind the scenes, the relationship is reportedly frayed.\par \par \uc2 To the southern board now, President Biden recently announcing another executive action which will make it easier for undocumented spouses of American citizens to apply for permanent residency without leaving the country, while certainly not all people crossing the border pose problems, authorities recently charged two illegal immigrants with the killing of Josela Neray in Texas. She was just 12 years old and her family is devastated.\par \par \uc2 Rachel Morin, a Maryland mother of five, was killed last August. Authorities recently charged an illegal immigrant for the crime. The White House expressed condolences but critics say it is not enough and the administration has been far too soft on the border Shannon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mike Emanuel, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Delaware Senator Chris Coons. He sits on the Foreign Relations and Judiciary Committees and is co-chair of President Biden's re- election campaign.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday", Senator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE), BIDEN CAMPAIGN NATIONAL CO-CHAIR: Thanks, Shannon. Great to be on with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, we just had some reporting on the border, so let's start there. Just days ago, the president took executive action. This time an order that will essentially give people a pathway to citizenship who have been here for years illegally. They won't have to stay -- they will be able to stay in the U.S. They won't have to leave.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federation for American Immigration Reform\uc2  says this, "Parole-in-place is a concept created out of thin air. Parole authorizes the government to permit the entry of aliens into the United States for temporary periods of time, on a case by case basis, for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit. It was not intended that it be used to grant quasi-legal status to illegal aliens already in the country."\par \par \uc2 So, rather than enforcing existing laws, why is the president creating another executive order, another pathway, that not only rewards people for being here illegally for years, potentially hundreds of thousands of them, that may attract even more?\par \par \uc2 COONS: So, Shannon, President Biden has recently signed two executive orders on the border and immigration, the one you're just referring to, which allows those who have been here more than 10 years and are married to U.S. citizens, a pathway towards a green card. This follows a program that also existed under former President Trump, where members of the U.S. military, who have someone who's here without documentation but is married to a member of our armed services, to get a green card.\par \par \uc2 The other executive order President Biden signed strengthens border enforcement, makes it harder for those coming to our southern border to seek asylum. In balance, we've already seen the number of encounters at the border go down as a result, but a stronger, more effective action to address border security and our broken immigration system would be real legislation passed by Congress. It'd be more permanent. It would bring more resources. It would actually solve the problem.\par \par \uc2 And my friend, conservative Republican Senator \uc2 James Lankford\uc2  and several others, Senators Murphy and Sinema, put forward a bipartisan deal that would have done that a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't signed into law only because former President Trump campaigned heavily against it the day before we took it up. The former president wants and issue for the election. President Biden is trying to solve this problem for our country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you know -- and -- first of all, Republicans had a number of objections to what they saw coming out of that bipartisan negotiation and there likely would not, many of them, have voted for it anyway.\par \par \uc2 But you've also got the president coming in on day one, we've talked about this, repealing numerous executive orders, some of them paralleling what he's doing now, trying to fix the situation.\par \par \uc2 I mean our new Fox polling shows that 63 percent of people disapprove of how he's doing on immigration. When they're asked head-to-head with President Trump, they give President Trump a nine-point advantage on that.\par \par \uc2 We are seeing record numbers of people showing up. We've got reporting on crimes involving rapes, murders, kidnappings, all kinds of things. They wouldn't have happened absent people being in this country illegally so they could commit those crimes.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something from Democrat \uc2 Wes Moore\uc2 . He's the governor of Maryland. One of his constituents, Rachel Morin, was killed.\par \par \uc2 The suspect -- she's a mother of five -- is somebody who came here illegally. That's got to play out. But here's what he's saying in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. WES MOORE (D), MARYLAND: I'm infuriated. And our city is still mourning. When people think about the border crisis and they think that this is only impacting a handful of states, you know, Harford County, in our state, where this -- where this -- where this brutal murder happened, is 1,800 miles away from the border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And yet every state now is a border state.\par \par \uc2 How does the buck not stop with the president's on this issue?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, Shannon, President Biden, in his State of the Union Address this year, extended his hand to former President Trump and said, instead of blocking legislation, instead of campaigning against legislation that I believe would have passed if it had gotten to the floor, work with me. Let's solve this problem. Let's put down the tools and find a lasting solution.\par \par \uc2 There was a bipartisan solution ready for us to take up and pass. And I heard from the colleagues in the Senate --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But it would not have stopped these people who are in a number of these recent cases --\par \par \uc2 COONS: -- on both sides, Shannon, that they were ready to vote for that deal --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay.\par \par \uc2 COONS: -- after months of hard work, crafting a real bipartisan solution.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It still would have allowed thousands of people into this country, whether they showed up and were just let in because of detention issues, which, you know, we know there are empty ICE beds. We know that there are problems with funding.\par \par \uc2 But also the CBP One app would have allowed a lot of these folks in. So, when we talk about these recent crimes and, gosh, we've had a list there, tragic, nobody wants them to happen.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Correct.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But that legislation would not have stopped those people from coming into this country.\par \par \uc2 COONS: You can't assume that every person seeking asylum in this country is going to commit a crime, Shannon. And, in fact --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Correct. It only matters that it's one, I mean, when it's an American life.\par \par \uc2 COONS: What's your question, Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the question is, if -- if -- and we know the vast majority of people coming here are simply seeking a better life or asylum from a dangerous situation at home. But because the situation has become so porous at the border, it does allow for bad actors. And whether it's five or 10, or 15, these are American lives that have been devastated because of them being able to manipulate and leverage the system as it now exists.\par \par \uc2 COONS: I agree. Shannon, we need to get control of our border.\par \par \uc2 And I hope folks will pay attention to what the former president has said and done on law enforcement. He called for defunding the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . It's the \uc2 FBI\uc2  that is enforcing federal laws and is helping make our country safer.\par \par \uc2 We also have seen a significant drop in crime under President Biden, who has invested in law enforcement nationally. The American Rescue Plan funded federal, state and local law enforcement at record levels.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, as folks look about who's better positioned to keep us safe going forward, I hope folks won't overlook that former President Trump incited the riot at the Capitol on January 6th --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, that --\par \par \uc2 COONS: -- where law enforcement officers who protect all of us in the Capitol --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That --\par \par \uc2 COONS: -- every day were assaulted, and he is a convicted felon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And --\par \par \uc2 COONS: I think that is in sharp contrast --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay.\par \par \uc2 COONS: -- to President Biden's defense of the rule of law and the agenda he's putting forward to strengthen our law enforcement and our border security.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay, and that is a whole 'nother show. And listen, we're going to get to some of that later because we are talking about cases here at the Supreme Court that involved January 6th defendants, who I think most of the people out there think, if you assault a law enforcement officer, that's a crime.\par \par \uc2 But let me talk to you about what else is coming from the court because you sit on the Judiciary Committee. You have great interest, as all Americans do, in what's happening here.\par \par \uc2 You know, there's been some praise from the administration on things like the Mifepristone decision, the abortion drug, 9-0, leaving that on the market, the justices did that. And also an 8-1 decision this week saying, if you're under a domestic violence order, you should not have access to a gun.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, the president will brag about things like defying the Supreme Court who told him he could not forgive billions of dollars in student loans.\par \par \uc2 So, you talk about law and order. Will this president respect what comes from this court, with respect to the presidential immunity, with respect to January 6th, or does he just pick and choose the decisions he likes in the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Look, I think there's a sharp contrast between former President Trump and President Biden in terms of their respect for the rule of law and how they approach both law enforcement and our legal system. Former President Trump was in a courtroom in New York, where he attacked the prosecutor, the prosecution, the judge, the jury, the whole process so many times that before he was ultimately convicted by a jury of his peers of 34 felonies, he was subject to a gag order.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, we just saw evidence of this, this past week. His own son was convicted here in a court in Wilmington, Delaware, and he did not ever attack or criticize or question the judge, the prosecutor, the jury, the process. In fact, he said he wouldn't use the pardon power.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He has -- exactly.\par \par \uc2 COONS: That is a sharp and clear contrast in how they respect the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But he has repeatedly -- he has repeatedly attacked the Supreme Court. And he has said he doesn't care what they said on student loans.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Look, I -- I have raised questions, as have many of us in the Senate, about the lack of a binding code of ethics for the Supreme Court. One of the justices, Justice Thomas, has accepted millions of dollars of unreported gifts of travel and hospitality over many years. And it is the only court -- it is the only group of federal judges or justices who have no biding code of ethics.\par \par \uc2 We recently tried to pass through the Senate the Supreme Court Ethics, Recusal and Transparency Act and it was blocked by my Republican colleagues.\par \par \uc2 Every other federal judge in our country has a binding code of ethics. So does the Senate. So does the House. I think the Supreme Court should as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Senator, I'll ask our next guest about that, your colleague, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , on the Judiciary Committee, the ranking member as well.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, they do have guidelines, filings and ethics and otherwise, all nine of them signed a letter to you guys over in the Senate, all nine of these justices. But the debate over the ethics legislation will continue. We'll talk about that with Senator Graham.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Senator. Appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Joining me live, South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , ranking member of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, let's start there.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Should there be a more binding, more concrete ethics code for the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: The worst thing that could happen is for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and the House to determine what cases the Supreme Court hears. The bill that he talked about was killed on the floor of the Senate because it would allow lower court judges to determine when a Supreme Court justice should be recused.\par \par \uc2 It was a constitutional overreach. They're trying to micromanage the Roberts court. They're trying to destroy Alito and Thomas because they don't like the fact they're conservative judges.\par \par \uc2 I like Chris but, in his world, the people over there are part of a political team that when Democrats fell at the ballot box, people over there will fix the problem from a liberal perspective \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  changed the court.\par \par \uc2 They're squealing like stuck pigs because the Supreme Court no longer is a political body, it is actually looking at the Constitution and making constitutionally sound decisions versus political decisions. That's the problem liberals have with the court.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So the president, as you know, blasted them a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 A couple days ago at this fundraiser, he said they're completely out of kilter and a lot of pundits think that is a winning argument for him.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Rubin writing over at "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " says, "The more focus on the Supreme Court, the better for Democrats. The court is broken, unpopular, and in dire need of reform. Biden knows it and should make court reform a key campaign issue."\par \par \uc2 He even suggested it in the remarks that the court is thinking about making IVF illegal or taking away protections for IVF.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: So if Democrats can't convince you that there's something bad wrong with the court, they're going to lose because most Americans believe that they're not safer under President Biden than they were Trump.\par \par \uc2 Chris Coons, a dear friend, said that there's a good case to be made that you're safer today than you were when President Trump was president. If you believe that, you shouldn't be allowed to drive. That's silly. That's dangerously wrong. The border is beyond broken.\par \par \uc2 You have wars in Ukraine. You have the Mideast on fire. When it comes to enforcing the law, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been beyond reckless. He's taken a parole statute that's limited in nature and has given a million people parole when, on average, Trump and Biden's administration gave 5,600 people parole.\par \par \uc2 Laken Riley's murderer was paroled because they had lack of capacity in El Paso. They let him out under the parole statute because they were full. There's nothing in the statute that says you can go because you're full, and he killed this lady.\par \par \uc2 On and on and on, all of these women who've been raped and murdered have one thing in common. The people that killed them, raped them and murdered them were in our custody and let go, I think, illegally.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  better hope and pray there's presidential immunity because when he allowed the killer of Laken Riley to be released on parole because lack of capacity, I think he's subject not only to lawsuit but criminal prosecution if there's not presidential immunity.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Plus, I talked about with Senator Coons, that's a small number of people. I mean, obviously, most people who are coming here, they want a better life. They want opportunity.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ACLU\uc2 , in praising this latest move by the president, allowing people who are here, they're married to American citizens, said this, "The policy keeps committed, loving families together, which strengthens all of our communities. It's popular with voters. The act by the president is the type of humane and common-sense action that has made America stronger."\par \par \uc2 The Vice President said the same thing. We want to keep families that already exist together.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  -- from the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  point of view, that's true. From the average American's point of view, what Biden did is a disaster. He took a can of gasoline and he poured it on a fire.\par \par \uc2 He unilaterally gave 500,000 people legal status, calling it parole in place. There's nothing in our law. He's the most lawless president ever on immigration, Biden is.\par \par \uc2 There's nothing in our law called parole in place. He just -- there's nothing in our law allows him to forgive student loans. If you're out there working and didn't go to college, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's forgiving student loans and you're paying for them. This is beyond lawless and beyond dangerous.\par \par \uc2 So the idea is spreading around the world. Biden just gave amnesty legally to half a million people. Maybe I should keep coming. The reason the border's broken is Biden chose to overturn every policy Trump had. We had the most secure border in December 2020, the most lawless border now because of policy changes by Biden.\par \par \uc2 And it's not a small deal, it's a big deal. The largest number of people on the terrorist watch list came in last year, only to be exceeded this year. I've never been more worried about a 9/11 than I am now, originating from a broken border started by a terrible decision to withdraw in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: The world is on fire, folks, and Biden's not going to fix it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so why haven't you guys fixed it? That's what he says, right over here in the Capitol, that you guys know the problem, you agree there's a problem, and you haven't fixed it.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: You know who I am? I was in the Gang of Eight.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I remember.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: The bill they introduced was a joke when it came to parole. Biden has paroled a million people in this country illegally, in my view. Trump and \uc2 Obama\uc2  presidencies, 5,600 people paroled based on two limited factors. The bill had nothing to do with parole. It allowed 5,000 people a day to come. That just incentivizes more illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 The border was secure. The wall was almost finished. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  issued 96 executive orders changing remain in Mexico. Folks, the reason we're overrun now is because of the policy choices of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: He could fix this without any congressional action. He chooses not to because he's an open borders president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I will say for the record, because we've had your friend Senator Lankford on the show who said that 5,000, once you hit that in a week, everything closes down, nobody after that. You guys can debate that. But --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, you're going to get 4,999 every day.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, well, let's talk about foreign policy because there's a lot brewing between the White House --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- and Prime Minister \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Ahead of his speech coming here on Capitol Hill, "Politico" reports this. The headline, "No One Knows What He's Going To Say, White House Fears are Mounting About His Visit."\par \par \uc2 Senator Chris Murphy said he's only about himself. And they're worried that he's going to come here and criticize our American president for not supporting them enough. They've been very public about that. Should you welcome a foreign leader to come speak on the floor, in the House when you know possibly they're going to go after our sitting president?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: This is just not a foreign leader. It is the leader of Israel, our best friend in the region, one of our best friends in the world. Israel is surrounded by Islamic extremists who want to cut their throats literally.\par \par \uc2 And here's what you need to understand. All those people that want to kill Israel want to kill you, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , and Iran. So I will welcome him here.\par \par \uc2 Here's what bothers me the most. Slow-walking weapons to Israel makes the war go on longer. We need to be all in, in helping Israel. Give them the weapons they need to win a war they can't afford to lose. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says they'll keep doing it again and again and again until they destroy it. I believe \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The great Satan's Iran who's activating \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . So the administration's tried to use weapons in Ukraine and Israel to micromanage wars. They've tried to give Ukraine just enough to stay in the fight and not to win. They've been slow on artillery, slow on F-16s, slow on tanks. Now they're slow with Israel.\par \par \uc2 But I got a call just a few minutes ago. The Israelis say they see some progress, but I promise you this, when we come back, there will be oversight on the weapons delivery systems to Israel. Congress has voted in a bipartisan way to give Israel the bombs and the missiles they need to win a war they can't afford to lose.\par \par \uc2 And we will be looking over Biden's shoulder to see if he's honoring the commitments made by Congress. The fact we're having this debate emboldens \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . The fact that we will not sanction the \uc2 International Criminal Court\uc2  for charging the Israeli Prime Minister and Defense Minister's war criminals is outrageous.\par \par \uc2 Every time Biden does this, it juices up the terrorists. They see this as a separation between Israel and the United States, and it makes the war go on longer. And it's aiding and abetting the enemy even though they don't want to.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Keep us updated on those phone calls, Senator. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I will.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thanks for coming with us, live.\par \par \uc2 All right. Up next, we're going to take a deep dive into all the biggest, thorniest cases at the Supreme Court. They're coming this week within days. Our expert legal panel is up next here on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are coming to you live from the Supreme Court. 14 cases left to be decided in the next week. At the top of nearly every watch list is a decision about President Trump's assertion that he has immunity, shielding him from criminal liability in Special Counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 's case against him. Smith cites his actions during the final days of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 There's also a case involving a January 6th defendant and whether the Justice Department wrongly charged him under a specific obstruction statute that was also used to charge hundreds of others January 6th defendants and President Trump, too. There's also an abortion case, a case testing the boundaries of authority for federal agencies, another one that will impact whether cities and states can ban homeless encampments, and much more.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now to discuss the Court's biggest week of the year, Fox News contributor and \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  Law Professor Jonathan Turley, also the author of a brand-new book, "The Indispensable Right: Free Speech in an Age of Rage." And former Principal Deputy Assistant Attorney General Tom Dupree.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, good to see you again, different setting.\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER PRINCIPAL DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: A field trip.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But -- it is a field trip to where all the action is going to be this week. So let's start on the immunity case because there are those who feel like it should have been delivered long ago. An opinion piece in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  says something's rotten about how long it's taking the justices. This court has lost the benefit of the doubt for myriad reasons, including its willingness to act quickly in cases that benefit Republican interests. Tom, they say there's something going on here.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: I totally disagree with that, Shannon. I -- it looks quiet this morning, but there's a beehive of activity behind those marble walls. They're finishing up the immunity decision and the other key decisions we're waiting for.\par \par \uc2 On the immunity decision, look, the Supreme Court is going to be writing for the ages. This is a case that is not just going to resolve or address the cases against President Trump. It's going to be a rule of constitutional law that will affect all United States presidents going forward. They want to get it right.\par \par \uc2 And I, for one, am not going to fault them if they take an extra week or an extra two weeks to make sure that they write a thorough, correct, accurate, constitutionally sound opinion.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and you referenced this statement from Justice Gorsuch during the arguments. He said, we are writing a rule for the ages. That takes time.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: It does, and people need to understand is that this is a critical line between the president and the rest of the world, particularly Congress and the courts.\par \par \uc2 There are some issues still remaining in the Constitution that are ambiguous. This is one of those, and the court has always shied away from sort of tackling this issue. They now have to. And that means they have to get it right.\par \par \uc2 And what you heard from the justices on both sides of the ideological spectrum, I thought was really serious efforts to try to get it right. Not that they agreed, but unlike the media, unlike the commentators, those nine justices had a civil, and one of the best discussions I've ever heard in the Supreme Court is they tried to probe where this line is.\par \par \uc2 And none of the justices seemed that satisfied. Well, let me put it this way. The court as a whole didn't feel satisfied with the extreme positions of either the Trump team or the lower court. They seem to be looking for something more nuanced.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and the whole conversation about whether if they gave the sweeping immunity that the Trump team would like, the worries about allowing somebody to take office knowing they could do anything with no repercussions at all, it sounds like they're going to find some level of immunity and then have to decide what were the official acts that would get some of that protection, and that could take some time. It could kick this thing back to the lower court, extending the whole case.\par \par \uc2 OK, the other big one that we're waiting on, well, there are many. It depends on your opinion, but January 6th, there's a defendant who brought his claim saying this obstruction statute that they used was not right.\par \par \uc2 Now, it applied to hundreds of others. It's part of the case against President Trump. I want to read the statute. It says, "Whoever corruptly obstructs, influences, or impedes any official proceeding or attempts to do so shall be fined or imprisoned. Jonathan, that gets to what happened on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, there is a huge amount at stake here because obstruction of an official proceeding was the common denominator running through hundreds of cases. The Department of Justice is like a gambler in Vegas that would only bet on red. I mean, this is the red chip, you know, the red slot in roulette.\par \par \uc2 And if it's eliminated, all bets are off. And I think the court very well could narrow this because the underlying statute was designed after the Enron case and dealt with the destruction of documents. And the lower court, the trial court, said, I don't think this fits.\par \par \uc2 The D.C. Circuit said it's close enough for us. Clearly, many of the justices were having trouble with extending this law this far. If they narrow this interpretation, it will not only knock out hundreds of criminal counts, it'll knock out counts pending against President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, that \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  has brought.\par \par \uc2 OK, now, Tom and I have a favorite. We're the walking nerds of this whole thing. There's this case that talks about \uc2 Chevron\uc2  Deference and what it does, it gives enormous power to federal agencies where you would say these are the experts or the bureaucrats who know this subject matter best to make decisions about how federal regulations impact everything from the car you drive, the phone you use, the shampoo, how you use your land.\par \par \uc2 The Center for American Progress said this about that case. The case cannot be -- the stakes could not be higher. The outcome could affect nearly every policy area, health care, civil rights, workers' rights, education, environmental justice, financial regulation, to name just a few.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: For once, I agree with the \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2 . This is an important and fascinating case. And look, you put your finger on it. This case does fundamentally affect the balance of power in our government and in our society.\par \par \uc2 The question basically is if you have a law that involves a federal agency's regulatory power, and that law is ambiguous or open to debate, who gets to say what the law means? Is it the federal bureaucrats or is it a judge? Most people would say it's a judge.\par \par \uc2 In fact, in our nation's earliest famous constitutional law case, Marbury v. Madison, the Supreme Court said it's the power of the courts to say what the law is. And I anticipate the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  in this case is going to reset that balance and say that in that situation, it's the power of the courts, not the federal bureaucrats, to say what the law really means.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You shall know maybe as early as Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, there are these other federal cases, of course, playing out with the president. The one dealing with the documents and the storage and the handling of these classified documents down at Mar-a-Lago, the judge there, Aileen Cannon, was appointed by President Trump and "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " had an interesting piece this week said, that when she was assigned the case, there were other judges who went to her to say to sort of pressure her like, you don't have the experience for this. Maybe you shouldn't take this case