 try and shift the blame on this, but you're the leader of the free world. And when he walked off the west front of the Capitol on January 20th of 2021, the first thing he did, Shannon, was sign executive orders repealing the key cornerstones of the Trump immigration policy. He repealed remain in Mexico. He repealed the asylum reforms. He - he put an end to the construction of the border barrier and wall and he left it sitting there, literally billions of dollars, down a rat hole. And the border wall that was being built sat there and rusted. They - they burned the money rather than finish the job. That's how more concerned he was about playing politics and erasing Trump's legacy than he was about protecting the American people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 REED: He owns every one of these tragedies as a result.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are out of time, panel. We've got to go. But we thank you so much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 WALLING: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, I sat down with retired Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 . He opens up about his nearly 30 years here on the highest court in the land.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Retired Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  spent nearly three decades here on the Supreme Court, wading through many difficult, historic cases. He's got a book out now "Reading the Constitution: Why I Chose Pragmatism Not Textualism."\par \par \uc2 I sat down with Justice Breyer to talk about why he - how he tackled the cases, the most challenging ones, his worries about the court as an institution and whether he misses being over here on the bench.\par \par \uc2 It's our Sunday special.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I think it's remarkable that you boil things down in a way that whether you're a lawyer or not, if you have any interest in what's going on in jurisprudence you're going to enjoy this book.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN BREYER\uc2 , RETIRED SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What was the goal for you?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: What was the goal? The goal - I've been a judge, hard to believe, 40 years. And I think most people, they don't know what we do. What is an appeals court judge do? What does a Supreme Court judge do? And I want them to understand. I want them to see why the cases are difficult. I want them to see the different approaches people can take. I, of course, have my preferences.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You do.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: And I want them to see why. And then I'd like to say enough about people who don't agree with me that they get a fair picture of that and they can make up their minds.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you worry about sort of the hit the Supreme Court has taken in the recent years? If you see the Gallup polls, you see the Pew reports. Do justices worry about that?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Or do you feel like we're doing our job? You do worry about the court as an institution?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Of course.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And how people perceive it?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Of course. Of course you do. I mean it takes you a few years and - because the first two years, I promise you, wandering around that place, and you think, how did I ever get appointed? You don't tell anyone you're thinking that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You keep it on the inside.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Yes. Yes. But - but, can I really do this. And I was told by one president, I'll tell you something, the applause wears off very fast.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, when it comes to issues that you feel are ripe as a justice, and you all get a vote in what cases come before you.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But there are political implications potentially.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Or at least the public would view it as having a public impact. How tough is it to vote on whether or not you will hear that particular dispute?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: It depends on the case. It depends on the case. I - when I was a law clerk, I worked for Arthur Goldberg.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: He was a justice.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: And, 1964, that's ten years after Brown. And there is still, in my opinion, what that court was worried about and interested in is, how are we going to get Brown enforced? Can we? Where will we get the help? Not too much help. Not too much help outside the court. But we want to get it enforced.\par \par \uc2 You listen too much to popular opinion and you're not going to have a court. You never listen and you don't know whether or not the laws will be enforced, you'll have a rule of law. And you say, what's the answer to that? This is not computer science.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There's no formula.\par \par \uc2 BREYER: No.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you miss it at all?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You, obviously, stay very busy. But do you miss the - your colleagues and the work?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: Of course. Of course. The difference, I think, if you're a professor, which I have been for some time, right, you know pretty well if you spend the time that you should, what the cases have decided, what people have written. But when you are a judge, you also have to take into account, how is that going to affect this now? And how is it going to affect this in the future? And you'll not get a good answer. And you won't be sure. And you'll just try to do your best in - in those matters. It isn't something where there are clear answers in most - in difficult cases. And there are many difficult cases.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Our thanks to Justice Breyer.\par \par \uc2 And this programming note. Tune into "Fox News Democracy '24," special coverage of the CNN presidential debate simulcast. Fox News Channel coverage begins Thursday, June 27th at 8:00 p.m. Eastern. And then the CNN presidential debate simulcast will begin at 9:00 p.m. Eastern and right here on your local Fox affiliate I'll join you for a special half-hour of pregame at 8:30 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today from Capitol Hill and the Supreme Court. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a wonderful week. We'll see you right back here for our next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020240623ek6n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden's Pathetic Slogans Aren't Stopping Trump; Team Biden Presses The Panic Button; Three Dead, 13 Hurt in Shooting Outside Arkansas Store; Heat Wave Expected to Continue this Weekend\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Laura Ingraham, Kevin Corke, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 7,173 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: What a week. All right, Monday on Special Report, we begin our series looking at voter's top issues ahead of the first presidential debate. We start with immigration and the border crisis. Thanks for watching Special Report. I'm Shannon Bream in Washington. We'll be live from the Supreme Court, Fox News Sunday. The Ingraham Angle is next.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, everyone. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is The Ingraham Angle from Washington tonight. As always, thanks so much for joining us. Now desperate to stop the MAGA Express, the Biden campaign has tried out more slogans than Leo DiCaprio was tried out 22- year-olds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're going to build back better. We have to, we must, we will, because that's who we are. Today in commerce (ph) aren't making much fun of Bidenomics anymore. They're thinking maybe it works to build from the middle out and the bottom up. January 6th happened when he unleashed an insurrection. Now he's running again, and it's clearly unhinged. Let me tell you about this ultra MAGA agenda, it's extreme as most MAGA things are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, is any of that landing? Well, while the national head-to-head polls are tightening a bit. Historically, this is some of the best swing state polling that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has ever seen. He's leading in five key battleground states, even in the elusive Pennsylvania. And whoa, check out Biden's approval ratings in some of these states. 36 percent in Arizona, 35 percent in Georgia, 39 percent in Michigan, Pennsylvania, and 38 percent in Minnesota and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Wow. The Wilmington wonder, Is that what we're calling him tonight like he's wildly unpopular. And it's not a communications problem, comms problem, as we say here in Washington. It's a policy problem. His policies are killing America, literally, by giving illegal alien gang members and killers a bus ticket and a free phone, come on in