nder ideology play into this? And isn't this just rank religious discrimination by the state of Vermont?\par \par \uc2 MALLORY SLEIGHT, \uc2 ADF\uc2  LEGAL COUNSEL: It absolutely is. And really, gender ideology doesn't play into this. In fact, the department chose the Gantts out of all of their foster parents because they're so great with kids who are dependent on opioids.\par \par \uc2 And yet, because of their religious beliefs, the department then changes their minds and says, not only can you not foster this child, you can't foster any infant baby, even for a couple hours for respite care because of your religious beliefs.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Bryan, I've got to say, I've looked into this case, and you have an exemplary record, unless I'm missing something, of what you've done for kids who are unwanted in the state of Vermont. And you and your wife are like, incredible. And you've gotten praise.\par \par \uc2 The licensing caseworker has said that I hope we continue to find more families like yours. And the resource coordinator said the whole department agrees that you're the perfect home and first choice for this baby.\par \par \uc2 Again, Pastor, I mean, your religious beliefs, your belief in God, and the Bible means that all those words, I guess, they don't matter anymore.\par \par \uc2 GANTT: Apparently not. You know, again, we have had such a wonderful relationship with the department for seven years. You know, it's just our heart as Christians to care for these kids that are in need.\par \par \uc2 I pastor downtown, and I see the needs of family. I see the drug crisis every day from my office as I look outside. And we just want to be part of this solution.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, Mallory, not only do they want to fuel more homelessness and drug addiction by pushing legalization everywhere, but now they want to take away foster homes from unwanted babies because they hate Christians. I'm sorry, I'm being very blunt tonight. But that seems what is going on. They get you both ways.\par \par \uc2 SLEIGHT: Only a couple days ago, the department sent out another email to everybody on their list begging for any ideas they had or solutions because they're out of options. And it's such a placement crisis. And they have so many children that they can't place that they're begging for help.\par \par \uc2 And yet, we represent two families right here who have adopted five kids and they're told you cannot adopt or foster because of your religious beliefs.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Christians need not apply. Bryan and Mallory, will be following this. Thank you. Now the left doesn't want Christians to adopt. And now it turns out they would prefer that you don't have a baby at all.\par \par \uc2 The Massachusetts Department of Health just launched a $1 million taxpayer funded campaign warning about the dangers of pro-life pregnancy centers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Whether you need pregnancy care or abortion care, avoid anti- abortion centers. They may look like medical clinics, but can put your health at risk. They may mislead you about your options if you're pregnant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK. So, what dangerous things do these crisis pregnancy centers do? Well, they do things like provide free testing, free ultrasounds, baby items and counseling for alternatives to abortion. Oh, the horror.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Teresa Larkin, executive director of Your Options Medical, a pregnancy crisis center in Massachusetts. Teresa, what are the state bureaucrats really afraid of here?\par \par \uc2 TERESA LARKIN, YOUR OPTIONS MEDICAL EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: I can only surmise that they're afraid of women having their babies. As you alluded to, you know, instead of supporting and encouraging the services that we do provide, as you said, pregnancy testing, material resources, parenting classes, referrals for homeless help and other important essential services, they're focusing on what we don't do, which is provide abortions.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, they just want as many abortions as possible to occur in the state of Massachusetts. By the way, one of the most important states, as we all know, for American independence and freedom and the struggle for freedom. And this is how the left speaks about crisis pregnancy centers. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The malicious and manipulative efforts by so-called crisis pregnancy centers, to woo women into their centers by appearing as if they are a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  or the like, and then manipulating them and convincing them not to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, this guy, that Dan Goldman is a monster. So now, it's a -- it's heinous and I guess violent to not encourage a woman to, you know, to kill her own offspring. That's the monstrous thing. I mean, up is down, left is right.\par \par \uc2 Teresa, the hope out there is that when women walk into these crisis pregnancy centers, when they actually get to see their own infants, preborn babies in the womb, aren't they more likely to have that child, bring that child to term?\par \par \uc2 LARKIN: Yes, they absolutely are. You know, one of the things is women want to know when they're facing an unplanned pregnancy, they know that they're about to make one of the most important decisions of their life. They want information. They want to know.\par \par \uc2 And when they do see that ultrasound and they know that it's a baby, over 60 percent of the time they do change their mind. And also, there's studies that routinely show that over 60 percent of women that had abortions say they would not have had an abortion had they known that support and resources and financial aid, one person to believe in them would have been there for them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Teresa, more information is usually better, the left says, but not here. Very curious. Actually, not curious at all. Teresa, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 LARKIN: You're welcome. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. We've all heard the phrase hog wild. Well, a wild hog bomb could be coming your way, depending on where you live. A rancher on the front lines joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Ninety-year-old David Reisman says he keeps his head on a swivel ever since he was attacked by a pair of wild hogs right outside his front door.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I'm standing inside my parents' house with a hammer and a knife trying to defend myself and defend my family.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Neighbors say they think they're coming out of the woods nearby and plowing through their yards, in some cases causing thousands of dollars-worth of damage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wild hogs, they're now wreaking havoc across multiple states, and officials are warning that without intervention, a feral swine bomb, that's what they call it, could be coming our way.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Derrick Hunt, a rancher in Texas. Derek, now, for people who don't know really what this is or why this matters, how big can these hogs get and what damage do they really do? I mean, are they dangerous? Is it just property damage?\par \par \uc2 DERRICK HUNT, TEXAS RANCHER: Laura, these boar hogs, which are the male hogs, I've seen them up as big as 350 pounds. And yes, they do damage to our crops. They've damaged hay on our ranch at $150 a bale.\par \par \uc2 But they get into, you know, they'll get into watermelons, corn, any kind of crop you can imagine. We see, the best we can tell in the state of Texas, about $50 million in damage to agriculture per year.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, you sent us a video from when you captured a pack of hogs. Is that what we call them, a pack of hogs? Now explain the process of how you capture them and what happens after you do that.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: So, Laura, we have traps set up around our ranches and they have a - - their -- they have a cell capture system on them. So, when the hogs walk in the traps, we get a signal and we get a picture and we can remote trap them. We can release the gates.\par \par \uc2 And we try to get the whole sounder. We wait till all of them get in there. And a big group of hogs is called a sounder.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, friends of mine actually pay money to hunt wild hogs. There's a guy in Florida, I know, who takes people out hunting in the sugar cane. He's one of the few licensed in Florida to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, what happens after you trap them? I mean, you euthanize them humanely or what? I mean, they are a complete menace. I mean, I'm a big animal person, but this is invasive at this point.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Absolutely. Absolutely. The trappers, they have a big buying collective and they'll purchase them, and they go to a slaughterhouse just like a cow would. And believe it or not, they're exported to Europe quite a bit. Europeans love to eat the hogs.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, wait a second. You got to really smoke those because I've tried wild hog sausage and not great. Not my fave. Let me tell you that.\par \par \uc2 But for people to understand this, Derrick, the wild hog population back in 1982, OK. Well, here's where it is in 2023. OK, so it was pretty minimal and now it's spread. I mean, look at that map now where it is.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: It's crazy. They're in every county in the state of Texas other than El Paso County. In Texas, the best we can figure out in 2024, you got a population of about 2.6 million. And so, you can understand the math. The closest we can find is about a 30 percent offtake or harvest of those hogs per year.\par \par \uc2 And you need a 66 percent harvest just to keep the population level, Laura. Crazy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: People shooting them from helicopters with over-unders, right? That usually does