G0020240622ek6l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Putin Says Russia May Deliver Weapons To North Korea; Rare Public Show Of Friendship Between Leaders; Supreme Court To Release Opinions On Major Cases; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Debate In One Week; Immigration Accused Of Rape And Murder Previously Caught And Deported Three Times Before Reentering U.S. Illegally Last Year; Video Released Of Moments When Three Sheriff's Deputies Shot During Confrontation In Illinois; California State Supreme Court Blocks Ballot Initiative Requiring Voter Approval For Any Future State Tax Increase; President Biden And Former President Trump Prepare For First Presidential Debate; \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Set To Decide On Presidential Immunity Case\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Gillian Turner, Jennifer Griffin, Peter Doocy, Shannon  Bream, Nate Foy, Mike Tobin, William La Jeunesse, Mary Katharine Ham \par \uc2 8,038 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: A lot of people don't know that. Great burrowers.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: No, they live in streams.\par \par \uc2 They are not burrowers.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Wait a minute. How did they know that the -- it was still good? Do they taste it?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, they took them out and you can see them.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Do you want to eat those cherries?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No. Would you?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: No.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Come on, fudge, eat the cherries.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: You try it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. Yes, I'm not going to react to that anymore.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Cherries, beavers.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's -- that was for the drag queen.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's it for us tonight. Have a great night, everybody. Over to Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Much easier toss than last night. Thank you, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It was.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", two U.S. adversaries raising eyebrows in national security circles causing real concern here in Washington, as both countries' leaders tout their nuclear ambitions today.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , saying he will revisit his country's policies on using nuclear weapons, while Iran is drastically expanding one of its not-so-secret nuclear sites.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Russia. Chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin has details tonight live from the Pentagon. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 More nuclear saber rattling from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  during his second stop in Asia in search of friends, weapons for his war in Ukraine, and a return to the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 The veiled threat came Thursday during a press conference in Vietnam. Putin said he was thinking of changing Russia's nuclear doctrine, lowering the threshold for the first use of a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 Currently, Russian doctrine allows nuclear weapons to be used only in response to a nuclear strike or in the event of a conventional attack that Moscow views as an existential threat.\par \par \uc2 In a master class of verbal jujitsu, Putin blamed the West for working on low-yield nuclear devices, when, in fact, it's Russia that is doing so.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translator): We must pay attention to this and we are paying attention.\par \par \uc2 This is connected to my statement of what we are thinking about possible changes in our strategy. We do not need preemptive strike yet, because in counter strike, the enemy will guarantee to be destroyed.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The threat came one day after Putin met with Kim Jong-un, his first trip to North Korea in 24 years, and signed a defense treaty, promising to provide military assistance to each other if either is attacked, a rare public show of friendship inside the hermit nation.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: It demonstrates the desperation that a country like Russia needs to align itself with the DPRK to subjugate the people of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The head of the House Intelligence Committee warned again about Russia's plans to put nuclear weapons in space.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2  (R-OH): The administration is sleepwalking into an irreversible Day Zero. This threat would mean that our economic, international security, and social systems come to a grinding halt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Putin's efforts in North Korea may have already backfired. South Korea, which until now has only provided humanitarian aid to Ukraine, said today, it would now consider lethal aid to Kyiv, not exactly the response Putin was looking for. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: and Jen, another topic. The Pentagon pushing back on reporting that the humanitarian pier it built off the coast of Gaza would be disassembled early. What about that?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, rough seas and bad weather, Bret, have stymied a deliveries. The troubled pier assembled by the U.S. military has been operational just 11 days since it was anchored on May 17.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Pentagon announced the pier which had been disassembled and towed to Israel, in anticipation of more bad weather was reassembled and re-anchored allowing some aid to begin flowing again.\par \par \uc2 The pier was assembled to bypass Israel, which was preventing enough aid from flowing through the land routes into Gaza controls. The US has spent $230 million to send 9.1 million pounds of food and supplies into Gaza; much of it still waiting in Gaza warehouses for distribution. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. We'll follow that. Jennifer, thanks. Senior state department officials, meantime, say they have grave concerns over Iran's efforts to advance its nuclear weapons program.\par \par \uc2 The country is now expanding a top nuclear site and doing it quickly. State Department correspondent Gillian Turner has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The \uc2 U.N.\uc2 's top nuclear watchdog has confirmed one of Iran's most secretive nuclear sites is undergoing a dramatic expansion, alarming top U.S. officials.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW MILLER\uc2 , SPOKESMAN, UNITED STATES STATE DEPARTMENT: Iran continues to expand its nuclear program in ways that have no credible peaceful purpose. We remain deeply concerned with Iran's nuclear activities and we'll continue to vigilantly monitor them. Iran must cooperate with the \uc2 IAEA\uc2  without further delay.\par \par \uc2 TURNER (voice over): A leaked draft document first obtained by Reuters reveals Iran's plan to triple the site's production of enriched uranium with brand new centrifuges.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Blinken met with senior Israeli officials today, just days after Prime Minister Netanyahu accused the U.S. of slow walking arms to Israel. A claim the U.S. denies.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhi