y is to be stage right, which means that next week, the last word in the debate will go to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you. We are one week away from full coverage here on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 We may be just hours away from blockbuster \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  opinions on presidential immunity, and other major issues.\par \par \uc2 Justices released four opinions today, including one on taxes that has a number of people scratching their heads. Fox News chief legal correspondent, anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" Shannon Bream, explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES MOORE, PETITIONER: If you haven't received any income, how can you be required to pay income taxes?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (voice over): According to the Supreme Court, you can.\par \par \uc2 Charles and Kathleen Moore, partnered with a friend to invest in a company operating and providing tools to farmers in India.\par \par \uc2 And while claiming they never saw a return on their investment other than the joy they say they got in helping to make a difference half a world away, they did get a tax bill for nearly $15,000. The company did make or realize income, but it never distributed any to the Moores. The \uc2 IRS\uc2  still build them based on the mandatory repatriation tax, a part of the Trump-era tax bill, passed by Congress back in 2017.\par \par \uc2 The Moores sued, calling it unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Supreme Court held 7-2 that Congress did not exceed its authority in crafting that one-time special tax.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kavanaugh, writing for the majority, "Congress has long taxed shareholders of an entity on the entity's undistributed income. This Court has long upheld taxes of that kind, and we do the same today."\par \par \uc2 But Kavanaugh was clear to note what the Court did not decide. Whether today's opinion would apply to any so-called wealth tax on the assets, not income of the richest Americans. Kavanaugh call that a hypothetical the Court is avoiding for now.\par \par \uc2 But Justice Thomas, in a dissent joined by Justice Gorsuch, said the 16th Amendment which allows for federal income tax only applies to income actually realized by the taxpayer. "The text and history of the Amendment make clear that it requires a distinction between income and the source from which that income is derived. And, the only way to draw such a distinction is with a realization requirement".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): The Court said, if it had followed the Moore's arguments all the way to the extreme, that it could have created such upheaval to the U.S. tax system that it could have cost trillions in incoming tax revenue. Ultimately, saying this, the Constitution does not require that fiscal calamity. Bret, we'll do this again at 10:00 a.m. Eastern on Friday, next round of opinions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, there are a lot of opinions yet to come. Right? I mean, this docket, they've got a lot of work to do here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They do. And it really has not been in the 17 years I've been covering the court, this late in the term that we've had this many big ones we are waiting on. There is everything from presidential immunity, which, of course, impacts the federal and legal cases against President Trump. But all future presidents as well.\par \par \uc2 We've got the January 6th obstruction charges against hundreds of defendants, including President Trump, which you've got abortion, you've got guns, you've got social media censorship, you've also got federal regulatory power. So, just to note, we've got 19 left, and the court has added next Wednesday as an opinion day as well. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you will be busy. Shannon Bream outside the Court. Shannon, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talk with two political consultants. One Republican one Democrat about the presidential race and the latest polling. And then, dramatic video, showing the moments when three law enforcement officers were shot.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET O'DONNELL, VETERAN GOP DEBATE COACH: It's very clear from the polling data that the American people are feeling like their lives are in chaos.\par \par \uc2 MITCH LANDRIEU, JOE BIDEN CAMPAIGN NATIONAL CO-CHAIR: This is going to be a very close election. There's no reason for anybody to believe that it won't be.\par \par \uc2 He's ready to go. He demonstrates that all the time.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH): He's thinking about how to translate these really, really important topics into a message that works. And obviously, he's as good as anybody. That's why he's leading all the polls.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, we are one week -- one week from the first presidential debate, consequential. We're going to take a look at the presidential race, the latest polling.\par \par \uc2 Joining us, Republican media consultant Alex Castellanos and Democratic strategist Matt Bennett. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 You know, we have the new polls out and one that was interested, motivated to vote in the presidential election, extremely is now 66 percent. You see, Republicans at 74, Democrats at 64. And very is at 16 percent.\par \par \uc2 People are starting to plug in well before traditional Labor Day movement. And this debate is a big deal early.\par \par \uc2 ALEX CASTELLANOS, REPUBLICAN MEDIA CONSULTANT: It's a big deal early. I think, though Republicans should control their high hopes here. Because I think Biden has performed at big events like States of the Union. They give him his debate Viagra, and he's good for, you know, a good 30 minutes. If he's still debating after four hours, we'll have to call the doctors.\par \par \uc2 But and Trump is going to be much more disciplined I think this time, he 