y 13th, he crossed undetected in El Paso, Texas, and entered the United States as a got-away. He is one of just under 1.9 million known got-aways under President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . We're averaging about 500 per day over the past week, and there are 194,000 known got-aways so far this fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Martinez Hernandez is from El Salvador. He's charged with first degree murder, rape, and assault. He is separately accused of another homicide in El Salvador and assaulting a woman and nine-year-old girl in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 The 37-year-old Morin went missing while going for a run last August. Search crews found her body the next day. Today her family received a call from former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Morin's mother Patty described it as comforting, saying, quote, "I am deeply touched by President Trump's kindness and concern. He was genuine and truly wanted to know how our family was coping."\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Martinez Hernandez is in a Maryland jail cell awaiting trial. ICE has lodged an immigration detainer against him. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy in New York. Nate, Thanks.\par \par \uc2 FOY: You got it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has raided a home connected to Oakland Mayor Sheng Thao just a day after a recall campaign to oust her from office received enough signatures to qualify for the November ballot. Property records indicate Thao is associated with the property but does not own it. Recall organizers say the mayor has blood on her hands amid rising crime, auto theft, and street robberies.\par \par \uc2 We have dramatic video tonight of the moments when three sheriff's deputies were shot during a confrontation in Illinois. Senior correspondent Mike Tobin shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Shots fired, shots fired.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Stunning video showing the peril law enforcement officers can face in an instant. The deputies and officers with the Ogle County Emergency Response Team pull a wounded officer to safety while still under fire. The alleged gunman, 32-year-old Jonathon Gounaris, was arraigned today on charges of attempted first degree murder, aggravated discharge of a firearm, and possession of a gun without an Illinois firearm owner's identification card.\par \par \uc2 The incident was June 12th in a remote area northwest of Dixon, Illinois, known as Lost Lake. Gounaris's mother called 911 saying her son was threatening to kill himself, harm others, and was armed. Deputies attempted to negotiate with Gounaris for an hour-and-a-half. A phone was even thrown into the house.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: After more than 60 attempted calls were made with no answer, it was authorized to enter the residence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Wait, wait, wait.\par \par \uc2 (GUNFIRE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Shots fired.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: One task force member was protected by body armor when shot. Another was hit in the arm. Deputy Jason Ketter was shot in the face, triaged on the scene, and rushed to a hospital in Rockford.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN VANVICKLE, OGLE COUNTY, ILLINOIS SHERIFF: The suspect Jonathan G. Gounaris, 32, was located inside the front door of the residence with a gunshot wound to the stomach. Located near or on Gounaris were multiple firearms.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Deputy Ketter underwent surgery and left the hospital Friday to the applause of fellow officers.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Gounaris appeared in court today by video. He has been treated for a gunshot wound to the stomach. The stiffest charge he faces, attempted first degree murder, carries a maximum penalty of 80 years in prison. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, Californians will not get a chance to vote on new tax policy. We'll take you live out there and tell you why.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. WSVN in Miami with the arrest of rapper Travis Scott on trespassing and disorderly intoxication charges. Police say they were called to the Miami Beach Marina early this morning where residents reported people fighting on a yacht. They say Scott refused multiple requests to leave.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look at Rickwood Field in Birmingham, Alabama, from \uc2 FOX Sports\uc2 . One of the big stories there tonight from FOX 6, the historic field hosts a game between the \uc2 St. Louis Cardinals\uc2  and the \uc2 San Francisco Giants\uc2 . The contest in the country's oldest professional ballpark is in honor of the Negro Leagues. There will be a special tribute to Willie Mays who played there as a young man. Mays, as we told you, died Tuesday at the age of 93. You can see the game on FOX Broadcast Network starting at the top of the hour.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tropical storm Alberto is bearing down on Mexico tonight. The first named storm of the season has left at least four people dead so far. It is already affecting the southern U.S. We'll continue to follow that.\par \par \uc2 New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  has signed a bill that would allow parents to block their children from getting social media posts suggested by a platform's algorithm. Feeds on apps such as TikTok and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  would be limited for people under age 18 to post from accounts they follow. The measure would also block platforms from sending minors notifications on suggested posts between midnight and 6:00 a.m.\par \par \uc2 Californians will not be allowed to decide on a ballot initiative this November that would have required voter approval for any future state tax hikes. The state supreme court says that can only be done by changing the state constitution. National correspondent William La Jeunesse has details tonight from Los Angeles. Good evening, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: What this means, Bret, is Governor Newsom has successfully denied Californians the opportunity to vote on a proposition that had already qualified for the November ballot by an overwhelming margin. The Taxpayer Protection Act would have given voters the right to approve or reject new state and local taxes, and stop government agencies from imposing hidden taxes disguised as fees. But Governor Newsom and the Democrat supermajority in Sacramento said the initiative would deprive the state of money for necessary services. They also argued the act represented an illegal revision of the state's constitution, rather than just an amendment.\par \par \uc2 Well, the court agreed unanimously, with six of the seven judges appointed by Newsom or Governor Jerry Brown. The initiative sponsors say the decision proves California is, quote, a one party state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JON COUPAL\uc2 , HOWARD JARVIS TAXPAYERS ASSOCIATION: This is absolute power. And this is why the legislature seems consumed with all manner of issues that have nothing to do with the betterment of California but everything to do with the exercise of political power.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Critics also accuse the governor of hypocrisy. Newsom frequently lectures Republicans about protecting democracy, whereas he actually went to court to preemptively stop a vote using taxpayer money to file the suit.\par \par \uc2 Nationally, this is also a big win for Democrats because had the act been on the ballot, it could have turned out thousands of conservative and moderate voters that could have made a difference in six very close House elections here in the battle in control of Congress. Bret, late today, the governor thanked the Supreme Court for protecting the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks mixed today after taking yesterday off for the Juneteenth holiday. The Dow surging about 300 points. The S&P 500 lost 14 for the day after briefly topping 5,500 for the first time. The Nasdaq dropped 141.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the latest on the presidential race and this week's key \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  rulings.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Reuters identifies 14 graveyards expanding quickly in Sudan's war-torn Darfur region. It reports starvation and disease taking a mounting toll as combatants block food and medical aid. One author calls the cemeteries the canaries in the coalmine in Sudan.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Jerusalem. One of the big stories there tonight, a company drilling for natural gas off the coast of northern Israel discovers a 3,300-year-old ship and its cargo. Authorities say it is one of the oldest known examples of a ship sailing far from land. It indicates the navigation abilities of ancient sea farers were more advanced than previously thought.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  SENIOR ADVISER: We've never had a presidential debate where it could have as volatile an impact on the election.\par \par \uc2 KATE BEDINGFIELD\uc2 , CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: That first debate in 2020 did not go well for him. Everybody who has an exception that that's the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that's going to show up next week, I don't think that's true.\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES\uc2 , CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: If Biden goes out there and messes up, it's game over. If he walks out there and a week later he is lower in the poll, it's panic in the party.\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: This is what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to do. He's going to focus on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's record. His record now as a convicted felon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is all the talk. It's one week from tonight, the first presidential debate. You'll see it simulcast right here on FOX News Channel with complete coverage. The headline is the coin toss has happened, and President Biden has chosen the right side podium for this debate, and the former president Trump will have the last word. Just to remind people, take you back to September of 2020, the first debate last time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Make sure you, in fact, let people know --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: He doesn't want to answer the question.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: -- your Senator. I'm not going to answer the question.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Why won't you answer that question?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because the question is, the question is, the question is --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : -- radical left.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Will you shut up, man?\par \par \uc2 That was really a productive segment, wasn't it?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Keep yapping, man.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: The people understand, Joe. For 47 years you have done nothing. They understand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, there you go.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Mary Katharine Ham, columnist for "Outkick", Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico," and Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 . I was talking to those pollsters, Democrat, Republican, Matthew, earlier about how consequential this one debate may be. This early, and it's really notable that they are not even going to be official nominees by the time the debate starts.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: Absolutely. I think it's important for former President Trump, he has had kind of a rocky news cycle with the conviction in New York. It's starting to show up in polls like the new FOX poll. So I think he needs to go into the debate next week and he really needs to reframe the election once more about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's performance in office, because when you look at the internals of our poll here at FOX, you see that Trump continues to win on the issues, especially the economy, especially immigration. If he can reset things about the debate, make it about the issues rather than about the conviction, I think he will have a successful night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mary Katharine, we see a slight shift in independents in some of the recent polls in recent days, including ours. But if you look at swing state polls, the Real Clear Politics average of all the key states, the former president is leading in the states he needs lead in, some of them significantly, that he lost before.\par \par \uc2 MARY KATHARINE HAM, FOX NEWS MEDIA CONTRIBUTOR: Right, and that is a huge deal. And you balance it against the slight shift that we are seeing, which I would like to see more of to know whether it's a blip or a pattern here.\par \par \uc2 But what was good for both of these guys is going into a big moment like this, you can view this as a tied game, quite realistically, and they probably should, particularly Trump, because he didn't do well against Biden last time, because he was too punchy, because he wasn't, maybe, taking it as seriously as he should. There's reports that his debate prep has been pretty extensive this time around, having policy briefings if not mock debates. Biden is a little bit more traditional. I think both of them want to highlight the disorder and chaos of the leadership of the other, and what the risk is that we'll descend into disorder and chaos on the stage, which is what they want to avoid. A temperate performance, particularly from Trump, I think would do well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Olivia, I mentioned that Iowa poll, "Des Moines Register", that said 60 plus percent of the people said they were already exhausted. So that is something to considers as we head into that first debate as well.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": It is. We have seen the two of them going at it before. But I think that one thing that we haven't seen is it's been unprecedented that we don't have any primary debates. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  really didn't have one that he did. Trump refused to. And now we finally get to see them come together. I have been thinking about how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was on Capitol Hill not too long ago, and he got a ton of praise from the Senate and from the House because he had kind of pulled back what Republicans would say would maybe be his worst instincts. And they are hoping that they see him doing exactly what he did on the Hill on the debate stage, which is being himself but not going out and creating sort of a scandalous moment.\par \par \uc2 HAM: I would say that moment he came out and gave a little spiel after his meeting on the Hill hit all the right notes. He talked about the economy. He talked about inflation. He talked about the border. He talked about crime. And then he was like, I'm out of here, thank you, unity. That tone will help him immensely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I bet that President Biden is going to prepare to try to stoke the former president in a number of ways, including calling him a convicted felon.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Absolutely. And he will try to troll Trump, as he often does in these fundraisers, which is really the only time we get to see Biden talk extemporaneously.\par \par \uc2 But I think the rules of the debate are interesting, too. They're muting the mics when the other candidate is talking. That does offer Trump an opportunity to just kind of sit back. I think that the longer Biden talks, usually the worst it gets for Biden. And so the danger there is that Trump steps on Biden right before Biden begins to unravel. And that's a place where, again, some restraint on Trump's part could pay off big at the end of the debate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meanwhile, the Supreme Court, massive things are being decided. We could have them tomorrow, we could have them next week. But the biggest in play right now is the immunity case, which really factors into the former president and this case about immunity. Here's the "Politico" playbook, essentially three outcomes. Trump's claim is rejected, which could clear the way for a criminal trial on the charges, although whether that would be before the election is still unclear, the court finds that the former president has complete immunity and the federal case headed up by Special Counsel \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  is dead, dramatically expanding the scope of executive powers in the process, or the justices make a split decision requiring Judge Chutkan to separate the actions Trump took in his official capacity as president from those he took as a political candidate, which almost certainly push the case's start past the elections. It's pretty significant, Mary Katharine.\par \par \uc2 HAM: It's hugely significant. And that's part of the reason that they have been deciding it for a while. What's interesting to me since I don't know the outcome of this yet is that some of the tea leaf reading tends to extend this attack on -- to discredit the Supreme Court. And I think when you say, hey, they might decide this wrong so that we can't convict him before the election, you are a little bit giving away the game about what is going on here and appreciate that the justices are not playing that particular game.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Olivia, thoughts?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: I think that this will have huge implications for the presidential race, for other presidents going forward. And a lot of legal experts, people in politics are watching to see whether the Supreme Court defines what is official action, whether they give more parameters of what is covered in immunity. And we're very curious to see what happens.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And Matthew, there is also implications for January 6th folks that are in jail, being held on different charges.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: That's a separate case involving the obstruction charge. And there the Supreme Court could rule in a way that invalidates many of the January 6th convictions.\par \par \uc2 I think on the larger question of immunity, it is funny to me that the same people who are talking about how important this case is are attacking the court for taking its time. Well, if it's an important case, they should take their time and they should judge deliberately. I think what we will see at the end of the day is a decision that's very narrow and that will probably kick the trial until after the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How much do you think the Supreme Court will factor into this election? Back in 2016, it was a big factor for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's win over Hillary Clinton. How much this time?\par \par \uc2 HAM: I mean, we keep ending up in a situation where our candidates sort of put either law enforcement or the justice system, including \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , in this position where they can make a huge impact. In this case, look, I'm glad that they considered it quickly. Some argued that they should have let it ride. I think it's important to decide the immunity before you get to extra parts of the process that can, indeed, punish the person and take away rights from them. And it's going to be consequential, and it's going to be consequential in the public mind in addition to the actual legal decision.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is also abortion case in here, Olivia, and clearly abortion is going to be an issue that Democrats believe is going to be gold for them in different states.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: We are already seeing that happening in House races where they are beating the drums about abortion. When Trump was on the Hill, he tried to say let this be decided on a state by state case. I think we can many out on top. But he also acknowledged that Republicans were hurt last cycle from abortion and Roe v. Wade. So I think Republicans are trying to figure out what their message is and see if they can at least sort of avoid having enough blowback on them.\par \par \uc2 HAM: I think there is also the issue of \uc2 Chevron\uc2  which might end up coming down and making a lot of people of the left and a lot of the machinery of the left sort of lose its mind during this summer. We'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. There's lot of lose their mind moments on both sides.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Just so long as we don't have to watch that 2020 debate again.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's right. All right, panel. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight a SPECIAL REPORT salute in Alaska. All the way out in Alaska, a trapped baby moose was rescued by a man and two police officers. Spencer Warren arrived at work, found that animal stuck between the seaplane and the dock at Beluga Lake. Warren called the police. Officers Morgan Tracy and Charles Lee respond to the scene. Officer Lee parked his vehicle in front of the baby's mother to prevent her from interfering with the rescue while the officer helped Warren pull the moose out of the water. We don't often have moose rescue stories. We thought we would end with that one.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, research at the International Space Station benefiting both professional and amateur athletes. We look at some of the sporting equipment developed by \uc2 NASA\uc2 , in some cases tested in space.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240621ek6k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Rejects Bump Stock Ban; Awaiting Major Gun Rights Ruling From High Court; Democratic Lawmakers Blast Conservative Justices; \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Launches Attacks As Gaza Peace Talks Underway; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Meets Pope Amid Abortion Policy Criticism; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  To Celebrate In West Palm Beach; President Biden To Attend Fundraiser Along With Hollywood Celebrities And Big Donors From Entertainment Industry; Scientists Using International Space Station To Conducts Health Care Related Experiments; Biden Campaign Claims Age Not Issue In Presidential Election Since President Biden And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Are Nearly Same Age; President Biden Meets With Pope Francis During \uc2 G7\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, Bret Baier, Danamarie McNicholl, William La  Jeunesse, David Spunt, Chad Pergram, Shannon Bream, Alex Hogan, Jacqui  Heinrich, Byron York \par \uc2 7,683 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD J.R., FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Ethan, his mother, and the rescuers, met at a city council meeting where they all receive lifesaving awards.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: God bless.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Congratulations to those officers and thank God for that young --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Katie, 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Wonderful. OK, it is Flag Day, which started in 1777, commemorates the adoption of the American flag by Congress. So, happy Flag Day, everyone.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you. That's it for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FORD: Happy Flag Day.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Have a great night. We'll see you. On --\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. Welcome to Los Angeles. I'm Trace Gallagher, in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and Pope Francis share their Catholic faith against the backdrop of big disagreements over abortion. Former President Trump gets a big birthday bash as he turns 78 years old, and we look at how research in space is affecting your health here on Earth.\par \par \uc2 But "BREAKING TONIGHT", the Supreme Court at the center of attention this evening. The conservative wing leads the charge overturning a Trump-era gun regulation.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, progressives fire up their base and up their ire at the supreme. So, with attacks on one justice as a, "insurrectionist sympathizer". We have "TEAM COVERAGE" tonight. Shannon Bream tells us what it all means. Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill with the details on the Democratic attacks. But we begin with David Spunt in Washington on the specifics of the bump stop -- stock ruling. David, good evening.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Trace, good evening to you. Big ruling as well, while this may be about executive power, ultimately, it has some serious challenges in the middle of an election year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): This is a bump stock and attachment to a semi-automatic weapon that enables it to operate at speeds rivalling a fully automatic machine gun. But the Supreme Court's six conservatives ruled it's not an illegal machine gun, and the ATF overstepped its authority, banning the attachments. Following the 2017 Las Vegas massacre where a gunman used weapons fitted with bump stocks to open fire on a concert, killing nearly 60 and wounding hundreds, then-President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  directed the ATF to ban the devices, reversing previous Republican and Democratic administrations.\par \par \uc2 That ban carried over to the Biden administration. It's no more with Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  writing, "We conclude that a semi-automatic rifle equipped with a bump stock is not a machine gun because it does not fire more than one shot by a single function of the trigger.\par \par \uc2 Michael Cargill, a Texas gun store owner and the key plaintiff, successfully sued.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL CARGILL, OWNER, CENTRAL TEXAS GUN WORKS: This is about the government. The ATF, an administrative agency who walked into my home and decided that something that was legal, it now decided that is illegal, and that they are going to turn me into a felon if I don't give that up.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Three liberal justices dissented with Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  writing, "The majority's artificially narrow definition hamstrings the government's efforts to keep machine guns from gunman like the Las Vegas shooter."\par \par \uc2 In a statement following the Court's ruling, President Biden doubled down. "We know thoughts and prayers are not enough. I call on Congress to ban bump stocks, pass an assault weapon ban, and take additional action to save lives."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): And Trace, in the coming days, the Supreme Court is poised to rule on another gun case with Second Amendment implications, challenging a federal law banning those under domestic violence, restraining orders from keeping a handgun, we expect that at some point in the next few weeks. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: David Spunt but love for us and Washington. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today's bump stock ruling is just the latest fuel for Democrats, escalating their attacks on the Supreme Court. The senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram has that part of the story tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Democrats' escalating attacks on conservative Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Justice Alito is an insurrectionist sympathizer, joined by his right-wing buddy, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ,\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Democrats accused justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  of questionable ethics. Thomas for traveling on lavish getaways on the dimes of GOP megadonor.\par \par \uc2 SEN. SHELDON WHITEHOUSE (D-RI): He had done it 60 times. He was at vacation taking feign. My Lord.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Alito for controversial flags flying at his homes.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): The legacy of the Roberts' Court will be one of unchecked corruption.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Thomas's lawyer says ethics rules don't require him to report such trips so long as donors don't have business before the Court.\par \par \uc2 Alito says his wife flew the flags. Republicans say the Democrats complaints are camouflage for what really needles the left.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ERIC SCHMITT (R-MO): Democrats don't like the fact that the judges are making certain -- the justices are making certain decisions.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): One Democrat demands Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  penalized justices.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): He can say that Justice Alito no longer rights majority opinion. No longer presides over the Circuit.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Republicans appalled by the ultimatums.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): They want the Supreme Court to become subservient to the Congress.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Republicans say the attacks are just politics.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): This is an election year and they are trying to make much ado about nothing.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): But Democrats don't seem to disagree when it comes to an electoral strategy.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES Democrats, in election after election after election, since the fall of Roe v. Wade in 2022 are winning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): So, Democrats are campaigning this fall against the GOP, former President Trump, and the Supreme Court. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Chad Pergram, live for us on Capitol Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Chief Justice Roberts is supposed to be the guardian of the Court's reputation. In my judgement and the judgement of so many Americans, he is derelict in this -- that responsibility, whether it comes to Court packing, in terms of the individual districts and forum shopping, whether it comes to ethics of the members of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's talk more about how the Democrats are going after some of the Supreme Court justices. Joining us now, Fox News chief legal correspondent and anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY", Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, it's great to see you. I want to pick up where Senator Schumer left off there. So, you know, kind of going after the Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  as being derelict in his duties. It's interesting the whole narrative here, because over the past few weeks, we've heard a lot from the Democrats about respecting the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 And now, it appears that they've taken a few swipes at the Supreme Court, maybe more than a few, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Yes. And remember, it was Senator Schumer, who not that long ago, stood out there on the steps in front of the Supreme Court and name called justices Gorsuch and Kavanaugh talking about unleashing a whirlwind. You're not going to know what hit you about upcoming decisions that they hadn't even issued yet.\par \par \uc2 He later said that wasn't the best use of words. He didn't want to sound like he was threatening them. But a lot of people took it that way.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, when you look at polling about the Court a few years ago, when it was majority Democrat appointees, the left had no problem with it. In fact, they rated it highly in polling. That has changed over the last few years, as it's now a six-three when you look at Republican appointees versus Democrat appointees.\par \par \uc2 But you've seen, we've all seen, we share with our viewers in recent days, there are plenty of 9-0 decisions that these justices work together on, where they clearly set aside ideology and try to follow what the law says. That's the rule rather than the exception. Those 5-4, 6-3s much more rare.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, let's go back now if we can to the Supreme Court in validating the bump stock ban. I mean, this was a Trump ban, Shannon. Right?\par \par \uc2 And his press secretary said the following here, Karoline Leavitt, "The Court has spoken, their decision should be respected. President Trump has been and always will be a fierce defender of American's Second Amendment rights, and he is proud to be endorsed by the \uc2 NRA\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 So, they accept it. It appears they have accepted this. Democrats, meantime are livid at this ruling.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the interesting thing is that they are -- the people who were happy with the Court yesterday on the Mifepristone 9-0 abortion drug ruling. Some of them are very unhappy with the Court today. So, they like it depend on the decisions they are getting from a lot of folks out there.\par \par \uc2 But yes, this decision on the bump stock, that bump stock came along during the Trump administration after that horrific Vegas shooting in 2017. But what the justices said is the ATF went too far. The statutory language did not fit, taking a bump stock and classifying it as a "machine gun" under the statute.\par \par \uc2 Now, they went on to say, Congress is the place that's needs to be fixed. We're not saying that this is a good or bad policy, we are just saying, under statutory language, the ATF went too far. Now, Congress, the ball is in your Court, if you want to clarify.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And finally, Shannon, Supreme Court did not rule on the Trump immunity. What does this mean for the pending cases going forward?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, the next day, the Court has told us we're going to get opinions and this can change is next Thursday. So, right now, that's the next chance we could get that immunity case.\par \par \uc2 A lot of us think after sitting through the arguments there that they are going to have a very narrow. Finding it's not going to give either side what they want. Complete immunity or no immunity at all.\par \par \uc2 I think they're probably working on a compromise behind the scenes. But what that could mean is they send it back to the lower court with some instruction on deciding what are private acts versus official acts of the former president when he was in office.\par \par \uc2 If anything, that's just going to add time on getting that case moving forward before the election.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Shannon, thank you. Meantime, who is coming on "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We have got Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  with us. Is he -- well, on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 He allegedly, according to oddsmakers out in Vegas is at the top of the V.P. list, we'll see about that. And then, the ranking member on House Intel, \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2  is with us too, t