offered their image and their likeness for the campaign to use to access their millions of followers online.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, the golden state has a bunch of money giving out to both candidates. William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Bret Baier looks at how research done in space is impacting your health here on earth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: What kinds of things are you learning from space, in space that could save lives?\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON, NASA ADMINISTRATOR: Well, we are learning so much. We can experiment and we can do our research on full-time basis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We continue our series on space with a look at medical research being done at the International Space Station. My colleague Bret Baier explains how the findings could make treatments for effective and more widely used.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's SPECIAL REPORT spotlight, "Destination Space," we look at advancements in science and medicine thanks to research in low earth orbit. \uc2 NASA\uc2  Administrator Bill Nelson recently told Congress about possible major breakthroughs when it comes to efforts to fight disease. We hear more about those projects from the administrator and how they could save lives for decades to come.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON, NASA ADMINISTRATOR: There is a lot of excitement about this and many other kinds of research.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: From pharmaceutical research to improved disease detection and enhanced medical device technology, \uc2 NASA\uc2  is helping advance the world of medicine.\par \par \uc2 There have to be doctors who have spent their lives trying to find these things here on earth that are tremendously excited about the prospects.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: It's too bad that it has taken this long. You know, I did this protein crystal growth experiment back in January of 1986, but we didn't have a permanent laboratory in space. And just now we are beginning to see the payoffs of this drug research.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Over the years, pharmaceutical company Merck has launched several protein crystallization research projects to the International Space Station that could benefit cancer treatment here on earth.\par \par \uc2 So space is allowing kind of new testing of pharmaceuticals?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Absolutely. For example, the drug that was developed down here on earth that has been so phenomenally successful, Keytruda, we have taken that to space in zero g, and they have been able to form those crystals in such larger and more uniform pattern.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The microgravity exposure has made the drug more effective and easier to administer to patients.\par \par \uc2 And the hope is that there is going to be more and more breakthroughs not only in the cancer drugs but in all kinds of different.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Stem cell research is very promising.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Full power, and liftoff.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In January, \uc2 Northrop Grumman\uc2  and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  helped deliver the \uc2 Mayo Clinic\uc2 's stem cell experiment to the International Space Station.\par \par \uc2 DR. ABBA ZUBAIR, MAYO CLINIC PATHOLOGIST: Growing stem cells has been very challenging in the lab. We start thinking out of the box.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dr. Abba Zubair is a laboratory medicine and pathology specialist at the \uc2 Mayo Clinic\uc2  in Florida.\par \par \uc2 ZUBAIR: Stroke is something I passionately care about because this is what killed my other. So I've been looking for ways to address strokes. We thought maybe we could roll this out in space. If they can grow faster, we can treat more patients with that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In space, scientists can grow more stem cells, and like protein crystals, they are more uniform and their ability to control immune cells is enhanced.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: We can form a 3-D dimensional of what they call an organoid, which is a piece of tissue. And as a result of that, they can then in space use different drugs on that 3-D dimensional tissue and determine what is the most effective.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It just couldn't be any better.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The results have been worth the interstellar trip.\par \par \uc2 There is just a lot of excitement around it. Do you hear that?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Well, you can probably tell it in the sound of my voice, yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right now, the research is still in its earliest stages. Dr. Zubair estimates it will take several years before patients can be treated with space grown stem cells. But an expanding footprint in orbit could expedite that process.\par \par \uc2 ZUBAIR: There are patients that are waiting and they really want this going. I think with more funding and more access, maybe the timeline would be shorter.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dr. Zubair hopes one day soon he too can conduct his research from the International Space Station.\par \par \uc2 ZUBAIR: I am waiting to do my experiment myself. So please take me to the moon or ISS.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The environment in space give scientists a national laboratory to test how certain factors impact humans.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL LOCKNEY, NASA TECHNOLOGY TRANSFER PROGRAM: The effects of microgravity and radiation on the body, they mimic aging. So we understand a lot about what happens to the body in space, what happens to the body as you age.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: We can do this continuously in this research workplace called the International Space Station, and that's where we're having some dramatic breakthroughs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): The Chinese astronauts are back from space with the samples they took with them, including stem cells, protein crystals, and living molecules. They'll study how microgravity impacted those samples and search f