t it. Audit it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  (PH) has --\par \par \uc2 WALLING: All right.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You can erase --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Trust, but verify. We're on you, Elliott (PH). Nice try.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Shannon Bream in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 President Biden addresses whether he would commute the sentence for his son for his multiple felony convictions.\par \par \uc2 New York police arrest a suspect armed to the teeth who may have been planning a terror attack.\par \par \uc2 And our series on space exploration examines the search for extraterrestrial life.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT", dueling field trips for the presumptive presidential nominees, while President Biden makes news in Italy about possible executive action regarding his son, Hunter, former President Trump was on Capitol Hill for his first visit since the riots on January 6th, 2021.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump spoke with House and Senate Republicans and addressed the Business Roundtable. He also spoke with Fox News congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie about his eventual running mate. Let's bring her in right now. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening to you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 If it wasn't appearing before, it certainly is after today. President Trump has the support of the majority of House and Senate Republicans. And in his first exclusive interview after meeting with them, he extended an olive branch to one political opponent, while showing empathy for another.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Trump who do you want for GOP leader?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): For the first time in four years, and the first time since the January 6 riot, former President Trump back near Capitol Hill, trying to bring House and Senate Republicans together ahead of the election.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: It was a great meeting. There is tremendous unity in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): After his whirlwind tour of Washington, talking business, policy, campaigns, and burying the hatchet with GOP Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Trump spoke exclusively to Fox about it all. And gave us a hint about his V.P. pick.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: A lot of folks surrounding you, some of them are on the shortlist for V.P.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Was your pick for V.P. in that room with you?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Probably, yes. Probably. I don't want to go. But probably, I think, we'll probably announce it during the convention.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Do you know who your V.P. is?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I have sort of a pretty good idea. Look, we have some really talented people. I have a pretty good idea.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Trump also made news today by throwing his support behind former Maryland governor and Senate candidate \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 . Despite his own campaign and the \uc2 RNC\uc2  slamming Hogan, for telling Americans to respect Trump's New York verdict.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Are you going to support \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  to win that Senate seat?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, I'd like to see him win. I think he has a good chance to win. I would like to see him win.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, you are endorsing him?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, nobody has asked me that. But essentially, I would be endorsing him. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): But Hogan's camp shot back today, saying, "Governor Hogan has been clear he is not supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , just as he didn't in 2016 and 2020."\par \par \uc2 Trump also commented on the Hunter Biden conviction and whether or not President Biden should pardon his son or commute the sentence.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I can say a lot of things that Hunter did were already killed by the statute of limitations. So, in that regard, they have already benefited, as you know.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You're a father, though.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You understand what it means to be a father. What would you do in that situation?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I think it's a very serious thing. I understand. That whole subject, I understand it pretty well, because I've had it with people have it in their family. It's a very tough thing. It's a very tough situation for a father. It's a very tough situation for a brother or sister.\par \par \uc2 And it goes on and it's not stopping, whether it's alcohol or drugs, or whatever it may be. It's a tough thing. And so that's a tough -- it's a tough moment for their family. It's a tough moment for any family involved in that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, showing quite a bit of empathy there, Shannon. But he doesn't go as far as to say whether he would pardon his own son if he were in that similar situation. Trump also addressed those comments he made about the \uc2 RNC\uc2  host city, Milwaukee, because Democrats are really going after him right now.\par \par \uc2 He is saying -- he basically told me he was talking about crime in the city when he called the city horrible. And he was talking about election integrity, adding, I love Milwaukee. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, it sounds like a number of lawmakers in that meeting have the same version, he does. We'll talk about it later with the panel. Aishah, great interview. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Got it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden is now on the record. He will not commute the eventual sentence for his son after his conviction on felony gun charges. During his news conference today with Ukraine's president in Italy, President Biden reiterated he will not pardon his son, but this was the first time he addressed the commutation question.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich reports tonight from Bari, Italy. Hello, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening to you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials have been uncharacteristically blunt. This summit was meant to deliver lasting support for Ukraine because Biden's future in office isn't guaranteed. The reality that came into sharper view with French and U.K. leaders also facing headwinds.\par \par \uc2 But Biden also had personal problems to shoulder on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Would you commute Hunter's sentence?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Amid handshake, sealing a new 10-year pledge to build Ukraine's defenses with the 2024 election, throwing future aid in doubt, President Biden couldn't escape questions about his convicted son, Hunter, as Hunter's children looked on.\par \par \uc2 Biden so wanted the focus to remain on Ukraine, he sniped at a question about Gaza.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I wish you guys would love to play by the -- by the rules a little bit. I'm here to talk about a critical situation in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Russia is saber rattling. Warships armed with hypersonic missiles carrying out drills near Cuba, seen as a response to Biden greenlighting limited Ukrainian strikes within Russian territory. Biden said today, it's as far as he'll go.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We have not changed our position on that (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): But an analysis from the Institute for the Study of war shows Biden only approved 16 percent of what Ukraine could do. Still out of reach, all Russian air bases, the overwhelming majority of ground forces, and logistics spaces, and the vast majority of Russian air defense systems.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you want Ukraine to win, why would you weaken them by not allowing them to be on the offensive?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL HOFFMAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: The only place where Putin is been successful, and it's not surprising because he is a KGB guy, he's been a propaganda operation to deter the Biden administration from giving Ukraine what they need and when they need it.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Zelenskyy express thanks for the pledge, meant to outlive the next administration. Plus, a new $50 billion loan underwritten by the U.S. and backed by frozen Russian assets.\par \par \uc2 Experts say the Kremlin's response is still playing out. Russian prosecutors today, announcing Wall Street Journal reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  will stand trial on what the U.S. deems sham espionage charges.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN\uc2 : I think what Putin is trying to do by putting him on trial is to enhance Russia's leverage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The U.S. Ukraine agreement comes one month before \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies converge in D.C., heading off a replay of last year's tensions, when Ukraine was denied an invitation to the alliance. President Biden said, the new agreement includes major commitments from Ukraine on democratic reforms needed to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, Jacqui, also a strong warning to China at this summit not to help Russia in its war on Ukraine. There's been a lot of discussion of that here in Washington. But does Zelenskyy also think it's a concern or not?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: He seemed to indicate in an answer today that he didn't think it was an issue. He said that he had a firm conversation with Chinese President Xi, and got assurances that China wouldn't sell weapons to Russia. It's unclear when this conversation happened, though, their last talk that we know of was April of last year.\par \par \uc2 However, President Biden chimed in didn't leave that unaddressed. He countered Zelenskyy, saying that China is helping Russia with "the ability and technology to produce weapons. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Jacqui Heinrich on the road. Safe travels. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has unanimously preserved access to a medication used in nearly two thirds of all abortions in the U.S. last year. But rather than getting to the merits of the case on \uc2 FDA\uc2  guidelines regarding mifepristone. The justices, instead, found the pro-life doctors who brought the case did not have legal standing to sue.\par \par \uc2 Attorney Erin Hawley, who argued on behalf of the doctor's call today's decision disappointing, adding, "The \uc2 FDA\uc2 's own label says that roughly one in 25 women who take chemical abortion drugs will end up in the emergency room."\par \par \uc2 And while it's a tough loss for pro-life advocates, they are celebrating some language in the unanimous opinion that says "Federal conscious protections provide broad coverage and will shield a doctor who doesn't want to provide care in violation of those protections."\par \par \uc2 Both President Biden and Vice President Harris are warning of other threats to abortion access.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We must remain clear eyed about the threats to reproductive freedom in America and we must remain vigilant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are awaiting another abortion ruling from the court on whether the Biden administration can force hospitals to provide emergency abortions in states like Idaho, where there is a near total ban on abortion with an exception to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 A man who said to have had extreme jihadist views and armed with a cache of weapons and New York police equipment is in custody tonight. Experts believe he was planning something they are calling "very bad".\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in New York tonight with specifics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELINDA KATZ\uc2 , DISTRICT ATTORNEY, QUEENS, NEW YORK: We need to protect our city.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A possible attack thwarted in New York City. 27-year-old, Judd Sanson, charged today on multiple criminal weapons possession charges after the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  pulled over his Ford Explorer over a blacked-out license plate only to make a disturbing discovery, a stockpile of weapons.\par \par \uc2 Inside the SUV, police found 11 weapons, a loaded nine-millimeter handgun, nine loaded magazines, switchblades, metal knives, a hatchet, an axe, a weighted whip, stun gun, and a baton.\par \par \uc2 KATZ: At the moment we don't have terrorism charges, but we are still continuing the investigation, especially considering the language that was used.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Prosecutors say alarming phrases were etched on the baton, like, "left me no choice"; "you going to learn today"; and an Arabic phrase that translates to, "Ask in God for forgiveness."\par \par \uc2 The New York Post report Sanson shared extreme jihadist views online. A police source telling The Post, "something very bad was potentially averted. The sum of the parts had the potential for something awful."\par \par \uc2 Police also found a mask commonly used as an anti-government symbol. \uc2 NYPD\uc2  uniform gear, including handcuffs and a logoed bulletproof vest, and a vest worn by employees working in the city subway system.\par \par \uc2 KATZ: He had which I find extremely concerning, an \uc2 NYPD\uc2  bulletproof vest and a transit reflective uniform, which as we know in the city of New York really gives you access to almost anywhere you want to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): This arrest comes after \uc2 FBI\uc2  director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  warned last week about the nation's vulnerability to terrorism, characterizing it as "being on a whole another level now."\par \par \uc2 Wray said he was concerned about lone wolf actors or small groups drawing twisted inspiration from terrorists abroad. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bryan Llenas in New York. Thank you, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 Well, House lawmakers are threatening extreme measures against colleges that do not condemn anti-Semitism on campus. Wednesday, dozens of protesters caused a major disturbance at a school in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins has the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Hey, are you --\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Last night at Cal State, nearly 100 protesters seized a student services building, vandalizing windows, and blocking exits. Trapping employees for hours.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: There is only one solution, Intifada revolution!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Raise your hands if you're a Zionist.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Raise your hands if you're a Zionist.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is your chance to get out.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): It's been a week of the anti-Israel protests from California to New York, with growing signs of anti-Semitism.\par \par \uc2 Even the homes of the Brooklyn Museum's leadership, including its Jewish directors were defaced yesterday with upside down red triangles painted on doors. The same symbol \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's militant wing uses to identify Israeli targets.\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON SMITH (R-MO): To the universities listening, if you think we will lose focus, interest, or forget about this, you couldn't be more wrong.\par \par \uc2 REP. RICHARD NEAL (D-MA): We are saddened that the hateful actions and rhetoric that is too often targeted Jewish students has continued.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: At a House hearing today, bipartisan condemnation over campus anti-Semitism and university leadership failures, demanding schools