an unsecured hatch on the top of the delivery tanker that overturned and ignited beneath an Interstate 95 bridge a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Video evidence obtained by investigators shows the cover to the 16-inch manhole was not properly secured when the driver took the truck from the terminal to a Philadelphia convenience store.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Charlotte. One of the big stories there tonight from our affiliate, WJZY: The first round of the U.S. Open is being played at Pinehurst Resort. Scottie Scheffler is the favorite. Tiger Woods is also in the field. It is the third major tournament of the year.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  is endorsing the Democratic challenger running against progressive squad incumbent \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  in a competitive house primary in New York.\par \par \uc2 Clinton threw her support behind Westchester County Executive \uc2 George Latimer\uc2  in a post on X. The endorsement came despite Bowman raising racism allegations against Latimer during an interview earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors in the Georgia election interference case against former President Trump are asking for the dismissal of an appeal to a ruling allowing the Fulton County district attorney to stay on the case. \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  is accused of having an inappropriate relationship with a fellow prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump and 18 others face charges of trying to overturn the 2020 election results in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Israeli tanks are advancing deeper into the Gazan city of Rafah tonight. The move comes as \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  terrorists fire additional rockets from Lebanon into northern Israel.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Trey Yingst is in Tel Aviv tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tensions along the Israel-Lebanon border are boiling. \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  retaliating with its single largest rocket barrage in a day after Israel killed a top \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  commander this week. Hundreds of rockets along with dozens of attack drones were launched from southern Lebanon as the Iran backed group vows to escalate the ongoing conflict.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): We will increase our operations in intensity, force, quantity and quality and let him wait for us in the field.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): Israeli airstrikes have pummeled southern Lebanon in recent days, going after \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  strongholds and rocket launching positions. Efforts are ongoing to reach an understanding with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  to avoid the situation unraveling into a larger war. But time is running out.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MENCER, ISRAELI GOVERNMENT SPOKESMAN: Whether through diplomatic efforts or otherwise, Israel will restore security on our northern border. Israel will respond with force to all aggressions by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): Reports indicate that American officials are urging the Israelis to use restraint and not escalate the ongoing conflict.\par \par \uc2 Although with fires raging across northern Israel, and no end in sight for the war in Gaza, Israeli officials could launch a larger offensive action without the blessing of their U.S. allies.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2  (RET.), FORMER JOINT CHIEFS CHAIRMAN: You see the conflict in north of Israel continued to reach concerning levels and we want to tackle all of those issues and the best way to do that is through a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Tonight, Israel launched another strike in southern Lebanon reportedly targeting high profile \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  members, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey, we always appreciate you and the team working around the clock. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, severe flooding in South Florida pumps emergency measures, we're going to have a live report.\par \par \uc2 Plus, how private enterprise is fueling the search for life on other planets, Bret Baier explains.\par \par \uc2 First, beyond our borders tonight, tensions in Italy's lower house erupt into a fist fight and opposition lawmaker was injured and sent to a hospital. The issue was a controversial government proposal opponents say will further impoverished the poor southern region.\par \par \uc2 Masses of Muslim pilgrims are in the Saudi city of Mecca tonight. Today they circle the Kaaba, Islam's holiest sites a day before heading to the nearby desert area of MENA to officially open the Hajj.\par \par \uc2 That is the annual Islamic pilgrimage that takes the faithful along the routes used by the Prophet Muhammad.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London, one of the big stories there tonight, Wimbledon announces record prize money of nearly $64 million. The grass court Grand Slam runs from July 1st to the 14th. The tournament made almost $69 million last year.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Border tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now, with Fox weather alerts, several areas of South Florida are under flash flood emergencies tonight. A tropical disturbance has dumped several inches of rain on the region and more is coming.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Danamarie McNicholl is in West Palm Beach tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DANAMARIE MCNICHOLL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tropical torrential downpours across Florida breaking rainfall records and creating life threatening conditions.\par \par \uc2 Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  declaring a state of emergency for five counties after three days of flash flooding, which left stranded cars, downed power lines and water in businesses and homes.\par \par \uc2 Emergency responders in Miami, Fort Lauderdale, Sarasota and Fort Myers say they've made over 100 water rescues so far this week. Sarasota broke an 84- year record with nearly 6.5 inches of rain falling in 24 hours according to the National Weather Service.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's very crazy. We live here since we were like little, and this is crazy.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL: On Wednesday, hazardous road conditions shut down part of I-95, Florida's busiest interstate. People resorting to kayaking in the streets to get around.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN PIERCE, MIAMI RESIDENT: On my side it's a little bit flooded, but when you go down here, the water kept rising. It kept rising from, like, ankle high, then it kept riding more to like knee high.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL: The National Hurricane Center is categorizing Invest 90-L as a tropical disturbance. From Sarasota to Miami, the storm has unleashed more than two feet of rain across south Florida in just two days, according to FOX Weather. Yesterday, all major airports in south Florida experienced ground delays, some up to seven hours, due to standing water on the runway causing hundreds of cancellations and delays, including the \uc2 Florida Panthers\uc2 , who finally made it to Edmonton for tonight's Stanley Cup Finals game.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL (on camera): And we aren't out of the clear just yet. Additional rain is on the way. Up to 10 inches in some area. Most of south Florida is under a flash flood warning until 8:00 p.m. tonight. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Danamarie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So the head of the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  says the agency was too hands off in oversight of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  before a January mid-air emergency and a new \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  737 Max-9. \uc2 Mike Whitaker\uc2  made the comment during a Senate hearing today. He says the agency has permanently boosted the use of in-person inspectors and will visit a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  factory on South Carolina Friday. The manufacturer has experienced a series of high-profile problems in recent months and years.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  is blaming what it calls a spacesuit discomfort issue for the cancelation of a planned spacewalk outside of the International Space Station today. The repair mission by two U.S. astronauts was called off about an hour before it was set to begin. Not clear, exactly, what caused the problem.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we continued our series that looks at advancements in space. \uc2 NASA\uc2  scientists are working to determine whether there is life on other planets, and they are using private companies to help with the research. My colleague, Bret Baier, explains why \uc2 NASA\uc2  says we are likely not alone in the universe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON, NASA ADMINISTRATOR: We don't need to go back to the moon just for the moon. We are going back to learn new things in order for us to go to Mars and beyond.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Space travel will also expand in the near future. Astronauts are expected to return to the moon.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: That goes to the moon and will orbit the moon on this flight next year with four astronauts.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And there are plans for a Mars sample return mission.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: It's getting samples. And it's drilling with this drill, creating these core samples about the size of a cigar. We're trying to figure out right now how we're going to go back and get them and bring them back to earth so that we got an idea of whether or not there was life there.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN BENNER\uc2 , FIREBIRD DIAGNOSTICS: \uc2 NASA\uc2 's mission is to go out, among other things and discover whether or not we are alone.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Firebird Diagnostics founder \uc2 Steven Benner\uc2  says his company sells so-called alien DNA. The synthetic structures have helped diagnose cases of COVID-19, cancer, and HIV.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: You can make copies of the DNA, make copies of the copies of the copies, and therefore amplify DNA. We can also could that with the alien DNA we construct in our laboratory.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Traditional earth-based DNA has four nucleotides, or building blocks. Firebird synthetic samples have up to eight, allowing for more sensitive testing and eliminating false positives.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: It allows you to get that needle in the haystack without having to worry about all the background information.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As NASA prepares to return to Mars, it's working with Benner's team to prepare for possible life on the red planet.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: We go into the laboratory and try to make alternative forms of DNA just to understand what possible forms of alternative DNA might be out there used by Vulcans or Klingons, or other species that we might encounter, especially on Mars or Europa, and other places in the solar system where there is water.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can searching for life on other planets benefit life here on earth?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: There is so much we don't know, but your question is by finding out other life, it's certainly going to help us understand better who we are and where we are in this vast cosmos.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is the biggest question we get when we talk about space, all the UFO sightings, all the things, the testimony on Capitol Hill had really people scratching their heads, feel like they are not learning everything that the government knows, obviously. You are the \uc2 NASA\uc2  administrator. What can you tell people out there?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Well, if you ask me directly do I think that there are aliens here on earth, I don't think so. I don't absolutely know. And I don't think the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is hiding anything from anybody. But, if you ask me, do I think there is life out there in the cosmos? I asked our \uc2 NASA\uc2  scientists that question. How many possibilities in the vastness of this universe is there that there's another planet like earth that would be habitable for lief as something like we know it? They said at least a trillion. I said a trillion? They said maybe 2 trillion.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Planets that are possibly habitable?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: That's straight from our scientists. And you and I, but particularly our children and grandchildren, are going to find out the answer to that question.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And are we going to be ready for it when we do find it?\par \par \uc2 NELSON: The distances are so vast that it will be a long time before we can go that far. But that doesn't mean that we cannot have some kind of understanding of what's out there and maybe even some kind of communication.\par \par \uc2 As a matter of fact, Bret, what I have already asked our scientists to do, since we are in this era of A.I., artificial intelligence, could you build into the spacecrafts software so that in a spacecraft like Voyager, that is out in Interstellar space that is beyond our solar system, that if it came upon another spacecraft, that it could real time learn to communicate with that other spacecraft? I think that's one of the possibilities we could use artificial intelligence for.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is starting to get to science fiction stuff.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: That's what we do at \uc2 NASA\uc2 . They make the impossible possible. It's a bunch of wizards around here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we look at pharmaceutical research and stem cell studies taking place in orbit. Administrator Nelson talks about some of the medical breakthroughs scientists are finding in zero gravity.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the Biden and Trump dueling field trips today.\par \par \uc2 And later, the Supreme Court makes a major decision in an abortion case.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: It just was a great meeting. There is tremendous unity in the Republican Party. We want to see borders. We want to see a strong military. We want to see money not wasted all over the world. And we have great common sense. A lot of very smart people in this room, and a lot of people that love our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's bring in our panel to talk about President Trump's return to Capitol Hill today. We've got FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, "Politico" White House reporter Daniel Lippman, and Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com. And Juan, we were all talking during the commercial, like this was brief and to the point for him.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: It was rare.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We were like, where is the rest of the speech?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think a lot of people, especially in the Senate, were pleased. I mean, to me, the sight of him shaking hands with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , I would not have bet you on that one, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Really?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Three years ago, remember when McConnell said he was responsible for January 6th. The law enforcement people should take responsible, take action. He is not impeaching. The wife resigned. Now he's back in town, back on Capitol Hill, and has a positive reception.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like things went a little different with the House group where they wanted policy prescriptions, I think, going into the fall elections. And he was more general and had criticisms of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's daughter, talking in general about -- he said something critical about Milwaukee where the convention will be held. So I think some of that apparently came off as people thinking he was just rambling. But overall, I think history will look back on this day and say wow, in three years, he was back on Capitol Hill and in charge.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and Katie, as Aishah was reporting, a number of lawmakers have come forward to say his remarks on Milwaukee were misconstrued, that they were about the crime situation there or other issues. That's the way he is painting it, too. But to see people shaking hands that didn't like each other two or three years ago, the reality is you are in an election year. They all want to win, and they need each other.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Welcome to Washington where people fight it out either here or on the campaign trail and get back together. The reality is you are in an election year. They all want to win, and they need each other.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Welcome to Washington where people fight it out either here or on the campaign trail and then they get back together because, exactly --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And they end up on the same ticket sometimes.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Exactly. They want to win, and they're very close, we are about a mouth out from the RNC Convention in Milwaukee. They understand that the American people are on their side on the big issues like the economy and immigration and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  needs them to be supporting him, especially when it comes to these tight margins. And if he were to win and beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in November, these Republicans understand that they -- it's a team sport. If they want to actually get their policy prescriptions through, they want to hold onto the House, which talking to activists say that it's more likely that they're seeing that could happen now. They want to expand that margin in the Senate, not just for the sake of getting Trump's agenda through, but also to confirm nominees for the president which last time around was very, very difficult for him. And so they're looking at this as a team sport, and they can't get what they want if they don't win.\par \par \uc2 BOLDUAN: It sounds like they realized that when it comes to \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , because you remember right after the 34 guilty conviction against President Trump in Manhattan, he tweeted out, let's respect the process, that kind of thing. And one of Trump's top advisers basically tweeted back, your campaign is over. That's the end of it. But here is what President Trump said to Aishah Hasnie when she asked him about \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Are you going to support \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  to win that Senate seat?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Yes, I would like to see him win. I think he has a good chance to win. I would like to see him win. And we got to take the majority. We have to straighten out our country. I know other people made some strong statements. But I can just say from my standpoint, I'm about the party and I'm about the country, and I would like to see him win.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So you are endorsing him?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, nobody has asked me that, but essentially I would be endorsing him, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, the Hogan campaign apparently says thanks but no thanks, with this statement, "Governor Hogan has been clear he is not supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just as he didn't in 2016 and 2020." But Daniel, maybe that's what he needs to say to win in Maryland so then President Trump may get his vote on some key issues in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL LIPPMAN, "POLITICO": Of course. And you know that Democratic oppo research groups, the Angela Alsobrooks campaign which is running against Hogan is clipping that right now and turning that into a TV ad so that they can tell Democratic voters in Maryland, hey, this is a Trump guy, even though hogan was a longtime critic of Trump. But in a blue state like Maryland or in states like New York where you have Mike Lawler and lots of other New York congressmen who are Republicans who will need to distance themselves, any positive words from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that is political toxin for them. And so I think he was answering that question from your colleague, but not the best day for the Hogan campaign.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They say, again, no thanks.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, President Trump is working to make inroads with young voters, and it sounds like some of this is landing. First of all, I want to put up this poll, voters 18to 34 right now if it were head-to-head, this is from Quinnipiac, President Trump has one point advantage here with the younger crowd, which is quite a swing from four years ago. And here's a little bit of him going to where the younger folks are. Here he is with wrestler Logan Paul.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And down we go --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Katie, how powerful is that kind of stuff with the TikTok crew out there?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: If you contract it with what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been doing to try to and win over young voters, he has thrown out a lot of government handouts like student loan relief, for example. He has tried to get on TikTok, but he is not getting the big endorsements like the Logan Pauls. And Trump is going to these places and these audiences that are sympathetic to him, and the macho style at UFC. They're pulling those voters in.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the issues, the number one thing for young voters is affordability for housing. And under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  interest rates for housing were three percent, below three percent, four percent. Now they're eight percent to get a home. And so they are looking at that and saying between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in terms of the economy, I'm better off under Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " has a report on Democrats doing events, things like having a dance party, a comedy show, a place to chill out. Sometimes there's free beer, manicures, boot shines, rent check sweepstakes, a handout of contraceptive pills or cooling towels, Juan. Will it work?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The whole thing is you've got to reach people where they are, right, and you've got to go to them and give them what they want.\par \par \uc2 But I must say, when I look at these numbers, I think about some big issues for young people, and abortion is huge. And I think, again, about people who are not paying attention right now, and I think that's why we see these kinds of, let's go to the -- what do you call it, MMA event and UFC. And I think when they pay attention, I'm not sure that they are going to stay in that tie.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, well, stick around. You mentioned the issue of abortion, so up next we're going to talk about that big decision from the Supreme Court today. And then, a rout in the congressional baseball game we told you about last night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: Though I am relieved by today's decision made by the court, no one should be celebrating. This decision should have been an obvious one.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER SENIOR COUNSELOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: The Constitution gives us life, liberty, and the pursuit the happiness. The first right is life. I think the Democrats will overplay their hand on abortion.\par \par \uc2 STEVE VLADECK, GEORGETOWN UNIVERSITY LAW PROFESSOR: The Supreme Court is as conservative as we have seen in our lifetimes. What I read in this unanimous rejection in the case today is the Supreme Court saying slow down Fifth Circuit. We're not going there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are back with our panel. Daniel, I'll start with you, because, let me just read a little bit from Justice Kavanaugh. A 9-0 decision, which people act like we never get those. We've gotten a lot lately. And basically, it wasn't on the merits. He said these pro-life doctors who brought the case did not have standing. So they didn't get to the actual argument about the \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulating this medication. But Justice Kavanaugh writing for the 9-0 says "The plaintiffs have sincere legal, moral, ideological, and policy objections to elective abortion and to \uc2 FDA\uc2 's relaxed regulation of mifepristone. But under Article III of the Constitution, those kinds of objections alone do not establish a justiciable case or controversy in federal court." So that doesn't mean this debate is over. This case is over.\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: Exactly. And Democratic progressive groups, they are worried if Trump comes back that he is going to order the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to make it harder for people to get this drug. What's been interesting since the Dobbs arrangement is that abortion has actually gone up a little bit, 0.2 percent. And you see two-thirds of Americans who are against the ruling. So, the Supreme Court has to be mindful of their own credibility, because they have to look at standing and say, hey, if these people did not have standing to sue the -- to stop this, then it's a 9-0.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Katie, what about those who are very critical of the court right now, especially the justices they consider to be conservatives? I mean, they all stuck together on this and said the case is not right.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: It's just interesting, you go back to the Dobbs decision and the aftermath of that. And the assassination attempt on Justice Kavanaugh, the constant raucous protesting outside of the justices' homes, the continued attacks on the court by Senator Dick Durbin and Sheldon Whitehouse saying the Dobbs decision was made purely for ideological rightwing reasons, when it was made on constitutional reasons and constitutional grounds. And now you have this ruling, 9-0, again, going back to the constitutional issue which Justice Kavanaugh wrote about.\par \par \uc2 So the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  who brought this case is vowing to continue to move forward to try and find some ways to make their case on the merits. As you mentioned, it wasn't made in terms of the details of the case, just simply on a technicality. But politically, Democrats certainly are not going to let this narrative go, and the vice president has been dispatched all around the country in swing states to try and get voters to the polls. And they have been successful in elections and special elections in the aftermath of Dobbs on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So Juan, even the left should count this, and pro-choice groups, as a win for them because this pill, the access was never cut off, the Supreme Court allowed it to remain available on the market, it is a political loss for them because they can't campaign against this Supreme Court passed another restriction on your abortion. But in an election year, that may be something they would want to be able to take to voters.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think right now, given that the way the courts and some of the religious groups have been handling IVF, invitro fertilization, the issue is very alive for those on the left who say there's still real concern about a woman's right to handle pregnancy, even getting pregnant now, and that there are people on the right who want to take away control of your bodies from you.\par \par \uc2 Now, the thing about today is I don't think it brings any closer to a consensus on how government -- what government's role should be with regard to abortion because of the standing issue that you so aptly described. But I think that you're right in saying people on the left are pretty much relieved that they didn't take away their right to the pill.\par \par \uc2 But the way I think of it in political terms, Shannon, is what's the motivator here? And picking up on what Katie was just talking about, if you look at it for the pro-choice people, abortion still remains a great motivator to get people out to vote. What happened today I think is neutral for people who are pro-life.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we know that there were pro-life folks who were very happy about the conscience language that was in this 9-0 opinion that seems to give real voice to protecting doctors against things that they don't want to be involved with, including abortion. So there is that to unpack as well.\par \par \uc2 OK. Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 LIPPMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And that is it. The Republicans take down a 31 to 11 victory over the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Wow, you should have suited up, Juan. They could have used you last night.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was the final out from last night's Congressional baseball game. The GOP hammered Democrats 31 to 11 at Nationals Park. This is not a defensive battle. It was their fourth straight victory. The Democrats briefly held a five to four lead, but a 10-run seventh inning by the Republicans fueled the blowout. The game was momentarily interrupted by climate protesters, but play went on. More than $2 million raised for charity.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Shannon Bream in Washington. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240614ek6d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Meets With GOP Lawmakers In Washington; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Won't Pardon Son; \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Busts Suspect With Weapons Cache, Body Armor; Anti-Israel Mob Threatens Zionists On NYC Subway; Biden Holds Rare News Conference With Zelenskyy; Zelenskyy: China Said Won't Give Russia Weapons; Ukraine Aid To Be Backed By Frozen Russia Assets; Biden And Zelenskyy Sign 10-Year Security Agreement\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Benjamin Hall, Brett Velicovich, Lisa Boothe,  Aishah Hasnie, Bryan Llenas \par \uc2 9,594 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: I would also like to thank the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  for their support, both parties, both chambers. Thank you and thanks to every American heart that does not betray freedom and supports us. Slava Ukraini.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: Now, what we'll do is we're going to take two questions from American reporters and two question each from two American reporters and a question each from two Ukrainian supporters, reporters. The first person I'm to call is Colleen Long and Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 COLLEEN LONG, ASSOCIATED PRESS: Thanks, Mr. President. About two weeks ago you changed course to allow Ukraine to fire U.S. weapons into Russia. Given the reported successes, would you consider further expanding the parameters on U.S. weapons into Russia, even despite your concerns about escalation?\par \par \uc2 And on the news from home, you're going through something that so many American families go through, the intersection of addiction and the criminal justice system, but you're not like most families. Was your son able to get a fair trial? Do you believe the Justice Department operated independently of politics? And for President --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Let me answer your question, you're asking this question.\par \par \uc2 LONG: OK.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: With regard to the first question, it is clear that the near broad, meaning just across the line of the border with Russia and Ukraine, that it makes a lot of sense for Ukraine to be able to take out or combat what is coming across that border. In terms of long-range weapons, long-range weapons into the interior of Russia, we have not changed our position on that sort. With regard to the question regarding the family, I'm extremely proud of my son, Hunter. He has overcome an addiction. He is one of the brightest, most decent men I know, and I am satisfied that I'm not going to do anything. I said I'd buy by the jury decision. I will do that, and I will not pardon him.\par \par \uc2 LONG: President Zelenskyy, a number of leaders here in Italy, including President Biden, are facing upcoming election challenges. How will the security agreement signed tonight, and the other promises of support continue if they are not in office? And what's your contingency plan if they don't?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Thank you for this question. May I ask? First and foremost, I'd like to thank the people of the nations, first and foremost, the United States, to the countries in Europe and on other continents who have supported us since the very beginning of this absolutely unjust war of Russia against the people of Ukraine. That is it.\par \par \uc2 They have been killing people, homes and territory, all that is very important. It is part of life, but first and foremost we are speaking about people and lives of people. You understand? And this war was unjust since the very beginning, the war of this evil, whose name is Putin, the war against the people of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And he has killed so many people. To say that it is not him, there was a military man who did it, the last one is just an instrument of his, and he is playing this instrument. And therefore, it is important for us that since the very beginning we were supported by people, by nations, because they understood that we share common values. We simply want to live and the people understood.\par \par \uc2 They imagined what will happen if such evil attacks them and therefore we were supported by people. And I thank President Biden and other leaders who since the very beginning of the invasion, Putin's invasion, started to support us. They, based on their values, they were based on the voice of their people, and it is impossible without people.\par \par \uc2 And I am sure that this nation chooses leaders and presidents, and it seems to me that no matter whom the nation chooses first and foremost, it seems to me that everything depends on the unity within this or that state. And if the people are with us, any leader will be with us in this struggle for freedom.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The (INAUDIBLE), can you report it?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Yes, yes, yes, it was a pleasure. Thanks so much. Yes, please Inter.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE), Inter-TV channel. So, today, during the \uc2 G7\uc2  meeting the discussion focused on developing Ukraine's air defense system based on the most advanced, most are complexes, and also on enhancing long-range capabilities. So, my question is, can you provide any details on the initiative and about the readiness of our allies to take part in it? Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'd be happy to respond to that. We have acquired commitment from five countries so far for Patriot batteries and other air defense systems, as well as we let it be known to those countries that are expecting from us, air defense systems in the future -- that they're going to have to wait. Everything we have is going to go to Ukraine until their needs are met, and then we will make good on the commitments we made to other countries.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: I think President Biden already answered your question, really. He knows, and all other partners, they know that urgently we need seven Patriot systems. Yes, to save our cities, not all of them, it's a pity, but urgently seven. And we discussed the possibility of having five of them, it's true, but the partners work on it. It doesn't mean that tomorrow we will have these five systems, but we see in the closest future good result for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You'll have some relatively quickly. American reporter, Josh Wingrove, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOSH WINGROVE, BLOOMBERG: Thank you, Mr. President. I have a question for President Zelenskyy shortly on the announcements, but if you don't mind, I'd like to ask you about your discussions on the situation in Gaza here at the summit. You were asked just a short time ago about it after the skydiving demonstration.\par \par \uc2 Can you give us your assessment of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's response? And do you believe that they are trying to work towards a deal, or is this response working against a deal? And what is your message to allies, including those here at the \uc2 G7\uc2 , about what more, if anything, the U.S. can do to drive towards a peace agreement. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I wish you guys would play by the rules a little bit. I'm here to talk about a critical situation in Ukraine. You asked me another subject. I'll be happy to answer it in detail later. But the bottom line is that we've made an agreement. I've laid out an approach that has been endorsed by the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2 , by the \uc2 G7\uc2 , by the Israelis, and the biggest hang-up so far is \uc2 Hamas\uc2  refusing to sign on even though they have submitted something similar. Whether or not it comes to fruition remains to be seen. We're going to continue to push. I don't have a final answer for you.\par \par \uc2 WINGROVE: And to President Biden's point, a question about today's discussions. President Zelensky, the $50 billion today, you've had the supplemental, of course, from the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  recently. Can you give us an assessment of the situation on the battlefield right now and what has been given now.\par \par \uc2 How long will this get you in terms of either stopping the Russian advance or making headway on this? And how long will it last you if indeed future leaders or current leaders are unable to reach consensus on further aid packages? And, President Biden, I'd welcome your assessment of the situation currently on the battlefield and what difference the supplemental has made as well, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY (through translation): Thank you for your question. Indeed, we were expecting the fundamental package of this support within the \uc2 Congress of the United States of America\uc2 , and truly it was a long pause for our warriors, first and foremost, but it is important. We are grateful that in the very end we have this supplement, and this will for sure strengthen our warriors.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this has given the opportunity to the enemy within this pause to try to occupy Kharkiv, but that attempt was stopped by our warriors. They were repelled, the enemy was repelled, and we, despite everything, disrupted all their plans, and it seems to me that that is the most important thing.\par \par \uc2 What this supplement that will arrive gives us, it enables us to fully equip the reserves, those guys, those brigades that are ready, so that they provide for the opportunity to rotate our units on the battlefield so that they can have some rest, so that the brigades can regenerate, so that other brigades enter the battlefield instead of them with equipment.\par \par \uc2 This is what the supplement gives them, so the raise of morale, but also the raise of strength of our brigades. And it seems to me that this is the most important. For how long this will be enough? Look, we without package have been holding the lines for eight months and the Russians had no success.\par \par \uc2 And therefore, the question on for how long it will be enough? No, I think the question has to be for how long the unity will last, the unity in the United States together with the European leaders. How these or those elections will influence this unity.\par \par \uc2 It seems to me that we should look on this exactly this way, to preserve unity, to preserve the integrity of the world, integrity of the Democratic world. Because if Ukraine does not withstand, the democracy of many countries will not be able to withstand, and I'm sure of that.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: By the way, the idea that we had to wait until we passed the legislation overall, even held up by a small majority of our Republican colleagues, is just terrible. And there's a lot more money coming beyond what's already come in the other tranches that are available now that we pass the legislation, so they'll have what they need and get it there as quickly as we possibly can.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Thank you so much, Mr. President. Telegraf, please.\par \par \uc2 YAROSLAV ZHARINOV, TELEGRAF UA: Good evening. My name is Yaroslav Zharinov, Telegraf U.A. Thank you for this opportunity. I have a long way from Kyiv and have enough time to prepare such long question. Firstly, to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Mr. President, the additional Ukraine Supplemental Appropriations Act that you signed mandated the submission of strategy war for the war in Ukraine within 45 days after its enactment.\par \par \uc2 This deadline passed on June 8th, and now yet the international community has not seen this strategy. Has it been developed? And if the strategy is classified, what step does your administration plan to take to hostel Ukraine victory in the world? It's my first question. The second will be to the President Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What was the last part of your question?\par \par \uc2 ZHARINOV: Has it been developed this strategy and what steps does your administration plan to take to victory Ukraine in the world?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The steps we're going to take to make it has -- Ukraine has victory and that Russia does not prevail is continued support when we just signed, we signed that and a significant number of nations have signed it. We have convinced the \uc2 G7\uc2 , not convinced we've got supported the \uc2 G7\uc2 , and quite frankly, 48 other countries.\par \par \uc2 We sat with the Prime Minister of Japan, South Korea. We have 50 nations who signed up beyond \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the \uc2 G7\uc2 . And so, we're going to stay as long as it takes. With regard to the plan, that is a plan in process now. We're discussing with our Ukrainian friends exactly what it would be. We have a lot of movement toward that.\par \par \uc2 We know the outlines of it. We have not done the detail of it all. But we know what Ukraine is capable of doing when given the material to defend themselves, and that's exactly what they're doing now.\par \par \uc2 ZHARINOV (through translation): And my second question is to President Zelenskyy. It will be in Ukraine. And recently you have made a couple of sharp statements regarding China, and there are rumors in press regarding the possible supplies of Russia's weapons to China.\par \par \uc2 Apart from that, China is actively promoting its own peace plan among certain countries. What are the motives of Beijing now? And would it be possible to change the vision of China regarding our war? And which role the U.S. can play in this? And the final question, is China a partner of Russia in these crimes that it commits.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: I understand that your trip was long. So, first of all, I had phone conversation with a leader of China by phone. He said that he will not sell any weapon to Russia. That is the first. We'll see with you, we'll see, but he said to me. If he is a respectable person, he will not, because he gave me the word. The second, our, you know that -- you know, very good with details how our peace formula is very open for everybody basing on charter.\par \par \uc2 Yes, your nation and you know that it's a basis on next principles territorial integrity, sovereignty, nuclear security, food security.\par \par \uc2 If China has alternative view on it, it can prepare alternative peace formula. If we share common views on it, like with, globally, with all the world, I think so.\par \par \uc2 So, if they share the same way to peace, we will find dialogue.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: By the way, China is not supplying weapons, but the ability to produce those weapons and the technology available to do it. So, it is in fact helping Russia.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all so very much.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: These concludes our press conference. Thank you, everybody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, would you could be a --\par \par \uc2 Would you --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, you --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Let's dig into that last comment by President Biden and see exactly what he said there. But an interesting comment at the end by \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2 . And we're going to turn this around in just a moment. But he basically said he had spoken with President Xi of China, and had assurances from President Xi that he would not sell any weapons to Russia, to use in the war against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, he's already got one weapon, in a sense exchange between the two countries because China is buying up a lot of Russian oil and helping them to make a lot of money during this period of war.\par \par \uc2 So, the bottom line of this \uc2 G7\uc2  meeting that we just witnessed this news conference after is that they signed a security PAC that the administration says, "sends Russia a signal of our resolve. Meaning, U.S. resolve in this matter.\par \par \uc2 Another big focus today, the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders agreeing to loan Ukraine, essentially, it's a complicated financial deal. But it's $50 billion dollars to be paid back with the interest on frozen Russian assets.\par \par \uc2 So, these sanctions have locked up an enormous amount billions of dollars of Russian assets. And the idea is that the interest on that, that the money that that's spinning off would be used to support the war in Ukraine. It's an inventive idea whether or not it can work, we will see.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in correspondent Benjamin Hall, who is the host of a new Fox Nation special, "ANTISEMITISM IN AMERICA", available now.\par \par \uc2 Ben, great to have you with us this afternoon. Your thoughts on what we just heard. It was an interesting news conference, and they went a little bit longer than we thought they might, in terms of answering a few questions there. What did you -- what did you make of it?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Well, absolutely. And I think, Martha, that the big picture of this was the message that we've heard from President Biden, for some years now that he will stand side by side with Ukraine, the Russia can't wait us out, that we are going to stay as long as it takes the pieces there.\par \par \uc2 And he set out these three things. He said there was the 10-year bilateral peace agreement, which is Zelenskyy said was legal. Then he obviously said that there was the $50 billion loans he was going to arrange.\par \par \uc2 And then, he talked about sanctions, which he thinks is a key area to focus on as well. So, those are the three things he wants to focus on. But aside of those, as other than the background, you have the idea of all these elections are going to take place, first and foremost in the U.S. in November.\par \par \uc2 And it does feel that the point of today was send a message to say that the U.S. is going to be side by side with Ukraine, no matter what happens in November. And that's why they were putting these plans in place now.\par \par \uc2 The fact is that many of these can be overturned if President Trump was to win and President Trump was speaking earlier today. And he already spoke about trying to bring back the money being spent from overseas.\par \par \uc2 So, the message is strong. President Biden saying we are side by side with you. But of course, so many questions underlying that about whether or not it can continue, depending on the results in November.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's a great point. Now, we've seen what's happening in Europe. You see further right-wing candidates gaining traction in the legislative arms of governments in France, in Germany, where \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  is -- Scholz's position is a little bit in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 Macron, also in the similar position. You know, it -- when -- I listened Zelenskyy talk, he's talking about countries that have shared values. And last week we were in Normandy. And C.Q. Brown today, the commander in chief of the U.S. forces, excuse me, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, you know, also referenced Normandy and the bond that exists between European countries against countries that are not like minded like Russia when it comes to the elements of freedom.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a very -- it feels like a seminal moment in terms of how Russia will act based on what happens in all these places, Ben.\par \par \uc2 HALL: Well, absolutely. And President Putin is watching very closely to see what happens in these elections. And look, I'll go quickly to talk about the Russian ships that are in Cuba at the moment.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 HALL: That is a very clear message that President Biden likes to send out. And so, he will be looking at all these countries, he'll be using different methods to try and make them change the ways. But today, the message was also about unity.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy said it many, many times, and we are unified over this. And I do think that even if, you know, all these leaders sitting there today, except for Meloni, the Italian leader are at risk of losing their jobs quite quickly.\par \par \uc2 And all of those likely to move them further to the right in those countries.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't, by any means, be mean that those countries are going to turn away from Ukraine. Inside Europe, there is a shared understanding, except for a couple of countries like Hungary that they will stand side by side.\par \par \uc2 I think what many people are asking is can they raise their spending on military to the two percent?\par \par \uc2 The majority of countries sitting at that table don't yet, and I think that's a key point when it comes into how any presidents in the U.S. should be looking at it. We didn't hear President Biden talk about that today.\par \par \uc2 But the European countries want to come side by side and look forward, and passes elections, and that's something they have to do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, absolutely. And we've heard former President Trump essentially say if you don't pay your fair share, then, you're on your own. So, we'll see where that goes. Ben, thank you very much. Great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 HALL: Thank -- thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joining me now, Brett Velicovich, national security analyst and Fox News contributor. He's been to Ukraine many times since the war began, and was with the U.S. military in Iraq and Afghanistan. Brett, good to have you here. What's your takeaway from what you heard? What jumped out at you from this news conference that just ended moments ago?\par \par \uc2 BRETT VELICOVICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, for me, this $50 billion from the \uc2 G7\uc2 , it's a historic agreement. It intensifies pressure on Russia, and the move sets of precedent for how international law can work to address state sponsored aggression coming from Russia.\par \par \uc2 And I just returned from another trip to Ukraine this week. And it's clear from my conversations on the ground that those soldiers in the trenches, they need international support more than ever. And we, as Americans are supposed to be the beacon of that international support and defending democracy. And the other countries, they tend to follow our lead, which is exactly why we can't just walk away from them.\par \par \uc2 You know, the Ukrainians, they are significantly hurting for manpower, materials, money to really effectively defend their country. And they've got this underlying fear of it all going away.\par \par \uc2 I get asked this question a lot, as I'm sure many other Americans there do. Are we going to leave them? Are we going to leave them like we did in Afghanistan, and you can take that from, you know, our previous actions and having these long-term consequences with our allies strategically of what we did there in Afghanistan, whether you're a diplomat or a businessman or humanitarian in Ukraine, these are the types of questions that Ukrainian leaders ask American.\par \par \uc2 So, this continued commitment to them as important, the billions in funds, they are going to come from interest accumulating from Frozen Russian assets. That's key. You know, they are getting about $3 billion in interest alone, but they're not allowed to release these assets fully yet. So, there is still some work to -- some more work to do there. But it doesn't mark a significant, you know, move in international financial sanctions and support from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And I've never really felt that the previous sanctions against Russia have been working strategically. I mean, our leadership, they need to face that reality. And they have got to come up with more creative solutions like this, to supply the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 By my estimates, we have over 16,000 sanctions on Russian individuals and entities currently in place. That's nearly six times the amount compared to before the full-scale war began. But yet, Russian banks, their military industrial complex, they have adapted. They developed workarounds to evade it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 VELICOVICH: So, by reallocating these Russian assets, it really sends a strong message of deterrence and the consequences of violating these laws.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to put a couple of maps up for everybody at home. The first one is June of 2023. And the next one is the current map. This is May of 2024. These are from the Institute of the Study of War.\par \par \uc2 Not a lot of movement between these two. The red shows the section that the Russian offensive took over. And there is not a lot of movements. So, if President Biden said just recently, Brett, that the goal is to get Russia completely out of Ukraine\par \par \uc2 And when I asked John Kirby about that, he said, yes, that means Crimea, Donetsk, Luhansk, all of it, out of Ukraine. So, that's what victory looks like. How long, how far away are we from that? And are we giving them the weapons that they need to achieve that in a timely manner or a more timely manner, which would be great. There is a lot of people dying in this war.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's difficult to say. I think this war is going to, unfortunately, last for quite some time. Ukraine has been extremely resilient. That's a fact. I mean, the Ukrainian people are fighting democracy for us. And so, we need to do whatever we can to support them.\par \par \uc2 We need to provide them with more weapons, we need to provide them with more capabilities and support this long-term regional stability and security. And I think this financial support does that.\par \par \uc2 It also potentially helps create these more diplomatic efforts to start, and look for some peaceful resolution to this conflict, encouraging Russia basically, to maybe engage in some meaningful negotiations to hopefully stop this war.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will see. Putin seems pretty determined and, you know, better than anybody, having spent this time on the ground. So, Brett, thank you very much. We'll talk to you soon.\par \par \uc2 VELICOVICH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Be well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, also breaking today, a lot going on this afternoon. Former President Trump issued a direct rallying cry to Republicans. He was back on Capitol Hill for the first time since January 6, really. Less than five months to go until the election day.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Byron Donalds, who is on all the lists that you see as a potential vice-presidential candidate with President Trump. We'll talk to him about what the message was today, what was achieved, when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How was it seeing the former president?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): We had a good meeting.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Didn't he say anything about you?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did you talk to him directly?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: What?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did you talk to him directly?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Yes. But we -- he took questions from the audience and it was an entirely positive session.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former President Trump back in Washington, D.C. today, meeting with GOP lawmakers and saying that there is tremendous unity in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We want to see voters. We want to see strong military. We want to see money not wasted all over the world. We don't want to see Russian ships right off the coast of Florida, which is what they are right now. That's unthinkable. We want to see just success for our country. And we don't have success right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With that, let's bring in Republican Florida Congressman Byron Donalds, who was at the House meeting and is reportedly on the Trump VP shortlist. He's also part of our new Fox Nation special "Antisemitism in America," which is Ben Hall's new Fox Nation special.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome. Good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 As has been pointed out, this was President Trump's first trip back to Capitol Hill since January the 6th. What was it like in that room?\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): It was great. You know, the president was really talking about his mission, his agenda. He talked a lot about wanting to make sure he could do everything he could to help House Republicans keep our majority and actually increase it, and what his personal time investments would be to helping members up here on Capitol Hill who are in tough re-elections. And also candidates who are in tough free elections, where we're trying to pick up seats to get some of the Democrats off of Capitol Hill and get our country back on track. That's what he was talking about.\par \par \uc2 He also expanded in some policy areas, really thinking deeply about ways to make America great again, because we are a struggling nation right now with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as president. And so, he's taking the time to think through all those things, talk with the members of the House about them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, congressman, there was a report in Politico today that says that the former president is demanding that Speaker Mike Johnson help him overturn his New York conviction. It says Trump was still angry when he made the call, according to those who have heard accounts of it from Johnson, dropping frequent F bombs as he spoke with the soft spoken and pious GOP leader, "We have to overturn this," Trump insisted. What's your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, I don't have a reaction, because I'm not aware of this call even took place. I talked to Mike Johnson, it seems like every other day, not heard anything about this. So let me go back and actually find out what's happened in here.\par \par \uc2 Let me just say for the record, of course, the president has a right to be upset. What happened in Lower Manhattan was a travesty of justice, and now you have the Democrats trying to have this moral equivalency between Hunter Biden being convicted in Delaware. What happened in Lower Manhattan was a violation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 Nobody's above the law, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not below the law. So if there are things that happen up here on Capitol Hill, where we are funding departments that are violating the constitutional rights of citizens, no matter who that citizen might be, Congress has a responsibility to look into that and to act as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And what can Congress do about that?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, primarily, there's been talk about looking at some of the funding that goes to local jurisdictions that violate the constitutional rights of citizens, because all these local prosecutors, all these local areas, they all want federal funds. Well, if you're going to take federal money, you have a responsibility to protect the constitutional rights of every citizen, you don't get to pick and choose.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately in lower Manhattan, Judge Merchan and \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , they chose to violate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's due process rights.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We expect that Bragg and Colangelo will testify before the House Judiciary Committee. What would you want to -- I know you're on oversight but what would you hope they would be pressed on in that room, in that hearing, congressman?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, I think the key thing is why did they try to go through this scheme, and it was a scheme. It's important people to understand why go through this scheme of resurrecting suppose it misdemeanor that has passed the statute of limitations using a federal election law knowing that the \uc2 FEC\uc2  had already looked at this case, and they decided to proceed anyway.\par \par \uc2 I think with respect to Mr. Colangelo, what lawyer do you know, I know a lot of lawyers in this business, especially what lawyer leaves a prominent job being the number three at main justice to go down to be a frontline prosecutor in some state jurisdiction in Lower Manhattan unless you have a political axe to grind. And also, have there been any communications between Mr. Colangelo, the White House counsel, and anybody at the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 These are important issues to ferret out because we are witnessing the political persecution of the Republican nominee by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Last question, these are the current names that we understand are on the VP shortlist. We know that the former president may have ideas of his own separate from this, but these are the ones who are the most discussed. And several of you are in that room today, do you want to be the vice president of the United States, Congressman Donalds?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Oh of course I would. But, I mean, look right now it's about making sure we get to our convention. We are going to make sure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is our nominee. That is going to happen no matter what. And then it's about victory this November.\par \par \uc2 I think whoever on that list or any other list that might come up, they're going to do a great job helping \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  become the 47th president of the United States. That's the mission at hand. Whoever becomes the person on the ticket, that's just going to be to help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  get our country back on track.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So is this something like, you know, I saw JD Vance was in that room, I believe Tim Scott was in the room. Is this something that you guys talk about when you're hanging out?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: No. The kind of comes up is just like all of the palace intrigue and all the speculation. But at the end of the day, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to make this decision on his own by himself, and he now has the true advantage of having been president for four years.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately for America, he had time away from that post. So I think he knows exactly what he needs. I look forward to that choice. And I'm going to support him no matter what he does.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Have you been vetted? Have you been asked for information?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: That one I'm not going to get into. I've been very clear that whatever is going on in that process, I believe, between the president and his team, not going to get into it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Congressman Byron Donalds, always pleasure to speak with you, sir. Thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former President Trump has just sat down for an exclusive new interview with Fox. We are going to bring you that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking moments ago, President Biden's first on camera reactions since his son, Hunter, was convicted on all three charges in his gun trial. And here's what the President said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: I'm extremely proud of my son Hunter. He is overcoming addiction. He is one of the brightest, most decent men I know. And I am satisfied that I'm not going to do anything. I said I bide by the jury decision, I will do that. And I will not pardon him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Let's bring in Lisa Boothe, host of the "Truth with Lisa Boothe" which is hard for me to say, podcast and Fox News contributor, Jack Brewer, former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player and Chairman of the Jack Brewer foundation and Philippe Reines, former Deputy Assistant Secretary of State and senior adviser to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , now a CKR solutions principal. Great to have all of you with us today.\par \par \uc2 Lisa, let me start with you. Your reaction to what the President had to say. We weren't sure if he would get a question about Hunter situation, but he did.\par \par \uc2 LISA BOOTHE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think he's lying. I think he absolutely will try to pardon his son \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . I don't believe him for a second. I think this is political speak. And then you'll turn around and do the opposite after the election. So I don't believe him.\par \par \uc2 I think that the verdict against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  does muddy the waters a little bit, but more so than that the lawfare against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has helped them. I mean, as long as they don't throw the guy in jail, it is unified Republicans for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It is giving a reason to those who may -- maybe were on the fence about supporting him to turn out.\par \par \uc2 Because they made this election about something so much bigger than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This election is about saving the Republicans about the rule of law. And I know people like me who were previously already enthusiastic, Martha, I would walk through broken glass in the middle of a house on fire to support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to try to save this election.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Philippe, you want to agree with that right? You would also do that?\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPE REINES, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF STATE: I got to tell you, I've never met Lisa but I think Lisa should be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's vice president, because that's the most full-throated endorsement I've ever seen. Look, I would say --\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: I don't think it's going to happen tonight, you know, I'm available.\par \par \uc2 REINES: I'd almost have to switch my vote. It's sad what's happening with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . I think it's, you know, obviously it takes a real nick out of what the Republicans are saying about the corruption in the Department of Justice when your own son is going to jail, I realized it would be convenient for the President to pardon him or commute his sentence, but he's not.\par \par \uc2 We'll see if Lisa is right. I don't think he is, personally. I hope that Hunter is let out of jail at some point.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the trial, I think that this is crazy, this idea that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is benefiting from going on Trump being found a felon. If that was true, his lawyers wouldn't be spending so much time trying to delay the other three trials. He would just be saying bring 'em on right now and he'd be pleading guilty.\par \par \uc2 That is not the case. The numbers have tightened because of it. There are some specific demographic groups that are softening because of it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting. So let's take a look at some of these polls. This is poll number two, Monmouth 56% say they agree or lean agree with the verdict in Trump's New York trial. So that goes to Philippe's point there. And then, this one is another Monmouth poll. Trump and Biden remain in a statistical tie from April's poll despite the Trump guilty verdict. So the polls have not moved from last from April to now, Jack Brewer. What do you say about that? Jack, can you hear us OK? Looks like he's having some trouble with his link up.\par \par \uc2 Lisa, your thoughts on those polls because it looks like people are not so sure about the verdict or about Trump-Biden, very tight.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Well, you know, again, I do think the verdict has helped \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the rallying cry. We're seeing the Kavanaugh effect again, where you have people like Susan Collins, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and even \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  saying, you know what, it's probably bad when you have a judge who donated to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who donated a stop Republicans who seems committed to doing just that with lawfare. So I think you have an awakening going on there.\par \par \uc2 Also, in the Monmouth poll that you pointed out, 71% of Republicans were enthusiastic about the rematch between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  whereas only 46% of Democrats are enthusiastic about this rematch. So it has had a motivating impact for Republicans to turnout.\par \par \uc2 And as I said before on the show and, you know, on the network, elections are building Bronx, right? And so, Republicans are building a broader base of support. We're starting out there, and then you build from there. So it has had a motivating impact. And then you also look at the $53 million raised within the 24 hours after the guilty verdict as well tell us that story. So I maintain what I previously said.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quick thought, Philippe, and I'm sorry to Jack Brewer, I think we lost his signal. You know, every day there's more video, I could play another one but I'm not going to right now, of the President sort of wandering and looking up into the sky, not quite sure where he is. And a lot of people, you know, who you've worked with in the past have called for him they think that there needs to be another alternative here.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any more thoughts on that, Philippe, today?\par \par \uc2 REINES: Well, this is a great opportunity to wish former president Trump and early happy 78th birthday. He's closing in on 80. We've got two guys that are not spring chickens. So if someone wants a young guy, we're all at a look.\par \par \uc2 Look, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his campaign, the Republican Party and the right wing want to continue with this \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can barely stand up routine. And then, when he does stand up in the debates and the State of the Union had to call him on drugs, then that's great expectations for us, because this is silly.\par \par \uc2 We just watched him with President Zelenskyy. I understand you can pick up any kind of video of anytime. There's plenty of video of President Trump where he looks at it, he's mumbling, he's not making sense.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: He's not falling off --\par \par \uc2 REINES: He shouldn't be driving cars --\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: He's shaking the air.\par \par \uc2 REINES: These guys don't drive themselves.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. There's a lot going on in the world.\par \par \uc2 REINES: Lisa Boothe for vice president.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: I will endorse that message.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Byron Donalds just told me he wants it, so you're going to have to duke it out. Thank you, guys. Great to see you both, Philippe and Lisa, thank you guys. Apologies to Jack Brewer, hopefully getting back on the show soon.\par \par \uc2 So breaking right now, an exclusive new interview with former president Trump who just spoke with our congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie after his meetings with Republican lawmakers in Washington, and Aishah joins us now. Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha, good to see you. Well, yes, Trump has snuck in a quick interview with me as he is now on his way back to Mar-a-Lago after quite a whirlwind tour of Capitol Hill, at least outside Capitol Hill all across Washington, DC talking with Republicans in the House and the Senate, really trying to unite the party, not only behind him but really to be able to tell voters that you will be in good hands if you reelect Senate and House Republicans. He wants to win back majorities in both chambers.\par \par \uc2 And as he departs, if he didn't already create some chaos around the city of buzz, he also made a ton of news in my one on one exclusive interview. I asked him if he had chosen his vice president, if he knew it. I think Martha you'll remember I believe he was telling you and Bret Baier on the national stage during a town hall in Iowa that he already knew his VP pick. And here's what he told me, listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Do you know who your VP is? Because you told us at a town hall you already knew back in the Iowa caucuses.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I have sort of a pretty good idea. Look, we have some really talented people. I have a pretty good idea. But I think it's probably I'll do it the way it's usually done. We announce that --\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Male? Female? Can you give us a hint?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, he knows that Democrats are watching his every move, and right now they are blowing something up that he said about the \uc2 RNC\uc2  host city, Milwaukee, apparently calling the city horrible. I asked him to explain, Martha, and he said that he was talking about the crime in the city and the election integrity issues. And he says that Democrats are just making a big fuss about nothing.\par \par \uc2 He also made some pretty big news on the campaign trail. You'll remember that there was some back and forth between the Trump campaign, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  and \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , the former governor of Maryland because he was telling voters and Americans to respect that New York verdict that came down against the former president. And I asked him, do you support -- will you support \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  and will you endorse him? And he said, yes, I guess I am here endorsing \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 . He's going to be behind him 100%.\par \par \uc2 I also asked him about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and I also asked him about many, many more things, including his conversation with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , coming face to face with the GOP leader for the first time. He said he had a pleasant conversation with the leader after four years of bad blood. A lot more news from that interview. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting. Aishah, thank you very much. Little bearing hatchet there between McConnell and Trump today as they unify to support the former president as he runs again. Aishah, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You got it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So a man charged with having a car full of weapons, ammo, body armor and more reportedly posted extremist jihadist messages online and may have been planning something "very bad" according to law enforcement and the New York Post.\par \par \uc2 So here is what police say the officers found when they stopped an SUV with a blacked out license plate in Queens yesterday. Axes, knives, a Glock gun, nine loaded magazines, a stun gun, handcuffs, an orange vest that transit workers wear and an \uc2 NYPD\uc2  bulletproof vest. Where did he get those?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY MADDREY, \uc2 NYPD\uc2  CHIEF OF DEPARTMENT: members of this department make gun arrest, weapons of arrest for knives every day. But arrest of this magnitude, the amount of ammunition \uc2 NYPD\uc2  paraphernalia in the car was significant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that comes just days after the Feds arrested eight migrants from Tajikistan, a source telling fox that they are suspected of having ties to \uc2 ISIS\uc2 . Former New York congressman and candidate for governor \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  standing by, but first national correspondent Bryan Llenas reporting live here in New York City. Hi, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, look, it's the kind of story that makes you ask yourself what if? What if \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers patrolling Queens, New York at 1:30 in the morning did not decide to stop a Ford Explorer for having a blacked out license plate. Could there have been a terror attack?\par \par \uc2 Well in court today, prosecutors say this car stop could have been a disaster for the country. The suspect 27-year-old Judd Sanson was arraigned less than an hour ago on multiple charges, including multiple criminal weapons possession charges and a charge for an obscured license plate.\par \par \uc2 The Queens district attorney says they are continuing their investigation, but at this time no formal terrorism charges. Not only was Sanson traveling with a loaded .9 millimeter handgun and nine loaded magazines, we're learning more about the stockpile of weapons in that car. There were 10 weapons, an axe, weighted whip, stun gun, machete, two switch blades, two metal knives, a hatchet and expandable baton.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say on that baton they found written phrases etched including left me no choice, you going to learn today in an Arabic phrase that translates to I asked in God for forgiveness. They also found official \uc2 NYPD\uc2  gear like handcuffs and police body armor, and orange \uc2 MTA\uc2  vest, the kind worn by New York City subway employees and a V for Vendetta mask, an anti- government symbol.\par \par \uc2 This comes after \uc2 FBI\uc2  director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  warned last week about our nation's vulnerability to terrorism, saying that the threat right now is at a whole another level. Wray says he's concerned lone wolf or small groups will draw a twisted inspiration from war in Israel and the ISIS-K attack, Martha, at the Russia concert hall earlier this year that left some 140 dead. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bryan, thank you very much. And a new warning about how the feds screen migrants coming into the country. The \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2 's inspector general says the technology and the procedures that they're using, not really fully effective. No kidding.\par \par \uc2 "DHS will remain at risk of admitting dangerous persons into the country or enabling asylum seekers who may pose a significant threat to public safety and national security." DHS says it agrees with all of the report's recommendations and is starting to fix the problem.\par \par \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  joins me now, former New York Republican Congressman,