. We need more money for you know, border patrol, we need more money for all these things. And yet the money that exists $86 billion, and they haven't updated their technology. And they said these people were vetted, who came across the border.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this should give everyone no security sense whatsoever. What about what happened in New York with this individual? What does that sound like to you? And are you concerned that there are more people with designs on New York City or New York State?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: There certainly are more people out there who are planning no good criminal behavior at different levels, in different cities and elsewhere. We need to make sure that law enforcement is properly-funded and supported, that they have the tools that they need to be able to conduct these investigations that are prosecutors are actually focused on prosecuting violent criminals, as opposed to seeing resources getting spent elsewhere, as we have witnessed since the first day that \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  has come into his position in one borough.\par \par \uc2 And he's not the only district attorney inside of the city of New York who has shown priorities that had been out of whack. So thankfully, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  has been on top of this case. But what I'm concerned about is that as more illegal migrants keep coming into the country, they might continue to organize, they may continue to ramp up their activities that could be gang related activities. And we may be going backwards towards that time when your MS-13 just a few years ago was ramping up and committing violent acts.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. There's no reason to think we're not when you see this Venezuelan gang activity and the attacks on police officers. I want to play this really disturbing video for you. This is New York anti-Israel protesters chanting on a subway. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Raise your hand if you're a Zionist.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS: Raise your hand if you're a Zionist.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is your chance to get out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS: This is your chance to get out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK, no Zionist we're good. We don't want no Zionist here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No Zionist here, telling people they should get off that that subway train if they're Jewish, or if they support the Jewish state. Did you ever think you would live to see this moment in New York, in the subway?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: No, absolutely not. I'm Jewish. I went through kindergarten through 12th grade College Law School, four years of active duty, I never experienced anti-Semitism. I didn't have people around me who had experienced anti-Semitism. And I thought maybe that I would be the first of what would be many generations that would not have to any longer experience this.\par \par \uc2 What we are witnessing on a daily basis, just in New York, you show one example there. There were people who had their homes vandalized in Brooklyn. I saw a story that came out of western New York in Buffalo, where there was a day school that was blocked of government funding, because it was a Jewish affiliated day school.\par \par \uc2 This is happening on a daily basis, and this is now crossing into the line of terrorism. Yes, there's been his free speech conversation at one point where we're saying that, hey, there are certain signs here that this is beyond free speech. And people who are using October 7th as a rallying cry, that's a red flag.\par \par \uc2 But what we are witnessing right now is outright terrorism. I say this, identify, confront it and crush it. You can't be silence, you end up embracing it, elevating it and powering it. Even if you do nothing you have to step up and lead. You have to identify it and eliminate it\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Amazing that these people feel comfortable walking onto a New York subway and chanting these things. It is absolutely shocking. Former Congressman, former governor run for governor, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , thank you very much. Good to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for coming. So the Supreme Court issuing a unanimous ruling today protecting access to the abortion pill Mifepristone, the pill accounted for almost two thirds, think about this, two thirds of all abortion that happens in the United States is by pill.\par \par \uc2 So their opinion finding that the federal courts are the wrong forum to address the concern about the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's action. So it was a standing issue, not an issue wasting any sort of judgment on this pill. But an issue of whether there was the standing to bring up before the court.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says that this does not change the fact that the court overturned Roe v Wade. Well, that's correct. He also slammed Republicans and their "extreme and dangerous agenda" to ban abortion nationwide. So more to come, we're probably going to get more Supreme Court decision coming tomorrow and one of them might be the immunity case for the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So we'll be watching that very closely here tomorrow on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for being with us today, on this Thursday, June 13th. "The Story" goes on. I will see you back here tomorrow at 3:00. Your World starts right about now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240614ek6d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Migrants Flood Border Despite New Biden Order; High Cost of Migrant Crisis in New York City; Fallout After Trump's Rally in the South Bronx; Biden Wraps Up Trip To France; Four Israeli Hostages Rescued From Gaza; Interview With Sen. Tom Cotton (R-AR); House Republicans Send Criminal Referrals Against Hunter and James Biden for Lying to Congress; A number of Democrats are warning that if President Trump is re-elected that he will be a dictator. Naor Pakciarz is Interviewed about Surviving October 7th and the Hostages\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Matt Finn, Peter Doocy, Trey Yingst, Tammy Bruce \par \uc2 10,015 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 A stark scene in Europe this week as veterans of Normandy return to the country they liberated years ago and meet with the leader fighting to save his country now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WAR VETERAN: You're the savior of the people.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: No, no, no. You saved European.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): After an emotional commemoration of D-Day in Normandy, President Biden meeting with \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders in Italy, facing increasing criticism from European allies for standing by Israel in its war against \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , while trying to bolster support for Ukraine as it struggles in its war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk with Republican Tom Cotton who serves on the Senate Intel and Armed Services Committees.\par \par \uc2 Then, five months out from Election Day, President Biden finally takes executive action to try to secure America's broken border, one of the top issues voters say is hurting the country.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This action will help us to gain control of our border, restore order into the process.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Biden's order is not a border security plan. It's a concession to the fact that he has lost control over our border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: More than 8 million migrant encounters at the border since President Biden took office. Will his moves be enough to stem the flow of migrants that have overwhelmed major cities in both red and blue states, including the nation's biggest, New York City?\par \par \uc2 We'll ask Democratic Congressman \uc2 Ritchie Torres\uc2  whose South Bronx district played host to a major Trump rally last month.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON SMITH (R-MO): When someone lies to Congress, that is a crime, even if you are the president's son.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: New trouble for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  as House Republicans accuse him of lying to Congress at the same time his defense team prepares to fight back against the prosecution's case in his felony gun trial.\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel on the political blowback for the president.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in New York.\par \par \uc2 In the headlines today, celebrations in Israel this weekend as four Israelis are rescued in the largest hostage rescue operation since the latest war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  began. The four reunited with family after 246 days in captivity. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says 274 Palestinians were killed during the raid.\par \par \uc2 And the pier built by the U.S. military in Gaza is back up and taking in food and humanitarian aid for Palestinians, after it was blown apart in high winds and heavy seas last month.\par \par \uc2 Voters across Europe are set to decide the direction of the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's parliament today. Early polls suggest they could shift the world's largest trading block to the right amid concerns over the war in Ukraine and migration.\par \par \uc2 Those issues sure to be a top priority as \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders prepare to meet in Italy this week, including President Biden.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll get reaction from Republican Senator Tom Cotton.\par \par \uc2 But first, we've got team coverage from Trey Yingst in Israel, and where we began with White House correspondent Peter Doocy in Paris.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 President Biden spent part of his last day here in Paris greeting U.S. embassy staff. He's on his way right now to go lay a wreath at a World War I memorial all while warning that he thinks another big war in this part of Europe could be possible if the west stops sending nine figure munitions packages to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You know, you know Putin not going to stop at Ukraine. It's not just Ukraine. It's about much more than Ukraine, all of Europe will be threatened. We're not going to let that happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Later this week, the \uc2 G7\uc2  where Western allies will work not just to help Ukraine but also figure out how to counter China economically. President Biden claimed during a coffee with \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , that he's been getting complaints lately from Xi. This clip is going to be subtitled because the president was speaking so softly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My last discussion with Xi, he said to me, why are you talking about putting tariffs on some of your vehicles?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden has not acknowledged the large pro-Palestine protest at the White House Saturday that wrapped all the way around campus. Instead, he's keeping all the talk about the war in Gaza focused on the rescued hostages.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I want to echo President Macron's comments welcoming the safe rescue of four hostages that were returned to their families in Israel. We won't stop working until all the hostages come home and a ceasefire is reached. That is essential to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Before attending this week's \uc2 G7\uc2  in Italy, which is a very short flight from here, President Biden is actually going to fly home to Delaware for a couple days which means he will be home if there is a verdict in his son's trial by midweek -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We'll talk much more about that later in the show. Peter Doocy, thank you very much from Paris.\par \par \uc2 Let's go now to Trey Yingst in Tel Aviv, Israel, where people are celebrating the return of those four rescued hostages.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Shannon. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Almost 250 days into the war, the Israeli people received a rare piece of good news amid the conflict.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice-over): Twenty-six-year-old Noa Argamani climbs into an Israeli helicopter after 246 days in \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity. She's heading home.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL DANIEL HAGARI, ISRAELI ARMY SPOKESPERSON: Israeli special forces conducted complex hostage rescue mission and successfully rescued four of our hostages from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  captivity in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The daring rescue operation taking place in central Gaza at two locations. Israeli forces engaged in firefights with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  gunmen before securing the hostages.\par \par \uc2 As helicopters arrived at an Israeli hospital, crowds cheered. Four Israelis after more than months in Gaza were reunited with their loved ones. A father with his daughter, a man with his friends, a mother with her son.\par \par \uc2 ALMOG MEIR JAN, ORIT MEIR, MOTHER OF RESCUED HOSTAGE: Thank you for bringing my son to me, to us. I'm so excited I could hug him today.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The story of light does not come without dark. During the operation, one Israeli officer named Arnon Zamura succumbed to his wounds sustained during the battle and according to the \uc2 Hamas\uc2 -run Palestinian health ministry, over 200 people, the majority civilians, were killed during the operation, that included extensive Israeli air strikes.\par \par \uc2 They did not send us warnings or leaflets, nothing, Abdul Darwish explains, body parts spread in the streets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): The rescue operation illustrated the contradiction of war. For Palestinians, it was a day of death and destruction. For the Israeli people, exactly 35 weeks after Black Saturday, it was a day of hope -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst live in Israel for us -- thank you, Trey.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Arkansas Senator Tom Cotton.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to the show.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with your reaction to that daring rescue and the good news.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Shannon, thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 What a wonderful day for the people of Israel to have four of these hostages rescued in a truly heroic, well-executed mission yesterday.\par \par \uc2 I had a chance to speak with Prime Minister Netanyahu shortly after the mission. He commended the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  and \uc2 Shin Bet\uc2  and the Israeli police who helped execute this operation.\par \par \uc2 I'm very dismayed by sources from the progressive left here in America, to include "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " and "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", who are somehow condemning Israel for saving four hostages at the cost of supposed usually more than 200 Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 Now, we can't take at face value what \uc2 Hamas\uc2  says through the supposed Gaza Ministry of Health, but my advice to them would be, if you don't want your people killed in hostage rescue missions, then you shouldn't take hostages in the first place. You should release them once you have, and you certainly shouldn't hide them in civilian areas.\par \par \uc2 So, hats off to the Israeli security forces for a fantastic day in rescuing these four hostages.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So we understand there are still Americans being held. There has been discussion about whether our own special forces should be operating in that region. I know that, you know, there's great concern about U.S. forces being on the ground. There there's been a pledge that won't happen.\par \par \uc2 But what about our hostages and getting them out?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, Shannon our military has several units that are specifically trained in hostage rescue. They always have to be available and ready to rescue American citizens wherever they may be. Those are our responsibilities, and not Israel's or any other nations.\par \par \uc2 That said, the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  and security forces have the expertise operating in Gaza and working with the American government and specifically our intelligence services as well. I'm confident that if Israel's government had a chance to rescue American citizens, they would do the exact same for our citizens that they would do for Israel.\par \par \uc2 But our hostage rescue teams always have to be available. We simply wouldn't want to have any kind of conflict inside or with Israel's hostage rescue teams. We want to work with them hand in glove to make sure every hostage gets back alive.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. And there are more than a hundred of them we're still trying to figure out how they come home.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the president gave an interview to "Time" magazine a few days ago and he was asked this: Some in Israel have suggested that Netanyahu is prolonging the war for his own political self-preservation. Do you believe that?\par \par \uc2 President Biden responded: I'm not going to comment on that. But then added this: There's every reason for people to draw that conclusion.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that response from him about our ally in the region, and what do you say to critics who say that is exactly what Netanyahu is doing?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, this is just another slander by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  against \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  and the Israeli government at large.\par \par \uc2 President -- or Prime Minister Netanyahu's policies are very popular. There is a war cabinet that is signing off on missions like you saw yesterday.\par \par \uc2 At every turn, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has tried to limit Israel's freedom of action, their ability to defend themselves and to put more pressure on Israel, not pressure on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and its patrons in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Just look at what happened yesterday outside the White House, you had anti- American pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  lunatics defacing and desecrating the statues of our great veterans which is a plain violation of federal law. But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's government allowed it to happen and I bet we're not going to see any arrests or prosecutions for violating that law.\par \par \uc2 I'll introduce legislation this week that imposes stiffer penalties so we can lock these lunatics up, but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  thinks that these pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2 , anti-American lunatics should be guiding American policy towards Israel.\par \par \uc2 What we should be doing is backing Israel to the hilt and if we had done that since the October 7th attacks, this war would probably already be over, hostages would be freed and there'd be less civilian casualties and suffering in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is instead catering to the small pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  wing of the Democratic Party, instead of backing Israel, like the large pro-Israel majority of the American people want us to.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, that's what the polling shows.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about another hot spot, Ukraine. The president's been spending time with \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , Ukraine's president. He said something this week in talking with him, he apologized for the delay of aid, this latest round that come through -- came through, and he said -- he blames some of our very conservative members who were holding it up, says, "We finally got it done".\par \par \uc2 You blocked one of the earlier packages. You had concerns about it. There were changes to it. You did ultimately vote for it.\par \par \uc2 Do you think he's pointing the finger at you?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, Shannon, he should only be pointing the finger at himself. For more than two years, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  pussyfooted around and didn't give Ukraine the weapons it needed to defend itself. At every turn in this war, Ukraine has asked for certain types or quantities of weapons, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has refused, only to reverse himself three, or six, or nine months later when it was too late.\par \par \uc2 But take a step back and look at the broader context. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is largely responsible for tempting \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to do what he's always wanted to do, which is invade and annex Ukraine back to Russia.\par \par \uc2 And if you notice, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  only tends to invade Ukraine when Democrats are president. He did it under \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . He did it under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It did not happen under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For four years, we had peace and stability. And in fact, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reversed \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's failed policy of sending Ukrainians blankets and ponchos. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in office, we sent them the Javelins that were essential for winning the battle of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 And the way to have peace in Europe and for that matter peace and stability around the world is to remove \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  from the White House on Election Day this year and return \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That's how --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Ok, you --\par \par \uc2 COTTON: -- we get back to peace and stability.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You talk about annexation. So I want to bring this up because there are reports that what President Trump plans to do to end that conflict is to potentially push Ukraine to give up Crimea, parts of Donbas.\par \par \uc2 If that is the plan, do you agree with that strategy? And would that be reporting -- rewarding Putin in order to wrap this thing up in the way that he intended to start it and take some of that territory? Is that just giving him what he wanted?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, Shannon, President Trump and his campaign has said that any reports of plans like that are not authorized and they're not coming from the president himself.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, President Trump has said that he strongly supports Ukraine's strength and survival. He had a strong relationship when he was in office with President Zelenskyy. President Trump is the one that provided Ukraine the weapons they needed to fend off this Russian evasion that happened in large part because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's weakness.\par \par \uc2 I don't think President Trump wants to prejudge what the situation will be come January nor do I, in part because we have no idea how much worse \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can screw things up. We have to judge the circumstances as they exist next year when he returns to office and hopefully -- hopefully, when we have a Republican majority in Congress as well to make decisions about what best protects America's interest and the interest of our allies and partners.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, some of the talk, obviously, is about you being on the short list for vice president.\par \par \uc2 Can you confirm whether or not you're being vetted formally?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, Shannon, as I've said I think only one person knows who's on the short list and that would be President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I mean, paperwork requests for those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Shannon, I think that he'll make the right -- he'll make a choice when he's ready to make that choice. I'm confident he's going to make a good choice for the ticket and the party, more importantly for the country.\par \par \uc2 And I think that he'll -- again, he'll make that choice when the time is right. I'm focused on helping him win this election and helping Republicans win that majority in the Congress so we can begin to reverse the damage that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has inflicted on this country for four years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I think it's fair to say that you all have made statements about things like \uc2 NATO\uc2 , international organizations, America's role on the global stage that are different. I mean, would you as vice president advocate for policies that may not line up with your commander in chief? Do you think you all are too different on important issues of foreign policy?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: No, I think President Trump and I have long been aligned on foreign policy. We both subscribe to what you might say was \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 's vision, which is peace through strength. For four years with President Trump in the White House, we had peace and stability around the globe. We didn't have wars breaking out in Europe and the Middle East and being threatened in Asia.\par \par \uc2 We believe that you sometimes have to use military force in discriminant fashion the way President Trump did when he authorized a strike against Iran's terrorist mastermind \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2 . We believe that we need to pressure allies to take more responsibility for funding our common defense or taking responsibility for what's happening in their own region, in part because America needs to take the lead against China in the Western Pacific to preserve peace and stability there.\par \par \uc2 So I believe President Trump and I are aligned on foreign policy, just like \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was, which is what a peace through strength strategy, which is where the vast majority of not just Republicans, but normal Americans think. Only these pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2 , anti-American lunatics in the Democratic Party think that America is to blame for what's happening in the world or that we should retreat from strength and confidence in the world.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we hope to hear more from President Trump on the -- maybe debate stage on the campaign trail about his plans for Ukraine. In the meantime, Senator, thank you for your time. Keep us updated on that paperwork.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, border chaos. Thousands of migrants still crossing into the U.S. daily despite the President's executive actions.\par \par \uc2 Fox News will take you live to the border and then New York Congressman Democrat \uc2 Ritchie Torres\uc2  is here, joins us live in studio to react, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: We haven't shut down asylum. What we've done is further strengthen the border through executive action, which can be challenged and will be challenged in the courts, which is why we need Congress to act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas Friday after President Biden issued executive actions, the White House says are aimed at controlling the flow of migrants illegally crossing the border.\par \par \uc2 But how is the reality playing out on the ground? Well, Matt Finn is live at the border in California with a look at the real-world impact.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon. Border Patrol agents here tell us that this area just outside of San Diego for the past three years has been a hot spot because human smugglers know that there are gaping holes in our border wall here and they can easily push illegal migrants through.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN: Just outside of San Diego, a Fox camera capturing yet another group of 40 to 50 migrants illegally crossing in Jacumba coming from all over the world, including China, India, Vietnam and Mauritania.\par \par \uc2 One cuffed migrant posed for cameras as he was loaded onto a bus. This week, facing mounting political pressure and concerning poll numbers, President Biden announced an executive order that suspends and limits the entry of non-citizens, which will remain in effect until the daily average of migrant encounters drops below 1,500 for seven consecutive days.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: I'm moving past Republican obstruction and using the executive authorities available to me as president to do what I can on my own to address the border. Migrants will be restricted from receiving asylum at our southern border unless they seek it after entering through an established lawful process.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Biden claims that migrants who now cross illegally will be ineligible for asylum unless they have exceptionally compelling circumstances.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I will terminate every single open borders policy of the Biden administration as soon as I take the oath of office.\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, TEXAS DPS SPOKESMAN: There has not been any immediate change as far as any significance of a decrease in legal border crossings since the announcement.\par \par \uc2 FINN: From China?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): This week, Fox Crews spoke with migrants from across the globe arriving to the major American San Diego metropolitan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (on camera): From Egypt.\par \par \uc2 FINN: From Egypt?\par \par \uc2 MEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FINN: All from Egypt?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yeah, group.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From Egypt.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Egypt.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: North America.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Egypt.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Jordan?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Jordan? Why did you come to America? Egypt, why did you come to America?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For a job.\par \par \uc2 FINN: For a job?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 FINN: You know it's illegal to cross the border like this, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Are you OK?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I'm OK.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FINN: And Shannon, it looks like President Biden's executive order on immigration could be indefinite. The threshold for it to end is when that daily average hits about 1,500. The latest numbers obtained by Fox News show we're sitting at about 3,900 right now.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Matt Finn, live at the border. Thank you very much, Matt.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now in studio, New York Congressman Democrat \uc2 Ritchie Torres\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 REP. RITCHIE TORRES (D-NY): It's a pleasure to be here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: In person.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, it's very nice to see you. Let's talk about New York City because there's been an influx of some estimates 175,000 migrants in a relatively short period of time. New \uc2 York City Council\uc2  Member Vickie Paladino says this. We're spending more taxpayer money to care for foreign nationals than we are on the annual budgets of \uc2 NYPD\uc2 , FDNY, and the Department of Sanitation combined.\par \par \uc2 I know your reaction this week to some of the executive action by the president was wary. You don't want to be indistinguishable, you said, from Republicans. You worried about excluding or erasing certain communities of color.\par \par \uc2 So what do you say to the people of color here, who are your constituents or people who live in this town who were worried about the system being overwhelmed, public safety, education? Mayor Adams says it could destroy the city.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: The concerns are fair. I mean, the migrant crisis has put an enormous strain on the social safety net and shelter system of New York City.\par \par \uc2 Look, we in the United States have a dysfunctional asylum system. Anyone, anywhere can cross the border, claim asylum, enter the country, and Border Patrol has no emergency authority to limit crossings in the event of a surge. And so that's the gap that the President's executive order is filling.\par \par \uc2 But in the end, an executive order is no substitute for an act of Congress. Only Congress can fix what is broken in our asylum system.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you know, both sides obviously point the picture, point the finger at each other. House Republicans will say, we passed H.R. 2. It is very comprehensive. The White House and the President will say, well, what about that deal in the Senate that you guys had together? It seems that it doesn't matter who's in charge. This problem is intractable. It doesn't get solved.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: No, there's a key. There's only one party that has a bicameral, bipartisan compromise on border security, and it's the Democratic Party. We, the Democrats, negotiated a border security compromise with the Republicans. There were a number of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A small handful.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Most notably \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Senate minority leader.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Who then didn't vote for it?\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Who was for the bill before he was against it? And then he was pressured by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to oppose the legislation. But those Republicans who are actively obstructing and opposing the border security compromise are -- have no interest in actually solving the problem.\par \par \uc2 Their interest is in playing politics and demagoguing the issue against President Biden. You know, there's a difference between governing and grandstanding. And governing requires compromise. And if you refuse to compromise, if you let the perfect be the enemy of the good, you're not part of the solution. You're part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, as you know, Republicans had a number of problems with that bigger package. Many of them saying that they felt like they had no say in it. It was negotiated to a point where they couldn't go along with what was in it.\par \par \uc2 But when we look at this issue of immigration, it's not good for the White House or the president. We've got new polling out this week in a number of key states, Arizona, Florida, Nevada, and Virginia. And when people were asked, who do you handle -- who do you trust to handle immigration more? Double digits. You can see there on our screen. They think President Trump has a much better handle on this.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Look, I would encourage you to look at New York 3. So Long Island was ground zero for the red wave in New York. And \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2  was able to win back New York 3 by an even larger margin than we lost it in 2022. And he was largely campaigning on border security.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2  has shown that Democrats can proactively own the issue of border security. And so I don't think we should recede it to the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Will it be enough in time? Because when you look at, you know, a 20- point deficit for President Biden in these really critical states, that's got to be a warning sign to the White House. And does it then make you question, as some do, that this executive action is just a political ploy in an election year?\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Look, the president is acting because of congressional inaction, right? Congress needs to do its job. And, you know, keep in mind that the political establishment has a history of underestimating President Biden. I mean, he has proven the conventional wisdom wrong. And I suspect he's going to outperform the polls and outperform the predictions of the political pundits and the prognosticators.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We know they always get a lot tighter. As people actually now have to make a decision, there are third party candidates that poll with some significant numbers. But once people get to the ballot box or early vote, they've got to make a decision. And these often tighten up.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about all of these recent reports that are questioning the President's ability to run, to be effective for another four years. You've seen the polls. It's not just Republicans. It's Democrats and Independents that have questions about this.\par \par \uc2 "The Atlantic" had a really tough piece out yesterday referring to the President as sort of another \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , that she doesn't know when it's time to leave. And it costs the country that seat. It costs Democrats that seat.\par \par \uc2 They say this about him. It says, "He remains a comprehensively weak incumbent weighed down by the same liabilities that burden him from the start, beginning with the largest and completely unfixable one: At 81, he is much too old to run for president."\par \par \uc2 You said last year, 80 is not ideal for the age of a candidate running. But here we are. What do you make of these reports? And do you think the President is up to another four years?\par \par \uc2 TORRES: I have full confidence in the President. It is true that the president is old, but so is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It's a geriatric presidential race.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Why do you think they poll differently? Because people seem less concerned about President Trump's age.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: I think there's a narrative that has set in on President Biden. But if you actually look at his record, it speaks for itself. He's had one of the most productively bipartisan presidencies in recent memory.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he brought Democrats and Republicans together to provide health care to veterans exposed to toxic substances, brought Democrats and Republicans together to make some of the largest investments in infrastructure in more than half a century, brought Democrats and Republicans together to address the national security risk around TikTok. So I think when you judge him based on substance rather than narrative, I think he deserves re-election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let me ask you about that big rally that President Trump had a couple of weeks ago in your district. It's a very diverse district. A lot of folks showed up. We talked to some of them out there.\par \par \uc2 One gentleman, William River, says you are taking everything from blacks and browns everywhere. It's Hispanics or the other people who don't have a lot. You're taking it and giving it to the illegal aliens, which is totally wrong. You talked about why he was attending this.\par \par \uc2 We have many other similar statements from folks. Why do you think your constituency is moving? And poll numbers show us, too, that Hispanic and black voters are moving to President Trump in a way they didn't in 2020.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Well, first, the Bronx is going to overwhelmingly vote for President Biden, despite a few anecdotes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We would be shocked if it were otherwise.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: But you know, the Latino vote has never been a monolith. I mean, in 2000, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  won 40% of the Latino vote. You know, Puerto Ricans and Dominicans in the South Bronx are different from Cubans and Venezuelans in Florida, who are different from Mexicans in California, who are different from Mexicans in South Texas.\par \par \uc2 So the Latino vote has been a bipartisan vote for a long time. And despite what the polls say, we should be campaigning as if we're 10 or 20 points behind. We should never take any constituency for granted. We should campaign for every single vote.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's always good advice for any campaign. Despite any polls, be fighting for every single vote. Congressman, thank you for making the time to come in.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right. Up next, former President Trump makes a massive fundraising haul in a space once dominated by Democrats less than three weeks ahead of the first presidential debate.\par \par \uc2 Our Sunday panel is here to break down new polls, showing significant movement with key voting blocks that could make the difference for either candidate come November.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former president Trump is set to hold a rally in battleground Nevada today, after raking in big fundraising dollars in California over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Fox News senior correspondent Alicia Acuna is live on the ground in Las Vegas with the very latest. Hello, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Former president Trump arrives here in the Silver State after striking gold with big dollar donors in California.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Silicon Valley is not a place Republicans typically tap for campaign cash. But former president Trump got a boost after tech investors, \uc2 David Sacks\uc2  and \uc2 Chamath Palihapitiya\uc2 , held a packed private event in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SACKS\uc2 , CO-FOUNDER, CRAFT VENTURES: A various sort of a liberal bastion. And so we thought that five million might be a big lift. But as it turns out, we 