h.\par \par \uc2 FORD, JR.: But wages are going up also. It's 14. So whatever the number, it's just been 14.9 million jobs created.\par \par \uc2 Look, we can quarrel about it all day. But if we lost 14.9 million jobs, guess what --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We featured that --\par \par \uc2 FORD, JR.: -- the tweets would be saying, Biden is the reason and should be blamed for it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So to the -- to the economic issue, to this point in Virginia, where there is a tie, head-to-head, and President Biden won by 10 points, more than 10 points there. Last time around, they're tied.\par \par \uc2 And one of the questions we asked there, what about your family's financial situation? We'll put this up. I'm holding steady 43 percent, 40 percent of people say falling behind.\par \par \uc2 Lee, you get to the ballot box in October as Harold said. People are going to vote based on their own economics, their own pocketbook.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: You know, down -- and the U.C. household debt right now, being very high for a lot of families, for families who want to get their first home, interest rates are higher for some people there.\par \par \uc2 They're in a home. They are concerned, prioritizing upward economic mobility, but it's harder to be able to afford that larger house.\par \par \uc2 The economy, as an issue to decide your vote in November, is a bigger issue for people who are not benefiting from this economy right now.\par \par \uc2 I think that the point, whether it's talking about Virginia, Arizona, Florida, Nevada, for voters, a lot of them are talking about the border as a top priority. They're talking about the economy as a top priority. And they're not talking as much about some of these other issues.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, to the conversation about abortion being on the ballot, they also have a back to blue measure in Arizona. They also have a state-wide amendment to combat child sex trafficking.\par \par \uc2 And I think at the end of the 