ing for answers from Garland on the former President Trump's felony conviction last week.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Texas Congressman Chip Roy, member of the House Judiciary Committee. Congressman Roy, they've given us the transcript of the Biden interview, so why do you all need the tape?\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY(R-TX): Well, first of all, we want to be able to see the demeanor of the president when that was, in fact, the primary rationale for special counsel Hur in saying that he wasn't going to pursue charges against the president.\par \par \uc2 When I was engaging with the attorney general, I asked him point blank, I said, look, I said, did you all claim privilege when it came to the actual transcript? He, of course, said no, they didn't.\par \par \uc2 But he had already just testified, literally moments before, that the transcript is the same as the audio, that the only distinction really goes to the heart of the demeanor of the president.\par \par \uc2 But when the demeanor of the president is at the core of why the special counsel said, hey, I'm not going to pursue the charges, then it leaves you with only one ability to, you know, take something away from it, was that they're going after President Trump because they're saying he is mentally fit to be able to stand trial, but that President Biden is not mentally fit to stand trial.\par \par \uc2 We think we ought to be able to see the audio. And if, look, we think the demeanor is what he said it is, fine. If there's no issue, fine. But we ought to be able to see it. It's an impeachment inquiry. We have the right to see it as members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Garland was asked about potential coordination between the DOJ and the D.A.'s office. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Will the Department of Justice provide to the committee all documents, all correspondence between the department and \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's office and \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 ' office and Letitia James' office?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The offices you're referring to are independent offices of state government.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I get that. I get that. Will you provide those communications?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: If we make a request, we'll refer it to our Office of Legislative Affairs.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: But see, here's the thing. You come in here and you lodge this attack that it's a conspiracy theory that there is coordinated lawfare against Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, your reaction.\par \par \uc2 ROY: Yeah, Matt did a great job there in engaging with the Attorney General, because it's very clear what's going on here, right? I mean, you had the deputy to the third-ranking member of the Attorney General at the DOJ's office goes and works with the district attorney in New York to then target former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And then you see now this kind of level of coordination, but the Attorney General doesn't want to respond to that. He just simply wants to say, oh no, those are state offices. I can't comment on that. Yes, you can.\par \par \uc2 Matt was exactly right. If you're in communication with those offices, you can answer those. If you're going to claim that it's conspiracy theory, then just dismiss all of that by saying, look, here are all our communications. We weren't actually coordinating with those offices. Bull.\par \par \uc2 Everybody who can watch this sees that it's a politicized attack on the former president. And look, I haven't always agreed with the former president. You haven't always agreed with the former president. The former president is being targeted and attacked by this Department of Justice, but also leftists throughout the country, and especially in Bragg's office, and they're doing it for political reasons. It's undermining the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 I just gave a speech on the floor of the House on this very topic. The rule of law is what sets our country apart.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. They don't care about that. They don't care about the rule of law. They don't care about norms, the Constitution, the Declaration. They think it's all racist and it all has to be replaced. And the indignant responses today were not at all surprising. Congressman, great to have you on. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, should you return your shopping carts? And why are millennials turning to the stars for advice? Jimmy Failla in all that and more, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, now it's time for WTF, What the Failla. Joining me now, Jimmy Failla, host of Fox News Saturday night. All right, Jimmy, one Oklahoma police officer, I think he's like doing his best impression of the Kool-Aid man chasing his car thief, escaping on foot. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Don't move.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FAILLA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Wow. Listen, I mean, really, really cool. No, it is dangerous. As a New Yorker, I'm just jealous that their cops are allowed to arrest people, but a shout out to him for the workout.\par \par \uc2 And this is a reminder, like we can joke about this, but it's a reminder just how important the cops are. Don't ever forget, Laura, Democrats wanted to defund the police, except they didn't do it because Biden thought the police were a British rock band. So, shout out to the cops.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Sting keeps getting his royalties. All right, Jimmy, one mom right now is dividing the internet over something she will not do. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I'm not getting my groceries into my car, getting my children into the car, and then leaving them in the car to go return the cart.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: My gosh.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jimmy.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Do you return your carts?\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yeah, of course you do. OK, fellas, you need to know this. Returning the cart is the door test. Do you remember back in the day when we didn't have power locks? If you unlocked somebody's door and they didn't reach over to unlock yours, while you were walking around the car. Yeah, this is an inconsiderate person.\par \par \uc2 But I don't know what's more annoying, that she doesn't return the cart or that she feels the need to issue a proclamation, like she's a revolutionary. Like, I don't return my carts. I'm like a rebel. Oh, whatever.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's L.A. Some junkie's probably going to steal it anyway, but she's a dope.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I mean, first of all, the worst is when you can't get a parking space because everybody's carts are in the parking space.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: And then a stray cart hits your car. You know, screw people like that. These are the worst people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, that is a total litmus test. All right, a recent poll, Jimmy, shows that millennials are really, really turning to astrology. That's their religion. Eighty-one percent are using it for relationship guidance, 79 percent for self-care, love that phrase, and health, 65 percent for life choices, 65 percent for career advice. Jimmy, what are the stars telling you tonight? 4\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Well, it does differ by gender. Let me give you this. Forty percent of women want to date a guy who's the same side as them, while 90 percent of men want to date a girl who's easy, OK? You can throw all the sign stuff out the window. Nobody cares.\par \par \uc2 Moral of the story, there's a lot of people who need direction in life. You know, a lot of broken people. But I'd rather them go to a side kick than go to crazies like the woman on TikTok. So, I guess I got to co-sign this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: By the way, when you go to -- if today is your birthday, you can always find something that's accurate. How do they write those? There's always something. Have a humorous side, like long walks on the beach. There's always something. You think puppies are cute. I mean, they really do a pretty good job of that.\par \par \uc2 All right, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  executive editor stepped down this weekend. I know you were lighting candles for this, following a $77 million loss last year. But the Post writers were upset over the lack of diversity they see at the top.\par \par \uc2 They didn't care about the financial crisis when they were lectured. They said, well, how many people did you interview for DEI people for the top job?\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And the guy's like, are you listening to me? They're fanatics.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: No, nobody. Now there's a part of me that doesn't want the Post to go out, because millions of cat owners will lose something to line their litter box with. So, I am kind of rooting for the cat owners in this equation.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No one's reading you. No one's reading you. But who did you interview for the top job? Did you get the -- oh my gosh. Jimmy, great to see you, as always.\par \par \uc2 Don't forget, catch Jimmy on the road. He's going to be in Dallas this Friday. And Jesse is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240605ek6400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump Vows To Appeal Hush Money Conviction; Biden Unveils Ceasefire Plan; Interview With Speaker Of The House Mike Johnson (R-LA); Voters Views On Inflation Hurting Biden; Senator Daines On Why 2024 Is Different; Political Fallout After Trump New York Trial Conviction; Republicans Follow Trump Lead and Attack Justice System After Verdict; Speaker Johnson Urges Supreme Court to Step in on Trump Appeal; Trump's Conviction Impact on 2024 Unclear; Trump Yet to Support a National Abortion Ban; Voters Views on Inflation Hurting Biden; Chief Justice Roberts Declines a Meeting Request from Capitol Hill Democrats; Charles Djou is Interviewed about the Anniversary of D-day and the ABMC\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Jonathan Turley, Trey Yingst, Aishah Hasnie, Juan  Williams, Marc Thiessen \par \uc2 9,997 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 June 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump vows to appeal his historic guilty conviction this week in New York, calling the fight far from over.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT & 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is a case that should not have been brought. We're going to be appealing this scam.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's irresponsible for anyone to say this was rigged just because they don't like the verdict.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): So how is the verdict playing with voters?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it's a complete failure of our justice system.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it shows that our system of justice works.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And what's next for the first former U.S. president convicted of a crime?\par \par \uc2 We'll ask our legal panel about Trump's prospects on appeal and the possibility of jail time, and get reaction from Biden surrogate, Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 . And two Trump allies, House Speaker Mike Johnson and Senator Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what will the verdict mean for down ballot races come November?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS REPORTER: Are you going to tell your candidates to talk about this?\par \par \uc2 SEN. STEVE DAINES (R-MT): Well, look, they need to be prepared, and they all agree that this has been a sham trial.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Aishah Hasnie has an exclusive from Montana with Senator Steve Daines, head of the Republican group trying to flip control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And our Sunday panel on why Chief Justice Roberts declined an invitation to meet with Democrats over the Alito flag controversy.\par \par \uc2 Then, world leaders prepare to mark 80 years since Allied troops stormed the beaches of Normandy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: D-Day is arguably the single most important day in the last century, not just for the United States but for Western civilization.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All this week on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 President Trump raising tens of millions of dollars in new donations and planning to appeal his criminal conviction, as his legal team juggles several other state and federal cases, none of them likely to be resolved before the November election.\par \par \uc2 He's speaking out this weekend in an exclusive interview with "FOX & Friends" on the FOX News Channel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I did absolutely nothing wrong. I mean, absolutely -- think of it, you know I hate when they say a bookkeeping this and that, it's not. It's called, think of it, expense. I use the word expense, legal expense.\par \par \uc2 I pay a lawyer. He wasn't a fixer. A bookkeeper without ever speaking to me because she did the right thing who's been with me for years marks it down as legal expense. In other words, I paid a legal expense. It's marked down as a legal expense. And they say that's a fraud.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In a moment, House Speaker Mike Johnson joins us with reaction to the verdict and to news that the president is expected to announce executive actions on the border by Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 But first, what what's next for President Trump and his legal team as \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's federal gun trial is also set to kick off on Monday?\par \par \uc2 Let's break it down with \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law professor Jonathan Turley, also a FOX News contributor, and former Principal Deputy Assistant Attorney General Tom Dupree.\par \par \uc2 Fancy meeting you here. We are back together to talk about what's happened and what is still to come.\par \par \uc2 Okay, let's start with this -- sentencing comes on July 11th. "Reuters" asked the question, could Trump go to prison? In deciding the sentence, Merchan, the judge, may weigh the underlying seriousness of the charges due to their ties to the 2016 election, as well as Trump's decision to go to trial rather than accept responsibility by pleading guilty.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, you and I spent a lot of time in this in this courthouse watching this whole thing play out. Would anything surprise you from Judge Merchan?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No, after watching him in court, quite frankly, I would have said that the very concept of jail for something like this would be otherworldly and ridiculous. After watching him in the courtroom, I must say I have some doubts in my mind. But it would go against the overwhelming weight of past cases. You've got a nonviolent offense by a first offender, elderly defendant, I would be very surprised.\par \par \uc2 But there is a menu of other options from community service to home confinement. In my view, a conditional dismissal is what most judges would be thinking of which is basically a go out and send no more type of warning. You're still under supervision. So if you commit a new offense, then they get really tough on you.\par \par \uc2 I -- the -- if he does go with something that is like community service or confining him to Trump Tower, I think Trump probably could get a suspension pending appeal from a higher court and that may weigh into the calculation for Judge Merchan.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's talk about appeal because, obviously, that's coming. "\uc2 Telegraph\uc2 " outlines what it says are probably some of the best bright spots for the Trump legal team on that front. Stormy Daniel's testimony, the statute itself, the vagueness of the underlying crime, the fact that defense expert witness Brad Smith, that former \uc2 FEC\uc2  commissioner, wasn't allowed or at least severely limited in what he would be able to say, and the issue of presidential immunity which we obviously are all waiting on that from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 What do you think is the best option on appeal, Tom?\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER PRINCIPAL DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: I think they have a number of options at their disposal. I think maybe the best one is just really challenging this notion that they took. The D.A. took this kind of collection of business fraud, misdemeanors, kind of did some legal alchemy put it in a pot, add in some election fraud and presto, they've got 34 felonies.\par \par \uc2 The jury was instructed along those lines. The jury clearly had some difficulty grappling with the instructions. They actually asked the judge to read those instructions back to them.\par \par \uc2 And so, I suspect the Trump legal team is going to come out pretty hard on the idea that there was an underlying predicate crime that was sufficient to convert this from a misdemeanor to a felony. I think they've also got arguments on the exclusion of the bulk of their experts' testimony. I think that the Trump legal team will say look the jury needed to be able to understand the ins and outs of election law in order to decide this case and the judge prevented us from putting on the testimony the jury needed to hear.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, everyone, it's hindsight 20/20, how the prosecution did, what the defense did. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says this under the headline "How Trump's team blew it". This is a guest essay, this is an opinion piece. But they write this: Instead of telling a simple story, Mr. Trump's defense was a haphazard cacophony of denials and personal attacks. It failed to focus the jury on the weaknesses in the prosecution's case and instead tried to fight everything and everyone even when it gained little by doing so.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, your response to that?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, there's always Monday morning quarterbacking. I've talked to some members of the defense team. They feel that they did the best that they could and quite frankly, you could have been Clarence Darrow and lost this case because, you know, I was hoping for a hung jury. I viewed this is either hung jury or conviction case.\par \par \uc2 And I said we had to wait for the instructions. Once the instructions came out, I said, it looks like an almost guarantee for conviction. There was not a lot of room there.\par \par \uc2 And this is why I tell people, you shouldn't blame this jury necessarily. Those instructions were real really a poison pill instruction set. I mean they left very little room for the jury. The jury was told over and over again and the judge said nothing as the prosecutor said there were election violations in this case and they were ordered by Trump, and the defense objected to those and that Judge Merchan just overruled every one of those.\par \par \uc2 So if for the -- what they heard in that courtroom, it's not too surprising in terms of a conviction. But it did create this target rich environment for appeal. But that appeal is likely to play out after the election.\par \par \uc2 But for most Americans, we obviously are divided as we are politically. But this is something that we're not used to. For many of us, this was a political prosecution and in my view, we haven't had this type of politicalization of the criminal justice system since John Adams, and it's -- it's really been an awakening for many people looking at this and saying maybe this is where our rage is taking us and we have to look again at what we becoming if this will be the new norm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah and there are calls we're going to talk about throughout the show today for Republicans to respond in kind, to do what they accuse Democrats of doing here, which is using the legal system for a political purpose. We'll discuss u the calls for that and where they go if that's their best strategy.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile I want to make sure we hit on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's case because that gun case, unless there is a last minute deal, goes to trial starting tomorrow with jury selection. In one of the filings, the DOJ talks about what they would to include as evidence. They say this: The defendant's laptop is real. It will be introduced as a trial exhibit and it contains significant evidence of the defendant's guilt. Moreover, the evidence on his laptop is corroborated by independent sources including witnesses. It is also corroborated by the defendant's own admissions in his book.\par \par \uc2 This is both a legal and political headache now for the -- for the Biden family.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Oh, for sure, Shannon, and this trial is going to be fascinating to watch play out. The Justice Department is saying that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  basically lied on these forms that he filled out when he went to go buy a firearm, and what they are going to use to prove their case is his own testimony in his own book where he talked about how he was struggling with addiction. They're going to be calling people very close to him, his friends, his family who are also going to testify that he was addicted to controlled substances at the time he filled out these forms and the time he possessed the firearm.\par \par \uc2 I've seen the defense's arguments I don't find them very persuasive. I think they're going to argue that even though he was addicted, maybe he didn't know he was addicted, or maybe he was addicted on Monday, but his addiction had ended by Tuesday when he bought the gun. I don't find them persuasive but it is going to be fascinating seeing the Justice Department move forward with this prosecution next week.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well and so much of what we've heard this week or this year is that the campaign would play out in courtrooms and not necessarily on the campaign trail. So we will watch this one and pick your brains again very soon on this.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan and Tom, thank you. Appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 Okay. We turn now to the Middle East because there is news from overseas amid cautious hopes that a new Israeli proposed ceasefire plane could finally get the hostages still held in Gaza home to their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 FOX News foreign correspondent Trey Yingst is on the ground in Tel Aviv with the very latest on negotiations as that clock is ticking -- Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Shannon. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 We are closely monitoring developments out of the Middle East amid reports of a possible ceasefire between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Friday, President Biden laid out a plan for a proposal that if accepted would be implemented in three stages. The first stage would see a six-week pause in the fighting with Israeli troops withdrawing from Gaza's major cities along with an exchange of female and wounded Israeli hostages for Palestinian prisoners. The second stage would lead to a full negotiated ceasefire and the release of all remaining hostages, and then finally, a third stage would involve a major reconstruction effort for Gaza and the release of dead Israelis that are being held by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Qatari negotiators relayed all of these details of the plan to \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and in a statement to FOX News, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  said, quote: The Islamic resistance movement positively views what was included in the speech of U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The president urged Israeli hardliners to accept this agreement and bring an end to the war.\par \par \uc2 There is growing anticipation about the fate of this proposal, understanding that it is a last ditch effort amid failed diplomatic efforts.\par \par \uc2 Back to you -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Trey Yingst in Israel, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to speak about this and much more, Speaker of the House Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Speaker, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Hey, Shannon. Great to be with you as always.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay so let's start there in the Middle East because you are trying to get something to the floor that deals with the \uc2 ICC\uc2 . There are a number of international bodies that are condemning Israel or the prime minister in their case asking for arrest warrants.\par \par \uc2 It seemed like the White House was interested in doing something that would push back on the \uc2 ICC\uc2 . Now it seems that they have changed course, that's not going to be the case.\par \par \uc2 House Foreign Affairs Committee Chair Michael McCaul says this: We want something that can become law. We're not really interested in a messaging bill.\par \par \uc2 And it sounds like there is bipartisan agreement doing something to push back on the \uc2 ICC\uc2 , but with the signal we're getting from the White House now what could you pass that you think the president might sign?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, we hope to actually determine that by this afternoon these negotiations have been ongoing with our Democrat colleagues and we are working very hard as Chairman McCaul indicated there, we -- we don't want this to just be a political exercise. We think it's important for Republicans and Democrats to stand together and send a message to the international community that the abuses of the \uc2 International Criminal Court\uc2 , the \uc2 ICC\uc2 , cannot be allowed to go forward.\par \par \uc2 And the idea that President Biden would backtrack on that, that would -- he would disagree with us that we should impose sanctions on \uc2 ICC\uc2  officials when they are threatening to arrest the prime minister of Israel and the defense minister of Israel in the midst of their war for survival is to us unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 So, look, I hope that the White House sees the light on this. We've got to do this and we need to do it next week.\par \par \uc2 And my intention is to bring that legislation to the floor. If we have to do it Republicans only, we will, but I certainly hope the Democrats will come along with us on it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and you did get Democrats to come along now on this invitation to Prime Minister Netanyahu to come speak and address Congress. You did get \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  to join when it seemed like they may not at one point.\par \par \uc2 But not everybody's excited, including Democrat or independent Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . He says: It's a very sad day for our country that Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  has been invited by leaders from both parties to address a joint meeting of the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 . Netanyahu, he calls him, a war criminal, says he's not going to attend.\par \par \uc2 He does in a fuller statement say Israel's got a right to defend itself but not to kill tens of thousands of civilians, destroy universities, hospitals, homes.\par \par \uc2 How do you respond to his characterization?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  is parroting the talking points of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the ayatollah in Iran. If that's the side he wants to choose, so be it.\par \par \uc2 The -- our Democrat colleagues have to make a choice, are they going to stand with our most important ally in the Middle East, at this most desperate time as has traditionally been the case in Washington. We've had bipartisan agreement that we have to stand in solidarity with Israel.\par \par \uc2 Or are they going to take this new side and stand with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and the ayatollah? \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  has chosen a side, I hope that our other colleagues in the Democrat Party will stand up and do the right thing. We desperately need it. Israel desperately needs it.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to Prime Minister Netanyahu as recently as Wednesday about all of this and he's deeply concerned you know we did issue that invitation for the joint address to Congress. I'm really grateful it finally went out I would note that I sent the draft of the letter the joint letter back in mid-March to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 . It took him quite some time to agree.\par \par \uc2 But we have finally gotten that done. Prime Minister Netanyahu is anxious to come and address us, and I hope that we can get that scheduled here in the -- in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to get to the Trump verdict and talk through that. But quickly, I want to ask you about the news this morning. We've confirmed that the White House saying the president is going to take executive action on the border.\par \par \uc2 You've been pushing him on to do that for a long time. We don't know what it is yet, but do you give him credit for at least taking that step?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I don't, Shannon, it's too little too late. Now, he's trying to desperately show the American people somehow that he wants to address the issue that he himself created.\par \par \uc2 We documented 64 specific executive actions that President Biden and Secretary Mayorkas at DHS took over the -- over the course of the last three and a half years, beginning on the first day that President Biden took office, to open the border wide. They did it intentionally. And it has had catastrophic effects upon our country that we'll be living with for decades to come.\par \par \uc2 And now he wants to issue some sort of executive order, I guess, to show that, oh, no, he really does care about the issue. The only reason he's doing that is because the polls say that it's the biggest issue in America. Every state's a border state. I've traveled to over 29 states, done events in 117 cities, as of yesterday, over the last six months. There is an energy out there.\par \par \uc2 People are motivated. And the first question that comes up in almost every public forum is, what about that open border and why in the world would President Biden allow it? He did more than allow it. He engineered it. And everybody knows that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we'll see what he spins out, we think, as early as Tuesday of this week.\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to ask you about the verdict. Clearly, you think it's a travesty, as many people do. And I think having watched the trial, there are many grounds, fertile territory, for the Trump team to use on appeal. But that's going to take time. In the meantime, there are a number of Republicans out there saying, you guys need to turn around and do this same thing.\par \par \uc2 John Yoo writing this, "Republicans have to bring charges against Democratic officers, even presidents. Only retaliation in kind can produce the deterrence necessary to enforce a political version of mutual assured destruction."\par \par \uc2 The other side, Peggy Noonan, writing about how high the temperature is, says this. "We have to ease up. We have to stop enjoying our hate so much. We can't go on indefinitely like this."\par \par \uc2 Are you worried that Republicans will be perceived as doing the very thing you're accusing Democrats of, of using the legal system for political purposes, if you follow, John Yoo's advice?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Here's what I believe, Shannon. Listen, we are the rule of law party. Chaos is not a conservative value. And we have to fight back, and we will, with everything in our arsenal. But we do that within the confines of the rule of law. We believe in our institutions.\par \par \uc2 We are conservatives, and we are trying to conserve the greatest country in the history of the world and its institutions are an important part of that. Our system of justice is an important part of that. So what we'll do with our tools that we have in Congress, in the House, is we'll use our oversight responsibility.\par \par \uc2 We've already done that. You saw our Judiciary Chairman, Jim Jordan, issue requests for \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , the DA in Manhattan, and for the lead prosecutor, Colangelo, to show up for a hearing on June 13th in our select committee on the weaponization of the federal government, because that's what they've done. And the purpose of the hearing is to investigate what these prosecutors are doing at the state and federal level to use politics, you know, political retribution in the court system to go after political opponents, federal officials like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That's a really important thing for us to delve into.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to look at Special Counsel Jack Smith, who we believe is abusing his authority as well. We have the funding streams. We have mechanisms to try to get control of that. We'll be doing that within the confines of our jurisdiction. And I think that can have the desired effect and show the American people we're not going to tolerate this. Shannon, at the end of the day, people are losing their faith in our system of justice itself, and that's a serious threat.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we're almost out of time, but I want to quickly ask you about something that may need clarification. You said on Friday you think the Supreme Court should step in. You said, I think that the justices on the court, I know many of them personally, I think they're deeply concerned.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of your critics out there, somebody who covers the court with me, part of the Supreme Court press corps, Mark Joseph Stern, wrote this. He says, "Fascinating to hear the GOP Speaker of the House admit to having private conversations with multiple justices who expressed concern to him about Trump's conviction. I'm sure those justices will recuse from any eventual appeal to avoid sitting on a case they've prejudged."\par \par \uc2 I didn't take your comments to mean that specific conversation, but want to give you a chance to clarify.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No, thank you. Of course, I haven't had conversations with the justices. I just know their character, their personality. I know what they've said in the past. They are deeply concerned, as we are, about maintaining our system of justice. If you walk into the Supreme Court building, Shannon, you and I know, chiseled on the marble above the front door is equal justice under law. They are concerned about maintaining that idea. We all are, because it's a necessary ingredient to keep a constitutional republic. That's what I was referring to.\par \par \uc2 I think they're concerned about using courtrooms for politics and political retribution. We all are. We all should be. Every American. That's what I was referring to. And I think in their -- in their heart of hearts, they're concerned about it, just like we are.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll see if the Trump team takes a more expedited route trying to get to the justices and see how that plays out. Mr. Speaker, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thanks, Shannon. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, we'll bring in both a Biden supporter, Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , and Trump supporter Senator Tim Scott to talk polls, campaigns, and shifting political positions for both candidates. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The good news is that I think we've set a record beyond all records for fundraising. That's like a poll.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, President Trump, from an exclusive interview with Fox & Friends this weekend on how the guilty verdict against him in the New York hush money trial has led to a flood of new contributions into the tens of millions to his presidential election campaign. Somebody's campaigning with him.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, South Carolina Senator Tim Scott. Welcome back, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Thank you very much. Good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's talk about this. The Republicans' spin on this has been it's going to motivate your base, that angry voters are motivated voters and they are angry about this verdict. But Politico adds this, saying Trump simply cannot beat President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's relying solely on the votes of people who think his legal travails are a politically motivated scam.\par \par \uc2 "Trump's only path to victory is a coalition that includes many Republicans and independents who find him deplorable but think a second Biden term would be even more so."\par \par \uc2 So what is the plan from President Trump to reach out to people who may not love him at this point? Maybe some \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  voters, independents, others who are skeptics. Other than the grievances, what about campaigning on policy and reaching out to those folks directly?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Shannon, there's no doubt that this verdict has actually brought unifying our party. Without any question, what we've seen is never Trumpers calling me and saying, Tim, I'm on the bandwagon now. I've seen this two- tiered justice system working against the president of the United States. It could work against me, too. I've seen donors who've been on the sidelines this entire process now jumping in. One of the reasons why we saw more than $50 million raised in 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 But you're 100% right. The issues that are going to drive the results of this November 5th election will be, can \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  close the southern border? The answer is he already has closed the southern border.\par \par \uc2 Can he bring inflation down under 2%? The answer is it was 1.4% when he left office. Can he create jobs for the poorest Americans? Good news is he created more than 7 million jobs with the highest percentage going to women, African-Americans, Hispanics and Asians.\par \par \uc2 What we know about four years under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is we had low unemployment, low inflation, high enthusiasm, law and order in our streets. And we've had the exact opposite under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . We've had incredibly crushing inflation leading to high interest rates, crime in the poorest communities, devastating single mothers like the one that raised me.\par \par \uc2 And we've had the greatest invasion in American history across our southern border, left wide open, unsafe, because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on day one started unraveling border security put in place by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and we get word that he may be possibly reinstituting some of that this week given the reality of what's happening there. Now, you sound like somebody who would be campaigning with the president potentially on the ticket. The betting websites all have you in the number one slot potentially to be VP. You and \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  going back and forth this week.\par \par \uc2 But one of the issues where it seems like there may be a split with you and President Trump is this issue of abortion. Back when you were campaigning in Iowa, when you were running for the nomination, you name-checked him and others who you felt would not set a limit, some type of federal ban. President Trump has said now that he's not going to sign a federal ban. This is to be left to the states.\par \par \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  said this when that news came out. He said, "President Trump's retreat on the right to life is a slap in the face to the millions of pro- life Americans who voted for him. A majority of Americans long to see minimum national protections for the unborn in federal law. But today, too many Republican politicians are all too ready to wash their hands of the battle for life."\par \par \uc2 I know you are passionate about this, and we have talked about the fact that there are states that do not have a limit on abortion up to a due date. Are you willing to concede that position, or would you fight to push President Trump, if you're his vice president, to reconsider that federal ban that I know you care so much about?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, Shannon, you're 100% right that the Democrats -- Republicans are all pro-life, honestly. And the Democrats in the Senate have already voted to allow for abortions up until the day of birth. But it goes beyond that.\par \par \uc2 The former governor of Virginia, a pediatrician Democrat, said perhaps even infanticide is OK. I find that amoral. Without question, however, the Supreme Court has already ruled that this is a state's issue.\par \par \uc2 President Trump and Speaker Johnson have both said that this will remain a state's issue. It is consistent with the Supreme Court, and we must make sure that we allow the American people to see the absolute extreme position of insanity on the left and a state's rights issue on the right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But knowing that you feel that's so extreme, wouldn't you want to at least have a conversation? Do you think that President Trump, who respects you, would listen to you on this issue if you're 