revious four.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, Shannon, there's no doubt that inflation was lower before COVID, but COVID happened and inflation started to rise under President Trump and then continued to rise under President Biden.\par \par \uc2 There was bipartisan spending under both presidents. And much of a good that led to the vaccines that led to America, having the record lowest unemployment in the world that has led to America having the best recovery from inflation in the Western world.\par \par \uc2 And so the question now is, how do we continue to get inflation down? And what are we going to do on housing and childcare on taking on monopolies? The president has a clear vision.\par \par \uc2 And I guess what I would love to see for President Trump is, what is he going to do in the future? How is he going to get prices down?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, OK. I would say he will want to take credit for the vaccine getting done on his watch on Operation Warp Speed.\par \par \uc2 But let me turn to this. You've spent the weekend with a lot of big tech folks and some of them have been quietly, some of them are openly, talking about throwing support and money over to President Trump or to possible third-party spoiler, RFK, Jr.\par \par \uc2 "Puck" says this, "An election cycle ago, Silicon Valley Republicans had to go into witness protection to support Trump. Now an influential cohort of billionaires and quasi-billionaires are getting ready to write checks for 45."\par \par \uc2 Also "Axios" quoting tech investor, \uc2 David Sacks\uc2 , says, "I have -- I'd say at this point, bigger disagreements with Biden than with Trump."\par \par \uc2 He, by the way, is supposed to host a big dollar fundraiser in Silicon Valley this week for President Trump.\par \par \uc2 What is your message to him about whether he has a better deal with Biden or Trump when it comes to tech issues?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, look, Silicon Valley folks are independent. They wouldn't be entrepreneurs if they weren't contrarian and independent.\par \par \uc2 And our party needs to make a case that we're going to have a better vision for entrepreneurship, for investment and things like the CHIPS Act to bring semiconductors back for investments and advanced manufacturing, for wealth generation in a modern economy across the country. And that's what this convening was.\par \par \uc2 I still believe that 70, 80 -- to 80 percent of Silicon Valley entrepreneurs are for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But you're right. It's not 95 or 100 percent. And that's why we need to do work to show that we still are the party of entrepreneurship and innovation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I know that's kept you busy this weekend, Congressman. So thanks for making time for us. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, first, Senate Republican campaign chair, Senator Steve Daines, talks strategy at his Montana home with our Aishah Hasnie, as the GOP tries to win back control of the Senate in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Then, our Sunday panel, weighs in on the partisan clash over demands that Justice Alito recuse from key cases. And the Chief Justice tells Democrat senators thanks, but no thanks to a one party invitation.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Tuesday is Election Day in a handful of states with New Jersey, New Mexico, South Dakota, and Montana, all holding presidential and state primaries.\par \par \uc2 Republicans see Montana as one of their best opportunities to try to pick up a Senate seat this November and flip the balance of power here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Fox News congressional correspondent, Aishah Hasnie, sat down with the Senate GOP's campaign chair, Senator Steve Daines, at his Montana home to discuss the odds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Montana Senator, Steve Daines, always has his eye on the target.\par \par \uc2 That passion is why he is in charge of winning back the Senate for Republicans. And for the first time ever --\par \par \uc2 SEN. STEVE DAINES (R-MT): You got it.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The NRSC chairman exclusively invited us to his home --\par \par \uc2 DAINES: There's a mountain lion up there.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: -- to talk strategy.\par \par \uc2 Do you think you'll win this year?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, we -- we'll get to 51.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: As a GOP victory could run right through his backyard.\par \par \uc2 Montana is one of three toss-up states in a highly favorable map for Republicans. With West Virginia poised to turn red, Daines only needs to pick up one more state.\par \par \uc2 So 2022 was a bust for Senate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So what makes 2024 different?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Number one, we've got to find candidates. They can win that just primary elections, but general elections have a broader appeal.\par \par \uc2 Number two, we've got a change from moving from Election Day to election month.\par \par \uc2 We are putting way too much voter turnout on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Daines is also counting on former president Trump to turn out voters, despite the conviction in the New York hush money trial.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: We talk frequently.\par \par \uc2 Again, we work very, very closely together.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But Trump's legal drama is now spilling down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Maryland Senate candidate, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , told his followers on X to respect the verdict. That hit a nerve with a Trump campaign senior advisor who shot back, "You just ended your campaign."\par \par \uc2 DAINES: I still think \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  can flip Maryland, but \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 's going to run his campaign. He'll run his race. And president Trump will run his race.\par \par \uc2 I thought, you know, the trial was a complete sham.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Daines' toughest battle, however, may be the dramatic fundraising gap with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: It's building out that grassroots kind of digital fundraising effort that's going to be very important for us in '24.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And this year's prize, he says, is personal.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: So many Montanans are very, very worried about the future of their grandkids. I am.\par \par \uc2 The future of this country is going to be decided in '24.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And, Shannon, there's already talk about what's next for Steve Daines, if he does win the Senate.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has encouraged him to replace GOP leader, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . Daines tells me, though, he doesn't have aspirations for that. Not a flat out no, but he says he likes to spend as much time as possible here at home.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Aisha Hasnie in Montana. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday group to break that down. "The Hill" national political reporter, Julia Manchester. Fox News Senior Political Analyst, Juan Williams. Resident fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist and Fox News contributor, Marc Thiessen. And the Ronald Reagan Institute Director, Roger Zakheim.\par \par \uc2 Great to have all of you here with us. There's a lot to pick apart in that piece. But let's start about -- talking about the \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  situation because he tweeted out that people should respect the legal process and the verdict and immediately got slammed by a Trump campaign official who said, your campaign's over.\par \par \uc2 Roger, they know that if they want to try to pick up that seat, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 's going to have to be as independent and broadly appealing as he can be.\par \par \uc2 ROGER ZAKHEIM, DIRECTOR, THE \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2  INSTITUTE: It might have helped \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  in Maryland when that tweet came out. And I think \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  knows how to 