EO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, Jill Biden, Laura, as you pointed out, said this campaign is about a choice between good and evil and you're pushing abortion. That's your only argument.\par \par \uc2 Look, you know, the big problem they're having here, that is number nine, abortion, among the most important issues, according to this new ABC News poll. The abortion play may not work this time around.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, talk about a negative, cynical campaign. Abortion, January 6, I guess they throw in a little weed for good measure. That's about it. All right, Raymond, thank you. Great to see you. Yeah, why not?\par \par \uc2 All right. That's it for us tonight. Make sure to follow me on social media. I had a little trouble getting to Texas yesterday. You may have heard. Well, it was great being on with Judge Jeanine.\par \par \uc2 Remember, it is America now and forever. And Jesse takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240530ek5t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Cohen Claims He Never Wanted White House Role; House Panels Consider Holding Garland In Contempt; Fox Poll: Abortion A "Deal-Breaker" For 15 Percent Of Voters; Report: Harris Jokes About California Governor Run; Palestinians Plead For Medical Attention; Health And Health Services Department Bars Research Organization EcoHealth Alliance From Receiving Federal Grants; House Republicans Hold Hearing On Recent Increase Of Chinese Nationals Entering U.S. Illegally; Residents And Lawmakers In Missouri Push For Congressional Act To Compensate Those Exposed To Radiation Linked To Government Nuclear Weapons Programs; Republican Congressmembers Attend Former President Trump Criminal Trial; President Biden Criticized For Falsely Claiming Inflation At Nine Percent When He Entered Office\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Nate Foy, Peter Doocy, David Spunt, C.B. Cotton,  Chad Pergram, Rich Edson, Griff Jenkins, Bret Baier, Guy Benson \par \uc2 7,843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Completed most of her class work remotely and online, spent her teenage years not doing things we did, but earning associates, bachelors, and master's degrees. She is also the founder and CEO of a leadership institute supporting STEM and the arts.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, you go girl.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I love it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And you go girl. Congratulations to you.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: STEM!\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes!\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right. That's it for us, everyone. Have a great night. We'll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Shannon Bream, in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 House lawmakers tried to force President Biden to give specific military aid to Israel.\par \par \uc2 President Biden invokes executive privilege over the audio recordings of his interview with a special counsel. And a major development in the search for the origins of the coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 But first, "BREAKING TONIGHT", the defense tries to paint former Trump lawyer and self-proclaimed fixer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  as a serial liar, suicidal, and bent on revenge.\par \par \uc2 Cohen has been the key prosecution witness in its criminal case against former President Trump. His cross examination has been difficult. Correspondent Nate Foy is outside the New York State Supreme Court building. Hello, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 So, the importance of \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 's testimony is reflected by how long it's taking. He took the stand on Monday of this week. He's been here all week. He'll be back when court resumes at 9:30 Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 Of course, he has a documented history of lying. Cohen has admitted to lying under oath several times. And today, Trump's lawyer said he did it again.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Trump attorney, Todd Blanche, accused \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  of lying under oath again today. Cohen told the jury he informed Trump of the Stormy Daniels' deal during a phone call, through Trump's head of security, Keith Schiller.\par \par \uc2 Blanche challenged Cohen, saying, "That was a lie. You did not talk to President Trump." Cohen question that. Responding, "I'm not certain that's accurate."\par \par \uc2 Text between Cohen and Schiller show Cohen instead wanted to talk about harassing phone calls he received. Cohen now maintains he spoke to Schiller and Trump in the same phone call.\par \par \uc2 Trump can't publicly respond to Cohen because of the court's gag order. But he did say this while leaving court tonight.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I think it was a very interesting day, and it was a fascinating day. And it shows what a scam this whole thing is.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): In contrast to testimony from several witnesses, Cohen said today he never wanted to work in Trump's White House, asserting instead, he wanted to be considered for a role.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the trial, Daniels' former lawyer, \uc2 Keith Davidson\uc2 , told the court that Cohen appeared suicidal after not receiving a White House position.\par \par \uc2 Attorney \uc2 Bob Costello\uc2 , whom Cohen consulted with in 2018, told Fox today after testifying on Capitol Hill Wednesday, that Cohen completed the Daniels' deal by himself to gain Trump's favor, and ultimately become his White House chief of staff or attorney general.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTELLO\uc2 , FORMER COHEN LEGAL ADVISER: He said specifically, and I cross examined him on this. This was my idea. It was his idea to take care of the NDA because he had been contacted by a lawyer for Stormy Daniels, who said she was going to claim that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had sex with her.\par \par \uc2 He kept on saying I have nothing on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Cohen is the prosecution's final witness, as this trial nears its end.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This is finishing up. At some point, it will be finished.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Trump's lawyers say they expect to finish Cohen's cross examination by the morning break on Monday. After that, lawyers for former President Trump planned to call Brad Smith to the stand. He's an expert witness and expert on campaign finance law. Trump's lawyers also indicate they are considering calling more witnesses forward. They didn't mention \uc2 Bob Costello\uc2  by name, but Costello told Fox this morning, it's possible that he'll be called to testify. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Nate Foy in New York. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is asserting executive privilege over audio of his interview with the Special Counsel, concerning his handling of classified documents. Now, House Republicans say well, they want to hold the president's attorney general in contempt of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is at the Justice Department tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You guys are not doing oversight. You are doing overkill.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A tense hearing today on Capitol Hill is Republicans and Democrats fought over whether to hold Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  in contempt of Congress.\par \par \uc2 In a party line vote, the House Judiciary Committee voted 18 to 15 in favor of holding Garland in contempt after he refused to turn over audio files of the interview between President Biden and then-Special Counsel Robert Hur last October.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JEFF VAN DREW\uc2  (D-NJ): They are going to reveal whether Special Counsel Hur's assessment was correct, and our president mishandled classified documents, or they will reveal he's incompetent.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Hur investigated but declined to criminally charge Biden or anyone with a crime after classified documents were discovered at multiple properties connected to the president.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JERROLD NADLER\uc2  (D-NY): What high-profile witness would ever voluntarily cooperate with law enforcement investigation if they knew that audio files that they conversations would be released to committees who are seeking those files for purely political purposes.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Hours before the hearing, the president invoked executive privilege over the audio files, keeping them away from Congress, while legally protecting Garland. And today told Fox News, he is not afraid.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , UNITED STATES ATTORNEY GENERAL: We have gone to extraordinary lengths to ensure that the committees get responses to their legitimate requests. But this is not one.\par \par \uc2 To the contrary, this is one that would harm our ability in the future to successfully pursue sensitive investigations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The oversight committee was supposed to meet at 11:00 Eastern Time this morning. They push their meeting back to about two hours from now, 8:00 p.m. Eastern Time tonight, because several Republican members went to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's trial today in Manhattan, as far as the next step in the process. All of this, Shannon, would need to go to the full House for a vote.\par \par \uc2 It's all up to Speaker Mike Johnson, who when asked about this today, was non-committal and gave no timetable. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. And so, David, switching gears but sticking with the DOJ, what can you tell us about the attorney general and all of this talk we've heard about reclassifying marijuana?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And this was big news today from the justice department. We've been expecting this somewhat for the past couple of weeks. But today, officially, the justice department submitted a new rule to the federal register to reclassify and lower the danger of marijuana.\par \par \uc2 It was formerly in a classified level one, it's going to go to a level three, which would make it a little bit easier for research purposes and allow the federal government to officially recognize the medical benefits of marijuana.\par \par \uc2 We have a new Fox News poll, actually out to show you what the American people think about marijuana. Brand-new poll found that 69 percent favor legalizing the drug nationally, but the justice department and the White House, Shannon, are not there yet to talk about a national legalization of marijuana. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. The debate continues. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There were no campaign events scheduled for President Biden today. Tonight, we look at more of the Fox polling about which issues are deal breakers for various constituencies.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy joins us now. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And Shannon, we didn't lay eyes on President Biden today, aside from a video where he talked a little bit about what David Spunt just laid out that the government is closer to reclassifying marijuana.\par \par \uc2 Turns out though, marijuana not one of the big issues that is helping voters -- that are helping voters make up their minds -- but this is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It's causing chaos for women --\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Abortion, the deal breaker for 15 percent of registered voters in a brand-new Fox poll. More than any other issue, broken down by party, 24 percent of Democrats say abortion is a deal breaker compared to just nine percent of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The top deal breaker for a quarter of Republicans, border security.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The good news is the polls are through the roof.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Trump's trials could ultimately benefit him because of something President Biden didn't do.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY (R-UT): I would have pardon President Trump. Why? Because it makes me, President Biden, the big guy. And the person I pardoned, the little guy.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I'm not going to speak to pardons from here.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): A day after a pair of presidential debates were announced, democratic insiders are expressing doubt.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Trump could certainly say, I never agreed to those specifics in the letter.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: And he probably will, and that's how this all falls apart.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Even though he's basically said, I would debate him anywhere, anytime.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Vice President Harris accepted an invitation to debate a Trump running mate to be named later. As POLITICO reports, "Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has joked to friends that she may return to California to run for governor if Democrats lose the White House this fall.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Have you heard --\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The Vice President Harris is telling friends that she may go back to California and run for governor if the election does not go her way.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: That is news to me.\par \par \uc2 She has been an amazing partner, and the president appreciates her, appreciates her leadership, and that's all I'll say to that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): The vice president's team says that's not going to happen in 2026 when \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's term is up, because they believe she will be here serving out a second term as President Biden's V.P. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Peter Doocy at the White House. Thanks, Peter.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were down. The Dow lost 39 After briefly topping the 40,000-mark for the first time. The S&P 500 fell 11. NASDAQ was off 44.\par \par \uc2 We have learned that the wife of embattled New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  has breast cancer. The senator is on trial right now on bribery and corruption charges. His wife's trial has delayed -- has been delayed until July.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent C.B. Cotton is in New York on that tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 C.B. COTTON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  arrives to federal district court in Manhattan with initial witnesses and his bribery trial taking the stand.\par \par \uc2 But today, Menendez himself made his own announcement. His wife, Nadine Menendez is being treated for Stage Three breast cancer, and will need a mastectomy. The senator shared the diagnosis and a statement issued via his Senate office, reading, in part, quote,\par \par \uc2 "We are, of course, concerned about the seriousness and advanced stage of the disease. We hope and pray for the best results."\par \par \uc2 Word of her diagnosis comes less than a day after her husband's lawyers told jurors she was mostly to blame for the alleged bribery scheme. Back in April, Nadine Menendez's lawyers requested a delay for her trial on charges in the case, citing a serious health issue. She is pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat New Jersey senator, has also pleaded not guilty. Prosecutors say he traded his political influence for cash, gold bars, home furnishings, and a new \uc2 Mercedes Benz\uc2  convertible, accusing him of improperly acting on behalf of the Egyptian and Qatari governments.\par \par \uc2 Menendez is on trial with two other New Jersey businessman who maintained their innocence after being accused of funneling bribes to the couple.\par \par \uc2 The judge has said the case could last nearly two months and the trials first witness, an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent who raided the Menendez' home back in June 2022, describe to the jury today, the more than $400,000 cash and gold found inside.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COTTON (on camera): During the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent's testimony, jurors got an up- close look at some of the evidence in this case, passing around several different sized gold bars, and wads of cash. And then later, the defense began its cross examination of that \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: C.B. Cotton in New York for us tonight. Thank you, C.B.\par \par \uc2 Up next, House lawmakers trying to force President Biden to send certain military aid to Israel. We've got a live report.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX5 in New York as a 12-year-old girl is shot, another woman stabbed after two people opened fire during a massive brawl in Queens.\par \par \uc2 Police say the fight involved 10 kids. Authorities say they are still looking for two suspects.\par \par \uc2 FOX26 in Houston, as the company that owns the barge that hit the Pelican Island bridge near Galveston, Wednesday, says the vessel broke loose from its toe before drifting into the causeway.\par \par \uc2 The collision stopped traffic on the bridge that connects Pelican Island to Galveston for hours.\par \par \uc2 No injuries were reported and the bridge is now partially open to those leaving the island.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Louisville, one of the big stories there tonight from our affiliate WDRB. The PGA Championship gets underway at Valhalla Golf Club. It is the fourth time the tournament has been held there. Xander Schauffele has the first-round lead after breaking a tournament record, and tying the all-time major record low round by shooting a 62.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY", from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Chicago and university police cleared an encampment of anti-Israel demonstrators this morning at \uc2 DePaul University\uc2 's Lincoln Park Campus.\par \par \uc2 The DePaul divestment coalition began its demonstration April 30th. Today, officers ordered protesters to disperse. Police say demonstrators left the encampment voluntarily.\par \par \uc2 Also today, U.C. Irvine classes went remote after several people were detained last night as an anti-Israel protest escalated prompting a massive police response. Police also cleared an encampment there.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", House Republicans bring to the floor a bill that would force President Biden to lift his hold on certain military aid to Israel. Registered voters seem evenly split on U.S. policy toward Israel. 32 percent say the U.S. is too supportive. 30 percent say not supportive enough. And 33 percent say about right.\par \par \uc2 Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill tonight with the very latest. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good evening.\par \par \uc2 Republicans want to force the administration to unlock heavy weapons for Israel, daring Democrats to vote no. Democrats accused the GOP of trying to expose their internal fractures.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN GOLDMAN (D-NY): As a Jew, I am frankly sick and tired of the Republican Party using Israel as a political weapon to divide us. If you truly care about Israel, please stop using it as a divisive political pawn.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The final vote, 224 to 187. 16 Democrats voted yes. Three GOP members voted no. The Democratic split over Israel was on display outside the capitol. Progressive congressional aides protested against what they called, "funding Israel's war on civilians."\par \par \uc2 Organizers instructed aides to wear masks and not carry congressional I.D.s.\par \par \uc2 SAM ELGHANAYAN, KHANNA LEGISLATIVE ASSISTANT: I'm here on my personal time, not as a member of the office or representative for the member.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: There was a message here for people to wear masks and also -- and also to take off your I.D.s. Why? Why did they ask them to take off their I.D.s?\par \par \uc2 ELGHANAYAN: You can --\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: And why did they ask them to wear masks? Not to identify who their demonstrators were?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The administration just released $1 billion in aid to Israel for other weapons. But Republicans said that's not what they agreed to last month.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): That's a pittance. That is a window dressing. That is to try to give him political cover. And now, they are defying the will of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  will not put the House bill on the floor. Schumer wants to guard against the GOP, exploiting vulnerable Democrats. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Chad Pergram on the Hill. Thank you, Chad.\par \par \uc2 Israel says it is sending more troops into Rafah and that it's wearing down \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists. The news comes as the U.S. military finishes the installation of a structure designed to facilitate humanitarian aid into Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Trey Yingst has our update tonight from Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: After weeks of construction, the American floating pier off the coast of Gaza is complete. While aid trucks are expected to pick up new deliveries of supplies in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 The project is drawing backlash from some Palestinian civilians.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NADIA AL-DEBS, DISPLACED PALESTINIAN (through translator): I strongly object to these ships and the American ships.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): Despite allowing more aid to enter Gaza, Israel has expanded strikes against the strip in recent days, going after \uc2 Hamas\uc2  on the ground across the Palestinian territory.\par \par \uc2 At a hospital in central Gaza. injured patients wait, some for treatment, others to leave the enclave.\par \par \uc2 NI'MA ALI IMAD, MOTHER (through translator): We hope that you'll be able to open the Rafah border crossing for humanitarian cases, so that they can receive treatment and be provided with medication.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice over): This Palestinian mother pleads to Israel as she tends to her injured son, Mohamed. Israeli forces have pushed deeper into Gaza's third largest city of Rafah in recent days. The move cutting the only exit for Palestinians from Gaza, and forcing hundreds of thousands to evacuate further north.\par \par \uc2 Newly released satellite images illustrate the movement of civilians. Showing recently clear tents and encampments.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli military says the evacuations are necessary as intense battles against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in the south of Gaza rage on. The militant group is continuing to launch new ambushes against \uc2 IDF\uc2  troops, using their network of tunnels.\par \par \uc2 YOAV GALLANT, MINISTER OF DEFENSE, ISRAEL (through translator): Several tunnels have been destroyed by our troops and additional tunnels will be destroyed soon. This activity will intensify.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Currently, there are key disagreements in the Israeli war Cabinet about the next steps for the war in Gaza. Amid those discussions, Israeli officials are determining how to address ongoing threats from a Lebanese militant group, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the federal government cuts funding for an American group with ties to the Chinese lab that some blame for the coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  thanks Chinese leader Xi Jinping for Beijing's proposals on ending the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The two leaders met in the Chinese capital. They reaffirmed a no limits partnership they say as grown deeper, as both countries face increasing disputes with the West.\par \par \uc2 Slovak authorities charged a man with attempting to assassinate populist Prime Minister \uc2 Robert Fico\uc2 . They say the suspect acted alone in a politically motivated attack that laid bare the extreme polarization gripping the small Central European country.\par \par \uc2 Fico was in serious but stable condition after being shot multiple times in the stomach.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Chiba, Japan. One of the big stories there tonight. A \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  strike group's flagship aircraft carrier leaves its Japanese homeport.\par \par \uc2 The departure of the USS \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  wraps up nearly nine years of deployment in the Indo Pacific. Comes at a time of growing tension in the face of an increasingly assertive China in that region.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has upheld the authority of the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2 . The semi-independent federal agency is designed to regulate mortgages, car loans, and other lending practices.\par \par \uc2 It's been criticized by conservatives and business groups for wielding too much unchecked power. But, the court's 72 majority said the agency's funding mechanism is constitutional.\par \par \uc2 Two pharmaceutical companies say they will terminate the development of their experimental treatment for ALS, also known as Lou Gehrig's disease. \uc2 Biogen\uc2  and Ionis say the treatment's failed to produce improvement in patient in an early to mid-stage study.\par \par \uc2 This marks another setback in the search for new treatments for ALS, a rare neurodegenerative disease that affects the voluntary control of limbs and leads to breathing trouble. Most patients die from the disease within five years.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's "What Happened to Wuhan" segment, the Biden administration is cutting funding to the American nonprofit organization tied to the Chinese lab that may have been ground zero for the coronavirus. Here is senior national correspondent Rich Edson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS STATE DEPARTMENT CORRESPONDENT: The Health and Health Services Department has barred controversial research organization \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2  from receiving federal grants.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Was Dr. Daszak a good steward of taxpayer dollars?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He was not.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: \uc2 HHS\uc2  notified \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2 's President Dr. \uc2 Peter Daszak\uc2  of the ban yesterday. Today the select subcommittee on the coronavirus pandemic questioned the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2 's deputy director about his agency's oversight of federal grants.\par \par \uc2 REP. BRAD WENSTRUP, (R-OH) HOUSE CORONAVIRUS SUBCOMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: It is up to the grantee to oversee themselves. This is a recipe for waste, fraud, abuse, and deception.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Hhs accuses \uc2 EcoHealth\uc2  of failing to adequately monitor experiments it funded at China's Wuhan Institute of Virology. It also says \uc2 EcoHealth\uc2  neglected to keep the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  informed and provide notes from those experiments.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did EcoHealth ever produce the requested notebooks?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They have not.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: In its letter to Daszak, \uc2 HHS\uc2  writes, quote, "Debarment is generally for a period not to exceed three years," but says that could last shorter or longer depending on circumstances. \uc2 EcoHealth\uc2  says it will appeal and that, quote, "We disagree strongly with decision and will present evidence to refute each of these allegations." Republicans maintain the organization funded dangerous research that may have sparked the pandemic. Democrats say the subcommittee has no proof of that.\par \par \uc2 RAUL RUIZ, (D-CA) HOUSE CORONAVIRUS SUBCOMMITTEE: It has not substantiated any allegations that federal grant funding for \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2  created the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): The subcommittee has scheduled another hearing next week on \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2 . Chairman Brad Wenstrup has subpoenaed Dr. David Morens, a former top adviser to Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 . Fauci is scheduled to appear before this subcommittee June 3rd. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Rich Edson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 House lawmakers are taking a closer look at the influx of Chinese nationals into the U.S. Almost 28,000 have illegally crossed the border in the past seven months. Correspondent Griff Jenkins has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: They are coming in from China, 31,000, 32,000 over the last few months, and they are all military age, and they mostly are men.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It's one of candidate Trump's favorite new lines on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And it sounds like to me, are they trying to build a little army in our country? Is that what they are trying to do?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: No evidence to suggest a shadow army is being organized. But new data from CBP shows a surge of Chinese nationals at the U.S.'s southern border. So far this fiscal year, a record breaking 27,583 Chinese nationals have illegally crossed, surpassing the total of 24,125 last fiscal year. It's a nearly 8,000 percent increase from fiscal year 2021, which saw a mere 342. Eighty-five percent of the 27,000 plus were single adults, raising concerns among border officials.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, it absolutely does raise concerns. Whenever there's a large increase in numbers concerned with who these individuals are and what they are doing.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: At a House hearing this afternoon, Republicans sounded the alarm while Democrats dismissed the issue as partisan politics.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK LECHLEITNER, ICE DEPUTY DIRECTOR: This surge of Chinese nationals at our border, without recent precedent, presents a major national security vulnerability ripe for exploitation.\par \par \uc2 REP. YVETTE CLARK, (D-NY): We're here today because Republicans are demagoguing and exploiting the xenophobic and white supremacist ideologies that are fueling the immigration debate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And just moments ago, Shannon, DHS and the Justice Department announced a new recent arrivals docket intended to speed up the asylum claims process so individuals who do not qualify can be removed more quickly. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Griff, also, there are reports today -- you have been covering this today about two Jordanian nationals attempting to breach, we think, security at Quantico. What's the latest on that?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: We've confirmed the story. It happened on May 3rd, two Jordanian nationals in a box truck. Now, at the gate, the driver ignored directions to stop. The two men were detained. They were turned over to ICE. No weapons were found in the vehicle. A base spokesperson says the men told officers they were working for an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  subcontractor, but \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has not confirmed that. The acting ICE director confirmed the men are in custody and going through removal proceedings.\par \par \uc2 But Shannon, it's unclear whether they are illegally in the U.S. and whether they pose a national security risk. DHS and ICE have not responded to our inquiries.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, you have a way of getting answers on these stories. So Griff, we thank you. We'll stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right up next, our series on toxic towns continues by efforts by victims to get compensation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH, (D-MO): \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  and \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  on the same side fighting for the same thing for our Missourians. You need to listen.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Residents from communities impacted by radiation exposure were in Washington today. They are calling on House Speaker Mike Johnson to hold a vote on legislation and would offer compensation for their illnesses. As part of our series on toxic towns, we have explained how the nuclear weapons program in St. Louis may have sickened thousands in the region. My colleague Bret Baier takes a closer look at their fight for assistance.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 The Radiation Exposure Compensation Act expires next month. As we told you previously in our series, the bill passed the Senate earlier this year but has yet to be considered in the House. It's just one effort that would offer payments to those sickened by toxic sites in parts of Missouri and other locations throughout the country. Tonight, we look at how the fight for assistance is a race against the clock.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIM VISINTINE, COLDWATER CREEK, JUST THE FACTS: This Radiation Exposure Compensation Act that we are looking for -- there's no windfall for anybody. It's a drop in the bucket.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kim Visintine is another former resident of Coldwater Creek and is fighting for compensation for very personal reasons.\par \par \uc2 VISINTINE: My son was born with a glioblastoma multiforme. And we were told that he was one in a million, that children just don't get this cancer.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Zach had first neurosurgery within a week. He started chemo soon after as Kim and her husband consulted specialists in an effort to cure the rare cancer.\par \par \uc2 VISINTINE: Having full coverage of insurance and my husband having full conch, our out-of-pocket costs for out-of-network and specialists after a year of treatment was $100,000.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Zach lost his battle with cancer in 2006. As Kim and her husband began to process that loss, she also began to ask why this happened to their son.\par \par \uc2 VISINTINE: It wasn't until years later with social media that I reconnected with a lot of grade school friends and friends that I grew up with in the neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She started the group Coldwater Creek Just the Facts, Please, and began mapping reports of illnesses in the region.\par \par \uc2 VISINTINE: We're like, oh my God, all these illnesses are around the creek, and this is our common denominator, common link.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But efforts to win compensation for their illnesses have faced hurdles.\par \par \uc2 VISINTINE: Even if we get all this compensation, we will never qualify because my son -- I was exposed, hence his disease. But by the time he was born, I was living out of the zip code that's affected.\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH, (D-MO): In fact, there are RICA claimants in all 50 states. We are talking about legislation that impacts every single member of Congress's constituents.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: While Kim and others would not receive payments, she says the impact extends further than just individual assistance.\par \par \uc2 VISINTINE: If it gives somebody a chance to just breathe and pay their bills, great. I think that's wonderful. But beyond the restitution, if we are part of the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act, that opens us up to community grants and funding for the whole area.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you know when the House will take up the bill, or if President Biden would sign it if it gets through?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2 , (R-MO): I do know that the president would sign it, so all eyes are on the House now, Bret. The ball is in their court.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Missouri Republican Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  invited Dawn Chapman to this year's State of the Union address.\par \par \uc2 DAWN CHAPMAN, JUST MOMS STL: We are pushing for everything we have, because the truth is, it's too late for us. It's too late for me. It's too late for my kids.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: During her time in Washington she met with other lawmakers in hopes of gaining support for the legislation.\par \par \uc2 CHAPMAN: We have been able to see what has happened in other communities. So we know what we can ask for.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: House Speaker Mike Johnson's office notes the legislation would cost approximately $60 billion, and it expands a program that should be winding down.\par \par \uc2 BUSH: The people standing up here today are proof that World War II is still killing people in this country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A spokesperson for Johnson tells FOX he is, quote, "working closely with interested members and stakeholders to chart a path forward for the House."\par \par \uc2 It's a tough environment, right? I mean, you're a deficit hawk. You are concerned about spending. This is spending, but clearly it's to right the wrongs of the past.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: This is an instance where the government is the one who has caused this harm. Right now, I will tell you who is paying for this. The American people are paying for it, the people of my state are paying for it, in some cases, literally with their lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): We'll continue to follow what happens with that legislation and the cleanup for those toxic sites around the country. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on presidential politics, executive privilege, and the latest FOX polls.\par \par \uc2 And then remembering the man known as America's pastor.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: We have a lot of congressmen and some senators down today, and they are all in arms over this. They can't even believe it.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2 , (R-FL): We are here voluntarily supporting our friend, President Trump, the leader of our movement, the leader of our party, and soon, again, the leader of this great country.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 , (D-MD): They are all attracted to the Mecca of the courthouse in order to demonstrate their complete obeisance and sycophancy to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, let's see what our panel thinks. Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com and host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , and Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico."\par \par \uc2 OK, so you heard what was said there. We're here voluntarily. But they can say things he can't under this gag order. These are the headlines from mainstream media, with some doubts, "Republicans flock to court to kiss the ring during Trump criminal trial." "Trump allies flock to New York to say what he can't," and "Trump grip tightens with high profile loyalty tests." Is that what this is, Guy?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: That would be part of it. I'm not sure how beneficial it is to him having people from his party there to back him up. Of course, he is not allowed to talk about this case in the gag order, which seems totally crazy to me. So he is having allies come up and show solidarity. That's fine.\par \par \uc2 I think what's more helpful to him, and this is why he came out of court today and started reading quotes from other networks, some analysts and commentators who are not usually supportive to him or friendly to him, they saw what went down in court today on this cross-examination of \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , and they were using words like "devastating."\par \par \uc2 So I think in terms of maybe moving the needle in terms of public opinion, that is a stronger argument to make than, look at these Republicans supporting the Republican nominee for president.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, to your point about Cohen today, we have this. "Blanche highlights Cohen's history of lying." "Trump lawyer Todd Blanche repeatedly questioned former Trump attorney \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  on Thursday about the lies he has made under oath. Dating back to 2017 Blanche highlighted several instances where Cohen lied under oath, including to Congress about a Trump Tower Moscow project and federal investigators from Special Counsel \uc2 Robert Mueller\uc2 's office."\par \par \uc2 Olivia, some of our folks who were there in the courthouse today said at times it seemed like the jury was bored. There was such a long list of lies that they were getting worn-out hearing about them.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": And I think the defense was trying to make this argument, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  cannot be trusted. And you saw them repeatedly trying to poke holes in his story, especially that he spoke to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about securing a financial deal to kill the Stormy Daniels story. And at one point you heard Blanche just yelling "You're lying." And Cohen is trying to fight him back. And that's what they were trying to deliver as the message to the jury before they rest their case.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And part of our new polling asks people, and we break this down by ideology, do you think that President Trump is getting a fair take within the legal system? Democrats clearly, 89 percent say, yes, they think it's fair, 13 percent of the Republicans by contrast. But independents, a slight majority of them, 52 percent, Matthew, they do think he is getting a fair shake.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: It's a pretty narrow majority there on the independents. And I think that explains why the trials haven't hurt Trump's position in the polling. These trials have been going on, the indictments have been going on. Trump has been leading in the polls for six months.\par \par \uc2 And I differ slightly with Guy. I do think having the surrogates there helps Trump. Look, he is forced into a situation where this is a courtroom campaign. He has to be there at least four days a week. And other -- you have to show some type of party unity and amplify your message somehow. Bringing in the legislators, bringing in the potential V.P. pick seems to me a pretty savvy way to do it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So one of the other legal fights that may soon ramp up is happening on Capitol Hill. You've got these House committees voting on whether or not to hold A.G. Garland in contempt because DOJ says they are not going to release the audio recordings of President Biden being interviewed by Special Counsel Robert Hur. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says this, "President Biden has exerted executive privilege to deny House Republicans access to recordings of his interview with a special counsel", that was about the government documents. The move is intended to shield Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  from prosecution if House Republicans succeed in their effort to hold him in contempt for refusing to turn over audio of his conversations with Robert Hur."\par \par \uc2 Olivia, they took step one in one of the committees today. Another one may do so tonight, too.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Yes, and it was partisan. The second committee, the Oversight Committee had to waited for some of their members to come back from New York who went to the Trump trial. The White House is arguing that this is purely partisan, that they're just trying to get the audio to splice it and use it. And Republicans are arguing that they are not cooperating. But that would be pretty valuable audio because Hur had basically argued that, at times his memory was a big issue. And he could potentially use that to the jury over the classified documents case. And so, of course Republicans want to obtain it. And they were hinting today that they are leaning more into an idea of a lawsuit to get that audio.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Matthew, we know these things take time. And really if it's just about making sure the audio doesn't get out so it's not used for campaign ads and all that kind of stuff, it could be a moot point, quickly.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: \uc2 Sure\uc2 , but it raises the issue, why is Biden trying to suppress this audio when the transcript is already released? And the reason is he wants voters to forget about his atrocious press conference on the day that the Hur report was released. That \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who melted down in the Oval Office is a one-term president. His campaign needs to shift the ground quickly and present a more palatable face to the electorate if he wants to win a second term.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So speaking of campaigning, we've got all this new FOX polling out too on so many things, but including dealbreakers, what people, their issues, what is going to get them to the ballot box and make their decision for them. On dealbreaker issues in our new FOX News poll, abortion is at the top of the list, 15 percent, but followed very closely by economy, inflation, and border security.\par \par \uc2 So, Guy, this is a bit of good news for the White House. Democrats have successfully used this issue just about every time it's come up since Dobbs overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Right. And it's a dealbreaker issue for some pro-lifers, obviously. But, among those who prize this issue in terms of their number one consideration, they lean Democratic. I don't think too many of those people aren't sure who they are going to vote for, right? If you are fanatically pro-abortion rights, you are not going to vote for the Republican Party this year no matter what. I think people who see a dealbreaker issue is, for example, the economy or inflation, those might be swing voters. And we see President Trump leading by double digits over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on that issue set.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And let's talk about the economy and inflation. It keeps showing up everywhere. And the president has been out there talking about this. And more than once he has now said that when he took the rains, essentially inflation was at nine percent. Neil Cavuto tried to go back and forth with Jared Bernstein, White House economic adviser, about this and pin him down about why the president is saying something that is demonstrably false. Here's a little bit of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think inflation has gone slightly up. It was at nine percent when I came in.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS HOST: It was never, ever, ever nine percent when you came into office. So why does he keep saying it?\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Well, first of all, let me point out that in that very quote you played, the president talked about how concerned he was for households struggling with prices that he consistently --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's not what I asked you.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: The president was making the point that I think is unequivocally true. The factors that took inflation to nine percent were in place when he took office.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That is not what he said, Jared.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, Matthew he is off talking points with this. Clearly, that's not accurate, just statistical, objective information that the president is sharing.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: He's wrong with the figure. He's wrong that the conditions were in place. Biden is spending, the recovery act is what drove a lot of this inflation. And moreover, they are wrong now when they say everything is honky dory with the economy. When you have prices raised by a fifth since Biden took office -- gas, food, rents, interest rates, consumers and voters are suffering in this economy. And until they change their messaging, they are going to continue to see poor approval numbers on this issue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, we do. We don't have these made up, but I wanted to bring these up, too, from our polling today. When people are asked about the condition of the economy, 30 percent say good or excellent, 70 percent say fair or poor. And that number, Olivia, also translates over when we ask people how is your personal situation or your family doing? Very much the same.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: And look, the economy is one of the top priorities you can count on almost every election. Biden has maybe been able to move slightly in his favor, but he needs to do a lot of work to sell that he is making people's lives better and easier before we get to the ballot boxes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, Guy, has time run out?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: If he keeps lying about inflation, that's not going to help. He said this nine percent lie, which is insulting untrue thing last week. He got fact checked a little bit, then he came out and he said it again this week. And clearly they cannot defend it. Try as they might -- great job by Neil Cavuto there -- there's nothing there. There is no substance to come back at. So he is in denial. And he is trying to ask us to all be in denial, too. And that might not go well for him with voters.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think we're going to hear the nine percent again, or do you think there's going to be enough of a crackdown at the White House that they say you cannot keep saying --\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: We can hear it again. With Biden, you never know.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That is a vote that we're going to hear again.\par \par \uc2 All right, guys, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON, (R-LA) HOUSE SPEAKER: \uc2 Billy Graham\uc2  is such a towering figure in my life as he is in all of our lives, and such a singular figure, and the leading ambassador for the Kingdom of our lifetimes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A bronze statue of the late Reverend \uc2 Billy Graham\uc2  was unveiled this morning inside the U.S. capital. Graham preached in person to more than 210 million people worldwide. His personal relationships with American presidents from \uc2 Dwight Eisenhower\uc2  to \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  earned him the nickname America's Pastor. Graham died in 2018 at the age of 99.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, the U.S. removes Cuba from its list of uncooperative countries against terrorism.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR. We are always 6:00 p.m. eastern, 3:00 on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Shannon Bream here in Washington. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is up next. Bye.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240517ek5g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  Testifies In \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Criminal Trial; Jury Selection In \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  Corruption And Bribery Trial; White House Maintains Israel Support Is "Ironclad"; Anti-Israel Protesters Disrupt Duke Graduation; Republicans Look To Flip Manchin Senate Seat; Manchin Endorses Elliott For Senate Seat; West Virginia Votes On Open Senate Seat Tomorrow; Trump And Biden Signal Willingness To Debate; Recent Poll Shows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Leading President Biden On Issues Of Economy And Israeli-Palestinian Conflict; Former Trump Lawyer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  Testifies In Hush Money Criminal Trial Of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ; Jury Being Selected For Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  Corruption Trial; Secretary Of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  Criticizes Israel's Prosecution Of War In Gaza\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Nate Foy, Chad Pergram, Peter Doocy, Garrett Tenney,  Bill Melugin, Mike Emanuel, Brit Hume, Larry Kudlow, Charles Payne,  Kayleigh McEnany, Harold Ford \par \uc2 7,959 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Let's go to you, Harold.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Go.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: How do you call them? The what? (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 Anthony Burgess, a school bus driver in Florida is having students write down their favorite quotes and words of encouragement to decorate the interior of the school bus. Among the stand out quotes, your mindset can change everything. Thank you, man.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's wonderful.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, Harold. Have a great show everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Jesse, that was kind of like a half a thing. That wasn't One More Thing.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That was a teeny little One More Thing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. All right. We'll see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. We are coming to you tonight live just down the hall from THE FIVE in the Fox News World Headquarters in New York. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 The federal corruption and bribery trial for New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  gets underway. The Biden administration touts what it calls its ironclad support of Israel, but its top diplomat gets critical of the Israeli war effort.\par \par \uc2 And former President Trump scores big in new swing state polling, we'll break down the numbers.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, the star witness of the prosecution case against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  takes the stand. Former Trump lawyer and self-described fixer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  is implicating his old boss in the scheme to silence and adult film star's claim that they had a sexual encounter.\par \par \uc2 But Cohen will soon face a defense cross examination that will directly question his credibility.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Nate Foy is outside the New York State Supreme Court again tonight with the latest. Good evening, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. At the end of court tonight, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  testified that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  personally approved of his reimbursement plan after Cohen paid $130,000 to adult film actress Stormy Daniels.\par \par \uc2 Now, Cohen says that Trump and former \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  CFO \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  disguise the payments as Cohen's income after Trump won the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any messages for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  today? Do you plan on telling the truth?\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): He's admitted to lying under oath several times. But today the prosecution's star witness \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  promised the truth, telling the jury former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  participated in a scheme to pay off adult film actress Stormy Daniels.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: That we call a legal expense, a legal expense.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Cohen told the court Trump wanted the story taken care of to protect his image with women after the release of the Access Hollywood tape.\par \par \uc2 Cohen testified Trump told him, "Push it out as long as you can, just get past the election. If I win, it has no relevance. And if I lose, I don't even care."\par \par \uc2 Cohen eventually wire Daniels the money using a home equity line of credit to conceal the payment from his own wife. Cohen testified he told Trump first saying, "Everything required Mr. Trump's sign off. On top of that, I wanted the money back."\par \par \uc2 According to Cohen, former \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  CFO \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  facilitated his reimbursement. Trump himself can address Cohen publicly because he's bound by the court's gag order.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We have a corrupt judge and we have the judge that's highly conflicted. There's no fraud here. There's no crime here. This is four weeks of keeping me from not campaigning.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Cohen is a convicted felon and served prison time partly for his dealings with Daniels.\par \par \uc2 Today outside the courthouse, Republican supporters of Trump say prosecutors are now using Cohen to influence this year's election.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH): Whether you love \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , whether you like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or whether you're a Democrat or don't care about politics, what's going on inside that courtroom is a threat to American democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): When court resumes tomorrow at 9:30, Cohen will again be on the witness stand under direct examination.\par \par \uc2 So, as you mentioned, Trump's lawyers still haven't had a chance to push back on some of the claims that he made during his testimony today, and to push back on his overall credibility, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More on this with the panel. Nate, thanks.\par \par \uc2 As mentioned, jury selection is underway in the federal corruption and bribery trial of New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 . He has pleaded not guilty. Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram here in New York with details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  has represented New Jersey in the Senate since 2007. But the Feds accused him of also representing Qatar and Egypt.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors allege the senator of writing ghost letters to other senators to unlock $300 million for Egypt. And then there are the shiny objects, gold bars that one businessman is accused of giving to Menendez to curry favor with Qatar. Menendez chaired the Foreign Relations Committee, but the indictment forced him to see the gavel.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ (D-NJ): It opens a dangerous door for the Justice Department to take the normal engagement of members of Congress with a foreign government and to transform those engagements into a charge of being a foreign agent.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): This is not his first run in with the law. Menendez stood on trial for unrelated bribery charges in 2017. Back then, fellow New Jersey Democrat \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  testified on his behalf but Booker has all been given up on Menendez this time.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER (D-NJ): I'm not going to follow that day-to-day.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: You talk to him at all lately about the trial or anything?\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: I have not discussed the trial in any detail or depth.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Jurors failed to reach a verdict in Menendez's last trial resulting in a hung jury, that cleared the way for the senator to run again and win in 2018.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Today is resurrection day. And I want to thank God once again for allowing me to stand before you as an innocent man.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): The senator's wife Nadine also faces trial in the summer, expect his defense counsel to try to cast blame on her, selling favors on behalf of her husband.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Now, jury selection continues tomorrow, the trial could run through the Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 Now, Menendez he still might run as an independent, the filing deadline, that is June 4th, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's top diplomat is delivering some of the strongest criticism yet of Israel's conduct of the war in Gaza. Secretary of State \uc2 Anthony Blinken\uc2  is maligning both Israeli tactics in what he claims is a lack of results.\par \par \uc2 President Biden meantime continues to receive heavy criticism from both Republicans and some Democrats over his refusal to supply Israel with some weapons because of its military operations in Rafah.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy joins us live now from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Just a few minutes ago as we were coming to air, President Biden was leaving a reception in the Rose Garden and a reporter asked him how confident he is that he can bring home the hostages to which President Biden said, never give up hope, it was not on camera, but it's also not really consistent with what other Biden officials who have seen the intelligence about the hostages told us today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My name is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I work for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden's team is hoping to clarify what the White House means by ironclad.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: His commitment to Israel is ironclad. ironclad doesn't mean you never disagree.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): It also means according to a report in The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , offering Israel valuable assistance in an effort to persuade it to hold back including sensitive intelligence to help the Israeli military pinpoint the location of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders and find the group's hidden tunnels.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): Conditionally, having any kind of condition on offering them the ability to micro target \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is absurd.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan rattled off 10 bullet points to clarify President Biden's position on the war. And the headline:\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: We do not believe what is happening in Gaza is a genocide.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): But they do believe it's time for Israel to get out of Gaza if they can't explain in detail a plan to protect innocent civilians.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: If they leave and get out of Gaza as we believe they need to do. Then, you're going to have a vacuum and a vacuum that's likely to be filled by chaos, by anarchy, and ultimately by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  again.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): A ceasefire deal hinges on hostage releases, but officials claimed not to know how many remain.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: We know there are deceased hostages. And we know there are living hostages.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): And Sullivan sees parallels between the Israel \uc2 Hamas\uc2  war and efforts to find peace between Ireland and Northern Ireland led by George Mitchell.\par \par \uc2 SULLIVAN: Senator Mitchell said quite famously that negotiations are a thousand days of failure in one day of success. And right now, we're in the former days rather than the latter day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): And the numbers of women and children thought who have been killed in this war had been reduced down by about 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 The United Nations says they're not just changing the numbers based on nothing but that the Gaza Ministry of Health has a better idea now of casualties. The current tally is 4,959 women and 7,797 children killed. That is still a staggering amount of lives lost, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, definitely. Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 One of the most popular entertainers, comedians in the world has now felt the effects of anti-Israel demonstrations on college campuses. Protesters heckled Jerry Seinfeld Sunday during his commencement address at \uc2 Duke University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also several incidents at several other schools around the country including \uc2 DePaul University in Chicago\uc2 . That's where Correspondent Garrett Tenney is tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Anti-Israel protests disrupting college graduations across the country this weekend.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Jerry Seinfeld, candidate for the honorary degree of Doctor of Arts.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): In North Carolina, dozens of students walked out of \uc2 Duke University\uc2 's graduation ceremony as comedian Jerry Seinfeld who is Jewish and has publicly voiced support for Israel took the stage to deliver his commencement address.\par \par \uc2 Some students cheering on the protesters while many in the crowd booed as the group left and it was a similar scene in Richmond, Virginia where dozens of graduating students walked out as Governor \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  gave their commencement address.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 U.C. Berkeley\uc2 , hundreds of protesters repeatedly interrupted the graduation program with loud pro-Palestinian chants. Not everyone in attendance was happy about the disruption.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I can't hear the speakers at all. And you know, we worked hard for four years. And truly, this is a Berkeley degree. We're extremely excited.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You know, our son came here to study engineering, not idiocy.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): The White House is bracing for similar protests next weekend when President Biden is scheduled to deliver the commencement address at Morehouse College in Atlanta, where some students and faculty say they want the president's invitation rescinded.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The general feeling on the ground is that we don't want him here. Frankly, the issue on cop city and the genocide in Palestine and just the straw that broke the camel's back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): Here at \uc2 DePaul University\uc2 , students at this encampment say they are now preparing for the possibility of police involvement after negotiations with school officials broke down over the weekend, and students ignored the school's deadline to clear out by noon Sunday. Graduation here, still a month away, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney in Chicago. Garrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we look at the race to replace one longtime Democratic senator, we'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says Ukrainian troops are engaging in intense battles with the advancing Russian army in two border areas of Ukraine. Outgunned and outnumbered, Ukrainian soldiers are trying to hold back a significant Russian ground offensive in recent days.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the death toll from a Russian apartment building collapse blamed on Ukrainian shelling rose to 15.\par \par \uc2 Twin natural disasters in Indonesia, rescuers recovered dozens of bodies after monsoon rains triggered flash floods on Sumatra Island bringing down torrents of cold lava and mud. 43 people are confirmed dead so far, another 15 missing.\par \par \uc2 Mount Ibu volcano erupts spewing thick grey ash and dark clouds more than 16,000 feet into the sky for five minutes. Officials are telling residents and tourists to stay at least three miles away.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  announced it will invest $4.3 billion in France this year, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  planning to expand its next generation cloud and A.I. infrastructure bringing up to 25,000 of the most advanced graphics processing units or GPUs to France by the end of 2025.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More than 2 million people from Texas to Florida have been or are still on alert for powerful and dangerous thunderstorms. Tonight, the systems are capable of producing softball sized hail, hurricane force wind gusts, and possible tornadoes. The threat of severe extreme weather will last, we're told, over the next few days.\par \par \uc2 The central and southern U.S. have reportedly and recently been logged down with some 60 deadly tornadoes, severe weather outbreaks over the past few weeks.\par \par \uc2 The demolition of a large section of the Francis Scott Key Bridge, took place a short time ago. The span crumbled into the water in just seconds. The longest trestle toppled away from the ground.\par \par \uc2 Dali, the grounded Dali container ship and slid off its bow, sending the wall of water splashing back as you see here toward the vessel. The Dali crashed into the bridge in late March.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today as investors awaited key inflation data The Dow lost 81. The S&P 500 fell one. The NASDAQ gained 47.\par \par \uc2 West Virginia voters begin the task of replacing longtime Democratic Senator Joe Manchin tomorrow. Their Senate primary is one of six open races. Democrats are hoping to keep this fall. Correspondent Bill Melugin is in Charleston, West Virginia tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Four Republicans, the road to retaking the Senate may start in West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Senator Joe Manchin's retirement makes this race the most likely flip for the GOP, according to Fox analysis.\par \par \uc2 Current West Virginia Governor Jim justice has been endorsed by former President Trump in a state he won by nearly 40 points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 His primary opponent is West Virginia Congressman Alex Mooney, a member of the House Freedom Caucus. He is endorsed by Ted Cruz, and he's been calling himself the true Trump candidate in the race.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEX MOONEY (R-WV): I have a voting record for 10 years as a congressman and 100 percent pro-life, and supported Trump's agenda. I'm running against a former Democrat Jim justice, who still has liberal views on many issues and might use to act like a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JIM JUSTICE (R-WV): If that be the case, why is Trump endorsed me and did not endorse him? I've been a governor for 7-1/2 years. I've seen Alex Mooney one time as our congressman. It's absolutely very weak.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: West Virginians tell Fox, a Trump endorsement goes a long way here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it carries a lot of weight, because we're a Trump country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would have backed the governor anyway, but it helps with Trump. Trump's demand.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): As for the Democratic primary, it's a three-way race between former Marine, Zach Shrewsbury, a self-described populace leftist on the progressive wing of the party. And \uc2 Don Blankenship\uc2 , a former coal CEO, who spent a year in prison for violating safety standards.\par \par \uc2 But Joe Manchin is backing Glenn Elliott, the mayor of Wheeling.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR GLENN ELLIOTT (D), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, WEST VIRGINIA: I think a lot of voters in history in West Virginia have gotten frustrated with the Democratic Party, but I don't think they've left for good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, the West Virginia voters, we've been talking to out here, say their biggest issues of concern are obviously the coal mining industry, fentanyl, and the economy.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, they will hit the polls, they will make their voices heard. They will decide who will officially be matching up this November to replace Senator Joe Manchin. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin, live in West Virginia. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, new polls on the presidential race and the increasing prospects of the nominees, yes, debating.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around this country are covering today. FOX5 in New York, has a person of interest connected to the brutal choking and raping of a woman in New York is now in custody. Kashaan Parks, faces a slew of charges, including rape, assault, sex abuse, and strangulation. Police say he has two prior arrests.\par \par \uc2 Fox 17 in Nashville Tennessee, as five officers hailed as heroes for taking down the Covenant school shooter last year receive another national award for their valor. The officers were honored in Washington over the weekend. A former student at the school killed three children and three adults before being fatally shot.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Kansas City, courtesy of EarthCam. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX4, our affiliate. The \uc2 National Football League\uc2  schedule will start September 5th, with the defending Super Bowl champion, Chiefs, hosting the \uc2 Baltimore Ravens\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It will be a rematch of last season's AFC Championship Game. The rest of the league schedule set to be released Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: New polls over the weekend show former President Trump, leading in five crucial battleground states. This comes as the drumbeat gets louder for debates between the two presumptive nominees.\par \par \uc2 Here is chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former President Trump leads President Biden in five critical battleground states: Michigan, Arizona, Nevada, Georgia, and Pennsylvania among registered voters.\par \par \uc2 Among likely voters, that same poll from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and Siena College reveals a tighter race with Mr. Trump leading in five states. With Mr. Biden ahead in Michigan and narrowly behind Mr. Trump in Wisconsin, and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  just came out with a poll. That shows us leading everywhere by a lot.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): It is unclear at the moment if Trump and Biden will face off on the debate stage this fall, though both sound eager to do it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, when will you debate President Trump?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Set it up.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice over): Trump use similar language on his social media platform.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Let's set it up right now. I am ready to go anywhere that you are. We're going to win and we're going to win big.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The \uc2 Commission on Presidential Debates\uc2  announced locations and dates for general election debates last November. Monday, September 16th, at \uc2 Texas State University\uc2  in San Marcos, Texas. Tuesday, October 1st at \uc2 Virginia State University\uc2  in Petersburg, Virginia. Wednesday, October 9th, at \uc2 the University of Utah\uc2  in Salt Lake City, Utah.\par \par \uc2 The one skeptical \uc2 RNC\uc2  has signaled a willingness to participate in \uc2 CPD\uc2  events and is arguing for holding debate sooner.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The latest polling reveals a shift among young, black, and Hispanic voters desiring change and unhappy about the economy and military action in Gaza. All key constituencies which could tear apart the president's democratic coalition. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What's your biggest concern?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The borders.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think democracy -- keeping democracy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Reproductive freedoms is probably the most important.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I just wish the economy was better.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would also say the economy, inflation is a big issue as well.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The borders is important.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Economy is the big one, and immigration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the newest polls out. The New York Times Philadelphia Inquirer, Siena College poll just out. Most important issue, just like you hear those folks: economy, 22 percent, then, you see immigration and abortion. Economic conditions, how do you describe them? Excellent is only three percent. Good, only fair, 27 percent. Poor, 49 percent. And who do you trust better on the economy? Biden at 38 percent, former President Trump at 58 percent.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some perspective tonight from FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume and Larry Ludlow of FOX Business. Good evening, gentlemen. First to you, Brit. What do you make of the latest polls. It tracks with other polls we've seen about the trust on the economy and how big that issue is.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, you put that together with the states where Trump appears to be ahead. And remember, Mr. Trump does not need to win all those battleground states. If he holds the states he held in 2020 and wins a couple of those states, you could pick at any number of them you could pick to be the two, he's president. And if the election were held today, I think this poll indicates he would be elected. Obviously, we've got a long way to go. It's only May. But the issues favor him. Abortion favors the Democrats, but the economy and immigration and a number of other issues favor the president. And the people are upset about his performance generally. Age is not an issue they cite, but you know it's in there working against Mr. Biden. So I think that's where the matter stands now. It's not quite fair to say it's Trump's race to lose, but it's getting there slowly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Larry, Reuters, "U.S. consumer sentiment at six month low, inflation exceptions rise. Larger than expected drop in sentiment reported by the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  Friday was across all age, income, and education groups as well as political party affiliation." You've got to think that that's part of this equation when you look at those polls.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS HOST: Yes, it's been pretty much -- this was for May, and pretty much you see the same thing January, February, March, and April. Consumer confidence plunging, inflation fears rising, actual inflation has gone up quite a bit, about 4.5 percent at an annual rate. That's the wrong direction. The Fed's target is two. We will get a new number on Wednesday. The Cleveland fed is looking for a difficult number, maybe 5 percent at an annual rate.\par \par \uc2 This just bodes very poorly for Mr. Biden. Bidenomics is unpopular because it hasn't worked. And there is an affordability crisis going on. Wages are rising more slowly than prices, and that has plagued Biden, except for a few months, pretty much his entire presidency. I don't see that changing, frankly. If anything, the numbers are getting worse.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Larry, they stopped talking about Bidenomics. They are saying the numbers still look good for the economy overall. But here is a national economic adviser on the Trump tax cuts expiration. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD\uc2 , NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: The expiration of President Trump's 2017 tax package next year will put the issue of tax fairness front and center. Do we want a tax system that favors the wealthy or the middle class? Tax fairness is central to the president's approach to building an economy that works for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know you worked for President Trump, but when those tax cuts do expire, when the pledge is nobody will see taxes increase under $400,000 a year, that won't hold true, right?\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: It's not true. Look, this is the progressive point of view, with all respect to Ms. Brainard, study after study has shown, \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2 , various faculties of, heaven forbid, the Ivy League schools and so forth. Poverty went down, inequality went down, unemployment went down for all the minority groups. The biggest gainers were the middle and the lower income classes. I mean, you had just -- weekly wages under Mr. Trump went up about nine percent at an annual rate, OK? Weekly wages have fallen over six percent under Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 If you start raising corporate taxes, raise business taxes, raise individual taxes, you're going to see a sinking economy across the board. And, frankly, if a rising tide lifted all boats during the successful Trump tax cuts, what you will have here is a sinking economy will sink all the boats.\par \par \uc2 I never understood this. The data is so clear. I don't care what side you're on. Even some of my Democratic economic friends, who are much more moderate than this White House, are saying don't do away with all these tax -- that's a $5 trillion tax hike. It's exactly what the economy does not need.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, we are talking a lot about the economy. We see it as still the number one issue. But if you think about the Israel-Gaza situation, in this new poll, the Siena College poll, it has, who do you trust better on the Israel -- Israeli-Palestinian conflict? Trump at 51 percent, Biden at 36 percent. How much do you think that all factors into this moment now? This is, again, a snapshot in time.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, in the end, foreign policy is an issue in all these presidential elections. It is the primary business of the presidency because the executive branch of the federal government is the primary agency for dealing with foreign affairs. States and localities and the private sector have very little to do with it. So this is going to matter.\par \par \uc2 And for the life of me, Bret, I cannot figure out what the political strategy is being pursued now by the Biden administration. On the one hand, they are supporting Israel still to some extent, saying that the support for Israel is ironclad and all the rest of it. But they are publicly criticizing Israel, threatening to withhold aid and the rest of it. It seems to me, I guess they are supposed to somehow have it both ways. My guess is they are getting the worst of both worlds. People who are supporting Israel aren't happy with him, and the people who are supporting the Palestinians aren't happy with him either. My view is that's a loser for the president. One more thing that's a loser for him.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: It's incredible to me. I mean, Brit, you have been around a long time, foreign policy. Peace and prosperity always the issues in presidential campaigns. Over the weekend, Secretary of State Blinken came out and said Biden now wants Israel completely out of Gaza. They will not sell weapons because they are breaking international humanitarian guidelines, and furthermore, they are withholding key intelligence on \uc2 Hamas\uc2  from Israel. It's almost like, whose side are we on here?\par \par \uc2 And people are smart. They are not dumb. They pick up on this -- 75, 80 percent want Israel to end \uc2 Hamas\uc2  all together. This should be an easy issue on this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, Brit. We are five-and-a-half, a little bit more than that, months away from Election Day. Right now, you just said that the position for the former president looks pretty good, as you look at the map, as you look at the polls, as you look at the issues. A lot can happen in a short time. Paint the picture of how the Biden team comes back from what looks like an uphill battle right now.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I would say it's not the way they are trying to do it now. It doesn't look to me like there is much of anything that's going to work. Now, we are going to hear endlessly about January 6th. We're going to hear endlessly about the threat to democracy. I'm not sure how many people really believe that, but certainly Democrats all do. And remember, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  motivates Democrats more than he motivates -- in the past, at least, he has motivated Democrats to come out and vote more than he has motivated Republicans. And that is the one thing that does help the president, is if this were an ordinary garden variety Republican candidate, I think the president would be done, the race would be over. But Trump is controversial and remains so. And that's -- that is an issue that he needs to confront, because the Democrats are going to milk it for all it's worth.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Larry?\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: With 100,000 people turning out in Wildwood, New Jersey, 100,000 people, New Jersey may be in play. A lot of states may be in play that no one dreamed possible in play.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will see. Cover it all. Larry, Brit, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Thank you. Appreciate it very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel as Former President Trump's one time fixer, now accuser takes the stand against him. Just a few blocks with away, jury selection gets underway for current Senator, Democrat \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 's criminal trial. We'll bring it to you all.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: We paid a lawyer a legal expense, legal expense. It's perfectly marked down. The other side doesn't know how to handle it, but they say let's indict him anyway.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE, (R-OH): \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  is the prosecution's star witness. This guy is a convicted felon.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE CONWAY, ATTORNEY: The government has essentially pre-corroborated \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  in a lot of ways.\par \par \uc2 FAREED ZAKARIA, CNN HOST, "FAREED ZAKARIA GPS": And I doubt the New York indictment would have been brought against a defendant whose name was not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some different takes as \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  takes the stand. The prosecution laying out their case just down the road here against former President Trump. "The New York Post," Jonathan Turley writes, "This may be the week \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's case against Trump falls apart. The assumption was that no rational prosecutor would base a major criminal case almost entirely on the testimony of \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , who was recently denounced by a judge as a serial perjurer peddling perverse theories in court. The calculus of Alven Bragg is now obvious. He is counting on the jury convicting Trump regardless of the evidence."\par \par \uc2 What about that and this case? Let's bring in our panel, Charles Payne of FOX Business, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE, and "OUTNUMBERED" co-host \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 . Kayleigh, I know you have been following this. It's been interesting to see the actual case versus all the things that have been covered ad nauseum about it, and the actual crime here and the felony that's underneath all of this. What are your thoughts as we get this first star witness day?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: At the end of the day you have to have a fact pattern that matches the law. That is what any prosecutor is after. And thus far the facts do not match the law. In fact, Jonathan Turley went on in that piece that you just cited to say, look, this might not even get to a jury because, in earnest, this should be dismissed by the judge. After all the witnesses are heard, there will be a motion for a direct verdict from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's team, and that should be sustained, unless there is some big new revelation.\par \par \uc2 One thing we heard today is you get the distinct feeling that Trump, yes, was well-informed of things that were going on, but that he then would direct and micromanage and send things out. From that moment, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , go talk to Weisselberg, you guys figure this out. You don't get the sense of someone directing every little payment, while he's in the Oval Office, by the way, signing away checks. One witness, \uc2 Madeleine Westerhout\uc2 , said I often saw him just sign things. That's what he did. He had a million things going on. He just signed things. So absent some smoking gun, the facts just simply don't seem to match the law.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, Jonathan Turley has been clear that he thinks that this falls apart. However, there are legal experts who think that there is more to this and that they think the prosecution will eventually deliver and that they haven't yet.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Thanks for having me. I think that two things can be right. Maybe this case should not have been brought. Maybe Fareed Zakaria is right, if his name were not Trump, this would not be brought. But it has been. So now we are at a point where the most pivotal and dispositive part for the prosecution is now two things. What was the motivation for President Trump in this matter? Number two, and was he involved in whatever they chose to do, which I think is what Kayleigh was saying. Maybe he was not aware and maybe he did something or signed things that he didn't know what was going on. We'll find out.\par \par \uc2 As long as there is a factual -- some facts are in dispute, they will get this to the jury. I hear what Jonathan is saying. If the prosecution -- excuse me, if the defense in the cross examination is really able to go after him in an intense way, Cohen, and undermine him, maybe Jonathan is right.\par \par \uc2 But I think there's still some shoes to fall here. And I think today he held serve, Cohen. But as Kayleigh and I know, those of us who have gone to law school, the next few days are going to be very challenging. They are going to be intense and informative. And we will see how Mr. Cohen holds up during that time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We saw the defense on the Stormy Daniels cross, Charles, say, do you hate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? And she essentially said yes. \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has been on TikTok and every social media saying not only that, but I want to put him behind bars. That's a different animal.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I will speak for those of us who haven't gone to law school.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You've got to be kidding me. Really, I'm so confused. Really, just, honestly, the crime I don't see. Why would a man not want his wife to know that he might have gone to bed with a porn star? Listen, they are rewriting history, right? So you get to last week, right, you get the witness in there. And she changes her story so much, so much. Even Bill Maher had to admit, listen, this is not what she told me in an interview. And they're dropping hints about the Me Too stuff and he was physically imposing.\par \par \uc2 And the same thing here, all this is is a political stunt. And so after these testimonies you see the articles. All the Me Too women, remember? So it feels like it's feeding into a larger, orchestrated narrative to stop President Trump from winning the election. It does not feel like a real trial. Most people still aren't sure what the crime is, and certainly whatever it might be because it's the first time it's ever been charged ever, there is not even -- I don't see anywhere where it's been proven at all that some kind of crime took place, something deliberate, nefarious, and out of the ordinary took place.\par \par \uc2 FORD: All I'm saying about being a lawyer is where we are at in the case. I don't disagree with you where weather this thing should have been brought or not. But where we are now, if you are the defendant, you can't sit back and make the case this should not have been brought. You're now in a big fight. And the question becomes can that defense team cross-examine --\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I honestly believe that --\par \par \uc2 FORD: I hear everything you're saying.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: From the defense's point of view, the only thing they have to worry about is the judge. You're right, it's going to go to the jury because this judge seems to have it out for President Trump. And the bottom, it's just like they blew it with the Hollywood guy, Weinstein, they're blowing it again. But they don't care that they're blowing it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me weigh in really quickly. Meantime, just down the street, the jury is being selected for the \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  case, which is a bribery case. We don't hear a lot about it anyplace else, but it's happening. And there are very few people weighing in on it.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, and we know that the last case against him was dismissed. He got out of that one. But this case apparently is much stronger. You have his wife and him, perhaps at odds. Her case doesn't come until July. So a lot of cards falling there. But it is interesting. This would be kind of the trial of the year, the \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  case, but it is vastly and largely ignored because everyone is focused on what is happening downtown.\par \par \uc2 FORD: It was a hung jury I think in the first one.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: It was a hung jury, yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: And then they decided not to pursue it after that.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gold bars, Mercedes, and --\par \par \uc2 FORD: A lot going on.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: If nothing else, he's got at least three Lifetime movies in this one, at least three.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 's blunt message to Israel.\par \par \uc2 And then it has been said, golf is a good walk spoiled. We're going to show you some folks who do not subscribe to that.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Israel has not acted in a manner that's consists with international humanitarian law. A lot of armed \uc2 Hamas\uc2  will be left no matter what they do in Rafah. Or if they leave and get out of Gaza, as we believe they need to do, then you're going to have a vacuum, and a vacuum is likely to be filled by chaos, by anarchy, and ultimately by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  again.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PETE RICKETTS\uc2 , (R-NE) SENATE FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE: This just demonstrates their incompetence. Secretary Blinken was talking about how we've got to offer them a better way. A better way like Afghanistan? How well did that work out?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some harsh criticism. Associated Press writes it this way, Blinken said U.S. -- "Blinken delivers some of the strongest U.S. public criticism of Israel's conduct of the war in Gaza." This was the secretary of state back in October.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: The message that I bring to Israel is this -- you may be strong enough on your own to defend yourself, but as long as America exists, you will never ever have to. We will always be there by your side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So what about this? We are back with the panel. Harold, there is a split here, and it's clear, and it may be hurting the president, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 FORD: It could be. I would say two things. Two things can be right. In this instance here, I think the administration may have made a mistake not giving them the weapons that they wanted, and I think it's entirely fair for them at the raise the points that are being raised. Israel is going to win this effort. Whether it's tomorrow, whether it's a month, whether it's three months, they are going to win.\par \par \uc2 In winning, they're going to also have to take on the responsibility in governing an area and governing a group of people, millions of people, who don't want to be under their control and, frankly, I don't think they want them to be under their control.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, we're going to need allies in that region to be allies with us in helping to figure out the future.\par \par \uc2 So I hear what Secretary Blinken is saying, but I think they made a mistake not giving them the weapons and still having this conversation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kayleigh?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: I think a lot of people see foreign policy of confusion, one where words and actions differ, one where in word we are a strong supporter of Israel. But when you read some of the stories out, you read from Peter Baker at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " about a call in February where Biden said to Netanyahu, we might have to reconsider our support for the war. You read in the pages of "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " this week, the headline, U.S. offers Israel intelligence an effort to avoid Rafah invasion. The intelligence was apparently sensitive intelligence, and you wonder if we are bartering with intelligence that should go to our ally. You look at all of these things, and perhaps pausing support if there is a Rafah invasion. To what degree we don't know, we really don't know --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just analyze that for a second, Charles. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " about the intelligence, the U.S. is "offering Israel valuable assistance in an effort to persuade it to hold back, including sensitive intelligence to help the Israeli military pinpoint the location of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  leaders and find the group's hidden tunnels." Why wouldn't we be giving that information at the beginning?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It does not make sense why we would have held it back in the first place. And listen, I'm sure Israel appreciates it, but this is something that they have been doing for a long time. We get back to -- I think we've drifted a long ways from October 7th and the atrocities that happened at that particular moment. And the death toll is sad. But when the Biden administration Israel come up with a formula for how they're not going to hit civilians, that should be a demand nor \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , not Israel. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  -- we should demand from \uc2 Hamas\uc2  how you plan to keep civilians safe, or we could cut to the chase, have these people give themselves up, because, to your point, Israel is going to win this. They are determined to win this because what happened to them was the worst thing that happened to Jewish people on this planet since Adolf Hitler.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I wanted to save time for this one soundbite. This is the vice president, this conference today. And we haven't heard from her a lot. Here she is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have to know that sometimes people will open the door for you and leave it open. Sometimes they won't. And then you need to kick that -- door down.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Excuse my language.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dropped the f-bomb there at that conference, Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I don't know -- I haven't seen that. I don't know what that means. But I know this. If someone killed 45,000 Americans, it would be hard- pressed to tell me as an American to tell my government to not to do everything that you can to find our hostages and to show those people and ensure those people don't do it again. So that's why I stand with Israel in this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You don't want to comment about the vice president?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll leave it there, we'll leave it there. I got it, I got it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I found it exhilarating, exhausting, inspiring. I found the short putts really difficult because I was pretty shaky on the greens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: First time in five years the speed golf pairs championship returned to England. Competitors tried to shoot the lowest score in the shortest amount of time, with both numbers used to determine the total result. Golfers Luke Bone and Luke Willett played 18 holes in what we believe is a world record time of 37 minutes. Wow.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we talk polling and the state of the presidential race with left and right. We're doing it fair, balanced, and unafraid. Thanks for watching. Here's Laura.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240514ek5d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Chaos Erupts As 2024 Election Nears\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Pete Hegseth, Charlie Hurt, Jeanine Pirro, Jason  Chaffetz, Jimmy Failla \par \uc2 7,623 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEW HOST: And welcome to "Hannity."\par \par \uc2 And tonight, springtime is in full gear across the country and that means two things. The weather is warm and the country's lunatics, they are out and about. Now, some are live action roles, you know, playing as terrorists and riding for a cause they know little to nothing about. Others, they're brawling on airplanes for no apparent reason, can't make it up. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO CLIP PLAYS)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We have a lot more of that tape. Insanity in the air.\par \par \uc2 But that's not all. We even have more bad behavior this week inside a New York City courtroom, yeah, we're porn star Stormy Daniels horrified jurors with immaterial and graphic details, weird jokes, and a story about seeing dead people.\par \par \uc2 Another witness who has just to testify is also on his worst behavior, soliciting, let's see, gifts on TikTok while wearing a shirt depicting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  behind bars. Can't make it up. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP ATTORNEY: Smash those hearts, smash the blue hearts, and so on, the like button, all that crap. Do that just so that we can show MAGA, I say this every single night, it's funny because it's generally the same (EXPLETIVE DELETED) trolls.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Judge Jeanine was in the courtroom today and we'll join us with more from Biden donor, of course, Juan Merchan's kangaroo courtroom. That's straight ahead tonight.\par \par \uc2 But, first, well, weak knees at the DNC, get this, your Democratic Party, full of radicals, they're so terrified of their own members, according to a report from "Politico," quote, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's top advisors are worried the Democratic convention could erupt in protest so they're now pushing to move it semi online.\par \par \uc2 What? An online convention in 2024, that's kind of pathetic, don't you think? Think of the slogan here, the Democratic Party, where our core voters are s