ts Israel to Gaza. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has claimed responsibility for the attack that injured seven people, including one person who is in critical condition. That crossing, which is critical in getting humanitarian aid into Gaza is now closed -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Jeff Paul reporting for us from Israel -- Jeff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, GOP Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , ranking member of the Senate Intel Committee.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to FOX News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So let's start there. It sounds like this hostage negotiation deal is falling apart. Secretary Blinken said \uc2 Hamas\uc2  just needs to say yes, and not only does it not sound like they're saying yes, they've got this shelling this morning at an aid crossing that is going to shut it down.\par \par \uc2 You have been there in recent days with the prime minister. They say they're moving in on Rafah. Where do we go from here?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, it's their war and they have a right to fight it. These are degenerate savages, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  -- I mean, just think about the report you -- that I just heard a second ago, okay? Everybody's saying, send in humanitarian aid, these poor people are starving.\par \par \uc2 Well, that's what we -- they tried to do and these guys are launching mortar attacks against the humanitarian aid that's coming in.\par \par \uc2 You know, that a lot of the humanitarian aid that's getting in, and Israel's allowing it in anyways because they know they need to do it, but \uc2 Hamas\uc2  -- this stuff's being sold in the marketplace.\par \par \uc2 This administration -- this is Israel's war. We should basically say, we are on the side of the people who were the victims on October 7th, of a bunch of savages and lunatics who came across that border and massacred and killed and kidnapped and raped and took hostages back with them. These people need to be defeated.\par \par \uc2 How is Israel supposed to coexist to live side by side, alongside next to these people?\par \par \uc2 And when they talk about displaced people, there are 80,000 Israelis right now that are displaced from the north. They have to live -- I saw them. They are living in hotels, their kids are going to school online, in conference rooms at hotels. They can't go north because \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is attacking them in north -- in northern Israel.\par \par \uc2 So this is a time where we need to have not a weak and feeble president, but someone who makes it very clear that we are on Israel's side. They are the good guys in this fight and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  are the evil ones. And these terrible civilian casualties that are happening is because \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is using them as human shields.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and I think that a lot of people as we watch these campus protests and demonstrations spread would agree that nobody wants civilians to be killed in this process, but in many of those cases, clearly, they have crossed the line to actually threatening fellow students, those kinds of things. We're hearing now from some international students who are speaking up saying they worry that because they may be suspended or disciplined, it could cost them their student visa to be here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that's going too far? Do you think that's an appropriate response?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- or he hasn't done it again because he's weak -- he's weak and he's feeble, but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at very early on should have said as I asked him to do back in October, if you're in the streets of the United States, you're here as a visitor, on a visa, okay? You're not an American. You're here on a visa, and you're here to teach or you're here to go to school.\par \par \uc2 And you're out there chanting on behalf of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and these terrorist groups, who, by the way, also hate America, we should revoke your visa. If you are here in this country as a visitor on a visa, and you are defacing statues on George Washington, ripping down American flags, putting up Palestinian flags, you should have your visa revoked and eliminated.\par \par \uc2 And I -- and he should have been very clear about whose side we're on from the very beginning. But there -- look, there's a bunch of Democratic donors, major Democratic donors who are behind the groups that are funding all this.\par \par \uc2 I saw the other day, I think it was in UCLA, there's thousands of dollars of plywood that they were using as barricades against the police. They're committing acts of violence, not just acts of antisemitism, acts of violence, of vandalism, threatening people.\par \par \uc2 I saw another video of a Jewish student who had to get police, had to basically shame the police into escorting him so that he could go into an area of the university where he had every right to be because of these zombies, these antisemitic left-wing nutcases that the president should have been very firm about.\par \par \uc2 And that's why everything in America is in chaos, from our border to our campuses, total chaos because we have a weak and we have a feeble president who cannot communicate and refuses to take a strong line on this because he's afraid of people voting against him in November who support these crazy ideas.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, do you give him some credit for the speech this week? He clearly denounced what has crossed the line, saying, listen, we got to have free speech protections, we have a First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 He did come out and make stronger remarks than that. But at the same time, we haven't seen that we know of action by the DOJ.\par \par \uc2 Are you surprised there aren't some civil rights investigations? Maybe they've been launched, but certainly not publicly.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, he gave a short speech off of note cards seven days into this crisis. This -- we should have heard from him from the very beginning. Look, these are students from all over the country. Think about this, the people who -- you know who's not getting any coverage right now, the kids who paid $80,000 a year to go to these universities who can't take finals, can't use the library, can't go to class, live -- many of whom have dropped out of school, who's fighting for them?\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should have spoken about them very early in this process. His failure to address this early on and to make very clear who side he was on has just fed this, has just increased this. He should have been very clear and defined himself early on about this and they're not doing anything about it as far as I can see.\par \par \uc2 I mean, where -- where are the -- I want to see if they put as much resource and time into investigating these people as they've investigated other people in the last couple years.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we know the Department of Education has launched some investigations. We'll talk about that a little bit later in the show.\par \par \uc2 But I want to get to a couple of other domestic issues with you. The issue of abortion has been a very difficult one for Republicans. You seem to be of the mindset that you need to embrace it and try to control the conversation in a better way and actually talk about it, not run from it.\par \par \uc2 Democrats though feel like it's a -- it's a great case to make against you.\par \par \uc2 Here's the vice president in Florida days ago when the six-week ban in Florida went into effect.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Since Roe was overturned, every time reproductive freedom has been on the ballot, the people of America voted for freedom.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now, the Florida Supreme Court has said an abortion measure can be on the Florida ballot there in November. How worried are you about that motivating the base?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, what I'm more worried about is that no one ever asked the Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  or any Democrat what restrictions do they support. Which abortion should not be allowed? They never tell you.\par \par \uc2 Should someone be allowed to do an abortion on the day of birth, after birth, nine months? Should pass the point of viability? Well, they will never tell you which abortions should not be allowed, okay?\par \par \uc2 They're always demanding to know what exceptions do you support, but they never tell you which restrictions they would support. They are extremists. They believe abortion should be allowed at any time for any reason basically and paid for by taxpayers. No one ever asked \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  that. No one ever asked the Democrats that.\par \par \uc2 So they should -- they should be -- someone should start asking them what their position is on that, and then we can sort of define and decide, all right, what are the policy options here.\par \par \uc2 I don't -- I've never viewed abortion as a political issue. I believe it is a moral issue. It is the conflict between two rights, a woman's right to choose and an unborn human being's right to live.\par \par \uc2 And I err on the side of life because without life, none of the other rights matter. There's no right to speech. There's no right to vote. There's no right to do anything if you're not alive.\par \par \uc2 And we have to have laws that define how we protect human life. But these people will never tell you what the law -- what abortion should not be allowed. They are extremists. They are radical extremists.\par \par \uc2 And it is journalistic malpractice for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to repeatedly be allowed to do interviews and never be asked -- \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , what abortions should not be allowed in America? At which point is it too far and too bad? Never answers that, never.\par \par \uc2 She's an extremist. They're extremists.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I will say for Democrats who are willing to come on the show and have, we do have that conversation with them and I agree with you, it seems like they also have failed to coalesce around a message for where their limits are in many cases.\par \par \uc2 Before you go, though, you're in Florida. You're spending time with President Trump. Everyone says you're on his VP short list, but that there may be a technical glitch with having two people from the same state when it comes down to an Electoral College vote.\par \par \uc2 Would you leave the state of Florida or change your residency if you were asked to join the ticket?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, first of all, the vice-presidential choice for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be made by one person, and that's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and all this other stuff is just speculation. And I get it, you know, political reporters have to cover political topics. The primary's over, the general's six months away, so they're going to speculate on the VP thing.\par \par \uc2 Here's the good news, and I saw it this weekend. The amount of talent that we have in the Republican Party is extraordinary. He doesn't just have a bunch of choices for VP, he has a bunch of choices for cabinet. He's going to have an extraordinary group of talented people that can serve this country in multiple roles, and that's a decision he's going to have to make.\par \par \uc2 And I would just say, forget leaving me aside for a moment. I think that before anyone decides to move from their state, you better make sure you move to a state where there's not some DA that makes a career after going after Republicans. Because what we're seeing all over the country right now is the weaponization of our criminal justice system, not just to go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which is now well documented, right?\par \par \uc2 You have a Biden-supporting judge, a Biden prosecutor in the most liberal county in America on ridiculous charges, and the rest of the world is watching that, saying, you know, this is the stuff that America used to sanction other countries for doing --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: -- what's happening here in America. But I think that that's something that we need to be focused on, because I do think that has an impact on people's thinking about getting into politics.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, if you do move, not to New York. That won't be your choice. We got that. But -- but if you decide --\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Yeah. That's a pretty good -- that's a pretty good guess on that one, that's for sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: If you decide that you have anything to tell us about that, or if you, you know, want to consider your residency elsewhere, let us know. Senator, thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, joining me now from the House, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2 , Ranking Member of the House Intel Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, great to have you with us. I want to start where I did with the senator on what's unfolding in Israel. I mean, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  taking credit for shelling that has caused Israeli casualties, closing an aid crossing. Was that the whole purpose? I mean, it doesn't sound like they are reliable negotiators or, you know, good faith partners in any of this trying to get the hostages home.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM HIMES (D-CT): Yeah, well, of course, they're not good faith negotiators. They're terrorists, right? And -- and, you know, they will do whatever they need to do to advance their own interests.\par \par \uc2 Now, in any negotiation, particularly a negotiation like this, you know, people have interests, even if they are terrorists. And, obviously, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has an interest in ceasing, at least temporarily, the obliteration of their military capability. So, you know, you never -- you never sound too optimistic about any negotiation in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, if we can get, whatever it is, a temporary break in the fighting so that the hostages can be released -- that's the thing that never gets mentioned here -- that, I think, would be a good step in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 And, Shannon, if you'll give me just one minute, you know, I work very closely with \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 . We're both on the Gang of Eight. I don't know that I've ever seen him quite as over-caffeinated this morning. But he said that you never asked Democrats on the topic of abortion where they should be baloney, malarkey.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, as you know, prior to the Dobbs decision in the Supreme Court, Democrats in most of America supported the concept of Roe v. Wade. And the concept was that you could have an abortion up to the point of viability. And states could decide, you know, what that was.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, again, I'm not quite sure if Marco just had too much coffee this morning. But, you know, the notion that we're extremists on this topic is just madness.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But I have asked a number of Democrats, because there are multiple states in this country that do allow abortion until a due date. That's factual. And I've asked Democrats whether they're OK with that. And many of them have had a very hard time answering that question for me.\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Yeah, but, Shannon, here's the thing, right? You know, if an abortion occurs prior to natural birth, it doesn't occur because mom says, oh, I changed my mind. It occurs because there is the rarest, the absolute rarest of medical events that puts the life of the mother in danger or the life of the child in danger.\par \par \uc2 You know, to have \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  go on national television and say that Democrats are extremists that believe you should be able to kill a child, I mean, frankly, that's what's wrong with our politics. And you know that. And I know that.\par \par \uc2 You know, again, those situations where a mother has carried a baby to term and something happens in the last week, that is the most horrendous of situations. And if a doctor says you're going to die if you deliver that, that is the most horrendous and, you know, personal of situations. But it's also vanishingly rare.\par \par \uc2 And you know and I know, based on what the people of Kansas did in their plebiscite about -- about abortion, that in this country, most Americans believe, as most Democrats believe that, you know, this is a decision up to the point of viability that should be made by a woman and her doctor, not by a bunch of guys wearing ties and suits in national legislatures.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I will say the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which has been aligned with \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , so clearly not a pro-life organization. They document this and say there are actually thousands of late term abortions, third term or third trimester abortions every year. And so people do have to have a conversation about the fact that that does happen.\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Yeah, that's right. And again, most of those late abortions are medically related. I mean, if -- if -- if you're -- if the idea is that there are women out there who carry a child for seven months and, you know, needless to say, I have not carried a child. But if you've carried a child for seven or eight months and the idea is that lots of women then say, oh, guess what? I don't want to do this. That's just not what happens. And OK, I don't -- I haven't actually looked at the Guttmacher numbers, thousands.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, as you know, this is a country of some 330-340 million people. So, again, all I'm saying is that, you know, for \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  to say that nobody ever asks people, Democrats in particular, where they are on abortion, nonsense.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are generally where the American people are, which is where the world was before the Dobbs decision that until the point of viability, this is a decision to be made between a woman and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll see, because states are now hammering this out. There is a patchwork that is very, very diverse across this country. But let me get back to the Middle East, because I want to make sure that we get you on this.\par \par \uc2 There are a number of senators who are now worried about the fact that the administration seems to be, according to numerous reports, the -- considering the idea of bringing people here from Gaza. A number of senators who are worried about the guardrails on that have written to the president saying this, "With more than a third of Gazans supporting the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants, we are not confident that your administration can adequately vet this high-risk population for terrorist ties and sympathies before admitting them to the U.S."\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes on the heels of an inspector general report from DHS saying they really blew it repeatedly with Afghan vets -- excuse me, vetting Afghan refugees, saying this, "DHS may have admitted or paroled individuals into the U.S. who pose a risk to national security and the safety of local communities. They admit there was a lot of trouble with doing this with the Afghan refugees." Are you confident you can tell the American people that people coming from Gaza will be appropriately vetted before they take up residence here?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Well, what I -- what I can tell you is that this is not a thing, right? The only way that people can apply for refugee status is if they go where they are, where they are, and begin a very lengthy process of vetting. And then once they're vetting and vetted in whatever location and they are, they are further vetted by DHS and the federal government and others, and then they are admitted.\par \par \uc2 And while, you know, a number of Republican senators and over-caffeinated Florida senators may want to be selling an awful lot of fear right now, I would challenge you to come up