c2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Are you ready to choose freedom over democracy for democracy? Imagine what we can do next. Four more years. Pause.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Four more years. Four more years. Four more years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Four more years. Pause.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He did not say pause.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, I mean, every moment is scripted. Every moment is a disaster. And he may have very well risked his entire election in that moment. It's the cascade of them every week, every day. The reinforcements that he said they can't read.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, no. If Trump said pause, it would be every late-night show. It would be every comedian, every tedious commentator on The View, oh, he said pause. But they treat it like he's talking, you know, at a dog charity event or something. Pause. W-A-S.\par \par \uc2 All right. Biden hosted -- Raymond --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: -- got four more seconds. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Biden hosted a big abortion rally in Tampa, and he was, you know, messy as ever.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Pick him up on an election day. Call. Do you need a ride to the polls? Don't mess with the movement of America. Let's get it done.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Besides, I used to drive an 18-wheeler.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, he never did that. He did not drive an 18-wheeler.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wait a second.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Biden's been in politics for 30 years, Laura. And now he's a civil rights activist. He was at the top of his class. He got three degrees. He was raised in the Puerto Rican community. And now we have Trucker Biden. He's the Tom Ripley of politics.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, wait a second. He also did that thing again where he turned and did that weird robotic thing, like that old Michael Jackson, the robot.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The ghost handshake. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He's still waiting. He's still waiting for someone to show up. All right. One moment was actually gross, really gross during that abortion event, Raymond. Now, this is failed Florida candidate for governor, \uc2 Nikki Fried\uc2 . Watch Biden as she discusses abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: And then we come back here to state of Florida where \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  felt like he needed to run for president. And so, 15 weeks wasn't good enough. We had to go to six weeks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, this is blasphemous and so insidious. I mean, to invoke the cross and Jesus Christ while we're talking about the extermination of innocent children. I mean, no matter where you fall on the abortion debate, to bring religion into it in this way, when you've got a Catholic there who's been defying his church for years, supporting abortion on demand when his church clearly says you can't do that as a Catholic in good standing. This is particularly odious, I have to say. And I was amazed.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He is an ardent. He is an ardent practicing Catholic, Raymond, just like \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . So just get off their backs.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Raymond. All right, that's it for us tonight. Tomorrow, I'm going to be on with Martha McCallum on The Story, so tune in for that. Around 3 o'clock Eastern. And make sure to follow me on social media. Of course, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and X. All the comings and goings of the Angle there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching. Remember, it is America, sometimes it doesn't seem so, but it's America now and forever. Jesse Watters, he takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240425ek4o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Protesters Clash With Police At Universities Nationwide; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Signs Foreign Aid Bill Into Law; Supreme Court Debates Competing State And Federal Laws On Abortion Access; $95 Billion Foreign Aid Supplemental For Ukraine, Israel And Taiwan; Congress Authorizes $61 Billion; Zelenskyy Assured Of Support Despite Election Outcome; Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  (R-MO) Pushes For More Testing Of Missouri Towns Possibly Affected By Nuclear Waste From Manhattan Project; Gang Member On Parole Facing Charges Of Shooting Los Angeles County Sheriff's Deputy; House Speaker Mike Johnson Visits \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  To Denounces Student Protestors Of Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  War; Social Media Company TikTok Facing Possible Ban From \uc2 U.S. Congress\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Brit Hume, David Spunt, Rich Edson, Steve Harrigan,  Greg Palkot, Bret Baier, Bill Melugin, Mollie Hemingway \par \uc2 7,772 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Tyrus Live Comedy Tour in states all over the country.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Where are you going to be on the 27th of September?\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: I will be where I'm at. I'd be where I'd be and that's all you need to know. In cities everywhere.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: That's fantastic.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Judge, do you have a bird call? You could do a bird call.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No. An owl?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Shannon Bream in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, as anti-Israel protests continue to spread to college campuses across the nation, the speaker of the House expressed his support to Jewish students at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 . Officials there say they're making progress in conversations with protest leaders.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan is on the Columbia campus for us tonight. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Shannon. The Speaker of the House warned the protesters here today that calling out the National Guard was a real possibility.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): Neither Israel nor these Jewish students on this campus will ever stand alone.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): House Speaker Mike Johnson met with Jewish students at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , adding to the growing calls for university president Minouche Shafik to step down.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: We just can't allow this kind of hatred and anti-Semitism to flourish on our campuses. And it must be stopped in its tracks.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): The clock is ticking as Tuesday's midnight deadline to dismantle the tents on campus came and went. The university says it's making progress and extended talks with the student negotiators for another 48 hours while being vague about what will happen if they don't comply.\par \par \uc2 For now, students agreed to remove some tents, kick out nonstudents and eliminate hate speech.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Israel, go to hell.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Some Jewish students and professors say the university's president is still not doing enough.\par \par \uc2 SHAI DAVIDAI, COLUMBIA BUSINESS SCHOOL PROFESSOR: You know who the only people not on the negotiation table? The Jews. She's negotiating with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  supporters with Islamic Jihad supporters.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Tensions are growing across the country, with clashes at the \uc2 University of Texas\uc2  and Austin, and the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2  and more schools joining in.\par \par \uc2 Fox News research shows that more than half of the nation's top 50 universities had protests in the past week. Students at Princeton are said to be planning an encampment like the one at Columbia violating school policy.\par \par \uc2 And while some members of Congress support the protesters.\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR (D-MN): I am incredibly moved by your courage and bravery.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Others want to revoke federal funding international student visas if their terms are violated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): The speaker told the protesters that they were being endorsed by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and that they should stop wasting their parent's money. The students responded with a chant. Mike, you suck. Shannon, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we will talk about that and more with the panel coming up. Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are pressuring President Biden for some kind of action concerning these anti-Israel protests. At the same time, President Biden signs a foreign aid bill sending billions of dollars to Israel, Ukraine and Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson is at the White House tonight. Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, good evening, Shannon. Shortly after signing that bill at the White House, President Biden said it was a good day for the United States, for Europe and for world peace, as other events in the U.S. are also drawing international attention.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My commitment to Israel, I want to make clear again, is ironclad. The security of Israel is critical.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): President Biden today signed a foreign aid bill including more than $26 billion in funding for Israel. As critics say he isn't doing enough to address campus protests spreading across the country with some lauding and parroting terrorist organizations.\par \par \uc2 While the president made no mention today of those demonstrations, the White House says officials will forcefully call out anti-Semitism.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Protests must be peaceful. You know, students must be safe. When we see violent rhetoric, we have to call that out. When we see physical intimidation or grotesque anti-Semitic remarks, we have to speak that out.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): Tomorrow, President Biden will be in the New York area with no campus visits on his scheduled. While at Columbia today, House Speaker Mike Johnson and several other Republicans pressed the administration to revoke federal funding for schools that failed to control threats towards Jewish students.\par \par \uc2 Johnson suggested that if the anti-Semitic demonstrations continue, there couldn't be a role for the National Guard. And he said he'll tell the president that directly.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: My intention is to call President Biden after we leave here and share with him what we have seen with our own two eyes and demand that he take action.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): These protests are also gaining international attention, especially in Israel, where Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  called the anti-Semitism on college campuses in the United States, "horrific."\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: Anti-Semitic mobs have taken over leading universities. It has to be condemned and condemned unequivocally. But that's not what happened. The response of several university presidents was shameful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Next month, President Biden is scheduled to speak at Morehouse College's commencement. Some faculty, students and alumni are calling on the historically black college to rescind that invitation over the president support of Israel. White House says that President Biden is looking forward to celebrating with graduates next month, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Rich Edson at the White House. Thank you, Rich.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justices are considering arguments heard today about whether state abortion restrictions can be invalidated by federal law, mandating emergency care which the Biden administration argues may include abortion.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has details from outside the court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Today's case originates in Idaho, but could have wide ranging implications for states with strict abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 At issue now, whether Idaho's near total abortion ban is at odds with a federal law protecting patients in emergency situations, including pregnant women. The Biden administration sued Idaho arguing abortions may need to be performed when the woman's life and health are at stake. The justices divided with some questioning the federal government's power.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE SAMUEL ALITO, SUPREME COURT: How can you impose restrictions on what Idaho can criminalize, simply because hospitals in Idaho have chosen to participate in Medicare?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): But the court's liberal justices questioned the state's authority.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE ELENA KAGAN, SUPREME COURT: It's not all the state's way. Within these rare cases, there's a significant number where the woman is -- her life is not imperiled, but she's going to lose her reproductive organs. She's going to lose the ability to have children in the future. Unless an abortion takes place.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Idaho's law only provides an abortion exception to save a woman's life. The Justice Department argues the 1986 law ensures patients get appropriate stabilizing treatment in emergency situations, including abortions if necessary.\par \par \uc2 Today's arguments highlight a divisive social issue playing out on the campaign trail. The justices held separate arguments last month over nationwide access to the abortion drug mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Shannon, decisions in both cases will come from the Supreme Court by the end of June setting up an intense few months before the election, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and David of course, the court here's another very high stakes case tomorrow, the last one of this term. Tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: It's a big one, it deals with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and whether or not he should be immune from federal prosecution.\par \par \uc2 The former president says he should be immune, he was charged by Special Counsel Jack Smith for allegedly trying to overturn the 2020 election results. He claims the alleged acts happened within his official role as president and he can't be charged.\par \par \uc2 Special Counsel Smith says Trump's behavior continued into his post presidency. This trial originally was supposed to happen in March. It's been delayed because of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And depending on what the Supreme Court does, we can have a trial later this summer, perhaps August, September, or if they rule in Trump's favor, we may not have one at all, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, we will cover that trial start to finish tomorrow. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, three Republicans joined all 29 of the Democrats in the Arizona House of Representatives to pass a measure to repeal the state's near total ban on abortions. That bill now goes to the State Senate.\par \par \uc2 The ban is based on a 19th century law that was recently upheld by the state's Supreme Court and allows abortions only in cases when the mother's life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): It's not every day, you can say you made the world truly a better place. But I think the Senate can say that tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I know that history is going to judge this well. It was the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): It's not a whole lot of money for us, but it is very significant step for them.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They don't always agree. But when it matters most, they stepped up and did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, let's bring in Fox News Chief Political Analyst Brit Hume to talk about this now. New massive foreign aid package that has been signed and is on its way.\par \par \uc2 Brit, good to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. There are people in Washington who don't love the way that this was passed on both the House and Senate side. But it is a done deal. It now flows to numerous foreign aid policies and priorities for this administration.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, that's true, Shannon, and yes, there's a lot of complaining about it, particularly among some Republicans in the House. But let's remember this, these measures passed the House and Senate with very strong bipartisan majorities, which ought to be something that I think people will look at and say, well, what do you know, it's an election year that these parties are each other's throats. And we have an outbreak of bipartisanship on major foreign -- on major foreign policy issue, the funding of support for Israel, Ukraine, and Taiwan. That's a very big deal.\par \par \uc2 And let's remember this, Shannon, the hallmark of American foreign policy post World War II, all through the Cold War was continuity. Yes, there were disagreements here and there about whether we should do this or that.\par \par \uc2 But over the long haul, it was a bipartisan consensus, that containing communism, and restraining its spread was the way to go. And in the end, that policy held and prevailed.\par \par \uc2 I'm not going to start predicting that we're in the land of milk and honey now with the partisanship as heavy as it is. But this was a big moment. And I think it may have had the effect of enlarging the speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 There he was, you know, involved in this and getting the bills through the House with difficulty. And there he was again today at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , making a far stronger statement against the protests that have broken out in caused all these trouble with these campuses, far stronger statement than President Biden has made. So, something's happening here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Brit, as you mentioned, these measures to get through the House, it was greatly bipartisan. I mean, the votes were almost evenly split on some of these aid packages, but that's where the far left of the speaker's party is unhappy. Here's Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , who keeps you know jostling that sabre about possibly voting him out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): Speaker Johnson refuses to use his power as speaker of the House, to do any type of negotiating to secure the southern border.\par \par \uc2 He needs to do the right thing to resign and allow us to move forward in a controlled process. If he doesn't do so, he will be vacated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, the far right of his party is making this argument. Does it cost him Brit, or do we as we hear -- are hearing some rumblings from inside Trump world that the former president wants this to calm down, cool it and to present a more united front?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, remember, he stood side by side with Johnson down at Mar-a-Lago recently and suggested that he supports the Speaker now that you know, you can never count on that lasting any longer than Trump's mood.\par \par \uc2 But at the moment, that's where it seems to be. And I would also add this, Democrats are glad that these measures passed the House.\par \par \uc2 And they are to some extent, at least grateful to Mike Johnson for bringing about and there's at least a distinct possibility, if not, you know, I wouldn't bet on it necessarily. But there's at least a possibility that if \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and her little crew tried to oust him, that he will be saved, his speakership would be saved with the help of Democratic votes, it will have a distinct majority of Republican votes to keep him there.\par \par \uc2 And what these -- what these Republicans who are complaining that he's working with Democrats are trying to do is counting on the Democrats to help them oust him.\par \par \uc2 I think number of Democrats may not play ball with that. And he may not -- and if he survives this, I think he'll be strengthened, I think indeed he may already be strengthened.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, I think you're right about the Democrats. We need them publicly and privately, frankly, have told me too, that they would vote to save him. So, we'll see how that plays out. Brit Hume, always great to have you.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Stocks were mixed today. The Dow lost 43, the S&P 500 was up one, NASDAQ gained 16.\par \par \uc2 Up next, an exclusive interview with Ukraine's president following the passage of that huge aid bill by Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: I want to thank you for such significant support. A very important support to President Biden, to Congress, to Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Hospital authorities in Gaza say an Israeli airstrike late Tuesday hit a house in the city of Rafah, killing at least, three people.\par \par \uc2 Video shows mourners sitting by the bodies wrapped in bags before prayer rituals were performed by dozens of men.\par \par \uc2 The strike happened as the Israeli military signals it plans an offensive on the city, where hundreds of thousands of people have taken refuge during the war.\par \par \uc2 Israel says it does plan to evacuate civilians from Rafah during the anticipated offensive.\par \par \uc2 Russia has vetoed a \uc2 U.N.\uc2  resolution sponsored by the U.S. and Japan, calling on all nations to prevent a dangerous nuclear arms race in outer space.\par \par \uc2 The resolution calls on all countries not to develop or deploy nuclear arms or other weapons of mass destruction in space. Those weapons are banned under a 1967 international treaty that included the U.S. and Russia.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says he will work with whoever wins the November U.S. presidential election. He talks exclusively today with Fox News Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot about the state of the war with Russia, and the new aid, just authorized by Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): As the war rages in Ukraine, the embattled people here were breathing a sigh of relief, after six months on hold, Congress and President Biden, OK'd a foreign aid bill that would deliver $61 billion to the Ukrainian military and its fight with Russia.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Come. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translator): Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): No one was more relieved than Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , who spoke with Fox News soon after the Senate acted.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: The moment when we waited for this, six-month decisions -- $61 billion, hopes to -- I'm thankful to people of America. What I wanted to say, I am thankful, not the first time, and I hope that you and the Americans will feel the result of their help. A result to save Ukraine and Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): The hitch now is getting the weaponry to the troops in Ukraine, who have been outgunned and who have lost ground to the Russians.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon says some could be sent soon from nearby Germany.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: God bless, we will get it as quick as possible.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Is that important that you get the weaponry, these air defense, the long-range missiles as fast as possible?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: As fast as possible, otherwise, they will push us back. Our people know about it, and Americans have to know about it.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): Zelenskyy is not only fighting Russia, but also fighting for attention. He watched as the U.S. and other allies pitched in to defend Israel, fending off an attack from Iran.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Do you wished all those planes were helping you here too?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: We saw it. And that's why I think it's as -- if we want to be real allies, Ukraine and Western countries, they have to show the same on our territory.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Zelenskyy also monitors American politics, not just Republican lawmakers raising questions, but a possible new president who could take a harder line on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Would you be able to work with President Trump if he is elected? And is president next year?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Of course, we will. Of course, we will work with the United States anywhere, because it's our strategic partner and the leader lead on -- in the world.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): This, as the military and civilian casualties' mount.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: How do you help to keep the morale, the spirit of the country up during these very difficult times?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: That is the most difficult question. For me. It's, you know, the question without real answer.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice over): In the end, though, with the war already two years and two months in, the question remains, whether Ukraine can beat Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his quest for domination.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Will you achieve victory?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: We have to. We don't have alternative. I don't know the kind of this victory. I don't know the country today. I don't know how it will be. I'm not sure that everybody will be happy. But I know that we don't have any alternative. That's why we have to win, and we will.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): That's why we have to wins, Zelenskyy said, and we will. A very emotional Ukrainian president, determined, and a bit tired. It's been a long war. There is more ahead, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Certainly is. Greg Palkot, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Bret Baier, with a look at expiring legislation affecting people who were sickened during the effort to build the first atomic bomb.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, Australian police arrests seven teenagers accused of following a violent extremist ideology in raids all across Sydney.\par \par \uc2 A judge has extended a ban on social media platform X, sharing video of a knife attack on a bishop that led to this criminal investigation. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  arrives in Shanghai to begin a critical trip to China. He laid it just hours after the Senate passed long stalled legislation to send aid to Taiwan, Israel, and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The trip comes following a conversation between presidents Biden and Xi, and a visit by Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Chiba, Japan. One of the big stories there tonight. Japan's first moon lander survives a third weeks long freezing lunar night.\par \par \uc2 The country's space agency says it's gotten an image from the device, three months after it landed on the moon.\par \par \uc2 Temperatures can fall to minus 274 degrees Fahrenheit during a lunar night.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: New Jersey Democratic Congressman \uc2 Donald Payne Jr.\uc2  has died at age 65. The sixth-term representative suffered from heart problems for years. New Jersey Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  called Payne a steadfast champion for the people of the state.\par \par \uc2 With his passing now, the House balance of power sits at 217 Republicans and 212 Democrats with six vacancies.\par \par \uc2 The Port of Baltimore is preparing to open a deeper channel for commercial ships to get in and out for a few days. Thursday's move will be a significant step toward reopening the major maritime shipping hub. It's been closed to most traffic since a cargo ship hit the Francis Scott Key Bridge last month, causing the bridge to collapse. The new channel will be 35 feet deep allowing five of the seven cargo ships that have been stuck in the port to leave.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we begin a new series looking at toxic towns in the U.S. In Missouri, residents and lawmakers are pushing for Speaker Mike Johnson to allow a vote on a bill that compensates anyone with cancer or other illnesses as a result of the Manhattan Project. Although the U.S. dropped the first atomic bomb almost 80 years ago, my colleague Bret Baier examines why in some parts of the country community members are still fighting to get nuclear waste fully cleaned up.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 The Radiation Exposure Compensation Act was enacted in 1990. The law provides payments for anyone who may have contracted certain cancers and serious illnesses as a result of the effort to build the first atomic bomb. It is set to expire this year. Some lawmakers are pushing to renew the legislation and expand it as mounting evidence shows locations beyond the test sites may have been exposing residents to toxic chemicals for decades.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: From 1945 to 1962, the U.S. conducted nearly 200 atmospheric nuclear weapons tests. Thousands of workers were hired to mine for uranium throughout the west.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: While some search for uranium, others work with it but stay a safe distance away from it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: While others were essential in developing the weapons at secret sites.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The greatest of war secrets on which thousands of these Americans work.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Radiation Exposure Compensation Act would eventually cover these workers and individuals who lived near test sites if they developed cancer or other serious illnesses.\par \par \uc2 What changes are in your legislation?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2 , (R-MO): Bret, what my legislation would do is number one reauthorize this program that for years has provided life-saving compensation for folks who the federal government has poisoned.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One place excluded from the act -- Missouri. Now its senator, \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , is working to change that. He wants parts of his state and other locations to be included in a revamped bill.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: The victims have never been compensated by the government for the exposure the government put them through.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So why is Missouri left out and other states left out?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Well, it was originally written, this program, back in 1990. And a lot of it, Bret, was back then we didn't know the extent of the government's nuclear processing or radiation tests. A lot of it was still veto.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Karen Nickel grew up in north St. Louis County. She says she and her family suffered from several illnesses.\par \par \uc2 KAREN NICKEL, JUST MOMS STL: I have lupus and I also have psoriatic arthritis and some other autoimmune disease.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Missouri State Democratic Representative Chantelle Nickson-Clark also grew up near Nickel. She is a two-time breast cancer survivor and comes from a family with a history of cancers.\par \par \uc2 CHANTELLE NICKSON-CLARK, (D) MISSOURI STATE HOUSE: To be diagnosed with two different types cancers definitely has been a disappointment in my life.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Both believe a nearby creek could be part of the high rates of cancer and illness in the region.\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: Lupus is an environmental trigger, they do know that, connected to some radiation.\par \par \uc2 NICKSON-CLARK: As data reflects there's nothing concrete coming from a family where cancer just runs all through my family. We believe that that is a contributing factor.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  says so, too. A 2019 health assessment determined exposure could have resulted in elevated risks for developing certain cancers.\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: This is no small creek. This is more like a little river.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of residents there are saying more testing should be done. Where are you on this?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: More testing needs to be done. The residents are absolutely correct.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Before the danger was determined, the creek was a frequent playground for locals.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Back in the 1950s, folks went out and built homes there. It was a nice suburban area. It's still a beautiful area.\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: There were a lot of children in the neighborhood. And kids spent day -- sunlight to sunset in the park there and playing in the creek.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Several decades later, Nickel says a shocking number of her friends and classmates have suffered from various illnesses.\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: It's just person after person after person that have passed away from rare cancers and whatnot. The amount of illnesses and immune diseases, it's astronomical.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Karen met her neighbor, Dawn Chapman, as they both were trying to learn more about the radioactive waste in their neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 DAWN CHAPMAN, JUST MOMS STL: Unlike Karen, I didn't grow up next to this. It was staggering to see how many people were ill, and then, again, be able to look back in my community where I grew up, there is none of that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chapman and Nickel now both live near a landfill where an underground fire has been burning for more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 CHAPMAN: The longer this fire burns, the more unstable the ground becomes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chapman first learned about the fire at West Lake Landfill in 2013.\par \par \uc2 CHAPMAN: I had been smelling something for a little while, kind of like when you cut an onion. That's what our eyes were doing. That's what it felt like, like this very intense burning that was like, oh, my God, what's happening to me?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She called the \uc2 Missouri Natural Resources Department\uc2 . What she learned would change everything.\par \par \uc2 CHAPMAN: They started talking about the Manhattan Project and there being radioactive waste, and he was concerned the fire was going to hit it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chapman and Nickel founded their organization Just Moms STL shortly after meeting in 2013. Since then, they have worked it uncover how toxic sites, such as Coldwater Creek and the nearby West Lake Landfill, came to be in St. Louis. Over the last decade, they have pressured authorities to clean them up.\par \par \uc2 We have interviewed some of these families, heard their stories. I assume you have talked to them, as well?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Oh, absolutely. The government has done all of this. It has imposed these hardships on these working people. And, Bret, I just want to say, again, these working folks, they are the heroes of this story.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): In our next installment, we're learning more about how the St. Louis area was involved in developing the atomic bomb and what officials knew about the possible contamination there. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a known gang member out on parole is accused of shooting a sheriff's deputy in the back.\par \par \uc2 And later, the panel on campus protests and President Biden's muted response.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We have another example of America's crime crisis tonight. A known gang member already out on parole is facing charges of shooting a Los Angeles County sheriff's deputy. Correspondent Bill Melugin is there tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ma'am, it's 2-12 Mary one. I have been shot. It hit the vest.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: A Los Angeles County sheriff's deputy radios for help moments after he was shot in the back while waiting at a red light on his motorcycle. The deputy's ballistic vest saved his life. The photo, provided by a law enforcement source, shows he was just inches away from a potentially fatal shot to his head.\par \par \uc2 This violent incident putting another spotlight on California's soft on crime policies. Investigators believe career criminal and known gang member Raymundo Duran was the trigger man. He is now in custody, arrested hours after the shooting with a gun on him. The L.A. County sheriff says surveillance video and witness statements tied him to the shooting.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT LUNA, LOS ANGELES COUNTY SHERIFF: That he has a very extensive criminal history, I could tell you by bulleted points it took up over half the page. And a lot of the acts were violent.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Multiple law enforcement sources tell FOX Duran has a prior conviction for manslaughter after he stabbed his co-worker to death in 2001. In 2021, he was convicted of felony evading after leading police on this pursuit in Los Angeles County. But he only spent months behind bars for it. In 2022, Duran was convicted again for being a felon in possession of a firearm. L.A. D.A. George Gascon claimed today his office will hold Duran accountable.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE GASCON, (D) LOS ANGELES COUNTY DISTRICT ATTORNEY: It will lead hopefully to a conviction if we are able to prove the case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Shannon, there are obviously questions now as to how a suspect with this kind of criminal history could be out on the streets. I reached out to L.A. D.A. George Gascon's office yesterday to ask yesterday how they prosecuted his prior cases and how they plan to prosecute this one, but I haven't heard back from them. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, the latest installment in Bret Baier's documentary series "The Unauthorized Histories" is now available on FOX Nation. It's a five-part look at the history of Silicon Valley. The programs trace the rising power of today's tech giants. Here's a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: These new revolutionaries saw themselves as upstarts who would change the world. Few suspected that within just a few decades they themselves would become the establishment, with powers that extend far beyond the Valley itself.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would even argue Silicon Valley has become everything that it professed to hate. Silicon Valley is the consolidated authority.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS MILLER: They are not traditional rebels because they're structuring their success with stock options and hiring to and from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , selling to the Pentagon. These are not revolutionaries of the traditional sort.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So I think a lot of these executives need to take a good look at themselves today and why they initially set out to do this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You can watch all five episodes of "The Unauthorized History of Silicon Valley" streaming now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel discusses President Biden's response to anti-Israel campus demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 First, here's a look at what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX 5 in New York as doctors put a mechanical pump in a patient's heart paired with a new kidney from a genetically modified pig. Physicians say Lisa Pisano is recovering well following last month's procedure, taking a few steps with her walker this week. She is just the second patient to receive a kidney from a pig.\par \par \uc2 WRAL in Raleigh, Durham, with the crash of a small plane at the airport there. A University of North Carolina health physician and the pilot were taken to a hospital. The doctor is said to be in good condition. No word yet on the pilot.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Los Angeles. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX 11, 2005 Heisman Trophy winner Reggie Bush is getting his trophy back. The \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2  returned the award in 2010 following an investigation that found Bush received what were at the time impermissible benefits. Heisman Trust President Michael Comerford says his group considered the enormous changes in college athletics over the last several years that now make some forms of athlete compensation acceptable.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON, (R-LA) HOUSE SPEAKER: The madness has to stop. The Congress will not be silent as Jewish students are expected to run for their lives and stay home from their classes.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We want to see this be peaceful. We'll let the universities handle that process on how they are dealing on the ground.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Those kids are getting cheated out of education because a handful of pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  demonstrators have decided they're going to disrupt these institutions. Let's get control.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has the capacity to fix this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's bring in our panel to talk about what is going on on campuses, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers, and Jason Riley, "Wall Street Journal" columnist and senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute.\par \par \uc2 OK, we all saw the Speaker of the House take his visit up to Columbia today with a number of other members of House leadership, and as he was there, a lot of chanting, a lot of calling him down. But that visual, Mollie, is again sparking what Secretary Pompeo brought up there. There needs to be a more full-throated response from the White House a lot of folks think.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: There absolutely needs to be much more response from all sorts of people. This didn't all just happen at Columbia the other day. This has been brewing for decades. It arises from a leftwing ideology that teaches hatred for different groups, and we have seen this fester for a long period of time.\par \par \uc2 I am heartened by how many people are upset at seeing this current incarnation of it. It presents a good opportunity for people to renounce it once and for all. Obviously, people are still waiting for President Biden to be a little more clear in his renunciation of some of the attacks that you're seeing on campus.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Because he has said he denounces and condemns the protestors, but also he condemns people who don't understand what is going on in Palestine, I believe that's what he said.\par \par \uc2 OK, so I want to put this up. This is "The New York Post" cover saying "Columbia surrenders." The headlines there say "Pro-terrorist students get slap on the wrist as profs join protests." Jason, what do you make of this, because there's so much pressure on the school administrators to get this balance right about free speech and protecting kids. Not an easy task.\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Oh, I think it's not as hard as --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Easier than we're being led to believe.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Shannon, I think it is time to do what some Republican officials are now calling for, which is to bring in the feds, that the National Guard should get involved. And there is a precedent for this. Back in the 1950s when President Eisenhower was in office, we had mobs of white southern Democrats resisting court orders, resisting Supreme Court rulings, threatening, harassing physically students -- black students trying to go to school. And Ike called in the National Guard and the 104th Airborne Division and got things under control and protected those kids.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think that Jewish students in 2024 deserve any less protection than black students did in 1957. And so if Biden wants to get serious about cracking down on this, I think it's time to call in the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Francesca, you cover the White House. Do you think that's in the offing, any discussion of taking that action? Because I'm quite sure the White House is also worried about the optics of sending in people in uniform to shut down these protesters.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": So the White House is has underscored that the president wants to continue implementing his antisemitism national strategy which includes college campuses. When you dig into that, though, it mostly calls on schools to denounce antisemitism, to create things such as task forces rather than talks about action that the president would take himself.\par \par \uc2 And one critique that I have heard from the left on the Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  war consistently is that when the president talks about these issues, it's often in written statements. Today he did speak about the war when he signed the national security legislation. But typically, he's been putting out these statements and said, and they want to see him hold listening groups on this issue. And he is under pressure on the left to call for a cease-fire as well. So he's facing pressure from the right and the left to be more public.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Jason, you want to --\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Obviously, he has got some political things that are holding him back here. These are young people he's worried about losing. These are young people that are leading the protests on campus. He is worried about losing young people to RFK Jr. or Cornel West. And he has got to win Michigan, a swing state with a large Arab American population that doesn't like what is he doing for Israel.\par \par \uc2 And finally, he has got a party that is really mealy-mouthed about law and order in general, unless \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or his supporters are on the receiving end of it. They want to erase the border. They want to defund police. They want to let criminals not be prosecuted by these progressive D.A.s, and so forth. So these are the things that are holding him back, I think, from being much more forceful on this issue.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: But the way that he has responded to it, by the way, is to not to be on college campuses so far at all. He is doing two commencement addresses, but he hasn't went to the college campuses to sit down with students and talk to them about this. And that was part of the critique that progressives have had for him is that, can you imagine \uc2 Obama\uc2  not going to college towns is what one of them said to me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and they have been, as we know, tightly controlled with his campaign activities, too, because these protesters are showing up everywhere, and that's another threat he has got to deal with where he is out there campaigning.\par \par \uc2 To this issue, though, of cracking down on the college campuses, Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  says this, "Calling in police enforcement on nonviolent demonstrations of young students on campus is an escalatory, reckless, and dangerous act. It represents a heinous failure of leadership that puts people's lives at risk. I condemn it in the strongest possible terms." So we have seen them come in in some places. The students quickly processed out back to these encampments. How does it work?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: We have also not seen very much done by certain campuses at all. You've seen certain colleges where when people disrupt the educational environment for other students they are immediately expelled. You saw at the \uc2 University of Texas\uc2 . They brought troops in to clear the situation there. There are all sorts of things that people can do to restore order to the campus environment.\par \par \uc2 The situation, though, again, is that many of these campuses teach this leftwing ideology that is so racist and bigoted against other groups. And a lot of people have favored it. The media have cheered it on. A lot of people in the Democratic Party have cheered it on until this moment, which is now suddenly causing them a problem because they have two of their special interest groups that are clashing here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we're looking at live pictures here at Yale's campus to see how they are dealing with it there. It looks pretty quiet at this point.\par \par \uc2 But another issue for young people is this issue of TikTok, because now we have this measure that's passed. It's gotten to the president's desk on TikTok. But, at the same time, what we're finding out is that the administration apparently, or the campaign, I should say, plans to, Francesca, stay on TikTok after publicly talking about the condemnation of it, the dangers of it and signing something to get rid of it.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, it won't be banned until this time next year, assuming that even goes through. But I will say when it comes to the court, the reason that the ban that the Trump administration tried to put in place was blocked was because the court determined that the president didn't have that kind of executive authority. This is legislation. This isn't an executive action.\par \par \uc2 The other problem with it, according to the judge, was that they didn't come up with another -- enough other options. And so this could be seen as, instead of a 45-day ban, they are giving them an entire year to try to divest. So we'll see what the courts have to say.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Jason, does it resonate with young voters? Because TikTok is like, we've got our army out there we can mobilize and we're going to do this. You saw the response on Capitol Hill that came in when they said to people, hey, the House is getting ready to do this. The offices were flooded and overwhelmed with people.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: I have some sympathy with defenders of TikTok. I mean, I understand that this shouldn't be on government devices, that there is a national security issue. I understand the mental health concerns. I'm familiar with that literature, particularly of young women, and so forth.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the idea that the government should be censoring so- called misinformation on social media is, to me, a bridge too far. And that is what the people who want to ban TikTok, both Democrats and Republicans, are calling for doing it in the name of, making sure we're not exposed to misinformation.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Quick final word?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Giving \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that power in particular to decide what is information, misinformation, when he has been such a major player in censorship is a problem.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the Supreme Court is looking at many of those questions. So we will stay tuned to that.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight -- check this out. A very special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Reading definitely helps you escape, because sometimes this place can be chaotic and you will just get caught up in a lot of stuff that's going on around here. Reading is just like that place that just brings you back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Every week, Nana Ampofo and her group of \uc2 DePaul University\uc2  peers head to Cook County jail in Chicago to discuss books with inmates. Ampofo comes up with thought-provoking questions for the group about a book that everyone reads. And the one rule of the club, no questions permitted about why any of these members are in jail. Just a discussion.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our second installment in toxic towns looks at nuclear waste in Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching. I'm Shannon Bream. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is up next. That's it from Washington.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240425ek4o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Anti-Israel Protests Sweep Campuses Nationwide; Former President Donald Trump Gag Order Hearing And Case Testimony; Decision Pending On \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Gag Order Violation; Sen. Mark Warner (D-VA), Is Being Interviewed About Netanyahu, Denouncing United States Plan To Impose Sanctions On An Israeli Military Unit, Over Alleged Human Rights Abuses; Biden Bashes Republicans Over Reproductive Rights; Anti-Israel Protests Cloud Biden Re-Election Effort; United States Cites Israeli Violations; Sen. Mark Warner (D-VA) Interviewed on Accusations Of Antisemitism In Pro- Palestinian College Campus Protests And Congressional Bill Calling For Divestment Of Chinese Government In TikTok; Judge In El Paso, Texas, Dropping State Charges Against Illegal Immigrants Accused Of Storming U.S. Border; Analysts Examine Potential Political Impact Of Israel-Palestine Issue For U.S. Presidential Election\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jonathan Turley, Nate Foy, Steve Harrigan, David  Spunt, Jacqui Heinrich, Gillian Turner, Danamarie McNicholl, Guy Benson \par \uc2 7,616 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: 10 days away, I'm going to be completely sold out my entire tour.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Where is -- where is Naperville?\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: It is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Where is Decatur?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Illinois.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Illinois. You know what? The one percent you were breathing --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Illinois, we've gotten it down. Thanks, Tyrus. Have a good night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm going to see that show, Jesse. I think that could be good.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right. Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you. Good evening. Welcome to Washington I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, Fox News has just learned House Speaker Mike Johnson will meet with Columbia University Jewish students tomorrow. He will also take questions from reporters at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , where protests have forced the school to shift to virtual classes. This comes as police on or near college campuses in several cities are preparing for another night of possible confrontation with anti-Israel demonstrators. Protester encampments are growing in many places across the country, and many people there are resisting police orders to leave.\par \par \uc2 New York University\uc2  students have been participating in the demonstrations in recent days. Correspondent Steve Harrigan is on the scene there tonight. Good evening, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. They've been going out already for about five hours here at its peak 1500 people and they don't seem to be going anywhere soon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): From coast to coast, Ivy League's large universities, small colleges and even high schools, pro-Palestinian protests are spreading and becoming increasingly disruptive.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're here to demand that the university divest from weapons corporations and from the Israeli occupation.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Students in \uc2 New York University\uc2  walked out of class Tuesday, carrying signs to "resist by any means."\par \par \uc2 More than 100 were arrested Monday night following a violent scuffle with police. Plywood wall was put up by the school to block the area. As many question who is funding and organizing these protests?\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY: We can't have outside agitators come in and be disruptive to our city.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Encampments like those seen at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  are also forming at \uc2 UC Berkeley\uc2 , \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  and at MIT, Tufts and Emerson in the Boston area, with classes disrupted and some campuses essentially shut down. Jewish students tell Fox they feel unsafe.\par \par \uc2 MIKA BARDIN, \uc2 YALE UNIVERSITY\uc2  STUDENT: They have blocked Jewish students access to center parts of campus just because of their religion.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Critics of the protests say chants of we are \uc2 Hamas\uc2  shows support for a terrorist organization and that schools must do more.\par \par \uc2 BILL BENNETT, FORMER EDUCATION SECRETARY: We need to maintain the order and to assert their authority and say go to the library if you're students enrolled here and read about what happened in the Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): Columbia calls its current plan hybrid education. That means for $66,000 a year in tuition, you basically get Zoom calls. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan live in \uc2 NYU\uc2 . Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the Trump trial. The judge in the criminal trial against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  heard arguments today about whether the presumptive Republican presidential nominee is violating the court's gag order. No decision has been made or now says of yet, but following that hearing, the prosecution continued to build its case with additional testimony today.\par \par \uc2 For a complete wrap up, Correspondent Nate Foy once again outside the New York Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former American media CEO and President David Pecker testified today, he made a secret agreement to benefit former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's 2016 campaign.\par \par \uc2 Pecker testified, "I would be the eyes and ears" working with Trump's former lawyer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  to suppress negative stories about Trump and publish negative stories about his opponents, including former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and former Secretary of State then presidential candidate \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump says this trial is benefiting his current political adversary President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's a very unfair situation. We're locked up in a courtroom and this guy is out there campaigning.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Pecker testified he and Cohen identified and suppressed stories from former Playboy model Karen McDougal and a false story from a Trump Tower doorman.\par \par \uc2 Today, Judge Juan Merchan reserved his decision about whether or not Trump violated the court's gag order by sharing articles online about witnesses in the case.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : then somebody's name is mentioned somewhere deep in the article and I end up in violation of a gag order. I think it's a disgrace. It's totally unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Trump's lawyer Todd Blanche claimed the former president's post represented responses to attacks from his opponents, but after failing to provide an example, Merchan scolded him saying, "Mr. Blanche, you are losing all credibility with the court."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Bret, the conversation away from the jury today, Prosecutor Joshua Steinglass revealed the crime that Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  did not include in his indictment of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It's New York Law Section 17 part 152. And what we can take from it is that Trump is accused of falsifying business records to aid a conspiracy to win the 2016 presidential election, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy in New York. Nate, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the release of hundreds of pages of documents relating to former President Trump's classified materials case. Remember, this is down in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is at the Justice Department tonight to tell us what we're learning inside.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  allegedly promised a second term pardon if a close aide was charged with lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . That aid now Trump's codefendant in Florida Walt Nauta, that accusation made by a Trump associate in an interview with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , someone dubbed person 16, who declined to be recorded by agents insisting that having his voice recorded was a far bigger risk for him in the Trump World.\par \par \uc2 Newly unclassified documents paint a fuller picture of the unprecedented \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid of Trump's Mar-a-Lago estate in August 2022. The investigation we now know was dubbed plasmic echo. The new documents also provide a specific timeline and reveal 30 \uc2 FBI\uc2  personnel were unseen entering Trump's residence at 8:59 a.m. Eastern leaving at 6:38 p.m.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: I personally approved the decision to seek a search warrant in this matter.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): According to the documents Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  escalated the probe into a full blown investigation in March of 2022, five months before the raid, the mysterious person 16 claims he warned Trump whatever you have, give everything back. Let them come here and get everything. Don't give them a noble reason to indict you because they will.\par \par \uc2 In June 2023, Special Counsel Jack Smith indicted Trump for violating the Espionage Act accusing the former president of illegally keeping more than 300 highly classified documents and committing obstruction by not returning them as required under federal law. Trump has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The former president Bret has made the argument that these documents could be considered personal. Judge Cannon who he appointed has declined to drop the case as the former president would like. It's still on the calendar for May 20th to start jury selection, but we expect that date to slide as both sides have indicated they are OK with the delay later this summer, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the Justice Department. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Unconstitutional gag order by a highly conflicted judge that should recuse themselves. Very simple, this is all Biden just in case anybody has any question.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The former president at court today, let's get some analysis now from George Washington University Law School Professor Jonathan Turley. Jonathan, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 First on the gag order. We haven't heard from the judge yet, he'll make a ruling at some point.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: He will and look, the former president is in violation of court.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, it's 10 violations.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yes, it's pretty clear that he violated the gag order. They -- what he's been arguing is that the gag order itself is the problem, not me. And he does have some, I think, reason to say that. I mean, when it comes to \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , you know, Cohen's on the night on the news almost every night attacking the president, not just legally, but politically. He'll continue to do that after he testifies.\par \par \uc2 And yet, this order saying you can't really respond to him. I think a lot of people view that as being too broad a scope and the judge doesn't seem to have any sort of inclination to narrow it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I've heard you talk about the legal side of this and how it's cobbled together the charges. Now we hear another kind of avenue that the prosecution is going down.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right. Well, there's the second misdemeanor, another one coming out of the New York code that said, you illegally conspired to promote your own campaign.\par \par \uc2 And as you sort of work your mind around that, they're saying that because you miss identified these funds and treated them as legal expenses, you were illegally trying to win that campaign.\par \par \uc2 Once again, most of us looking at this, or at least many of us are saying, wait, I mean, first of all, you can prove all these facts because they're not being contested, these -- all these facts will be proven and there's no crime. You're allowed to pay -- to have an NDA, you're allowed to have your attorney --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Disclosure agreement.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right. That type of agreement. It's done all the time.\par \par \uc2 And they're trying to zap these dead misdemeanors back into life. And it's left this muddle in courtroom. I think they're relying on the fact that the jury may not look beyond the identity of the defendant and not really seriously question these linkages.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And so, the prospect of this jury coming to a guilty verdict, we don't know how this is going to lay out. You think that legally, it's just not a strong case. Politically, does it make a big difference do you think?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yes, that's the real question here. I think, look, I think it's a legally absurd case. But I think part of the motivation here is to get this trial before the election occurred. They've succeeded in that.\par \par \uc2 I'm just not too sure anyone's listening. I mean, it's not like this is news to many people, even supporters for Trump, about these alleged affairs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally, the documents that we had released from Judge Cannon down in Florida, any surprises there other than perhaps he was told numerous times, let's give these documents back?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: It's very surprising how small the original request was over three documents, and you're left -- and he got advice from his people saying, for the love of Mike, just turned over the docs. And had he done that, this would not have happened.\par \par \uc2 The pardon offer is surprising. But once again, that is the testimony of one individual that is going to be tested at this trial. It seems very unlikely this trial is going to occur before the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you don't think that the judge is going to be pressured to kind of push this for? Jack Smith, obviously, the special counsel would like that to happen.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: He definitely wants that to happen. That seems to be his overriding priority. Just as a criminal defense attorney, I don't see how you get from here to there before the election, there's so much evidence here that is still being contested. I think the odds are against it, but you have a very motivated special counsel that's doing all it can to make that happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Strong earnings led to another positive day. I'm over here. On Wall Street, the Dow gained 264, the S&P 500 was up 60. The NASDAQ surged 245.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's first quarter net income plummeted 55 percent as falling global sales and price cuts sliced into the electric vehicle maker's revenue and profit margins.\par \par \uc2 The Austin Texas Company said it made 1.13 billion from January through March compared to 2.51 billion in the same period a year ago. Revenue was