n abortion.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Next week, one of the nation's most extreme anti-abortion laws take effect here in Florida.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Tying a patchwork of restrictions across 21 states back to Trump, who said should be left up to the states to decide.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Trump has literally taken us back 160 years. It is wrong. The Supreme Court was wrong. This should be a constitutional right in the federal constitution. A federal right.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Florida's Republican governor signed the state's six-week abortion ban into law. He is hitting back now.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: He is coming down to try to support a constitutional amendment that will mandate abortion up until the moment of birth. That will eliminate parental consent for minors. Floridians are not buying what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is selling.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden may not win Florida, a state that's grown more red since Obama won 12 years ago.\par \par \uc2 But with anti-Israel protests rocking elite college campuses, he may also have to worry about divisions in his base. Biden condemned both anti- Semitism and "people who don't understand what's going on with Palestinians yesterday," critics panning that as his own Charlottesville moment, which on ironically, Biden brought up again today.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They asked the then-President Trump, what he thought, and he said, I think they're very fine people on both sides. That's when I decided I had to run for real.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): President Biden has not used any of his prepared remarks to call out anti-Semitism on campuses, despite a statement he issued on Passover, calling on Americans to do just that. Saying that silence is complicity.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Press Secretary Andrew Bates defended the president, saying he made a call to action, although that call followed four paragraphs on efforts to get a ceasefire and a hostage deal more aid into Gaza and also a two-state solution, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Biden administration groups Israel with the worst in the world for Human Rights. We'll take you to the State Department and talk to the chairman of the Senate Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, Wall Street Journal reporter, \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2 , will remain jailed on espionage charges until at least late June.\par \par \uc2 A Moscow court is rejecting his appeal that sought to end his pre-trial detention.\par \par \uc2 Gershkovich was detained in late March of last year, while on a reporting trip. He is denying those spying charges against him.\par \par \uc2 Growing concerns in Haiti about a shortage of life saving medication and equipment. Brutal gangs are tightening their grip of the capital of Port- au-Prince and beyond.\par \par \uc2 They are blocking roads, forcing the closure of the main airport, and keeping supply containers stuck at the country's largest seaport.\par \par \uc2 Argentina's libertarian president is revving up his policies on the country's deep fiscal deficit. \uc2 Javier Milei\uc2  is deepening spending cuts and austerity moves that squibs purchasing power as he hopes to bring down rampant inflation in Argentina. The country posted a third straight monthly fiscal surplus in March.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rio de Janeiro from EarthCam. One of the big stories there tonight, thousands of Brazilians celebrate St. George's Day. He is Rio de Janeiro's state and city patron.\par \par \uc2 Celebration started with a dawn mass, with faithful crowding inside and outside of St. George's Parish.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Biden administration is grouping Israel with regimes in Iran, Russia, Afghanistan, and Sudan, on its list of the world's most egregious human rights violators.\par \par \uc2 The State Department report comes as U.S. relations with Israel are under significant strain over the Jewish states conflict and war with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and treatment of Palestinian civilians inside Gaza.\par \par \uc2 State Department correspondent Gillian Turner has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: Do we have a double standard? The answer is no.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Secretary \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is rolling out the State Department's Human Rights Report, defending the decision to equate Israel's government with some of the deadliest regimes around the world.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: As we are looking at human rights and the condition of human rights around the world, we apply the same standard to everyone.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But today, an explosive acknowledgement from Ambassador \uc2 David Satterfield\uc2 , President Biden's humanitarian envoy to the Mideast. He says there is, in fact, a very different standard for Israel's conduct.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SATTERFIELD\uc2 , UNITED STATES SPECIAL ENVOY FOR THE MIDDLE EAST: We require from Israel, because it is the right and moral thing to do. A very different standard of conduct.\par \par \uc2 As the president made clear on his visit to Israel, shortly after October 7th. It's not just strategically important for you to do this. It's the morally right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The Human Rights Report includes Israel on page one, along with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in Afghanistan, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in Russia, and Chinese Communists carrying out genocide in Xinjiang.\par \par \uc2 The report section on Israel reads, "We continue to urgently raise concerns surrounding the deaths of and injuries to tens of thousands of Palestinian civilians in Gaza."\par \par \uc2 Critics insist, equating Israel's behavior with \uc2 Hamas\uc2  perverts the truth.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET.), FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The Israelis didn't start this. \uc2 Hamas\uc2  started this fight, and we should be supporting them to clean out \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . And we don't do it, we kind of equivocate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, the State Department's condemnation of Israel's human rights record, yesterday, coincided with the first day of Pesach, a major Jewish holiday.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of egregious human rights violators, Bret, Secretary Blinken right now is on route to China. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian Turner live at the State Department. Gillian, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Joining us, Senate Intelligence Committee Chairman Mark Warner from Virginia. Senator, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER (D-VA): Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, this effort to cut off aid to an Israeli military unit accused of human rights abuses. \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  posting, "At a time when our soldiers are fighting the monsters of terror, the intention to impose a sanction on a unit in the \uc2 IDF\uc2  is the height of absurdity and a moral low." That's from the prime minister. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, my thoughts are that Israel is a strong ally and we need stand by Israel. We're going to do that later tonight when earlier today over 80 percent of the Senate voted in favor of the aid package that includes Ukraine, Israel, banning TikTok as well, or divesting TikTok. Let me be clear.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll talk about that in a second.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Israel is stronger when it has allies. Remember, just a week ago when, in a remarkable show, Israel and the United States shot down all the Iranian missiles. That was helped because the British, the French, the Jordanians, the Saudis all sided with Israel. And maintaining those alliances are more important than ever right now. And those alliances are frayed when we see some of the, frankly, excesses in terms of what happened in Gaza with now 34,000 people killed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So we see these protests around the country, \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , \uc2 NYU\uc2 , Yale. It's expanding, a lot of different schools all over the place. How do you read that? I mean, some of these protesters are saying we support \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Some of them are saying they are pro-October 7th. Some of them are pretty antisemitic.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Absolutely. And I think that's outrageous. People do have a right to protest. That is a First Amendment right in this country. But they don't have a right, as we have seen at some of these universities, to shut down the university. And if you break the law, as we saw at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , you ought to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think that the administration has been strong enough, the president has been strong enough in his words or even his actions?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I think he has called out antisemitism. We are in a period of Passover right now. Now more than ever we need to stand up against antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Twenty-five Senate Republicans sent a letter to the attorney general and the Education Secretary Cardona, saying, "President Biden issued a statement on Sunday purporting to condemn the outbreak of antisemitism. If that statement was serious, it must be accompanied by immediate action from your departments. Rioting violates federal law. Violence or attempted violence against anyone because of their Jewish heritage violates federal law." So I guess I'm asking, should more be done?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: I stand against antisemitism in any form. And I also believe as we have seen on some of these protests, when they end up literally shutting down a university, I think those who break the law need to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You mentioned TikTok, and it's a part of this bill. It is a divesture from the company tied to China, ByteDance. Is that fair to say? It's not a banning.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Absolutely. It is not a ban. It is saying that an enterprise that literally potentially collects 170 million Americans' data and has the potential to influence what you see in a way of foreign interference, I believe is a national security threat. And candidly, it was then President Trump in 2019 who first raised this issue. He was right then. I'm not sure, he seems to have changed his view now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He does seem to have changed. He just sent out a message the other day saying, "Just so everyone knows, especially the young people, crooked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is responsible for banning TikTok. He is the one pushing it to close and doing it to help his friends over at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  become richer and more dominant."\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Bret, you know, you don't hear me often saying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was right. He was right in 2019 to raise the national security concerns. Nothing has changed in terms of the Chinese Communist Party's ability to influence this very popular social media platform. I believe it is not a ban. It will be a divesture. We can have a American company, we can have a French company, a British company, a Brazilian company.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just not the CCP.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Just not the CCP. And the fact that -- look at the House. Over 80 percent of the House voted for this divesture.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But knowing what you know, it is a serious threat? I tell my kids, kids, really, it is.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: It is a serious threat, and as a matter of fact virtually every industrial country in the world has banned at least its government use. And most major news organizations have said to their journalists don't get on TikTok because chances are the Chinese Communist Party and their agents may be surveilling you. That is a national security threat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So after, as we expect, President Biden to sign this into law, how long do you think that process takes?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: One of the things that were changed from the House version was to give this divesture up to a year. This is a complex deal. This is a very valuable piece of property. And literally about 40 percent of the investors are Americans. I hope they will roll over to some new controlling entity. And it could actually be those Americans who are already invested in TikTok.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I love having the Intel Committee folks, especially the chairman on, and have you come on a lot. And I always end with, what's keeping you up at night?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, one of the things that's kept me up at night for a long time was whether the United States was going to step up and do the right thing and stand by Ukraine in terms of pushing back against \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . I think that, I'm going to rest a little easier.\par \par \uc2 But I worry now six months out from a national election that we could see a level of foreign interference in our election. We've got enough disagreements amongst Americans, but because people know interference in our foreign elections is cheap by foreign adversaries, unfortunately a lot of Americans believe conspiracy theories. And as we've talked before on this show, with artificial intelligence tools now, they can bring about the level of deepfake at a scale and speed that's been unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 So I hope at the end of the day, with all our divisions we can make sure that this election is decided by Americans and not by foreign influence. And that's one of the things that keeps me up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, we appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, why state charges are being dropped against almost 150 migrants accused of storming into the U.S. illegally.\par \par \uc2 And later, the panel on the spreading of the anti-Israel protests on college campuses and the former president's trial.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The city of Baltimore is suing the owner and manager of the Dali cargo ship that crashed into the Francis Scott Key Bridge in late March. The city believes the two companies should be completely liable for the deadly collapse. The allegations are that the disaster is the result of carelessness, negligence, and recklessness. The company has previously filed a petition asking a court to cap their liability.\par \par \uc2 A judge in El Paso is dropping state charges against dozens of illegal immigrants accused of storming the border earlier this month. The group still faces federal charges. Correspondent Danamarie McNicholl is in El Paso tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DANAMARIE MCNICHOLL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The migrant crisis not only playing out on the front lines of the southern border, but also in the justice system after an El Paso judge dropped state rioting charges against 140 migrants accused of cutting razor wire and storming government fencing on April 12th.\par \par \uc2 The judge said it was due to a lack of evidence that every person charged was involved in the rioting. It comes just weeks after this viral incident. More than 200 migrants were arrested. But another El Paso judge ordered they be released, citing the state wasn't ready to proceed on a bond hearing. El Paso D.A. Bill Hicks responded saying that is untrue, emphasizing Texas was prepared to address each and every case.\par \par \uc2 ICE tells us a majority of the migrants involved in the March incident have been turned over to enforcement and removal operations, but former Border Patrol chief Victor Manjarrez says that's not going to stem the tide.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR MANJARREZ, FORMER CHIEF PATROL AGENT: These numbers are going to continue because, again, there is no consequence at all.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL: In El Paso migrants crossing continue in high numbers. The FOX drone team captured multiple groups of migrants being escorted on to buses by Border Patrol. CBP sources tell us El Paso sector saw over 1,000 each day over the past four days.\par \par \uc2 MANJARREZ: In fact, we tell them, right, go a gate. Go to Gate 40, and you will be processed. It's safe and orderly. Could you imagine how a smuggling organization is selling that? Because no one crosses for free.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLL (on camera): And I just got off the phone with the El Paso D.A. about the April 12th incident. He says he does not agree with the dropped rioting charges. For that reason, he brought those charges in front of a grand jury today, and they decided there was enough evidence of a riot. So this all goes back to the courtroom. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Danamarie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The latest installment in my FOX Nation documentary series "The Unauthorized Histories" is now available on FOX Nation. It's a five-part look at the history of Silicon Valley. The programs trace the rising power of today's tech giants. Here is an excerpt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How did an industry that promised to break down barriers and unleash individual achievement become a powerful political force that some say dominates Washington today?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you know how much it costs to buy Congress? Well, big tech does.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, HOOVER INSTITUTE SENIOR FELLOW: It was all under the patina that we came out of the 60s. We are the good people. And we're going to use our newfound riches and power not like our parents did. We're going to do it to make a new, more, I guess, inclusive America. But make no mistake about it, they make -- Silicon Valley grandees make Rockefeller or Carnegie or Jay Gould look like pikers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can now watch all five episodes of "The Unauthorized History of Silicon Valley" streaming now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on anti-Israel protests and the second day of testimony in the trial of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 10 in Phoenix as a mistrial is declared in the trial of an Arizona rancher charged with fatally shooting an unarmed migrant on his property near the U.S.-Mexico border. Jurors failed to reach a verdict in the case of George Alan Kelly. The county's attorney's office will decide whether to retry Kelly or drop the case.\par \par \uc2 FOX 17 in Nashville as Tennessee State House members pass legislation allowing teachers and other school staff to carry concealed handguns on campus. The move comes following earlier approval by the State Senate. It happens one year after a former student gunned down three children and three adults at Christian school in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Los Angeles from FOX 11, our affiliate there. One of the stories there tonight, women's golf sensation Nelly Korda puts bid for six successive win on the \uc2 LPGA\uc2  tour on hold. She is withdrawing from this week's tournament at Wilshire Country Club, citing exhaustion. She has won her last five events, making her one of the only three female pros to ever accomplish that feat. Sunday's win at the Chevron Championship was second career major title.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Free, free Palestine!\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Free, free Palestine!\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: What's going on at the college level, in these colleges, Columbia, \uc2 NYU\uc2 , and others is a disgrace. And it's really on Biden. He has the wrong signal.\par \par \uc2 BILL BENNETT, FORMER EDUCATION SECRETARY: The president should condemn it in totally unambiguous terms. He was very wishy-washy yesterday.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FORMER OBAMA ADVISER: President Biden has been unequivocal in his comments. Silence is complicity. Threatening and intimidation is antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, these protests have picked up. As you look at a map around the country, top colleges are seeing a lot more protests. Anti-Israel, sometimes very antisemitic. We've heard lawmakers weigh in on all sides. Where does this go and what does it mean politically?\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel and start there, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, Josh Holmes, co-host of "The Ruthless" podcast, former chief of staff to Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist.\par \par \uc2 Leslie, Mark Levin on his show had a compilation of public reports, and he tweeted out "The dark money Democrat billionaires and Islamist networks paid for and organized this." He cited a number of different articles. One of them "The New York Post," "Groups backed behind Israel-bashing protests backing \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks got $15 million plus from \uc2 George Soros\uc2 . The Open Society's Foundation records shows Soros' grant making network gave $13.7 million through the Tides Center." It goes on. And there's a number of different public reports that examine the money that funnels into these organizations. We do see it is pretty organized. The tents all look the same. And it's expanding. What do you make of that effort and what it means politically for Democrats?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Politically for Democrats, look, when you look at Michigan, as an example, and you have the very large Arab population, that could certainly hurt \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  because it's going to come down to five, and Michigan is one of them. And there is a growing of this.\par \par \uc2 Here's my problem. And I think you guys know, I think I'm the only member of the Jewish tribe on the panel. I'm married to a Muslim. I have sympathy and empathy on both sides of this issue, strongly. And to be anti-Israel is one thing. And we have a right as Americans to do that. To be pro- Palestinian, wanting a cease-fire in Gaza, concerned about forthcoming famine, that's also covered under our First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 To support \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , a terrorist organization, absolutely not. And to be antisemitic, absolutely not. You can be anti-Israel and not be antisemitic. There are many very prominent Jews, Jon Stewart, just to name one, who are not antisemitic but are anti-Israel's stance on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Politically, I think this actually could hurt the president. However, Bret, what the people who are protesting, I'm not saying pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  or antisemitic, those who are pro-Palestine, anti-Israel -- what they forget is the alternative is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . If they stay home, it could be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . If they vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it could be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . If they vote for a third-party candidate, it could be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That's not going to get them their two-state solution.\par \par \uc2 And honestly, breaking the law and closing universities isn't going to do that either, unless we do end up with a Vietnam situation where the protests we had then are civil rights.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just to follow up on that original thing about the funding, here is a former Treasury antiterrorism analyst. He is talking about Students for Justice in Palestine, these groups that are popping up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SCHANZER, FORMER TREASURY DEPARTMENT ANALYST: There's now 200 campuses that have SJP. They use a lot of the same rhetoric. They use a lot of the same devices on campuses, the different means of protest. There is a lot of commonality that we see nationwide. They have been wielded as a student army by this group. An obfuscated money trail to put it mildly, but we can see that there are organizers behind all of this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Guy?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, some of the hate from these rallies is certainly organic. And that's undeniable and very disturbing. But the money behind it is, I think, a question that needs to be explored, not just by the government, if there is any particular ties to terrorism, but by some enterprising journalists here and elsewhere. Follow the money is a mantra in journalism. This is ripe for that. I think that's important to point out.\par \par \uc2 And he was just talking about SJP, that organization. In the very earliest days after October 7th, before Israel had even begun its military response, SJP was out there agitating, really in favor of \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , celebrating the attack against innocent Israeli civilians. And they put on some of their posters which were distributed by the national group images of paragliders, a clear reference to one of the ways that terrorists infiltrated Israeli territory. That is a glorification of mass murder, and those are chapters that are all over the country on almost every campus right now. That is extremely upsetting, particularly to Jewish students who see this happening on their campuses and in many cases wonder if administrations actually have their back.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's interesting, Josh, they had to change their chants. It was we apt cease-fire. But \uc2 Hamas\uc2  turned down the last two cease-fire negotiations.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, "RUTHLESS" PODCAST HOST: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now it's not cease-fire they want basically \uc2 Hamas\uc2  to win.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: It's pro-\uc2 Hamas\uc2 . There is nobody out there that is chanting "two- state solution, two-state solution." That's not what's happening here. I mean, look, the cold reality is that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has a constituency within the progressive left in this country. And it is a constituency even more -- I'm not saying that to be provocative. It's a fact. But it's a constituency, even more sadly, I think the Biden administration has concluded they can't win a national election without. It' spart of the reason why you saw \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  out saying, of course, I condemn antisemitism. I also condemn people who don't understand the plight of the Palestinians. You're triangulating, attempting to not to lose ground with a constituency that's out in front of all of us. And this is a progressive Democratic voting base, which is a scary proposition.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Leslie, are you worried about Chicago, the DNC? Could it be 1968 all over again?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Look, I'm honest, you guys know that. Yes, there is a concern. My concern for -- there should be no violence. What makes our nation great is our freedom and our ability to have that freedom to speak out against our government, to speak out against other governments, not be arrested for that.\par \par \uc2 But you can't stop traffic. Again, I don't see this being effective. What is effective is voting. And if you're going to stay home or vote for the guy that's going to be least effective in what you want, which is a cease- fire, a two-state solution, trying to head off the famine, then you are barking up the wrong tree, and your protests aren't going to lead to your goal.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. Obviously, Republicans see a political vulnerability here for Democrats. You have speaker Johnson going to Columbia tomorrow to talk to students, hold a press conference. On the flipside, you have President Biden in Florida talking about abortion, something that he sees and they see as a major vulnerability for Republicans. I mean, that's what we are setting up here for the next six months.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Biden would much rather talk about abortion through the framing that he wants to use, which is often, I think, a distortion than this issue, because when someone asked the question yesterday about the antisemitic protests on college campuses, instantly we got this both-sides- ism that I thought was just morally repulsive, frankly, coming from the president.\par \par \uc2 And another reporter jumped in to ask him about abortion, his favorite subject, and he was sort of nasty to that reporter who was throwing him a lifeline, which was a bit odd. But yes, this is definitely sort of the shakeup for the upcoming election. Democrats are going to try to focus as much as they can on the flaws of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and abortion, and Republicans clearly have a lot of other targets to talk about politically.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And 10 seconds, how much does the Trump trial dominate -- or trials dominate this political conversation?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: I think it dominates the political conversation. I think it has not even a little bit of impact on the American electorate at this point. I think that's showing up in poll after poll. People are focused on their own stuff and what you can do as president and rather than what this sort of charade going on in a courtroom.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will see. We'll follow it all. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 All right, it's time for "Tuesday Xtras." First up, D.M. Simon asks, "Did you ever think you would see this level of polarization in the U.S.?" You know, it is polarized. You can check out my X feed, it can be a dark place from time to time. But we look at history, and I look at history a lot. We have been in some really dark places for our country and managed to pull through. So there's hope.\par \par \uc2 Next up, Cynthia wonders "It seems that in the U.S. and abroad there is turmoil. Would China now make a move on Taiwan before the elections? If so, what significant role would or could the U.S. play?" It's a great question. Most of the experts we talk to say it's a couple years away them to make that kind of move. China is in a tough spot economically.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Brooks posts "I have enjoyed reading your history books. What topic are you going to tackle next?" I can't tell you. It's a surprise. There's all the books. Check them out.\par \par \uc2 Fair, balanced, and unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240424ek4n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Anti-Israel Protests Sweep U.S. College Campuses; Biden To Talk About Abortion Rights Amid Protests; United States Accuses Israel Of Human Rights Abuses In Gaza; Awaiting Judge's Decision On Trump Gag Order; DOJ Paying $138 For Mishandling Nassar Case; Melania Trump Headlines Fundraiser For LGBT Group; MMA Fighter Captures 8-Foot Gator\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Steve Harrigan, Jacqui Heinrich, Robert Wolf,  Howie Kurtz, Clay Travis, Gillian Turner, Pete Hegseth, David Spunt, Nate  Foy \par \uc2 8,262 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, and this is THE STORY. The spread of anti-Semitism in America, reaching even more college campuses this afternoon. As many look for leadership, the president chooses what is sometimes referred to as both sides-ism in his first on-the-record comments on these hot tensions. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You condemn the anti-Semitic protests on college campuses?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I condemn the anti-Semitic protests. That's why I've set up a program to deal with that. I also condemn those who don't understand what's going on with the Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: More on that from the President in just a moment, but first let's get straight to Senior Correspondent Steve Harrigan, who is reporting live at \uc2 NYU\uc2  on the action there this afternoon. Hi, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, the crowd here has been going at it for about two hours. The crowd is getting bigger, and they are getting louder as well. We're hearing chants of free, free Palestine. This is called a nationwide strike and already more than a thousand people involved in it here.\par \par \uc2 This is a movement that started at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 . It spread to \uc2 NYU\uc2  and Yale, other elite universities across the Northeast, even out to Berkeley, now in California. Some high schools too beginning to get involved as well. This has really been a hard thing for administrators to deal with.\par \par \uc2 They seem to have been caught flat-footed so far. They don't know how to deal with it. They sweep away the tents. They call in the police, but days later those tents are back. It's really brought online in-campus education, online learning here at the campus to a standstill in many places. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Steve, we'll keep a close eye on it. We'll go back there as the situation merits. Thank you very much. We're also going to talk with Clay Travis, get his unique perspective on this in just a little while as well. But back to President Biden, who today is campaigning in Florida.\par \par \uc2 And at this hour, there is no more response from him on the shocking growth of these anti-Israel protests. He is speaking in Florida, though, about abortion rights, an issue that he thinks could work for him in the 2024 election in that state. Vice President Harris also talking about abortion and health care, she's in Wisconsin today.\par \par \uc2 She did, though, tell a local TV station when asked point-blank about these protests saying that she's deeply concerned about growing anti-Semitism in the United States. That was the extent of her comments there.\par \par \uc2 In moments, Media Buzz Host Howie Kurtz, also \uc2 Hogan Gidley\uc2 , former Trump White House Principal Deputy Secretary; \uc2 Obama\uc2  advisor Robert Wolf, former \uc2 Obama\uc2  advisor, and first we go to White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich with the very latest on the president today. Hi, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha, you know the president's been in front of cameras more than a few times since all this spiraled out of control and he has not seized any of those moments to speak out firmly against this. He has tweeted 25 times since Saturday, including nine times about climate, but no mention of the protests on these campuses.\par \par \uc2 When our Fox producer shouted that question you just played, he did issue that both sides condemnation. And the White House sent a statement on Sunday that was pretty strong, but it was from the Deputy Press Secretary, Andrew Bates, who just defended President Biden speaking to reporters on Air Force One.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW BATES, WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: In the president's Passover statement, he directly addressed the vile comments that circulated over the weekend with a call to action echoing the rhetoric of terrorist organizations, especially in the wake of the worst massacre committed against the Jewish people since the Holocaust is despicable. We are of course monitoring these situations closely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But the president's Passover statement only referenced what's happening at schools after four paragraphs about the rituals of Passover, efforts to get a ceasefire and hostage deal, a two-state solution, and humanitarian aid into Gaza. Biden said in the fifth paragraph, we must speak out against the alarming surge of anti-Semitism in our schools, communities, and online. Silence is complicity.\par \par \uc2 Even in recent days we've seen harassment and calls for violence against Jews. This blatant anti-Semitism is reprehensible and dangerous. It has no place on college campuses or anywhere in the country. We will see if the president says anything in Florida. He walks straight past the reporters on his way out of the White House, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jacqui, thank you very much. With that, we bring in "MEDIA BUZZ" Host Howie Kurtz, \uc2 Hogan Gidley\uc2  a former Trump Re-election Campaign National Press Secretary, and former White House Principal Deputy Press Secretary now \uc2 America First Policy Institute\uc2  Senior Advisor for Strategic Communications -- that's a lot of titles, Hogan -- And Robert Wolf, former Economic Advisor to President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , Founder and CEO of 32 Advisors, and a Fox News Contributor.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's interesting to note that there hasn't been any sort of whole-of-government response to this. And \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2  pointed out in a very interesting piece today, calling on President Biden to take a leadership role here, that the Department of Education and the DOJ should be enacted to move in, to try to protect these students,\par \par \uc2 get them to have a safe environment to go to class in. But he pulled up the \uc2 Department of Education Office for Civil Rights\uc2 , and really sadly, I think, this is what is on the front page of the Department of Education for Civil Rights office: "The majority of our \uc2 OCR\uc2  staff are working remotely because of the pandemic." So, please send us an email. Robert, what can we expect from our federal government and from President Biden? What should he be? What should his response be? And all of this enormous government that we have that seems to be not put to use whatsoever in what we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: So, Martha, thanks. As you know, it's personal to me. I'm an Ivy League guy. I'm Jewish and my family was impacted by the Holocaust. So, I've been -- I'm taking the other side of this. President Biden has been unequivocal in his comments. Silence is complicity, threatening and intimidation is anti-Semitism.\par \par \uc2 They started the first national strategy for combating anti-Semitism. No one has been stronger on Israel since October 7th, making sure they got funding, including putting ammunition there in advance. He's also been fighting in a bipartisan way, fighting for more aid to Israel, and that finally is going to pass.\par \par \uc2 And I would also say I was recently at a \uc2 U.N.\uc2  summit with the first gentleman, Doug Emhoff, who is talking about anti-Semitism and violence and these threats on a continuous basis. So, I think they are articulating that this is critically important and that anti-Semitism, there is a hard line in the sand and you don't cross it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, well talking is one thing. Statements have gone out. We have an image here of the Little Rock Nine from Arkansas in 1957 breaking the desegregation barrier. Obviously, that led to a lot of strife during the civil rights effort. On the right-hand side of your screen, you're seeing Palestinian supporter, students, who seem to have won Howie Kurtz because students who feel unsafe, mostly Jewish students, have basically been sent home from \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 . So, where is the police protection that eventually came for students, black students, who wanted to safely go to schools in America? Where is that protection for these Jewish students to finish their semester at places like Columbia?\par \par \uc2 HOWIE KURTZ, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, more places like Columbia and \uc2 NYU\uc2  and Yale should call in their local police force and all the arrests that we had last week. But now, it does seem spiraling out of control. And look, President Biden as the Commander-in-Chief should own this story about the spread of anti-Semitic poison, about the walkouts, about the arrests, and also about the switch to virtual learning at these very expensive schools. He could give a speech.\par \par \uc2 So, what happens? He gives two sentences to a tossed off reporter's question when you can't really hear it very well, and it's very terse, and I think that may be because he is trying to not further alienate the anti- Israel wing, the squad, and others in his own party, but he really should be out there. He should absolutely take ownership of this issue and back it up with actions as you were suggesting.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, another piece out today criticizes President Biden and basically says that he's letting Dearborn Michigan dictate his policy on all of this, Hogan. What's your response to that? And I know you obviously worked for President Trump who wants to be president again. This is a sticky situation. How, how do you think it should be dealt with from the executive office?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN GIDLEY\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP RE-ELECTION CAMPAIGN NATIONAL PRESS SECRETARY: Well, I'm glad you brought up Dearborn because this is what these actions by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or inactions, if you will, are all about. This has been going on for days and we didn't hear a single word from this administration. And finally, we do. And of course, he botches this as well.\par \par \uc2 And I'm sorry, but you can say on one hand how he's so tough in support of Israel, but at the same time he's caught on hot mic saying he's going to go have a come-to-Jesus moment with \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , how he's working for a ceasefire. These people on college campuses are shouting death to America. They're shouting, I am -- we are \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , they're talking about placating, they're talking about supporting terrorism.\par \par \uc2 This should be a no-brainer for the administration to come out, but as is with most things, it's about politics. He knows he's got a problem in this election. He knows he has a problem with the folks out there who don't support Israel. He knows he has a problem in Michigan. So, what he's trying to do is play both sides of this issue. It is a serious problem for him, and he's left college campuses exposed to a lot of danger and a lot of vitriol.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I just have a few seconds left, but I want to give Robert a very quick response on that. Should President Biden, you know, allow law enforcement to come in and make sure that these Jewish students can safely get to their classrooms from their dorms and give them the right to an education that they have paid for?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Campuses absolutely have to be safe. I agree with what Howie said. The police need to go in to make sure that these threats and intimidation don't cross the line to violence. We have to do that, but this is not a time to call in the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, all right, gentlemen, thank you very much for your input there. Great to have all three of you with us today.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 GIDLEY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, as we've been talking about a walkout at \uc2 NYU\uc2  this hour, which is ongoing right now downtown in New York City, fairly large gathering, looks peaceful at this moment. We're going to keep a close eye on it, of course, as the anti-Israel contagion spreads to some of the country's most elite universities all across America, really. Clay Travis with an interesting observation and take on what we're seeing here, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Israel is a terror state!\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Israel is a terror state!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: One, two, three, four.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: One, two, three, four.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Occupation no more!\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Occupation no more.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Down with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ! Victory for Israel! Down with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ! Victory for Israel!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Protesters facing off against each other last night near \uc2 Columbia University in New York City\uc2 . Classes, now going hybrid. They have canceled the rest of the school year, which only goes for about another week, apparently, at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 . But the students are going to be at home, which is not the college experience that they paid for or had hoped for. They should have the right to be in their classrooms, learning, and experiencing all of that. But this is the situation as you see it.\par \par \uc2 Similar scenes at \uc2 NYU\uc2 . Right now, there's a walkout underway downtown in Manhattan. "OUTKICK" founder Clay Travis is standing by.\par \par \uc2 But first, let's check in with senior correspondent Steve Harrigan, reporting from New York. Hi, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, they're calling this a nationwide strike, and it doesn't look like they are going anywhere soon. The numbers about 1,500. We're hearing them chant, free, free Palestine. And some questions now being raised about who's behind this organization, and who is funding it, especially after \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  called in police, took away all the tents, and then just days later, a whole new 100 tents suddenly appeared.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAZ DAUGHTRY, ASSISTANT COMMISSIONER, NEW YORK POLICE DEPARTMENT: Ask yourself this question. You need to ask yourself this question. Look at the tents. They all were the same color. They all were the same type of tents, the same ones that we saw on \uc2 NYU\uc2 , the same ones that we see in Colombia. To me, I think somebody is funding this.\par \par \uc2 Also, they are professional agitators in there that are just looking for something to add -- to be agitated about, which is the protests.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: More than 100 people were arrested outside of \uc2 NYU\uc2  overnight. It's not clear how many of them were students. When you look at the arrest records, many of the ages 24, 25, 27.\par \par \uc2 And the big question is how long is this going to go on? You mentioned that Colombia is pretty much shut down for on campus learning that could go through the end of the term and even to graduation on May 15th. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Sad state of affairs. Steve, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in, "OUTKICK" founder, Clay Travis. Clay, always good to see you. Thank you very much for being here today. It's interesting to note that \uc2 Robert Kraft\uc2  and others, the owner of the Patriots, you know, has been discussing pulling his money donations that he has made to Columbia.\par \par \uc2 Here is a quick soundbite from him from last night on "HANNITY". Watch.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT KRAFT\uc2 , OWNER OF THE PATRIOTS: The same is happening now in this great country that offered so many opportunities to our families, who came here as immigrants. And we have to keep it open. We can't have this intimidation that's filtering through all these colleges.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Talking about anti-Semitism that we see all across these universities. Clay, what's your take on where we are today on this?\par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Oh, I got so many thoughts, Martha. Thanks for having me on. To start with, when Charlottesville happened, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was ridiculed, reviled, attacked on a level that frankly, hasn't come close to what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is being held accountable.\par \par \uc2 And remember, Biden himself said that the reason he ran for president was because of what he saw at Charlottesville. Well, now we have hundreds, if not thousands of protests that are every bit is anti-Semitic, far worse, in many ways, honestly, than what we saw in Charlottesville in terms of the scope and significance of them.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is failing when it comes to standing up for Israel and Israel's right to defend itself. And Martha, we've said this on "Clay and Buck" for a while, but I'm starting to see other people kind of have this take too.\par \par \uc2 I just want to give you a hypothetical. Pretend that in Mexico, a cartel group had come across the United States border, killed 1,200 people in the United States in cold blood because they were American, then taken over 100 of our citizens hostage and going back across the southern border.\par \par \uc2 What would we do to Mexican people who might be innocent, but in any way, were harboring that group that had attacked the United States, killed over 1,200 of our people and kidnapped over 100 of them?\par \par \uc2 We would rain down holy hell upon that group. And every American with a functional brain would understand why we were doing it. I don't understand why anyone with a functional brain in America right now, doesn't see that analogy as to what Israel is doing. The worst single day for Jewish people since the Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 Everybody wants to run around and try to compare Trump to Adolf Hitler on the far-left in America. Well, the most devastating deadly day for Jewish people since the Holocaust just happened on October 7th, and these idiot imbecilic kids, and everybody else affiliated with them is actually marching in support of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in many respects in what they did.\par \par \uc2 I find it, frankly, absolutely unacceptable. And I think all of the adults are failing here, in terms of what they are countenancing on so many of these different university campuses.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Interesting point. So, may I ask you a couple of questions/ With regard to Charlottesville, I think it's a very good point to make, because it was anti-Semitism in Charlottesville.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said, drove him, inspired him to run for president.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: That's right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And he was extraordinarily angry about this anti-Semitism. He said, this is not America. You know, that we cannot talk about it, and he criticized Trump in that moment, with the same sort of suggest -- same sort of comment that he made today -- or yesterday, rather, which is, you know, well, I understand both sides. I understand the Palestinian side, I understand the Israel side.\par \par \uc2 So, we're -- and we all know, you know, that the anger that we sometimes see from President Biden, what happened to that anger? How come he is not still feeling it when he sees these students, I -- one I talk to yesterday, who was poked in the eye with a Palestinian flag, a Jewish student.\par \par \uc2 Another one I spoke to this week who had to move to her grandmother's house because she can't live on campus anymore.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: I think it's a fabulous question, Martha. And remember, the very fine people comment is actually a lie the way it was attributed to Trump.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: I want to just kind of remind people, Trump said,