to post about it.\par \par \uc2 And the one time he did mention it on camera, it was in response to a question from FOX -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We're looking for a more full-throated pushback. Thank you very much, Hillary.\par \par \uc2 And as anti-Israel protests boil across elite universities, President Biden now focusing his campaign on abortion today.\par \par \uc2 What Karl Rove makes of it and how former President Trump is handling it is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  campaigning in Florida today making abortion rights the focus, this as he's facing heat on the handling of the Israel- \uc2 Hamas\uc2  war and those growing anti-Israel protests.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to FOX's Mark Meredith. He's in Tampa, Florida, with the very latest -- Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's visit down to Florida was brief, but he's hoping his message will resonate with voters between now and November, the campaign trying to make abortion a top-line issue, especially in this state, which is about to impose a six-week ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden says too many Floridians are being put in a dangerous situation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Criminalizing reproductive health care for -- before women even know whether they're pregnant, I mean, this is bizarre. I can put -- you can put a doctor in prison if he takes care of a patient.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The Biden/Harris campaign is spending a lot of time, energy and money trying to make the abortion issue front and center in swing states.\par \par \uc2 Polling shows it is a top five issue, but that the economy ranks higher in terms of voters' concerns. Former President Trump, who calls Florida home, says he believes the abortion issue should be decided on the state level. He's yet to say how he would vote on a ballot initiative in Florida which would essentially expand abortion access.\par \par \uc2 But we did hear today from Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who blasted Biden's visit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): ... is, Floridians are not buying what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is selling. And, in November, we're going to play an instrumental role in sending him back to Delaware, where he belongs.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: One of the big questions, of course, will Democrats turn out in greater numbers this election with abortion being on the ballot?\par \par \uc2 Republicans say they don't think that's going to be the case, but the president's visit today, Charles, was all about trying to get that voter turnout -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Mark, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, let's get the read on all of this from Karl Rove, FOX News contributor, former White House deputy chief of staff -- Karl.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Good afternoon, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 They're trying -- in, Florida, they're trying abortion, obviously, particularly -- it helped a lot, I think, in the midterms, certainly. But of all the things out there, though, is this going to be enough? Because it feels like there's so many issues that this White House has been caught offsides with it seems like they don't have an answer for.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Look, no, it's not enough to save the Democrats. The inflation issue, the illegal immigration issue, the cost of government, all of these things are going to be bigger and more important than I think abortion is going to be.\par \par \uc2 But it is both a challenge and an opportunity for Republicans. My view is that it's not enough simply to say, we're going to leave it up to the state. I think people do want to know, if you're going to leave it up to the states, what would you do if you were either voting in a referendum or if you were a member of the legislature?\par \par \uc2 And Republicans are on solid ground, I think, if they say something along the lines of, if I were doing this at a state level, I think that 15 or 16 weeks is a reasonable period to allow a woman to seek an abortion. And after that point, there ought to be three exceptions, rape, incest, life, and the mother. I believe that parents ought to be involved in the decisions of their teenage daughters.\par \par \uc2 And we should not have abortions in the second and third trimester because that's a very morally dangerous thing for us to be engaged in.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: And I think, if they did that, they -- whether you agreed with them or not, they would have a position that would be defensible and sound reasonable to the vast majority of people who might be inclined to vote for a Republican for president.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Karl, earlier today, outside of court, former President Trump has had some comments that -- on these anti-Israel protests that are happening all over these college campuses. Let's take a listen. I want to get your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : What's going on at the college level, at the colleges, the Columbia, NYU and others, is a disgrace. And it's a -- it's really on Biden. He has the wrong signal. He's got the wrong tone. He's got the wrong words. He doesn't know who he's backing. And it's a mess.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Karl, he doesn't know who he's backing because it feels like he wants to back both.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 No, President Biden's statement yesterday was mystifying. If we need -- if this is a moment where we needed moral clarity, where was the president of the United States? I thought it was very smart of the former president to go into the courtroom today, not talking about the case that he is in court about, but instead talking about the big issues that Americans are thinking and talking about as they see it played out on their television.\par \par \uc2 And this is a point at which we ought to, as a country, say, we will not accept antisemitism on our great institutions of higher education. And the idea that you could go onto a college campus and break the rules and set up these encampments and protest the acts of Israel and engage in antisemitic behavior towards Jewish students is unacceptable on our campuses.\par \par \uc2 And the police have every right to come in and remove you. And the students have every likelihood of being suspended from school if they continue to engage in this kind of activity. No, and we need moral clarity. We don't need, on the one hand, this and, on the other hand, that, as President Biden gave us yesterday.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. It's not working. And it's actually, in my opinion, probably making things worse.\par \par \uc2 Karl, it's always great to talk with you. Thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, folks, that's it for us right now. But, remember tomorrow, FOX Business, I have got a very special town hall. It's called "Making Money: Unbreakable Investor." You have to be in the stock market, bottom line. You must be in there. And I will tell you why tomorrow. Check me out.\par \par \uc2 Now here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240423ek4n0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Testimony Begins In \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Criminal Trial; Safety Concerns Over Growing Anti-Israel Protests; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Condemns Protests And Promotes Green Agenda; \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  Vying To Unseat Bob Casey; Suspect Arrested Over Break-In At Mayor Residence; State Controls Proposed Voter Initiative Language; Republicans Divided After Johnson Supports Foreign Aid; Some House Republicans Criticize Speaker Mike Johnson for Allowing Vote on Ukraine Aid Package; Trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for Falsification of Business Records Related to Hush Money Payments Commences; California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  Claims If Trump Elected President He Would Sign National Abortion Ban; Large Pro-Palestinian Protests Continue at College Campuses Across U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Bryan Llenas, Nate Foy, Alexis  McAdams, Jonathan Hunt, William La Jeunesse, Brit Hume, Ben Domenech, Chad  Pergram \par \uc2 7,783 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Claiming her 5th consecutive LPGA tour win. Time the record, she did the traditional cannonball into the water in Woodland, Texas after she won.\par \par \uc2 What a gold -- what a golden age for women sports.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Are there alligators there? I mean, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, that's it for us have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Dana, have you ever wanted to touch Jesse's hair?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I wouldn't -- no.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, OK. I'm just asking.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we're following two major stories this evening. Anti- Israel protests pop up at more schools across the country despite efforts by administrators and police. \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  canceled in person class amid concern about violence. We'll bring you there.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with opening statements and the start of testimony in the historic trial of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Correspondent Nate Foy is outside the state Supreme Court in New York with the latest today, day one in this trial. Good evening, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. During opening statements, the Manhattan D.A.'s office accused former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of conspiring to influence the 2016 presidential election while Trump and his lawyers maintain he did nothing wrong and they say this trial is an effort to influence this year's election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): During opening statements in former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Manhattan criminal trial, prosecutor Matthew Colangelo said, "This case is about a criminal conspiracy and a cover up involving Trump, his former lawyer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , and the state's first witness, former American media CEO and President David Pecker.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors argued the three engaged in a publication practice known as catch and kill were people with damaging stories about Trump received money in exchange for silence.\par \par \uc2 Cohen paid adult film actress Stormy Daniels $130,000 to suppress her story about an affair that Trump denies. Prosecutors say Trump then reimbursed Cohen but fraudulently listed those payments as legal expenses.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The case as to bookkeeping, which is a very minor thing in terms of the law.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice over): Trump's lawyer Todd Blanche said today all payments to Cohen represented legitimate legal expenses, adding Cohen received $420,000 from Trump over three times more than the 130,000 Cohen paid Daniels.\par \par \uc2 Blanche also explained to the jury that nondisclosure agreements are not illegal, and neither is trying to influence an election. Blanche called that, "democracy." While Trump maintains the Manhattan D.A.'s office is interfering with this year's election.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This has never happened before. When the state tries to insert itself in federal elections, never nobody's ever seen it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Tomorrow before David Pecker's testimony continues, Judge Juan Merchan will rule on whether or not former President Trump violated the court's gag order with several social media posts and if so, what is possible punishment might be. Court will end early again tomorrow at 2:00 p.m. if the schedule holds because of the Passover holiday, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate, today the former president's lawyers also reached this agreement with the New York Attorney General's office over the $175 million bond in his civil fraud trial just down the road. What are you hearing about that agreement?\par \par \uc2 FOY: Yes, Bret, that's right. The $175 million bond will need to be held in cash. Knight Specialty Insurance Group will not be able to move any of that money and will need to provide financial statements every single month to the New York Attorney General's office to prove that that cash is there. The New York Attorney General's office said this hearing was necessary while Trump's lawyer Alina Habba said today it was a total waste of taxpayer money and time, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy outside the courthouse. Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We are in the second week of large scale anti-Israel protests at \uc2 Columbia University in New York\uc2 . Demonstrations are also being held at several other schools, \uc2 NYU\uc2  added today. Those protests are raising safety concerns and more security is being added to campuses.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexis McAdams is at Columbia tonight. Good evening, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening from \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  where those protests continue tonight and they're spreading across the country and other campuses. You can see it behind me with that major encampment here on this campus in New York. Now there are growing calls here at Columbia for the president to step down as the president of America \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says that he is disappointed and also says he condemns the anti-Semitic rhetoric, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Do you condemn the anti-Semitic protests on college campuses?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I condemn the anti-Semitic protests. That's why I've set up a program to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 I also condemn those who don't understand what's going on with the Palestinians (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Should the \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  president resign?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I didn't know that. I'll have to find out more.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Should she resign?\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): The protests all started here at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  leading a rabbi at the Ivy League school to tell Jewish students to go home because they're not safe.\par \par \uc2 And now that fear may be spreading as protests are popping up at campuses across the country, Columbia, \uc2 NYU\uc2 , Yale and the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  all today, and more still coming in.\par \par \uc2 This morning, chaos on campus at Yale, Bret, you can see it here, groups of students waving flags and chanting pro-Palestinian slogans there. Police at Yale gave students a warning to get out or get arrested and there were dozens of arrests, watch.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE) high street or Wall Street? No one's stopping you from leaving. If you do not leave, you will be arrested. If you can't leave, if you do not leave, you will be arrested.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (voice over): Hundreds of tents are back on Columbia's campus. Last week the university called in the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  to sweep the so called Gaza solidarity encampment. More than 100 students were arrested for trespassing. And those students were then suspended from school.\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  posting to Truth Social saying in part, the Palestinian protests at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  have closed that college down. But the area surrounding the courthouse in downtown Manhattan has closed up like a drum with New York site finest all over the place. Why not send some to Columbia to protect Jewish students and others.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): Back out here live, we have seen lots of police out here, Bret, they're on standby. \uc2 NYU\uc2  and \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  have not asked the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  to come in to help clear out these encampments. But they're ready if they get that call, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 As we just showed you, President Biden condemned the anti-Israel protests after promoting his green agenda today to one of his core support groups on this Earth Day. White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Celebrating Earth Day at a national park, President Biden framed himself as the biggest defender of environmental justice in announcing $7 billion in grants to bring solar panels to low income communities and he played up the jobs factor as the schism between environmentalists and businesses grows widens on multiple fronts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Just five percent of registered voters think climate change is the most important issue the country is facing, according to a new NBC poll, but President Biden is trying to engage young voters with the politics of it.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Despite the overwhelming devastation in red and blue states. There's still those who deny climate is in crisis. My MAGA Republican friends don't seem to think it's in crisis.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden may need support wherever he can get it. That survey also showing interest in the upcoming election is at its lowest point since 2012. And though Biden has narrowed Trump's lead from five points to two, he's still trailing on the issues like inflation by 22 points.\par \par \uc2 Biden trying to connect climate change to jobs, even as some in his party say appeasing environmentalists has real costs. Pennsylvania's governor nervous about Biden's ban on new exports of liquefied natural gas.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA): I think if the pause goes on for a long time, it has the potential to cost us jobs. That's why I want this to be as quick as possible.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Celebrating Earth Day with the president progressive Democrat \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio Cortez\uc2  turn the conversation to the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): It is especially important that we remember the power of young people shaping this country today of all days, as we once again witness the leadership of those peaceful student led protests on campuses like Columbia, Yale, Berkeley and many others.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You know, I learned a long time ago, listen to that lady.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): So, Biden later issued a both sides condemnation denouncing anti-Semitic protests as well as people who, "don't understand what's going on with Palestinians." He declined to weigh in on whether \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 's president should resign.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow he heads to Florida to campaign on abortion, the biggest single issue in that poll where he leads Trump, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 This is the final day before tomorrow's primary election in the key swing state of Pennsylvania. Voters there, well, they're already focused on the big \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race in Pennsylvania in November. Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in Allentown tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): At a sporting clay shooting range in the Lehigh Valley today, Pennsylvania's Republican Senate hopeful \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  alongside Congressman Jim Jordan took political shots at his opponent more than six months before the general election.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MCCORMICK (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE OF PENNSYLVANIA: Don't think that Bob Casey being so passive means he's not dangerous.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): Three term Democrat Senator Bob Casey is facing an uphill reelection battle amid concerns about inflation and waning democratic enthusiasm from young voters.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, dozens of pro-Palestinian protesters interrupted Casey's campaign event.\par \par \uc2 In an exclusive interview with Fox News, McCormick reacted to the growing anti-Semitic protests on campuses.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: I think it's a disgrace really a disgrace. I think it's really important our public officials be very clear and strong with their moral clarity. I think \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is example in Pennsylvania has been very strong, very clear voice. Bob Casey, my opponent has been very unclear.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): McCormick a combat veteran and former hedge fund CEO is endorsed by former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 But in 2022, Trump endorsed his opponent Dr. Oz who went on to lose to Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , in part, thanks to relentless attacks calling Oz a carpet bagger. Casey says McCormick is just like us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB CASEY (D-PA): First of all, he doesn't live in Pennsylvania. He lied about living in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice over): A claim McCormick denies.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: I'm a seventh generation in Pennsylvania and I've lived the majority of my life in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: What do you make of them using the Dr. Oz playbook against you?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: I think it's not going to stick. I think they're trying to make something true that's not true.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): McCormick says he sometimes lives in Connecticut because his daughter from another marriage lives there. The Republican says he'll beat Casey because ultimately voters care about the issues inflation and immigration. The result of this race will likely decide which party controls the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas in Allentown. Bryan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is refusing to hear an appeal brought by Republican Arizona Senate candidate Kari Lake and a Republican state Senate candidate challenging the use of electronic voting machines in Arizona. Lake wanted the court to require paper ballots and a hand count. Arizona's Democratic Party says the decision is the latest blow to Lake's signature talking point about election denial.\par \par \uc2 Tech and financial stocks led the way on Wall Street ahead of a busy week for earnings reports, the Dow gained 254, the S&P 500 was up 43, the NASDAQ jumped 169 today.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a break in at the home of the mayor in one of the country's largest cities. We'll bring you there.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in Las Vegas with the groundbreaking ceremony of the Brightline West high speed rail project. It aims to connect Nevada with Southern California. The estimated completion date is Summer of 2028, right around the start of the Los Angeles Olympics. Brightline will receive a near record federal grant to a private company of $3 billion.\par \par \uc2 Fox 45 in Baltimore where a 200 ton hydraulic salvage grab is helping with the recovery operations of the Francis Scott Key Bridge wreckage. The equipment completed a week long barge journey from Galveston, Texas to arrive at Sparrows Point this weekend. It will help recover some of the 50,000 tons of wreckage at that disaster site.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Hilton Head Island. One of the big stories there tonight from \uc2 Fox Carolina\uc2 , finished his off a four shot victory at the rain delayed \uc2 RBC\uc2  heritage tournament on Hilton Head Island. Play was concluded this morning. He has now won four of his last five starts and the Masters do.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "BREAKING TONIGHT", a suspect is in custody following a break in at the home of Los Angeles Mayor Karen Bass, Sunday morning. And we're learning it's not his first serious brush with the law.\par \par \uc2 Chief Correspondent Jonathan Hunt has details tonight from outside the mayor's home.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 We have learned tonight that the man arrested here at the mayor's official residence, 29-year-old Ephraim Hunter was charged in 2015, with attempted murder and kidnapping in Massachusetts, and ultimately served seven years in that case. We are also being told by officers investigating the break in here that it was not random.\par \par \uc2 They believed that Hunter knew this is where Mayor Bass lives. And we told, the mayor, seen here on the grounds of the property, just hours after the break-in, run to what is essentially a panic room in the house.\par \par \uc2 Police arrived within minutes and arrested Hunter, who had cut his hand while breaking in.\par \par \uc2 It's unclear at this point, whether he was armed with any type of weapon. Now, Mayor Bass was at an event to unveil her new budget proposal today. She thanked the \uc2 LAPD\uc2  during a brief mention of the break in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS, MAYOR OF LOS ANGELS, CALIFORNIA: I am fine, my family is fine, and we are going to do everything we can to keep Angelenos safe and I believe our budget makes significant contributions and investments toward keeping Angelenos safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: It's not the firs- time bass has been a victim of a break-in. Back in 2022, when she was running for mayor, her private home was broken into, and two guns stolen.\par \par \uc2 Now, what we don't know, in this case, Bret, is what the intruder's intentions were. Simply to steal from the mayor's residence or to find and harm her in some way. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tune in, and follow that. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of California, there's a growing controversy in that state tonight over the title of a ballot initiative aimed at addressing gender issues in schools. National correspondent William La Jeunesse, shows us from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA, ATTORNEY GENERAL OF CALIFORNIA: You're not allowed to discriminate based on gender identity in California.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): And that's where the battle over parental rights begins.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE PEARSON, ATTORNEY, PROTECT KIDS CALIFORNIA: This is a national issue. And in terms of fighting in California, I believe that we are at war.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): A war over 100 words that define a parental notification bill sponsored by the group, Protect Kids California. By statute, however, the attorney general, in this case, Democrat \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 , controls the title and summary of an initiative he opposes.\par \par \uc2 BONTA: This policy puts them in harm's way: emotionally, psychologically, mentally, physically.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Supporters say the initiative would require schools to notify parents if a child wants to identify as a different gender. Block transgender athletes from female sports, and require students use facilities consistent with their birth, gender.\par \par \uc2 DEAN MCGEE, EDUCATIONAL FREEDOM ATTORNEY, LIBERTY JUSTICE CENTER: A majority of Californians we know support the idea of parental notification.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): By law, Bonta is required to prepare a "true and impartial title and summary." Supporters of the measure say he's not doing that.\par \par \uc2 He changed Protect Kids of California Act to restricts rights of transgender youth initiative. He also changed female students should have fair and safe opportunities to compete. Two, prohibit transgender female students from participating in female sports.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's inflammatory, it's prejudicial, it's incomplete, and it's inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice over): The parents' group took Bonta to court, where the judge, a former lawyer for Democratic Governor Jerry Brown tentatively agreed with Bonta.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Now, if the judge sides instead with the parents' group, that would force a rewrite, triggering a new 180-day window to gather signatures.\par \par \uc2 Otherwise, it's unlikely this initiative will make it onto the 2024 or 26th ballot. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, an update on aid to Ukraine and whether the House Speaker can hold on to his job.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. The head of Israeli military intelligence resigns over the failures surrounding \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's unprecedented attack last October.\par \par \uc2 Major General Aharon Haliva is the first senior figure to step down over his role dealing with the deadliest assault in Israel's history.\par \par \uc2 Japan reports North Korea launched at least one ballistic missile toward the north east today. The weapon reached a maximum altitude of 30 miles, and flew as far as 155 miles before landing in the waters east of the Korean Peninsula.\par \par \uc2 Japan says the launch violates \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  resolutions.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Earth from the International Space Station. What of the big stories there tonight. Six new astronauts are introduced by the \uc2 European Space Agency\uc2 . Five are from Europe, the six is an Australian. They are now eligible for missions to the International Space Station. They have just graduated from a year of basic training. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, way "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  appeared deeply at odds today over a small city's ban on homeless people sleeping in public places. The case involves Grants Pass, Oregon it featured emotional statements from the bench over society's competing civic responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 At issue is whether the enforcement of generally applicable laws regulating camping on public property, but targeting the homeless constitutes cruel and unusual punishment prohibited by the eighth amendment, we'll follow this.\par \par \uc2 The Ukraine aid bill that passed the House over the weekend is now on its way to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  for debate and possible passage tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 This comes as House Speaker Mike Johnson continues to resist calls for his ouster. Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram is on Capitol Hill with the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: The right furious at House Speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We expected a more conservative Speaker.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , still threatening to take out the Speaker.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): Mike Johnson has betrayed America. He's betrayed Republican voters. Some Republicans exasperated at the infighting.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE LAWLER (R-NY): To all of my colleagues, cut your crybaby nonsense out, focus on the task at hand.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Over the weekend, Greene never triggered her resolution to oust Johnson. He likely would have lost because she liked the votes.\par \par \uc2 The Ukraine bill served as a de facto vote in favor of Johnson, especially from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 REP. AMI BERA, (D-CA): I think Speaker Johnson has been sincere, been honest in reaching across the aisle and working together.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Johnson appeared today in McAllen, Texas, but the right is fuming over what the foreign aid plan lacks.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY, (R-TX): I'm frustrated there was zero border security in the bill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2 , (R-FL): We have terrorists, drugs, criminals flocking across our southern border. There is nothing in this bill.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Conservatives demand the chance to add border security to the plan.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE LEE\uc2 , (R-UT): Under no circumstances would I vote for the bill as passed by the House of Representatives. This is a good deal for Ukraine and Zelenskyy. It's a really good deal for \uc2 Hamas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Ohio Republican J.D. Vance opposes helping Ukraine. He faces a backlash from Ukraine advocates.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): I challenge J.D. Vance to go to Ukraine and get a briefing from the Ukrainian military and talk to the Ukrainian people, then tell me what you think.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Also in the bill, a provision forcing China to drop its investment in TikTok. A final vote comes Tuesday or Wednesday. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: You can choose democracy or autocracy.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON, (R-LA) HOUSE SPEAKER: We did our work here, and I think history will judge it well.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: America is going to defend democracy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): This would not have passed without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I want to thank the House speaker and Hakeem Jefferies working together in a bipartisan fashion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Supporters of that Ukraine aid bill speaking out. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " says this in an op-ed, "The evolution of Mike Johnson on Ukraine. The Speaker's tortuous path to embracing Ukraine aid is the result of many factors. High level intelligence briefings as a House leader, his faith, the council of three committee chairs named Mike, and a realization that the GOP would never unite on Ukraine. Ultimately Johnson put a Ukraine bill on the floor and he may lose his job because of it."\par \par \uc2 And just so you know, the three Mikes mentioned there, Michael McCaul, \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2 . We are told there's a fourth Mike, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  may have been part of those briefings as well.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about where things stand for the House Republicans and the House speaker tonight. Joining us, FOX News chief political analyst Brit Hume. Brit, good evening. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think Johnson is at the moment in a stronger position than one might have imagined to this date, largely because the bill was passed with a great many -- the Ukraine bill with a great many Democratic votes. Democrats are grateful that he brought it to the floor. It was a priority of the administration and of a great many Republicans, resisted by a subset of the Republican caucus. And Democrats may very well be prepared now to vote with -- vote for him in any measure to try to deprive him of his job. So I think he is in a stronger position. He is being praised by "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," which is not always a good thing for a Republican, but nonetheless, there it is.\par \par \uc2 And the thing that's so clear about this, Bret, is that this bill and supporting Ukraine is really kind of a no-brainer, when you think about it. Russia is clearly an adversary of the United States. Russia mounts this attack. Ukraine resists more effectively than anyone thought possibly at first, has inflicted enormous damage on Russian military forces and the overall Russian military. And all of this has come at a cost of to American dollars to some extent, of course, about a-sixth of the Pentagon budget is what it's been, but at no cost in American lives. There aren't any American soldiers fighting there. So when you hear complaints about an endless war, remember this. It may be a long war, but there are no Americans fighting in it. That's a pretty good deal, when you think about it, or at least it ought to be, a fact I think is lost on too many Republicans trying to oust the speaker.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are Republicans who specifically had problem with Ukraine aid and that number and all of the details of it, but several Republicans in that caucus that had a problem with the legislation pointed to the border not being a part of it, or leading the way. Here is Congressman Chip Roy, again.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY, (R-TX): It's bad for our party to go out and tell the American people you are going to secure the border of the United States when you are watching 24,000 Chinese nationals coming here, 85 percent of whom are adults, which you're watching Laken Riley get killed by somebody who was paroled by the policies of this administration. And you tell the American people you're going to secure the border. And they you get up and say, oh, I can't.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So what do you think of that, Brit?\par \par \uc2 HUME: You wonder sometimes if these people can count. The House passed a border security bill, very popular among House Republicans. They sent it to the Senate and it went absolutely nowhere because the Senate is controlled by Democrats and the Democrats didn't want it. Then there was a negotiation between the Senate and the Republicans and Democrats over a bill to deal with the border. It had many good things in it. It was endorsed by the Border Patrol union. And it was killed because House member, Republicans said they weren't going to vote for it and it was dead on arrival.\par \par \uc2 So it wasn't as if an effort hasn't been made. It has. They just simply didn't have the votes to pass. So now comes this, and the hope among Republicans was that they could lever the Democrats desire for the Ukraine bill and other things in a way of getting a border security bill passed. They didn't have the votes to do that, and so it failed. And now they did these other things, which are important, which are important, just as the border is, but separate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Slim majorities, tough to govern.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the latest on the presidential race and the trial of Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As we go to break, former Associate Press correspondent Terry Anderson has died. Anderson was a globetrotting reporter who became one of America's longest held hostages. He was held for nearly seven years after being kidnapped in Lebanon in 1985. He detailed his experience in a bestselling memoir entitled "Den of Lions." Terry Anderson was 76.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There are still those who deny the climate is in crisis. Our MAGA Republican friends don't seem to think it's in crisis.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: These are all Biden trials. This is done as election interference. Once you read the poll numbers, they're very good.\par \par \uc2 QUENTIN FULKS, BIDEN DEPUTY CAMPAIGN MANAGER: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sees the world through country clubs at Mar-a-Lago while President Biden sees it from the kitchen tables. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is only in this campaign for himself.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ, (R-TX): The Democrats, the Biden Justice Department, are more than happy to abuse the justice system to try to destroy their opposition.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Campaigning is going to have a different look this election season. Right now, the former president is on trial. The trial started today. And obviously he will be campaigning whenever he gets on camera in New York. The president is bouncing around.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the Real Clear Politics average of polls, the recent polls, there is a slight lead, obviously within the margin of error here. it's an average of polls, so there's not really a margin, per se. But that is where you see the race, pretty much tied.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " writes "The next president may be chosen by indifference. For all the buildup of presidential politics and the yearslong conversation centered on the determination of the most powerful person in politics, that conversation has been awfully repetitive recently. Americans are not enthusiastic about engaging in it once again." NBC kind of matches that. Election interest is low heading into the Biden-Trump rematch, now at 64 percent compared to other years.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Ben Domenech, editor at large for "The Spectator" host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, former \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  communications director Douglas Heye, and "Axios" political reporter Stef Kight. Stef, it is interesting to see the courtroom, and whenever the former president comes out, he is essentially campaigning every time he talks to reporters in one way or another.\par \par \uc2 STEF KIGHT, POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": He certainly is. We know that Trump and his campaign are going to make the most of the fact that he is required to be in court for much of these proceedings. And he is not afraid of embracing the moment. We saw him speak directly to the camera recently during the jury selection process this week. This is moving forward despite the fact that Trump's defense has been trying to delay the start of these proceedings. It's starting anyways, and Trump is deciding to go ahead and campaign there.\par \par \uc2 And of course, he has made the fact that he is a target of the justice system a key part of his narrative, a key part of his bid to the American people. So we're going to continue to see him make that a focus.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Doug, asked if he can get a fair trial, 50 percent of voters think he is being held to the same legal standard as everyone else. Obviously, he's in a courtroom in Manhattan. If you look at the courtroom calendar, this is like planes coming into Dulles. You've got all kinds of different things stacked up one after another. And it's going to be just a very unique election year.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE, FORMER REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Obviously, we had a unique election year in 2020 when people had to campaign in a different manner. This is wholly different. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not going to be as active on the campaign trail as we typically define it. But we are going to see him every day that he is in trial in the microphones, and then on weekends, he is going to be a weekend warrior. He is supposed to be in North Carolina this weekend. He's going to reschedule that. It was canceled for some weather. We are going to see a lot of Friday, Saturday, Sunday Trump doing traditional campaign events. During the week he is going to be nowhere but New York, it seems.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about this trial? Today, David Pecker, former head of "The National Enquirer" and that group testifying about basically this grab effort to get negative stories out, to pay for them. It's the beginning of laying the groundwork for prosecutors using this hush money, they alleged, to influence the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Look, I believe this is entirely a sideshow. I don't think that the American people care about this at all. I think the president would be wise to take up doodling on his legal pad and then auctioning them off after every day to the highest bidder. I don't think that this is a situation that actually relates to the American people at all.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that he could actually turn some of this to his benefit when it comes to the attention paid, meaning if you can't go out there, if you can't have the kind of rallies that you normally have that he's used to his benefit in the past, you can at least use the fact that there are going to be all these cameras on you at the end of each court experience to make a point, to talk about something else that's happening in the country that people actually do care about. And I think that that's something that the former president should do, and do to the best of his ability.\par \par \uc2 We already know all of this is baked in. The way that people's opinions are going to be affected by this, there is just nothing that's going to change based on this trial.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We cover it every day, Stef, and we will, all the details of each day in court. But there are some places that are obviously covered ad nauseam him and have gone through every single detail, including four times that he might have fallen asleep, everything that happens inside the courtroom. Do you think that he is going to suddenly do more events in New York, that \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rallies are in our future, or Brooklyn or Staten Island. Is that how this campaign is going to go?\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: You could see that happening. Of course, Trump knows. He is very aware of the fact that the media is covering every inch of this trial. He has always been very aware of how to work the media. It's something he's very comfortable with. He has always been a showman. And we're seeing that right now, the way that he is taking advantage of the fact that all the news outlets are paying attention to this trial. And this is a historic trial. You have to put it in the context of we've never seen a former president of the United States face this kind of criminal charge before. And so he is using this to his advantage, of course, and I think we're going to continue to see Trump and his campaign try to find creative ways to bring the right kind of attention to his campaign and keep the spotlight off of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Democrats want to keep the spotlight on the issue of abortion. NBC on the issues finds immigration and inflation ranked the highest by far, most important issues for voters. But Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  of California, eh would like to go back to abortion. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GAVIN NEWSOM\uc2 , (D) CALIFORNIA: If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  becomes president of the United States, he will sign a national abortion ban, period.\par \par \uc2 When we're focusing on him, I do worry electorally that he has a slight advantage. And so I'm a little concerned about the overindulgence, the consequential nature of a criminal trial, as profound and outsized in some respect, but not more outsized than democracy, more outsized than Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dobbs, again. President Biden is going to be campaigning in Florida on the issue of abortion tomorrow. But you saw that list of issues that are important, and you wonder how much importance abortion will have.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Clearly, in the last cycle and in special elections that we've seen, it has been a big motivator for Democrats. But those are lower propensity elections. Fewer voters come out for those. It is easier if you can raise your vote total to five percent in a district to have a bigger outcome.\par \par \uc2 But clearly this is what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Democrats want to be talking about, because if you go through polling on every other issue, Biden is completely underwater on the issue of crime, on immigration, on prices and inflation, the economy, and so forth. It is why Democrats are going to continue to talk about it. But let's also be honest. There is no way the House of Representatives and the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  is going to send any president a nationwide abortion ban in the next --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you also had Former President Trump saying it's up to the states, trying to take the issue off the table.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Of course, and one other thing is that if \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is saying that, he's basically saying the Democrats have lost the Senate. They haven't just lost the Senate. They lost it to the point of 60 votes in order to have that kind of thing happen. It's not going to happen. It's completely a joke and it's not something that I think -- at the end of the day, when we know voters, we know that they prioritize certain issues over and over and over again. And while abortion is definitely a motivator, a helper for the Democratic base, it is not going to be the deciding factor in 2024 no matter how much Democrats try to manifest it as such.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will see.\par \par \uc2 Up next, anti-Israel protests and the TikTok bill. Later, everybody cuts loose with one star marking a major movie anniversary.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do we hear that? There they are.\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, you are looking live, \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , those protests continue, anti-Israel protests. We have seen all kinds of things, pro- \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . We've seen 1,000 October 7ths should happen over the past few days. And there has been an effort. They had to go to virtual school, according to the \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  president for safety of Jewish students. This as Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  weighed in at an event today talking about Columbia, as did the president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): We once again witness the leadership of those peaceful student-led protests on campuses like Columbia, Yale, Berkeley, and many others.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I learned a long time ago to listen to that lady.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you condemn the antisemitic protests on college campuses?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I condemn the antisemitic protests. That's why I've set up a program to deal with that. I also condemn those who don't understand what's going on with the Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So both sides of that. Senator Fetterman came out with a tweet, he said "I fully agree with the White House. these protests are antisemitic, unconscionable, and dangerous. Add some tiki torches and it's Charlottesville for these Jewish students. To Columbia president, do your job or resign so Columbia can find someone who will." He's referring back to Charlottesville, and this is President Biden, then Vice President Biden, on Charlottesville, referencing formal President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He said there were, quote, some very fine people on both sides. Very fine people on both sides? With those words, the president of the United States assigned a moral equivalence between those spreading hate with those with the courage to stand against it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So Senator Fetterman making that connection. We are back with the panel. Ben, it is quite something, very fine people on both sides.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Look, if I was the president of any of these institutions and I saw a student who was in the midst of this type of protest, my suggestion would be that they return to their dorm room, put everything they have in a box, and then afterwards we would talk about what would happen to the box.\par \par \uc2 This is not a situation that should be tolerable on any level. It is something that is absolutely abhorrent. It violates their honor codes. It violates their own internal rules, it violates every kind of rule of decency that we can understand, and it puts the Jewish students on these campuses at risk, and it cannot be tolerated. And the fact that this is happening at America's highest level institutions, Ivy League institutions that we have looked up to as a group, as a community, for so many years is absolutely abhorrent to me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Robert Kraft\uc2 , owner of the Patriots, came out with a statement saying "I'm deeply saddened at the virulent hate that continues to grow on campus and throughout our country. I am no longer confident that Columbia can protect its students and staff, and I am not comfortable supporting the university until corrective action is taken." So it's spreading here, Stef.\par \par \uc2 KIGHT: Yes, this would be the third president of an Ivy League university to step down if this does end up reaching that point since \uc2 Hamas\uc2 's attack on Israel on October 7th. And we continue to see this problem on university campuses. And we continue to see how heated of an issue this is, and how much the Biden administration really struggles to navigate this heated political issue. We've seen them kind of go back and forth, it seems, on one hand. They are supporting Israel, and then criticizing Netanyahu and criticizing Israel's attack, and then back again trying to push back on the rise in antisemitism in the U.S. that we have seen in recent weeks and months.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Doug, you see the headlines here, "Harvard Crimson," "Harvard Yard closed until Friday in anticipation of pro-Palestine protests." "Politico", "\uc2 U.C. Berkeley\uc2 's campaign is in turmoil. It's unlike anything in recent memory." "Axios," "Police arrest dozens of pro-Palestine protesters at Yale." It's a number of campuses, not just Columbia.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: No, and I'm on the board of visitors for my alma mater, North Carolina at Chapel Hill, and we had an ugly day after October 7th. We had a lack of leadership that didn't respond to it. Fortunately for UNC, and this is what all of this comes down to, UNC has a new interim chancellor, Lee Roberts, who is showing real leadership. All of these schools where there's a lack of leadership, listening to all voices, and making it very clear of what is acceptable and what is absolutely unacceptable only feeds this.\par \par \uc2 And just real quickly. Congresswoman AOC said that these are student-led protests. If you look closely, all of the tents are identical, same shape, same colors. There is money funding this. These are not student-led.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But we don't know where that money is. We haven't found that thread. We're looking, but where that money is coming from.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: It's the BSD, the boycott, divest, and sanction movement that targets Israel not just on college campuses but in public libraries and every organization that you can think of. These are organized protests.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN BACON, ACTOR: Actor years, that just blows my mind. Things look a little different around here. I'd say the thing that looks the most different is me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Man, 40 years. Actor Kevin Bacon returns to the Utah high school where the iconic movie "Footloose" was filmed. Payson High School is scheduled to be torn down next spring, was hosting what's like to be its final prom. Bacon agreed to return when students said they would put together 5,000 care packages for his charity, Six Degrees. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, the Hunter Biden legal team takes a page out of Former President Trump's playbook. We'll obviously cover the trial in New York as well.\par \par \uc2 If you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here's, Laura\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240423ek4m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 New Signs of Strains in U.S.-Israel Relations; House Failing on Border Security?; Senate Prepares to Pass Foreign Aid Bill; Anti-Israel Protests Continue; Opening Statements Delivered in Trump Hush Money Trial\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Foy, Jeff Paul, Chad Pergram, Alexis McAdams, Andy McCarthy,  Sandra Smith \par \uc2 7,980 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 FOX News Alert: today's session in the New York criminal trial against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wrapping up. What went down?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 Opening statements, the first witness, today, the jury saw it. Andy McCarthy and John Yoo on what we can glean from it in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 First to Nate Foy outside the courthouse, with the very latest on what went down.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 During opening statements, the DA's office argued that Trump engaged in a criminal conspiracy and cover-up leading up to the 2016 presidential election by suppressing negative stories about his personal life, whereas Trump's lawyers argued the former president is innocent, with Todd Blanche, President Trump's lawyer, saying there's nothing illegal about nondisclosure agreements and there's nothing illegal about trying to influence an election.\par \par \uc2 He called that democracy. After court today, Trump criticized Manhattan DA \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 . Trump referenced how federal prosecutors declined to bring a case against him on the same allegations. And he says Bragg is now interfering with this year's presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This has never happened before, where the state tries to insert itself in federal elections, never. Nobody's ever seen it.\par \par \uc2 But federal elections took a total pass on them. They said, essentially, nothing was done wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Sandra, here is the state's first witness, David Pecker, the former CEO and president of \uc2 American Media\uc2 , which publishes The National Enquirer.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say Trump worked with Pecker and his former lawyer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  to suppress those damaging stories about his personal life, including allegations of an affair that Trump denies with adult film actress Stormy Daniels. Trump is accused of misrepresenting the reimbursement payments to Cohen.\par \par \uc2 But Trump's lawyer, Todd Blanche, said in court today that all payments to Cohen represented legitimate legal expenses. Blanche argues Cohen received $420,000 from Trump. That's over three times more than what Cohen paid Daniels. Blanche also said Cohen is obsessed with Trump and reminded the jury of Cohen's history of lying under oath.\par \par \uc2 Now, tomorrow, Sandra, before Pecker's testimony continues, Judge Juan Merchan will rule on if former President Trump violated the court's gag order and, if he did, what any potential punishment would be. We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Nate Foy live out of the courthouse here in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The prosecution labeling this a criminal conspiracy. Where do our next guests see this going?\par \par \uc2 With us now, former assistant U.S. attorney and FOX News contributor Andy McCarthy is here and former Deputy Assistant Attorney General John Yoo.\par \par \uc2 Great to have both of you gentlemen. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 So first to you, Andy. Where do you see this going next?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, obviously, we're going to start with Pecker, who's going to be the first witness.\par \par \uc2 I think Pecker and \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  are being offered basically to fill a big hole in Bragg's case. Bragg, his theory of the prosecution is that Trump stole the 2016 election, but he hasn't actually charged him with a conspiracy to do that, nor is trying to influence an election or suppress negative information a crime.\par \par \uc2 And what he has charged are 34 book entries that didn't occur until 2017. So he's in the irrational position of trying to say the 2016 election was stolen by activity that took place the following year. He needs to try to persuade the jury that there's some kind of nefarious scheme that leads up to the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 And what Pecker and Cohen are for is to outline the discussions that they had about this catch-and-kill scheme to purchase the disclosure rights of these stories and bury them, which it may be unsavory. It's -- certainly, the things that underlie those stories were unsavory, but that's not a crime.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Interesting, John, that the former president and his team are spending a lot of time as he steps out of the courtroom just ripping into the credibility of this case. Well, this is former Attorney General Bill Barr. I can say he's not exactly a big Trump fan of the moment. Here he is ripping the hush money trial. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: If he's convicted in New York, that thing is a travesty. It'll ultimately be overturned. That will come after the election.\par \par \uc2 But there's -- in my opinion, there's no crime there at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So where does the prosecution go with this, John?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: There's the problem for the prosecution.\par \par \uc2 It's as Andy said. They're pounding the facts because they don't have the law on their side. This is one of those cases where actually Trump has a law, as the attorney general, Attorney General Barr just mentioned, because the job of the DA in Manhattan is not to police federal elections.\par \par \uc2 It's not the job of the DA to say, I'm going to enforce federal election law and I'm going to punish \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for things he might have did for a nationwide election. That's up to the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2 . That's up to the Federal Elections Commission.\par \par \uc2 That's why, as I think Andy suggested, you're going to see the prosecution go over in excruciating detail all the sordid facts of this, because they don't want the jury to ask,why is it that we're sitting in the New York City courtroom talking about federal election fraud if any of that existed?\par \par \uc2 This is not the right place and this is not the right form. So, I think Trump, and I think Attorney General Barr is right, they have a really good case on appeal to get this overturned, although that may take after the election.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Andy, for the former president's part, when he steps out of that courtroom, what we continue to hear from him is, I should be campaigning. I mean, this is a huge detriment to his campaign not to be out on the trail while his -- the current president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , is.\par \par \uc2 His continued claim that this is just not fair, you have to start to think that's going to resonate as people see the prosecution weaker, weaker in its case.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, Sandra, no one ever wants to be indicted, obviously, but these are the lemons he's got. He's trying to turn them into lemonade.\par \par \uc2 He's got a lot of free publicity and I think he's got a very powerful message, that we have a two-tiered justice system. It's inconceivable that anyone other than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I'm tempted to say even any Republican other than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , would have been prosecuted in this case.\par \par \uc2 And Bragg five years to bring it so it would be timed for trial in the middle of the 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, so, John, bring this home for us. This resumes tomorrow morning. It will be another big day. What happens?\par \par \uc2 YOO: You're going to see the prosecution continue to bring on The National Enquirer, publisher David Pecker.\par \par \uc2 Their effort is to try to show Trump and the head of National Enquirer and \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  engaged in this conspiracy to violate the election laws. People should be taking a step back and asking, why is this case, as Andy said, being brought on frivolous grounds? It wouldn't be brought against any other person.\par \par \uc2 It's being brought because it's targeting not just a former president, but a current candidate of the Republican Party for November 2024, where they're in the lead.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And, Andy, yes...\par \par \uc2 YOO: People should ask, why is that being done?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, and they are.\par \par \uc2 And, Andy, a final word to you on that. That's another thing the former president has warned, the precedent that this sets. Final thought.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And to John's point about how limited Bragg's jurisdiction is, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  won the 2016 election in New York substantially and she won in Manhattan by about 80 points. So you have to ask if this DA is undertaking to enforce federal law, on whose behalf is he doing that?\par \par \uc2 Because he only represents Manhattan. He's not supposed to be bringing a case on behalf of all progressives across America who are disappointed that Hillary didn't win the election.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Andy, John, appreciate the discussion. Thanks to both of you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 YOO: And, as Martha mentioned, as the markets were closing, we'd cover them. And here you go. A quick look at Wall Street. After the closing bell, it did kick off the week with gains. The S&P 500 and Nasdaq snapped a six- day losing streak. Investors seem optimistic to begin the week with the Dow up 253 points, big earnings coming in.\par \par \uc2 And there seems to be some easing tensions overseas, so Dow up about 250 to kick off the week.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, with Trump in and out of this courtroom trial, how will it all play out with voters along the campaign trail?\par \par \uc2 Plus, anti-Israel protests continuing to rock university campuses, police making arrests at Yale, while we see another demonstration at Columbia. What will it take to get a handle on this? We will hear from a Jewish Yale student who just faced it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm allowed to protest.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This is what took me off and takes me off the campaign trail, because I should be in Georgia now. I should be in Florida now. I should be in a lot of different places right now campaigning. And I'm sitting here. And this will go on for a long time.\par \par \uc2 It's very unfair. The judge is conflicted, as you know. It's very unfair what's going on. And I should be allowed to campaign.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  fired up over not being on the campaign trail. What will be the political fallout from all of this?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is The Hill's Julia Manchester and USA Today's Francesca Chambers.\par \par \uc2 Good to have both of you.\par \par \uc2 Julia, to you first on this. Isn't he missing out? I mean, you have got President Biden all over, three stops in Pennsylvania last week, and the former president is having to sit in a courtroom.\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: That's a really good point, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Look, if we go back to last week, that was definitely an interesting comparison to see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stuck in a courtroom in Manhattan while \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is talking about the economy in the critical swing state of Pennsylvania. He's in Virginia today and going to be in Florida later this week.\par \par \uc2 So we're seeing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  really getting around, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is continuing to rely on these courthouse appearances, which is getting quite a bit of media attention, national and local attention.\par \par \uc2 Now, one hiccup I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did have this weekend was, during his rally in North Carolina, or it was supposed to be his rally in North Carolina, that was canceled due to weather. So that was another lost opportunity for the former president to address supporters and really campaign.\par \par \uc2 Now, that will obviously be rescheduled, but it really shows the fine line he's walking here with campaigning.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Francesca, does this give the current president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the edge since he can be out campaigning?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Well, and when you talk about North Carolina in particular, it's a state that Democrats now see as a potential pickup opportunity for them, and Florida as well.\par \par \uc2 And some might say that's potentially a stretch based on the recent voting history, but Democrats feel like that based on abortion rights and abortion rights literally being on the ballot when it comes to Florida, for instance.\par \par \uc2 And the reason that President Biden will be there tomorrow is to, one, show that this is an issue that he cares about, not just the vice president, but, two, because the campaigns as, they're taking advantage of opportunities right now, like the abortion ban going into -- or the six- week abortion ban going to effect next week in Florida, to be able to make their message.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, interesting you mentioned that. We can pull up some of the most recent FOX News polling on the Georgia presidential preference right now, Biden at 45. He's gone down from the 2020 vote to Trump's 51 Michigan 46 percent to Biden to 49 percent to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania, you can put that up on the screen neck and neck, 48-48, Wisconsin right now the same, 48 President Biden, 48 percent former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So I don't know, Francesca. Please do react to that, as it seems that the former president is narrowing some of those margins in some of the key states.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And suburban women is an area that the Biden campaign says that it will be targeting to try and boost its numbers in those states on the issue of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 But you have also heard President Biden, I think, shift his messaging a little bit on the economy right now, focusing more on tangible things that he would do, including in a second term, versus making as many comparisons to things like I have already done infrastructure for you. Here's all the ways that I think I'm better than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the economy.\par \par \uc2 Because voters just weren't feeling those things. So now he's focusing on more things like affordable childcare, what he could use the money for if he raises taxes on billionaires.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Gotcha. We will dig more into that in just a second and the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 But, first, Julia, to you on this. This is the former Attorney General Bill Barr, who we just heard from a moment ago, but also this. Over the weekend, he had this to say to Neil on his decision to support former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: You, for all your problems with your former boss and all the issues that he raised in office and all the damage you argue he had done, are still supporting him over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That's surprised you.\par \par \uc2 It didn't surprise me. I know you had said before you would support the Republican ticket. And you are, but he's heading that ticket for all those warts and problems you cited. Why?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Right. Well, yes, I'm not happy with the choice. I think this is a terrible choice for the country, but it is a choice. At the end of the day, we have to select between two different individuals.\par \par \uc2 And I have said all along that I think it's my duty to pick the person I think will do the least harm to the country. And I think that -- to me, that's clearly Trump and the Republican administration, which I think is important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Julia, your reaction to that, not happy with the choice, but he will support former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MANCHESTER: You know, I think he's saying how a lot of voters feel whether they would support Trump or Biden. This is not a popular choice for much of America. It's not an enthusiastic choice.\par \par \uc2 You see the bases of both parties really obviously resoundingly electing Trump and Biden to be their standard-bearers in November. But we see that a lot of the country is -- quite frankly, finds this choice not palatable going into November. They don't want to see a 2020 round two.\par \par \uc2 I think there was really a want for maybe some new blood, but we saw that the No Labels effort obviously did not work. Now, there is a third choice, and that third choice does seem to be RFK Jr., or he is a third choice and he could certainly stand to be a spoiler.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of -- and we don't really know who he would pull from. Conventional wisdom says he might pull from, hurt Biden in the long run, as opposed to Trump, but we're also seeing polling that shows that he will hurt Trump. So he's a wild card to watch, obviously.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Really interesting. And we're seeing that lack of enthusiasm for the upcoming presidential race certainly showing up in the polls as well.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to both of you for joining us. Julia, Francesca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 FOX News Alert to what we are learning out of \uc2 NYU\uc2  at this hour, as we have been covering the protests on \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 . Now to \uc2 NYU\uc2 . Our affiliate \uc2 NYU\uc2  is bringing us this live -- live feed from the \uc2 NYU\uc2  campus a moment ago. It showed right outside the Stern school of business.\par \par \uc2 It appears that the students are shoving signs up in the face of a camera there on campus. But we're just getting word of this right now. \uc2 NYU\uc2  has tweeted out just minutes ago: "Following a breach in the barriers set up on campus by the -- Fountain Walker.\par \par \uc2 Head of \uc2 NYU\uc2  global campus safety just delivered the following message: "To those who are congregating on the plaza, the one safety requirement we made was that no additional protesters could enter the university. With the breach of the barricades, that requirement was violated and we witnessed disorderly, disruptive and antagonizing behavior that has interfered with the safety and security of our community. We cannot tolerate people getting hurt. You will need to clear the plaza by 4:00."\par \par \uc2 That ship has sailed. We're 20 minutes past that deadline, you will notice.\par \par \uc2 "If you leave now, no one will face any consequences for today's actions,no discipline, no police."\par \par \uc2 Well, again, that's 20 minutes past that deadline, and these demonstrations continue on the \uc2 NYU\uc2  campus. And a reminder that, when we just heard from a member of Congress, \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , a few minutes ago leading up to President Biden's speech on climate change, she referenced these demonstrations as peaceful and student-led.\par \par \uc2 So they have been asked to leave, this demonstration, to leave the campus. And it is still continuing. So we will see if, as promised, there are any consequences to their actions. We're watching this live out of \uc2 NYU\uc2 , New York City.\par \par \uc2 More after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Anti-Israel protests across U.S. campuses continuing, as we look live now, one under way at \uc2 NYU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Coming up, hear what one Yale student says he encountered on campus.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: FOX News Alert, as we continue to look at growing demonstrations coast to coast, this one now at \uc2 NYU\uc2  here in New York City, a growing number of what appears to be students gathering there.\par \par \uc2 The university says that barriers were breached. They have been asked to leave by 4:00, 25 minutes ago. They have not. We're waiting to see what happens next.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McAdams has been reporting on the scene at Columbia as those protests continue today, now another one -- Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, you remember just the other day, right, they got rid of all of these tents. You can check it out here to our left. And now there's more than 100 back here on campus. So the university did allow us to come back on. We did try to talk to some of the demonstrators. They weren't interested in talking to us just yet.\par \par \uc2 But just a short time ago, the president of the United States, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , condemning a lot of these protests and a lot of the antisemitic remarks. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do you condemn the antisemitic protests on college campuses?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I condemn the antisemitic protests. That's why I have set up a program to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 I also condemn those who don't understand what's going on with the Palestinians and their -- how they're being...\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Should the \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  president resign?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I didn't know that. I will find out more about that.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Should she resign?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: This afternoon, a handful of protesters arrested in front of the gates at \uc2 Columbia University, New York\uc2 , just hours after the president, Minouche Shafik, shifted all students to virtual classes, calling for a reset. So we will have to see what that means here.\par \par \uc2 So, now dozens of tents, as you can take a live look right now, are back up on campus. The university called in the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  just the other day to sweep the so-called Gaza solidarity encampment. More than 100 students then were arrested for trespassing, and then those students were suspended.\par \par \uc2 The protests have erupted here at Columbia, but they're popping up across the country, right, like at \uc2 NYU\uc2 , Sandra, where you just saw there are also tents and hundreds of students rallying. And this morning, chaos on campus at \uc2 Yale University\uc2  in New Haven, Connecticut, and at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  in Ann Arbor, groups of students waving flags and chanting pro- Palestinian slogans, police at Yale telling students to either get out or get arrested.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 XAVIER WESTERGAARD, COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY STUDENT: The president of \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  had a fantastic opportunity in front of Congress to show the world last Wednesday that she stood against antisemitism, and all she did was lie to Congress.\par \par \uc2 She said that students had been suspended. She said that several professors who had said vile, violent, inciting things about the Jewish people were being removed. And it's turned out that all of these students are more than welcome to be on campus. Minouche Shafik needs to resign. And if she doesn't want to resign, she better do something about this because this is going from bad to worse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: So we will have to see what plays out here with the president of Columbia, but these protesters here over here to our left are not fond of her either.\par \par \uc2 So it's not just the Jewish students that are calling for her to resign and their parents who pay all this money, right, to send them to these schools, but also these protesters. Once again, we did try to talk to them, tried to get in here and take a look at their encampment. We were not invited in, so we will keep you posted on if we hear anything from them -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, Alexis McAdams, keep us posted live there at Columbia for us, as we're watching what's happening play out now at \uc2 NYU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Our next guest says he encountered exactly what we're discussing, the growing threats on our college campuses. Netanel Crispe is the \uc2 Yale University\uc2  student seen in this video who says he felt terrified during this on-campus incident. It happened on Saturday. Netanel joins us now.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we did reach out to the \uc2 Yale University\uc2  president for an interview and have not yet heard back.\par \par \uc2 Netanel, thank you very much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 Please do weigh in now on these growing protests that are happening all over the country, but in New York City, we have been watching \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 , to the north of here, obviously what's happening in New Haven, and now \uc2 NYU\uc2  campus, we have got these live pictures up of a growing number of students protesting.\par \par \uc2 What has this moment been like for you? And what have you personally experienced on campus?\par \par \uc2 NETANEL CRISPE, \uc2 YALE UNIVERSITY\uc2  STUDENT: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 First off, thank you so much for having me. And to all the Jews out there, happy and kosher Passover. As I mentioned, it's truly terrifying. As a Jewish student at Yale for the first time, I feel like I'm just not welcome.\par \par \uc2 And that's been made abundantly clear to me and my peers by this kind of action. As we traverse campus, we are harassed. We are flipped off. We have people chanting "Free Palestine" at us no matter where we go. We have our daily activities interrupted. We can't go into finals and study when there are people shouting and using blow horns until 3:00 a.m. in the morning and taking over our dining halls and our libraries.\par \par \uc2 And most recently, as I have seen in that video, when I have tried after the Jewish Sabbath ended to go on campus, I was physically barred by these protesters, really these rioters, as they noticed that I was Jewish and they surrounded me and obstructed my ability to move around the common space, which I have every right to walk around as much as anybody else.\par \par \uc2 As they allowed other students who clearly were not identifiably Jewish to walk in and out, only we were impeded. Later on, they stole an Israeli flag from me and they assaulted my friend, jabbing her in the eye with a Palestinian flag, sending her to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 And throughout all this, Yale just remains unwilling to take the necessary steps to really stand up for its values.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Wow. I mean, anybody who sees that, who is looking on reasonably, and Netanel says, wow, you should not have to go through this. And this is happening.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I spoke with a student that attends \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  on our program this afternoon. He witnesses other Jews being spit on as they walk through the campus, being violently threatened as they walk through their campus. They don't feel safe. They have got the rabbi there saying, go home. You're not safe.\par \par \uc2 And while all this is happening, you have got a member of Congress leading into the president of the United States speech today. This is Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , and I will get your reaction to this, praising the protests that are happening on these college campuses as peaceful. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): It is especially important that we remember the power of young people shaping this country today, of all days, as we once again witness the leadership of those peaceful student-led protests on campuses,like Columbia, Yale, Berkeley, and many others.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Peaceful student-led protests. Your reaction to that, Netanel?\par \par \uc2 CRISPE: I would ask, where is the peace with Jewish students being shoved and grabbed, having arms wrapped around them, obstructed in every moment?\par \par \uc2 Where is the peace in people tearing down the American flag at Yale's World War I memorial and attempting to burn it on the heart of campus? Where is the peace in stealing other items from Jewish students and from assaulting and jabbing a Jewish student in the eye with a flag? There's no peace at these events.\par \par \uc2 These have gone to the wild. This is a mob, is a riot. And Yale has not only failed to proactively engage with it and try to prevent it and stand up for its principal values using real action to expel and discipline these students, but instead has done the complete opposite. They continue to present empty threats.\par \par \uc2 And, in this case, as happened with us just Saturday night, they removed the police presence and security from the scene, leaving Jewish students like myself completely at the mercy of this mob. So there's no peace to this. This is people who are supporting terrorism, glorifying martyring terrorists with signs and memorials that they have set up, and calling for intifada and the murder of Jews around the world.\par \par \uc2 So there's no peace here.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We wish the best for you. And please do stay safe.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, looking into some of these campuses and what the ability is for police to intervene when these things are happening, we know Columbia's campus doesn't allow the police to come on unless they are specifically called by the university.\par \par \uc2 But I'm being told \uc2 NYU\uc2  is a bit of a different situation, and there might be more -- the police might have more control to come on campus when something like this does happen. We're digging into that.\par \par \uc2 And, Netanel, it is so important that we cover this for you, because this is just an incredible time for all of you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CRISPE: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, to the new border brawl, as passage of an aid package for Israel and Ukraine closes in, why agents from the Border Patrol union are speaking out. One of them is here.\par \par \uc2 And as those pump prices have many drivers seeing red, what are the impacts as the Biden administration touts its Earth Day push to go green?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The Senate set to take up that $95 billion for an aid package after House passage this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Many Border Patrol agents expressing extreme disappointment as it lacks anything for the border here. We will talk to one of them in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 First to Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill with the political fallout -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The lack of border security is one of the biggest disappointments among conservatives with the foreign aid package. The right implored Mike Johnson to add border security to the bill, especially if the House voted to aid Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY (R-TX): There was zero border security in the bill. And, by the way, to be very clear, that border bill, H.R.2, that was voted on today, will have no force of law, was not part of the package, was done as a cover vote and gave cover to five Democrats who voted for it.\par \par \uc2 It's mind-bogglingly stupid to have put that on the floor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Conservatives are upset that the bill sends money to Israel, Ukraine and Taiwan, yet is not paid for. That's why the right is fuming at House Speaker Mike Johnson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORY MILLS (R-FL): We continue to reach in our pockets to pay for these endless interventionism, this neoconservative neoliberal warfare, when it's not our responsibility. This should be being handled by Germany, by Italy, by France, the people who won't even pay 2 percent of GDP of \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  did not try to unseat Johnson over the weekend, but that remains a threat.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, some Republicans are arguing with each other over money for Ukraine. Ohio's J.D. Vance opposes helping Ukraine. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is one of Ukraine's biggest advocates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): It's another thing to go. I challenge J.D. Vance to go to Ukraine and get a briefing from the Ukrainian military and talk to the Ukrainian people. Then tell me what you think.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: GOP Florida Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  says the border is the number one issue facing the country, not the border of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Scott wants to add border provisions to the bill. The Senate likely votes on the bill tomorrow or Wednesday -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We will be watching for that with you. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Agents with the national Border Patrol union say they are beyond disappointed as this package makes its way to passage. The union's vice president, Art Del Cueto, is one of them. He's here to tell us why.\par \par \uc2 Art, your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 ART DEL CUETO, VICE PRESIDENT, BORDER PATROL UNION: I mean, it's -- honestly, it's a slap in the face to not just the men and women that are out there on the front lines, but to the American public.\par \par \uc2 And I believe, right after the bill had been sent up, somebody said that, hey, now our allies and the people who've trained in the front lines can sleep comfortably knowing that America has their back, all the while the law enforcement officials and the agents that are out there putting their lives on the line, the American public at night near the border, they need to find out who has their back, because obviously this bill doesn't.\par \par \uc2 And, hopefully, there are some provisions that are brought up. But that's - - again, the big sticking point a couple of months ago with one of the border bills that the National Border Patrol Council was in favor on, one of the big sticking points was, hey, we don't want to do this because it sends money overseas.\par \par \uc2 Yet here we are, fast-forward, and now there's a bill by itself that sends money overseas and completely ignored the border in its totality.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes. Art, what you're saying is so important. So make sure you can lean into that microphone the best you can, so we can pick up every word you're saying.\par \par \uc2 But the point beyond even the basics of what you're laying out there is just, give us a plan. I mean, you all are facing this crisis at the border every single day. You know what it takes. You know the resources you need. Just, what's the plan? That -- those details are vague and rarely ever laid out or given to you.\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: Right.\par \par \uc2 And that's why it's extremely frustrating. Like I said, we have backed up bills before that all we wanted to do was bring them up to the floor, look at them and make amendments, that you can make amendments and fix.\par \par \uc2 But all that has been continuously ignored. But here we are where we're at now.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: We have a bill that only covers overseas and completely ignores Americans, law enforcement on the line, our borders.\par \par \uc2 It's a complete slap in the face to everybody on that border.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Art, if you could, with so much news that is happening all over the country right now and it being a presidential election year and we're all over the place, we're coming out of these -- the protests and the former president's trial happening here in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Our eyes are a little bit off the border at the moment. And people depend on those live images to show us what's happening every hour of every day there. How would you characterize this point in the border crisis, Art?\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: It continues to be chaotic.\par \par \uc2 And you're right. Sometimes, the new cycle will concentrate on other things.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Right.\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: But we need to continuously be talking about it, because we don't want it to become the norm.\par \par \uc2 For the longest time, we saw groups that would enter in different areas, and people would make, like, a bigger deal out of it.\par \par \uc2 Now, unfortunately, it's become the norm. You're seeing groups in caravans continuously come to the border. You're seeing drugs enter the United States at record numbers. And a lot of people don't talk about it because it's become the norm. It's absolutely not the norm.\par \par \uc2 And, at the end, the only ones that are gaining from this, because it's not the Republican and Democrats that are losing.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: It's the American public that's losing. And it's the drug cartels that continue to line their pockets because of this chaos that are winning. And that's the problem.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And that is a big warning from you.\par \par \uc2 Art Del Cueto, thank you for joining us today. I appreciate your time, sir.\par \par \uc2 Are Iranian proxies back to targeting U.S. troops in Syria? Why a series of rocket attacks on the heels of Israel and Iran's actions are raising that very question. We will have the latest for you.\par \par \uc2 Plus, new information as we see anti-Israel protests now at \uc2 New York University\uc2 . This is a live look from our affiliate WNYW, outside \uc2 NYU\uc2 . This is obviously Tisch Hall, as you can see on the sign there outside the School of Business. A growing number of students, as well as others, are gathering there. They have been told to disperse. They have not.\par \par \uc2 We're following this protest live at \uc2 NYU\uc2 . We will have more in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: A U.S. official confirming a U.S. military base in Syria was attacked for the first time since February 4, the aggression following Israel's counterattack on Iran last week and comes as tensions in the Middle East are escalating.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Jeff Paul has been following it all from Tel Aviv for us.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it's been nearly three months since we have seen any sort of attacks on U.S. military bases here in the Middle East, but that, of course, all changing yesterday, when U.S. forces had to respond to several rockets that were seemingly fired at a nearby base.\par \par \uc2 All of this happening in Northeastern Syria. A U.S. official tells FOX News that U.S. forces had to respond to at least five rockets that were fired from Zummar, Iraq, by an Iranian proxy group. No U.S. service members were killed or injured, and the base was not damaged.\par \par \uc2 This is the 171st attack on U.S.