ery time Donald opens his mouth, you know that something non-truthful is coming out of it. We also know that he's not a good defendant. He's not a good witness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He does tend to get frustrated with judges, with attorneys in the courtroom. He's given to hyperbole at times. This is different than a campaign stop or a press conference. I mean it's under oath. Would - would he take the stand and do you have any worries about that?\par \par \uc2 SCHARF: I think that's going to be a decision for President Trump and the trial team depending on how the trial progresses. I would say that I think if he does take the stand, President Trump will be a compelling witness. He did nothing wrong here. The truth is absolutely on his side. So, that's a decision that's going to have to be made down the road. But one way or the other, I have full confidence in both President Trump and the team that he has around him.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How worried are you about \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , because he says people are going to be surprised by some of what they're going to hear. He says he's not the linchpin to this whole thing. In fact, Lanny Davis, who's been a legal advisor to him, says this. "The case against Trump involves multiple witnesses besides Cohen to corroborate the crime of paying illegal hush money under New York law, as well as under federal law, backed up to multiple witnesses, documents, text messages, telephone call records and documents."\par \par \uc2 You guys clearly are going to go after Cohen, but he says it's not just me, there are all kinds of other things that will back up my story.\par \par \uc2 SCHARF: Well, look, I'm somewhat limited in what I can say here because of the wildly unconstitutional gag order entered against President Trump by Judge Merchan in this case, which just goes to show how outrageous this whole process has been.\par \par \uc2 With respect to \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , as we've said consistently in pleadings, this is a man who numerous courts have found guilty of perjury in the past. And I think ultimately the jury is going to have to weigh the credibility of the various witnesses. But I truly believe the truth is on President Trump's side and there's nothing that any of these witnesses or anything the New York D.A. can do to change this basic fact.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Would the legal team consider this idea that's being floated out there of, when you get to the jury, before they get to deliberations, asking for lesser included offense, meaning, giving them an opportunity to say, we will convict on misdemeanors instead of these felonies? Or do you guys want to go for broke, it's felonies or nothing?\par \par \uc2 SCHARF: Well, one of the craziest things about this case, and you teased this a little bit in your intro, is the fact that what we're dealing with here are misdemeanor offenses that through this weird, legal alchemical (ph) process, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  has attempted to convert into felony offenses. If these were merely misdemeanors, there's no question the statute of limitation would not allow this case to have been brought in the first place. So, that's a real legal problem with - with the case itself, and I expect that that will play out in court in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. And we're out of time, but I know that you are part of the Supreme Court decision or case that's going to be argued on the immunity issue too. We'll have you back to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 SCHARF: We'd love that. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Will Scharf, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, we're going to take you back live to Israel to get the very latest on the ground as the world awaits Israel's response to Iran's barrage of drones and missiles amid new warnings from Iran to both Israel and the U.S., next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RISHI SUNAK, BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: And what we now need is for calm heads to prevail. We'll be working with our allies to deescalate the situation. And I look forward to speaking to \uc2 G-7\uc2  leaders later this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will keep track of that. That was British Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  this morning in London.\par \par \uc2 Iran launching an unprecedented air attack against Israel. The U.S. military helping to shoot down dozens of the hundreds of drones and missiles fired at the Jewish state.\par \par \uc2 Fox News foreign correspondent Trey Yingst is live on the ground in Israel around the clock. He's got the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good morning.\par \par \uc2 As we speak, the Israeli war cabinet is meeting, trying to determine if and how Israel will respond to this unprecedented Iranian attack overnight that included hundreds of drones and missiles. Just before the meeting began, war cabinet member \uc2 Benny Gantz\uc2 , the former defense minister, released a statement saying that Israel was reviewing its options and wanted to build an international coalition to respond against Iran. He also said Israel reserved the right to respond at a time and place that best suits Israel.\par \par \uc2 Right now the Israeli people are bracing for the possibility of continued attacks from Iranian proxies across the region. Remember, they are still fighting the war inside Gaza. They've faced renewed fire from Yemen and from Lebanon. And again, this fire overnight, the first time in history that Iran launched an attack on Israeli soil.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst, we appreciate you, your team, your crew. Stay safe. And thank you for giving us a picture of - a window into everything that's going on there. We appreciate your work.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020240414ek4e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Issues Warning To Iran; Trump Says Things Are Working At State Level; House Passes Reauthorization Without Warrant Rule; Afghan National With Terror Ties; Agents Encounter Hundreds Of Migrants Each Day; Highly- Trained Teams Compete Amid Combat Scenarios; Navy Under Secretary States U.S. Military Behind Schedule In Building New Submarines To Replace Retiring Ones; Numerous Intelligence Sources Indicate Iran Set To Attack Israel Despite Previously Biden Administration Warnings; Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Gives Speech On Abortion Rights In Arizona; President Trump Holds Press Conference With House Speaker Mike Johnson\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Aishah Hasnie, Chad Pergram, Grady  Trimble, Mike Tobin, Madison Scarpino, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 7,608 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LISA KENNEDY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I do think Miley Cyrus had the better version of Jolene.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Still a great version.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: \uc2 Sure\uc2 . You -- absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you to Fox Channel --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: For Dolly, on her version.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thank you to Fox News Channel, Fox Nation, and especially Greg Gutfeld.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Who helped me through this tough period? I don't think I could have written this book without your help.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And for the psilocybin.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, that's it for us. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Is that really what it says, Kennedy? KENNEDY: Oh, yes. The word for word.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I need your -- see. OK. All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", the U.S. is moving additional assets into the Middle East as Israel prepares for Iranian retaliation over the death of one of its military leaders. A new report out of the region states the attack could come today or tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has a familiar warning for Iran tonight, but also seems to concede an attack could be coming soon.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich picks up the story tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Since \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacked Israel on October 7th, President Biden has repeatedly warned Iran not to escalate conflict in the region through its proxies. But now that Iran is vowing direct action, including potentially against U.S. troops as well, President Biden is going a step beyond the word, don't.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don't want to get into secure information, but my expectation is sooner than later.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): With an imminent attack on Israel expected, President Biden delivering a short warning to Iran.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Don't.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Or else, what?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Turning around to spell it out.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We are devoted to the defense of Israel. We will support Israel, we will defend -- help defend Israel, and Iran will not succeed. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Iran reportedly issued the Biden administration a warning of its own earlier this week through intermediaries.\par \par \uc2 If the U.S. gets involved, American forces in the region will be attacked. It's not clear if defensive acts like intercepting Iranian missiles would trigger that.\par \par \uc2 But Biden's critics say America is already involved because of inaction.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: We've lost deterrence. Right? The Iranians have already attacked Israel once on October 7th, through their proxy, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . They are continuing to fire on American interest in the region, now, over 100 times since October 7th. And so, what we're talking about in the next 24, or 48 hours is just a continuation of a failed policy to actually protect Israel.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Iran reportedly told Arab allies it holds the U.S. responsible for a likely Israeli strike that killed Iranian general in Syria. Israel has not taken responsibility for it, and the U.S. says it was not involved.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon now moving more assets into the region to bolster deterrence and increased force protection for American troops, the CENTCOM commander in Israel to coordinate.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL HOFFMAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: What happens safter Iran launches their strike? How does Israel respond? How do we respond?\par \par \uc2 It's not a good time to go wobbly if you're the president of the United States. This is one of those times when leadership really matters and message discipline matters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The U.S. has said publicly and privately that it will help Israel defend itself against Iranian attacks but officials are reportedly pressing Israel for advanced notice before any retaliatory action after top Pentagon brass complained that they were in the dark ahead of the strike in Damascus that put us forces in the region at risk. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , says he will testify in his upcoming criminal trial about payments to a former adult film actress before the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 The former president making that comment while speaking to reporters following a meeting with House Speaker Mike Johnson in Florida. He also attacked President Biden on his mental competence, defended his pro-life stance and send a signal of support to his embattled guest.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is in Palm Beach, Florida tonight with the latest. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Yes, former President Trump, making a lot of news today in what was supposed to be a press conference about election integrity. Let's start off with the major news that he says. He is willing to testify in his New York criminal court case that begins on Monday. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT AND PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I would testify. Absolutely. It's a scam. It's a scam. That's not a trail. That's not a trial. That's a scam.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): So, he also could not miss an opportunity to talk about the threat from Iran to Israel, saying, this country has a lot of issues to fix starting in November.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We have a president that doesn't know where he is. He can speak. The whole world is collapsing. The world is on fire. They have no respect for our country anymore.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): Trump also took a question on abortion and whether or not he's pro-life or if he is pro-choice. He wouldn't say, Bret, how he feels personally. Only saying that it's now up to each state.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We broke Roe v. Wade, and we did something that nobody thought was possible. We gave it back to the states, and the states are working very brilliantly, in some cases conservative, in some cases, not conservative. But they are working and It's working the way it's supposed to.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): And finally, Trump throwing speaker Mike Johnson a major lifeline today, as Johnson faces a motion to vacate filed by Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump, all but quashing that effort as Johnson stood right next to him.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're getting along very well with the Speaker, and I get along very well with Marjorie. We have a Speaker. He was voted in, and it was a complicated process. And I think very -- it's not an easy situation for any Speaker.\par \par \uc2 And I think he's doing a very good job. He's doing about as good as you're going to do. And I'm sure that Marjorie understands that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, Bret, all of this, again, coming out as Speaker Johnson announced that House Republicans will introduce a bill about election integrity. That would require every voter to prove their citizenship. Republicans essentially daring Democrats to vote against this bill.\par \par \uc2 A White House spokesperson released a scathing statement in response, calling the bill a stunt and writing this. "As was proven by the midterms, the American people continue to be revolted by the debunked 2020 election conspiracy theories that cost brave police officers their lives, just like they're revolted by the violent rhetoric that accompanies the repetition of those dangerous lies.\par \par \uc2 Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie in Palm Beach, with Steve Doocy fan behind her. Aishah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  has paid some of President Biden's legal fees despite the campaign's criticism of similar behavior from former President Trump and his campaign. The fees helped cover costs related to the special counsel probe of the president's handling of classified documents. A Trump campaign spokesman is calling it "lies in his hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  spokesman tells Fox News Digital, "There is no comparison the DNC does not spend a single penny of grassroots donors money on legal bills, unlike \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who actively solicits legal fees from his supporters and has drawn down every bank account he can get his hands on like a personal piggy bank." The back and forth over paying for legal fees.\par \par \uc2 The House has passed a bill to reauthorize and reform a key government surveillance tool. The measure does not include broad restrictions on how the \uc2 FBI\uc2  uses the crucial program to search for Americans data, and that has led to some extra steps.\par \par \uc2 Senior congressional correspondent Chad Pergram, explains tonight, live from Capitol Hill. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, strange bedfellows and strange congressional customs when passing the FISA bill. The vote 273 to 147. But the House rejected a GOP plan that required warrants to eavesdrop on Americans. The left opposed the warrant mandate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA): We could have been saved from 9/11 if we didn't have to have the additional warrants.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): It was like the world flipped upside down. The House defeated the amendment that on a tie vote, by rule, tie votes lose in the House, a technicality some embraced.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The amendment is not adopted.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): I feel like we're living in bizarro world.\par \par \uc2 The FISA authorities were used against BLM lingers. And it was members of the Congressional Black Caucus that killed the warrant requirement.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): And while the House passed the bill, it cannot go to the Senate just yet.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Miss Lee of Florida moves to reconsider the vote on passage of H.R. 7888.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For what purposes gentleman from Ohio seek recognition?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2  (R-OH) (text): I have a motion at the desk.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The clerk will read the motion.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN COLE, READING CLERK OF THE UNITED STATES HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES: Mr. Turner of Ohio makes a motion to table the motion to reconsider.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Now, allow me to interpret all that. FISA bill opponents blocked a routine motion declaring the bill finished. So, the House votes next week to complete the bill.\par \par \uc2 If that fails, Congress is stuck. The FISA deadline is April 20th. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you for the translation. Congressional speak. Chad Pergram, live on the Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 An Afghan national with terrorist ties is in federal custody tonight. But authorities say how he arrived there is troubling. He was apprehended at the southern border and then released twice into the U.S. Senior correspondent Mike Tobin has the disturbing details from El Paso, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voice over): On the terror watch list, but Mohammad Kharwin from Afghanistan roamed free in the U.S. for 11 months. Picked up and released twice.\par \par \uc2 A federal source says Kharwin entered the U.S. at San Ysidro, California. In March of 2023. He was not yet on the watch list, so, Kharwin was set free.\par \par \uc2 But federal agents acknowledge, Kharwin is tied to \uc2 Hezb-e-Islami\uc2 , a radical group said to be responsible for the deaths of nine American civilians and soldiers in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 When information about his ties to the group was developed, Kharwin was picked up again in February of this year. For reasons unexplained, federal prosecutors did not give that information to a judge, so, Kharwin was released on bail.\par \par \uc2 He has been picked up again, but a federal source says, "Based on the information available, there is no reason to believe this individual entered the country as part of a terrorist mission."\par \par \uc2 The border remains porous. Our cameras caught around 30 migrants reaching the wall in El Paso Friday morning. Their body language indicates they are confident they will get in. The city of El Paso says agents average just shy of 1,000 such migrant encounters every day.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT ALMONTE, FORMER UNITED STATES MARSHAL, EL PASO: It's important that we do something to secure the border because we're experiencing too many criminal that have already come into our country illegally.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice over): Releasing pictures of a new stash house that was raided in El Paso, agency, they have found 136 depots for human smuggling this year. That's already half the total of last fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR MANJARREZ, FORMER CHIEF BORDER PATROL AGENT: That's becoming the normal course of business. Right? It's a stash house almost every day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): Customs and Border Protection just released new numbers showing 76 people on the terror watch list were picked up trying to get into the U.S. this last fiscal year. The numbers also show that traffic here at the border is down since September, but up significantly since President Biden took office.\par \par \uc2 Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin, along the border in El Paso. Mike, Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Renewed inflation fears and stocks tumbling today. The Dow lost 476. The S&P 500 was down 76. The Nasdaq fell 267.\par \par \uc2 For the week the Dow was off two and a third percentage points. The S&P 500 fell 1-1/2. The NASDAQ was down about a half.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as interest rates remain sky high, we look at another obstacle for people trying to buy a new home. We'll bring you that story.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. The Kremlin says a draft peace agreement negotiated by Russia and Ukraine and the early days of conflict could serve as a starting point for talks to end the fighting.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine has since rejected the proposal. The reportedly included provisions for Ukraine's neutral status and put limits on its armed forces while delaying talks on the status of Russian occupied areas.\par \par \uc2 Thailand's foreign minister says he has urged Burma's military authorities to avoid a violent response to its army's loss of an important border trading town.\par \par \uc2 The hasty escape by Burmese forces seated virtual control of the busy trading town to gorillas of the ethnic Karen National Union and its allies.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Rome. One of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  will visit Indonesia, East Timor, Papua New Guinea, and Singapore in September. The Vatican announced that trip today. It would be the longest such journey for Francis's papacy.\par \par \uc2 The trip comes amid concerns about the Pope's health. He suffered from respiratory problems all winter and had to curtail his participation in Holy Week events.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A new Biden administration rules states oil and gas companies will have to pay more to drill on public lands. They must also satisfy stronger requirements to clean up old or abandoned wells. The Interior Department regulations raise royalty rates for oil drilling by more than one-third. Montana Republican Senator Steve Daines, in response, saying the administration will stop at nothing to appease the Climate Lobby at the expense of the pocketbooks of working families.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we examine another major obstacle to people looking to purchase a new place to live.\par \par \uc2 The cost of home insurance is up almost five percent compared to last year. That combines with already sky-high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Grady Trimble takes a look tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The price of the American Dream of homeownership is higher than ever. And some of the biggest expenses have nothing to do with your mortgage payment.\par \par \uc2 Homeowner's insurance costs for example, are up 4.6 percent from last year, according to the latest inflation report.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They are at historical levels right now, but they are moderating. The fact that the rate increases are moderating is a good sign that we are seeing more stability in the home insurance market.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): The reason for the rise, experts say, is twofold. The cost of repairs and the materials you need to do them are going up. And the number of people filing claims is climbing after more and more severe natural disasters.\par \par \uc2 The Economist makes the case in its cover story this week, climate change is only going to make it worse, potentially wiping out about a 10th of the value of the world's housing by 2050.\par \par \uc2 CHASE GARDNER, MANAGER, INSURIFY DATA INSIGHTS: These companies are taking a look at their numbers and they're having to adjust it to a new level of risk expectation.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): The insurance comparison platform, \uc2 Insurify\uc2 , projects homeowner's insurance premiums are on track declined by six percent by the end of the year, hitting more than $2,500 on average.\par \par \uc2 GARDNER: These companies, you know, are going to have to keep raising rates to keep up with an increasing number of natural disasters.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (voice over): \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  says as of 2022, insurance combined with utilities, property taxes, and home improvement expenses account for more than half of the costs of owning a home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (on camera): More recent data from \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  finds nearly one in 10 insured homeowners aren't confident that they'd be able to afford their insurance premiums at their next renewal date. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Grady, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we go inside one of the toughest tests for America's elite warriors.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX54 in Augusta as \uc2 Tiger Woods\uc2  makes the cut for a record 24th consecutive time at the Masters. The 15-time major winner is at one over par, 336 holes. Texas's Scottie Scheffler is one of three players sharing the lead at six under as the second round wraps up.\par \par \uc2 Fox 13 in Memphis as a police officer and a teenage suspect are killed early this morning. A second officer in critical condition. The officers were shot when they approached a suspicious car. They returned fire striking two suspects.\par \par \uc2 FOX5 in New York, as several high school age students and a bus driver are hurt when the bus collides with another vehicle this morning in Westchester County. The injuries did not appear to be serious. The driver of the SUV was also transported to a hospital.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at San Francisco from FOX2, one of the big stories there tonight. Oakland votes in favor of changing the name of the city's airport to San Francisco Bay Oakland International Airport.\par \par \uc2 This comes despite San Francisco threatening a lawsuit over what it says is a trademark violation. San Francisco officials say the name change will confuse travelers, especially, those flying in from abroad.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look, "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY", from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight, we take you inside one of the toughest tests in the U.S. military.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Madison Scarpino gets a preview of the Army's Best Ranger Competition, where elite warriors have their physical and mental limits taken to the extreme. She reports tonight from Fort Moore, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MADISON SCARPINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL MULTIMEDIA REPORTER (voice over): An annual showcase of the country's toughest soldiers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: True American heroes out there., busting their butts every day to prepare themselves not just for the competition, but for what could happen in the future against our adversaries.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): The Best Ranger Competition starts with 56 teams from 29 units from across the army.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. ERIC KIM, UNITED STATES MILITARY ACADEMY: The competitors out here are just high caliber athletes and soldiers and just a brotherhood that we have amongst each other. That's just the best part of the competition.\par \par \uc2 Nice Folks.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): By day three, only one team will get the coveted Best Ranger title.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. CHANCEY TILLERY, UNITED STATES MILITARY ACADEMY: It's good to have that mental toughness going forward and understanding that there are a lot of best rangers here, and they are the best of the best for a reason.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Two, one, (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): 32 challenges mimic real combat situations.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO: This is the perfect example. Right now, Rangers are shooting pistols at different targets. And as you can see, the key to this challenge is precision and focus.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): Fox got the chance to get a small taste of the action. Before the competition started. We jumped out of a 34-foot tower the minimum height that simulates jumping out of an aircraft.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let's go.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): We also went through an obstacle course called The Punisher. While the name sounds daunting, it's nothing compared to what the Rangers do.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the competitions Rangers get little to no sleep. The soldiers will run upwards of 60 miles. For about half of that, they will be carrying dozens of pounds of weight. By Saturday, half the teams will be eliminated.\par \par \uc2 In Fort Moore, Georgia, Madison Scarpino, Fox News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: China is surging sales to Russia of machine tools, micro-electronics and other technology used to produce weapons for its war against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. assessment says about 90 percent of Russia's micro-electronics came from China last year. They were used to make missiles, tanks, and aircraft. Nearly 70 percent of Russia's approximately $900 million in machine tool imports in the last quarter of 2023 came exclusively from China.\par \par \uc2 There are growing concerns tonight about a shortage of American nuclear submarine power. This comes as China increases its fleet.\par \par \uc2 Here is chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ERIK RAVEN, UNITED STATES NAVY UNDER SECRETARY: Submarines have a unique role in securing the peace. Their ability to move unchallenged through all around the world is an incredible asymmetric advantage.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: The problem, the U.S. does not have enough submarines for itself. Old subs are being retired faster than they can be replaced. The Pentagon is 17 nuclear attack submarines short of Navy requirements and needs to build 2.33 Virginia class submarines per year to meet its August commitments. Undersecretary of the Navy Erik Raven overseas Navy strategy and shipbuilding.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. submarine industrial base is an estimated 33 years behind schedule. Why is it so behind and backed up?\par \par \uc2 RAVEN: In 2011 the United States basically doubled its orders for nuclear powered submarines. All told, in the last 15 years, our demand for industry to produce submarines has gone up by roughly five times.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Republican Senator Roger Wicker has raised alarm bells, inserting $3.4 billion into the Ukraine funding supplemental to jumpstart the anemic ship building base.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER WICKER, (R-MS) \uc2 SENATE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE\uc2 : We have yet to take the actions necessary to ensure that our industrial base can support both the United States and Australia. The basic fact is this -- our defense industrial base is not where it needs to be.\par \par \uc2 RAVEN: We're going through a generational change in the workforce at our shipyards, and also our suppliers. And so we need to build new talent, bring in more Americans to work in the sector. And also on supply chain, COVID was a significant interruption in our ability to do business. Over the next several decades we need to hire about 100,000 Americans to build submarines. We're partnering with even community colleges and nonprofits.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: One of those nonprofits is BlueForge Alliance, a campaign to attract young people into submarine building, an appeal to patriotism, a modern day "Uncle Sam wants you."\par \par \uc2 KILEY WREN, BLUEFORCE ALLIANCE CEO: There are literally tens of thousands of opportunities over the coming decade and more for long term, family sustaining, generational jobs that have a really important purpose. The ability to serve your country in a job that you go to every day is an enormous sense of pride.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: How would the supplemental help you with the investment in the industrial base?\par \par \uc2 RAVEN: It accelerates everything. Six months ago, the president asked Congress for $3.4 billion to accelerate investments into industry so we can produce the submarines that we need. And I urge the Congress to pass that as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: In this year's defense budget proposal, the Navy is seeking a total of $3.9 billion for the submarine industrial base, a $2.9 billion increase from last year.\par \par \uc2 China right now is building a submarine wall. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 RAVEN: I don't focus as much on what our adversaries are doing as accelerating our capabilities. We have an incredibly capable submarine force that's capable of tackling any challenge that our adversaries may pose.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: So you can breakthrough any submarine wall?\par \par \uc2 RAVEN: Our submarines are incredibly capable.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: At the Pentagon, Jennifer Griffin, FOX News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  shareholders have just overwhelmingly approved the company's sale to Nippon Steel of Japan for $14.1 billion in cash. Now, the Biden administration has objected on economic and national security grounds. More than 98 percent of the shares voting at a special investor meeting approved the takeover. That deal, however, still needs approval from the Biden Justice Department as well as other cabinet secretaries.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the Iran threat, the meeting between former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Speaker of the House, plus that new version of Candidate Casino.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- Iran in this moment?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Don't.\par \par \uc2 To any country, any organization, anyone thinking of taking advantage of the situation, I have one word -- don't.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: To any actor, state or non-state, trying to take advantage of this cris to attack Israel, don't.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2 , DEFENSE SECRETARY: Anyone thinking about trying to take advantage of this atrocity, we have just one word -- don't.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm not sure Iran is getting that word because they are doing, according to reports in the region. This is "The Wall Street Journal" saying an "Iranian attack expected on Israel in next two days." "The scenarios involve attacks by Tehran's proxies in Syria and Iraq for which Iran delivered drones at warehouses in recent days, according to advisers of the IRGC and the Syrian government. Another option would be to strike Israeli embassies, notably in the Arab world, to show them that friendly ties with Tel Aviv could be costly, these people said." It's one of several reports throughout the region saying the same thing.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Marc Thiessen, Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist, and Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 . Marc, the "don't" doesn't seem like it's working.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": It is not. And look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was absolutely correct what he said in that press conference today with Mike Johnson. The Iranians were broke when he came into office because of the crippling sanctions he had put on the regime. He also warned them that if you kill a -- he drew a red line and said if you kill a single American, we will -- either you or your proxies, we will not strike the proxy, we will strike you. And when they crossed that red line he killed \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And that restored deterrence in the region, and it lasted until \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came in and allowed them to kill Americans with impunity. They killed Americans on October 7th. There were Americans killed there. Their proxy, \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , is holding Americans hostage. And then they kept firing hundreds of missile attacks on U.S. troops and eventually killed an American. So this is weakness. And you and I both served in the Pentagon when Rumsfeld was there, and he said weakness is provocative. That is a very important lesson to remember. And we're seeing how provocative it is right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I was covering it. You were working for Rumsfeld.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you are right. This is the former CIA director, former secretary of state, Leslie, and also a person who was warned and was under the threat of attack from Iranian terrorists, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , about this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: What we're talking about in the next 24 or 48 hours is just a continuation of a failed policy to actually protect Israel. It won't surprise me if it's a strike that has some level of ambiguity so that the Iranians won't unite the whole world against them. So think, maybe, it could be a cyber strike. It could be a strike through a proxy.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, they may just decide to come over the top and launch missiles directly from Iran into an Israeli asset somewhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's not often, Leslie, we talk so openly about an attack that's going to happen. Everybody across the board is saying that it is. Israel is preparing for it. The question is what happens afterwards.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, a number of things here to unpack. First, to me, you don't show your cards, OK? So, to me don't, don't. We don't need to tell you what we're going to do. Use your imagination.\par \par \uc2 Two, I understand what Marc said regarding October 7th. But we are talking about retaliation, so says Iran, for Israel allegedly attacking their guys, right? And some people would say what was Israel thinking?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: For directing terrorist attacks --\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: And how many mistakes can we have?\par \par \uc2 First of all, Iran does not have the capability to fight Israel, certainly not the United States. They don't have it. Not only do they not have the money or the weaponry. But who does is \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . And \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is right there on the Lebanon border with Israel, and that is a huge concern. But I am confident not just in our president, I'm confident in our Pentagon, I'm confident in our military, our forces throughout the world and our allies to keep their word to Israel, as we always have, and to protect them.\par \par \uc2 Iran would be a parking lot if they truly attacked Israel. I mean, what, in minutes? When you look at how many nuclear weapons Israel has, depending on what report you believe, this would be a suicide mission by Iran to -- I think their bark is bigger than their bite here. I certainly hope I'm right on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, you know, the thing is, Matthew, and responding to Leslie, we've heard that "don't" all the way for months now about proxy attacks. It was don't about the Houthis, which to this day continue attacks on U.S. bases in the Middle East and even ships. We're adding more to the region in response to these threats, but I get the ambiguity, Leslie, but Matthew, the "don't" is still going.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: Bret, administration policy has been all over the map. I mean, the clips we showed at the beginning of the segment had administration officials declaring solidarity with Israel after October 7th, whereas just a week ago President Biden was now calling on Israel for a unilateral cease-fire and to stop the offensive in Gaza and not go into the last \uc2 Hamas\uc2  redoubt of Rafah.\par \par \uc2 For weeks the administration rhetoric has been more focused on Israeli malfeasance than what \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has been doing or what Iran has been doing. And now suddenly they turn around and wonder why Iran feels emboldened to retaliate for this strike against one of their terrorist commanders that was hiding out in Syria.\par \par \uc2 The administration has lost the plot on the Middle East. They have to recognize that the real threat here is Iran. And we have a common threat between Israel and the United States, which is Iran, and it's time we stopped treating the Iranians like they are some kind of off the board piece that doesn't matter. Maximum pressure, sanctions, and hit their proxies hard, and even, as Trump did and as the Israelis did, go after Iranian assets directly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. I want to play a soundbite from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , vice president, she was in Arizona on a now familiar topic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Overturning Roe was just the opening act, just the opening act of a larger strategy to take women's rights and freedoms, part of a full-on attack, state by state, on reproductive freedom. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the architect of this healthcare crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, the vice president on the attack there in Arizona. Our latest poll says voters trust President Biden more on election integrity and the issue of abortion. But then you look at the economy and immigration, and it's completely different to the former president. Marc, your assessment of this, and obviously we are going to see a lot of it before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Well, you look at that poll, the number one and number two issue for the voters are the ones they don't trust \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on. The ones that they care less about are less driving the voters are the ones that Democrats have strength on.\par \par \uc2 Look, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  -- \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the most unpopular president in the history of the presidential polling. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is less popular than him. She literally -- they would probably -- they would probably think about getting rid of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  if they didn't know that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  -- they would have to ditch \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as well, and the party of identity policies is not going to ditch the first black woman vice president and keep her from the White House.\par \par \uc2 So she is going to go out there. She is going to talk about abortion because it's literally, other than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the only issue they have to campaign on, because he is underwater by double digits on every single other issue that voters say they care about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, the vice president is on the move campaigning.\par \par \uc2 We're going to take a little trip down the road to a place we call Candidate Casino. And it's the vice presidential version, not with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , but on the other side. Who will former President Trump pick? You have $100 in chips. Start with you, Leslie. Who are you going with?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: I'm going $20 on Tim Scott, $40 on \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 , and $40 on \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 . And because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says he wants a woman, I put more money on the women.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I'm going to put $80, Bret, on \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 . And -- I'm sorry, the rest of my money I'm going to put in honor of \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  to vote to wine, women, and song.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you. That's good to be back on the table. Thank you for your bets. Marc?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It's $40 on Rubio, $20 on Tim Scott, $20 on Stefanik, $10 on \uc2 Sarah Sanders\uc2 , and $10 on \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  as the dark horses.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, spreading the money around. All right, panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Friday Lightning Round. We'll talk about FISA, plus Winners and Losers, and later "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I'm not a big fan of FISA, but I told everybody, I said do what you want. They put a lot of checks and balances on. And I guess it's down to two years now so that it would come due in the early part of my administration on the basis that we live up to the polls.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Mar-a-Lago with House Speaker Mike Johnson. A big vote today on Capitol Hill. "The Hill" writes it this way, "House approves contentious FISA bill in bipartisan vote. The House in a bipartisan vote reauthorized the nation's warrantless surveillance powers. The chamber cleared the bill which will extend and reform Section 702 of the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act, FISA, 273 to 147 vote." They still have to clear it out of the House on Monday.\par \par \uc2 We're back with the panel. Matthew important, number one, and number two, the fact that the former president was standing alongside the House speaker today and what message that sent.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I think it's important vote. There are real concerns about FISA abuse and the actions of some of our national security agencies when it comes to surveillance. But I think this two-year bind dough gives the house Republicans and some of their progressive allies on this issue a chance to build in real accountability measures to make sure those abuses don't occur, especially when we're under this threat, Bret, where the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director is warning of ISIS-K attacks when we have this problem at the border.\par \par \uc2 How important it is for Mike Johnson that Trump is by his side? I think very important. I think it sends a strong signal to Johnson's enemies like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  of Georgia. The question is, when it comes to that Ukraine vote, which Speaker Johnson has promised in the coming weeks, will Trump be there with him? And will Johnson be able to withstand the pressure after that vote goes through?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, the supplemental, obviously, there is more things than just Ukraine in there, as we have been reporting. But, to your point, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director on these specific threats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: There was already a heightened risk of violence in the United States before October 7th. Our most immediate concern has been that individuals or small groups will draw some kind of twisted inspiration from the events in the Middle East to carry out attacks here at home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You look at the terror watch list encounters, this is from the southern border, Leslie. These numbers are staggering from FY 21, 22, 23 and this is year-to-date, what we're seeing, people specifically captured or intercepted along the southern border with terror watchlist names.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: The most recent one was an Afghan man who the judge was not told by ICE the classified information that he had been on the terrorist watchlist. Some people point fingers at ICE. Some people point fingers at the judge. I would point fingers at the inability to have so much information given to so many people who need more people who need more money. And that money could have been possible if legislation had been passed the Republicans held up and didn't want passed to help our Border Patrol --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Oh, Leslie.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: -- to help our judges and the legislative system with immigration, to help ICE, and so forth.\par \par \uc2 And I also want to point out, even though we get scared at who is coming over our border, I'm old enough to remember that on 9/11 19 people were here legally on visas, whether they be student visas, whether they be work visas, or whether they be visitor visas.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, Marc.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, the president has the same laws on the book today that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had when there wasn't be a border crisis like this. So he could enforce the border right now. So the blame is on Congress is absolutely false.\par \par \uc2 But this just shows also why its' really important that the House pass the FISA bill, because right now our open borders are putting us at grave risk of a terrorist attack, but if the House had not passed the FISA authority and hamstrung the intelligence community to try and stop an attack, they would have shared the blame for something that happened.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, let's do a quick Winners and Losers. Matthew, first. Winner, then loser.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: My winner this week are the three Americans who were rescued off a remote Micronesian island by the Coast Guard after writing out "HELP" with palm leaves there. You can see that. They definitely won the week.\par \par \uc2 My loser is Shohei Ohtani's former translator Ippei Mizuhara who was indicted for stealing $16 million of Shohei Ohtani's money for sports gambling. And one of the emails in the indictment, Bret, Mizuhara writes that "I guess I'm not good at that sports betting thing." That's not the only thing he's bad at.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, lost in translation. OK, Leslie, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Winner, Oakland County, Michigan, jury who found James and Jennifer Crumbley and sent them to prison, found them guilty, parents of the Michigan school shooter.\par \par \uc2 And my loser, the state of Arizona for taking the state back to the 1800s and for punishing women and for doctors along with that decision.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, winner and loser, Marc.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: My winner is Speaker Johnson, got FISA passed and got an endorsed from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Now he's got to get Ukraine over the finish line. My loser is \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 . Emerson said if you're going after the king, you better kill him. She failed. I'm looking forward to a stronger majority where we can go back to not caring what \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, make it a great weekend. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 You know that sound. It's Friday. It's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is not a time for panic. It is a time for heightened vigilance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't trust you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would you call it a crisis at the southern border?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I would.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think what he is doing is a mistake.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Any Jewish person that votes for a Democrat or votes for Biden should have their head examined.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is there anything the general public can take away from this?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, this could happen to you, too.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On his death bed, I don't know if he said a penance or not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A very strange, complicated, disturbing life.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Let me give you a hug.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We don't feel like this is a snake in the grass waiting for his chance to bite us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can't we rise up for our country and not worry about our politics?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He would sign off on a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The states would determine by vote or legislation or, perhaps, both.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I will never stop to deliver student debt relief.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why would you water your neighbor's flowers while your house is burning down?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was a man's world. They didn't know how capable us women were, did they?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are one tiny part of this vast universe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Watching "The Flintstones." I still miss that show, although, I could never translate late "yabba dabba doo."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Japanese prime minister there.\par \par \uc2 Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, jury selection begins in former President Trump's first criminal trial. We'll bring you there.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Please join Shannon Bream on FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guest, White House national security communications adviser John Kirby, Louisiana Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2 , and Trump attorney Will Scharf.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight, tonight and all this week. a lot of news. That's it for SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. I, personally, looking forward to Masters time on the T.V. "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE" is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240413ek4c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Turn Back The Clock: Biden: Extremists Don't Want People Of Color In U.S.; Media Can Warp The Perception: Woman Who Met At \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  Raves About Former President; Now: Trump, Speaker Johnson Hold News Conference; Trump, Johnson, Unveil Election Integrity Bill; Unlocking Their Minds: Companies Look To Offer Magic Mushrooms While At Work\b0\par\par\uc2 Kennedy Montgomery, Richard Fowler, Jeanine Pirro, Pete Hegseth,  Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 9,898 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: By the way, we are going to be exploring the implications of this, certainly what's going on in Israel -- \uc2 Ehud Olmert\uc2 , the former prime minister, my special guest tomorrow. We're going to look at the impact for the economy in the world as this could potentially escalate. We're on that tomorrow. See you then. THE FIVE is now.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY MONTGOMERY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Hello, everyone! I'm Kennedy along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Richard Fowler, Pete Hegseth, and Greg Gutfeld. It is 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 We are awaiting former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and House Speaker Mike Johnson to talk about election integrity. They are at Mar-a-Lago and we will bring it to you live the moment it happens. But now, let's get to this.\par \par \uc2 Desperate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is in a panic as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hoovers up Black support like no Republican in history. The big guy trying to win back the key voter group by giving a live virtual address at Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 's annual civil rights conference.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There are real threats we face. There are more extreme voices out there who simply don't want to see people of color in the future of our country. They want to turn back the clock. Voter suppression, election suppression, ripping away reproductive freedom, getting affirmative action, gutting it, and attacking diversity across American life.\par \par \uc2 These extremists are determined to erase the progress we've made. But together, we are determined to make history not erase it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 K. MONTGOMERY: The problem is The Don is catching on with African Americans. The latest aggregated Fox News national surveys show \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tripling his support among Black men and Black women since 2020. And sleepy zoom call is no match for The Don's retail politics game. Trump hitting up a \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  in Atlanta and handing out over 30 milkshakes.\par \par \uc2 One supporter is going viral after giving the former president a big old hug. And what she said about the media should have Democrats quaking in their boots. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAELAH MONTGOMERY, FOUNDER, CONSERVE THE CULTURE: I don't care what media tells you, Mr. Trump. We support you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 K. MONTGOMERY: That woman later giving the media more jazz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 M. MONTGOMERY: We were able to not only let him know that regardless of what social media says, you know, I know they're trying to make us think we're supposed to hate you, but we don't. And additionally, it was a learning experience for my students because they were able to see and experience firsthand how the media can warp the perception of an opinion or a person.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 K. MONTGOMERY: She's absolutely right, Richard. So, this is very interesting because the president so far in some of these battleground polls is doubling his support, particularly with Black men. And what are the issues affecting them? They're not voting as a blob. They're individuals who are as concerned about the economy and immigration as any other voter in the country. Does this surprise you?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: No, it doesn't. I've actually done some reporting on this for both Forbes and BET. And I think the media in general, right, including right here at our network, I think what we've gotten wrong is I think we see the elections, especially for Black male voters in this binary of voting for Trump or voting for Biden when the truth of the matter is there's three choices. They could either choose -- they could also choose to sit this election out.\par \par \uc2 I've done two stories about how Black male --\par \par \uc2 K. MONTGOMERY: Maybe four choices?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Maybe -- and that could be four choices, sure. But when you talk to Black male voters, right, no matter where they live, and I've done reporting all across the country on this, what you find is they'll tell you the same thing. On average, they're the bottom of all of the quality of life indicators in this country. And they'll tell you that life hasn't gotten much better, whether it was George Bush, whether it was \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , whether it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , whether it was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . They still feel that their lives have not improved under any presidency, which is why the idea of them sitting this election out is so easy for them to do.\par \par \uc2 But I think there's an interesting electoral marker to consider when you think about Black men. In 2023 in Ohio, where reproductive health was on the ballot -- reproductive access for women was on the ballot, the group that voted in the highest percentage for protecting a woman's right to have reproductive health or access to abortion was actually Black men by eight points. They voted 88 percent to protect a woman's right to have an abortion, outvoting white women, outvoting Black women, outvoting every other subgroup, which says to me as an analyst that these voters, they might be up for grabs but when they show up to vote at the polls, they tend to vote on issues that you wouldn't expect them to vote on.\par \par \uc2 K. MONTGOMERY: But one of the issues that they are voting on, Judge, is immigration. And, you know, one of the things that they are not getting from this administration is reassurance that the President and his administration are going to protect their jobs.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, they're certainly not getting any reassurance from this administration. And in fact, I think one of the reasons that we're seeing more of the African-American community move toward \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is because of immigration, because of the fact, if you look at places like Chicago, you know, the minority community, the Black and the Brown community in the inner cities are losing to the immigrants who are taking over places where they had after-school programs or projects that kept kids out of gangs and all that. And they're prioritizing the immigrants over them, subjugating the African-American and the minority community.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, when -- I have to comment on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . When he gets up there and he's not willing to answer a question, he's zooming in to \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 's meeting there -- at least \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  showed up. I don't know if she answered any questions. But he said, there are people who want to turn back the clock. There are people who don't want to see you get ahead. I mean, that is the kind of evil messaging that is not part of the factual basis that people are living in today.\par \par \uc2 They look back at how their lives were four years ago, in fact, how their lives were right before \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office and they know it was better under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, you know what, you can threaten them, scare them, and tell them that, you know, these people are trying to put you back in chains, which I think is what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has said and it's just not the case. Blacks are smarter than that. Don't terrorize them.\par \par \uc2 K. MONTGOMERY: He also said, if you don't vote for me, you ain't Black.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 K. MONTGOMERY: He said that. He said that on a radio show. But, you know, Pete, we look at some of these numbers and it shouldn't be wholly surprising because the President has doubled his support. What happens if he doubles it again before the election? And how likely is that based on some of the immigration and economic numbers?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It's possible. I mean, these polls are definitely or probably voting for Trump. That seems fairly definitive. Yes, some could decide not to vote, but definitely or probably is pretty strong. And those numbers are staggering. We had them up for a second, but six percent of Black women in 2020 voted for Trump.\par \par \uc2 Oh, I think we've got the press conference.\par \par \uc2 K. MONTGOMERY: And speaking of Trump, Fox News Alert, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Speaker Mike Johnson holding a press conference right now. Let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have a border that's open. We have a lot of problems in our country. And we have an election problem and that's really what we're here to talk about today. Speaker Johnson's going to be briefing you on what we discussed, what we agree on. But I would like to demand that our border be closed because we have millions of people coming into our country.\par \par \uc2 Millions and millions of people are pouring in at levels that nobody's reporting, nobody's going to talk about, but I believe you could have 15 million already in, some are terrorists. They come from jails and prisons, they come from mental institutions and insane asylums. They come from all over the world, not just South America, they're coming from all over the world.\par \par \uc2 Venezuela announced that their crime is down 67 percent because of the fact that they've taken the gang members, the leaders, and the members. And they've deposited them very nicely into the United States of America, not just Venezuela. It's happening with the Congo, it's happening with countries all over, Africa, Asia, South America, all over the world. It's happening.\par \par \uc2 Our country is like a dumping ground and we're going to have it stopped. And Biden should do it immediately. He should close the border immediately. He needs no legislation. He doesn't need this gentleman. He doesn't need anybody. He can do it. I did it without any legislation. I had the best border we've had in ever, recorded history. He can close it immediately.\par \par \uc2 We had to stay in Mexico, remain in Mexico. We had catch and release in Mexico, not here. We had everything. It was perfect. It was a great situation we had. And now, we have the worst ever. I don't believe in the history of the world there's been a border like this. But we probably have 15 million people, and they come from places that you don't want to know about. And they're going to be big problems and it's getting worse.\par \par \uc2 It's migrant crime. It's a new category of crime, migrant crime. And I'm just demanding -- I just demand -- as a citizen, I demand the border has to be closed. Our country cannot take it. No country could take it. It's not sustainable by any country.\par \par \uc2 And we want to also mention election interference. And we want to talk about election integrity for a little while but basically that's what we're working on. And I'll introduce the speaker who you know very well. He's doing a really good job under very tough circumstances. And I appreciate he came to Mar-a-Lago. We have a great conference and there's a big turnout because people want this to be healed. They want our country to be healed. They want our country to be united.\par \par \uc2 And I think most people are united on the fact that they don't want people pouring in from prisons and jails and mental institutions. They don't want that to happen. They don't want it to take place. And it's not going to happen for long. And that's why I say that November 5th is going to be the most important day in the history of our country. That's the election. It's going to be the most important day in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much. And Speaker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): Thank you so much, Mr. President. And it's good to see you all. We're so delighted to be here with our former and future president in this beautiful facility. Mar-a-Lago is famous around the country and we're grateful to be here. With President Trump as our nominee in the party, we are very much looking forward to that historic day in November. Because we are going to grow the House majority, we're going to win the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , and we're going to win back the White House as well.\par \par \uc2 The American people need us to do that. They're excited for that day and November. It cannot get here soon enough. The Speaker of the House, when we're not in session, the Speaker is required to go around, fly around the country and be with all of our candidates and incumbents all around. I've been to 23 states now in the last several weeks. And everywhere we go, one of the first questions that people ask about is this issue of election integrity.\par \par \uc2 The borders, the number one issue in America. There's never been a political issue that scored so high in the polls as a matter of concern. And it doesn't matter where anyone lives, because as we say now, every state is a border state. They're deeply concerned about that. And election integrity is tied to border, the lack of border security.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has created a catastrophe, and he did it by design. We documented 64 specific executive actions that he took from the day he walked into the Oval Office and that his agencies took under Secretary Mayorkas to open the border wide, to invite everybody from around the world to come here, including hardened criminals and dangerous persons. And the result, we all know.\par \par \uc2 The official number is nine million encounters at the Southern Border alone, just since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office. But the actual number is probably close to 16 million illegals who come into the country because they desired it. They designed it. They engineered it to be that way. His actions did that. He ended the Remain in Mexico Policy under President Trump's administration. They reinstated catch and release that had been practiced under the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration. They stopped building the wall. They did everything exactly the opposite of what this president had achieved. And that's why we have this catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 It has all sorts of terrible effects on the American people. We know that fentanyl was the leading cause of death for Americans aged 18 to 49. We know that violent crimes are being committed on innocent Americans now. Laken Riley and many others who are now losing their lives because these dangerous people are in our country. And it must stop. And the American people are demanding that it does. And it doesn't matter which political party that any American is in. They have the same desire. They want safety and security. And we can supply that.\par \par \uc2 But among the problems that flows from this open border catastrophe is directly related to this threat to election integrity. Why is that? You need to understand something really important about federal law. Since 1993, the National Voter Registration Act, we call it the motor voter law, allows people to sign up to vote when they get a driver's license. If an individual only asserts or simply states that they are a citizen, they don't have to prove it. They can register that person to vote in a federal election.\par \par \uc2 And you see, states are currently prohibited -- believe it or not, the states are prohibited from asking someone to prove that they're a citizen. The federal voter registration form just has a check-up box. And if you do that, you're good. The states can't allow it. We think that's a serious problem. And so, what we're going to do is the House Republicans are introducing a bill that will require proof of citizenship to vote. It seems like common sense. I'm sure all of us would agree. We only want U.S. citizens to vote in U.S. elections. But there are some Democrats who don't want to do that.\par \par \uc2 We believe that one of their designs, one of the reasons for this open border, which everybody asks all around the country, why would they do this? Why would they allow this chaos? Why the violence? Because they want to turn these people into voters. Right now, the administration is encouraging illegals to go to their local welfare office to sign up for benefits.\par \par \uc2 Well, guess what? When you go to a welfare office, they also ask you if you would like to register to vote. And so, many people we think are going to do that. And you know what? The numbers are so high, there's so many millions of illegals in the country, that if only one out of a hundred voted, they would cast potentially hundreds of thousands of votes in the election. That could turn an election. This could be a tight election in our Congressional races around the country. It could, if there are enough votes, affect the presidential election. And so, that's why House Republicans are going to act.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to announce to you today here staying alongside President Trump that we will do everything within our power to ensure that we do have free and fair elections in this country. If we don't have that in a constitutional republic, we have nothing. It's the basis of who we are as a nation, and we owe that to the American people.\par \par \uc2 And so, what we're going to do is introduce legislation to require that every single person who registers to vote in a federal election must prove that they're an American citizen first. They have to prove it. That will be a new part of the federal law and a very important one. Our bill will establish new safeguards. It will put us on par, by the way, with virtually every other democracy around the world that also prohibits non-citizen voting. And this is a critical thing for us to do at a very critical time.\par \par \uc2 Our bill also will require states to remove non-citizen from their existing voter rolls. That's a big problem too. And it will provide access to databases from the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and the \uc2 Social Security Administration\uc2  to help the states administer this. As the entity that is responsible for regulating federal elections, Congress has this responsibility. We cannot wait for widespread fraud to occur, especially when the threat of fraud is growing with every single illegal immigrant that crosses that border.\par \par \uc2 This is something most Americans are deeply concerned about. The latest poll says 78 percent of the Americans who are polled say that preventing illegal immigrants from voting in our elections is a top priority. In every place I've gone around the country, whether it's out west, Midwest, Long Island, Deep South, it doesn't matter, everybody is concerned about this. We have a job to do.\par \par \uc2 Here's what you need to look for and I'll turn it back to the President. When we put this bill on the floor, you're going to see a record vote by Republicans and Democrats. You'll see that the Republicans stand for election integrity, and then we'll be able to ask this very important question to the Democrats. They're going to have to go on record. Do you believe that Americans and Americans alone should be the ones who vote in American elections? We're about to find out their answer, and I think that will be a very interesting one for everybody to see.\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, thank you again for hosting us today. I'll turn it back to you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes, sir. Thank you. Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Thank you. Do you have any questions?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you support \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 's motion to vacate or do you stand behind Speaker Johnson.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're getting along very well with the speaker, and I get along very well with Marjorie. We have a speaker. He was voted in, and it was a complicated process, and I think very -- it's not an easy situation for any speaker. I think he's doing a very good job. He's doing about as good as you're going to do. And I'm sure that Marjorie understands that. She's a very good friend of mine. And I know she has a lot of respect for the speaker.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And would you support the Ukrainian aid bill if the Speaker was too moved forward with it?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We're looking at it right now, and they're talking about it, and we're thinking about making it in the form of a loan instead of just a gift. We keep handing out gifts of billions and billions of dollars, and we'll take a look at it. But much more importantly to me is the fact that Europe has to step up, and they have to give money. They have to equalize. If they don't equalize -- I'm very upset about it, because they're affected much more than we are. The Ukrainian situation would have never happened if I was president. It would have never, ever happened. And everybody says that, including Democrats, that it happened. It's such an outrage.\par \par \uc2 People -- millions of people are dead right now, both sides. Millions of people are dead. Cities are blown to ashes. You'll never rebuild those cities. That's certainly not like they were, so beautiful. And this is something should have never happened. October 7th should have never happened in Israel. It should have never happened. What happened there was outrageous. Iran was broke when I was president. People weren't buying oil from Iran. They weren't allowed to. If they were going to buy oil from Iran, then they weren't going to do any business in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And I said it to China. I said it to everybody. They weren't doing business. They were broke. They didn't have money for \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . They didn't have money for \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . It would have never happened. October 7th would have never happened. It did happen, and now it's a disaster. And it's only getting worse. So, it's very sad. No, I stand with the speaker. We've had a very good relationship.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, while you were an office, you said that you would sign a federal abortion ban if Congress sent it to your desk. Why should Americans trust your word that you would not do it now if you were reelected?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because we don't need it any longer, because we broke Roe v. Wade. And we did something that nobody thought was possible. We gave it back to the states. And the states are working very brilliantly, in some cases conservative, in some cases not conservative. But they're working, and it's working the way as opposed to. Every legal scholar, real legal scholar, wanted to have it go back to the states, Democrat, Republican, Liberal, Conservative, and we're able to do that.\par \par \uc2 You know, what we did was give it back to the states, and now the states are working their way through it. And you're going -- you're having some very, very beautiful harmony, to be honest with you. You have -- well, you have some cases like Arizona that went back to like 1864, or something like that, and a judge made a ruling, but that's going to be changed by government. They're going to be changing that. I disagree with that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Excuse me. Please.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Just a follow-up. Over the -- over the last few decades, Mr. President, you have both considered yourself pro-choice and pro-life. Which one is it?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, you know exactly which one it is. And when I was in New York, and when I was a Democrat, also, just like \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  -- you know, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was a Democrat. We sort of followed a very similar path. But if you look at what we've done with Roe v. Wade, we did something that everyone said couldn't be done. And we got it done. And I give great credit to the Supreme Court and the justices for having the courage to do it.\par \par \uc2 What they did is very simply give it back to the state. And I'll tell you, the Democrats are the radicals on this because they're willing to have abortions in the seventh, eighth, ninth month. They're even willing, and you can call it what you want, but you go back to the governor of Virginia, the previous governor of Virginia, the Democrat governor of Virginia, we're talking execution of a baby after birth. And you can say what you want, but that's extreme and that's radical and nobody should have that. And it has to be ended.\par \par \uc2 Please go ahead. Go ahead, please.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you, sir. Mr. President, do you think the motion to vacate rules should be finally changed or do you think that it's just created chaos right now?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, it's unfortunate that people bring it up because right now we have much bigger problems. The country is a -- we're a nation in decline. We're a declining nation. We have tremendous inflation. The inflation is coming back at levels that nobody thought they really would have. If you look at the categories of inflation, they have the worst categories. Many categories are not included. And if you included that, your inflation number would be substantially higher than it is now, and it's already at records.\par \par \uc2 So, inflation is back, and a lot of bad things are happening in our country, but that's the least of it. You've got Russia could end up -- you could end up in a world war between Russia, Ukraine, and all of the chaos, and that's something should have never happened. It would have never happened. What's going on with Israel, October 7th, and what's going on with Israel could end up in a world war.\par \par \uc2 We have a president that can't put two sentences together. We have a president that can't find the stairs off a stage. We have a president that doesn't know what the hell he's doing, and we could end up in a world war. You know, we have just a little bit less than seven months now -- months before November 5th, but that's an eternity when people are incompetent.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Go ahead, please.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you support the House of FISA bill that was proposed this morning?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Which one?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The FISA bill? And did you talk to anyone --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I'm not a big fan of FISA. I looked at it, and I studied it, and I know it probably better than anybody. You know, they spite of my campaign. You do know that, right? And they did lots of other bad things. I'm not a big fan of FISA, but I told everybody, I said, do what you want. They put a lot of checks and balances on, and I guess it's down to two years now. So that it would come to the early part of my administration on the basis that we live up to the polls, because all the polls we just had another one come out. We're leading by a lot. But it comes out quickly. I said, you do what you want. But I'm not a big fan of FISA. I think it's terrible.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Bob.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are going to testify on your trial in New York?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, I would testify, absolutely. It's a scam. It's a scam. That's not a trial. That's not a trial. That's a scam. If you read Jonathan Turley, if you read Andy McCarthy, if you read the legal, they said there's not even a case there. That's election interference by the Biden Administration. They actually took their top guy, one of their top guys, put him into the DA's office to run it, and it's a shame.\par \par \uc2 What they have done is incredible. It's election interference, and it's got to stop. It's a third-world country. This country has never done it. But you read Jonathan Turley, you read Andrew McCarthy, you read the legal scholars. Every single one of them said that whole thing is a scam. It's not even a crime. And what they're doing is a crime. They are criminals. Bob?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why do you believe it is important for you to testify, take the stand in this upcoming trial, and what are you watching as jury selection begins in New York?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, you know, jury selection is largely luck. It depends who you get. It's very unfair that I'm having a trial there. It's very unfair that we have this judge who hates Trump and has tremendous conflict, as you know, tremendous conflict. Nobody can believe that this judge isn't recusing himself. The conflict is at a level that nobody's ever seen before.\par \par \uc2 So, I have that, and I have venue. We have all these things that we've asked for. They don't give us anything. It's a witch hunt that takes place in New York, and that is taking place. And it's very bad for New York, and it's 