cance of this, the former station chief in Moscow, FOX News contributor, much, much more.\par \par \uc2 Dan, you know what's wild about this is that there's a consistent theme here on an international security level, a military level, what the heck are they doing in space level, China, China, China. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 DAN HOFFMAN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, look, I think just the same way that we saw \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion with Sweden and Finland after \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  invaded Ukraine, we can thank Xi Jinping for reinvigorating the United States' alliance in Asia with South Korea, but also with the Philippines and with Japan.\par \par \uc2 Just when you look at what China is doing here in the United States, stealing our intellectual property, ubiquitous spying and then militarizing the South China Sea, and the largest, fastest-growing nuclear weapons program, they are a threat to peace all over the globe.\par \par \uc2 And they're aligned, of course, with Russia and keeping Russia in the fight in Ukraine. And so that's what we're seeing here, the Biden administration doubling down on our alliances with the Philippines and with Japan, who were right there dealing with China's bellicose behavior, as you note, in the South China Sea.\par \par \uc2 The Philippines have some brave soldiers out on Thomas Shoal in -- on a rusty warship, and the Chinese Coast Guard are aiming their water cannons at them. And, in spite of that, they're hanging in there and defying China's efforts to control that very important passageway for freedom of navigation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Dan, I was wondering.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the week, as you know, Fitch Investors -- it's a debt rating service -- came out with a warning and a potential downgrade, certainly for China's outlook, saying that it's running up a lot of debt, nothing to the degree that we are with our deficits and debt, but that they're a financial worry. Their economy's slowing down.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese president's had a devil at a time, even though a lot of American executives will still suck up to him and go there every time he tells him to, that he's in a world to hurt, that China's in a world to hurt. That could go both ways, right, because military actions have a nice way of sort of distracting your population, right?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: They can, and they can also make things a lot worse.\par \par \uc2 Look, China relies on spying because they can't innovate themselves. They have had a real problem with their real estate market and a bubble that burst on that. And some China experts with whom I speak believe that this downturn in the Chinese economy might induce Xi Jinping not to mount more aggressive military operations, to the extent that he might want to invade Taiwan and conquer Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 He might hold back on that with an eye towards improving his economy. But at the same time, his focus has been on this communist iron grip on his own population. We have seen multiple laws passed in China where, if you're a foreign business doing -- trying to do some work in China, the Chinese intelligence service owns everything that you're doing.\par \par \uc2 And every Chinese business has to report to Chinese intelligence. And that makes it difficult for foreign business to want to work there. And that's what we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Dan, thank you. I always learn a lot.\par \par \uc2 Dan Hoffman, the former CIA station chief in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're going to be looking at that, also looking at a trial that's finally coming on Monday for one \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 . Now, as far as any way to delay it or push it back, it doesn't look like that's possible, but he has been uniquely able to get a Hail Mary pass victory at the last moment.\par \par \uc2 But it's all slated now for Monday in New York. Jury selection will begin. Then what happens?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, there's no pushing back this one.\par \par \uc2 The hush money trial for \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  is still on, to commence next Monday, April 15, in New York, beginning with jury selection. They sort of lay things out. There -- I guess there are a couple of things that the former president and his legal team might be able to pull off to get that pushed back or to have some disruption, but doesn't look likely for now.\par \par \uc2 Tom Dupree is the former deputy assistant attorney general, expert on all things legal.\par \par \uc2 So I defer to you, Tom. Any last-minute type of Hail Mary past thing his team can take to push this thing off?\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER JUSTICE DEPARTMENT OFFICIAL: Neil, at this point, I think it's almost a virtual certainty that this trial is going to begin on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The president's lawyers have left no stone unturned in pushing various arguments and reasons and theories to persuade courts to bring this trial to a halt. But, so far, they have been rebuffed at every turn. It seems like, almost every day, there's one judge or another in New York saying, no, this can't be delayed. This is going forward on Monday.\par \par \uc2 So I think, at this point, the teams, both sides are just going to get prepared. They're going to strap on their battle armor and get ready to join the fight on Monday.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There is this other break glass moment, the last possible moment, where the president could fire his legal team and say, I have got to start from scratch or whatever. How likely is that?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: I would say it's unlikely.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, Neil, this is an extraordinary case, and I wouldn't put past extraordinary measures. But, by any standard, that would be pretty remarkable. And even if he were to do that, I don't know if even that would stop the trial.\par \par \uc2 It seems like pretty much every judge in New York has settled on April 15 being the day, and they have tried to stop it. So I think, at this point, it does seem pretty darn likely this is going forward come hell or high water.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, then you have to find fair and balanced jurors, right, who are open-minded.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the president and the legal team, quite rightly, given the liberal nature, slightly liberal nature of New York, that that wouldn't be a very fair judging pool. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Yes, and, look, it's -- to say that this is an unfavorable jury pool for Trump is an understatement.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Probably, 90 percent at least of the potential jurors in this case are going to be Biden voters.\par \par \uc2 I think what it's incumbent on the president's lawyers teams to do is be very aggressive in what's called voir dire. That's the process where lawyers get a chance to question prospective jurors about their biases, about whether they really can decide a case neutrally and independently and impartially.\par \par \uc2 It's really going to be a challenge for them. They're going to have to figure out how to get to the bottom of whether each and every juror on that jury is capable of rendering a fair and impartial verdict.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you about if he is convicted on any of these matters. He has no prior legal history or anything, run-ins with the law. You could make the argument that he wouldn't go to jail upon a conviction. I guess depends on how many convictions that -- play that out for me.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Yes, look, the way I see it, Neil, it's technically possible that he would get sentenced to jail. Each of the charges he's been accused of does carry the potential for a jail sentence.\par \par \uc2 But I think, for the reasons you mentioned, I personally would be shocked if ultimately he were sent to jail in this case and that, as you point out, he would be a first-time offender. There are certainly circumstances that I could see a judge saying as being mitigating or at least extraordinary that would counsel against a long jail sentence.\par \par \uc2 And there's a lot of water that has to go under the bridge before we even get to that point.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: So, although, yes, a jail sentence is theoretically possible, seems a bit unlikely at this point.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What if the judge has had it or will have it with him constantly talking about the case or bad-mounting participants in the case and just decides to say, you know what, you're going to cool your jets in jail for a little bit?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Yes, and, from time to time, I think maybe we're going to get to that point. We haven't yet.\par \par \uc2 And, look, this judge is aware of the need to run a tight ship. I understand why he doesn't want either Trump or, frankly, any party in the case trying this case in the media...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: ... or launching attacks on witnesses, on the court, on family members.\par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, he's got to preserve the president's First Amendment right to speak. And it's a fine line. And I think it's one the judge is going to have to walk throughout the course of this trial.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much, Tom. Great catching up with you.\par \par \uc2 Tom Dupree on all of that. And remember, kickoff time Monday.\par \par \uc2 All right, on to Alejandro Mayorkas. There is an impeachment effort to get him tossed from his Cabinet position. It is a Hail Mary pass to see -- talk about Hail Mary passes, to see that happen, but, right now, the fight is on even among Republicans and Democrats, some Republicans in the Senate who say, not so fast, this impeachment thing here in the Senate, not so soon.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): Senator Schumer wants to violate 200 years of Senate precedent and not hold a trial.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There are many people who get the sense that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  isn't fired up about this, but maybe you could share. Do all of your colleagues share your same rage at this?\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Well, I will let Senator McConnell speak for himself.\par \par \uc2 All I can tell you is that I'm pretty fired up about it. I'm not fired up about it on the basis of the politics. I think the Constitution gives the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  the duty and the responsibility to conduct a trial.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Apparently, six of his Republican colleagues in the Senate are not too keen about demanding that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  hold this Mayorkas impeachment trial, at least any time soon.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram, who has been covering this soap opera, has the latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL PRODUCER: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The Mayorkas impeachment trial may have been concluding right about now had the House sent the articles to the Senate earlier this week, but conservatives demanded more time. This allows them to cast a spotlight on the secretary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): We have had record numbers of known Chinese Communist Party members crossing the border, record numbers of people on the terrorist watch list crossing the border, and Alejandro Mayorkas says, what, that he's not responsible?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats believe they have the votes to quickly terminate a trial, but, in protest, the GOP is now using procedural maneuvers to block the Senate from doing simple noncontroversial things, like recessing until Monday.\par \par \uc2 Republicans want to make things difficult to underscore problems with Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SE