 success in this country. And that's what it should be about.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, the government --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: If she asked for universal school choice, I'd be all over it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Same here, Dana. I think there's a lot of constructive things you can do to elevate everybody. The fun part is why do -- why do we have to assume that we aren't part of this conversation? Like, if there is a task force, will anybody outside of the African-American community be allowed in that conversation?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We'll let you know.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, I know. Because like, if they're going to be -- you know, if the -- if the task force is going to be there deciding what they're going to do with my money, you know, I would like to be there and just kind of hang out and listen and go, no, I don't think that you know -- because I think it's a little bit arrogant for people to meet and talk about what they're going to do with my money.\par \par \uc2 You cannot punish people who are innocent of sins of the past. And I definitely think that by and large everybody is against this. The only people that want this are people who want stuff in general. I think most men would find this beneath them. And if you want more men to leave the Democrat Party, please push this by all means because no guy is going to say yes, I want money for something I didn't get -- that I didn't earn. That is like the most insulting thing.\par \par \uc2 And the reparations issue is another example of the compassion lie. You know, the left masks destruction as compassion because they never calculate its effects on everyone else. Shoveling money to one group means you're taking it from another. How do you think that's going to sit with that other group whether it's Whites or Asians or Hispanics or other Blacks? Think -- I mean, have you thought about that?\par \par \uc2 This is why you were right about the task force. This is the smartest thing that \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  ever did. The "task force" not -- where everything goes to die. When somebody comes into your office, Jesse, and says you know what, I really think you should give a segment to Johnny to go you know, to different rout. You know, we're going to do a task force for that. It's dead. That's what Gavin did with reparations.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's what we are going to do next. But just for a few minutes, we're going to go to a break. It's Scrabble for snowflakes. The beloved word game is getting a woke makeover.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Scrabble is dumbing itself down for the woke. The board game is getting updated to be more inclusive and less competitive. So, instead of competing, players collaborate, games are shorter, there are clue cards, and a no more scoring option so no one's feelings gets hurt. Right now, it's only available in Europe.\par \par \uc2 OK, so what I think is interesting about this, Jessica, is that they've removed certain words. They've banned racist, LBGTQ slurs from the tournaments, but there are also new words that are, you know, that the woke generation would be very comfortable with. So, is this more about wokeism this new Scrabble or is it about HDHD -- I mean, ADHD and the fact that they need friends and they need hints and they can't spell?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, first, in favor of removing slurs, I think we could probably all agree on that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Not so fast.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Careful, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: All right, we'll get to you. I always thought that you could play in teams -- or I have played in teams before even before this thing. But I think they just wanted to create a lighter version that would appeal to younger people. Scrabble can be a tough game.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So is life.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The game?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That is a tough game.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, Greg, the squares have been cream-colored for decades. Do you think they're going to make squares of different colors so not to offend people?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I hope so. I have never played Scrabble in my life.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You'd be so --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I never played it. So, I'm not sure how to handle this. This feels like a really solid Fox topic. You know, it happens in Europe. We haven't - - you know what I mean? But I've never played it, so I don't know. Maybe this is -- maybe this is a gateway drug so you get hooked on the real game. But playing a game without scoring, even if you suck at something is so anti-human. It's like scoring is part of your DNA, you know. You need to keep track.\par \par \uc2 I'm the world's worst tennis player but I hate volleying.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You're great at pickleball.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I would love -- that's true. But I love playing -- I love keeping score when I play tennis even if I lose 6-0 because I need to keep score. For example, I'm keeping score of THE FIVE right now.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You're losing in the fashion department.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No. He looks great.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Did you hear that?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know what, Jesse, I'm surprised that this new Scrabble didn't come with like four trophies so everybody feels like they won.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I know. Come on. I mean, even -- who was the blonde guy you played tennis against? Stevie Wonder kept score when he played Greg pickleball.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You said a blonde guy.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Stevie Wonder is blonde.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Stevie Wonder is blonde? Does he know he's not blonde?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Stop it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You're not going there.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Anyway, if you take out slurs, Dana is never going to win, so I just think that's -- I think it's unfair. I've played Scrabble a few times. I'm more into gam