ut the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: The \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  podcast at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Is she on your show?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Does she make news?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: At 8:00 Eastern.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Just watch him. OK. That's it for us, everybody. Have a great night. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: So, Judge, I understand that Vice President Harris is on Jesse's show. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, that's what I think.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's what I heard.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's what you understand.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's what I heard. OK.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Take care.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Good evening. Welcome, Washington. I'm Bret Baier. The Biden team hammers former President Trump's states' rights abortion policy after a major ruling in Arizona on that issue today, we'll take a look at it.\par \par \uc2 Parents of the Michigan school shooter are sentenced to at least a decade in prison for involuntary manslaughter, a precedent setting conviction and now sentencing.\par \par \uc2 Plus, confessions of a veteran still working in the heart of the mainstream media elite about what he calls his employer's leftist lurch.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, a horrific terror plot thwarted. \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials say a teenager in Idaho who planned what are described as horrific and violent plots against churches on behalf of the ISIS terror group is now in custody. What was he planning? Were there others involved? C\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt starts us off tonight with this breaking news from the Justice Department. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to u. A disturbing story. \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents arrested this 18-year-old student one day before they say he planned to attack more than 20 churches killing as many people as possible. The plan was thwarted, as you say, the attack never happened and tonight he is sitting in a jail cell. His name is Alexander Mercurio. He's 18 years old. We have his mugshot from the local sheriff's office in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say that he had other weapons including knives, fire to kill people in or around Coeur d'Alene, Idaho. He planned this attack for April 7th. Authorities say he telegraphed his plans to confidential human sources working for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to the complaint, material message one of the sources, "I don't know how much longer I can take being here. The siege of ignorance is strangling me and I don't have the desire to do anything except Jihad, a craving for mayhem and terror."\par \par \uc2 This photograph was taken just last week, Bret, shortly before he was arrested. At his home, police found a metal pipe handcuffs. A folding saw head covering, two canisters of butane fuel, a machete, hand sanitizer, a knife and an ISIS flag.\par \par \uc2 If convicted, he faces up to 20 years behind bars in federal prison. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  first learned of him in 2021 during an investigation into people using cryptocurrency to support terrorism. But investigators say his rhetoric recently intensified including this post. "The plan is basically this, lie to my dad and say I'm going on a walk, leave. Walk to a park, send a video which is a bayah video, expressing his allegiance to the Islamic State. Delete all my social media on my phone, then walk to the nearest church. Stop close by the church, equip the weapons and storm the temple. Kill as many people as possible before they inevitably dispersed or scattered, then burn the temple to the ground and flee the scene. Then move on to the next church rinse and repeat for all 21 plus churches in the town until killed."\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, Bret, the story not isolated. We did a quick search just through DOJ files, found 21 different cases with people living in the United States expressing some support or help to ISIS. And that's just since the beginning of 2023, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the Justice Department. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Arizona Supreme Court ruled today that the state could enforce an 1864 law that criminalizes nearly all abortions in the state of Arizona. The Biden team wasting no time pointing to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's position that abortion laws should be left to the states. The issue of abortion front and center at least this week in the 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Another thing that popped up here today are some comments by the president. The White House did not say whether Biden really thinks that Republicans are trying to kill Americans by undoing the Affordable Care Act. He said an interpretation of his words in that way was extreme. You can listen for yourself to what he said.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the White House is trying to keep the focus where they think it'll help Biden's reelection the most and that is on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They want to terminate the Affordable Care Act, terminate. And guess what, killing millions of Americans, take them off of health care insurance.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): President Biden taking aim at MAGA Republicans today before the White House held Trump responsible for Arizona's Supreme Court decision reviving a near total abortion ban from 1864. The ruling outlaws abortion from the time of conception except to save the life of the mother and punishes providers with prison time.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: When the president's predecessor handicapped three Supreme Court justices to overturn Roe v. Wade, it paved the way for the chaos and confusion we're seeing play out across the country today.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Enforcement is on hold for 14 days, pending additional arguments. But Biden has also seized on Trump's refusal to support or oppose a national ban.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My view is now that we have abortion where everybody wanted it from a legal standpoint.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Biden wrote, he's worried that since he's the one responsible for overturning Roe, the voters will hold him accountable in 2024. Well, I have news for Donald, they will.\par \par \uc2 But the president's been silent on another big issue. Extremist protesters chanting Death to America, Death to Israel in Dearborn, Michigan Friday.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Does the president condemn that?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Should we expect a statement from the president on that though, it was a pretty significant display?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I mean, you're hearing from me, I think that's important.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): In February, it took only two days for Biden to denounce a Wall Street Journal op-ed calling Dearborn America's Jihad capital. The editorial pointed to several local Imams denouncing America as a terrorist state and pushing to destroy Israel, one even calling to, "normalized Jihad."\par \par \uc2 Biden tweeted at the time, Americans know that blaming a group of people based on the words of a small few is wrong. That's exactly what can lead to Islamophobia and anti-Arab hate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And today, there were families of Americans still being held hostage by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  here at the White House. They met with the vice president but not with President Biden.\par \par \uc2 In a readout, the White House said that Harris underscored that there is no higher priority for both herself and the president than bringing those hostages home back, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A New York appeals court judges rejecting the latest bid by former President Trump to delay his criminal trial for falsifying business records concerning payments to a former adult film star. The former president's lawyers had wanted to trial postponed indefinitely while he fights a gag order that bars him from commenting about jurors, witnesses the judge and others connected to the case.\par \par \uc2 The decision means the first of the former presidents four criminal trials will start as scheduled. April 15th.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today ahead of a key inflation report. The Dow lost nine, S&P 500 was up eight, the NASDAQ gained 53.\par \par \uc2 And about face tonight by House Speaker Mike Johnson, the much anticipated Articles of Impeachment for Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas are now getting the slow walk to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . This is the Speaker himself, he's walking a political tightrope to try to hold on to his job while at the same time trying to get key legislation across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram has our story live from Capitol Hill. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. House Speaker Mike Johnson plan to send the Articles of Impeachment to the Senate tomorrow night. But Senate conservatives demanded a delay. They want more time to make the case that the Senate should hold a full trial. House impeachment managers were ready to go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. MARK GREEN (R-TN): We are ready to go tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED M