 But after a while we started covering Trump in a way that we were trying to damage his presidency, to find anything we could to harm him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Does he provide a number of specific examples here?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Here is one. During \uc2 Bob Mueller\uc2 's Russia investigation, he says, NPR had Democratic Congressman Adam Schiff on 25 times and followed his talking points on alleged collusion. When no collusion was found, NPR quickly moved on from Russia-gate and with no accountability.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So obviously NPR didn't think a lot about the Hunter Biden laptop story?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: No. And I vividly remember when NPR managing editor Terence Samuel said this, "We don't want to waste our time on stories that are not really stories, and we don't want to waste the listeners' and readers' time on stories that are pure distractions." Berliner now adds this, "I listened as one of NPR's best and most fair-minded journalist said it was good we weren't following the laptop story because it could help Trump." By the way, Samuel was later promoted, and a year-and-a-half later, as you recall, major news outlets confirmed the authenticity of the laptop.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, so here is another one. The riots after the George Floyd killing, does he comment on that?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Absolutely. Berliner says the message came right from the top. America's infestation was systemic racism which should be proclaimed loud and clear. And the author of a book "In Defense of Looting" was interviewed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So the question is, is NPR pandering to their audience? Is that what he is saying?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Well, yes, and that may not be so unusual. But that audience has changed, Bret. Now overwhelmingly white, just six percent black, and seven percent Hispanic. What's more, NPR issued a document on transgender coverage, asking staffers to avoid the term "biological sex." Other biases, says Berliner, a coming climate apocalypse, and constantly highlighting the suffering of Palestinians while downplaying the brutal \uc2 Hamas\uc2  atrocities against Israel that started the war on October 7th. And by the way, just before I came on air, I saw that NPR editor Edith Chapin had put out a memo to her staff saying she strongly disagrees with the criticism.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. So is this -- does he suggest that this is an isolated case? Do you see that, or do you think this is kind of the tip of the iceberg?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: In my view, not at all. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " famously fired editorial page editor \uc2 James Bennet\uc2  for the sin of running an online column by Republican Senator Tom Cotton. Now, an on-air revolt, as you recall, through MSNBC liberal hosts just forced their NBC forces to fire ousted \uc2 RNC\uc2  chief \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  just four days after she had been hired. And MSNBC boasts about not running many of Trump's primary victory speeches because they say, you know, he's just going to lie anyway. The common threat here, Bret, is silencing dissenting opinion.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's an amazing story, and we wanted to do it tonight because it just happened today. I doubt it's going to be many other places, if I had to guess.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: We shall see. That would confirm something, wouldn't it?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It would. Howie, thanks.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel on the Arizona abortion ruling, plus President Biden's comments about the ACA, Affordable Care Act. Later, "Tuesday Xtras."\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 5 in New York City where police are searching for a suspect who shoved a 68-year-old woman down the stairs of a Greek orthodox church in Queens. Startling surveillance video shows the man rummaging through her purse as the woman was on the ground. She is in critical condition tonight.\par \par \uc2 FOX 2 in San Francisco where \uc2 State Farm Insurance\uc2  is preparing to drop tens of thousands of policy holders due to significant wildfire risk. A recent state filing by the company shows zip codes where homeowners will be impacted, and State Farm is California's largest home insurer.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Orlando from our affiliate FOX 35. One of the big stories there tonight, the final Delta-4 rocket launch, the device carrying a secret reconnaissance pay load into orbit. The \uc2 United Launch Alliance\uc2  is replacing its fleet with the \uc2 Vulcan\uc2  rocket.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There are now 21 extreme state abortion bans in effect across the country.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): Women in Arizona deserve to make their own decision about an abortion. And that ended with this ruling. And that's because of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): We will continue to focus on the child in the pro-life movement. It's not about geography. It's about the well-being of the child. President Trump was a good pro-life president, and we'll see where this goes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, abortion is dominating, at least this week, the 2024 campaign. This as a court in Arizona striking down and saying that an 1864 law, you can rule all abortions essentially illegal. Abortion -- "Arizona abortion's bombshell tests Trump's new position," from Axios. "By declining to endorse a national abortion ban, Trump had hoped to insulate himself from the types of Democratic attacks that have proven so effective in election over the last two years. The Dobbs decision in which the Supreme Court rejected a federal right to abortion is cited repeatedly throughout the Arizona ruling, underscoring the difficult tightrope Trump is walking."\par \par \uc2 And, obviously, the Biden campaign hopped all over this today. Let's bring in our panel and start there, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , and "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers. Byron, significant, and it seems like politically harmful to Republicans kind of across the board.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Almost seems scripted. Trump comes out yesterday and makes this big statement about leaving abortion to the states. He's really trying to create a tent that enough Republicans can stand under to elect him in November. So he does that yesterday. Today, Arizona comes out after the Biden campaign jumps all over Trump and said states are going to do radical things and Trump is going to be at fault. And then they do this with 1864 law. It absolutely seems scripted. And Trump is going to get stuck with this time after time after time throughout this campaign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Francesca?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": In his video he actually said that he was the one who appointed these justices to the Supreme Court that led to the overturning of Roe. So the Biden campaign and Trump do agree on one thing, which is that it was his justices that helped to overturn Roe. And Democrats are absolutely going to be campaigning on this issue during the election. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has already said that she is going to be going to Arizona this week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's Tuesday, but this is a bad week for Republicans and the Trump campaign. If you look at the reaction to his original statement, Matthew. You look at Florida putting a six-week abortion ban on the ballot in November. And now Arizona -- it's not to say it's going to dominate from here until November, but at least right now it seems front and center.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: President Biden has been gaining ground in some horse race polls as well. So he has been enjoying a few good days. The problem with the position on abortion that leaves it to the states is you're saying, well, I'm not for anything. It's a negative position. Just leave it to the states, states' rights. You have to be for something in politics in order have a position, and in order to say I'm not for what the judge has ruled in Arizona. I'm for, say, a 15-week restriction with exceptions. That was what some people --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even through it probably wouldn't get through Congress and it would be constitutionally challenged?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: That's what I believe and I'm willing to debate on that. And we can have the debate on those terms. Instead, by saying I'm for whatever the states decide, you can be tarred with whatever the states decide for or against.\par \par \uc2 YORK: But this is also what a lot of conservatives, pro-lifers, and legal scholars wanted when Roe v. Wade was in effect. They basically said there is no right to abortion in the Constitution, therefore, it should be left to the states. The constitutionally proper way to handle this is to overturn Roe and leave it to the states. And what they really didn't consider was then that was going to set off warfare in all 50 states, and anybody who is running nationally would have to comment or be held to all of these battles that are going on around the country.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: But the electoral politics have favored Democrats so far in this. In FOX News' poll you see that most Americans --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We can put that up as you are talking.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: OK. Most Americans are in favor of abortion rights in this country. And you've even seen that tick up. I don't know if there is a slide that can show among Republicans in particular. You have even seen the support increase among Republicans as well for abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 And so this is an issue that Democrats believe can help them turn traditionally states that either traditionally have been red or purple, can help them turn them blue in the presidential race. And that's something you saw Trump himself essentially pointing to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right, which is what he was trying to avoid.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, words matter on campaigns. We saw two days of reaction to the word "bloodbath" when the former president was talking about electric vehicles and what that would do to the economy. Here is President Biden on the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My predecessor and his MAGA friends want to -- I love the phrase, the language he used. They want to terminate the Affordable Care Act, killing millions of Americans and take them off of healthcare.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Did the president mean to essentially accuse Republicans of murder?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I think you are taking the most extreme, extreme definition or extreme evaluation of what the president said.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. So here is \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 's statement, \uc2 RNC\uc2  response, "No matter how he spins it, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has waged a war on women and families that has caused people to struggle to send their kids to school, buy diapers, baby formula, afford childcare, or retire with the same standard of living they would have expected four years ago." It is tit-for-tat, Matthew, but it is just interesting words.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: And it's one more item in the catalogue of Biden's statements. Remember 2012 when he told the predominantly African American audience in Virginia that the Republicans were going to put you all back in chains, whether it was the debate where he just said he had no involvement in \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business and \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  wasn't involved with China. He will say anything to get elected. And he is going to be making more of these statements if he feels his position is threatened in what looks to be a very close toss-up election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As I mentioned, it's Tuesday. But on Capitol Hill, it's also a bit of chaos. Here is \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  possibly threatening Speaker Mike Johnson's position.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2 , (R-GA): We're losing Republican members of Congress under Mike Johnson. He is the not the Republican Speaker of the House. He is the Democrat Speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 REP. RAJA KRISHNAMOORTHI, (D-IL) HOUSE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE: If Speaker Johnson brings Ukraine aid to the floor, then \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  seeks to remove him, I think that Democrats would join in a motion to kill such a maneuver, because he should not be punished for doing the right thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, so all of this is pending. As you look at the split in the House, it's a very thin majority, and actually getting thinner, Byron. To be honest, if they had a motion to vacate, in other words, kicking the House speaker out, and there weren't enough people on the floor, you could end up with a situation where Hakeem Jefferies might have more people on the floor voting for him.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Yes, you could. Republicans down to 218. That's before \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  leaves. He's going to go sometime pretty soon. The fundamental problem is it shows the absolute insanity of agreeing to allow one member to file a motion to vacate. They could then be joined by a small number of people and actually take out a speaker. That's not theoretical. That's what they did to Kevin McCarthy. So now you saw Democrats say, well, our price to keep Mike Johnson in office would be if he brings up Ukraine aid for a vote. So, if Democrats keep a Republican speaker in office, there's going to be a price.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is former Speaker McCarthy on that Ukraine funding. There is Republican support for that, but take a listen to his advice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER: You are the majority. You are the speaker. You are the Republican majority in the House. I wouldn't sit down with the other three leaders. Why elevate the Democratic leader to being speaker? Go directly in with the president and negotiate. That's a stronger hand.\par \par \uc2 Use the strength of the majority to get it. So don't sit back and say, what can I do? Be the majority and sit back and say this is what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So we will see what is going to happen up on the Hill. But there's a lot of moving parts. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 OK, it's time for "Tuesday Xtras." It is Tuesday. I've said that three times. First up, Sarah asks, "What's your favorite part of your job?" Do you know what's great about it is it's different every day. You never know what the day is going it bring news-wise, and I work with a bunch of really great professionals.\par \par \uc2 Next up, Nicole wonders, "Who do you have in the Masters? Is this Rory's year? Do you think Rahm can repeat? What will help or hurt bringing the tours together?" Liv and PGA. "You can start there and then I'll pepper you with more golf questions." Wow, you've got a list there.\par \par \uc2 I like Rory this year in the Masters. I like Dustin Johnson as a dark horse maybe. He hits it a long way. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Bob asks on the golf theme, "What was your lowest score for 18 holes?" Bob, I shot a 67 long, long ago in a land far, far away. But now I'm a walking wallet and I just hand money to my friends.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our "Common Ground" segment features \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2  members \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  on the Democratic side, Dan Sullivan on the Republican side, what they're working on.\par \par \uc2 Remember, if you can't catch us live, set your DVR, 6:00 p.m. in the east, 3:00 p.m. on the west coast.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight from Washington. That's it for SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020240410ek4900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Crumbley Parents Sentenced To 10-15 Years In Prison; Victim's Families Give Emotional Impact Statements; Trump Abortion Stance Divides Some In GOP; Potential Impact Of Abortion Issue On '24 Election; White House Reacts To "Death To America" Chant At Michigan Rally; Shoplifting Migrants Attack \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Officers; Florida Raises Penalties For Retail Theft; Porch Piracy; Morgan Wallen Arrested For Throwing Chair Off Roof\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Garrett Tenney, Jeanine Pirro, Nat Foy, Rosanna  Scotto, Pete Hegseth \par \uc2 7,833 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, and this is THE STORY.\par \par \uc2 Breaking today for the first time in the United States. Parents whose son killed four teenagers will themselves spend 10 to 15 years in prison. James and Jennifer Crumbley, largely seen as non-remorseful in their testimony at trial, tried to turn that around today but they lost. Their son, Ethan, is serving a life sentence after he pled guilty to the shooting and killing of four of his classmates in Oxford, Michigan -- a horrific story that we covered here.\par \par \uc2 That happened more than two years ago on November the 30th. He was 15 years old at the time. Today, we listened to the excruciating testimony of what was lost by the families of 16-year-old, Tate Myre; 14-year-old, Hana St. Juliana; 17-year-old, Madisyn Baldwin, and 17-year-old Justin Shilling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BUCK MYRE, TATE MYRE'S FATHER: For our family, it's not time to celebrate. This tragedy has taken an incredible toll on our family.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE BEAUSOLEIL, MADISYN BALDWIN'S MOTHER: I would catch myself watching her and thinking to myself, how lucky am I? I'm the one that gets to be her mom. What did I do to deserve a perfect person?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG SHILLING, JUSTIN SHILLING'S FATHER: They say that time heals all wounds. Well, we're coming up on two and a half years now, and I can assure you that the wounds are still as fresh as they were on that tragic day.\par \par \uc2 STEVE ST. JULIANA, HANA ST. JULIANA'S FATHER: I will never walk her down the aisle as she begins the journey of starting her own family. I am forever denied the chance to hold her or her future children in my arms.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's so heartbreaking watching these families and the Crumbleys insist they had no idea what their son was planning. And listen to this warning from Ethan's mother. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER CRUMBLEY, MOTHER OF MI SCHOOL SHOOTER: Your child can make a failed decision, not just with a gun, but a knife, a vehicle, intentionally or unintentionally. If there's anything the general public can take away from this, is that this could happen to you too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This could happen to you too, she says. The five co-hosts, Judge Jeanine Pirro joins me, but first, the correspondent, Garrett Tenney, who wraps up the action today from Chicago. Watch. Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Martha, one of the arguments prosecutors made throughout these trials and throughout the sentencing is that the Crumbleys had shown little to no remorse over the actions of their son.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voiceover): And today, as you mentioned, both Jennifer and James Crumbley tried to change that by saying how sorry they are about what happened. But for the judge, it was too little too late. And she agreed with prosecutors, noting the Crumbleys provided unrestricted access to the gun, consistently characterized themselves as martyrs, ignored their son's spiraling mental health problems, and enabled his actions by ignoring all of the warning signs.\par \par \uc2 CHERYL MATTHEWS, MICHIGAN JUDGE: These convictions confirm repeated acts or lack of acts that could have halted an oncoming runaway train. About repeatedly ignoring things that would make a reasonable person feel the hair on the back of their neck stand out.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Those warning signs included disturbing drawings a teacher found on Ethan's math test the day of the shooting, including a gun, a bleeding shooting victim, and the words: "The thoughts won't stop. Help me." When school officials called to ask Ethan's parents to take him home for the day, they said no, despite Ethan telling a school counselor he was considering suicide.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors also shared at trial how seven months before the shooting, Ethan texted a friend about hearing voices saying, "I actually asked my dad to take me to the doctor yesterday, but he just gave me some pills and told me to suck it up. Like, it's at the point that I'm asking to go to the doctor. My mom laughed when I told her." Today, family members of the victims spoke directly to the Crumbleys about that.\par \par \uc2 BEAUSOLEIL: Because you decided that you didn't want to parent and listen to your son. You took the right away for me to be a mother.\par \par \uc2 JILL SOAVE, JUSTIN SHILLING'S MOTHER: You have failed your son and you have failed us all.\par \par \uc2 ST. JULIANA: Instead of acknowledging any mistakes, they continue to show no remorse. They take no accountability. They and their lawyers continue to try to change the narrative and portray the defendants as victims of the prosecution team.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): Two separate juries found both James and Jennifer Crumbley guilty of involuntary manslaughter. They have now been sentenced to 10 to 15 years in prison, but both say they will be filing appeals in their cases. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, Garrett. Judge Jeanine Pirro joins me now, co-host of "THE FIVE". Judge, you watched that judge on the stand today put these two people away for 10 to 15 years. Did you agree with her decision?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I absolutely agree with her decision. In fact, when I was the D.A., I convicted a parent of allowing a child access to a gun. That gun was used to kill another child. So, in terms of this is a mass shooting, this is the first of its kind, but there are prosecutors like me around the country who have done similar kinds of cases.\par \par \uc2 The issue is whether or not as a society, we are willing to recognize that we have to go beyond just the liability and responsibility of the person who does the shooting to whether or not there is vicarious liability or a special relationship of the person who is providing the gun. In some cases, it can be aiding and abetting, where you are not the person who actually does the shooting, but you are an individual who has a special relationship, in fact an obligation as a parent or a guardian, to make sure that your child is, you know, is protected.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, if a parent doesn't care for a child, the parent can be prosecuted. So, why is it taking us so long as a society to recognize that when you've got a deteriorating mental illness, a child who is asking both his mother and father to see a doctor, he says he's seeing blood everywhere, he talks about getting a gun, his father gets the gun, there's a picture of him with the gun, he goes to school with the gun.\par \par \uc2 The school calls the parents and the parents take no responsibility. That same day, the kid takes the gun and, you know, goes and kills four innocent people. You know, it's too, it's too -- people want to rely on this kind of sophisticated, well, he didn't pull the trigger. Well, he only got that gun because of his parents. And his parents were on, they had knowledge. They had reason to foresee that this would happen.\par \par \uc2 But more than that, in any situation, you could foresee it. This child was sending messages of blood everywhere. He was talking about the fact that he was seeing his clothes flying all over in the house. He had just lost his grandmother. He was a mess. The school knew he was a mess. The school called the parents. The parents have a responsibility here. And for those people who are concerned about a slippery slope, Martha, it's time for parents to recognize you have an obligation to the rest of society to protect them from someone you know can be a danger to them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you, what about the school? The school could have kicked him out that day. Let's put up the drawing that he had drawn that we have for people to take a look at. He said, "The thoughts won't stop, help me." It's a picture of a gun, blood everywhere. Now, we've all heard of cases where, you know, schools like go overboard with, you know, oh, your child went like this or something. This is very different. What about the - - no liability for the school? Why didn't they just say, sorry, you have to take him home. I don't care if you have work, you've got to take him out.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Now, you're -- Martha, talking about a civil liability, a civil case. It's up to the parents. I believe they have a case. They knew the child was in danger. They knew the child had threatened suicide. That's why they called their parents. And when the parents did not take the child out of school that day, the school then becomes in loco parentis, which is what all the Democrats want. You know, we're the parents when we have your kids. Well, there's an example of where that liability should lie.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Does the school have the right, the gun was in his backpack and they went into his classroom when they wanted him to leave with his parents and brought the backpack out. Based on what they're seeing on this math drawing, does the school have the right to open the backpack and inspect it?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Absolutely. They had reasonable cause to believe that there was something dangerous based on his history, based on what he was saying, and based upon the fact that they were all concerned.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what, it's a great point that you make because we hear so much about schools wanting to be in charge of these kids, right? But then you've got to take the whole responsibility, and clearly, they could have said, you know, you're expelled. He's out. He's out.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They didn't. They took a 5-year-old and threw him out of kindergarten.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly. Such a tragedy. Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Judge, how great to have you with us. Thank you so much for being here. We'll see you later on "THE FIVE".\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You got it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, later this hour, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  and Rosanna Scotto on the story that is breaking now of an assault on New York police officers this afternoon at a Target here in New York City. And Marc Thiessen on the tall mountain of abortion and the fierce battle between former President Trump and Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , where Marc says his party should stand in 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Arizona's Supreme Court says the state can enforce a law banning all abortions except when the mother's life is at stake. No exceptions for rape or incest. That law dates back to 1864. A fierce back-and-forth over abortion inside the Republican Party. Former President Trump says restrictions on the procedure should be up to the states.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  says he respectfully disagrees saying that most of the civilized world has a national limit up to 15 weeks. The former president responding in part, "Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is doing a great disservice to the Republican Party and to our country. At first, he wanted no abortions under any circumstances, then he was up to six weeks where you're allowed abortion. Now he's up to 15 weeks where you're allowed abortion, but what he doesn't understand or perhaps he does is the radical left Democrats who are destroying our country," he writes. "we'll never approve anything that the -- he or the Republicans want."\par \par \uc2 They love the issue. They want to keep it going for as long as Republicans will allow them to do so." And he goes on about the end of Roe v. Wade, in his Truth post.\par \par \uc2 So, here is Senator Graham today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I think, Trump is right in this (INAUDIBLE). The state must -- should have to a point. And as that child develops, I think, the American people find a consensus around 15 weeks that they'd like to limit abortion. So, I think it's going to be a debate that will continue. Where should America be? How should we get there? It's not about geography is about the wellbeing of the child.\par \par \uc2 And the good news for me, I think, as most Americans are where I'm at, some emotional issues. I think, President Trump was a good pro-life president, and we'll see where this goes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has been a longtime ally of the former president, and he went after him in a big way in those posts on Truth Social last night, as he tries to define where he believes the party needs to be on this, heading into the election.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in Marc Thiessen, former chief speechwriter for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  senior fellow, and \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  columnist, and a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Marc, always good to have you with us. What do you make of this clear dividing line between the current lead --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- candidate for the GOP in the presidential election and his former allies, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Well, I don't think he's his former ally, and I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tends he is --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Looks like Marc's shot is freezing up there.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: And he -- and he's --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's -- do we have 