d Machado are charged with robbery, assault, disorderly conduct, and resisting arrest, among other charges.\par \par \uc2 The three other migrants arrested are all back on the street without bail. According to the Post, they lived at Wards Island shelter on Randalls Island. They're charged with petty larceny and possession of stolen property. Police say the group tried stealing a gaming light, backpack, tools and snacks at a Target on the Upper East Side.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  put out a tweet writing, "While taking a report for another theft, six different criminals decided to thieve after a struggle foot chase, and an assist from officers in the 23rd Precinct. Five criminals were caught and cuffed. The one suspect who hasn't been arrested is accused of throwing rocks at the officer. Thankfully, that suspect missed. The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  said the officer's arm was injured in the attack. He was taken to the hospital where he was treated and released. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nate, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Got it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nate Foy reporting. So, you know, New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  slamming Texas Governor Greg Abbott for busing migrants to New York without warning or coordination. But a new report says the Biden administration has also been letting migrants fly into New York from overseas without any coordination or knowledge, or warning to local officials. They're flying in on their own dime, but I think for some of them it's a little cheaper than paying the cartels maybe. And it's a mass visa program that has not existed in recent history.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now, Rosanna Scotto, anchor of "Good Day New York" on Fox 5 here in New York City. And she's the host of a fantastic piece called "The Sanctuary Trap" that is playing on Fox Nation that you should definitely check out. She sat down for a lengthy interview with Mayor Adams, and he doesn't do that a lot. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is also here, former New York Republican congressman and former candidate for governor here. Great to have both of you with us. Two real New York experts on this.\par \par \uc2 So, Rosanna, let me ask, you know, when you look at the, you think about the attack in Times Square on the police officers and the migrant who was released, you know, giving everybody the double birds on the way out, you know, complete disregard for any law enforcement or rules in New York City. And here we have another situation like this.\par \par \uc2 ROSANNA SCOTTO, FOX 5 ANCHOR: We're not surprised. And as far as those migrants coming in and the dead of night, covert flights coming to New York City, we've been reporting that on Good Day New York. They've been coming in at Westchester airports, they've been coming in at JFK, we really don't have an idea of how many were brought in, but there are reports that the government has brought in over 33,000 migrants in the dead of night and brought them here.\par \par \uc2 Now last week, Mayor Adams was on our show and he said, you know, Governor Abbott was in town and I issued a big statement saying, Governor Abbott, I'll take you to one of the migrant shelters and show you what you've done by bringing all these migrants here. So I said to him, well, President Biden was in town just a few days before that fundraising, did you say the same thing to President Biden? He sidestepped that question.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a great question because, Lee, he loves to blame, you know, everything on Greg Abbott, but he doesn't want to blame the President, who never seems to have time for him when he's anywhere in the neighborhood. Here's Greg Abbott. I asked him about this on the weekend when I was anchoring Fox News Sunday. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREG ABBOTT (R), TEXAS GOVERNOR: You have a police officer who is killed, you have the train system in New York which is treacherous for travelers, you got police officers attacked in Times Square by illegal immigrants. He needs to step up and do his job as mayor and protect the people of his community as opposed to gimmicks about what's going on the border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you want the state of New York to understand about who's to blame here?\par \par \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R), FORMER NEW YORK CONGRESSMAN: First off, under the power of a city government, they should stop sanctuary city policies that incentivize and reward a legal entry, same thing in a state capitol like Albany where you have sanctuary state policies. In New York City, they passed a law not that long ago to allow noncitizens to vote. Unfortunately, a court jumped in and says, you can't do that's unconstitutional. But that is now being appealed to the state's highest court, the court of appeals.\par \par \uc2 It is important, though, when advocating to those who have the most amount of power. The reality is that right now inside the White House, this Biden administration has all sorts of tools to be able to better secure the border.\par \par \uc2 And I would like to see, regardless of whether you're a blue city, red city, blue state, red state, you advocate to the president of the United States and this administration to end catch and release, to bring back the Remain in Mexico policy. If you're going to get rid of Title 42 like what was done, it should only be done if there's a replacement. Finishing construction where there's vulnerabilities at a border. That advocacy to this president is so important, especially if he is a president of your own party.\par \par \uc2 And a great question by Rosanna. And really, we need to call a spade a spade here. And the powers that be right now in the White House, President proved that there are powers of a president where you can close a border. President Biden proved that there are powers of a president to open a border. Clearly, the President does not need a new act of Congress to do his job.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I just want to get a quick response from both of you, if I may. New York is obviously a deep blue state, but you almost won the governor's race here. What is your sense? We see nationally the polling of black and Hispanic voters that it is moving, it is becoming more conservative. You talked about different groups that are becoming more conservative in New York.\par \par \uc2 Rosanna, what's your sense of where New York is right now?\par \par \uc2 SCOTTO: I feel like crime is number one on many New Yorkers minds, no matter their color, creed, background, you name it, that's all anybody's talking about. And it's frustrating because day in, day out, we hear about migrant crime. And the mayor has tried his very best. He's caught in the sanctuary trap, right? But just recently, in fact, just days ago, five migrants were arrested for assaulting a man at Randalls Island and they were let go. And then not only that, the migrant squatters in Queens, eight of them, arrested for drugs and weapons. They were let go.\par \par \uc2 But ICE came in. They heard on their own, arrested them. We don't know whether they're going to be deported just yet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Quick thought on that, Lee.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: There's definitely a big trend that's going on during our campaign in 2022, even though the Asian American community is majority Democrat, we won the Asian American vote, won the Dominican vote. You see with Charlamagne tha God on the Breakfast Club, weighing in with this voice for the black community. He's being critical of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 So the trends are definitely there. But you can't take anything for granted. You have to show up and earn it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Ok. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , Rosanna Scotto, thank you. Great to have both of you here on set. I hope you'll come back soon. Great to have you both here.\par \par \uc2 So Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  cracking down on so called porch pirates who steal packages from right outside people's homes. It's part of a bill that also increases penalties for retail theft, including flash mobs organized on social media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA GOVERNOR: We're a law and order state. If you do the crime, you're going to do the time. We care more about protecting public safety and the rights of law abiding citizens than we do about coddling criminals or making excuses for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that's kind of talk Governor DeSantis has become known for. And he says that retail theft has already been dropping in Florida over the past four years. But he wants to send a message that this is not the state where you want to try it.\par \par \uc2 And this story getting so much attention. Country music star Morgan Wallen, huge star, could be facing jail time on charges of reckless endangerment and disorderly conduct after being caught on camera. This video purports to show a stool or a chair getting tossed off the top of one of those popular rooftop bars on Broadway in Nashville. Pete Hegseth is a new resident of Nashville, and he would like to weigh in on this incident from his hometown next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There's a look at Nashville today and that's courtesy of the Earth Cam. The moment that the police say that country music star Morgan Wallen tossed a chair off a Nashville rooftop bar, according to them. You can kind of see the object as it kind of flies by. You see something go right there, right? So it landed within two feet of police officers. Thank God it didn't hit them. So the star sometimes called the Bad Boy of Country now facing some real felony charges.\par \par \uc2 Fellow artist John Rich warning the 30-year-old it just takes one second to destroy all that he has built. He was very sympathetic. He said, you know, that he couldn't help but recall the destructive, crazy things that he did early on, but he just wanted to warn him and he said, I am rooting for him to turn this around.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Pete Hegseth, co-host of "Fox and Friends" and a Nashville resident. He's also the author of the upcoming book "The War on Warriors." You should check that out. Pete, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 What's your take on this, getting a ton of attention?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS HOST: I think John Rich is right. You bumped in with the song last night. Last night we let the liquor talk is the next line. And I think in Morgan Wallen's case that was probably the case. This was the first week of an opening of his buddy's new bar, Eric Church. If you come to Nashville, you know that well. Most of the big stars have bars, Blake Shelton, Jason Aldean, Kid Rock, John Rich.\par \par \uc2 Well, Eric Church opened his up. His friend Morgan Wallen came. And who knows what led to the circumstance of throwing something off the 6th floor. If you're there at 11:00 PM, which I believe is when this incident happened, there's a lot of people on the street, which means something could go sideways. Thank God it didn't.\par \par \uc2 And I do think, being that young, that good. I'm not a huge fan of Morgan Wallen, and I know a lot of people are. You can get caught up in a lot of things real quick. So these charges are serious. I think the NY or the Nashville police should take them seriously, treat him like anybody else. There shouldn't be preferential treatment here. And hopefully he does take it as a lesson to change that, because that's not great.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I want to put up famous other mug shots in music. You've got Johnny Cash, Tupac, David Crosby, Elvis, Frank Sinatra, Mick Jagger. Frank Sinatra, I looked it up, he was busted for seduction. Apparently he promised a woman that he would marry her if she went home with him, and that's what ended him up in jail. But Morgan Wallen, there's a long history of this. Let's see what some of the fans had to say. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You know, it's cool that we have a real rock star in country music.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's my guy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He's your guy?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everybody does dumb things every now and then.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm so disappointed in this situation. It's awful.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, I mean, he could have killed someone. Crazy stuff happens here all the time, I'm not surprised to be honest.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, a lot of stuff happening there all the time, Pete. His former ex-fiance, apparently there was a story floating around that he threw this chair because he saw that his ex-fiance, Katie Smith, had gotten married, eloped five days after she announced her engagement. She said, although it may seem it correlates to the timeline, I have no evidence to believe this incident had anything to do with my recent marriage announcement. And I pray for the very best for him. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Yes, you never know. I mean, it could have been, hey -- we've all been in these places. We've all been young, dumb and stupid, and drunk, and not thinking things through, thinking something's going to be funny. He just happens to be really famous and really talented with a lot of people around him. And the stool hit two feet from a police officer. It doesn't excuse a thing, but hopefully it causes a course correction through the consequences that come.\par \par \uc2 Problem is, we see so many things these days, Martha, that don't come with any consequences where people are affected. And we wish him the best because he's so talented. And if he's going through tough times then I hope he pulls through it quick (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. And as you say, you know, the law will deal with him and we'll see what happens. But we see a lot of law not dealing with a lot of things, so we'll see what happens with Morgan Wallen. But a huge, huge talent to be sure.\par \par \uc2 Great to see you, Pete. Thank you very much. Congratulations on the new book, "The War on the Warriors."\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And coming up here in our final minutes, the Catholic Church comes down hard against gender affirming surgery next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: $600 million, that is how much \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  is agreeing to pay to settle a class action lawsuit over the toxic train derailment in East Palestine, Ohio more than a year ago. But some people who live there say that they are worried it won't be enough to cover the medical bills and the other enormous expenses they incurred.\par \par \uc2 The company says it's already spent a billion dollars responding to the disaster. It's not admitting any wrongdoing. The feds say they expect to wrap up their investigation of the derailment by June. More on this with independent presidential candidate Robert F. Kennedy Junior, who we spoke to in East Palestine not that long ago. The people that we talked to there said that they had not seen any restitution from Norfolk southern so we will speak with RFK Jr. about that tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And the Vatican condemning gender transitioning surgery and surrogate pregnancy is calling them threats to human dignity. Chief religion correspondent Lauren Green here with me in New York on that. Hi, Lauren.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GREEN, FOX NEWS CHIEF RELIGION CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, you know, the Vatican's document on human dignity calls gender changing surgery a grave violation of God's gift, along with a host of other things like abortion and surrogate motherhood. The Vatican releasing the document Dignitas Infinita or Declaration of Human Dignity, to the press on Monday.\par \par \uc2 In it, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  clarifies his theological position that life is a gift from God, but there are negative influences impacting morality and culture. On the problem of surrogate motherhood, he states, the woman is detached from the child growing in her and becomes a mere means subservient to the arbitrary gain or desire of others. Now, on gender theory, Francis says we cannot separate the masculine and the feminine from God's work of creation.\par \par \uc2 While not naming transgenderism specifically, the document calls out the surgical procedure of changing one's sex, saying any sex change intervention as a rule, risks threatening the unique dignity the person has received from the moment of conception. And desiring a personal self determination, as gender theory prescribes, amounts to a concession to the age old temptation to make oneself God.\par \par \uc2 Now, advocates for the LGBTQ Catholics blasted the document. The executive director of New Ways Ministry stating, documents attempt to uphold and defend human dignity is weakened by its stunning lack of awareness of the actual lives of transgender and non-binary people. Now, the advocates also warn it could fuel violence and discrimination against trans people as well. So a lot of controversy about this document.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Lauren, thank you very much. Lauren Green reporting on that for us.\par \par \uc2 And that is THE STORY for this Tuesday. Thanks so much for being with us today. As always, THE STORY goes on so we will look forward to seeing you here tomorrow. As I said, we will talk to RFK Jr. Independent presidential candidate. Stick around a little bit of a down day, not too far for the Dow Jones industrials as they look towards the close. "Your World" starts right about now. We'll see you tomorrow, folks.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020240410ek4900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Biden Administration Targets Student Debt Relief; Cost For Rebuilding Francis Scott Key Bridge?; House Delays Sending Mayorkas Articles of Impeachment to Senate; Jury Selection Set to Begin Monday in Trump Hush Money Trial; Arizona Supreme Court Ruling Brings Abortion to Forefront; U.S. Tensions Mount Over Israel-\uc2 Hamas\uc2  War; Interview With Sen. Steve Daines (R-MT); Interview With Former U.S. Ambassador to Israel \uc2 David Friedman\b0\par\par\uc2 Trey Yingst, Mike Emanuel, Chad Pergram, Aishah Hasnie, Kat Timpf,  Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,274 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Invasion set, date set, Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  set. Only, he is not saying when that Rafah invasion is going to happen, only a week after that very tense phone call with President Biden, who worried it better not happen.\par \par \uc2 Now that it's on, are old bets off? Because Israel today reportedly warning Iran that, if it is attacked for that strike on an Iranian consulate in Syria, Israel is ready to bomb nuclear sites in Iran. It is getting that big, that scary that fast.\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk to the former U.S. Ambassador to Israel \uc2 David Friedman\uc2  on what could come next.\par \par \uc2 First, FOX team coverage with Trey Yingst to Tel Aviv on why Israel could be doubling down and Mike Emanuel in Washington on a White House that is now clearly fed up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. I'm very happy to have you on a very busy news day.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to it with Trey in Tel Aviv -- Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  says his troops will enter Gaza's southernmost city of Rafah. Currently, there are more than 1.4 million Palestinians that are sheltering there. The question now is where they will be evacuated to and how long that process might take.\par \par \uc2 This is a consequential decision if it does take place for a variety of reasons. Netanyahu says the move to destroy remaining \uc2 Hamas\uc2  battalions is needed to win the war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): This victory requires entering Rafah and eliminating the terrorist battalions there. This will happen. There is a date.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: It's day 186 of the war between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . The amount of aid going into Gaza is slowly increasing, with more than 400 trucks entering the enclave today.\par \par \uc2 After \uc2 IDF\uc2  soldiers withdrew from Southern Gaza over the weekend, some Palestinian civilians returned to the wreckage of their homes in the city of Khan Yunis. Recent reports estimate more than 50 percent of buildings in Gaza were damaged or destroyed since October 7.\par \par \uc2 While Israel still focuses on the ground inside Gaza, Israeli forces are on high alert in the northern part of this country amid continued attacks from the Iran-backed \uc2 Lebanese militant group Hezbollah\uc2  -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you for that, Trey. Be safe, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Mike Emanuel right now in Washington on how the White House is responding to all of this, because this was not in their game plan during that phone call last week -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Neil, you're exactly right.\par \par \uc2 This visit by a major Israeli opposition leader comes after Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  called for a snap election in Israel to get rid of the current prime minister. Last hour, Senate Majority Leader Schumer held a meeting with Israel opposition leader \uc2 Yair Lapid\uc2  at a time when the progressive left is pressuring President Biden to demand a cease-fire in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Lapid has been making the rounds here in Washington, meeting yesterday with Secretary of State \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2 , but Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  says political pressure won't stop the mission.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU (through translator): There is no force in the world that will stop us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Today, some disruption Capitol Hill, protesters in the Capitol Building basically disrupting operations in the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Some 50 people were arrested, one person wearing a shirt saying, "Biden's legacy equals genocide." Today, the Senate Republican leader said the president is reacting to pressure from the far left.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): America cannot afford to lose moral clarity about the conflict. But I'm afraid that too many of our leaders are. The president caved further to domestic political pressure. He indulged his radical base and called for an immediate cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: This afternoon, the national security adviser insisted the United States is behind its key allies in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, U.S. NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: The president said to the prime minister, and as was read out in his call, that America's support for Israel's security is ironclad and America's support for Israel's defense against threats from Iran and its proxies is ironclad.\par \par \uc2 And I'm not going to speak to operational details beyond that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Tense times at the White House and on Capitol Hill, protests on Capitol Hill and across Washington.\par \par \uc2 Congress is back in session, and the protesters are back calling for a permanent cease-fire -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mike Emanuel, thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 To \uc2 David Friedman\uc2 , the former U.S. ambassador to Israel, what he makes of all of this.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador, thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 This was exactly, that is, this idea of a Rafah invasion, what President Biden did not want Prime Minister Netanyahu to do. Now that the prime minister has committed to doing it, what do you think happens next?\par \par \uc2 DAVID FRIEDMAN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO ISRAEL: I think that Netanyahu has no choice but to continue the war.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the consequences of not invading Rafah is that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  survives with four battalions. That's 4,000 troops. It means that Sinwar survives with his life. It means that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  becomes, if you will, the darling of the terrorist world, not a title you or I would want, but it's something they were very covet.\par \par \uc2 And they live to kill Jews another day, ultimately to export that terrorism to America as well. And, Neil, I think it's important to understand Israel lives in this very, very, very dangerous part of the world on the basis of its deterrent capabilities, on the basis of the fact that these terrorists know that if they hit Israel, Israel hits back two, three, four times as hard.\par \par \uc2 None of these entities can defeat Israel. Only America can cause Israel to be defeated. And America can cause Israel to be defeated by telling them not to attack, by telling them to go do a cease-fire, go negotiate a deal with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . Don't attack \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  in the north.\par \par \uc2 And all these messages are doing nothing but empowering Israel's enemies. This daylight is creating a normal enormous, enormous momentum and enthusiasm by Israel's enemies to continue the war. It's having exactly the opposite effect of what I assume the Biden administration wants to achieve.\par \par \uc2 And so, I mean, the worst thing that America can do right now is to try to micromanage Israel's conduct and to limit their capabilities and limit their ability to fight this war. That just prolongs the war, emboldens the enemy and raises the price for the hostages.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And yet that's exactly what's going on, right?\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering. Obviously, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  took the calculation here that he had agreed to get more aid and passageways made into Gaza, something President Biden wanted him to do. But he knew that this Rafah decision, which would come later, would renew all these tensions with the White House, did it anyway.\par \par \uc2 What do you think happens now?\par \par \uc2 FRIEDMAN: Look, I think -- look, I hate to see this daylight. I hate to see these two countries that I love at loggerheads with each other.\par \par \uc2 But Israel's fighting for its life, and Biden is fighting for Michigan. And as between those fights, I'm with Israel on this. I mean, Israel has to survive this battle, and they can't survive without defeating \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . I mean, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has said openly, with great pride, you know, if we survive, we're going to go and do this again and again and again.\par \par \uc2 Not to mention the fact, Neil, that almost 100,000 Israelis have been displaced from the north. And every single day since this \uc2 Hamas\uc2  war began, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has been shooting at Israelis in the north, and there's no answer to that yet. And Israel needs to be empowered to complete that process and to show \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  that it's willing to fight back.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FRIEDMAN: Israel has the capabilities to win this war and to protect its citizens. The only means by which Israel fails to achieve that is by America putting handcuffs on Israel.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You put that very well, Ambassador.\par \par \uc2 And I raised this issue with Chris Coons, the Delaware senator, when he was with me last week about what we are attaching to aid right now with Israel. And I thought he was fairly clear on the subject, even though I don't know if he wanted to be. I want you to react to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If they go into Rafah and do this, would you say, all right, that's a game changer, Israel? We told you not to do it, and you did.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): I would be willing to put conditions on the delivery of military munitions for that ongoing campaign.\par \par \uc2 I would not sever ties between the United States and Israel. I wouldn't abandon Israel.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 COONS: But I would begin to condition the munitions that we provide for an ongoing campaign in Rafah.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, just to be clear, Ambassador, I was not asking about whether we would sever ties in Israel. I didn't think that would happen, but that aid would be on the line.\par \par \uc2 And it clearly is now, or it could be now. Does that part worry you?\par \par \uc2 FRIEDMAN: It worries me tremendously.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, I think Senator Coons has been a good friend of Israel over the years. I think he has, like others, succumbed to the political pressures. Look, what he's saying to Israel is, you're not going to win this war. You're just not going to win. You're not going to go into Rafah. You're not going to clean out the last four battalions. If you do, we're going to put some conditions on.\par \par \uc2 And then the war will be litigated by lawyers, rather than by generals, right? You will have these some -- some word salad of the terms on there which Israel gets its weapons, and it will be fighting over that meanwhile while Israel is trying to defeat one of the worst terrorist groups in the world.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, I mean, we talk about the pressure that's on Israel right now, but on \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , a lot of the opposition leaders have been taking pictures with \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  when they visit here.\par \par \uc2 Of course, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  famous for saying that this is a good time for Israel to have elections. That engendered a great deal of controversy. But we're already seeing within Israel some of the members of his coalition government are sniping at him directly, and that some Israelis indeed are tiring of \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How bad is this, Ambassador?\par \par \uc2 FRIEDMAN: I think you have to divide it into two buckets.\par \par \uc2 Politically, Netanyahu has his challenges. Look, every Israeli leader who's had a military failure, whether Golda Meir in the Yom Kippur War...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 FRIEDMAN: ... Menachem Begin in the Lebanon war, they have not done well politically in the aftermath of those failures.\par \par \uc2 And this was a colossal failure. So you do have that. And I'd say most of the country, I think, is unhappy with the fact that this happened in the first place. But, during the course of the war, the overwhelming majority of the people, even those who despise \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 , want to see him finish the war.\par \par \uc2 Finish the war, then have elections, and then let the chips fall where they may. But we're not changing horses in the middle of the race.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ambassador, thank you very much. Good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 David Friedman\uc2 , the former U.S. ambassador to Israel, pretty clearly speaks his mind, as does Ted Weisberg when it comes to money issues and all the sort of frictions that are on the markets right now as a result. Oil did come down a little bit today, but, again, more volatile investing, and a lot of folks worry that these Mideast tensions could explode.\par \par \uc2 Ted, what do you think of that and then, right now, the fact that these international issues could come home to roost for Wall Street?\par \par \uc2 TED WEISBERG, PRESIDENT, \uc2 SEAPORT SECURITIES CORPORATION\uc2 : Well, I mean, Neil, it's a real concern. It's a huge unknown.\par \par \uc2 And we know that markets can deal with good news, bad news, and always struggle with the unknowns. And so, yes, it is a huge problem. On the other hand, when you see where the market was last fall and how good the market performed in November and December of '23 and the first quarter of '24, up 10 percent for most of the popular averages, and, even today, as we speak with the increased tensions in the Middle East, yes, the market's gotten a little sloppy the last couple of days.\par \par \uc2 But, clearly, for some reason, the market seems to be willing to absorb a lot of these unknowns, if you will, geopolitical tensions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it always does affect trade in the region. We have seen the Red Sea, which was virtually impossible for cargo ships to peacefully get around, and get their goods moving. So there's a supply issue there.\par \par \uc2 And then there is the concern that it doesn't take much to scare the markets, the oil markets. They were rocketing last week on this tense phone call between the president and \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So they're clearly sensitive to that. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Well, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And it used to be all gold, and now -- now, to a large extent, it's energy, oil in particular.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Though gold -- gold is trading at all time highs as we speak. But the spikes, the fear spikes, if you will, seem to be off the table. But yet you can't argue where gold is trading.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, Neil, you can't argue where oil is trading. Oil has been inching higher for the last three or four months. I mean, we're just touching just shy of $90 a barrel, depending on whether you look at WTI or Brent. And there are a lot of folks that think we could spike to $100 without a lot of effort.\par \par \uc2 Now, is that Middle East tensions or is it the U.S. energy policies? Perhaps a little of both.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it's inflationary one way or the other, and that gets them scared about interest rate cuts that could be delayed, if not denied.\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How real a threat is that, real quickly?\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: I'm sorry, Neil? I lost -- say again, please.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This fear of inflation from all of this and the fact that it could delay rate cuts on the part of the Fed, how real is that?\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Well, I think it's very real.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy continues to do quite well. The unemployment numbers are almost nonexistent. Employment is good. The economy is good. Corporate earnings are very good. I mean, the Fed has got -- it's in a bit of a dilemma...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: ... because I know they talk -- a lot of people, including the Fed, talk about lowering rates, but it's going to be very difficult for them to lower rates meaningful in the current economic environment.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, you could probably make a case even that perhaps interest rates might even tick higher.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: But the fact is that the economy is doing well and the Fed, though they talk about lowering rates, the reality is, will they?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, will they?\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: And that's a big question mark.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that is haunting these guys right now. But gold is narrowly going its own way, hit another record today, eight day in a row it did that.\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ted Weisberg, thank you very much. Always great catching up with you, Ted.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, we're getting word right now that the speaker, Mike Johnson, is going to delay his delivery of this Homeland Security, Mayorkas impeachment article to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . That means that an impeachment of some sort is still on in the Senate, delayed, but certainly not denied -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (R) AND CURRENT U.S. PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: My view is, now that we have abortion where everybody wanted it from a legal standpoint, the states will determine by vote or legislation or perhaps both.\par \par \uc2 And whatever they decide must be the law of the land, in this case, the law of the state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, another state, the 16th, Arizona has really clamped down on what will be the law when it comes to abortion there, after Arizona's Supreme Court revived a near-total ban on abortion, actually invoking an 1864 law that forbids the procedure except when the mother's life is on the line and punishes some providers with prison time.\par \par \uc2 It was a 4-2 decision, as I said. This is a conservative court, but already it's raising concerns that this will boomerang on conservatives and Republicans in particular, who have been hoping that this issue as a political detriment will go away. It just seems to be sort of refired up right now.\par \par \uc2 Tiana Lowe is the political reporter, gives us her take on this.\par \par \uc2 Tiana, what do you think? Again, regardless of people's position abortion itself and whether this was right or wrong decision, we know it's a political grenade. So who does it blow up on?\par \par \uc2 TIANA LOWE DOESCHER, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: There's a reason why \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is racing to Arizona to capitalize on this.\par \par \uc2 Look, a recent Wall Street Journal poll of all the swing states found that Trump has a double-digit lead across all the swing states when it comes to issues like inflation, the economy, foreign policy. The one area where Biden and Harris have maintained their lead is abortion.\par \par \uc2 And Arizona is not a very red electorate anymore. It has become increasingly purple, where somewhere like Florida has gotten increasingly red. And this 15-week ban that Republican Governor \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  passed two years ago, that was really in the sweet spot of what was OK for Arizona voters, who are very much in the average of the country.\par \par \uc2 You know, 15-week ban puts -- put Arizona in line with Europe. This near- total ban, which has no exceptions for rape and incest, look, even three in four Republicans polled by \uc2 UPenn\uc2  just last year expressed that they still support exceptions for abortions in cases of rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 This law was originally passed 160 years ago...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 LOWE DOESCHER: ... before Arizona was even a state and before the 13th Amendment was passed, let alone women having the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 And this can backfire, because you have the pro-choice advocates, who have already half-a-million signatories on a ballot proposal that would enshrine much more unfettered abortion access into Arizona state law. If that ballot proposition does make it on that Election Day ballot, that could be a huge boon to Biden, who needs to win Arizona and is right now down about 4.5 points below \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in head-to-head polling in the state.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Florida is looking at doing the same thing right now after a similar crackdown there, and this could be a ballot issue on their ballot as well.\par \par \uc2 A number of other states that are contemplating the same. I think we're up to 16 now, a varying degree of severity on this, how strict you are with abortions.\par \par \uc2 But I am wondering. There are others who say this is a tired old sort of argument for Democrats to try to zing Republicans, that it's the economy, stupid, it's the perception of the economy, stupid, and a lot of people, abortion or no as an issue, this is not as galvanizing as it used to be.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 LOWE DOESCHER: So, the economy does remain a major issue. Immigration has even superseded the economy, even though prices are up 18 percent since Biden took office. Immigration is still more important nationally now. It just overtook the economy, and especially in a place like Arizona that is a crucial border state.\par \par \uc2 But the difference is that the Arizona GOP has been in shambles for many years. This is Kari Lake's second election. She is not popular in the state at all. She's going against \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 , who is very much moderating. Like, he's running away from the progressive label for a reason because he thinks he can win.\par \par \uc2 And, unfortunately for someone like President Trump, who has really expressed pro-life federalism, very much in line with what the pro-life movement was until Roe v. Wade was overturned, which is return it to the states, Kari Lake in 2022 said that it was great that Arizona had this 160- year-old trigger law, and she specified that it had no exceptions, that the only exception was for life of the mother, not for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 So those words really backfire on her. And that's why she's come out today calling on \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  to do something about this law, to effectively reinstate that Ducey standard of a 15-week abortion ban. In other states, it might not matter so much, again, where you have a big margin of support with highly competent governors like \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  or \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , sure.\par \par \uc2 But Arizona GOP has just been flailing for almost a decade now. And so this can be a live issue if Arizona Republicans don't get their act together.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. And you mentioned \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , of course, the governor of Arizona, issued an executive order last year that would give all the power on abortion laws to the state attorney general, who similarly was against what the Supreme Court did there. But it's a hot issue again. We will follow it very, very closely.\par \par \uc2 But, Tiana, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Tiana Lowe on that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, updating you on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his various legal battles. He's lost what was the last shot at delaying this hush money trial in New York, now still on for April 15.\par \par \uc2 What's at stake -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, as you know, the House was expected to, maybe this week, get impeachment articles on Homeland Security Secretary Mayorkas to the Senate, but it's holding off until next week, so the Senate can get its act together on the same matter -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  running out of options and apparently running out of time. He's exhausted all efforts right now to delay that hush money trial slated to begin next week with jury selection in New York on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The significance of all of this with Katie Cherkasky. She really helps me just follow all of these trials and where they stand.\par \par \uc2 But this is the one that's ready for launch. How do you think it's going to go, Katie?\par \par \uc2 KATIE CHERKASKY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, it looks like it is going to go forward, absent Trump firing his entire defense team on the morning of trial, still not off the table, something that defendants can and have done in other cases.\par \par \uc2 But he does have, in my opinion, a very strong defense going into the New York case. So, perhaps he's looking for a win ahead of the election, and certainly that would give him some momentum.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, he can do that. He can fire his entire defense team, but that means you need a whole new crop of lawyers, and you can't just sneak them back under different names or aliases. How does that work?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Well, there are certain circumstances where a criminal defendant does not wish to continue with their current team, and so they let the court know that they have lost confidence in their team or they wish to have different counsel.\par \par \uc2 And the Supreme Court has been very clear that, in criminal cases, a defendant is entitled to remove their team and replace their team entirely if they wish to do so before proceeding to trial. In this case, the judge has made some passing remarks about one of Trump's attorneys not being prepared in some ways. He may be able to use that as a justification.\par \par \uc2 This is only, again, he wanted to do. But if he wants to move forward, then, certainly, I think that they obviously are prepared to go to trial, and look for that acquittal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, let me ask you, would his own legal team now say, boss, this might be the best route to go? You might have to fire us and start anew.\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: That would certainly not be the first time a criminal defendant has done something like that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: And, obviously, there's a lot of strategy that goes into defending a criminal case. And so certainly that is an option that all defense attorneys are aware exist.\par \par \uc2 But, ultimately, it is the defendant's sole decision who they're represented by.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, we have the other issue, the documents cases, some of these others, that the president wanted protection, that immunity and all of this. That doesn't look like it's happening. There are a lot of things festering on some other cases.\par \par \uc2 But is it fair to say this might be the only one that will get off the launchpad before the election?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: As it stands right now, with the Supreme Court considering the immunity case at the end of April, we're unclear about when they would issue a decision in that.\par \par \uc2 Then the other cases are essentially on hold as a result of that finding. So, of course, the Georgia case, we don't really know exactly where that stands, but there is some progress moving ahead while the appellate court is still considering the removal of \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 . So, again, very unclear on the timelines with that, but I would say the New York hush money case, even if it was continued for some reason, may still be able to get in under the wire there before the election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, on the Supreme Court, as in deciding the whole immunity thing, how much do they weigh or should they weigh -- I know justice is blind and all that -- a guy who's running for the highest office in the land and how this might be disruptive to that process?\par \par \uc2 Or should that even enter their minds?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Well, it's interesting, because the overarching issue in that case deals with the office of the presidency and how any sort of criminal process interferes with the executive branch at large.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of their decision in the case, they said that they would put that on an expedited schedule in order to somehow accommodate these artificial timelines before the election. But, certainly, that's not the primary consideration for when they're going to rule on the case, but how could they possibly ignore it?\par \par \uc2 It's obviously the elephant in the room there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You must have this all out on a spreadsheet organized meticulously, knowing you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because I have mine right now on an Etch A Sketch, which is probably the same thing.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because I just shake it, and then I get a different result.\par \par \uc2 Katie, great seeing you again. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we told you right now, they're making an effort, of course, in the House, as you know, by the slimmest of votes, to go ahead and impeach Alejandro Mayorkas, the homeland security secretary.\par \par \uc2 They're having a devil of time, at least in the Senate there, deciding where they go from here. So the House is holding off on sending some of these articles of impeachment to the Senate, so they have time to digest exactly how they will do this in that body.\par \par \uc2 What Senator Daines thinks of all of this, Steve Daines of Montana -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): We're going to try and resolve this issue as quickly as possible. Impeachment should never be used to settle policy disagreements.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, be that as it may, and he is the leader of the Senate, but \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  might not want to face this impeachment trial issue, but it is on right now.\par \par \uc2 The only thing we learned today is that the House is delaying sending over the articles of impeachment, a sort of formality, if you will, to get the ball rolling. And how it proceeds from here, I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 But I know Chad Pergram does. He is a student of this institution.\par \par \uc2 So, Chad, what happens now?\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  hasn't said much about how he would handle an impeachment trial for Alejandro Mayorkas. Well, he doesn't have to. That's because a spokesman for House Speaker Mike Johnson says the House will delay sending the articles to the Senate until next week.\par \par \uc2 The House aimed to send the articles over to the Senate tomorrow night. Now they have punted for a week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): The speaker has -- we asked him to delay sending over the articles until Monday to at least give us a full week.\par \par \uc2 We will see if Senator Schumer honors the extra time. This is unconscionable, people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republicans want more time to level pressure against the Senate to hold a full trial and not just dismiss the articles, but even the House members who will prosecute the case were in the dark about the plan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But they haven't told you that? As an impeachment manager, no one has said to you when they're supposed to do it, other than last week saying, oh, we're going to take them over tomorrow afternoon?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): The last thing I heard -- and I am an impeachment manager and those articles of impeachment have my name on them. I have not been told that we may be holding them now. You're the one that told me that. So, apparently, you're reading the news quicker than I am.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now, the plan was to swear in the senators as jurors on Thursday, but even Republican leaders were coy when asked about a possible delay.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Has there been any discussion among Republican leaders, talk about this about now possibly requesting the House to delay sending over the impeachment articles, so you can make a better case to have a more full Senate trial?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: I don't think we have any advice to give the speaker about how to handle the issue on either -- on timing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Either way, the Senate will likely vote to dismiss the articles of impeachment if Democrats hold together.\par \par \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  of Montana says he wants to see the articles first. He faces a tough reelection this fall -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Chad, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Senator Steve Daines, Montana senator, National Republican Senatorial Committee chair, joins us.\par \par \uc2 Chairman, Senator, very good to see you.\par \par \uc2 The White House is responding to all these developments, Senator, saying that: "If Republicans actually believed this impeachment was so urgent for the security of the border, then they wouldn't have delayed sending it to the Senate for two months or now delayed it again for another week. It lays bear how baseless and devoid of substance this whole P.R. stunt is."\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. STEVE DAINES (R-MT): Well, it's -- I'm not surprised, first of all, that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  was hoping those articles would come to the Senate this week, on a Thursday afternoon, so they could get buried for the weekend.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats don't want to talk about this. Look, the House has spoken. It was a yearlong process of hearings, a lot of investigation. They voted to push two articles of impeachment over to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . We have a constitutional responsibility. We are accountable to the American people to have a trial.\par \par \uc2 We now sit as jurors. Mayorkas has been impeached. The question is, should he be removed from office or not? That is what the will of the Senate will determine. So we need to have full accountability, take it in the full daylight here in front of the American people. It's the number one issue, Neil, for voters across the country.\par \par \uc2 It's not just a Republican issue. It's Republicans, independents, and Democrats are seeing this invasion on the southern border, these illegal migrants coming across. And this is not a immigration debate. This is a national security debate, as we're looking at the terror threat right now in this country because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's chaos and open border policies.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you're quite right to say the immigration issue is the overarching issue. It was in caucuses and primaries one after the other, but I don't believe this specific issue of impeaching Mayorkas was.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, I do want to get your thoughts on delaying this delivery of these Mayorkas articles until next week. Are you for that? Are you OK with that?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: I think it's the right thing to do. I mean, whether it gets shipped over to us on Wednesday for a -- the beginning of the impeachment on Thursday, literally, we will be sat down in the Senate chamber, we will be sworn in, we will sign a book as jurors.\par \par \uc2 We sit as a jury for these articles impeachment sent to us by the House. I think it's a good thing it's going to come over on Monday. That way, we have the week in front of the American people to have this deliberation. It'll be a very important vote, because Schumer wants to bury this. He doesn't want to talk about this anymore.\par \par \uc2 The American people want accountability. They're exasperated. We see what's going on. This is a solvable problem, but Mayorkas should be held accountable for what's happened. The Senate will be the jurors next week. It'll be in front of the American people, as it should be.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You touched on the idea that maybe Schumer doesn't want to deal with this, but a lot of your colleagues, Senator, on the Republican side don't seem all too eager to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 Do you think there are even more than a couple of dozen of your colleagues who would want to take this route, go this route, and impeach Mayorkas?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, let's be clear, Neil. He has been impeached. The House has impeached Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 Now the question is the trial. We sit as jurors. So, if one of my colleagues decides that they don't want to convict Mayorkas, that will be their choice as a U.S. senator. But we need to have the trial.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I'm asking -- I'm sorry. You're quite right. You're quite right, sir. And I apologize.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel there are enough votes for conviction just among your colleagues? And, of course, it's a Democratic-run Senate, by the tiniest of margins, I grant you, but that a lot of those colleagues don't want to take that extra step and that a conviction is not in the offing, even among a good many of your own fellow Republicans.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, look, I mean, if -- as a juror, you need to look at the evidence, as any thoughtful juror would. I will trust the judgment of my colleagues.\par \par \uc2 But, look, the reality is, the Democrats control the majority of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . It's highly likely Schumer will try to dismiss this outright up front and never have a trial. He realizes that this is a political crisis for the White House and for every single Senate Democrat who have supported President Biden with these open border policies, these policies that President Trump had put in place that were working that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  reversed and stopped.\par \par \uc2 This is a solvable problem. Again, if you look at it, for me, as a father of four, now a grandfather of six, I'm very concerned about the national security threat that's presented by this open border. We have a serious terrorism threat that's occurring as a result of now nearly 10 million illegals who have come across the border, many on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  terror watch list.\par \par \uc2 This is a national security issue. The American people are exasperated. They want something done. This is a chance first to put this out in full display with a trial in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  to show the evidence of what's been going on with the Biden administration and why we're at this point in our history.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator Daines, thank you for taking the time. We do appreciate that, sir.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, there are other issues they have to take up on Capitol Hill, including rebuilding that Baltimore bridge that collapsed a couple of weeks back. But coming up with the funds, that's a lot easier said than done.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie has more on that -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, so the Maryland delegation really wants to pay for the reconstruction of the Key Bridge by using 100 percent federal funds, but whether that's going to cost millions or billions, I will tell you next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it could cost $400 million and take quite a few years to rebuild the Keystone (sic) Bridge in Baltimore after that collapse, a moving target of at least $400 million, as I say.\par \par \uc2 And will the federal government pick up that entire tab? President Biden has already indicated that he wants the federal government to deal with it, and not necessarily Maryland in this case.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie has been following the back-and-forth on this, joins us from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 That's a really good guess. We have heard all sorts of numbers from $400 million to $2 billion, a lot of different numbers out there. The headline today was that the Maryland delegation essentially says they're going to come and propose, offer up a bill that's going to help pay for the reconstruction of the Key Bridge by using 100 percent federal funding.\par \par \uc2 The catch, though, is Neil, that lawmakers in this moment have no clue how much that actually is going to cost. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN CARDIN (D-MD): It depends on the design of the bridge, the modern technologies to make sure it protects. There's been a 3000 percent increase since 1975, when this bridge was constructed, in regards to the amount of traffic cargo that goes through the channel itself. So it needs to meet the current needs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, again, that doesn't really answer the question of the price tag.\par \par \uc2 And whatever the price tag is, Neil, there's already pushback from House Freedom Caucus members on just cutting a blank check. Instead, they're demanding pay-fors for this funding.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Andy Harris, who is a Freedom Caucus member and the only Republican member in the Maryland delegation, sidestepping the question today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY HARRIS (R-MD): Look, there are steps in this process. The first step is about cost-share. There is no debate about cost-share. In this tragedy, Maryland should not have to bear part of the cost. That's going to be the focus of, I believe, the first piece of legislation that comes through.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And, Neil, as is the case for quite literally every issue on the Hill right now, there is absolutely no real timeline on when this might actually happen -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, that's a shocker, Aishah. All right, thank you very much. Great reporting, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill with all of that.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we want to pass along, the president promised in his State of the Union address to sort of zing companies who he says aren't paying their fair share. Part of that effort is to start taxing stock buybacks. Companies, when they have a lot of money, don't know what to do with it, a lot of them buy back the stock. After all, they're run by shareholders, and they reward the shareholders.\par \par \uc2 Well, there's going to be a tax on that of about 1 percent on such a transaction every time it's done. It's meant to be a way to remind companies to pay your taxes, even though a good many of them do. This is yet an added thing on top of the 1 percent, of course, will be a starting figure. Some say it doesn't end there.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE ROWE, FOUNDER, MIKEROWEWORKS FOUNDATION: Nothing in the history of Western civilization has ever become more expensive more quickly than a four-year degree, not food, not health, not energy, not real estate, nothing.\par \par \uc2 People say, Mike, how could college get so expensive? The answer is, how could it not? We freed up a bottomless pile of free money and turned the screws on a whole generation of kids and told them they'd be finished if they didn't take this path.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, the administration is trying to make it easier for those who have taken out a lot of loans to get some relief, up to 30 million Americans now qualifying or will be qualifying for this relief of up to $20,000 of interest knocked off, some paid off entirely, as the administration continues to ease their burden, even as the price of a four- year degree continues to rocket, perhaps because we're doing these very things.\par \par \uc2 Julia Manchester, The Hill national politics reporter, Kat Timpf with us, co-host of "Gutfeld," "You Can't Joke About That: Why Everything Is Funny, Nothing Is Sacred, and We're All in This Together." That was a very, very funny book.\par \par \uc2 KAT TIMPF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Guys, welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 Kat, end it with you and your thoughts on this. You know the predictable response that why are we paying to forgive loans that some other Americans had to pay, did pay, and are getting no relief? You say?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes, I say -- and I have said this before, but I again don't even know why we keep calling it erasing or canceling or forgiving, when it's nothing of the sort.\par \par \uc2 It is, as you pointed out, actually just reassigning these payments to people, this money to people who didn't have anything to do with it at all and maybe didn't go to college themselves. I mean, this could cost to the tune of $100 billion or more to taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 And also, just, I happen to be someone who notices that people tend to behave based on incentives. And doing this, there's no incentive for colleges to charge any less and no incentive for people to not take out loans, students to not take out loans to go, no matter how much it costs or how big those loans are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I mean, I do know this.\par \par \uc2 And, Julia, I think we have chatted about this ourselves several times here, that the rate of college inflation, college cost, tuition, room and board, is running triple what the average inflation rate is, and has been for many decades. This only fuels it, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 JULIA MANCHESTER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE HILL: Yes, absolutely. I think there's this argument.\par \par \uc2 But I think, when we look at the political perspective to this as to why President Biden is doing this, he would argue this is a promise he made to his base. And this goes along with what he talked about on the campaign trail. But, at the same time, this is a base issue for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 And he is seeing young voters in his coalition being, quite frankly, less enthusiastic, and he needs those voters to turn out and vote for him in November. And there is this feeling among Democrats and progressives that this is going to help galvanize them.\par \par \uc2 So this is -- I think we're going to see maybe not only this initiative being pushed by President Biden. You're going to hear and you already have heard different language on other issues impacting young people. The Israel-Gaza war is obviously very important to a lot of young voters. They feel strongly about it. He's addressed that.\par \par \uc2 So this is -- the timing of this and doing it in a swing state like Wisconsin is not accidental.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it does have the desired impact.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you about that, Kat, because you could argue that this galvanizes young voters. But what about young voters who aren't getting this relief? What about young voters who didn't go to college? What about young voters who in fact did pay off their loans or chose other routes, like trade schools or the rest? What happens to the young electrician or the young plumber who starts saying, well, where's my gravy train?\par \par \uc2 Isn't it going to anger them?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes, and that's why I think messaging about what this really is, is so important, because if you just say, oh, it's cancellation of forgiveness, then you do sound kind of like a jerk if you're not for it.\par \par \uc2 Why wouldn't you want just somebody's loans to just go away when it would make things so much easier for that person? And they took all this money, and they're burdened by it. That does sound like a compassionate thing, but that's not what it actually is.\par \par \uc2 It is reassigning it often to that electrician or to that person who maybe went to a less expensive school or made other decisions because of this, that person that had absolutely nothing to do with it, and then, of course, that student in the future perhaps having to pay even more, as college becomes even more expensive the more and more government gets involved.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Guys, I wish we had more time. Unfortunately, we don't.\par \par \uc2 But it's going to be a big issue right now, because this is expanding, despite constitutional fights as to whether the president can do this, and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the former speaker, saying that, technically, he can't do this, that this is something that only the House can do. But that debate rages on.\par \par \uc2 Julia and Kat, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I did want to close with a quick peek at the corner of Wall and Broad today. Obviously, this frustration in the Middle East and how things are going is lifting up the price of gold, not helping stocks, and raising issues as to whether the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is going to be able to cut rates so soon. Doesn't look like so soon right now.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020240410ek4900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Special Edition Of "Hannity": Countdown To The Election\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Newt Gingrich, Kayleigh McEnany, Tomi Lahren,  Charles Payne, Sean Duffy, Tammy Bruce \par \uc2 7,985 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to the special edition of "Hannity": Countdown to the Election.\par \par \uc2 In just 210 days, that's it, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can finally be held accountable by you. And despite terrible approval numbers, the president is willing to do whatever he can do to cling on to power. That includes turning his back on our closest ally, the only democracy in the Middle East, all in an effort to appease people in Michigan chanting "death to America", in this specific town of Dearborn. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Death to America! Death to America! Death to America! Death to America!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We have a lot more on the threat from within. Joe's political ambition is clearly way, way more important, a priority than supporting an ally after the worst radical terrorist attack in their history, truly repulsive and that's saying a lot about Joe.\par \par \uc2 Also, transportation secretary, your buddy pothole Pete who enjoys a personal security detail, he doesn't see a problem with crime in D.C. I'm sure he's living in his nice little bubble, and one report is now calling it the trendy new splurge for millennials. Most of you would know it as buying groceries. We have a full report on the inflation crisis that is not going away under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right. But first, if you blinked, you might have missed it. The president was in Wisconsin today. He had a huge -- not so huge speech, under 15 minutes.\par \par \uc2 On the way there, Biden nearly tripped up the short little baby stairs, oh good, Joey, good job. Anyway of Air Force one but was thankfully able to stay upright this time. Remember, he abandoned the big boy stairs a long time ago..\par \par \uc2 Joe then spoke to a handful of voters about instructions that he got from his wife, always listen. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And Madison mayor, thank you -- where is she? There you are. Stand up.\par \par \uc2 I get instructions from my wife who's a school teacher. She said make sure you say hello to the mayor. I really like being with her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right after that fun opener by Joe -- well, he got to the real purpose of his speech. The president announced another bold new plan, basically, I would view it as kind of bribing voters with your tax dollars. Last week, it was a $500 billion green energy spending spree, full of handout out to all of Joe's allies. And interestingly, a lot of swing state money.\par \par \uc2 And this week, another round of student loan forgiveness by the way, a unilateral act at the Supreme Court of our land rule was unconstitutional but the Biden's, they never let the rule of law get in the way of a good time paid for with your money. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Tens of millions of people's debt was literally about to get cancelled, but then some of my Republican friends and elected officials and special interest sued us, and the Supreme Court blocked us. But that didn't -- well, that didn't stop us. I mean it sincerely, we continue to find alternatives paths to reduce student debt payment, that are not challengeable.\par \par \uc2 Folks, I will never stop to deliver student debt relief for hardworking Americans and it's only in the interest of America that we do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Defy the Supreme Court of the land, pick and choose what laws to enforce, not enforce, lie to the American people that the border is closed and secure when it's not, lie to the American people about never talking to your son, your brother or anybody for that matter about their foreign business deals. Interesting life of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, if successful, this will be a massive transfer of wealth from Americans that did not go to college to 30 million people that have college degrees including those that attended graduate school. They're the ones that put their signature to pay back the loan on it, not the rest of you.\par \par \uc2 And in case, you're wondering, there is no income cap on this round of student loan forgiveness. In other words, a welder, maybe make 60, 70 grand a year, will soon be paying the student loan of a tenured sociology professor for example making 250 grand. That sound fair to you? Is that the definition of fairness?\par \par \uc2 Now, meanwhile, the colleges, the universities win either way, show will never hold them accountable for jacking up prices year after year after year because as it turns out the whole middle class Joe act, that was all a big lie, too.\par \par \uc2 Now even some Democrats are having a hard time stomaching \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I wouldn't go out there and extol the miracle of the Biden economy. It just drives me crazy when he does that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Biden doesn't talk about the wars very often, should he?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTFIEID FEMALE: Absolutely, not just -- and Israel, Ukraine. We have superpower. He is the commander-in-chief, absolutely. He should speak out more and also we should cover what he's saying because often when he speaks, nobody listens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let's see. Donna Brazile, David Axelrod, James Carville, let's see, \uc2 Van Jones\uc2 , Maureen Dowd, a lot of prominent Democratic voices don't like Joe. Privately, most Democrats have been frustrated with Biden for years. Remember, Obama himself famously looked down on Joe, warning, quote, never underestimate Joe's ability to F things up.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, Biden was passed over to be Obama's successor. Despite all of her glaring problems, even \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  at the time was considered a better choice than Joe.\par \par \uc2 In reality, very few people actually like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It's clear his family is not telling him the truth about probably it would be a good idea to retire. He's been a horrible president for this country. He's in way over his head, always has been, and he's trying to get by win re-election by handing out your money and smearing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and lying about abortion, and calling Republicans racist and sexist and all the other names they call conservatives and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Now, earlier today, President Trump announced his position on abortion and he rightly believes, if you believe in the Constitution, that it should be left up to the states and for the people in every state to decide. That is called oh democracy. That is called states rights for example.\par \par \uc2 It's something Democrats claim to love and revere, not true. Trump emphasized that as president, he will respect every state's decision. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  said in the debate, with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , right here in this program when I had to press him again and again and again. Do you support abortion on demand in months seven, eight and nine, he finally answered and said, that should be a decision between a doctor, a woman and their conscience. So, no restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Now Trump made it clear he made it unequivocal, there will be no national abortion ban under his administration. In response, the Biden campaign, they do what they always do. They just flat out lied, telling supporters that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a secret plan to ban all abortions nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Now, you got to get ready for this, the only thing missing in that line was and the Russians are working with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to bring abortion back to make it illegal in America. The Russians weren't in that. I'm shocked.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats will be lying a lot in the next 210 days because they cannot run on their failed record. As Trump put it on Truth Social, quote, you know the Democrats are reeling when they have no response to my recent statement on abortion other than he's only kidding, he will change it or he won't do that, he'll do something else. Well, I guess this means that we will win the election because they are so bad on every issue, this is the only issue that they're focused on. Great point.\par \par \uc2 Now to be fair, there is one other thing that some Democrats are focused on and that is using the Justice Department of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as a political weapon against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . In fact, the Biden campaign recently posted an ad on X bragging Joe has way more cash on hand, thanks to all of the legal expenses that his Department of Justice is putting on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that he has incurred because of weaponized DOJ of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mike Lee of Utah summed it up pretty well. He said, quote, never thought any us president would ever resort to this, let alone brag about it and avoid condemnation by the press for doing so.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile that cash on hand gap is getting smaller, a massive, record setting $50 million Trump campaign fundraiser this past Saturday night in Palm Beach in Florida, in the free state where I live.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, here with more, FOX News contributor, former speaker of the House, Newt Gingrich is with us.\par \par \uc2 All right. Let's get your overall take on this. I think you would agree they can't run it on their record. You can't run on your record. You got to lie, smear, besmirch, slander even, and then weaponize your Justice Department and throw 91 charges against your opponent. I guess that's all you got, right?\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, and -- but also as you point out, they're in a position to simply try to buy votes. Most of the green initiative is buying votes. This whole student loan thing is buying votes. The effort they put into rewarding the teachers union is buying votes.\par \par \uc2 When you come down to it, the Democratic Party is a combination of a very, very left-wing ideology and a machine that has to have your money to survive. So I think what they're hoping is that they can get away with breaking the law.\par \par \uc2 You made a point here that really has to be I think focused on. The Supreme Court said that what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing on student loans is unconstitutional. Now, he's the first president I know of who simply ignores the Supreme Court. He wants to give away more of your money. He doesn't care what the court says. In fact he boasts he doesn't care what the court says.\par \par \uc2 So you have a routine lawbreaking and my fear, Sean, is when you get to September and October and none of this is working, you can't imagine what they'll do because they have no limits. They don't believe in the rule of law. They believe in the rule of power.\par \par \uc2 They're proving every day that they think they can get away with things that are crazy and that they don't care what the country thinks and they still have most of the left-wing propaganda media covering for them and protecting them. It's amazing how many things they get away with because nobody at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " or "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " or the three major networks, none of them will go out and take them on. None of them will tell the truth.\par \par \uc2 So people now know that, people know we have way too many illegal immigrants. People know we have too much crime. People know that when they go to the grocery store, they're not able to buy what they used to and if the price is the same, the size is smaller. If the size is the same, the price is higher, and I think all these things mean that the average American is going to condemn the Biden administration as bad for their lives and bad for America.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's hard to see other than just plain breaking the law and finding ways basically to make Trump in prison. I mean, we just had an election in Senegal, the winner was a reformer who only got out of jail 10 days before the election. And I thought there's certain parallel here if the Democrats have their way, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will spend all the fall campaign in jail.\par \par \uc2 It is despicable. It's dangerous. It undermines America and I think every American should be putting pressure on their House and Senate members of both parties to stand up to this tyrannical president who was violating the law every day.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And it does it with regularity. The entire 10 million plus illegal immigrants, there's a case and point about that. And you're right about using taxpayer money to buy votes. I mean that's -- you know, clearly and defying the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , and not enforcing the laws of the land, and picking and choosing which laws you will enforce and which you to choose not to.\par \par \uc2 We know their playbook, though. January 6, January 6, but they'll forget the main details of things that we've learned \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not try to commander the vehicle, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has four witnesses in the days leading up to January 6 that he was willing to authorize 10,000 troops, and in writing, the D.C. mayor that had to sign off on it, yeah, she said in writing she would not. We know the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  chief also was requesting in the days prior National Guard troops. So they'll demagogue January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Then they're going to get to the usual playbook, Republicans are racist, sexist, misogynist, I give the whole list. And then we have the issue of abortion, which I believe hurt Republicans in and I believe \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  was a case in point. And he lost by 16 points, he didn't have exceptions for rape, incest, the mother's life, and that hurt every Republican down ticket.\par \par \uc2 So my question for you is as the great historian and strategist that you are, what do you tell Republicans? I would argue that they've got a -- if they're being lied about on abortion, they better match every Democratic lie with the truth in an ad or however else they are messaging this, or they will risk being positioned by the Democrats? What is your advice?\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: My advice is to listen to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Trump has laid out a perfectly rational strategy which by the way Judge Ginsburg said back in the early 1990s in a speech that she thought Roe versus Wade was wrong, because it took the decision of abortion away from the American people and gave it to a group of lawyers. So in a funny way, he and \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  are on the same side.\par \par \uc2 Let the American people decide, different states will have different decisions. And I must say most of the Right to Life leaders understand exactly what President Trump's trying to do. He is trying to reach the largest possible number of babies saved, he's trying to create the best possible environment in the current culture and in the current laws and he's doing it in a way which is extraordinarily pro-life without alienating people with the kind of fear-mongering the Democrats have.\par \par \uc2 I thought what he did his statement was exactly right. And I think people read it carefully and think about it. We are a country that has to have this conversation. I personally and as you know Callista is very deeply committed to life. I personally would like to have the fewest possible abortions, period. We were very pleased to go to the 50th anniversary of a pregnancy group in Florida that has saved 23,000 lives during the history of the group.\par \par \uc2 I compare that to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  going to an abortion clinic where they're killing babies. I think the contrast can't be deeper and I think Trump is exactly in the right place.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, on most issues, Trump's understanding intuitively of where the American people are is better than any Republican pollster or professional, and I think candidates ought to pay attention just as I used to do with \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 . They ought to pay attention to what President Trump is saying, try to figure out why he's saying it and see to what degree they can learn by watching I think one of the most extraordinary political leaders of my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Speaker Gingrich, always great to have you. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 All right. Here now with more, co-host of "Outnumbered", \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , and the host of Outkick's "Tomi Lahren is Fearless", Tomi Lahren.\par \par \uc2 Kayleigh, let me start with you. Newt mentioned \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  and her opinion. We heard from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  today. There was a time that Democrats thought like \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , and there was a time when a Democratic president never thought I'd quote him on this show very often \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  said about abortion it should be legal but rare.\par \par \uc2 How far they have come especially when you listen to \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  when I pressed him hard and that debate with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , he said no exceptions. That is between a doctor, a woman and their conscience. No law banning abortion even in the eighth and ninth month none. Thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , HOST OF "OUTNUMBERED": That's the position of the Democratic Party and I would love to see a moment on the debate stage where you have President Trump and you have \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and you have President Trump look at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and say, when does a baby feel pain, President Biden? When? He couldn't answer that question and do you support a cut off at that point, eighth month, ninth month.\par \par \uc2 Remember President Trump did this very brilliantly with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in that third debate, and I believe won over a lot of conservatives and pro- lifers in doing so. That question -- the way you question Newsom, that needs to be done to Biden, to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . And then finally, I'd love to see the Republican Party shape out our pro-life agenda, pro-life message with support for vulnerable women during pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  has laid out nine points in the overturning of Roe, nine points where we can support mothers during pregnancies who are vulnerable and we need to get on that because that's a powerful message in a general election.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: The Democrats are bleeding their core constituency, their base, African Americans, Hispanic Americans, Asian-Americans, young people and suburban women.\par \par \uc2 Tomi Lahren, so obviously, they will try to demagogue this issue to death. What do you think -- what would be the best messaging to suburban moms and young people on the issue of abortion where I would argue probably a majority are either at first trimester, 15 weeks like Dobbs, but they certainly don't want to ban on abortion, not that I know any state that's going to do that?\par \par \uc2 TOMI LAHREN, OUTKICK HOST: Right. Well, the Democrats have something once again that we don't have, and they have cohesion and unity on their side. The Republican Party on this issue unfortunately does not. Now, I'm not saying that every conservative and every Republican has to have the same personal feelings on the issue, but every Republican if we want to win in November should take the issue to where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has taken it and said this is a states rights issue.\par \par \uc2 Every time and I spent the day going through the other news networks that have posted in my opinion lies about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's position on this issue and I have gone through the comments and I have corrected people. People that have said, oh, this is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted is the overturn of Roe v. Wade, don't believe him. They don't understand that the overturn of Roe v. Wade did exactly what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted it to do and what he talked about today which is return this issue to the states where it belongs.\par \par \uc2 Republicans need to have a cohesive message on this. They need to come out and say there's not going to be a national ban and abortion, because logistically, that's not going to happen anyway. And if they're able to do that and they're able to come together and unify on that issue no matter where they stand personally, we can win in November.\par \par \uc2 But when you have offshoots like former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  going off on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for talking about state's rights, that's where we're going to lose people, especially the suburban women. I remind those folks that are passionately pro-life and I love those folks, but if we do not win in November, none of your agenda when it comes to pro-life issues or anything else is going to happen if we continue to lose elections. We do not get this issue right, we will lose elections from here on out.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Exit question and, Kayleigh, I'll start with you. Where do you think the country is in terms of when abortion should be legal? Is it first trimester? Is it 15 weeks like Dobbs? Is it less than that? What do you think, the country? I'm not asking your personal opinion.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Depend on questions asked, but yes, it depends how questions asked, but there's a significant amount of polling that shows that people are for a 15 or a 20 week cut off. When it is explained in the right way, do you want to be with North Korea and China? I certainly don't. Do you want to be one of seven countries in the world that doesn't cut off abortion at the point in which a human life feels pain?\par \par \uc2 When it is messaged in the right way, the American people are with the movement. But it has to be message. We have to be bold on it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And 47 countries, if my memory serves me right, Tomi Lahren, including France and others, European countries, they put a cap on 12 weeks to 14 weeks. That's it, it's over, That was your window.\par \par \uc2 LAHREN: Right and I think that the states that are grappling with issue would be wise to take that up and they'd be wise to push for those limits. But I do uh firmly believe that the states need to decide this. California is never going to decide that and that's unfortunate. New York never going to decide that and that is unfortunate. But there are other states that will. They will be realistic on this issue and they and Republicans that stand with it.\par \par \uc2 We'll win those elections and they'll do a lot to save not only this issue but the rest of the country right along with it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Thank you both. Tomi, good to see you, and, Kayleigh, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, how sad is this? Okay, essential items, not luxuries. They're now officially luxuries for many Americans in Biden's America. People can't afford the bare necessities anymore.\par \par \uc2 Charles Payne reacts as we continue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. So many of you are feeling the burden of Bidenomics and it might cost Joe this in November, in the election. In its annual letter to shareholders, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  warned that inflation and interest rates may remain elevated and with prices already soaring across the board -- well, that could spell big trouble for Biden.\par \par \uc2 Now, things have become so bad that everyday items like groceries, they are quickly believe it or not, becoming a luxury, especially for young consumers. Now, meanwhile, many Americans are also struggling to afford housing. According to a recent survey, over 20 percent of people are now skipping meals and selling their belongings just to afford wherever it is they are living.\par \par \uc2 Renting a home is now officially cheaper than owning one in America's top 50 metro areas.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, the host of making money on FOX Business, Charles Payne.\par \par \uc2 You know, Charles, if -- if the old adage, peace and prosperity drives elections, which historically they have. Okay, we got war in the Middle East, we got war in Europe, we got now threats with Iran in the next, you know, 48 hours again, they could be attacking Israel that could blow up the entire region, and the economy being as bad as it is, and 60 percent of Americans living paycheck to paycheck and many of those people are buying bare necessities on credit cards. Well, that would say, on both fronts, he's losing badly on the economy.\par \par \uc2 How do people deal with this?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: You know, you're 100 percent right and I think adding injury to insult is that the insistence by President Biden and a whole lot of economists who are obviously carrying water that people are doing better than they think they are. You know, this whole notion that it's a vibe that you're not doing well, it's so insulting to the American public.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with the food bill, $445 more a month for the average family than a year ago. This year, OJ is up 46 percent, bacon up 23 percent, coffee up 22 percent, eggs up 19 percent.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  also in that letter warned of a spike in inflation. We already knew inflation was higher for longer that, you know, what we kind of -- we knew that was going to happen as a consequence of so-called free money. Free money isn't free. You can try to buy elections with it, but it's backfired miserably and \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  warning because of the government spending that does not stop, the spigot will not stop, the stimulus that's already in the system by the way at $