d mostly toward Gen Zers, that brought students and locals together in worship and prayer, singing and personal testimonies.\par \par \uc2 And at some point, the event moved from Stegman Coliseum at the University of Georgia to a parking lot, and pickup trucks became impromptu baptismal pools, their beds filled with water.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is insane, thousands of college students getting baptized in truck bays.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I was atheist until three years ago when Christ called my name.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: And he showed me that the love of Jesus is the greatest love story of all time.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now, Tonya Prewett, founder of Unite U.S., Unite Us, excuse me. And Nate Kearns, a junior at the University of Georgia. Nate, now we actually have video of the moment that you were baptized. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Nate, had you planned on getting baptized that night?\par \par \uc2 NATE KEARNS, RECENTLY BAPTIZED: I had not. No, ma'am. I just heard the call from the Lord and he said, be obedient. And I listened to him to take a step of faith and let my fraternity brothers watch that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wow. Tonya, I know you've organized multiple revivals like this at schools, including at Auburn, which we covered last year. What's going on here among our young people?\par \par \uc2 TONYA PREWETT, UNITE US FOUNDER: You know, first, I just want to say that these events can only be explained by a move of God. We have thousands of students showing up at these events and hundreds giving their lives to Jesus. Hundreds getting baptized. We're hearing testimonies from these students saying, hey, I walked in contemplating suicide, but I left with the most joy and peace I've ever experienced.\par \par \uc2 It's just amazing and it's moving. We have universities reaching out on our website every day saying we're desperate for revival. Please bring \uc2 Unite\uc2  to our university. It's just a work of God.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's incredible to see this. And again, at a time where so many people are so down about our youth. Nate, what was your relationship with religion, with Christ before last Wednesday?\par \par \uc2 KEARNS: Yes, my experience is very -- I grew up culturally Christian, went to church, but there was no intimate relationship with Christ. Wednesday, I had fully committed myself to Christ a month prior and I just felt the calling to obey the Lord.\par \par \uc2 And I'm here to say that for you at home, no matter how broken you think you are, Jesus Christ's love is more powerful than that. And he wants to enter into a personal relationship with you. If you call on the name of the Lord, you will not be forsaken.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wow. Well, I want to have everyone tonight, watching the Angle, see how your night ended once all the baptism had actually finished. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK. Tanya, first of all, I always cry when I hear Amazing Grace. It just always is -- it's one of those hymns that just -- it's just -- I can't -- like, I can't -- I better not even talk about it because that is so beautiful. And something is happening here in this country. There's a deep, deep, deep desire for something more than going out, getting drunk, you know, hooking up and, you know, doing whatever college kids do. Tonya, close it out.\par \par \uc2 PREWETT: Yes. So, I think we have college students all over our nation that are saying, you know what, I'm hungry for more and I'm desperate for hope. And in mentoring college students, I just heard about all the unimaginable pain that students are going through and they're seeking for something.\par \par \uc2 And what we're bringing to them is just hope and truth. And we offer salvation and freedom and connection. And our prayers that these students will leave this event and get connected in a local church or ministry and carry this on as a lifelong decision.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tonya, Nate, very, very cool. I'm so happy we did this segment tonight. Nate, I'm an Alabama fan, but tonight I'm a Georgia fan. All right, great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 All right. The Biden White House launches a new campaign strategy, and Raymond sees an eclipse that you may have missed. Seen and unseen, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for seen and unseen, where we reveal the stories behind the headlines. For that, we turn to Fox News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, the president, I see, was out on the stump today.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, he did look stumped now and then, Laura. As you know, the president has been having some difficulties on the road and campaigning is becoming something of a challenge for him. Here he is in Wisconsin today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: And when Kamala and I ran, this lead -- in this lead county, we made a commitment to fix our broken student loan system.\par \par \uc2 Folks, I will never stop to deliver student debt relief and hardworking Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, Laura, there's no way he can win voters over with that oratory. So, the White House has shifted strategy. You've heard of Uber Eats. Meet Biden Eats. A new delivery service where the president not only picks up your food, but he shares it with you at your home for political purposes. We recently showed you that piece where he went to an African- American family in South Carolina. You'll remember.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Mr. President, it's a pleasure serving you