ident has the power to help people. And that's --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's the whole purpose.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yeah. And we appreciate what you do for our country, so thank you.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thank you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yeah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Holy mackerel. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No, Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Holy mackerel. So that's all they have to say.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: My lord.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Raymond, tell us about the -- this eclipse mania. It seemed to grip the nation today. I was out there. I had a few moments. But tell us.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yeah, well, I'm going to get to that in a second. You know, look, I love the idea that it unified everybody. But again, the presidential candidates posted their own eclipse memes. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That was obviously \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's. Yeah, but then -- but then Biden posted a safety public safety ad. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Folks, enjoy the eclipse, but play it safe. Don't be silly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Don't be silly. Apparently, the day anchors at CNN didn't hear that, Laura.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Welcome back to Eclipse Across America.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is basically what's going to happen. If I can reenact the moon, we'll be going in front of the sun. Poor Sanchez at this point. And that's a partial. I think we've got a full eclipse there.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is kind of where things are right now.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yeah. Right there?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: There we go.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: All right. Just peeking out in Mazatlan.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Mazatlan.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: And eventually it will be a total. Zone of totality, which is going to be amazing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is CNN. And Laura, this thing was one of the most underwhelming events. I mean, all we had here in New Orleans was cloud covers. I mean, I've seen approaching hurricanes that were darker. What did you do?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I was outside gardening, as I always am, when I'm not working. And my dogs are outside and I put glasses on them. I'm not sure they like --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Oh, that sounds thrilling.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, Zoe didn't like it. She's always cockeyed. You know, she has that one cockeyed. Even the glasses were cockeyed. And Andy didn't like hers at all.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Maybe she stared into the sun during the last eclipse. That will hurt you. Don't do that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Raymond. Up next, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020240409ek4800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Astronomical Event Takes Over U.S. Skies; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Abortion Position; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Pushes Student Loan Relief; \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  Refuses To Give Congress \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Audio; Rights Comparison Between States; Patchwork Of Laws Frustrates Homeowner Efforts; Biden Administration Promises NATO Membership; \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  Planning Battle Against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  And Possibly \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  In Lebanon; House Speaker Mike Johnson Faces Possible Ouster If He Allows Vote On Ukraine Funding Bill; President Trump Releases Video Stating Legality Of Abortion Should Be State Decision; President Biden Proposes New Plan To Relieve Student Loan Debt\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, David Spunt, Jonathan Hunt, Madison  Scarpino, Danamarie McNicholl, Gillian Turner, Trey Yingst, Brit Hume,  Jessica Tarlov, Bill McGurn, Kimberly Strassel \par \uc2 7,572 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2024\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: He feels guilty.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Judge, do you want to go.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: No, I'll wait until tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Harold, do you want to go?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I do not. It's going to be a great game tonight. Who do you got?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'm watching Fox. I don't know what you're doing, Harold.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's great.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I don't know. All right, that's it for us, everybody. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, Dana, Perdue.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Hey, Perdue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Don't even need the points. All right, thanks.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No name calling.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  declines to endorse the national abortion ban. Tonight, the fallout from all sides.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, President Biden is going to try again to forgive student loan debt for millions of Americans after courts have shut down previous attempts.\par \par \uc2 And what rights do accused squatters have? Well, it depends on where they're squatting. We'll bring you that.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, I'm Bret Baier. Tens of millions of Americans look to the skies today to catch hopefully a protected glimpse of a rare total eclipse of the sun. Weather cooperated along much of the path of totality, the lighting people who had ventured from around the world to what were supposed to be prime viewing locations, many others witnessed varying degrees of a partial eclipse.\par \par \uc2 Chief Correspondent Jonathan Hunt has a complete wrap up tonight from one of those prime spots, Indianapolis. Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. It has been an incredible day for so many Americans, awe inspiring, breathtaking in its beauty, a cosmic coincidence that we won't see in the U.S. again for 20 years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (voice over): With telescopes and eyes pointed to the sky, tens of millions of Eclipse watches from Mexico to Maine took in a rare and extraordinary total eclipse of the sun.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was definitely tearworthy, once in a lifetime.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's incredible. It's everything people say it is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I felt like a -- I just felt like whoa.\par \par \uc2 HUNT (voice over): In the United States, the sky first darkened in Texas on the border with Mexico around 12:30 p.m. local time. With totality, the sun entirely covered by the moon experienced for roughly three to four plus minutes in more than a dozen states along the path of the eclipse, a stunning moment for all of us privileged enough to witness it.\par \par \uc2 It really does make you appreciate the beauty of the earth, the beauty of the moon and the sun, the beauty of the universe that we are so privileged to be a part of. It's just quite an extraordinary thing.\par \par \uc2 Those under clear skies saw partial sun coverage until just before totality when points of light appeared around the edges of the moon, the result of sunlight moving through valleys on the lunar surface.\par \par \uc2 And finally, one last bright spot like a diamond ring. Before the sun appeared as a black disc with only its outer atmosphere or corona visible, then the process reversed as the sun slowly reemerged.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S. the eclipse from start to finish lasted about an hour and 10 minutes, ending in northeastern Maine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When it got totally dark, I literally was like screaming the whole time. Actually cried a little tear too, little, because it was like overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 HUNT (voice over): With the moon blocking the sun, temperatures dropped, winds change direction, shadows felt differently and birds stopped chirping. Scientists took full advantage of the opportunity for research.\par \par \uc2 NOAH PETRO, NASA GODDARD SPACE FLIGHT CENTER: We learned a lot about the interaction of the sun with the Earth and atmosphere from these events. We learned about the size of the sun from these events. So, it is an educational experience, as well as an immersive one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): So, for scientists, today was a huge research opportunity. For the rest of us, the tens of thousands who were here at the Indianapolis Motor Speedway and the millions more who witnessed the eclipse firsthand across the country. It was a physical and emotional even a spiritual experience and a memory that will certainly last a lifetime, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan Hunt live in Indianapolis. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is now on the record about his current position on abortion. The presumptive Republican nominee released a video today saying the issue should be left to the states in the post Roe vs. Wade era. Now reaction from all sides coming in. Correspondent Madison Scarpino is in Palm Beach, Florida tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The states will determine by vote or legislation or perhaps both. And whatever they decide must be the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 MADISON SCARPINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Former President Trump clarified his stance on abortion and in speculation that he was qualifying his prolife position.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Many states will be different. Many will have a different number of weeks.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): The former president declined to endorse a federal abortion ban after his campaign floated a 15 week ban earlier this year. Trump said he supports it exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You must follow your heart or in many cases, your religion or your faith.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): But Trump's announcement was swiftly denounced by one of the nation's leading prolife groups and evangelicals, including former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  who said it was, "A slap in the face to millions of prolife Americans who previously voted for Trump."\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration also slammed Trump for his role in overturning Roe v. Wade and restricting abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If he were to be put back in a position where he could sign off on a law. He would sign off on a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (voice over): The latest Fox News poll shows that most voters believe abortion should be legal. It's an issue that ranks high on the list for voters in key swing states where Trump and Biden are campaigning this week. Trump raised more than $50 million at a private dinner on Saturday in Palm Beach, nearly doubling what Biden brought in from an event with former presidents Clinton and Obama last month.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of cash on hand, Trump trails Biden, who has $192 million to Trump's $93 million.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SCARPINO (on camera): Former President Trump also reiterated that he supports in vitro fertilization and says that under his leadership, the Republican Party will always support the miracle of life, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Madison Scarpino live in Palm Beach. Madison, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, a New York Appeals Court judge is rejecting former President Trump's bid to delay his April 15th criminal trial overpayments to a former adult film actress. The president is seeking to move that case out of Manhattan. It's the first of four criminal indictments slated to go to trial. This would be the first criminal trial ever of a former president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is once again trying to come up with a way to get American taxpayers to foot the bill for millions of people who took out loans to go to college. He has already been rebuffed by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  on this effort, but this is an election year and the efforts continue.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is at the White House tonight live on the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Top officials fanned out in swing states today to promote Biden's bid to fulfill a campaign promise he made ahead of his first term as he seeks a second. They say the majority of Americans with federally held student debt will benefit if this plan sticks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Folks, I will never stop to deliver student debt relief from hardworking Americans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): In swing state, Wisconsin, President Biden announcing plans to slash student debt for nearly 30 million Americans, wiping out up to $20,000 and accrued interest for borrowers who owe more now than when they took out their initial loan and also giving automatic relief to those who qualify for certain programs or have been in repayment for decades.\par \par \uc2 Trying to stave off legal challenges, the administration is framing it as a financial hardship program designed to address low value degrees and cancel runaway interest.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Some of my Republican friends and elected officials and special interest sued us and the Supreme Court blocked us. But that didn't -- that didn't stop us.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Critics say Biden is overstepping his authority again.\par \par \uc2 GREGG JARRETT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Biden is brazenly defying the law and then he has the audacity, the arrogance to brag about it.\par \par \uc2 What he's really doing is shredding the Constitution. He changed his method of loan forgiveness in the latest plan but you know, the same legal principles that make it unconstitutional still apply.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Republican state attorneys general already planning court challenges. Biden's campaign tying it back to Trump saying, "It's what's to be expected from a fraud like Trump who screwed over students with a fake university and is running to make himself and his friends even richer at our expense."\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Biden also trying to tamp down discontent within his base, making veiled threats to condition future aid to Israel if Netanyahu is government continues to ignore Biden's warnings against an operation in Rafah.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (D-MD): The president has made requests to the Netanyahu government, they have ignored those requests and we've sent more 2,000 pound bombs, we cannot revert back to that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Today, officials denied reports that Israel plans to go into Rafah within days, saying They've vowed to wait for a broader discussion on viable options and alternatives.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, as pressure mounts from the left, families of U.S. citizens being held hostage, our meeting with national security adviser Jake Sullivan here at the White House, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui, later this week, the House will deliver to the Senate articles of impeachment against Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas. What's the White House saying on that tonight?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Well, they seem pretty confident the Democrat led Senate isn't taking this anywhere. Instead framing it as a cynical political stunt. They wrote misstatement, "The worst part is this extreme Republicans have promoted this silly baseless stunt at the same time they've killed an actual bipartisan border security bill that would have addressed the challenges at the border and delivered needed resources to DHS."|\par \par \uc2 The White House also cited legal experts calling it unconstitutional, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live at the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed ahead of new inflation data this week. The Dow lost 11, the S&P 500 resolved two, the NASDAQ gained five.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is standing up to House Republicans tonight and could be facing a contempt of Congress vote himself. GOP lawmakers want audio recordings of President Biden's interview with the Special Counsel last fall. Attorney General Garland says no.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has the latest tonight from the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  refusing to turn over to Congress audio interviews between President Biden and then Special Counsel Robert Hur. The DOJ writing, the committee's interests may not be in receiving information in service of legitimate oversight or investigatory functions, but to serve political purposes that should have no role in the treatment of law enforcement files.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans threatened Garland with contempt of Congress if he didn't produce the audio. Hur interviewed Biden for parts of a two day period in October.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: I'm here today to announce the appointment of Robert Hur as a special counsel\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Garland appointed Hur as special counsel in early 2023 to investigate declassified documents found at several locations connected to the president, including his Wilmington, Delaware garage. Hur criticized the president's handling of the documents, though no criminal charges were filed.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Garland released the transcript of the interview. Several Democrats privately tell Fox News. They believe Republicans want the audio to make President Biden look bad during an election year. His memory mentioned several times in the report.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT HUR, SPECIAL COUNSEL: I could have written my report theoretically in a way that omitted references to the president's memory. But that would have been an incomplete and improper report.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Garland released Hur's report in full to the public weeks ago. The DOJ did cooperate in part today by handing over a transcript featuring the ghost writer of Biden's book.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): And Bret, late this afternoon, we received a statement from Republican House Oversight Chairman James Comer. He's not happy. He says in part, the Biden administration does not get to determine what Congress needs and does not need for its oversight of the executive branch. It's curious, the Biden administration is refusing to release the audio of President Biden's interview with the Special Counsel after releasing the transcript. Why shouldn't the American people be able to hear the actual audio of his answers?\par \par \uc2 No word yet on when or if possibly, a actual contempt vote could take place over in the House, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the Justice Department. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're hearing a lot about migrant squatters these days, different cities. So, what are the laws surrounding this activity? We'll take a look.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in New York as actor Jonathan Majors avoids jail time for assaulting his former girlfriend. He was sentenced to probation in order to complete a yearlong counseling program. The 34-year-old actor had faced up to a year behind bars after he was convicted of misdemeanor assault by a Manhattan jury in December.\par \par \uc2 Fox 17 in Nashville as country music star Morgan Wallen is arrested and accused of throwing a chair from a rooftop bar. The incident happened last night on six storey building and the chair landed close to two police officers. No one was hurt though. The singer's attorney says he is cooperating fully with authorities.\par \par \uc2 And Fox 4 in Billings, Montana with the start of a trial against \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2 's \uc2 BNSF Railway\uc2 , at issue are the lung cancer deaths of two people who lived in a small Montana town where thousands of people were exposed to asbestos from a mine.\par \par \uc2 Plaintiff say the railroad stored harmful materials in a large rail yard in the town and failed to contain the dust from them. The railway is expected to argue there is no proof the victims were exposed to above normal asbestos levels.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at State Farm stadium in Glendale, Arizona from Fox 10, our affiliate there. One of the big