r \uc2 GUTFELD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You made it through.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You blew my mind.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. And more than one percent. All right, our big tech future is all just a scam. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's cashierless checkout was a bunch of baloney.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So much for our cool tech future. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is ditching its cashierless checkout system that let people grab groceries off the shelf and just walk out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We used computer vision, deep learning algorithms, and sensor fusion much like you'd find in self-driving cars. We call it just walkout technology.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It turns out there isn't really any A.I. magic at all. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  reportedly relied on a thousand people in India watching videos to ensure accurate checkouts. So, they really just outsourced the cashiers.\par \par \uc2 Dana, you love this story?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I think it's great. I mean, I love this concept because I like to be able to get in and out. I've used one of these at an airport. I think -- I think it was Newark. I'm like, oh, wow, this is amazing. But then I found out something today that's pretty hilarious. So, if you're my height, you often have to ask somebody to reach something for you.\par \par \uc2 And apparently, it was -- like, there was a lot of confusion in India as to who should get charged because is it the tall person who reached it and gave it to the shorter person and there -- and it caused like some billing issues which I thought was pretty funny.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But how do you know it caused billing issues? Did they charge you or the other guy?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Because at an investors' conference, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  had to admit this at their thing.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Which is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, that they're doing it?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What are they going to do? If they have a thousand people watching you in India, what are they going to do, arrest you?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, they flag it. They don't let you -- because you can't get out the turn style unless you've paid.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Is that it? Because I've seen in airports that --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: There's no open -- you have to get in and out through --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You do?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Like, you put your receipt on the --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Greg?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It is creepy because the whole point is you think you're alone but there are room full -- room full of people watching you. And I do a lot of things when I assume I'm alone, you know, that I -- all of a sudden, I'm going, wow, they watched me do that? Sometimes I scratch a certain place.\par \par \uc2 But it does raise this amazing question. It's a -- it's mind-blowing. So, why didn't \uc2 Amazon\uc2  tell anybody? So, what if all tech is a fraud, right? That the Internet isn't really what you think it is but it's actually two billion people all doing this all at the same time. So, when I go, I'm going to \uc2 Google\uc2  how tall is Dana Perino. When I think that, I put it into \uc2 Google\uc2 , but actually there's about a thousand people running around in this giant library underground going oh, my God. And then they put it back.\par \par \uc2 What if you thought you had a self-driving car. I have a self-driving car. But there's a tiny person under the hood that's going like this. Think about this. Think about how you can fool everybody with A.I., that there is no A.I.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That it's actually billions of people enslaved.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: In India.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Jesse?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't think we should be enslaving Indians, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, I never said that. Jessica said that during the